/* Compiled from /content. Run node tools/build.mjs to rebuild. */
const LESSONS=[{"id":"s001","unit":"cardio","title":"Hemodynamic monitoring","objective":"Use hemodynamic trends alongside bedside findings to recognize changes in perfusion, communicate concerns, and evaluate the response to team-directed care.","concept":"Hemodynamic monitoring combines bedside assessment with measurements of pressure, flow, oxygenation, and fluid balance to help evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion. Pressure is not the same as volume: central venous pressure is an imperfect indicator of intravascular volume, and pulmonary artery occlusion pressure only estimates left-sided filling pressure under appropriate conditions. Trends interpreted with the patient’s condition are generally more useful than an isolated value.","recognize":"Look for changes in blood pressure, heart rate or rhythm, oxygen saturation, urine output, fluid balance, weight, and the patient’s overall condition. A pressure reading alone cannot establish whether circulating volume is adequate. For example, positive-pressure ventilation and altered cardiac or pulmonary conditions can affect pressure interpretation; compare findings over time and with other clinical information.","respond":"Assess the patient and the monitoring signal, then report concerning changes and relevant trends to the responsible clinician. Follow unit protocols for alarms, observations, and ordered monitoring or interventions. Share fluid balance, urine output, oxygenation, and recent changes in support or ventilation. Do not treat a single pressure value as a stand-alone instruction to give fluid or alter vasoactive therapy.","reassess":"After a team-directed intervention, reassess blood pressure, heart rate and rhythm, oxygenation, urine output, and other relevant perfusion findings, comparing them with the prior trend. Report persistent or worsening instability, a new rhythm concern, or a mismatch between monitor readings and the patient’s condition.","trap":"A filling pressure is like a gauge of pressure in a pipe, not a direct measurement of how much fluid is in the whole system. Treating one pressure value as a volume measurement can mislead, so interpret it with the patient’s condition and other trends.","caseStory":"A ventilated adult in intensive care becomes hypotensive and produces less urine. The inferior vena cava appears full on bedside ultrasound, but the left ventricle looks hyperdynamic, with little blood visible at the end of systole. The nurse must decide which findings to promptly share with the team rather than relying on one image alone.","caseReason":"A full-looking inferior vena cava does not rule out hypovolemia in a ventilated patient. The hyperdynamic left ventricle with little visible blood supports possible hypovolemia, while hypotension and reduced urine output add concern about perfusion. The nurse should communicate the findings together and follow the team’s assessment and plan.","teachback":"When a pressure reading and the patient’s other perfusion findings do not seem to agree, what information would you compare and communicate before drawing a conclusion?","questions":[{"stem":"A ventilated adult with hypotension has a pulmonary artery occlusion pressure that appears elevated. The patient is receiving high levels of positive end-expiratory pressure, and other bedside findings do not clearly indicate left-sided filling status. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Treat the pressure as an estimate that may be affected by ventilation, and interpret it with other findings and trends.","Consider the pressure a direct measurement of left ventricular volume and use it to settle the assessment.","Set aside the pressure and base the assessment only on the patient’s blood pressure and urine output."],"feedback":["Correct. Pulmonary artery occlusion pressure estimates left atrial pressure and may not accurately reflect left ventricular end-diastolic volume, particularly with high positive end-expiratory pressure.","This overstates what the measurement shows. The pressure is an indirect estimate, not a direct measurement of ventricular volume.","Blood pressure and urine output are important, but excluding other hemodynamic information would discard useful context. Interpret the pressure with the broader clinical picture."]},{"stem":"A critically ill adult’s monitor alarms for a new abnormal heart rhythm. The patient is awake, but the alarm has sounded repeatedly during the shift and staff are busy with another patient. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Assess the patient and the rhythm signal promptly, then follow alarm protocols and communicate any confirmed concern.","Silence the alarm because repeated alerts make a significant rhythm change less likely.","Wait for the next scheduled observation if the patient is awake and does not report symptoms."],"feedback":["Correct. Prompt assessment and protocol-based response help distinguish a true abnormal rhythm from a monitoring issue and reduce the risk of a missed event.","Repeated alarms can contribute to alarm fatigue, but that is a reason to respond reliably, not to assume an alert is unimportant.","Being awake does not establish that the rhythm is safe. A new rhythm concern warrants timely assessment rather than waiting for a scheduled check."]},{"stem":"After cardiac surgery, an adult receiving vasoactive medication has rapidly changing blood pressure. The arterial-line reading differs from an intermittent cuff reading, and the patient appears more poorly perfused than earlier. Which nursing action best supports the team’s assessment?","options":["Report the changing trend, the difference between readings, and the patient’s perfusion findings promptly.","Use the cuff value alone because an intermittent measurement is more reliable than an arterial line.","Use the arterial-line value alone because continuous monitoring makes bedside findings unnecessary."],"feedback":["Correct. An arterial line provides continuous readings, but measurements and patient findings should be interpreted together, especially during rapidly changing circumstances and vasoactive therapy.","This dismisses useful continuous information without establishing that the cuff is more reliable in this situation. The discrepancy and clinical change should be reported.","Continuous readings are useful, but they do not replace assessment of the patient. Reporting both the trend and bedside findings gives the team relevant context."]},{"stem":"A hypotensive adult receiving mechanical ventilation has a full-looking inferior vena cava on bedside ultrasound. The left ventricle appears hyperdynamic, with very little blood visible at the end of systole. Which interpretation best avoids being misled by a single finding?","options":["A full-looking vena cava does not exclude hypovolemia in a ventilated patient; share the combined findings with the team.","The full-looking vena cava establishes adequate volume, so the ventricular appearance can be disregarded.","The hyperdynamic ventricle proves a specific cause of hypotension, so further assessment is unnecessary."],"feedback":["Correct. In a ventilated patient, a full-looking inferior vena cava can occur despite hypovolemia; a hyperdynamic left ventricle with little blood visible supports considering that possibility.","This treats one ultrasound appearance as a definitive volume measurement, although ventilation can make the finding misleading.","The ventricular appearance is a useful clue but does not, by itself, establish a specific cause. Integrate it with the patient’s condition and communicate the concern."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Monitoring and Testing the Critical Care Patient","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/monitoring-and-testing-the-critical-care-patient"},{"title":"Hemodynamic monitoring - Surgical Treatment - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK6895/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Ventilated Hypotension","detail":"A ventilated adult becomes hypotensive and produces less urine, raising concern for impaired perfusion."},{"label":"Ultrasound Context","detail":"A full-looking vena cava does not exclude hypovolemia; a hyperdynamic left ventricle with little end-systolic blood adds context."},{"label":"Share Combined Findings","detail":"Promptly share the ultrasound, blood pressure, urine-output changes, ventilation, and fluid-balance trends with the responsible team."},{"label":"Trend After Action","detail":"After team-directed action, compare pressure, rhythm, oxygenation, and urine output with prior trends; report persistent instability or worsening perfusion."}]},{"id":"s002","unit":"cardio","title":"Acute coronary syndromes","objective":"By the end of this unit, the nurse will recognize patterns suggesting STEMI or NSTEMI, prioritize time-sensitive team escalation, and reassess for evolving instability.","concept":"Acute coronary syndromes arise when myocardial oxygen supply falls, commonly after an acute coronary event; infarction means ischemic myocardial injury with necrosis. STEMI and NSTEMI are categories of myocardial infarction distinguished in part by the presence or absence of persistent ST elevation. The ECG alone does not establish NSTEMI or measure the depth of injury. Ischemic symptoms, evolving ECG findings, and a changing cardiac troponin support diagnosis. Early recognition matters because troponin can lag, and threatened myocardium can worsen while treatment is delayed.","recognize":"Suspect an acute coronary syndrome with persistent or severe substernal pressure, radiation, breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or unusual weakness. Symptoms may be mild, absent, or described as indigestion; older adults may chiefly report dyspnea, and presentation can be atypical. Obtain and compare ECGs promptly. ST elevation suggests STEMI; ST depression or T wave inversion may occur with NSTEMI. A single early normal troponin does not exclude evolving MI.","respond":"Treat concerning symptoms or ischemic ECG changes as time sensitive: promptly notify the responsible clinician and activate the local acute coronary response. Facilitate prompt ECG acquisition, serial ECGs, and ordered troponin testing; communicate symptom onset, changes, and instability clearly. Support antiplatelet, anticoagulant, nitrate, and reperfusion care only as ordered or under local protocols. Do not wait for a troponin result before escalating suspected STEMI or instability.","reassess":"After actions or treatment, reassess discomfort and associated symptoms, vital signs, mental status, perfusion, and the evolving ECG and ordered troponin results. Report persistent or recurrent pain, new dyspnea or heart failure signs, hypotension, shock, or unstable rhythm urgently. With an inferoposterior pattern, raised neck veins and hypotension despite clear lungs raise concern for right ventricular involvement.","trap":"A normal first troponin is like an early photograph: it may miss a process still developing, so it cannot overrule a concerning presentation or ECG. STEMI and NSTEMI are ECG categories, not a guarantee of how deeply or extensively the heart muscle is injured.","caseStory":"A 68-year-old arrives with persistent pressure in the chest, nausea, and sweating. The first ECG shows ST elevation in contiguous leads, but the initial troponin is within the laboratory reference range. The nurse must decide whether to wait for repeat biomarkers or promptly escalate the ECG and symptoms.","caseReason":"The symptoms and ECG are concerning for STEMI, which requires urgent team evaluation for reperfusion. Troponin may rise after the onset of myocardial injury, so an initially normal result does not justify delaying escalation. The nurse communicates the findings and supports the response under local protocols.","teachback":"If a patient has concerning ischemic symptoms and an abnormal ECG but an initially normal troponin, what should you communicate and why should you avoid waiting for the next result before escalating?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient reports ongoing substernal pressure with sweating and nausea. The ECG shows new ST elevation in contiguous leads, while the first troponin result is not elevated. Which nursing interpretation and action are most appropriate?","options":["Suspect STEMI and promptly escalate the symptoms and ECG through the local acute coronary response without waiting for troponin to rise.","Consider infarction unlikely because the first troponin is normal, and continue routine observation until repeat testing is available.","Favor NSTEMI because biomarker elevation has not appeared, and arrange nonurgent review of the ECG changes."],"feedback":["ST elevation with ischemic symptoms is time sensitive, and troponin may lag behind myocardial injury. Prompt escalation is appropriate despite an initially normal result.","An early normal troponin does not exclude evolving MI and should not delay care for a concerning ECG and symptoms.","The ECG pattern and clinical presentation suggest possible STEMI; the absence of an early troponin rise does not make the findings nonurgent."]},{"stem":"A patient being evaluated for acute coronary syndrome has new ST depression, persistent discomfort, worsening breathlessness, and falling blood pressure. Which action should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Immediately communicate the deterioration and activate urgent team evaluation for instability under the local protocol.","Continue the planned observation and serial testing because NSTEMI generally follows a delayed angiography pathway.","Focus first on obtaining another troponin result, then contact the team if the result is elevated."],"feedback":["Worsening heart failure symptoms and hemodynamic instability are complications that warrant immediate escalation, even when the ECG does not show ST elevation.","Some stable NSTEMI patients have delayed angiography, but deterioration and instability make routine waiting inappropriate.","Troponin supports diagnosis but can lag; obtaining another result should not delay communication of instability."]},{"stem":"An older adult with diabetes reports unusual fatigue, nausea, and breathlessness but denies chest pain. The initial ECG has new ischemic changes, and the first troponin is not elevated. What is the best nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Recognize possible acute coronary syndrome despite the atypical symptoms and facilitate prompt team evaluation with serial ECG and ordered troponin testing.","Treat the presentation as unlikely to be cardiac because chest pressure is absent and the first troponin is not elevated.","Attribute the symptoms to a noncardiac cause unless a later ECG develops ST elevation."],"feedback":["Older adults may present chiefly with dyspnea, and MI can have mild or unrecognized symptoms. Ischemic ECG changes warrant evaluation even before troponin rises.","Absence of chest pain and a normal early troponin do not rule out ACS, particularly with new ischemic ECG findings.","NSTEMI may show ST depression or T wave changes rather than ST elevation, so waiting for ST elevation could miss an evolving event."]},{"stem":"A patient with an inferoposterior MI has less chest discomfort than earlier but is now hypotensive, with distended neck veins and clear lung fields. Which reassessment finding and interpretation should the nurse prioritize communicating?","options":["The combination of hypotension and distended neck veins with clear lungs raises concern for right ventricular involvement and warrants prompt escalation.","Clear lung fields make significant cardiac deterioration less likely, so the nurse should focus on whether chest discomfort returns.","Reduced discomfort suggests stabilization, so the nurse should continue routine reassessment without highlighting the blood pressure."],"feedback":["Right ventricular infarction can present with raised filling pressure, distended neck veins, clear lungs, and hypotension. This pattern merits urgent communication.","Clear lungs do not rule out right ventricular involvement or serious hemodynamic compromise in an inferoposterior MI.","Less discomfort does not negate hypotension and other signs of possible right ventricular dysfunction; the change requires escalation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Myocardial Infarction (MI) - Cardiology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/acute-myocardial-infarction-mi"},{"title":"Acute Myocardial Infarction (MI) - The Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/acute-myocardial-infarction-mi?media=print"}],"frames":[{"label":"ST Elevation Symptoms","detail":"Persistent chest pressure with nausea and sweating plus ST elevation in contiguous ECG leads signals a time-sensitive coronary emergency."},{"label":"Early Troponin Limitation","detail":"An initially normal troponin can lag behind myocardial injury, so it does not cancel concerning symptoms or an ischemic ECG."},{"label":"Activate Coronary Response","detail":"Promptly notify the clinician and activate the local acute coronary response; facilitate ECG assessment without waiting for repeat biomarkers."},{"label":"Recheck Ischemic Signs","detail":"Reassess discomfort, vital signs, perfusion, and evolving ECG; urgently report recurrent pain, hypotension, heart failure signs, or unstable rhythm."}]},{"id":"s003","unit":"cardio","title":"Cardiogenic shock","objective":"Recognize suspected cardiogenic shock from a cardiac cause and signs of impaired tissue perfusion, then escalate and reassess using a structured nursing approach.","concept":"Cardiogenic shock is inadequate tissue perfusion because a primary cardiac problem reduces effective cardiac output. Myocardial ischemia or infarction can impair contraction, rhythm disturbances can disrupt output, and acute structural or valvular problems can make forward flow ineffective. Reduced output compromises oxygen delivery, so cellular dysfunction may appear as altered mentation, oliguria, or elevated lactate. Identifying the cardiac mechanism alongside perfusion failure helps the team target the cause.","recognize":"Look for evidence of hypoperfusion such as new confusion, cool clammy extremities, weak rapid pulses, tachypnea, reduced urine output, or elevated lactate, together with a cardiac trigger such as suspected infarction, a significant rhythm disturbance, or an acute valve problem. Hypotension supports concern, but its absence does not exclude shock. Warm flushed skin may favor distributive physiology; mechanical interference with cardiac filling or emptying suggests obstructive shock.","respond":"Treat suspected cardiogenic shock as time critical: use ABCDE, summon the appropriate urgent clinical team, and communicate the suspected cardiac cause and perfusion trends using a structured handoff. Maintain frequent or continuous vital sign and oxygenation monitoring as available, obtain ordered tests, and prepare for team-directed treatment. Do not independently prescribe fluids or vasoactive drugs, or let a single blood pressure reading delay escalation.","reassess":"After any team-directed action, reassess mental status, pulse quality and rate, skin temperature and capillary refill, respiratory status, blood pressure trend, oxygen saturation, urine output, and ordered lactate or other results. Persistent or worsening hypoperfusion, new hypotension, or deteriorating breathing warrants immediate escalation.","trap":"Do not equate shock with low blood pressure: pressure is one dashboard gauge, while tissue perfusion is the system’s actual output. A patient can be normotensive early yet have poor cellular oxygen delivery, just as a steady gauge does not prove the system is supplying every outlet.","caseStory":"A 68-year-old develops sudden confusion and cool, clammy hands after reporting chest pressure; the pulse is rapid and weak, urine output is falling, and blood pressure remains near the patient's usual level. The monitor shows a new rhythm abnormality. What should the nurse prioritize communicating and doing?","caseReason":"These findings indicate hypoperfusion despite no new hypotension, and the chest symptoms plus rhythm change make a primary cardiac cause concerning. Use ABCDE and urgently notify the team, reporting the perfusion changes and suspected cardiogenic shock rather than waiting for hypotension or treating the rhythm independently.","teachback":"A patient has a suspected cardiac event, a normal blood pressure, and worsening confusion with reduced urine output. What findings would you report, and what would you do next?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with suspected myocardial infarction becomes confused and develops cool, clammy skin, a weak rapid pulse, and reduced urine output. Blood pressure remains near baseline. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Suspect cardiogenic shock because a primary cardiac event may reduce output and tissue perfusion despite preserved blood pressure.","Consider shock unlikely because blood pressure is not low, and wait for another measurement before escalating.","Favor distributive shock because confusion and reduced urine output point specifically to vasodilation rather than cardiac dysfunction."],"feedback":["This is the best interpretation because shock is defined by inadequate tissue perfusion, and hypotension is not required. The suspected cardiac event provides a possible primary cause of reduced output.","This is misleading because some patients with shock are normotensive, particularly early in the course. The other hypoperfusion cues warrant prompt escalation.","Confusion and reduced urine output can indicate hypoperfusion but do not identify distributive shock specifically. The suspected cardiac event makes a primary cardiac cause important to consider."]},{"stem":"A patient with a suspected acute infarction has a new rhythm abnormality, cool extremities, confusion, and falling urine output. Which nursing action is the priority?","options":["Use ABCDE, urgently notify the clinical team through the local escalation pathway, and report the cardiac concern and perfusion findings.","Wait for sustained hypotension before contacting the clinical team, while continuing routine vital sign checks.","Independently begin fluid or vasoactive treatment to improve perfusion before seeking the clinical team's direction."],"feedback":["This is the priority because the patient has concerning hypoperfusion signs and a possible cardiac cause. Structured assessment and urgent team communication should not wait for hypotension.","Waiting is unsafe because hypotension is common but not required for shock. The changing mental status and urine output already warrant escalation.","This exceeds independent nursing decision-making and the supplied material does not support starting these treatments without team direction. Escalate and prepare for treatment under orders or local protocols."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized adult suddenly develops an acute mitral valve problem and then has a weak rapid pulse, cool clammy skin, and new confusion. Which mechanism best explains concern for cardiogenic shock?","options":["The structural valve problem may make forward cardiac output ineffective, causing inadequate tissue perfusion.","The valve problem primarily causes mechanical obstruction of cardiac filling, which defines obstructive shock.","The valve problem causes relative intravascular volume inadequacy through vasodilation, which defines distributive shock."],"feedback":["This fits because acute mitral valve damage can compromise effective cardiac output, and the patient's findings indicate hypoperfusion.","Mechanical interference with filling is an obstructive mechanism, but an acute valve problem is a cardiac structural cause that can impair effective output.","Distributive shock involves vasodilation and relative inadequacy of intravascular volume. The acute valve problem instead supports a primary cardiac mechanism."]},{"stem":"After a team-directed intervention, an adult with suspected cardiogenic shock has blood pressure closer to baseline, but remains confused and has low urine output and an elevated lactate. What is the best nursing interpretation and response?","options":["Perfusion may still be inadequate; promptly report the persistent findings and continue reassessment rather than treating the pressure change as resolution.","Shock has resolved because blood pressure improved; reduce monitoring unless hypotension returns.","The elevated lactate alone confirms the cause is distributive shock, so focus communication on vasodilation."],"feedback":["This is best because persistent confusion, oliguria, and elevated lactate remain concerning for impaired tissue perfusion. A better blood pressure alone does not establish resolution.","This is misleading because hypotension is only one possible sign of shock. Ongoing hypoperfusion cues warrant continued monitoring and escalation.","Elevated lactate supports concern for tissue hypoperfusion but does not establish a specific shock mechanism. The cardiac context and persistent clinical findings must also be considered."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock"},{"title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?ruleredirectid=748"}],"frames":[{"label":"Cardiac Output Falls","detail":"Myocardial ischemia or a new rhythm disturbance can reduce cardiac output and compromise tissue perfusion."},{"label":"Hypoperfusion Without Hypotension","detail":"New confusion, cool clammy hands, weak rapid pulse, and falling urine output indicate hypoperfusion despite near-usual blood pressure."},{"label":"Urgent Team Escalation","detail":"Use ABCDE, summon the urgent clinical team, and communicate perfusion trends and the suspected cardiac cause."},{"label":"Recheck Perfusion","detail":"Recheck mentation, pulse, breathing, pressure trend, and urine output; immediately escalate worsening hypoperfusion or breathing."}]},{"id":"s004","unit":"cardio","title":"Heart failure and cardiomyopathy","objective":"Recognize acute heart failure and pulmonary edema, connect findings to impaired cardiac function, and prioritize safe nursing support and reassessment.","concept":"Acute heart failure can be new or a worsening of chronic disease, with impaired cardiac output, elevated ventricular filling pressures, or both. When left ventricular filling pressure rises, fluid can move into lung tissue and alveoli, causing pulmonary congestion and respiratory distress. Heart failure may occur despite a preserved ejection fraction. Identifying congestion, perfusion concerns, and possible causes helps nurses communicate urgency and evaluate response to treatment.","recognize":"Look for dyspnea, fatigue, crackles, jugular venous distention, edema, and possible S3; severe pulmonary edema may add diaphoresis, wheezing, cyanosis, or frothy sputum. Chest radiography can show edema, while echocardiography assesses cardiac function and possible causes. COPD can resemble pulmonary edema. BNP may help when the diagnosis is unclear, but a normal result does not rule out heart failure, particularly with obesity or preserved ejection fraction.","respond":"Treat acute respiratory distress as urgent: promptly notify the responsible clinician or emergency response team and support oxygenation and ventilation under orders and local protocols. Communicate respiratory findings, perfusion, vital-sign trends, and relevant cardiac history; anticipate evaluation for causes such as ischemia or arrhythmia. Prepare to support prescribed therapies and diagnostic testing. Do not independently initiate or adjust medications, or assume wheezing means COPD.","reassess":"After an intervention, reassess work of breathing, oxygenation, mental status, lung findings, blood pressure, and perfusion, comparing them with baseline and trends. Report worsening distress, new confusion, hypotension, or persistent hypoxemia promptly. If ventilation support is in use, monitor tolerance and signs of deterioration, including reduced alertness or concern for carbon dioxide retention.","trap":"A normal BNP is not a reliable off switch for the heart failure hypothesis, especially in obesity or preserved ejection fraction. Think of BNP as one instrument on a dashboard: interpret it alongside the examination, imaging, history, and response over time.","caseStory":"A patient with hypertension develops rapidly worsening breathlessness, diaphoresis, and widespread crackles. The patient is wheezing, has no reported history of COPD, and appears increasingly restless. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as a respiratory emergency while the cause is evaluated, or to wait for a definitive cardiac test.","caseReason":"The acute dyspnea, crackles, and diaphoresis are concerning for pulmonary edema, and wheezing does not exclude a cardiac cause. The nurse should promptly escalate, support oxygenation and ventilation under protocol, and communicate the findings while the team evaluates the cause. A chest radiograph and other cardiac assessment can help clarify the diagnosis.","teachback":"When a patient has acute breathlessness and wheezing, what findings and follow-up assessments would help you recognize possible pulmonary edema and communicate the need for urgent evaluation?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with no known COPD develops severe breathlessness, diaphoresis, and bilateral crackles. The patient is wheezing and has blood-tinged frothy sputum. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The findings are concerning for acute pulmonary edema and warrant urgent evaluation.","The wheezing makes an obstructive lung exacerbation the most likely explanation.","The sputum finding alone establishes a respiratory infection as the cause."],"feedback":["Severe dyspnea, crackles, diaphoresis, and frothy sputum are consistent with pulmonary edema; wheezing can also occur with left heart failure.","Wheezing does not distinguish COPD from pulmonary edema by itself, particularly without a known COPD history and with these additional findings.","The supplied material associates blood-tinged frothy sputum with pulmonary edema; it does not establish infection from sputum appearance alone."]},{"stem":"A patient with suspected acute heart failure becomes more distressed, with increasing work of breathing and new confusion. Which nursing action has the highest priority?","options":["Promptly activate the appropriate escalation pathway, support oxygenation under protocol, and report the change to the responsible team.","Wait for echocardiography to confirm impaired cardiac function before escalating care.","Focus first on obtaining a complete history of dietary and medication adherence."],"feedback":["Worsening respiratory distress and confusion require urgent escalation and support; confusion may reflect hypoxia or poor cerebral perfusion.","Echocardiography can help assess cardiac function and cause, but a patient with acute deterioration should not wait for testing before escalation.","Adherence can be relevant to cause, but collecting that history should not delay action for worsening distress and altered mental status."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized patient develops acute dyspnea after receiving intravenous fluids. Crackles are present, and the clinician is evaluating for pulmonary edema and other causes. Which nursing response best applies the acute heart failure principles?","options":["Escalate the new respiratory findings, support oxygenation under local protocol, and communicate the timing of fluid administration.","Assume the fluids caused the symptoms and defer assessment for other cardiac causes.","Wait to report the change until a BNP result is available."],"feedback":["Acute volume overload can contribute to pulmonary edema, so the timing is important to report; respiratory support and evaluation should proceed promptly.","Fluid overload is one possible cause, but acute pulmonary edema can also arise from ischemia, arrhythmia, or valvular disease, among other causes.","BNP may assist when the diagnosis is unclear, but it should not delay escalation for acute respiratory distress."]},{"stem":"A patient with obesity and suspected heart failure has a BNP result within the laboratory's normal range but remains breathless with pulmonary crackles. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["The result does not exclude heart failure; interpret it with the examination and further cardiac evaluation.","The result rules out acute heart failure, so the crackles should be considered unrelated.","The result confirms COPD as the cause of breathlessness."],"feedback":["BNP can be lower in obesity and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, so a normal result cannot exclude heart failure in this context.","A normal BNP does not reliably rule out heart failure, particularly in patients with obesity or preserved ejection fraction.","A BNP result does not establish COPD; the clinical findings and appropriate evaluation are needed to distinguish possible causes."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Heart Failure - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/acute-heart-failure"},{"title":"Pulmonary Edema - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/pulmonary-edema"}],"frames":[{"label":"Acute Respiratory Distress","detail":"Rapidly worsening breathlessness, diaphoresis, and widespread crackles raise concern for acute pulmonary congestion."},{"label":"Wheezing With Crackles","detail":"Wheezing does not exclude pulmonary edema, especially when acute distress and crackles occur without reported COPD history."},{"label":"Support Oxygenation","detail":"Promptly alert the clinician or emergency team and support oxygenation or ventilation under orders and local protocols."},{"label":"Reassess Breathing","detail":"Reassess work of breathing, oxygenation, alertness, blood pressure, and perfusion; report worsening distress, confusion, or persistent hypoxemia."}]},{"id":"s005","unit":"cardio","title":"Rhythm and perfusion","objective":"Use ECG pattern, symptoms, and perfusion cues together to recognize rhythm concerns and communicate nursing priorities to the care team.","concept":"Each heartbeat depends on an electrical impulse forming and traveling in an orderly sequence from the sinoatrial node through the atria, atrioventricular node, and ventricular conduction system. The ECG shows atrial activation in the P wave, ventricular activation in the QRS complex, and ventricular recovery in the T wave. Rhythm problems can arise from abnormal impulse formation or conduction; their clinical importance depends in part on whether they impair perfusion.","recognize":"Assess the patient alongside the monitor: palpitations, dyspnea, chest discomfort, presyncope, or syncope can accompany an arrhythmia. Read the rhythm systematically by checking atrial activity, ventricular rate and regularity, and the relationship between P waves and QRS complexes. A narrow QRS usually suggests a supraventricular origin; a wide QRS may reflect a ventricular rhythm or abnormal ventricular conduction.","respond":"Start with the patient’s immediate symptoms and signs of compromised perfusion, then verify the rhythm and communicate the findings promptly to the responsible clinician or team. Obtain or support a 12-lead ECG and rhythm monitoring under orders or local protocols, ideally while symptoms are present. Report relevant history and possible contributors, such as hypoxia, electrolyte disturbance, or medications; do not diagnose from the monitor alone or independently prescribe treatment.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess symptoms, pulse rate and regularity, and signs of hemodynamic compromise. Confirm whether the rhythm has changed and whether the patient’s condition has improved, using ECG evidence when available. Escalate promptly for persistent or worsening dyspnea, chest discomfort, presyncope, syncope, or a concerning rhythm change.","trap":"A tidy-looking rate is not proof that perfusion is adequate, just as a smoothly running pump can still fail to move enough fluid. A wide QRS does not by itself prove a ventricular origin, because supraventricular impulses can also conduct abnormally.","caseStory":"During a monitored shift, a fictional adult reports sudden palpitations and lightheadedness. The rhythm is irregular with narrow QRS complexes, and the monitor shows no distinct, repeating P waves. The nurse must decide whether to focus only on naming the rhythm or also assess perfusion and promptly communicate the findings.","caseReason":"The irregular narrow-complex pattern with absent discrete P waves is consistent with atrial fibrillation in the supplied reference. Lightheadedness makes assessment of the patient’s condition important, not optional. The nurse should verify the rhythm, assess symptoms and perfusion, and notify the care team rather than relying on the monitor label alone.","teachback":"When a rhythm changes, how will you combine the ECG pattern with the patient’s symptoms to decide what to report and what to reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"A fictional adult on telemetry reports new palpitations. The rhythm is irregular, QRS complexes are narrow, and the atrial tracing has continuous irregular activity without discrete P waves. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pattern is consistent with atrial fibrillation, and the nurse should assess symptoms and communicate the rhythm.","The pattern is most consistent with sinus arrhythmia because the ventricular rhythm is irregular.","The pattern establishes a ventricular rhythm because irregular timing indicates abnormal ventricular activation."],"feedback":["This fits the described atrial fibrillation pattern: irregular atrial activity without discrete P waves and an irregular narrow-complex rhythm. Assess the patient as well as the tracing.","Sinus arrhythmia generally has a one-to-one relationship between normal P waves and following QRS complexes, with gradual rate variation. Those cues are absent here.","An irregular rhythm does not establish ventricular origin. The narrow QRS suggests a supraventricular origin, although the full ECG and clinical context still matter."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult has a slow, regular ventricular rhythm. The ECG shows more P waves than QRS complexes, with no consistent relationship between them. The patient reports presyncope. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Assess the patient for compromised perfusion and promptly escalate the rhythm and symptoms to the care team.","Classify the rhythm as first-degree AV block and continue routine observation because each QRS is regular.","Focus first on measuring the exact ventricular rate, delaying communication until the rhythm has been fully classified."],"feedback":["More P waves than QRS complexes with no relationship between them is consistent with third-degree AV block. Presyncope raises concern for hemodynamic compromise, so patient assessment and prompt escalation take priority.","First-degree AV block has a P wave followed by a QRS for every beat, with a prolonged PR interval. The described lack of a consistent P-to-QRS relationship does not fit that pattern.","A detailed rate measurement can help characterize the rhythm, but it should not delay assessment and communication when presyncope accompanies a concerning bradyarrhythmia."]},{"stem":"In a different fictional setting, an adult develops a rapid, regular rhythm with broad QRS complexes and feels short of breath. Which nursing interpretation and action are most appropriate?","options":["Recognize a wide-complex tachyarrhythmia, assess the patient, and promptly communicate the rhythm and symptoms for clinical evaluation.","Assume the rhythm is supraventricular because it is regular, and wait for symptoms to resolve before reporting it.","Conclude that the rhythm is definitely ventricular from QRS width alone, without checking the patient or obtaining further ECG information."],"feedback":["A rapid rhythm with broad QRS complexes warrants attention to both the ECG and the patient’s shortness of breath. Broad complexes can reflect ventricular origin or supraventricular conduction with an abnormality, so communicate the finding without overclaiming its cause.","Regularity alone does not establish a supraventricular origin. Shortness of breath is a possible sign of hemodynamic impact and should not be used as a reason to delay reporting.","Broad QRS complexes can indicate ventricular origin, but they can also occur with supraventricular rhythms conducted with an intraventricular defect or preexcitation. The patient’s symptoms and further ECG assessment remain important."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult’s palpitations stop before a 12-lead ECG is obtained, and the current monitor shows a regular rhythm. The patient feels better. Which reassessment best avoids a misleading conclusion?","options":["Reassess symptoms and perfusion, document the current rhythm, and communicate that an ECG during symptoms may be needed if episodes recur.","Conclude that no arrhythmia occurred because the rhythm is now regular and the patient feels better.","Treat the earlier palpitations as confirmed atrial fibrillation and report that diagnosis without obtaining rhythm evidence."],"feedback":["A normal rhythm after symptoms resolve does not establish what rhythm occurred during the episode. Reassess the patient and communicate the value of capturing an ECG during symptoms or using ambulatory monitoring as selected by the care team.","Arrhythmias may be intermittent, so a regular rhythm after symptoms stop does not rule out an earlier rhythm disturbance. The relationship between symptoms and rhythm is best assessed with ECG evidence during symptoms when possible.","Palpitations alone do not establish atrial fibrillation. A rhythm diagnosis requires ECG evidence, preferably obtained during symptoms."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Arrhythmias - Cardiology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias-and-conduction-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias"},{"title":"Overview of Arrhythmias - Cardiovascular Disorders - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias-and-conduction-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias?mredirectid=1662&autoredirectid=20568&qt=arrhythmias&alt=sh"}],"frames":[{"label":"Irregular Atrial Rhythm","detail":"An irregular atrial rhythm can disrupt orderly conduction; palpitations and lightheadedness make the patient's condition central."},{"label":"Absent Repeating P Waves","detail":"Irregular narrow QRS complexes without distinct repeating P waves fit the supplied atrial-fibrillation pattern."},{"label":"Verify And Communicate","detail":"Verify the rhythm, assess symptoms and perfusion, and promptly notify the care team under local monitoring protocols."},{"label":"Recheck Symptoms And Rhythm","detail":"Reassess symptoms, pulse regularity, and rhythm; promptly escalate persistent lightheadedness, chest discomfort, dyspnea, or syncope."}]},{"id":"s006","unit":"cardio","title":"Aortic emergencies","objective":"Differentiate key patterns of aortic aneurysm complications and aortic dissection, then communicate urgent changes and reassess for progression.","concept":"The aorta’s wall has inner, middle, and outer layers. Aneurysms are outward bulges where the wall is weak; blood pressure can enlarge them, and rupture can cause internal bleeding and shock. In dissection, a tear in the inner lining lets blood separate the wall layers and create a false channel. A dissection can block branches, so its effects depend on which blood vessels are affected.","recognize":"A sudden, excruciating pain described as tearing or ripping strongly raises concern for dissection. Aneurysms may rupture, causing pain and internal bleeding severe enough to produce shock. Keep the mechanisms distinct: an aneurysm is a bulge, while a dissection separates wall layers. A clot can also form in an aneurysm and travel to block an artery.","respond":"Treat suspected rupture or dissection as an urgent threat: alert the appropriate team through local emergency pathways and report onset, pain quality, known aneurysm history, and any changes in condition or perfusion. Continue close observation and prepare for assessment or actions ordered by the team. Do not delay escalation because these disorders may develop over years or because the precise diagnosis is uncertain.","reassess":"After escalation, reassess pain, overall condition, and signs of shock or changing blood flow, and communicate trends promptly. A new report that a dissection is blocking a branch artery signals progression and warrants urgent team attention. Do not interpret a change in one finding as proof that the danger has passed.","trap":"Aneurysm and dissection are not two names for the same wall problem: an aneurysm is like a weak spot bulging outward, while a dissection is like layers peeling apart. A slow-developing disorder can still become an immediate emergency if it ruptures or obstructs blood flow.","caseStory":"An older adult with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm suddenly reports severe pain and becomes increasingly unwell, with findings concerning for shock. The nurse must decide whether to continue routine observation or urgently escalate a possible complication.","caseReason":"Aneurysm rupture can cause pain and internal bleeding serious enough to produce shock, so this pattern requires urgent escalation. The fact that aneurysms often develop over years does not make a sudden deterioration safe to observe routinely.","teachback":"When a patient has abrupt tearing pain or a known aneurysm with signs concerning for shock, what will you communicate urgently, and what changes will you reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in monitored care reports abrupt, excruciating pain described as tearing or ripping. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate urgently?","options":["Aortic dissection is a leading concern because this pain pattern is characteristic of it.","A stable fusiform aneurysm is most likely because many aneurysms have a tube-like shape.","An aneurysm-related clot has embolized, and the pain alone establishes that an artery is blocked."],"feedback":["Sudden, excruciating pain described as tearing or ripping is the characteristic pattern reported for aortic dissection and merits urgent communication.","Aneurysm shape does not explain away the sudden tearing pain; shape alone cannot establish that an aneurysm is stable.","Clots can break loose from aneurysms and block arteries, but pain alone does not establish that this has happened."]},{"stem":"An older adult with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm develops sudden pain and findings concerning for shock. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Treat rupture as a possibility and urgently alert the appropriate response team.","Continue routine observation because aneurysms generally develop over years.","Assume the findings indicate dissection rather than rupture and wait for the pain description to become clearer."],"feedback":["Aneurysm rupture can cause pain and internal bleeding severe enough to produce shock, so urgent escalation is appropriate.","Aneurysms may develop over years, but that does not rule out an acute rupture or make sudden deterioration safe to observe routinely.","The supplied pattern supports concern for rupture, and uncertainty about the exact diagnosis is not a reason to delay escalation."]},{"stem":"A patient with an aneurysm in the artery behind the knee is being evaluated. The team reports that a clot has broken loose and blocked another artery. How should the nurse interpret this finding?","options":["It is an embolic complication of the aneurysm because a clot has traveled and blocked an artery.","It confirms rupture of the aneurysm because any clot in an aneurysm means the wall has broken.","It is aortic dissection because separation of the aortic wall is the usual cause of a blocked artery in this setting."],"feedback":["A clot can form in an aneurysm, break loose, travel through the bloodstream, and block an artery; popliteal aneurysms are particularly associated with emboli.","A clot may form in an aneurysm because flow is sluggish, but its presence does not by itself establish rupture.","Dissection is separation of layers in the aortic wall; the described traveling clot is an embolic complication, not evidence of aortic dissection."]},{"stem":"A patient with suspected aortic dissection has been urgently escalated. Which new finding most strongly signals a complication that needs prompt communication to the team?","options":["The team reports that the advancing dissection has blocked a branch artery.","The patient continues to describe severe pain, with no new information about branch blood flow.","An x-ray shows calcium deposited in the wall of an aneurysm, without a reported change in blood flow."],"feedback":["An advancing dissection can close off branches and block blood flow, so a newly reported branch obstruction signals a serious complication.","Severe pain remains concerning, but this finding does not specifically indicate the branch-vessel obstruction described as a consequence of advancing dissection.","Calcium may accumulate in an aneurysm wall and make it easier to see on x-rays; without reported obstruction, this does not indicate the described dissection complication."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Aortic Aneurysms and Aortic Dissection - Overview of Aortic Aneurysms and Aortic Dissection - MSD Manual Consumer Version","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/aneurysms-and-aortic-dissection/overview-of-aortic-aneurysms-and-aortic-dissection?ruleredirectid=745"},{"title":"Overview of Aortic Aneurysms and Aortic Dissection - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders - MSD Manual Consumer Version","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/diseases-of-the-aorta-and-its-branches/overview-of-aortic-aneurysms-and-aortic-dissection?ruleredirectid=742autoredirectid=25832"}],"frames":[{"label":"Wall-Layer Injury","detail":"A weak aortic wall can bulge as an aneurysm; an inner-lining tear instead separates layers and creates a false channel."},{"label":"Sudden Tearing Pain","detail":"Sudden excruciating tearing pain, especially with worsening condition or shock in someone with a known aneurysm, signals possible rupture or dissection."},{"label":"Urgent Team Escalation","detail":"Alert the team through local emergency pathways; report onset, pain quality, aneurysm history, condition, and perfusion changes."},{"label":"Changing Perfusion","detail":"Trend pain, overall condition, and blood flow; new branch-vessel blockage or signs of shock require urgent team communication."}]},{"id":"s007","unit":"cardio","title":"Tamponade and chest trauma","objective":"Recognize suspected cardiac tamponade after chest trauma and promptly escalate for team-led assessment and treatment.","concept":"Tamponade occurs when blood or other fluid builds up in the pericardial sac and compresses the heart, limiting its filling and reducing cardiac output. Rapid traumatic bleeding can impair circulation with relatively little fluid. Penetrating chest wounds are a common traumatic cause; blunt injury can also cause it. Because the condition can progress to shock, nurses need to recognize the pattern and alert the team without waiting for every classic sign.","recognize":"Consider tamponade in a patient with chest trauma and worsening circulation, especially after a penetrating wound near the heart. Hypotension, muffled heart tones, and distended neck veins are suggestive, but the full triad is often absent: shock has other causes, assessment may be noisy, and hypovolemia can hide neck vein distention. Markedly reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance suggest tension pneumothorax as an alternative.","respond":"Treat suspected tamponade as an emergency: promptly notify the trauma or critical-care team and support immediate assessment under local protocols. Bedside echocardiography or E-FAST can help, including when hypotension persists despite resuscitation. Prepare to assist with team-directed care and definitive drainage by qualified clinicians. Do not wait for the full triad or treat a negative needle aspiration as ruling tamponade out.","reassess":"After team-directed resuscitation or drainage, reassess blood pressure, heart rate, mental status, skin perfusion, breathing, and neck veins, and report trends promptly. Persistent or worsening hypotension, signs of poor perfusion, or continuing concern despite an incomplete classic pattern warrants immediate escalation and further team assessment.","trap":"The classic triad is a warning pattern, not a required checklist; waiting for all three signs can delay recognition. A needle aspiration that yields no blood is not an all-clear, because fresh blood in the pericardium may have clotted.","caseStory":"After a penetrating wound to the anterior chest, an adult becomes increasingly hypotensive and pale during resuscitation. Neck veins are not distended, and heart sounds are hard to hear in the noisy room; the team is deciding whether to obtain bedside E-FAST.","caseReason":"The wound location and worsening hypotension raise concern for tamponade, even though the classic triad is incomplete. Hypovolemia can explain absent neck vein distention, and muffled tones may be difficult to assess in a noisy resuscitation. Prompt team notification and bedside imaging are more appropriate than waiting for all signs to appear.","teachback":"If a trauma patient is hypotensive but lacks one or more parts of the classic triad, what findings and next team-directed assessment would keep tamponade on your differential?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a penetrating wound near the sternum becomes hypotensive during resuscitation. Neck veins appear distended, heart sounds seem muffled, and bedside E-FAST shows fluid around the heart. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Suspect cardiac tamponade and promptly alert the trauma team for urgent management.","Favor tension pneumothorax and focus on the chest because neck vein distention is present.","Attribute the findings to blood loss alone because hypotension is common after trauma."],"feedback":["The injury location, impaired circulation, suggestive examination, and pericardial fluid together support suspected tamponade and urgent team management.","Tension pneumothorax is an important alternative, but this vignette gives pericardial fluid and does not describe markedly decreased breath sounds or hyperresonance.","Blood loss can cause hypotension, but it does not account for the full pattern, particularly fluid around the heart with suggestive examination findings."]},{"stem":"A patient with a penetrating chest injury remains hypotensive despite initial resuscitation. Neck veins are not visibly distended, and heart sounds are difficult to assess amid the resuscitation. What is the best nursing priority?","options":["Promptly notify the trauma team and support bedside E-FAST or echocardiographic assessment under local protocols.","Continue observing until neck vein distention and muffled heart sounds can both be confirmed.","Treat the absent neck vein finding as evidence against tamponade and defer further assessment."],"feedback":["Tamponade remains possible when the triad is incomplete; persistent hypotension is a reason for prompt escalation and bedside imaging.","Waiting for the full triad risks delay because its components may be absent or difficult to assess in trauma.","Hypovolemia can make neck vein distention absent, so this finding does not exclude tamponade."]},{"stem":"An adult several days after heart surgery develops worsening shortness of breath and low blood pressure. The neck veins appear distended, and the team is considering causes of impaired cardiac filling. Which nursing action best transfers the approach to this new context?","options":["Promptly alert the treating team to possible tamponade and support echocardiographic assessment under local protocols.","Assume tamponade is unlikely because the patient has no penetrating chest wound, and continue routine observation.","Use the trauma triad as a required screen and escalate only if muffled heart sounds are also confirmed."],"feedback":["Heart surgery is a recognized context for tamponade; the concerning findings warrant prompt team assessment, typically including echocardiography.","Tamponade can occur in nontraumatic settings, including after heart surgery, so absence of a wound does not rule it out.","The triad is not reliably present or easy to assess, so requiring every component could delay recognition."]},{"stem":"After a penetrating chest injury, a patient remains hypotensive and has cool, sweaty skin. The neck veins are not distended, and a clinician reports that needle aspiration yielded no blood. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate?","options":["Tamponade is not excluded; hypovolemia may hide neck vein distention, and clotted blood may prevent aspiration.","Tamponade is unlikely because both absent neck vein distention and a dry aspiration argue against pericardial blood.","The aspiration result confirms that ongoing blood loss is outside the pericardium and needs no further cardiac assessment."],"feedback":["The source material notes that hypovolemia can obscure neck vein distention and fresh pericardial blood may be clotted, so a dry aspiration does not exclude tamponade.","Both findings can mislead: neck vein distention may be absent with hypovolemia, and aspiration can fail when blood is clotted.","A dry aspiration does not establish where bleeding is occurring or remove the need for further team evaluation when suspicion persists."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/cardiac-tamponade"},{"title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries and Poisoning - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/injuries-and-poisoning/chest-injuries/cardiac-tamponade"}],"frames":[{"label":"Traumatic Heart Compression","detail":"Chest trauma can fill the pericardial sac with blood, restrict heart filling, and rapidly reduce cardiac output."},{"label":"Incomplete Classic Triad","detail":"After penetrating chest injury, worsening hypotension raises concern even when neck veins or heart sounds are inconclusive."},{"label":"Prompt Trauma Notification","detail":"Notify the trauma or critical-care team and support protocol-directed assessment; bedside E-FAST may help without waiting for the full triad."},{"label":"Persistent Hypotension","detail":"Reassess pressure, perfusion, breathing, and mental status; persistent or worsening hypotension requires immediate escalation."}]},{"id":"s008","unit":"cardio","title":"After cardiac surgery","objective":"Recognize possible cardiac tamponade in a critically ill adult after cardiac surgery and promptly escalate concerning changes for team evaluation.","concept":"Tamponade occurs when fluid under pressure in the pericardial sac limits cardiac filling, reducing preload and cardiac output and potentially causing shock. The speed of accumulation matters: rapid bleeding can become critical with far less fluid than slow accumulation. In critical care, interpret vital-sign trends and bedside findings together; a pericardial effusion with impaired filling is a hemodynamic problem, not merely an imaging observation.","recognize":"Suspect tamponade when hypotension or worsening perfusion occurs with muffled heart sounds and distended neck veins, but do not wait for all three findings. The triad is often incomplete or difficult to assess, and neck vein distention may be absent with hypovolemia. Consider tension pneumothorax as an alternative when hypotension and neck vein distention accompany markedly reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side.","respond":"Promptly notify the responsible cardiac surgery or critical care team and activate the local urgent response process for instability. Maintain appropriate monitoring, report the trend and associated findings, and support resuscitation as directed. Bedside echocardiography or E-FAST may help the team evaluate suspected tamponade. Do not delay escalation while waiting for a complete triad or attempt an advanced procedure independently.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess blood pressure, perfusion, heart sounds, neck veins, respiratory findings, and the direction of change. Confirm whether the team has evaluated the patient and whether bedside imaging is being pursued. Persistent or recurrent hypotension, worsening perfusion, or concerning imaging warrants urgent escalation even if some classic signs are absent.","trap":"The classic triad is a warning pattern, not a checklist that must be complete before you act. Think of it like a smoke alarm with several sensors: one quiet sensor does not prove there is no fire, especially when the patient’s condition is worsening.","caseStory":"A fictional adult returns to critical care after cardiac surgery and develops falling blood pressure with worsening perfusion. The nurse hears quiet heart sounds, but neck veins are not clearly distended and the unit is noisy. The decision is whether to wait for a complete triad or urgently alert the team and seek bedside evaluation.","caseReason":"The combination of worsening hemodynamics and quiet heart sounds raises concern for tamponade, while the absent or uncertain neck-vein finding does not rule it out. Prompt escalation and team-led bedside echocardiography are appropriate; waiting for every classic sign could delay evaluation.","teachback":"When a patient’s blood pressure worsens but one part of the classic tamponade triad is absent, what findings and trends would prompt you to escalate rather than wait?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in critical care after cardiac surgery develops hypotension and worsening perfusion. Heart sounds are muffled, neck veins appear distended, and bedside imaging shows pericardial fluid. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Suspect cardiac tamponade and urgently report the findings for team evaluation.","Suspect isolated hypovolemia because low blood pressure is the dominant finding.","Suspect tension pneumothorax because any neck-vein distention indicates thoracic pressure."],"feedback":["The combination of impaired perfusion, muffled heart sounds, distended neck veins, and pericardial fluid supports concern for tamponade and needs urgent team evaluation.","Hypovolemia can cause hypotension, but it does not account as well for the combined findings and pericardial fluid.","Tension pneumothorax is an important alternative, especially with markedly reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side; those cues are not described here."]},{"stem":"An adult recently returned from cardiac surgery becomes acutely hypotensive and poorly perfused. The nurse notes distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds, but bedside imaging has not yet been done. What is the best immediate nursing priority?","options":["Urgently notify the responsible team, activate the local response process, and maintain monitoring while evaluation is arranged.","Continue routine observation until all findings of the classic triad are confirmed.","Independently perform a pericardial procedure before contacting the responsible team."],"feedback":["The patient is unstable with findings concerning for tamponade, so immediate escalation and ongoing monitoring are appropriate while the team arranges evaluation.","The triad may be incomplete or difficult to recognize, and waiting for every feature can delay care.","Pericardial procedures are advanced interventions for appropriately trained clinicians, not an independent nursing action."]},{"stem":"After a penetrating chest injury, an adult becomes hypotensive. Neck veins are not distended, but E-FAST shows pericardial fluid. Which interpretation should guide the nurse’s communication with the trauma team?","options":["Tamponade remains a concern because rapid accumulation can impair filling and neck-vein distention may be absent.","Tamponade is unlikely because neck-vein distention must be present for the diagnosis.","The pericardial fluid is not relevant unless muffled heart sounds are also confirmed."],"feedback":["Rapid fluid accumulation can impair cardiac filling, and neck-vein distention may be absent in a hypovolemic trauma patient; the imaging and hypotension warrant urgent communication.","The classic triad is not a reliable rule-out pattern, and its findings may be absent or difficult to assess.","Muffled heart sounds can be difficult to hear, especially in a noisy resuscitation, so their absence does not make the imaging finding irrelevant."]},{"stem":"During reassessment, an adult after cardiac surgery remains hypotensive with distended neck veins. Bedside imaging does not show pericardial fluid, and the nurse finds markedly reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side. Which interpretation is most appropriate to escalate?","options":["Tension pneumothorax is an important alternative that needs urgent team evaluation.","Tamponade is confirmed because hypotension and neck-vein distention are sufficient by themselves.","The findings are reassuring because the imaging did not show pericardial fluid."],"feedback":["Hypotension and neck-vein distention with markedly decreased breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side are characteristic cues that support considering tension pneumothorax.","Those two findings alone do not confirm tamponade, and the respiratory findings point toward an important alternative.","The respiratory findings are concerning, and a lack of pericardial fluid on the described imaging does not make the patient’s instability reassuring."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries; Poisoning","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/cardiac-tamponade"},{"title":"Monitoring and Testing the Critical Care Patient - Critical Care Medicine - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/monitoring-and-testing-the-critical-care-patient?ruleredirectid=749"}],"frames":[{"label":"Postoperative Fluid Pressure","detail":"Pericardial fluid under pressure limits cardiac filling; rapid bleeding after surgery can cause critical deterioration with little accumulated fluid."},{"label":"Falling Pressure","detail":"Falling blood pressure and worsening perfusion with quiet heart sounds raise concern, even without clearly distended neck veins."},{"label":"Urgent Cardiac Team","detail":"Notify the responsible cardiac surgery or critical-care team and activate local urgent response procedures; bedside echocardiography may help."},{"label":"Worsening Perfusion","detail":"Reassess blood pressure, perfusion, heart sounds, and respiratory findings; persistent or recurrent hypotension warrants urgent escalation."}]},{"id":"s009","unit":"cardio","title":"After cardiac catheterization","objective":"Use post-catheterization cues to recognize access-site or systemic complications, prioritize nursing surveillance, and escalate changes promptly through the care team.","concept":"Catheterization can disrupt a vessel or impair hemostasis, producing access-site bleeding; femoral bleeding may track internally into the retroperitoneum without visible groin swelling. A local pulsatile mass or bruit suggests pseudoaneurysm, while a continuous bruit or thrill suggests an arteriovenous connection. Radial access lowers some vascular risks but does not eliminate occlusion or ischemia. Trends in vital signs, access-site appearance, distal perfusion, and urine output help reveal evolving complications.","recognize":"Compare current findings with the postprocedure baseline. Growing groin swelling or bruising suggests local bleeding; sudden instability after femoral access, especially with back pain and little visible swelling, raises concern for retroperitoneal bleeding. A pulsatile mass with a bruit points toward pseudoaneurysm. A cool, painful, discolored hand after radial access suggests impaired perfusion rather than routine access discomfort.","respond":"Prioritize a rapid focused assessment of vital signs, access site, distal pulses and perfusion, symptoms, and urine output. Report instability, expanding swelling, a new bruit or thrill, diminished perfusion, or concerning output promptly to the responsible clinician and activate local escalation procedures. Maintain monitoring and carry out ordered care; do not dismiss back pain because the groin looks normal or independently initiate treatment.","reassess":"After escalation or ordered hemostatic care, trend vital signs and symptoms, inspect the site, and compare distal pulses, color, warmth, and sensation with baseline. Track urine output as directed. Persistent instability, worsening pain, expanding swelling, a new perfusion deficit, or declining output warrants renewed urgent communication.","trap":"A clean-looking groin does not rule out serious femoral bleeding: think of blood as potentially leaking behind a wall, where it is hidden from inspection. Conversely, a small stable bruise is not equivalent to a pulsatile mass, a bruit, or changing hemodynamics.","caseStory":"A patient returns from femoral cardiac catheterization and becomes pale and lightheaded, with a falling blood pressure and new lower back discomfort. The groin dressing appears clean, without obvious swelling. What complication should the nurse consider, and what is the priority?","caseReason":"Instability with back pain after femoral access raises concern for retroperitoneal bleeding, which may not produce visible groin swelling. The nurse should promptly escalate the change, continue focused monitoring, and follow local procedures and clinician orders rather than being reassured by the clean dressing.","teachback":"After femoral catheterization, what combination of findings would make you suspect bleeding even when the access site looks normal, and how would you communicate that concern?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient develops new back pain and lightheadedness several hours after femoral cardiac catheterization. Blood pressure is trending downward, but the groin has no obvious swelling. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Possible retroperitoneal bleeding requiring prompt escalation","A likely small, stable bruise at the access site","A possible pseudoaneurysm because any bleeding is pulsatile"],"feedback":["Femoral bleeding can collect in the retroperitoneum without visible groin swelling, and back pain with hemodynamic change is concerning.","A stable local bruise does not adequately explain the downward blood pressure trend and new back pain.","A pseudoaneurysm is associated with a local pulsatile mass and bruit, neither of which is described."]},{"stem":"After femoral catheterization, a patient’s groin swelling is rapidly enlarging and the patient appears increasingly pale and dizzy. Which nursing action takes priority?","options":["Promptly alert the responsible clinician and activate local escalation procedures while continuing close monitoring","Continue routine observation and reassess the swelling at the next scheduled check","Focus first on documenting the dimensions of the swelling before contacting the care team"],"feedback":["Rapidly expanding swelling with signs of instability may indicate significant bleeding, so urgent communication and monitoring take priority.","Waiting for a routine check could delay care while bleeding and hemodynamic instability worsen.","Documentation is useful, but it should not delay escalation of a potentially serious complication."]},{"stem":"A patient has radial access for catheterization. During recovery, the hand becomes cool and painful, with new discoloration compared with the other hand. Which interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["Suspect impaired hand perfusion and promptly report the change while assessing distal perfusion","Consider the symptoms expected access-site soreness and continue routine checks only","Assume the radial approach rules out a vascular complication unless the wrist is swollen"],"feedback":["Coolness, pain, and discoloration may indicate hand ischemia, particularly if collateral circulation is inadequate; prompt assessment and escalation are appropriate.","Routine discomfort does not explain new changes in hand color and temperature, which require focused perfusion assessment.","Radial access reduces some complications but does not eliminate radial artery occlusion or possible hand ischemia."]},{"stem":"A patient with chronic kidney disease is recovering after catheterization with contrast. The access site is unchanged and vital signs are stable, but urine output is declining. What is the best nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Treat the output trend as a possible renal concern, continue tracking it, and report it to the care team","Conclude that the stable access site makes a procedure-related complication unlikely","Attribute the change to expected recovery and defer reassessment until the next routine check"],"feedback":["Contrast-associated renal injury is a concern, especially with underlying kidney disease; monitoring urine output and communicating a concerning trend are supported nursing actions.","A stable access site does not address renal risk, which is separate from local vascular complications.","Dismissing declining output could delay recognition of a renal problem; continue surveillance and report the change."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Cardiac Catheterization Risks and Complications - StatPearls ...","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531461/"},{"title":"Cardiac Catheterization - Cardiology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/cardiovascular-tests-and-procedures/cardiac-catheterization"}],"frames":[{"label":"Hidden Femoral Bleeding","detail":"Femoral access can bleed into the retroperitoneum, where blood loss may occur without visible groin swelling."},{"label":"Back Pain After Access","detail":"Falling pressure, pallor, lightheadedness, and new back discomfort after femoral catheterization raise concern despite a clean dressing."},{"label":"Focused Urgent Assessment","detail":"Assess vital signs, access site, distal perfusion, symptoms, and urine output; promptly report instability and follow local escalation procedures."},{"label":"Declining Perfusion","detail":"Trend vital signs, pain, site appearance, distal pulses, and urine output; persistent instability or worsening perfusion warrants renewed urgent communication."}]},{"id":"s010","unit":"cardio","title":"Conduction and QT risk","objective":"Recognize and communicate conduction and QT-related arrhythmia risk using the ECG pattern, symptoms, and clinical context to guide timely team-based nursing action.","concept":"Long QT syndromes reflect prolonged ventricular repolarization, which can enable torsades de pointes and, at times, ventricular fibrillation. Wolff-Parkinson-White involves an accessory atrioventricular connection that can pre-excite the ventricles and support reentry; atrial fibrillation conducted rapidly over that pathway can also become life-threatening. Distinguishing these mechanisms matters because their ECG clues and risk contexts differ.","recognize":"Look for a short PR interval, widened QRS, and delta wave as clues to manifest WPW; episodes of reentrant tachycardia are often abrupt and regular. A prolonged QTc raises concern for long QT risk, especially with syncope, relevant family history, or triggers such as exertion, loud sudden sounds, or sleep. An irregular, very rapid, wide-complex rhythm in WPW is especially concerning.","respond":"For syncope, a very rapid rhythm, or signs of instability, promptly alert the responsible clinician or emergency response team and follow local escalation and monitoring protocols. Communicate the rhythm pattern, symptoms, relevant medication exposures, and available electrolyte results. Do not independently select antiarrhythmic treatment or perform cardioversion; follow team direction, and clarify potentially risky medication orders when the rhythm is concerning for pre-excited atrial fibrillation.","reassess":"After escalation or an ordered intervention, reassess rhythm, symptoms, level of consciousness, and hemodynamic status, and communicate changes promptly. Review follow-up ECG findings and relevant medication or electrolyte information as available. Persistent instability, recurrent syncope, or a worsening rhythm warrants immediate escalation.","trap":"A normal resting QTc is not a reliable all-clear: some people with a disease-associated mutation have a normal measurement on a given ECG. Think of WPW and long QT as different hazards: one is an alternate electrical route, while the other is delayed recovery.","caseStory":"A patient reports sudden episodes of rapid, regular palpitations and nearly fainted while walking. The ECG between episodes shows a short PR interval, a widened QRS, and an initial slur in the QRS complex. The patient is now alert and stable, and the nurse must decide what to communicate and what should guide escalation if the rhythm changes.","caseReason":"The baseline ECG pattern is consistent with ventricular pre-excitation and supports concern for WPW rather than identifying long QT syndrome. The near-syncope and abrupt palpitations make prompt clinician communication and monitoring appropriate. A new irregular, very rapid, wide-complex rhythm would raise particular concern for rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation and require urgent escalation.","teachback":"When a resting ECG is normal, what symptoms, rhythm clues, or context would still lead you to communicate concern for an inherited conduction or QT-related risk?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient describes abrupt, regular episodes of rapid palpitations and light-headedness. Between episodes, the ECG shows a short PR interval, a widened QRS, and a slurred initial QRS upstroke. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pattern suggests ventricular pre-excitation through an accessory atrioventricular pathway, consistent with WPW.","The pattern primarily indicates prolonged ventricular repolarization and risk for torsades de pointes.","The pattern is most consistent with a nonspecific rhythm change that does not explain the episodes."],"feedback":["A short PR, widened QRS, and delta wave are characteristic clues to manifest WPW, which can support reentrant tachycardia.","Long QT risk is identified by prolonged rate-corrected QT, not this combination of pre-excitation findings.","The ECG pattern is meaningful in this clinical context and can explain abrupt episodes of reentrant tachycardia."]},{"stem":"A patient with known WPW suddenly develops an irregular, very rapid, wide-complex rhythm and becomes hypotensive. What is the nurse's priority action?","options":["Activate the emergency response pathway, continue close monitoring, and prepare to support team-directed synchronized cardioversion.","Give an ordered atrioventricular nodal slowing medication immediately to make the rhythm regular.","Wait for a repeat resting ECG before notifying the clinician because the rhythm may be transient."],"feedback":["The rhythm and hypotension suggest a potentially unstable, rapidly conducted arrhythmia; urgent escalation and team-directed cardioversion support are appropriate.","In pre-excited atrial fibrillation, very rapid conduction over the accessory pathway can be dangerous; do not independently proceed with this approach.","A highly rapid irregular wide-complex rhythm with hypotension requires immediate escalation, not delayed reassessment."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized patient taking a medication associated with QT prolongation reports palpitations and near-syncope. Recent laboratory results show low potassium and magnesium, and the monitor displays a prolonged QTc. Which nursing response is most appropriate?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician, report the symptoms, ECG and laboratory findings, and follow monitoring and medication-review protocols.","Treat the QTc finding as diagnostic of an inherited syndrome and independently discontinue the medication.","Reassure the patient because palpitations are common, and wait for a sustained arrhythmia before escalating."],"feedback":["Medication exposure, low potassium and magnesium, prolonged QTc, and near-syncope together warrant prompt communication and protocol-based monitoring and review.","A prolonged QTc can have acquired causes, and nurses should not independently diagnose an inherited syndrome or change treatment outside their authority.","Near-syncope with these risk factors merits escalation before a sustained arrhythmia occurs."]},{"stem":"A first-degree relative of a person with confirmed long QT syndrome fainted while swimming, but the relative's resting ECG has a QTc reported within the usual range. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication?","options":["The normal resting QTc does not rule out long QT syndrome; report the exertional syncope and family history for further clinical assessment.","The normal resting QTc excludes long QT syndrome, so the swimming-related fainting needs no further rhythm evaluation.","Swimming-related syncope establishes a specific long QT type, so no additional history or assessment is needed."],"feedback":["Some people with a disease-associated mutation have a normal QTc on a resting ECG; exertional syncope and family history still warrant communication and further assessment.","A normal QTc on one ECG does not exclude the condition, and the symptom and family history remain relevant.","Swimming can be a trigger associated with one subtype, but triggers are not specific enough to establish a genotype or end assessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Long QT Interval Syndromes - Cardiology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/arrhythmogenic-cardiac-disorders/long-qt-interval-syndromes"},{"title":"Wolff-Parkinson-White Syndrome (WPW Syndrome) - The Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/specific-cardiac-arrhythmias/wolff-parkinson-white-syndrome-wpw-syndrome"}],"frames":[{"label":"Distinct Rhythm Mechanisms","detail":"Long QT prolongs ventricular repolarization; WPW's accessory pathway pre-excites the ventricles and can support reentry."},{"label":"Pre-Excitation Clues","detail":"Short PR, widened QRS, and a delta wave with abrupt regular palpitations support WPW; an irregular, very rapid wide rhythm is especially concerning."},{"label":"Communicate Rhythm Changes","detail":"Promptly communicate near-syncope and ECG findings, monitor under local protocols, and urgently escalate instability or a concerning rhythm change."},{"label":"Recurrent Syncope","detail":"Reassess rhythm, symptoms, consciousness, and hemodynamics; recurrent syncope, instability, or worsening rhythm warrants immediate escalation."}]},{"id":"s011","unit":"cardio","title":"Valve disease and endocarditis","objective":"Recognize how abnormal valves can enable infective endocarditis, identify warning signs and complications, and coordinate timely nursing assessment and escalation.","concept":"Damaged, abnormal, or prosthetic valves are more vulnerable to organisms circulating in the blood. Organisms can adhere, multiply, and form vegetations that damage valves or break away as emboli. Valve leakage may cause heart failure, while infection can extend into nearby tissue and affect conduction. Acute illness may progress rapidly; subacute illness can be subtle, so trends and risk factors matter.","recognize":"Consider endocarditis when fever lacks an obvious source, especially with a valve disorder, prosthetic valve, intracardiac device, or injection drug use. A new or changing murmur, fatigue, weight loss, skin or eye spots, or embolic symptoms add concern. Sudden severe illness differs from a gradual course, but symptoms alone do not confirm the diagnosis.","respond":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician about suspected endocarditis and report risk factors, symptom timing, murmur changes, and possible embolic or heart failure signs. Support ordered blood cultures before antibiotics when feasible, and coordinate echocardiography and hospital evaluation as directed. Follow local protocols, monitor for deterioration, and do not independently prescribe treatment or dismiss concern because symptoms are subtle.","reassess":"After escalation or ordered interventions, reassess vital signs, breathing, perfusion, mental status, new neurologic symptoms, and changes in murmur or heart failure signs. Communicate worsening promptly, including sudden confusion, focal weakness, respiratory distress, or signs of shock, since these may indicate serious complications.","trap":"A quiet, gradual illness does not rule out a dangerous valve infection; it can be like a slow leak that causes major damage before it is obvious. A negative culture after antibiotics also does not by itself exclude endocarditis, because treatment can reduce the chance of growing organisms from blood.","caseStory":"A patient with a prosthetic heart valve reports several weeks of fatigue, mild fever, and weight loss. The nurse notices a change in the previously documented murmur, while the patient is currently alert and breathing comfortably. The decision is whether to treat the mild presentation as routine or promptly raise concern for endocarditis.","caseReason":"The prosthetic valve increases risk, and a gradual course with fatigue, mild fever, and weight loss fits possible subacute endocarditis. A changing murmur adds concern even without acute distress. The nurse should promptly notify the clinician and support ordered cultures and echocardiography rather than waiting for more severe symptoms.","teachback":"When a person with valve disease has subtle symptoms, what combination of risk factors and new findings would prompt you to raise concern for endocarditis, and what would you communicate first?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with a replacement heart valve has had mild fever, night sweats, and fatigue for several weeks. The nurse hears a murmur that differs from the documented baseline. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Subacute endocarditis is a concern, so promptly communicate the valve history, symptom course, and murmur change.","A mild fever makes valve infection unlikely, so routine observation is appropriate unless the patient becomes acutely ill.","The murmur change is sufficient to establish valve damage, so further clinical assessment is not needed."],"feedback":["A prosthetic valve raises risk, and subacute endocarditis can present gradually with mild fever and fatigue; a changing murmur strengthens concern and warrants prompt communication.","Subacute disease may be subtle for weeks or months, so mild symptoms do not make endocarditis unlikely, particularly with a prosthetic valve.","A murmur change supports concern but does not establish the diagnosis; blood cultures and echocardiography are used in the diagnostic evaluation."]},{"stem":"A patient with a known valve disorder develops a high fever, rapid heart rate, increasing breathlessness, and new confusion over a short period. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Escalate promptly to the responsible clinical team and report the rapid deterioration and possible heart failure or embolic signs.","Arrange routine follow-up because confusion and breathlessness are expected while a fever is present.","Wait for a complete set of laboratory results before communicating the change in condition."],"feedback":["Acute endocarditis can rapidly damage valves, cause heart failure, or produce embolic complications; this deterioration requires prompt escalation.","New breathlessness and confusion are not findings to dismiss as routine fever effects, particularly in a patient with valve disease.","The patient is worsening, and communication should not wait for test results; cultures and imaging are part of evaluation but do not replace timely escalation."]},{"stem":"A patient who injects drugs has fever, pleuritic chest pain, and cough. The nurse is preparing to update the clinician. Which interpretation is most useful to include?","options":["Right-sided endocarditis is a concern because infected emboli can travel to the lungs and cause respiratory symptoms.","Left-sided endocarditis is most likely because emboli from any valve usually present first as cough and pleuritic pain.","The respiratory symptoms make endocarditis unlikely because valve infection mainly causes skin findings."],"feedback":["Injection drug use is associated with right-sided, often tricuspid, infection; septic pulmonary emboli may cause cough and pleuritic pain.","Left-sided lesions can embolize to organs such as the brain, kidneys, and spleen, whereas right-sided lesions commonly produce septic pulmonary emboli.","Endocarditis can cause respiratory symptoms through pulmonary emboli, and skin findings are not required for the diagnosis."]},{"stem":"A patient with a prosthetic valve received antibiotics before blood cultures were collected. Cultures show no growth, but fever and fatigue continue. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Do not treat the negative cultures as ruling out endocarditis; promptly communicate persistent symptoms and the timing of antibiotics.","Consider endocarditis excluded because blood cultures are the only useful diagnostic evidence.","Assume the fever is unrelated to the valve because antibiotics were already started."],"feedback":["Prior antibiotics can reduce the number of organisms detected in blood cultures, and a prosthetic valve with persistent symptoms sustains concern for endocarditis.","Diagnosis uses clinical findings and imaging as well as cultures; cultures can be negative after antibiotics or with organisms that do not grow readily.","Antibiotic exposure does not establish that the fever is unrelated to the valve; the history and persistent symptoms should be communicated for further evaluation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Infective Endocarditis - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/endocarditis/infective-endocarditis"},{"title":"Infective Endocarditis - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/endocarditis/infective-endocarditis"}],"frames":[{"label":"Prosthetic Valve Risk","detail":"A prosthetic valve increases vulnerability to circulating organisms that can adhere, multiply, and form damaging vegetations."},{"label":"Changing Murmur","detail":"Weeks of fatigue, mild fever, and weight loss with a changed murmur raise concern despite comfortable breathing."},{"label":"Prompt Clinical Escalation","detail":"Notify the responsible clinician, report risk factors and symptom timing, and support ordered cultures and echocardiography."},{"label":"Watch for Deterioration","detail":"Reassess breathing, perfusion, and mental status; promptly report focal weakness, confusion, respiratory distress, or shock signs."}]},{"id":"s012","unit":"cardio","title":"Inflammatory heart disease","objective":"Recognize patterns of pericarditis, myocarditis, and evolving tamponade, then prioritize nursing assessment and timely escalation.","concept":"Pericarditis inflames the sac around the heart, causing pain and sometimes fluid buildup that can restrict filling and reduce cardiac output. Myocarditis inflames the heart muscle and can impair pumping or trigger abnormal rhythms. The conditions can overlap, so chest pain with breathing-related features may coexist with signs of heart failure or rhythm disturbance. Recognizing these links helps nurses identify deterioration without treating one symptom as a diagnosis.","recognize":"Pericarditis often causes sharp or dull chest pain worsened by breathing, coughing, movement, or swallowing and eased by sitting forward; an intermittent friction rub may be heard. Myocarditis may follow a recent illness and present with fatigue, breathlessness, edema, palpitations, or fainting. Hypotension, tachycardia, dyspnea, and distended neck veins raise concern for tamponade. Chest pain still requires evaluation for other serious causes.","respond":"Assess airway, breathing, circulation, vital signs, perfusion, symptoms, and cardiac rhythm; promptly communicate concerning changes to the responsible clinician or activate the local emergency response when indicated. Follow orders and unit protocols for monitoring and diagnostic preparation. Treat new chest pain as requiring clinical evaluation, not as pericarditis based on position alone. Do not independently diagnose, prescribe anti-inflammatory treatment, or dismiss deterioration because a rub is absent.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess symptoms, blood pressure, heart rate, breathing, oxygenation, perfusion, neck veins, and rhythm, comparing with prior findings. Report promptly any worsening breathlessness, hypotension, tachycardia, fainting, or other signs of reduced cardiac output. A quiet examination or temporary pain improvement does not establish that the underlying process is resolving.","trap":"An absent friction rub does not rule out pericarditis because the sound can come and go. Think of it like an intermittent alarm: silence at one moment is not proof that the underlying hazard has passed, especially if fluid is affecting cardiac filling.","caseStory":"A fictional adult reports sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breaths and improves when sitting forward, following a recent febrile illness. The nurse hears a scratchy precordial sound once, but it is absent on repeat examination. The patient now appears more breathless, and the nurse must decide whether the changing assessment can wait for routine review.","caseReason":"The positional, breathing-related pain and intermittent rub fit a pericarditis pattern, but neither finding confirms the cause or guarantees a mild course. Increasing breathlessness could signal worsening illness or fluid-related impairment, so the nurse should reassess vital signs and perfusion and promptly escalate the change rather than wait for the rub to return.","teachback":"When a patient with a pericarditis-like pain pattern becomes more breathless, which findings would you reassess and communicate, even if the friction rub is no longer audible?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult develops sharp substernal pain after a recent illness. The pain worsens with deep breathing and coughing, eases when sitting and leaning forward, and a scratchy precordial sound is heard on one examination but not the next. Which interpretation best fits these cues?","options":["The pattern supports pericardial inflammation, and the intermittent sound does not exclude it.","The pattern establishes myocardial inflammation because the pain followed an illness.","The pattern is typical of fluid-related tamponade even without evidence of impaired filling."],"feedback":["Breathing-related, positional pain is characteristic of pericarditis, and its friction rub may be intermittent. Further clinical evaluation is still needed.","Myocarditis can follow an illness, but this pain pattern and intermittent rub more specifically support pericardial involvement; the history does not establish myocarditis.","Effusion may accompany pericarditis, but tamponade requires concern for impaired cardiac filling and reduced output, not pain features alone."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with a known pericardial effusion becomes hypotensive and tachycardic, reports worsening dyspnea, and has distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Promptly escalate the findings as possible tamponade and support urgent assessment under local protocol.","Continue routine observation because a known effusion can be present without symptoms.","Document the findings and wait for a friction rub before notifying the responsible clinician."],"feedback":["Hypotension, tachycardia, dyspnea, and distended neck veins are concerning for impaired filling and reduced cardiac output; prompt escalation is appropriate.","Some effusions are asymptomatic, but this patient's new hemodynamic and respiratory findings suggest deterioration rather than an uncomplicated effusion.","A rub may be absent or intermittent and is not required for concern about tamponade; waiting could delay evaluation of reduced cardiac output."]},{"stem":"An adult reports a recent viral-like illness and now has new fatigue, breathlessness, ankle swelling, and episodes of a racing heartbeat. The nurse is deciding what concern to communicate to the clinical team. Which is the best interpretation?","options":["Myocardial inflammation is a concern because impaired pumping and abnormal rhythms can produce these symptoms.","Isolated pericardial inflammation is the best explanation because all inflammatory heart disease presents with positional pain.","A slow-developing pericardial effusion is the best explanation because it commonly causes palpitations and edema."],"feedback":["Myocarditis can follow an infectious illness and may cause heart failure symptoms, edema, and palpitations; communicating this concern supports appropriate evaluation.","Pericarditis may cause chest pain, but positional pain is not reported here, and the symptoms also raise concern for myocardial dysfunction or rhythm disturbance.","A slowly accumulating effusion may cause few symptoms, but the supplied clinical features do not make it the best explanation for this combination of possible heart failure and rhythm symptoms."]},{"stem":"An adult with a documented pericardial effusion is comfortable at rest, with currently normal blood pressure and no audible friction rub. Which nursing approach best accounts for the possibility that the findings may be misleading?","options":["Continue reassessment for changes in breathing, perfusion, vital signs, and neck veins, and escalate deterioration.","Conclude that tamponade is excluded because blood pressure is normal and the rub is absent.","Stop focused reassessment unless chest pain develops, because an effusion without pain is not clinically important."],"feedback":["A slowly accumulating effusion may cause few symptoms, and blood pressure or neck vein findings can remain normal unless pressure rises substantially; watch for change and escalate deterioration.","Normal findings at one assessment do not exclude evolving impairment, and a friction rub can be intermittent or absent.","Effusion can be painless, and symptoms vary with the amount and rate of fluid accumulation; continued assessment is important even without chest pain."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Pericarditis - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/myocarditis-and-pericarditis/pericarditis"},{"title":"Myocarditis - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/pericardial-disease-and-myocarditis/myocarditis"}],"frames":[{"label":"Inflamed Heart Sac","detail":"Pericardial inflammation can cause breathing-related chest pain, while fluid buildup may restrict filling and reduce cardiac output."},{"label":"Worsening Breathlessness","detail":"Sharp pain eased by sitting forward and an intermittent rub fit a pericarditis pattern, but increasing breathlessness raises concern."},{"label":"Escalate Changing Findings","detail":"Reassess vital signs and perfusion, then promptly communicate worsening breathlessness or other concerning changes to the clinician."},{"label":"Track Cardiac Output","detail":"Recheck blood pressure, heart rate, breathing, oxygenation, perfusion, and rhythm; report hypotension, fainting, or worsening dyspnea promptly."}]},{"id":"s013","unit":"cardio","title":"Hypertensive emergency","objective":"Distinguish severe blood pressure elevation with acute target-organ injury from elevation without such injury, then describe nursing priorities for monitored, team-directed care.","concept":"A hypertensive emergency is defined by markedly elevated blood pressure together with acute target-organ injury, not by a number alone. Excess pressure can disrupt vascular function and injure the brain, heart, kidneys, eyes, or aorta. Because longstanding hypertension can shift the body’s tolerance and abrupt lowering can impair perfusion, treatment is usually controlled and titrated, with the pace and approach shaped by the affected organ.","recognize":"Look for acute changes such as confusion, seizures, new focal deficits, visual disturbance, chest pain, dyspnea, reduced urine output, or signs of pulmonary edema, alongside a markedly elevated reading. Examine the whole picture and report relevant findings, including neurologic status, lung sounds, pulses, and available renal or cardiac tests. Anxiety or a high reading alone does not establish acute organ injury; focal deficits may indicate stroke rather than encephalopathy.","respond":"Escalate suspected acute organ injury promptly and communicate the blood pressure trend, symptoms, focused assessment, and relevant test findings to the treating team. Support ordered evaluation and, when prescribed, monitored titratable intravenous therapy in an appropriate critical-care setting. Follow local protocols and organ-specific instructions. Avoid trying to normalize pressure abruptly or using short-acting oral nifedipine for an emergency; do not treat the reading in isolation.","reassess":"After each intervention, reassess blood pressure trend, mental status and focal neurologic findings, chest symptoms, breathing and lung sounds, perfusion, and urine output as appropriate. Report worsening symptoms, new deficits, hypotension, or signs of deteriorating organ function immediately; these may require urgent reassessment of the plan.","trap":"A striking blood pressure reading is a warning light, not the diagnosis; acute organ injury determines whether this is an emergency. Think of lowering pressure like easing a strained system back into range, not abruptly shutting off its supply, because excessive reduction can compromise perfusion.","caseStory":"A patient with markedly elevated blood pressure becomes confused and reports blurred vision; examination reveals papilledema. The patient is afebrile and has no reported chest pain or dyspnea. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as an emergency or as an isolated high reading while awaiting further assessment.","caseReason":"Confusion and papilledema are concerning for acute cerebral target-organ injury, making this a suspected hypertensive emergency rather than an isolated elevation. The nurse should promptly escalate, report the neurologic findings, and support ordered evaluation and monitored treatment. A high reading without these findings would not, by itself, establish an emergency.","teachback":"When a patient’s blood pressure is markedly elevated, what acute findings would change your response from reporting an isolated elevation to escalating a suspected hypertensive emergency, and why should pressure not be lowered abruptly?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient has a markedly elevated blood pressure, new confusion, and papilledema on examination. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Suspect hypertensive emergency because the neurologic and eye findings indicate possible acute target-organ injury.","Consider hypertensive urgency because the blood pressure reading is the main finding and the patient has no chest pain.","Assume the confusion is caused by anxiety because severe readings commonly occur during distress."],"feedback":["Correct. Confusion and papilledema are concerning for cerebral target-organ injury and warrant prompt escalation and evaluation.","This is misleading because absence of chest pain does not exclude acute neurologic injury.","Anxiety can accompany severe elevation, but it does not adequately explain away signs suggesting organ injury."]},{"stem":"A patient with markedly elevated blood pressure develops acute dyspnea, crackles, and jugular venous distention. What is the nurse’s priority response?","options":["Promptly escalate suspected pulmonary edema and support ordered monitored intravenous treatment in the appropriate care setting.","Give a short-acting oral blood pressure medication to lower the reading before contacting the treating team.","Reassure the patient and arrange routine follow-up because dyspnea can occur with anxiety."],"feedback":["Correct. Dyspnea with crackles and jugular venous distention suggests pulmonary edema, an acute target-organ problem requiring prompt team-directed care.","Oral medications are not indicated for immediate emergency management because their effect can be variable and difficult to titrate.","This overlooks signs of possible acute cardiac involvement and delays needed escalation."]},{"stem":"A pregnant patient with markedly elevated blood pressure develops a seizure. Which nursing response best reflects the supported approach?","options":["Escalate urgently and support the obstetric team’s ordered emergency care, including monitoring and prescribed therapy.","Treat the episode as uncomplicated anxiety and repeat the blood pressure after the patient rests.","Independently select an oral antihypertensive and give it before notifying the obstetric team."],"feedback":["Correct. Seizure with severe hypertension in pregnancy raises concern for eclampsia and requires urgent team-directed care and close monitoring.","This dismisses a potentially life-threatening obstetric emergency and delays treatment.","Medication selection is not an independent nursing action; emergency treatment should be directed by the clinical team and local protocols."]},{"stem":"A patient with markedly elevated blood pressure has sudden unilateral weakness but is alert and oriented. Which interpretation and action are most appropriate?","options":["Treat this as a possible stroke, promptly escalate the new focal deficit, and support ordered neurologic evaluation rather than assuming the pressure caused it.","Diagnose hypertensive encephalopathy because any neurologic symptom with a high reading indicates pressure-related brain injury.","Wait for the blood pressure to normalize before reporting the weakness, since this will clarify whether it is clinically important."],"feedback":["Correct. A focal deficit with normal mental status can suggest stroke, and neurologic findings warrant prompt evaluation; elevated pressure may be a consequence rather than the cause.","This overinterprets the blood pressure and fails to distinguish a focal stroke presentation from global encephalopathy.","Delaying escalation risks missing an acute neurologic event, and rapid pressure normalization is not automatically appropriate."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Hypertensive Emergencies - Cardiology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/hypertension/hypertensive-emergencies"},{"title":"Hypertensive Emergency - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470371/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pressure With Organ Injury","detail":"A hypertensive emergency involves markedly elevated pressure together with acute injury to organs such as the brain, heart, or kidneys."},{"label":"Neurologic Injury Cues","detail":"Confusion and papilledema alongside markedly elevated pressure suggest acute cerebral target-organ injury, not an isolated high reading."},{"label":"Urgent Team Notification","detail":"Promptly escalate and report the pressure trend and neurologic findings; support ordered evaluation and monitored treatment under local protocols."},{"label":"Track Organ Function","detail":"Reassess mental status, neurologic findings, breathing, perfusion, and urine output; immediately report new deficits, hypotension, or worsening symptoms."}]},{"id":"s014","unit":"cardio","title":"Limb perfusion after revascularization","objective":"Recognize threatened limb and systemic reperfusion complications after lower-extremity revascularization and prioritize timely escalation and focused reassessment.","concept":"Revascularization restores flow to ischemic tissue, but injured muscle can release potassium, myoglobin, and other metabolites into circulation, contributing to acidosis, arrhythmias, respiratory or renal failure. Reperfusion can also increase local edema and pressure within muscle compartments, impairing circulation and nerve and muscle function. Thus, a technically successful procedure does not end surveillance: limb findings and systemic status both matter, and deterioration needs prompt team review.","recognize":"Compare the affected limb with its documented baseline and the opposite side, noting pain, color, temperature, pulses or Doppler signals, sensation, movement, and swelling. New pain at rest or with passive stretch, tense swelling, sensory change, or weakness raises concern for compartment syndrome; a worsening pulse or perfusion signal can suggest persistent or recurrent obstruction. Systemic deterioration or urine findings suggesting myoglobin may accompany muscle injury.","respond":"Treat new or worsening limb or systemic findings as urgent and promptly notify the vascular or critical-care team using the unit escalation pathway. Report trends, including sensory and motor changes, pain, swelling, pulse or Doppler findings, and vital-sign or rhythm changes. Obtain ordered tests and support monitoring. Do not assume a palpable pulse excludes compartment syndrome or independently change anticoagulation.","reassess":"After reporting and any ordered intervention, repeat and document the same limb and systemic assessments at the interval directed by the team or protocol. Escalate immediately for progressive pain, pain with passive stretch, swelling, new sensory or motor loss, declining perfusion signals, or signs of hyperkalemia, acidosis, arrhythmia, respiratory compromise, or renal injury.","trap":"A returned pulse is like a road reopened while a nearby tunnel is still filling with water: it does not prove that all tissue is safe. Continue watching for compartment pressure and systemic effects even when flow appears restored.","caseStory":"An adult returns to critical care after lower-extremity revascularization with an audible pedal Doppler signal. Over the next assessment, the calf becomes tense and swollen, pain increases when the toes are passively moved, and the patient reports new tingling. The nurse must decide whether the Doppler signal is reassuring enough to wait or whether to escalate now.","caseReason":"The new swelling, passive-stretch pain, and sensory change are concerning for compartment syndrome after reperfusion. An audible signal does not rule it out, so the nurse should promptly alert the team and report the focused assessment rather than wait for the next routine review.","teachback":"After revascularization, what limb and systemic changes would make you escalate even if a pulse or Doppler signal has returned?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult returns to critical care after lower-extremity revascularization. A pedal Doppler signal is now audible, but the calf is increasingly tense and swollen, pain worsens with passive toe movement, and new tingling is reported. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's next action?","options":["Suspect compartment syndrome and promptly escalate despite the audible signal.","Consider the findings expected after restored flow and continue routine monitoring.","Assume recurrent arterial obstruction and focus only on repeating pulse checks."],"feedback":["Tense swelling, pain with passive stretch, and sensory change after reperfusion raise concern for compartment syndrome and require prompt team review.","These findings are not safely dismissed as routine reperfusion changes; compartment pressure can impair circulation and nerve and muscle function.","Recurrent obstruction is possible with worsening perfusion, but the specific swelling and passive-stretch pain also indicate compartment syndrome, which must not be overlooked."]},{"stem":"A patient with prolonged limb ischemia develops worsening systemic status after revascularization. Blood tests show rising potassium and markers of muscle injury, acidosis is present, and the cardiac monitor now shows an irregular rhythm. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Urgently notify the team of possible systemic reperfusion injury and support ordered cardiac and laboratory monitoring.","Continue limb assessments first and report the laboratory changes at the next scheduled review.","Document the rhythm as a likely unrelated finding because the limb has been revascularized."],"feedback":["Potassium release, acidosis, muscle injury markers, and rhythm change are concerning for a serious systemic complication after reperfusion and warrant urgent escalation.","Limb assessment remains important, but waiting risks delaying review of potentially life-threatening systemic deterioration.","Arrhythmias can occur with systemic effects of ischemia and reperfusion, so the rhythm change should not be presumed unrelated."]},{"stem":"A patient has undergone thromboembolectomy for an acute lower-limb occlusion. The foot is warmer and the Doppler signal has improved, but the patient develops new difficulty lifting the foot and increasing sensory loss. What should the nurse do?","options":["Promptly escalate the new motor and sensory deficits as possible ongoing limb threat.","Treat the improved warmth and Doppler signal as evidence that the limb is safe for now.","Wait for pain to worsen before reporting, since pain is the clearest sign of impaired perfusion."],"feedback":["New weakness and sensory loss are urgent changes in limb function even when some signs of flow have improved, and need prompt team assessment.","Improved warmth and Doppler findings do not exclude threatened tissue or evolving neurologic injury.","Waiting for pain is unsafe; motor and sensory changes are important signs of worsening ischemia and require escalation."]},{"stem":"After revascularization, a patient says the foot pain has eased after analgesia. On reassessment, the foot is now cooler and paler than before, and the previously audible pedal Doppler signal cannot be found. Which response best addresses the misleading report of pain relief?","options":["Escalate the declining perfusion findings promptly and report the change from the documented baseline.","Accept the pain relief as evidence of successful reperfusion and continue the usual assessment schedule.","Repeat the Doppler assessment later, since analgesia can make the current findings difficult to interpret."],"feedback":["A cooler, paler foot and loss of a previously present signal suggest worsening perfusion; pain relief after analgesia does not negate these objective changes.","Pain relief alone does not establish adequate blood flow, especially when other limb findings have deteriorated.","A repeat assessment may help confirm a finding, but it should not delay escalation of a new loss of perfusion signal with color and temperature change."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Revascularization for Acute Limb Ischemia - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13212160/"},{"title":"Acute Limb Ischemia - Cardiology - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/peripheral-artery-disorders/acute-limb-ischemia"}],"frames":[{"label":"Reperfusion Tissue Stress","detail":"After revascularization, injured muscle can release harmful metabolites, while local edema may increase pressure and impair limb circulation."},{"label":"Tense Swollen Calf","detail":"Increasing calf tension, pain with passive toe movement, and new tingling raise concern for compartment syndrome despite an audible Doppler signal."},{"label":"Urgent Limb Escalation","detail":"Promptly notify the vascular or critical-care team, reporting pain, swelling, sensory changes, and pulse or Doppler findings."},{"label":"Repeat Limb Checks","detail":"Reassess and document limb and systemic findings as directed; immediately report progressive pain, weakness, declining signals, or systemic deterioration."}]},{"id":"s015","unit":"cardio","title":"Mechanical circulatory support","objective":"Explain how an intra-aortic balloon pump supports cardiac function and identify nursing cues that require prompt team communication.","concept":"An intra-aortic balloon pump provides temporary support through counterpulsation. It inflates during diastole after aortic valve closure, displacing blood and raising aortic diastolic pressure, which can augment coronary perfusion pressure. It deflates just before systole, reducing left ventricular afterload and work. Cardiac output may rise modestly, but improved pressure does not guarantee greater coronary blood flow or adequate tissue perfusion, and individual responses vary.","recognize":"Interpret the patient, arterial pressure waveform, ECG signal, and console together. Poor augmentation or device alarms may indicate a support problem, while ongoing signs of hypoperfusion may persist despite balloon support. A cool, pale limb, reduced pulse, or altered sensation on the access side raises concern for vascular compromise; access-site bleeding is another complication cue. Do not assume an augmented pressure proves adequate perfusion.","respond":"Maintain continuous monitoring and assess the patient’s perfusion and access site first. Check that the ECG and arterial pressure signals are available, and respond to alarms according to local protocol and your training. Promptly communicate poor augmentation, ongoing hypoperfusion, bleeding, or limb changes to the responsible clinician and IABP team. Do not independently change device timing or manipulate the catheter.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess the patient’s perfusion, arterial waveform and augmentation, access site, and distal limb pulse, color, temperature, and sensation. Compare findings over time and report persistent hypoperfusion, poor augmentation, bleeding, or new or worsening limb changes promptly, even if the pressure waveform appears improved.","trap":"An IABP is more like a timed assist than a replacement heart: it can reduce left ventricular workload and augment diastolic pressure, but it does not guarantee adequate organ or coronary blood flow. A stronger-looking pressure waveform is not a substitute for reassessing the patient and access limb.","caseStory":"An adult with cardiogenic shock is receiving IABP support, and the arterial tracing shows diastolic augmentation. The foot on the access side becomes cooler and paler, and its pulse is weaker than on the other side. The nurse must decide whether the waveform is reassuring or the limb findings need immediate communication.","caseReason":"The waveform suggests augmentation, but it does not rule out a vascular complication. A cool, pale limb with a weaker pulse is concerning for impaired limb perfusion, so the nurse should promptly notify the responsible clinician and IABP team and continue assessment under local protocol.","teachback":"If diastolic augmentation looks stronger but the access-side foot becomes pale and cool, what will you assess and communicate, and why is the waveform alone insufficient?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with cardiogenic shock is receiving IABP support. A nurse reviewing the device’s purpose is asked how balloon timing supports the left ventricle. Which explanation is most accurate?","options":["The balloon inflates during diastole after aortic valve closure, then deflates just before systole.","The balloon inflates during systole to raise the pressure the left ventricle generates for ejection.","The balloon remains inflated through systolic ejection to maintain continuous support of the aorta."],"feedback":["This describes counterpulsation: diastolic inflation augments aortic diastolic pressure, and pre-systolic deflation reduces left ventricular afterload.","Systolic inflation would oppose ejection rather than provide the described afterload reduction.","The balloon is timed to deflate before systole; remaining inflated through ejection does not describe the intended counterpulsation."]},{"stem":"An adult with an IABP has a visible diastolic augmentation on the arterial tracing. The access-side foot is now pale and cool, with a weaker pulse than the opposite foot. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Promptly report possible impaired limb perfusion to the responsible clinician and IABP team while continuing assessment.","Document the waveform as evidence of adequate support and reassess the foot at the next routine check.","Change the balloon timing independently to improve the access-side pulse before notifying the team."],"feedback":["New pallor, coolness, and a weaker pulse are concerning for limb ischemia and warrant prompt communication, even when augmentation is present.","A pressure waveform does not establish adequate limb perfusion, and delaying communication risks overlooking a vascular complication.","The limb findings require escalation, but nurses should not independently alter device timing or manipulate support outside their role and protocol."]},{"stem":"A patient scheduled for high-risk coronary intervention is being considered for IABP support. During the pre-procedure review, the nurse notes documented moderate to severe aortic regurgitation. What is the best nursing action?","options":["Alert the responsible clinician and IABP team to the documented regurgitation before support is initiated.","Proceed with the planned support and report the regurgitation only if the patient becomes hypotensive.","Treat the regurgitation as a reason to monitor augmentation more closely after the balloon is started."],"feedback":["Moderate to severe aortic regurgitation is listed as a contraindication, so the team should be alerted before IABP support is initiated.","Waiting for hypotension overlooks a documented contraindication that should be communicated before initiation.","Closer monitoring does not address the contraindication; the finding needs timely review by the responsible team."]},{"stem":"After the responsible team reviews IABP timing, the arterial tracing shows more prominent diastolic augmentation. The patient’s access-side foot, however, is becoming cooler and paler, and its pulse is harder to detect. Which reassessment finding should prompt escalation?","options":["The worsening distal limb findings, despite the more prominent augmentation on the tracing.","The more prominent augmentation, which indicates that peripheral perfusion has necessarily improved.","The continued need for IABP support, even though no new patient or device concern is present."],"feedback":["Worsening temperature, color, and pulse are concerning for impaired limb perfusion and require prompt escalation regardless of the tracing.","Augmentation does not guarantee adequate peripheral perfusion, so it cannot explain away the new limb changes.","Ongoing support alone is not the discriminating concern in this vignette; the worsening access-side limb findings are."]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Intra-aortic Balloon Pump: A Focused Review of ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11307388/"},{"title":"Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump - StatPearls","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK542233/?report=reader"}],"frames":[{"label":"Balloon Counterpulsation","detail":"The balloon inflates during diastole and deflates before systole, but improved pressure does not guarantee adequate tissue perfusion."},{"label":"Cool Access-Side Foot","detail":"A cooler, paler foot with a weaker pulse on the access side raises concern for impaired limb perfusion."},{"label":"Contact IABP Team","detail":"Promptly communicate limb changes, poor augmentation, bleeding, or ongoing hypoperfusion to the responsible clinician and IABP team."},{"label":"Recheck Limb Perfusion","detail":"Reassess distal pulse, color, temperature, sensation, access site, and waveform; promptly report persistent hypoperfusion or worsening limb changes."}]},{"id":"s016","unit":"resp","title":"Oxygenation versus ventilation","objective":"Distinguish impaired oxygenation from impaired ventilation and use that distinction to guide timely nursing assessment, communication, and reassessment.","concept":"Oxygenation is the movement of oxygen into arterial blood, while ventilation is the elimination of carbon dioxide from the body. Acute hypoxemic failure can occur when flooded or collapsed airspaces let blood pass through without adequate gas exchange, so added oxygen may not fully correct the problem. Ventilatory failure reflects inadequate carbon dioxide elimination. Both problems can coexist, so oxygen saturation alone does not describe the whole respiratory picture.","recognize":"Consider oxygenation impairment when saturation is low, especially with airspace disease such as pneumonia or pulmonary edema; pulse oximetry and arterial blood gases help assess oxygen status. Consider ventilatory impairment when carbon dioxide elimination is inadequate, assessed with blood gas measurements and the clinical picture. Dyspnea, rapid breathing, accessory muscle use, confusion, or altered consciousness signal concern but do not by themselves identify which function is impaired.","respond":"Prioritize the patient’s breathing, oxygenation, work of breathing, and mental status, and promptly communicate concerning changes to the clinical team. Apply ordered oxygen support and follow local escalation protocols; obtain or help arrange blood gas testing when directed. Report the response and relevant trends rather than relying on saturation alone. Do not independently change respiratory support or assume that supplemental oxygen resolves impaired ventilation.","reassess":"After an intervention, reassess saturation, breathing effort and rate, mental status, and the patient’s response to support. Review blood gas results when available and ordered. Persistent low saturation, worsening effort, altered consciousness, or failure to improve warrants prompt escalation to the clinical team.","trap":"A better saturation is like a brighter dashboard light: it tells you something about oxygenation, not whether carbon dioxide is being cleared. Do not treat pulse oximetry as a complete measure of respiratory function; consider the breathing pattern, mental status, and blood gas information too.","caseStory":"A patient admitted with pneumonia becomes more breathless and restless, with low oxygen saturation despite supplemental oxygen. The chest image shows bilateral airspace opacities, and the team requests blood gas testing. The nurse must decide whether the concern is oxygenation, ventilation, or both, and what change to report promptly.","caseReason":"The low saturation and airspace opacities support impaired oxygenation from lung disease, while restlessness and increased breathing effort signal clinical deterioration. Those signs alone do not establish whether carbon dioxide elimination is impaired. The nurse should promptly communicate the change, support ordered oxygen care, and use the blood gas and ongoing assessment to clarify the picture.","teachback":"If a patient’s oxygen saturation improves after support but the patient becomes more drowsy, what additional assessment information would you seek, and why is the saturation alone insufficient?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with pneumonia has worsening breathlessness and a low oxygen saturation despite supplemental oxygen. The chest image shows airspace opacities, and a blood gas shows low arterial oxygen with carbon dioxide that is not elevated. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The main demonstrated problem is impaired oxygenation, while the available result does not show carbon dioxide retention.","The main demonstrated problem is impaired ventilation because breathlessness indicates inadequate carbon dioxide elimination.","The oxygen saturation establishes that both oxygenation and ventilation are adequate despite the blood gas result."],"feedback":["Low arterial oxygen and airspace disease support impaired oxygenation; the reported carbon dioxide result does not show retention.","Breathlessness can accompany either problem and does not by itself demonstrate impaired carbon dioxide elimination.","A low saturation and low arterial oxygen indicate that oxygenation is not adequate; saturation also does not establish ventilation."]},{"stem":"A patient with acute respiratory illness becomes increasingly tachypneic and confused, and the oxygen saturation is falling. The nurse has an oxygen-support protocol and can contact the rapid response team. What is the best priority action?","options":["Promptly apply support within the protocol, reassess the patient, and escalate the deterioration to the team.","Wait for a blood gas result before providing protocol-based oxygen support or alerting the team.","Focus on obtaining a detailed medication history before addressing the falling saturation and confusion."],"feedback":["Falling saturation with altered consciousness signals deterioration; protocol-based support and prompt escalation should not wait for test results.","Blood gas testing may help clarify oxygenation and ventilation, but waiting delays response to worsening clinical signs.","Medication history can be useful, but it does not take priority over immediate respiratory deterioration."]},{"stem":"A patient with acute pulmonary edema has crackles, low oxygen saturation, and bilateral lung opacities. A clinician orders noninvasive positive pressure support, and the nurse is asked to monitor the response. Which interpretation best guides that monitoring?","options":["Airspace flooding can impair oxygenation, so follow saturation, work of breathing, mental status, and the response to ordered support.","Crackles primarily prove carbon dioxide retention, so saturation and oxygenation response are not useful to monitor.","Bilateral opacities establish a nonpulmonary cause, so respiratory reassessment can wait until the edema is treated."],"feedback":["Pulmonary edema can fill airspaces and impair gas exchange; monitoring both oxygenation and clinical response is appropriate.","Crackles indicate airway opening or lung findings, not proof of carbon dioxide retention; ventilation requires its own assessment.","Bilateral opacities can occur with pulmonary edema, and the patient’s respiratory status still requires close reassessment."]},{"stem":"A patient with COPD and chronic hypoventilation receives ordered supplemental oxygen. The saturation rises, but the patient becomes more drowsy. Which nursing interpretation and response is most appropriate?","options":["The improved saturation does not confirm adequate ventilation; promptly report the drowsiness and seek reassessment, including blood gas review when ordered.","The higher saturation confirms that respiratory failure has resolved, so the drowsiness can be monitored without escalation.","The drowsiness proves that oxygen has caused carbon dioxide retention, so independently stop the oxygen support."],"feedback":["Saturation reflects oxygenation rather than carbon dioxide elimination; new drowsiness warrants prompt communication and further assessment.","A higher saturation does not establish adequate ventilation, and altered consciousness is a concerning change.","Excessive correction of hypoxemia may reduce respiratory drive in chronic hypoventilation, but drowsiness alone does not prove its cause or justify independently stopping support."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Hypoxemic Respiratory Failure (AHRF), Including ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/acute-hypoxemic-respiratory-failure-ahrf-including-acute-respiratory-distress-syndrome-ards"},{"title":"Overview of Respiratory Failure - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/overview-of-respiratory-failure"}],"frames":[{"label":"Airspace Gas Exchange","detail":"Pneumonia can flood or collapse airspaces, allowing blood to pass with inadequate oxygen transfer; ventilation failure instead limits carbon dioxide elimination."},{"label":"Low Saturation Persists","detail":"Low saturation despite oxygen and bilateral opacities suggest impaired oxygenation, while restlessness and effort signal deterioration but do not identify ventilation status."},{"label":"Report Breathing Change","detail":"Promptly communicate worsening effort and saturation, support ordered oxygen, and help arrange blood gas testing when directed by the team."},{"label":"Track Whole Response","detail":"Recheck saturation, breathing effort, rate, and mental status; persistent hypoxemia, worsening effort, or altered consciousness warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s017","unit":"resp","title":"Mechanical ventilation and alarms","objective":"Interpret ventilator pressure and expiratory-flow cues to recognize possible resistance, reduced compliance, or intrinsic PEEP and communicate concerns promptly.","concept":"Mechanical ventilation creates airflow by positive pressure, so airway pressure reflects both resistance to flow and the effort needed to expand the lungs and chest wall. Peak pressure includes both components, while plateau pressure during an inspiratory hold reflects the elastic component when the patient is passive. Incomplete exhalation can leave intrinsic PEEP, increasing breathing effort and potentially reducing venous return and cardiac output.","recognize":"A rising peak pressure with little change in plateau pressure points toward increased resistance, such as a kinked or obstructed tube, secretions, or bronchospasm. When peak and plateau pressures rise together, reduced respiratory-system compliance is more likely. Expiratory flow that has not returned to baseline before the next breath suggests air trapping; assess the patient as well as the ventilator display.","respond":"First assess the patient, including breathing effort, oxygenation, chest movement, and hemodynamic status, while checking the visible circuit for an obvious problem. Promptly communicate the pressure trends and waveform findings to the respiratory therapist and responsible clinician. Follow orders and local protocols for actions such as checking or clearing suspected secretions; do not independently change ventilator settings or delay escalation when the patient is deteriorating.","reassess":"After a team-directed or protocol-based action, reassess the patient and compare peak and plateau pressures, expiratory flow, chest movement, oxygenation, and blood pressure with prior findings. Escalate promptly if pressure abnormalities persist or worsen, exhalation remains incomplete, work of breathing increases, or hemodynamic status declines.","trap":"Peak pressure is not a diagnosis: it is more like the total effort needed to push air through a passage and expand a spring. Comparing it with plateau pressure helps separate a flow-resistance problem from a compliance problem, while the expiratory waveform can reveal air trapping.","caseStory":"A patient receiving ventilation for an obstructive exacerbation develops a rising peak pressure alarm. Plateau pressure is unchanged, and the patient has visible increased breathing effort; the expiratory flow tracing does not return to baseline before the next breath. The nurse must decide what to assess and report first.","caseReason":"The unchanged plateau pressure makes a new increase in elastic pressure less likely, while the higher peak pressure suggests increased resistance. Incomplete exhalation raises concern for intrinsic PEEP, which can increase work of breathing and impair venous return. The nurse should assess the patient and visible circuit, then promptly communicate these findings to the respiratory therapist and clinician.","teachback":"If peak pressure rises but plateau pressure stays stable, what would you assess and report, and how would your interpretation differ if both pressures rose together?","questions":[{"stem":"A ventilated patient develops a rising peak pressure alarm. During a team assessment, plateau pressure is similar to its prior value, and the peak-to-plateau difference has widened. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these findings?","options":["Increased airflow resistance is likely; assess the visible circuit and patient, then promptly report possible tube obstruction, secretions, or bronchospasm.","Reduced lung compliance is most likely; conclude that the lungs have become stiffer and report only the plateau pressure.","Intrinsic PEEP is confirmed by the pressure difference; independently lower the ventilator rate before notifying the team."],"feedback":["Correct. A wider peak-to-plateau difference indicates a larger resistive component; tube kinking or plugging, airway secretions, and bronchospasm are possible causes to assess and report.","This is misleading because reduced compliance is suggested by increased elastic pressure, reflected in plateau pressure, rather than a widening resistive difference alone.","The pressure difference does not confirm intrinsic PEEP, and ventilator changes should not be made independently. Incomplete expiratory flow can suggest intrinsic PEEP and should be assessed and communicated."]},{"stem":"A patient with severe airflow obstruction becomes hypotensive while receiving mechanical ventilation. The expiratory flow tracing continues when the next breath begins, and the patient appears to be working harder to inhale. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly communicate concern for incomplete exhalation and possible intrinsic PEEP, while assessing the patient and hemodynamic status.","Treat the blood pressure change as proof of reduced lung compliance and focus the report on plateau pressure alone.","Assume the flow tracing is not clinically important because oxygen saturation is stable, and wait for the next scheduled assessment."],"feedback":["Correct. Persistent expiratory flow at the next breath suggests incomplete emptying and possible intrinsic PEEP; this can increase inspiratory work and reduce venous return, so the patient and team need prompt attention.","This is misleading because the expiratory-flow finding supports air trapping, not proof of reduced compliance. The hemodynamic change also warrants immediate assessment and communication.","Stable oxygen saturation does not rule out intrinsic PEEP or its effects on work of breathing and venous return. The persistent flow and hypotension should be reported without waiting for a routine check."]},{"stem":"After repositioning a mechanically ventilated patient with marked abdominal distention, both peak and plateau pressures rise, while the difference between them changes little. The patient has no obvious circuit kink. Which nursing interpretation and next step are most appropriate?","options":["Reduced respiratory-system compliance is a concern; assess chest movement and positioning, and promptly report the pressure trend and clinical context.","A new airway-resistance problem is established; focus only on airway secretions because plateau pressure has risen too.","The pattern proves intrinsic PEEP; reassure the team that the abdominal distention cannot affect ventilator pressures."],"feedback":["Correct. A rise in both pressures with little change in their difference suggests an increased elastic component. Abdominal restriction can reduce compliance, so assessment and communication of the timing and findings are appropriate.","This is misleading because a resistance increase would be more consistent with a widened peak-to-plateau difference. Secretions remain a possible concern when supported by other findings, but the pressure pattern points toward reduced compliance.","This is misleading because the pattern does not establish intrinsic PEEP, and abdominal restriction can increase elastic pressure by limiting chest-wall or diaphragmatic excursion. The findings should be reported for team assessment."]},{"stem":"A patient has suspected secretion plugging and receives airway care under the care plan and local protocol. Afterward, peak pressure decreases while plateau pressure remains near its prior value; oxygenation is unchanged. What is the best interpretation and reassessment?","options":["The resistive component may have improved; reassess breathing effort, pressure trends, and expiratory flow, and report persistent abnormalities.","Lung compliance has definitely improved; no further assessment is needed if oxygenation remains stable.","Intrinsic PEEP has been eliminated because peak pressure fell; stop monitoring the expiratory-flow tracing."],"feedback":["Correct. A lower peak pressure with a stable plateau pressure is consistent with reduced resistance, but it does not remove the need to reassess the patient and ventilator trends.","This overstates what the pressure change shows. Stable plateau pressure does not demonstrate improved compliance, and stable oxygenation alone does not replace reassessment.","A fall in peak pressure does not establish that intrinsic PEEP has resolved. Expiratory flow and the patient's work of breathing remain relevant reassessment cues."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Mechanical Ventilation - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/overview-of-mechanical-ventilation"},{"title":"Ventilatory Failure - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/ventilatory-failure"}],"frames":[{"label":"Positive Pressure Mechanics","detail":"Ventilator peak pressure reflects resistance and elastic load; plateau pressure during an inspiratory hold reflects elastic load in a passive patient."},{"label":"Resistance And Trapping","detail":"Rising peak with unchanged plateau suggests resistance; expiratory flow not returning to baseline signals possible air trapping and intrinsic PEEP."},{"label":"Check And Communicate","detail":"Assess the patient and visible circuit, then promptly report pressure trends and waveform findings to respiratory therapy and the responsible clinician."},{"label":"Compare After Action","detail":"Reassess effort, pressures, expiratory flow, oxygenation, and blood pressure; persistent abnormalities or declining stability require prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s018","unit":"resp","title":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome","objective":"Recognize the major features of ARDS and explain how nursing assessment and team-based care support lung protection and safe positioning.","concept":"ARDS is acute inflammatory lung injury with alveolar-capillary damage, leak, and alveolar flooding or collapse. Uneven injury makes some regions poorly compliant while other regions remain aerated, creating shunt and hypoxemia and making ventilation potentially injurious. Lung-protective ventilation limits further stress. Prone positioning can redistribute ventilation more evenly, recruit dependent regions, and improve ventilation-perfusion matching. Its value is not simply a better oxygen reading; it may protect lung mechanics.","recognize":"Suspect ARDS when acute respiratory decline follows an illness or injury and is accompanied by bilateral lung opacities and hypoxemia. Consider whether heart failure could explain the findings, since ARDS requires a non-cardiac explanation for the lung edema. Assess breathing effort, oxygenation, and the clinical course; communicate the pattern rather than treating one finding as diagnostic by itself.","respond":"Prioritize oxygenation, close observation, and prevention of additional lung injury. For a mechanically ventilated patient, support prescribed lung-protective ventilation and collaborate with the critical-care team about prone positioning when appropriate. Follow local protocols and coordinate turns with trained staff, protecting the airway, vascular access, and skin. Do not independently change ventilator settings or let improved oxygenation delay escalation for deterioration.","reassess":"After an intervention or position change, reassess oxygenation, breathing effort, hemodynamic tolerance, and the security of the airway and lines. Report worsening respiratory status, instability, or suspected device displacement promptly. Oxygenation improvement alone does not establish that lung injury has resolved or determine when proning should stop.","trap":"A better oxygen reading after proning is like a clearer view through one window: it does not prove the whole building is safe. Proning may help protect lung mechanics, so judge tolerance and the broader clinical picture with the team rather than using oxygenation alone to declare recovery or stop positioning.","caseStory":"A patient being treated for sepsis develops worsening hypoxemia and bilateral opacities on chest imaging. The team finds no evidence that heart failure explains the lung findings, and the patient is receiving mechanical ventilation. The nurse must communicate the pattern and help the team consider lung-protective care and positioning.","caseReason":"The acute decline, bilateral opacities, and hypoxemia after sepsis are consistent with concern for ARDS, while the lack of a cardiac explanation supports that concern. The nurse should communicate these findings and collaborate on prescribed protective strategies, including whether prone positioning is appropriate, while monitoring the patient and securing devices.","teachback":"A ventilated patient’s oxygenation improves while prone; what other findings would you assess before concluding that the patient is tolerating the intervention or that positioning can stop?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient develops worsening hypoxemia after severe pneumonia. Chest imaging shows bilateral opacities, and the team has found no evidence that heart failure explains the lung findings. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate?","options":["The findings raise concern for ARDS, and the team should consider the acute course and non-cardiac explanation together.","The bilateral opacities establish cardiogenic edema, so the nurse should focus communication on fluid overload.","The hypoxemia alone confirms ARDS, so other causes of respiratory failure do not need consideration."],"feedback":["This interpretation combines acute hypoxemia and bilateral opacities with the absence of a cardiac explanation, supporting concern for ARDS without treating one finding as definitive.","Bilateral opacities can occur in ARDS, and the case specifically says heart failure has not been found to explain them.","Hypoxemia is important but is not sufficient by itself; the clinical context and other possible explanations matter."]},{"stem":"A mechanically ventilated patient with ARDS remains severely hypoxemic despite prescribed lung-protective ventilation. The critical-care team has ordered prone positioning, and trained staff are available. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Coordinate the prescribed turn with the team, protect the airway and access, and monitor the patient’s tolerance.","Delay the turn until oxygenation worsens further, because positioning is useful only as a rescue measure.","Turn the patient without the team so positioning begins quickly, then check the airway and lines afterward."],"feedback":["Prone positioning is supported for appropriate ventilated patients with ARDS; team coordination and attention to the airway and access address recognized risks.","Proning is a management strategy for ARDS and need not be reserved only for further deterioration after an order is in place.","The turn carries risks such as airway or access displacement, so coordination and protection during the intervention are important."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving high-flow oxygen for COVID-related hypoxemic respiratory failure is awake, cooperative, and asks whether lying prone might help. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Discuss awake proning with the care team and support it under local protocol while continuing to monitor respiratory status.","Tell the patient awake proning is not useful unless an endotracheal tube is already in place.","Encourage prolonged prone positioning independently and defer escalation unless oxygenation fails to improve."],"feedback":["Evidence described for nonintubated proning includes potential benefit in COVID-related illness; team discussion, protocol-based support, and continued monitoring are appropriate.","The source describes interest and potential benefit in nonintubated patients, so proning is not limited to patients with an endotracheal tube.","Awake proning should not replace monitoring or timely escalation when respiratory status worsens, and its use should be coordinated with the care team."]},{"stem":"After a prone session, a ventilated patient’s oxygenation improves, but the patient develops new hemodynamic instability. Which reassessment and response are most appropriate?","options":["Assess overall tolerance, including hemodynamics, breathing, and device security, and promptly alert the team about the instability.","Treat the improved oxygenation as proof of recovery and continue positioning without reporting the new finding.","Use oxygenation alone to decide that proning should stop, without evaluating the patient’s other responses."],"feedback":["Prone positioning can improve oxygenation, but hemodynamic consequences are a recognized concern; the new instability warrants prompt communication and a broader assessment.","Improved oxygenation does not prove recovery, and new instability should not be ignored.","The decision to continue or stop proning should not rely solely on gas exchange; other clinical findings and the team’s assessment matter."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Prone Positioning in Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10965391"},{"title":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436002/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Inflammatory Lung Injury","detail":"After sepsis, alveolar-capillary injury can flood or collapse airspaces, creating shunt, hypoxemia, and uneven lung compliance."},{"label":"Bilateral Opacities","detail":"Acute hypoxemia with bilateral opacities and no cardiac explanation supports concern for ARDS, but no single finding establishes diagnosis."},{"label":"Protect And Coordinate","detail":"Communicate the pattern and collaborate on prescribed lung-protective care and prone positioning when appropriate, coordinating turns with trained staff."},{"label":"Check Position Tolerance","detail":"After positioning, reassess oxygenation, effort, hemodynamic tolerance, and airway and line security; worsening status or device displacement needs prompt reporting."}]},{"id":"s019","unit":"resp","title":"Pulmonary embolism","objective":"Recognize possible acute pulmonary embolism, connect its effects to clinical cues, and prioritize nursing assessment and escalation.","concept":"A pulmonary embolism usually occurs when a clot from a deep vein travels through the right side of the heart and obstructs pulmonary arteries. Obstruction and vasoconstriction can raise right ventricular workload, impair cardiac output, and disrupt ventilation and perfusion, contributing to hypoxemia or hemodynamic instability. The degree of effect varies with clot burden and cardiopulmonary reserve, so clinical status and right ventricular strain matter more than clot location alone.","recognize":"Consider pulmonary embolism with new, unexplained dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea, or tachycardia, especially alongside immobility, surgery, cancer, prior thromboembolism, or limb symptoms. Syncope or hypotension raises concern for severe impact. These cues are nonspecific: pneumonia, cardiac ischemia, heart failure, and other conditions can resemble PE, and a normal oxygen saturation or absent leg symptoms does not exclude it.","respond":"Assess respiratory effort, oxygenation, heart rate, blood pressure, mental status, and available monitoring; promptly communicate suspected PE and changes in stability to the responsible clinician or emergency response team under local protocols. Support ordered evaluation and treatment, which may include imaging and anticoagulation. Do not independently diagnose or prescribe, and do not let a nonspecific chest radiograph or initially normal oxygen saturation delay escalation when concern remains.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess dyspnea, work of breathing, oxygenation, heart rate, blood pressure, and mental status, comparing with prior findings and the response to ordered support. Escalate promptly for worsening respiratory distress, new hypotension, presyncope or syncope, or signs of deteriorating perfusion.","trap":"A normal oxygen saturation is like one clear window in a building: it does not show whether the whole structure is safe. PE can still be present when oxygenation appears normal, so interpret the complete clinical picture and escalate unexplained concerning changes.","caseStory":"A patient recovering from surgery with reduced mobility develops sudden shortness of breath and sharp pain with inspiration. The patient is tachycardic, has no obvious leg swelling, and currently has an oxygen saturation within the expected range. The nurse must decide whether to treat the findings as reassuring or communicate concern for PE promptly.","caseReason":"Recent surgery and reduced mobility increase thromboembolic risk, while sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and tachycardia are compatible with PE. Neither absent leg symptoms nor a currently expected oxygen saturation rules it out. The nurse should promptly report the concern and continue focused assessment rather than assume a benign cause.","teachback":"When a patient has new unexplained dyspnea but a normal oxygen saturation, what other cues would shape your concern for PE, and what change would prompt urgent escalation?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with active cancer and reduced mobility develops abrupt dyspnea, pleuritic chest discomfort, and tachycardia. The chest radiograph shows no clear alternative explanation, and there is no reported leg pain. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication?","options":["PE remains a concern because the symptoms and risk factors fit, even without leg symptoms or a diagnostic chest radiograph.","PE is unlikely because the patient has no leg pain, so the nurse should focus communication on other causes.","The chest radiograph makes PE sufficiently unlikely that additional concern should wait for oxygen saturation to fall."],"feedback":["Cancer and reduced mobility increase risk, and dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and tachycardia can occur with PE. Leg symptoms are often absent, and chest radiographs are commonly nonspecific.","This overweights the absence of leg symptoms; patients with PE often have no noticeable limb symptoms.","A chest radiograph is not a reliable exclusion test for PE, and waiting for desaturation could delay communication of a concerning change."]},{"stem":"A patient with suspected PE becomes suddenly light-headed and briefly loses consciousness. The blood pressure is now substantially lower than earlier, and the patient remains tachycardic. Which nursing priority is most appropriate?","options":["Promptly activate the appropriate urgent response and communicate the hemodynamic change while continuing immediate assessment.","Complete a detailed history of thromboembolism risk before contacting the responsible clinician.","Wait for ordered lung imaging because PE severity cannot be judged from symptoms and vital signs."],"feedback":["Syncope with new hypotension suggests potentially severe hemodynamic impact. Immediate escalation and continued assessment take priority over completing a history or waiting for imaging.","Risk history is useful, but gathering it should not delay escalation for acute loss of consciousness and falling blood pressure.","Imaging helps diagnosis, but this unstable change warrants urgent communication and response while evaluation proceeds."]},{"stem":"A patient with a long-term central venous catheter for cancer treatment reports new shortness of breath and has unexplained tachypnea. There is no recent surgery and no known leg swelling. Which action best transfers PE assessment to this setting?","options":["Include PE in the differential and promptly report the new symptoms and catheter-associated risk for clinician assessment.","Deprioritize PE because the usual source is a leg clot and this patient has no leg swelling.","Attribute the symptoms to cancer treatment unless oxygen saturation becomes abnormal."],"feedback":["Cancer and indwelling venous catheters are PE risk factors, and emboli can also originate in arm or central veins. New unexplained respiratory symptoms warrant communication even without leg swelling.","This assumes all emboli arise from symptomatic leg clots; central venous sources are also described, and leg symptoms are often absent.","PE symptoms are nonspecific, and oxygen saturation may be normal. Waiting for it to become abnormal risks overlooking a potentially serious cause."]},{"stem":"A patient under evaluation for PE has persistent unexplained dyspnea and tachycardia, but pulse oximetry remains within the expected range. Which reassessment interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Continue focused assessment and communicate persistent concern because normal oxygen saturation does not exclude PE.","Treat the oxygen reading as evidence against PE and defer further communication unless chest pain develops.","Assume the symptoms are anxiety because oxygenation is normal and reassessment can stop."],"feedback":["Oxygen saturation can be normal in PE, including with smaller clot burden or compensatory hyperventilation. Persistent unexplained symptoms still merit assessment and communication.","A normal reading does not exclude PE, and chest pain is not required for concern when dyspnea and tachycardia remain unexplained.","Normal oxygenation does not establish anxiety as the cause. Stopping reassessment could miss evolving respiratory or hemodynamic deterioration."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Pulmonary Embolism (PE) - Pulmonology - MSD Manual ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-embolism/pulmonary-embolism-pe"},{"title":"Pulmonary Embolism (PE) - Pulmonology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-embolism/pulmonary-embolism-pe?autoredirectid=25470&ruleredirectid=382"}],"frames":[{"label":"Clot Strains Heart","detail":"A traveling clot can obstruct pulmonary arteries, increase right ventricular workload, disrupt ventilation-perfusion matching, and impair cardiac output."},{"label":"Sudden Symptoms Risk","detail":"Postoperative immobility with sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and tachycardia raises PE concern despite expected saturation or absent leg swelling."},{"label":"Escalate PE Concern","detail":"Promptly communicate suspected PE and stability changes, assess vital signs and mental status, and support evaluation and treatment under local protocols."},{"label":"Watch Perfusion Decline","detail":"Reassess breathing, oxygenation, heart rate, blood pressure, and alertness; hypotension, syncope, or worsening distress requires prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s020","unit":"resp","title":"COPD and severe asthma","objective":"Explain how nurses recognize and escalate ventilatory deterioration in severe asthma and acute-on-chronic COPD while supporting team-directed care that limits respiratory workload.","concept":"In severe asthma, bronchoconstriction and air trapping make breathing increasingly effortful; inadequate ventilation can raise carbon dioxide and cause acidemia. In COPD exacerbations, airflow obstruction and dynamic hyperinflation can overload already vulnerable respiratory muscles. In both, the balance between breathing workload and muscle capacity matters. Team-directed support aims to address the cause and reduce workload, while severe asthma ventilation may prioritize limiting air trapping over normalizing carbon dioxide.","recognize":"Look for worsening breathlessness, rapid or shallow breathing, accessory muscle use, declining breath size, irregular breathing, or altered alertness. In severe asthma, quieter wheezing does not by itself prove improvement if effort or sensorium worsens. In COPD, compare findings with the person's baseline and assess clinical fatigue; blood gas results need interpretation alongside the examination.","respond":"Stay with a deteriorating patient, promptly alert the responsible clinician or rapid response team according to local protocol, and communicate the breathing pattern, effort, alertness, oxygenation, and trends. Support ordered treatment and monitoring, including noninvasive ventilation when selected by the team. Avoid independently changing ventilator settings or assuming that normalizing carbon dioxide is the priority in severe asthma.","reassess":"After an intervention, reassess work of breathing, respiratory pattern, alertness, oxygenation, and tolerance of support, then report trends to the team. Escalate promptly for worsening fatigue, declining alertness, shallow or irregular breathing, or worsening respiratory distress, even if wheezing or a single blood gas value appears less concerning.","trap":"A quieter chest is not always a safer chest: in severe asthma, worsening airflow and fatigue can make wheezing less audible. Think of ventilation as a traffic jam; pushing more traffic through before the road clears can worsen the backup, so the team may prioritize allowing more time for exhalation over rapidly normalizing carbon dioxide.","caseStory":"A patient with a severe asthma exacerbation has increasing accessory muscle use and is now answering in short phrases. Wheezing is less audible than earlier, and the patient is becoming drowsy. The nurse must decide whether the quieter chest suggests improvement or whether to escalate immediately.","caseReason":"The increasing effort, limited speech, and new drowsiness indicate possible worsening ventilatory failure, despite the quieter wheeze. The nurse should promptly alert the responsible team and report these changes rather than interpreting reduced wheezing alone as recovery.","teachback":"When a patient with obstructive lung disease becomes quieter or less interactive, what other findings would help you decide whether breathing is improving or failing, and what would you communicate to the team?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with severe asthma initially wheezed loudly but now has faint wheezing, shallow breaths, increasing accessory muscle use, and new confusion. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Airflow and ventilation may be worsening as fatigue develops, so the change requires urgent escalation.","The faint wheezing most likely means bronchoconstriction has resolved, so routine observation is sufficient.","The confusion is more likely unrelated because the patient is still breathing without assistance."],"feedback":["Correct. Less audible wheezing does not establish improvement when breathing becomes shallow, effort rises, and alertness declines; these findings warrant urgent team assessment.","This is misleading because a quieter chest can accompany severely restricted airflow, especially when fatigue is developing.","This overlooks that worsening ventilation can cause carbon dioxide retention and altered alertness, making the change clinically urgent."]},{"stem":"A patient with COPD is receiving team-selected noninvasive positive pressure ventilation for an acute exacerbation. The patient becomes less alert, has increasingly shallow breaths, and uses accessory muscles more than before. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly escalate the clinical deterioration to the responsible team and report the change in alertness, effort, and breathing pattern.","Wait for the next scheduled blood gas because a change in alertness alone cannot indicate treatment failure.","Remove the mask to rest the patient, then reassess after the patient has had time to recover."],"feedback":["Correct. Clinical deterioration, including worsening effort and sensorium, requires prompt assessment; blood gas results should not replace clinical judgment.","This delays escalation and gives excessive weight to a test rather than the patient's worsening clinical signs.","Removing support without team direction could worsen the patient's breathing and does not address the signs of deterioration."]},{"stem":"A patient with status asthmaticus is intubated in intensive care. The nurse notices that exhalation appears incomplete and the patient's blood pressure is falling. Which concern should the nurse raise first with the team?","options":["Air trapping may be increasing intrathoracic pressure and impairing venous return, so the team should assess for dynamic hyperinflation.","Carbon dioxide should be lowered as quickly as possible by increasing ventilation, because this is the safest way to prevent complications.","The falling blood pressure is expected during asthma ventilation and can be observed unless oxygenation also declines."],"feedback":["Correct. In severe asthma, incomplete exhalation can contribute to dynamic hyperinflation, which may impair venous return and cause hypotension; promptly report the pattern for team assessment.","This is misleading because aggressive ventilation to normalize carbon dioxide can worsen air trapping and its complications.","Hypotension is not a finding to dismiss in this context; air trapping can compromise circulation and warrants prompt communication."]},{"stem":"A patient with acute-on-chronic COPD is receiving noninvasive ventilation. A blood gas still shows elevated carbon dioxide, but the patient is more alert, breathing with less effort, and has a more regular pattern. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Interpret the blood gas alongside the improved clinical findings and continue close reassessment and team communication.","Treat the elevated carbon dioxide alone as proof that support has failed and prepare to remove noninvasive ventilation.","Assume the patient is fully recovered because alertness and breathing effort have improved."],"feedback":["Correct. Patients differ in their tolerance of elevated carbon dioxide, and clinical assessment is central; continue monitoring and communicate the overall trend.","This treats one measurement as decisive and disregards clinical improvement; blood gas values can be misleading when considered alone.","Improvement is encouraging but does not establish recovery; continued reassessment is needed for renewed fatigue or deterioration."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Asthma - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/asthma-and-related-disorders/asthma"},{"title":"Ventilatory Failure - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/ventilatory-failure"}],"frames":[{"label":"Obstructed Exhalation","detail":"Severe asthma and COPD can cause airflow obstruction and air trapping, increasing breathing workload and risking inadequate ventilation and fatigue."},{"label":"Quiet Chest Danger","detail":"In severe asthma, quieter wheezing alongside increasing effort, short phrases, or drowsiness can signal deterioration rather than recovery."},{"label":"Alert Team Promptly","detail":"Stay with the deteriorating patient and alert the clinician or rapid response team under local protocol, reporting effort, alertness, oxygenation, and trends."},{"label":"Drowsiness Escalation","detail":"Reassess breathing pattern, effort, alertness, oxygenation, and support tolerance; worsening fatigue or declining alertness warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s021","unit":"resp","title":"Pneumonia and aspiration","objective":"Differentiate likely aspiration pneumonitis from aspiration pneumonia by considering the event, symptom timing, and clinical trajectory, then prioritize nursing assessment and communication.","concept":"An aspiration event can produce mechanical airway obstruction, chemical injury, or bacterial infection, and more than one process may coexist. Acidic gastric material can rapidly inflame airways and alveoli, causing bronchoconstriction and edema that impair gas exchange. Aspiration of colonized material can seed infection. Both conditions may create dependent infiltrates, so imaging alone cannot distinguish them; onset and trajectory help guide nursing concern and team evaluation.","recognize":"Minutes to hours after witnessed large-volume emesis, abrupt cough, wheeze, tachypnea, or hypoxemia favors chemical pneumonitis or obstruction. Progressive fever, productive cough, and dyspnea over hours to days, especially after unwitnessed aspiration or with dysphagia, favors pneumonia. Patterns overlap. A high fever leans toward infection, but neither symptoms nor a radiograph alone settles the distinction.","respond":"First assess airway patency, work of breathing, oxygenation, and hemodynamic status. Position to support breathing and provide oxygen or suction only within your competence, orders, and local protocol. Promptly report the aspiration history, timing, material, trajectory, and new findings to the responsible clinician. Do not infer bacterial infection from an infiltrate or start antibiotics independently; treatment follows clinical evaluation and orders.","reassess":"After nursing actions, trend oxygenation, respiratory effort, mental status, cough, lung sounds, and vital signs. Escalate worsening hypoxemia, increasing respiratory distress, declining consciousness, or hemodynamic instability promptly. A progressively worsening course or new high fever also warrants timely clinician reassessment for possible infection.","trap":"A dependent opacity is a location clue, not proof of infection, like smoke damage that does not reveal whether the cause was a brief chemical release or a spreading fire. Sudden symptoms after aspiration do not automatically call for antibiotics, and early improvement does not rule out later superinfection.","caseStory":"A patient vomits while drowsy after a procedure and develops abrupt coughing, wheezing, and increased work of breathing. Imaging later shows a dependent infiltrate, but the patient has no progressive fever and begins to improve with supportive care. The nurse must decide what the pattern suggests and what to communicate.","caseReason":"The abrupt onset after a witnessed event favors chemical pneumonitis or airway obstruction over an infection that develops progressively. A dependent infiltrate cannot distinguish the two. The nurse should trend respiratory status, communicate the event and trajectory, and escalate any deterioration rather than assume antibiotics are needed.","teachback":"When a patient has a dependent infiltrate after suspected aspiration, what details about the event and subsequent clinical course would help you communicate whether chemical injury or infection is more likely?","questions":[{"stem":"A drowsy adult vomits, then within minutes develops abrupt coughing, wheezing, and hypoxemia. A radiograph shows a dependent infiltrate. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these findings?","options":["Suspect chemical pneumonitis or obstruction, assess respiratory status, and promptly report the event and findings.","Treat the infiltrate as proof of bacterial pneumonia and independently initiate antibiotic treatment.","Consider aspiration unlikely because symptoms began immediately, and continue routine observation without reporting."],"feedback":["Abrupt respiratory symptoms after witnessed emesis favor chemical injury or obstruction. The infiltrate does not establish infection, so respiratory assessment and communication are priorities.","A radiograph may show an infiltrate in either chemical pneumonitis or aspiration pneumonia. Antibiotic treatment requires clinical evaluation and an appropriate order.","Immediate symptoms after a witnessed aspiration event support, rather than exclude, chemical pneumonitis or obstruction. New hypoxemia and respiratory symptoms warrant prompt assessment and communication."]},{"stem":"A patient aspirates emesis and suddenly becomes increasingly distressed, with worsening oxygenation and reduced alertness. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Immediately assess airway and breathing, support oxygenation within protocol, and activate urgent help for deterioration.","Wait for fever or a sputum culture before escalating, because infection must be confirmed first.","Focus on arranging swallowing evaluation before addressing the acute respiratory change."],"feedback":["Worsening oxygenation, respiratory distress, and reduced alertness are urgent changes regardless of whether the cause is chemical injury, obstruction, or infection. Immediate assessment and escalation take priority.","Acute respiratory compromise needs action now; waiting for evidence of infection delays response to a potentially serious airway or lung injury.","Swallowing evaluation may help identify an underlying risk after stabilization, but it does not address the immediate deterioration."]},{"stem":"An adult recovering from a stroke has dysphagia and several days of worsening cough, productive sputum, fever, and dyspnea. No aspiration event was witnessed. Which nursing interpretation and communication are most appropriate?","options":["Report concern for aspiration pneumonia and the progressive course for clinician evaluation and treatment planning.","Assume chemical pneumonitis because aspiration may be unwitnessed, and report only if symptoms begin suddenly.","Conclude that aspiration is excluded without a witnessed event and focus only on routine oral care."],"feedback":["Dysphagia raises aspiration risk, and progressive symptoms over hours to days with fever and productive cough favor aspiration pneumonia. The nurse should communicate the pattern for clinician evaluation.","Chemical pneumonitis more often follows a witnessed event with abrupt symptoms. An unwitnessed or recurrent aspiration can still lead to pneumonia.","Many aspiration episodes are unwitnessed, and dysphagia is a relevant risk factor. The progressive respiratory and infectious features merit communication, not dismissal."]},{"stem":"A patient develops abrupt respiratory symptoms after aspirating gastric contents and initially improves with supportive care. The next day, the patient develops worsening dyspnea, productive cough, and a high fever. What is the best nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Recognize possible bacterial superinfection, reassess respiratory status, and promptly report the change to the clinician.","Consider the high fever expected in chemical pneumonitis and defer communication while the patient remains under observation.","Use the original dependent infiltrate alone to confirm infection and report no need for further clinical reassessment."],"feedback":["Chemical pneumonitis may improve, but bacterial superinfection can develop. Worsening symptoms and high fever warrant reassessment and prompt communication.","Fever can occur with chemical pneumonitis, but a worsening course and high fever increase concern for infection. The change should not be dismissed.","A dependent infiltrate does not distinguish pneumonitis from pneumonia. The patient's changing symptoms, rather than the original image alone, call for reassessment and communication."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Chemical Pneumonitis and Aspiration Pneumonia - The Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pneumonia/chemical-pneumonitis-and-aspiration-pneumonia"},{"title":"Aspiration Risk - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK470169/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Witnessed emesis","detail":"Minutes after large-volume emesis, acidic material may cause abrupt airway inflammation, bronchoconstriction, edema, or mechanical obstruction."},{"label":"Abrupt respiratory change","detail":"Sudden cough, wheeze, tachypnea, or hypoxemia favors chemical injury or obstruction; progressive fever and productive cough favor infection."},{"label":"Report event trajectory","detail":"Communicate aspiration timing, material, respiratory changes, and trajectory promptly; support breathing and use oxygen or suction only within competence and protocol."},{"label":"Trend for decline","detail":"Track oxygenation, effort, mentation, and vital signs; promptly escalate worsening hypoxemia, distress, consciousness, instability, or new high fever."}]},{"id":"s022","unit":"resp","title":"Pleural emergencies","objective":"Differentiate urgent pneumothorax cues from pleural fluid findings and coordinate timely, protocol-based nursing response and reassessment.","concept":"The pleural space normally contains a thin layer of fluid that helps the lungs move against the chest wall. Air entering that space can raise pressure and collapse the lung; in tension pneumothorax, trapped air also impairs venous return and can rapidly cause cardiovascular collapse. Fluid accumulation has different causes: systemic pressure changes often produce transudates, while local processes such as infection or cancer commonly produce exudates. Recognizing the distinction guides urgent escalation and evaluation.","recognize":"Pneumothorax may cause sudden or gradual dyspnea and pleuritic pain, with reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance on the affected side. In a mechanically ventilated patient, sudden hypotension with these findings is especially concerning for tension physiology. Pleural fluid is identified through examination and imaging, then often sampled for analysis. On imaging, peripheral air without lung markings suggests pneumothorax; fluid requires a different evaluation.","respond":"For suspected tension pneumothorax, alert the emergency or critical-care team immediately and follow local emergency protocols; do not delay escalation for a radiograph. Support the patient, monitor vital signs and oxygenation, and communicate the onset, ventilatory context, examination changes, and response to actions. Do not independently perform decompression or select treatment. For other pneumothoraces or effusions, coordinate imaging, ordered treatment, and follow-up with the responsible clinician.","reassess":"After team-directed intervention, reassess respiratory effort, oxygenation, breath sounds, blood pressure, and overall stability, comparing findings with the pre-intervention state. Report persistent or worsening respiratory distress, hypotension, or unequal breath sounds promptly. A stable appearance does not rule out a small pneumothorax, so complete ordered imaging and follow-up.","trap":"A tension pneumothorax is not simply a large air pocket; it behaves like a one-way valve, trapping air and compromising circulation. Waiting for an image in a deteriorating patient is like waiting for a gauge reading while a critical system is failing.","caseStory":"A mechanically ventilated adult suddenly becomes hypotensive and more difficult to oxygenate. Breath sounds are markedly reduced on one side, and percussion is hyperresonant there. A portable chest radiograph has been requested, but the patient is becoming less stable. What should the nurse prioritize?","caseReason":"The combination of sudden hypotension during positive-pressure ventilation and unilateral reduced breath sounds with hyperresonance strongly supports tension pneumothorax. The nurse should urgently alert the team and follow emergency protocols without waiting for radiographic confirmation; decompression is a team-directed emergency treatment, not an independent nursing procedure.","teachback":"What changes in a ventilated patient would make you escalate for possible tension pneumothorax before imaging, and what findings would you reassess after the team acts?","questions":[{"stem":"A mechanically ventilated adult develops abrupt hypotension, worsening respiratory status, and markedly reduced breath sounds with hyperresonance on one side. Imaging is not yet available. Which interpretation and nursing response are most appropriate?","options":["Suspect tension pneumothorax, alert the emergency team, and follow local protocols without waiting for imaging.","Suspect pleural effusion, document the unilateral findings, and wait for fluid analysis before escalating.","Suspect a small stable spontaneous pneumothorax, arrange routine follow-up imaging, and continue observation."],"feedback":["Sudden hypotension during positive-pressure ventilation with unilateral reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance is a concerning pattern for tension physiology. Escalation should not wait for a radiograph.","Pleural effusion is fluid in the pleural space and is evaluated with examination, imaging, and often fluid analysis. This option underreacts to the acute hypotension and pneumothorax cues.","Observation with follow-up may be appropriate for a stable, asymptomatic primary spontaneous pneumothorax. This patient is deteriorating and has findings that require emergency escalation."]},{"stem":"An adult with no known lung disease reports pleuritic chest pain, but has no respiratory or cardiac symptoms and remains physiologically stable. Imaging shows a primary spontaneous pneumothorax without progression on the initial follow-up film. Which nursing plan best supports the stated management approach?","options":["Coordinate continued observation and the ordered follow-up imaging, while watching for new symptoms or instability.","Prepare for immediate chest tube placement because any visible pneumothorax requires drainage.","Treat the finding as a tension emergency and request decompression before any further assessment."],"feedback":["The reference supports observation for a physiologically stable, asymptomatic primary spontaneous pneumothorax when follow-up imaging shows no progression. Nursing care includes monitoring and ensuring the ordered follow-up occurs.","The presence of a pneumothorax alone does not mean every primary spontaneous case requires a chest tube. The described stability and lack of respiratory or cardiac symptoms support observation.","Tension pneumothorax is an emergency diagnosed clinically when suspected, but the vignette gives no instability or tension features. This response would misclassify a stable case."]},{"stem":"Shortly after thoracentesis, an adult reports new pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea, with reduced breath sounds on the procedural side. The patient is currently stable. Which nursing interpretation and next step are best?","options":["Consider an iatrogenic pneumothorax, promptly notify the responsible clinician, and support ordered assessment and imaging.","Assume the symptoms reflect a transudative effusion, and wait for routine fluid chemistry before reporting them.","Diagnose tension pneumothorax from the symptoms alone and independently begin an invasive decompression procedure."],"feedback":["Thoracentesis is a recognized cause of iatrogenic pneumothorax, and new dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and reduced breath sounds warrant prompt team assessment. The patient is stable, so this calls for escalation and ordered evaluation rather than a nurse-made diagnosis.","Transudates are classified by pleural fluid laboratory characteristics, and this option dismisses new symptoms after a procedure that can cause pneumothorax. Report the change promptly.","The patient has no reported hypotension or other evidence of tension physiology, and nurses should not independently perform an invasive procedure. Escalate and follow local protocols."]},{"stem":"A supine patient being evaluated for possible pneumothorax has an initial chest radiograph that is difficult to interpret. The patient remains stable, but symptoms persist. Which nursing action best addresses the possibility of a misleading image?","options":["Communicate the persistent concern and ensure the team considers the deep sulcus sign and further ordered imaging.","Treat the absence of an obvious pleural line on the supine film as proof that pneumothorax is absent.","Assume that any peripheral lucency confirms pneumothorax and disregard the possibility of imaging mimics."],"feedback":["A pneumothorax may be difficult to see on a supine radiograph, and an abnormally deep costophrenic angle can be the only sign. Persistent concern should be communicated so the team can consider further evaluation.","Even large pneumothoraces may not be evident on supine films, so a nondiagnostic image does not reliably exclude the condition. Continued symptoms and clinical assessment matter.","Emphysematous bullae, skinfolds, and other artifacts can mimic pneumothorax on radiographs. Imaging should be interpreted with the clinical picture and appropriate additional assessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Pneumothorax - Pulmonology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pneumothorax"},{"title":"Pleural Effusion - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pleural-effusion"}],"frames":[{"label":"Sudden pleural air","detail":"Air entering the pleural space can collapse a lung; trapped pressure may impair venous return and rapidly cause cardiovascular collapse."},{"label":"Unilateral findings","detail":"Sudden hypotension during ventilation with one-sided reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance raises urgent concern for tension pneumothorax."},{"label":"Emergency escalation","detail":"Alert the emergency or critical-care team immediately and follow local protocols; do not wait for a radiograph or independently decompress."},{"label":"Reassess stability","detail":"After team-directed care, recheck oxygenation, effort, breath sounds, and blood pressure; promptly report persistent distress or hypotension."}]},{"id":"s023","unit":"resp","title":"Airway bleeding","objective":"Recognize potentially life-threatening airway bleeding and coordinate timely nursing actions that protect oxygenation while the team evaluates and controls the source.","concept":"Hemoptysis is blood coughed from below the vocal cords; bleeding from the nose or mouth and vomiting blood can look similar but have different sources. Most significant pulmonary bleeding comes from the higher-pressure bronchial circulation, although pulmonary vessels and other causes are possible. Blood and clots can obstruct airflow or spill into the unaffected lung, so the immediate clinical effect on breathing and circulation matters more than estimating volume alone.","recognize":"Ask whether blood followed coughing or vomiting, and look for nasal or oral bleeding. Bright blood in sputum and choking suggest a respiratory source; nausea and coffee-ground material suggest hematemesis. Assess distress, respiratory effort, oxygenation, breath sounds, and circulation. Fever, chronic lung disease, smoking, weight loss, anticoagulant use, or sudden chest pain and leg swelling help direct concern toward possible causes.","respond":"Treat respiratory distress, worsening oxygenation, or circulatory instability with significant bleeding as an emergency. Stay with the patient, activate the appropriate response, assess airway, breathing, and circulation, and communicate the onset, appearance, amount, trends, medications, and relevant history. Support oxygenation and positioning under orders or local protocol; if the bleeding side is known, the team may direct that side downward. Do not delay escalation to measure blood precisely.","reassess":"After nursing actions, repeatedly assess work of breathing, airway patency, oxygen saturation, mental status, breath sounds, bleeding, and vital-sign trends. Report increasing distress, reduced air entry, falling oxygenation, altered consciousness, or signs of poor perfusion immediately, even if a prior measurement appeared reassuring.","trap":"Do not treat a measured volume as the whole story: blood can threaten ventilation before a large amount is counted. Think of the airway as a narrow passage, where a clot can block flow like debris in a small pipe.","caseStory":"A patient with a history of bronchiectasis suddenly coughs bright red blood into sputum and becomes tachypneic and agitated. The oxygen saturation is falling, and the patient is struggling to speak. The nurse must decide whether to continue gathering details or escalate immediately.","caseReason":"The blood follows coughing and is mixed with sputum, supporting a respiratory source. More importantly, worsening breathing and oxygenation indicate immediate danger from airway obstruction or blood entering the other lung, so urgent escalation and airway, breathing, and circulation support take priority over estimating blood loss.","teachback":"When a patient coughs blood, what findings would make you escalate immediately, and how would you explain why the apparent volume alone is not enough to judge urgency?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with no known lung disease reports blood after an episode of coughing. The material is bright red and frothy, mixed with sputum, and the patient describes a choking sensation. Which interpretation best fits these cues?","options":["The pattern supports bleeding from the lower respiratory tract, with airway risk requiring prompt assessment.","The pattern most strongly supports hematemesis, so gastrointestinal symptoms should guide the initial response.","The pattern most strongly supports nasal bleeding, so a respiratory source is unlikely without visible nasal blood."],"feedback":["Coughing blood mixed with frothy sputum and choking are characteristic of true hemoptysis; assess airway and breathing promptly.","Nausea, vomiting, and dark or coffee-ground material are more suggestive of hematemesis than this presentation.","A nasopharyngeal source is more likely with postnasal drip or nasal bleeding without coughing; these cues point toward a respiratory source."]},{"stem":"A patient with a lung mass begins coughing substantial blood, becomes increasingly tachypneic, and is difficult to keep engaged in conversation. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Activate the emergency response and immediately assess airway, breathing, and circulation while supporting oxygenation under protocol.","Estimate the total blood loss over the next interval before deciding whether the change is urgent.","Complete a detailed history of smoking and weight loss before notifying the clinical team."],"feedback":["Distress and impaired interaction with significant bleeding suggest an immediate threat to ventilation or circulation; escalate and support ABCs without delay.","Exact volume is difficult to measure, and waiting to quantify bleeding can delay treatment when the patient is deteriorating.","Cancer history is relevant, but collecting more background information must not delay emergency assessment and escalation."]},{"stem":"A patient with a recently placed tracheostomy develops fresh blood at the tube and suddenly becomes restless. The oxygen saturation is not yet markedly changed. Which nursing response is most appropriate?","options":["Treat the bleeding and restlessness as an urgent airway warning, call for immediate team help, and assess breathing and circulation under local protocol.","Continue routine observation because a major oxygen saturation change has not yet occurred.","Assume the blood is minor irritation from the tube and document it for discussion at the next scheduled review."],"feedback":["Bleeding from an airway tube after recent tracheostomy is a red flag, and restlessness may signal worsening distress; escalate promptly rather than waiting for further decline.","A currently reassuring oxygen measurement does not make a concerning airway event safe to observe without escalation.","The timing and source make it unsafe to presume minor irritation; the team needs urgent notification and assessment."]},{"stem":"A patient with blood-streaked sputum reports abrupt sharp chest pain and new calf swelling. The patient is alert, and oxygen saturation is within the expected range. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's next communication?","options":["The normal oxygen reading does not exclude pulmonary embolism; promptly report the symptoms and relevant clotting risks for urgent evaluation.","The normal oxygen reading makes pulmonary embolism unlikely enough that routine follow-up is appropriate.","Blood-streaked sputum points to bronchitis, so the chest pain and calf swelling can be considered unrelated unless fever develops."],"feedback":["Abrupt chest pain and leg swelling are concerning for pulmonary embolism, and normal oxygen saturation does not exclude it; communicate the findings promptly.","A normal oxygen reading cannot rule out pulmonary embolism and should not be used to defer evaluation of these cues.","Bronchitis can cause blood-streaked sputum, but it does not account for the concerning combination of abrupt chest pain and calf swelling."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Pulmonary Hemorrhage","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-28032/"},{"title":"Hemoptysis - Pulmonology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/symptoms-of-pulmonary-disorders/hemoptysis"}],"frames":[{"label":"Blood source","detail":"Blood coughed from below the vocal cords differs from nasal or oral bleeding and blood vomited from the stomach."},{"label":"Airway threat cue","detail":"Bright blood mixed with sputum plus falling oxygenation, tachypnea, or difficulty speaking signals immediate respiratory danger."},{"label":"Activate response","detail":"Stay with the patient, activate the appropriate response, assess airway, breathing, and circulation, and communicate bleeding trends, medications, and history."},{"label":"Repeated airway checks","detail":"Reassess airway patency, effort, oxygenation, mentation, breath sounds, bleeding, and perfusion; escalate falling saturation or reduced air entry immediately."}]},{"id":"s024","unit":"resp","title":"Pulmonary hypertension","objective":"Interpret clinical cues of pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular strain to prioritize nursing assessment, communication, and reassessment.","concept":"Pulmonary hypertension means elevated pressure in the pulmonary circulation, arising from increased vascular resistance, pressure transmitted from the left heart, or increased pulmonary blood flow. Persistent pressure overload can enlarge and weaken the right ventricle, limiting output during exertion and causing systemic venous congestion. Hemodynamic assessment distinguishes pre-capillary, post-capillary, and combined patterns, helping direct evaluation toward lung or vascular disease versus left-heart causes.","recognize":"Progressive exertional dyspnea and fatigue are common; chest discomfort or exertional light-headedness can signal more severe disease. Right ventricular strain may appear as a parasternal lift or loud pulmonic component of S2, with jugular venous distention, liver congestion, or edema later. Lung auscultation can remain normal. Echocardiography estimates pressure; right heart catheterization confirms and characterizes hemodynamics.","respond":"Prioritize respiratory status, perfusion, symptoms of reduced output, and signs of venous congestion. Promptly communicate worsening dyspnea, presyncope, hypotension, or new right-heart-failure findings to the responsible clinician, using local escalation protocols. Support oxygenation and ordered treatment within nursing scope. Avoid assuming all pulmonary hypertension has the same cause or initiating pulmonary vasodilators independently.","reassess":"After an ordered intervention, reassess dyspnea, mentation, blood pressure, peripheral perfusion, jugular venous findings, and edema. Note whether symptoms and congestion are changing without signs of reduced preload or output. Escalate promptly for worsening presyncope, hypotension, cool extremities, increasing respiratory distress, or progressive congestion.","trap":"A normal lung examination does not rule out pulmonary hypertension; it is more like a quiet dashboard than proof that the engine is healthy. Also, right ventricular strain from lung disease is not interchangeable with pulmonary hypertension from left-heart disease, so treatment assumptions cannot be transferred automatically.","caseStory":"A patient with chronic obstructive lung disease reports worsening exertional breathlessness and fatigue, and now feels light-headed while walking. The nurse notes a loud pulmonic component of S2, jugular venous distention, and ankle edema, while lung auscultation is unchanged. The decision is whether to attribute the new findings only to lung disease or promptly report possible right ventricular strain.","caseReason":"Chronic lung disease can cause pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale, which overloads the right ventricle. The combination of exertional symptoms and systemic venous congestion warrants prompt communication and assessment; unchanged lung sounds do not exclude pulmonary hypertension or right-heart failure.","teachback":"When a patient with known lung disease develops exertional light-headedness and new venous congestion despite unchanged lung sounds, what findings would you report, and why would the lung examination not reassure you?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with chronic lung disease has progressive exertional dyspnea, a parasternal lift, a loud pulmonic component of S2, and new ankle edema. Lung auscultation is unchanged. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Pulmonary hypertension may be straining the right ventricle, with systemic venous congestion developing.","Unchanged lung sounds make pulmonary hypertension unlikely, so the edema is probably unrelated.","The findings establish left-heart disease as the cause and exclude pulmonary vascular or lung causes."],"feedback":["Chronic lung disease can contribute to pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular overload; edema is a possible later sign of right-heart failure.","Pulmonary auscultation is often normal in pulmonary hypertension, so unchanged lung sounds do not rule it out.","These findings suggest right ventricular strain but do not establish the cause; pulmonary hypertension has several possible origins."]},{"stem":"A patient being evaluated for pulmonary hypertension becomes increasingly breathless while walking and reports near-fainting. The nurse observes cool extremities and new jugular venous distention. What is the priority nursing response?","options":["Assess respiratory status and perfusion, then promptly communicate the deterioration through the local escalation process.","Encourage continued walking to determine whether the symptoms resolve as the patient becomes conditioned.","Independently begin a pulmonary vasodilator because the symptoms indicate elevated pulmonary pressure."],"feedback":["Worsening breathlessness, presyncope, cool extremities, and venous congestion raise concern for reduced output and right-heart strain, requiring prompt assessment and escalation.","Exertion may worsen symptoms when cardiac output is limited; continuing activity delays assessment of possible deterioration.","Pulmonary vasodilator therapy depends on the cause and clinical plan; nurses should not initiate it independently."]},{"stem":"A patient with known left ventricular diastolic dysfunction has elevated pulmonary pressures on echocardiography and increasing exertional fatigue. The care team is reviewing possible causes. Which nursing interpretation best supports that discussion?","options":["Left-heart filling pressure can be transmitted backward into the pulmonary circulation, so a post-capillary contribution is possible.","Elevated pulmonary pressure proves primary pulmonary arterial disease, so left-heart dysfunction is not relevant.","Exertional fatigue alone identifies chronic thromboembolic disease as the cause of the elevated pressure."],"feedback":["Left-heart disease can raise pulmonary venous pressure and contribute to post-capillary pulmonary hypertension; the cause requires clinical evaluation.","Pulmonary hypertension may result from left-heart disease, and echocardiographic pressure estimates do not by themselves establish primary pulmonary arterial disease.","Fatigue is a common but nonspecific symptom; it does not identify pulmonary artery obstruction as the cause."]},{"stem":"A patient with right-sided congestion receives an ordered diuretic and reports less ankle tightness later in the shift. Which reassessment finding should prompt the nurse to escalate concern about the response?","options":["New hypotension with worsening light-headedness and cool peripheral skin.","Persistent mild ankle edema without a change in respiratory symptoms or perfusion.","A normal lung examination with unchanged exertional breathlessness."],"feedback":["Diuresis can reduce preload; hypotension, worsening light-headedness, and cool skin suggest impaired perfusion and warrant prompt communication.","Persistent edema merits continued monitoring, but without deterioration it is less concerning than signs of reduced perfusion.","Normal lung sounds do not exclude pulmonary hypertension, but unchanged findings alone do not indicate the same immediate concern as worsening perfusion."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Pulmonary Hypertension - Pulmonology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-hypertension/pulmonary-hypertension"},{"title":"Right Heart Failure and Cor Pulmonale - Cardiology - MSD ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/right-heart-failure-and-cor-pulmonale"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pressure overload","detail":"Elevated pulmonary pressure can strain and weaken the right ventricle, limiting exertional output and causing systemic venous congestion."},{"label":"Possible right-heart strain","detail":"Exertional light-headedness with jugular venous distention and ankle edema suggests right-heart strain even when lung sounds remain unchanged."},{"label":"Report worsening","detail":"Promptly report worsening dyspnea, presyncope, hypotension, or new congestion; support oxygenation and ordered care within nursing scope."},{"label":"Trend perfusion","detail":"Recheck mentation, blood pressure, perfusion, dyspnea, and edema; escalate worsening presyncope, cool extremities, hypotension, or respiratory distress."}]},{"id":"s025","unit":"resp","title":"Thoracic injury and surgery","objective":"Recognize evolving respiratory or circulatory threats after thoracic injury and communicate the priority findings to the trauma team.","concept":"Thoracic trauma threatens breathing through lung injury or impaired chest-wall mechanics, and threatens circulation through bleeding, reduced venous return, or cardiac injury. A flail segment reflects multiple rib breaks that disconnect part of the chest wall; associated lung contusion may be a major cause of respiratory failure. Contusions can worsen after the initial assessment, so trends and the whole clinical picture matter more than one early image.","recognize":"Look for respiratory distress, increasing work of breathing, hypoxemia, chest pain, tenderness, crepitus, asymmetric movement, or a segment moving opposite the rest of the chest. Flail motion supports a flail chest, while hypotension with distress and markedly diminished breath sounds on one side raises concern for tension pneumothorax. A modest early radiograph does not exclude worsening lung contusion.","respond":"Use the trauma team and local emergency protocols to prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation findings. Promptly report new distress, hypotension, asymmetric breath sounds, paradoxical movement, or worsening oxygenation, and prepare for ordered evaluation or treatment. Do not delay escalation to obtain imaging when an immediately life-threatening injury is suspected, and do not independently perform invasive procedures or prescribe treatment.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess respiratory effort, oxygenation, chest movement, breath sounds, mental status, and hemodynamic trends, then communicate changes promptly. Escalate worsening distress or oxygenation, new hypotension, or unilateral loss of breath sounds, even if an earlier image or examination seemed reassuring.","trap":"A flail segment is a warning sign, but the visible chest-wall motion is not the whole problem; underlying lung contusion may drive respiratory failure. Think of the first chest image as an early snapshot, not a promise that the injury will remain stable.","caseStory":"After a high-energy collision, an adult has severe chest pain, shallow breathing, and a chest segment that moves inward during inspiration and outward during expiration. Oxygenation is worsening, although the first chest radiograph shows only limited lung changes. The nurse must decide whether to treat the finding as isolated rib pain or promptly escalate concern for a serious chest-wall and lung injury.","caseReason":"The paradoxical movement is consistent with a flail segment, and worsening oxygenation raises concern for impaired ventilation and associated pulmonary contusion. An early radiograph can underestimate lung injury, so the nurse should promptly communicate the respiratory trend and prepare for team-directed assessment and support rather than relying on the initial image.","teachback":"When a patient with chest trauma initially appears stable but later develops greater work of breathing, what findings would you reassess and communicate, and why might the first chest image not settle your concern?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult arrives after blunt chest trauma with severe pain, shallow respirations, and a chest-wall segment that moves inward on inspiration and outward on expiration. Oxygenation is declining, and the initial radiograph shows limited lung changes. Which interpretation best connects these findings?","options":["The motion indicates a flail segment, and associated lung contusion may be contributing to worsening respiratory compromise.","The limited radiograph makes a significant lung injury unlikely, so pain alone is the most useful explanation for the decline.","The opposite chest-wall motion primarily indicates a circulatory injury, so respiratory changes need not guide escalation."],"feedback":["Paradoxical movement is characteristic of a flail segment, and pulmonary contusion may cause respiratory deterioration even when early radiographic findings understate injury.","An early chest radiograph can underestimate pulmonary contusion, so worsening oxygenation should not be attributed to pain alone or dismissed.","Flail motion is a chest-wall and breathing-mechanics finding; it does not by itself establish a circulatory injury."]},{"stem":"A patient with thoracic trauma becomes hypotensive and acutely distressed. Breath sounds are markedly reduced on one side, and the trachea appears displaced away from that side. Which nursing priority is most appropriate?","options":["Immediately alert the trauma team to suspected tension pneumothorax and support urgent team-directed intervention without waiting for imaging.","Arrange a routine chest radiograph first because confirmation should precede escalation when the patient is still breathing.","Focus first on analgesia and reassess after the patient can take a deeper breath before reporting the respiratory findings."],"feedback":["Trauma with respiratory distress, hypotension, and unilateral diminished breath sounds is highly concerning for tension pneumothorax, which needs urgent attention.","The combination suggests an immediately life-threatening problem; waiting for imaging can delay needed intervention.","Analgesia may help chest-wall pain, but it does not address the urgent concern raised by hypotension and unilateral breath-sound loss."]},{"stem":"An adult with a penetrating chest wound has persistent pneumothorax after chest-tube placement, a substantial ongoing air leak, and difficulty ventilating. What concern should the nurse promptly communicate to the trauma team?","options":["A possible tracheobronchial injury warrants urgent further evaluation by the team.","An uncomplicated rib fracture is the likeliest explanation, so routine pain reassessment is the main next step.","A pulmonary contusion alone best explains the persistent air leak, so no additional injury evaluation is indicated."],"feedback":["Persistent pneumothorax, a large air leak, or difficulty ventilating after tube thoracostomy are cues for possible tracheobronchial injury and prompt further evaluation.","Rib fracture pain does not adequately explain the persistent pneumothorax, air leak, and difficulty ventilating.","A contusion may impair oxygenation, but it does not account for the specific combination of persistent pneumothorax and substantial air leak."]},{"stem":"An adult admitted after blunt chest trauma had a modest initial radiograph. Later, the patient develops increasing work of breathing and worsening oxygenation, while chest-wall motion is difficult to assess because the patient is mechanically ventilated. Which interpretation and response are best?","options":["Pulmonary contusion may be progressing despite the early image; reassess respiratory status and promptly report the deterioration.","The absence of visible paradoxical motion excludes flail chest during ventilation, so the oxygenation change is unlikely to reflect thoracic injury.","A modest initial image rules out clinically important lung injury, so continued observation without escalation is appropriate."],"feedback":["Pulmonary contusions can worsen over time, and early radiographs may underestimate injury; worsening respiratory status warrants reassessment and escalation.","Mechanical ventilation can alter the appearance of paradoxical motion, and its absence does not exclude a flail segment or explain away deterioration.","An initially modest radiograph does not rule out significant or evolving pulmonary contusion."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Thoracic Trauma","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534843/"},{"title":"Flail Chest - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/flail-chest"}],"frames":[{"label":"Blunt chest injury","detail":"Trauma can impair breathing through lung injury or chest-wall instability and threaten circulation through bleeding or reduced venous return."},{"label":"Paradoxical movement","detail":"A segment moving inward during inspiration with worsening oxygenation suggests flail chest and potentially evolving pulmonary contusion."},{"label":"Trauma escalation","detail":"Promptly report distress, paradoxical movement, hypoxemia, hypotension, or unequal breath sounds; follow trauma-team and local emergency protocols."},{"label":"Trend deterioration","detail":"Reassess chest movement, breath sounds, respiratory effort, oxygenation, mentation, and hemodynamics; promptly escalate worsening oxygenation or new hypotension."}]},{"id":"s026","unit":"resp","title":"Liberation from ventilation","objective":"Use a structured, team-based assessment to recognize readiness for a spontaneous breathing trial, distinguish it from readiness for extubation, and identify when to escalate concerns.","concept":"Liberation is guided by improvement of the problem that caused respiratory failure, not simply by reducing ventilator support in stages. A spontaneous breathing trial tests whether a patient can sustain breathing with little or no assistance. Passing that trial does not by itself establish that removing the endotracheal tube is safe: alertness, airway protection, cough, secretion burden, and airway patency also matter. Keeping these decisions distinct helps prevent avoidable extubation failure.","recognize":"Consider a trial when the respiratory problem has improved, the patient is not in shock, oxygenation is adequate on relatively low support, and breathing effort appears sustainable. During the trial, watch for rapid, shallow breathing, distress, or other clinical deterioration. A favorable breathing pattern or index can support judgment but cannot decide it alone. Separately assess alertness, cough, secretion management, and airway protection before extubation.","respond":"Review readiness daily with the bedside team and follow the unit protocol and clinician’s plan for a spontaneous breathing trial. Communicate changes in respiratory effort, oxygenation, hemodynamic stability, alertness, cough, and secretions; do not treat a passed trial as automatic clearance to remove the tube. Support prescribed sedation assessment and reduction protocols, and promptly escalate trial intolerance or concern about airway protection.","reassess":"After a trial or related nursing action, reassess respiratory effort, oxygenation, vital signs, alertness, cough, and secretion handling, and report trends to the team. Escalate promptly for worsening distress, unstable circulation, declining oxygenation, reduced alertness, or inability to manage secretions. After extubation, closely observe breathing and airway status.","trap":"A spontaneous breathing trial is like checking whether an engine runs without assistance; it does not prove that the vehicle is ready for every road condition. Extubation also depends on whether the patient can protect and maintain the airway, so a successful trial alone is not enough.","caseStory":"A patient recovering from pneumonia is awake, hemodynamically stable, and completes a spontaneous breathing trial without visible distress. The patient has a weak cough and needs frequent suctioning for secretions. The team is discussing extubation, and the nurse must decide what assessment and communication should guide that discussion.","caseReason":"The patient’s successful trial supports the ability to breathe with minimal support, but it does not establish the ability to protect the airway or clear secretions. The weak cough and frequent suctioning are important extubation concerns to report and reassess with the team before a decision is made.","teachback":"A patient passes a spontaneous breathing trial but has a weak cough and persistent secretions. What additional questions must the team answer before deciding whether extubation is appropriate?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with improving respiratory failure is stable and completes a spontaneous breathing trial with comfortable, sustained breathing. The patient is drowsy and does not cough effectively when prompted. Which interpretation should the nurse share with the team?","options":["The trial supports breathing readiness, but airway protection and cough still need assessment before extubation.","The trial establishes readiness for extubation because breathing was sustained without distress.","The patient should remain on full ventilator support because drowsiness always means the trial failed."],"feedback":["Correct. A successful trial addresses the ability to sustain breathing, while alertness, cough, and airway protection remain separate considerations for extubation.","This is misleading because trial success alone does not establish that the patient can protect the airway after tube removal.","Drowsiness warrants assessment of airway protection, but it does not automatically mean the breathing trial failed or require full support."]},{"stem":"A patient has improved from the illness causing respiratory failure and is being considered for a daily breathing trial. The nurse notes increasing respiratory effort and worsening oxygenation compared with earlier in the shift. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Report the change promptly and coordinate with the team before proceeding under the trial protocol.","Proceed with the trial because daily assessment means the patient should be tested regardless of current changes.","Recommend extubation to noninvasive ventilation because increased effort suggests the tube is no longer needed."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening effort and oxygenation are changes that should be communicated and reviewed before a trial proceeds under the local plan.","Daily assessment supports identifying readiness; it does not mean a trial should proceed despite concerning changes.","Noninvasive ventilation may be considered after extubation for selected high-risk patients, but these findings do not establish extubation readiness."]},{"stem":"An older patient with chronic cardiac disease completes a spontaneous breathing trial and has favorable blood gases. The patient is awake, has an effective cough, and manages secretions. Which plan is most consistent with the supplied guidance for this higher-risk context?","options":["Discuss extubation with the team and whether immediate noninvasive ventilation is appropriate for this higher-risk patient.","Continue invasive ventilation because older age and chronic cardiac disease rule out extubation after a successful trial.","Proceed with extubation without discussing post-extubation support because favorable blood gases settle the decision."],"feedback":["Correct. The patient has favorable trial and airway findings, and guidance supports considering initial noninvasive ventilation after extubation for selected patients at higher risk, including older adults with chronic cardiac disease.","These features increase concern for extubation failure but do not automatically rule out extubation after an otherwise favorable assessment.","Blood gases are one part of the decision; airway assessment and planning for support in a higher-risk patient also matter."]},{"stem":"A patient’s rapid shallow breathing index is below the commonly used threshold during assessment, but the patient becomes increasingly restless and breathes rapidly during the spontaneous breathing trial. Which interpretation is best?","options":["The index is only one supporting measure; the observed deterioration during the trial warrants communication and reassessment.","The index establishes trial success, so the observed rapid breathing can be disregarded unless blood gases worsen.","The index is irrelevant in every patient, so the nurse should base the decision only on the patient’s restlessness."],"feedback":["Correct. A single index is not perfectly predictive, and the patient’s clinical course during the trial should inform the team’s assessment.","A favorable index cannot override concerning clinical changes during the trial or establish success by itself.","The index can contribute to assessment, but it should be interpreted alongside breathing pattern, clinical tolerance, and other findings."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Liberation from Mechanical Ventilation - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/liberation-from-mechanical-ventilation?ruleredirectid=743ruleredirectid=450"},{"title":"Extubation - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK539804/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Improving Pneumonia","detail":"As pneumonia improves, an awake, stable patient completes a spontaneous breathing trial without visible distress."},{"label":"Airway Clearance Concern","detail":"Despite trial success, a weak cough and frequent suctioning signal difficulty clearing secretions and protecting the airway."},{"label":"Extubation Discussion","detail":"Report cough strength and secretion burden to the team; a passed trial alone does not clear extubation under the care plan."},{"label":"Post-Trial Monitoring","detail":"Reassess breathing, oxygenation, alertness, cough, and secretion handling; escalate worsening distress or inability to manage secretions."}]},{"id":"s027","unit":"neuro","title":"Neurologic assessment and brain injury","objective":"Interpret serial neurologic findings after brain injury and promptly communicate changes that may signal evolving intracranial injury.","concept":"Traumatic brain injury can cause immediate tissue damage and secondary injury as swelling or bleeding raises intracranial pressure. Because the skull cannot expand, increasing pressure can reduce cerebral perfusion and progress to ischemia or herniation. A single examination may not reveal this progression, so comparing level of consciousness, pupils, motor responses, and other neurologic findings over time helps nurses recognize deterioration and communicate its urgency.","recognize":"Look for changes from the patient's prior neurologic status, including worsening alertness, new confusion, focal weakness, seizures, or unequal or poorly reactive pupils. A period of apparent recovery after injury does not rule out an expanding epidural hematoma. Headache and vomiting are concerning in context but nonspecific; gradual confusion weeks after a seemingly minor injury can occur with chronic subdural hematoma, especially in older adults.","respond":"Prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation while promptly reporting neurologic deterioration to the responsible clinician or emergency response team under local protocol. Share the injury history, timing, serial findings, and relevant medications, and assist with ordered evaluation and imaging. Do not interpret a lucid interval or temporary improvement as reassurance, or delay escalation while waiting for a scheduled assessment or imaging result.","reassess":"After nursing actions, repeat and document the neurologic observations specified by the care plan, including consciousness, pupils, motor response, and vital signs, and compare them with prior findings. Escalate immediately for a declining level of consciousness, new focal deficit, worsening pupillary response, seizure, or other acute change.","trap":"A patient who becomes alert after a head injury is not necessarily out of danger. Think of an epidural hematoma as a leak that can keep filling a confined space: a brief quiet interval can precede rapid pressure and neurologic changes.","caseStory":"An adult is admitted after a head impact and initially becomes more conversational during observation. Later, the nurse finds the patient increasingly difficult to arouse, with a new one-sided weakness and a larger, poorly reactive pupil on the opposite side. The nurse must decide whether to continue routine observation or urgently communicate the change.","caseReason":"The interval of improved alertness does not exclude an evolving epidural hematoma, and subsequent declining consciousness with focal findings is a concerning change. The nurse should promptly escalate the serial findings and support urgent team evaluation rather than treating the earlier improvement as resolution.","teachback":"When a person with head trauma seems temporarily improved and then becomes harder to arouse, what changes would you compare with the prior examination, and how would you communicate the concern?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a temporal head injury was briefly unconscious, then conversed normally. During observation, the patient develops worsening alertness and weakness on one side. Which interpretation best fits this sequence?","options":["An evolving epidural hematoma is a concern despite the temporary period of improved alertness.","A concussion is established because the patient was briefly unconscious and then became alert.","A chronic subdural hematoma is most likely because symptoms followed a period without complaints."],"feedback":["A lucid interval followed by declining consciousness and focal weakness is a recognized pattern of epidural hematoma and warrants urgent communication.","Concussion can involve transient altered mental status, but the later neurologic deterioration and focal deficit require concern for a structural injury.","Chronic subdural hematoma more often causes gradual symptoms over weeks, rather than this rapid change after injury."]},{"stem":"An adult with known head trauma becomes less responsive than on the previous assessment. The nurse also notes a new pupillary asymmetry and reduced movement of one arm. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Promptly escalate the change to the responsible clinician or emergency response team and communicate the serial findings.","Continue the current observation schedule because pupillary asymmetry can be documented at the next planned check.","Wait for the patient to report a worsening headache before notifying the clinical team."],"feedback":["Declining consciousness with new pupillary and motor changes may indicate increasing intracranial pressure or herniation, so prompt escalation is appropriate.","A new neurologic change should not be deferred to a routine assessment interval.","Headache is not required for serious deterioration, and waiting for a symptom report could delay team evaluation."]},{"stem":"An older adult taking an anticoagulant is brought in for several weeks of increasing confusion and headache. The patient recalls a minor fall but initially seemed well. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication?","options":["A chronic subdural hematoma is a concern because symptoms can emerge gradually after relatively minor trauma in older adults.","An epidural hematoma is most likely because a lucid interval typically lasts several weeks before symptoms begin.","A concussion is most likely because delayed confusion is sufficient to establish a transient and reversible injury."],"feedback":["Chronic subdural hematoma can cause gradual headache and confusion over weeks, and it occurs more often in older adults and people taking anticoagulants.","An epidural hematoma may have a lucid interval, but the described weeks-long gradual course is more consistent with chronic subdural hematoma.","Concussion involves transient altered mental status; a progressive course over weeks needs evaluation for other injury, including chronic subdural hematoma."]},{"stem":"After a head injury, an adult has an initially reassuring examination and a CT report without acute hemorrhage. The patient later becomes more confused, although the vital signs have not changed. Which nursing response best addresses this potentially misleading information?","options":["Repeat and compare the neurologic assessment, then promptly report the new confusion despite the earlier CT and stable vital signs.","Treat the earlier CT and unchanged vital signs as evidence that intracranial injury is excluded.","Document the confusion as expected after head trauma and reassess only if a focal deficit appears."],"feedback":["A new change in mental status matters even after an initially reassuring CT; CT can be normal early in some injuries, and serial findings guide escalation.","An earlier CT and stable vital signs do not exclude an evolving neurologic problem or make a new mental-status change irrelevant.","Confusion can occur after head injury, but a change from baseline warrants reassessment and communication rather than waiting for weakness."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/traumatic-brain-injury-tbi/traumatic-brain-injury-tbi"},{"title":"Neuroimaging Update on Traumatic Brain Injury - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608606/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Interval Improvement","detail":"After head impact, apparent conversational improvement can precede evolving bleeding and rising pressure within the skull."},{"label":"Serial Neurologic Decline","detail":"Later reduced arousal, new one-sided weakness, and a poorly reactive enlarged pupil indicate urgent neurologic deterioration."},{"label":"Urgent Team Escalation","detail":"Promptly alert the responsible clinician or emergency team, sharing injury timing and serial findings under local protocol."},{"label":"Repeat Neurologic Checks","detail":"Repeat and compare consciousness, pupils, motor responses, and vital signs; immediately escalate further decline, focal deficit, or seizure."}]},{"id":"s028","unit":"neuro","title":"Stroke recognition","objective":"Recognize territory-linked signs of acute ischemic stroke and promptly communicate changes through the local stroke response.","concept":"An ischemic stroke occurs when a clot or other obstruction interrupts blood flow to a brain region. The severely deprived core may be irreversibly injured, while surrounding tissue can remain threatened but viable through collateral flow. This penumbra can shrink as injury evolves, so rapid recognition and coordinated evaluation matter. Deficits reflect the affected territory, and blood pressure changes can affect perfusion when autoregulation is impaired.","recognize":"Look for abrupt focal change, such as one-sided face or arm weakness, sensory loss, gaze preference, visual-field loss, language difficulty, or neglect. Middle cerebral artery involvement often emphasizes the face and arm; anterior cerebral artery involvement often emphasizes the opposite leg, with relative face and arm sparing. Dysarthria is not the same as aphasia and by itself localizes poorly.","respond":"Treat a sudden focal deficit as time-sensitive: promptly activate the local stroke response, stay with the patient, communicate when symptoms began or were first noticed if known, and support urgent clinician assessment and imaging under protocol. Report the pattern and any changes clearly. Do not dismiss mild or fluctuating signs, or independently attempt to normalize blood pressure.","reassess":"After nursing action, repeat and document focused neurologic findings, including face, limb strength and sensation, speech or language, gaze, vision, and attention, comparing them with the initial pattern. Escalate promptly for new, worsening, or fluctuating deficits, or findings that change the initial assessment.","trap":"A normal-looking leg does not rule out stroke: a middle cerebral artery event may chiefly affect the face and arm, while an anterior cerebral artery event more often affects the leg. Think of vascular territories as different circuits, not a single whole-body switch; slurred speech alone neither establishes aphasia nor precisely locates a lesion.","caseStory":"During rounds, a patient suddenly develops right facial and arm weakness, reduced sensation, and difficulty finding words; the right leg is less affected. The nurse must decide whether to activate the stroke response now or wait to see if the deficits resolve.","caseReason":"Sudden focal deficits fit acute stroke, and face-arm predominance with language difficulty is consistent with involvement of the language-dominant middle cerebral artery territory. The nurse should activate the local stroke response and report the observed onset and findings rather than wait, because threatened tissue may be lost as the ischemic penumbra shrinks.","teachback":"If a patient has sudden face and arm weakness with slurred speech but can understand and produce appropriate words, what would you assess and communicate to distinguish dysarthria from aphasia without delaying stroke response?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient suddenly develops right facial and arm weakness, reduced sensation, and difficulty understanding and producing language. The leg is less affected. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["A dominant-hemisphere middle cerebral artery pattern, with face and arm involvement and aphasia","An anterior cerebral artery pattern, with leg-predominant involvement and relative face sparing","A small-vessel lacunar pattern, with a classic subcortical syndrome and no cortical signs"],"feedback":["Correct. Face and arm predominance with aphasia is consistent with a middle cerebral artery infarct involving the language-dominant hemisphere.","This is less consistent because anterior cerebral artery infarction typically emphasizes contralateral leg deficits, with relative face and arm sparing.","This is less consistent because aphasia is a cortical sign, whereas the described small-vessel pattern lacks cortical dysfunction."]},{"stem":"A patient who was speaking and moving normally suddenly develops weakness and sensory loss mainly in the left leg, with the face and arm relatively spared. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Activate the local stroke response and promptly report the onset and leg-predominant findings","Continue serial observations first and alert the team only if weakness spreads to the face or arm","Delay escalation while completing a detailed history to determine which vascular territory is involved"],"feedback":["Correct. Sudden focal leg deficits can fit an anterior cerebral artery pattern, so prompt stroke response and communication are more important than waiting for additional signs.","This delays evaluation despite a sudden focal deficit; face or arm involvement is not required for a stroke pattern.","The nurse should communicate the urgent change promptly rather than wait to establish a precise vascular territory or complete the history."]},{"stem":"In a new patient, sudden left face and arm weakness occurs with a tendency to look toward the right and failure to attend to objects on the left. Which interpretation best connects these cues?","options":["A right, nondominant-hemisphere middle cerebral artery pattern with contralateral weakness and neglect","A left anterior cerebral artery pattern, since anterior cerebral artery infarcts chiefly affect the leg","A small-vessel lacunar pattern, since neglect is a typical sign without cortical involvement"],"feedback":["Correct. Right middle cerebral artery involvement can cause left face and arm deficits, gaze preference toward the lesion, and neglect associated with the nondominant cortex.","This is less consistent because anterior cerebral artery infarcts typically produce contralateral leg-predominant deficits rather than this face-arm and neglect pattern.","This is less consistent because neglect indicates cortical dysfunction, which is not expected in a classic lacunar syndrome."]},{"stem":"A patient has sudden arm weakness and slurred speech. The patient follows commands and names familiar objects correctly. Which nursing reassessment and interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Document dysarthria separately, reassess speech and language findings, and communicate the focal deficit through the stroke response","Classify the slurred speech as aphasia and use it alone to identify a dominant-hemisphere cortical lesion","Treat intact naming as evidence against stroke and continue routine observation before escalating"],"feedback":["Correct. Dysarthria can occur with cortical or subcortical infarcts and has limited localizing value; assess language separately and report the sudden focal change.","Slurred speech alone does not establish aphasia, and dysarthria by itself does not reliably localize the lesion.","Intact naming does not rule out stroke, especially when sudden focal weakness is present; routine observation should not delay escalation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Treatment / Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499997/"},{"title":"Ischemic Stroke - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK499997/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Threatened Brain Tissue","detail":"An abrupt interruption of cerebral blood flow threatens viable surrounding tissue as the ischemic penumbra can shrink over time."},{"label":"Sudden Focal Deficits","detail":"Sudden right face-arm weakness, sensory loss, and word-finding difficulty with less leg involvement suggest focal stroke."},{"label":"Activate Stroke Response","detail":"Activate the local stroke response promptly; communicate when symptoms began or were first noticed and support urgent assessment and imaging."},{"label":"Repeat Focused Findings","detail":"Repeat focused face, limb, speech, gaze, vision, and attention findings; escalate new, worsening, or fluctuating deficits promptly."}]},{"id":"s029","unit":"neuro","title":"Seizures and status epilepticus","objective":"Use bedside cues to recognize seizure activity and possible status epilepticus, prioritize immediate supportive nursing actions, and reassess for ongoing or recurrent seizure activity.","concept":"A seizure is abnormal electrical activity in the brain that temporarily disrupts function. Excessive excitation or inadequate inhibition can sustain or repeat that activity. Status epilepticus includes prolonged clinical or electrographic seizure activity, or recurrent seizures without recovery between them. It may be convulsive or nonconvulsive, so persistent impaired awareness can signal ongoing seizure activity even when dramatic movements are absent.","recognize":"Generalized convulsive activity with impaired awareness is an obvious emergency; repeated seizures without return to baseline are also concerning. Nonconvulsive activity may instead appear as persistent altered mental status, sometimes with subtle signs such as eye deviation, and requires EEG for diagnosis. Confusion or temporary weakness can follow a seizure, so assess the course rather than assuming either recovery or ongoing seizure.","respond":"Activate the emergency response and notify the appropriate clinician while supporting airway, breathing, and circulation. Position to help keep the airway open, provide oxygen and monitoring as directed, obtain vascular access through the appropriate team, and check bedside glucose. Follow local protocols and orders for medications and investigations. Do not delay immediate stabilization to pursue nonurgent imaging or laboratory work during active seizure activity.","reassess":"Track whether movements stop, awareness returns, breathing and oxygenation remain adequate, and vital signs are stable. Recheck glucose and observe for recurrent activity or subtle signs. Persistent impaired awareness or repeated seizures without recovery warrants prompt escalation and clinician evaluation for EEG monitoring.","trap":"Do not equate the end of visible convulsions with the end of a seizure; electrical activity can continue without obvious movements. Think of the EEG as a window into activity that bedside observation alone may not reveal.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult has a witnessed convulsive seizure that stops, but remains unresponsive and intermittently turns the eyes to one side. The nurse notes stable oxygen saturation and no return to the person's usual awareness. The decision is whether to treat this as routine recovery or escalate concern for ongoing seizure activity.","caseReason":"Persistent impaired awareness after visible movements stop can reflect a postictal state, but subtle eye deviation and lack of recovery also raise concern for nonconvulsive seizure activity. The nurse should promptly communicate these findings and request clinician evaluation, including consideration of EEG, while continuing supportive monitoring.","teachback":"After a seizure stops, what bedside findings would lead you to escalate concern for ongoing seizure activity rather than assume the person is simply recovering?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in the hospital has repeated brief episodes of unresponsiveness without regaining usual awareness between them. There are no generalized convulsions, but intermittent eye deviation is observed. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next communication?","options":["Ongoing nonconvulsive seizure activity is possible, so report the pattern promptly and seek clinician evaluation for EEG monitoring.","The episodes are most consistent with a postictal state, so document them and wait for awareness to return before notifying the team.","The absence of tonic-clonic movements makes a seizure unlikely, so focus on routine delirium screening before escalating."],"feedback":["Repeated episodes without recovery and subtle eye deviation can occur with nonconvulsive status, which requires EEG for diagnosis. Promptly communicate the findings and continue supportive observation.","A postictal state can include confusion, but repeated episodes without return to baseline and subtle motor signs should not be presumed to be routine recovery.","Nonconvulsive status may present with impaired mental status and little or no overt convulsive movement, so absence of generalized convulsions does not rule it out."]},{"stem":"A patient has ongoing generalized convulsive movements and impaired awareness. A colleague proposes obtaining a full set of laboratory samples before addressing the patient's breathing. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Support airway, breathing, and circulation, provide oxygen and monitoring as directed, and activate the emergency response while communicating the need for seizure treatment.","Complete the laboratory collection first so the team can identify the cause before initiating supportive care.","Wait for the movements to stop spontaneously, then assess airway and breathing to avoid interrupting the event."],"feedback":["Immediate supportive care and rapid team response take priority during active convulsive status; evaluation for causes follows stabilization and should not delay it.","Identifying the cause is important, but collecting a broad workup should not delay airway, breathing, and circulation support during active seizure activity.","Ongoing convulsive activity is an emergency; delaying supportive assessment until it stops risks missing respiratory or cardiovascular compromise."]},{"stem":"A pregnant adult develops a seizure and is confused afterward. The nurse also notes a markedly elevated blood pressure and communicates the concern to the obstetric and emergency teams. Which additional point is most relevant to raise?","options":["If eclampsia is suspected, magnesium sulfate is indicated, and the team should address seizure control urgently.","Because confusion can follow a seizure, magnesium sulfate should be considered only if generalized movements recur.","The nurse should prioritize obtaining brain imaging before raising eclampsia as a possible cause."],"feedback":["The supplied reference identifies intravenous magnesium sulfate as the antiepileptic drug of choice when eclampsia is suspected. The nurse should promptly communicate the concern and support timely team management.","Postictal confusion can occur, but it does not remove the urgency of evaluating suspected eclampsia or the indicated role of magnesium sulfate.","Neuroimaging may be part of evaluation, but suspected eclampsia requires urgent team attention; imaging should not displace immediate stabilization and communication."]},{"stem":"After a convulsive seizure ends, an adult is sleepy and confused and has temporary weakness in one arm. The nurse observes no recurrent movements, but awareness remains impaired. Which reassessment approach is best?","options":["Continue close assessment of awareness, breathing, vital signs, and recurrence; promptly report persistent impairment because postictal confusion or weakness does not exclude ongoing seizure activity.","Document the weakness as Todd paralysis and end frequent reassessment because temporary focal deficits are expected after seizures.","Treat the confusion as proof of nonconvulsive status and report that diagnosis without further bedside observation."],"feedback":["Postictal confusion and temporary focal deficits can occur, but ongoing impaired awareness also warrants concern and possible EEG evaluation. Serial assessment and timely communication help distinguish recovery from persistent seizure activity.","Todd paralysis may be a temporary postictal deficit, but assuming that explanation without reassessment could miss ongoing activity or another urgent problem.","Impaired awareness can suggest nonconvulsive status, but it does not establish that diagnosis; EEG monitoring is needed, alongside continued clinical assessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Status Epilepticus - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430686/"},{"title":"Seizure Disorders - Neurology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/seizure-disorders/seizure-disorders?ruleredirectid=743"}],"frames":[{"label":"Seizure Ends","detail":"Convulsive activity stops, but the hospitalized adult remains unresponsive with intermittent eye deviation and no return to usual awareness."},{"label":"Persistent Impaired Awareness","detail":"Persistent impaired awareness after movements stop, especially with eye deviation, may signal subtle ongoing seizure activity."},{"label":"Emergency Support","detail":"Notify the clinician and activate emergency response; support airway, breathing, circulation, monitoring, and glucose checks under local protocol."},{"label":"Monitor Recovery","detail":"Track awareness, breathing, oxygenation, and recurrent activity; persistent unresponsiveness warrants prompt escalation and clinician evaluation for EEG."}]},{"id":"s030","unit":"neuro","title":"Spinal cord injury and shock","objective":"Interpret key spinal cord injury and shock cues to prioritize nursing actions, team communication, and reassessment.","concept":"Spinal cord injury disrupts motor, sensory, and autonomic pathways; deficits depend on lesion level and extent. Primary mechanical damage can be followed by secondary swelling, ischemia, and inflammation. Injury above the mid-thoracic level may interrupt sympathetic tone, causing neurogenic shock with hypotension and bradycardia. Spinal shock is different: it describes temporary loss of reflexes and flaccid function after injury, not circulatory shock.","recognize":"Suspect injury after trauma or with progressive weakness, sensory change, or bowel or bladder dysfunction. A distinct neurologic level supports cord injury; cervical injury may impair breathing. Hypotension with bradycardia and warm, dry skin suggests neurogenic shock, but bleeding and other causes can coexist. Early flaccidity or absent reflexes may reflect spinal shock and does not alone establish a complete lesion.","respond":"Begin with airway, breathing, and circulation; support breathing and protect spinal alignment using local trauma protocols. Promptly communicate the mechanism, neurologic changes, vital-sign pattern, and respiratory concerns to the responsible team. Assist with ordered assessment and imaging, and anticipate specialty consultation. Do not delay stabilization for a detailed neurologic examination or assume hypotension is solely neurogenic.","reassess":"After action, reassess breathing, cough effectiveness, oxygenation, vital signs, perfusion, and motor and sensory findings. Compare serial observations with the initial examination and report changes clearly. Escalate promptly for worsening weakness or sensation, new breathing difficulty, persistent instability, or evolving bowel or bladder dysfunction.","trap":"Spinal shock is not the same as neurogenic shock: one is an early neurologic quieting, while the other is circulatory instability from lost sympathetic tone. Think of a quiet instrument panel versus a failing pressure-control system; one does not prove the other.","caseStory":"After a fall, an older adult has neck pain, weaker hand grips than leg movement, and reduced sensation below the shoulders. The patient is hypotensive and bradycardic with warm, dry skin, while assessment for other injuries is still underway. What should the nurse recognize and prioritize?","caseReason":"The disproportionate arm weakness after a neck injury suggests an incomplete cervical cord pattern, while hypotension with bradycardia and warm, dry skin is consistent with neurogenic shock. Both findings warrant prompt team communication and stabilization, while evaluation for concurrent bleeding continues. A detailed examination should not delay airway, breathing, and circulation priorities.","teachback":"In a new patient with suspected spinal cord injury, what findings would make you consider neurogenic shock, and how would you distinguish that concern from spinal shock?","questions":[{"stem":"Following a cervical injury, a patient has reduced movement and sensation below the neck. The patient is hypotensive and bradycardic with warm, dry skin. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pattern raises concern for neurogenic shock from disrupted sympathetic tone, while other shock causes still require assessment.","The pattern establishes spinal shock as the cause of the blood pressure and heart rate changes.","The pattern indicates hemorrhage is excluded because the skin is warm and dry."],"feedback":["A cervical cord injury can disrupt sympathetic tone and produce hypotension with bradycardia; concurrent causes such as bleeding still need consideration.","Spinal shock describes temporary neurologic flaccidity and loss of reflexes, not this circulatory pattern.","Warm, dry skin can accompany neurogenic shock, but it does not rule out concurrent hemorrhage."]},{"stem":"A patient in a vehicle collision has neck tenderness, new limb weakness, and labored breathing. The patient is not yet fully assessed for other injuries. Which nursing action should take priority?","options":["Address airway and breathing while maintaining spinal alignment and promptly activating the trauma response.","Complete a detailed sensory examination before intervening so the neurologic level is precisely documented.","Move the patient into a more comfortable position before notifying the team about the breathing change."],"feedback":["The primary survey prioritizes airway and breathing, while spinal alignment is protected and the team is alerted to an urgent change.","A detailed neurologic examination is useful when feasible, but should not delay immediate stabilization.","Unnecessary movement can worsen an unstable injury, and the breathing change needs prompt escalation."]},{"stem":"A patient with a history of cancer reports worsening back pain, progressive leg weakness, and new difficulty controlling urination, with no recent trauma. Which interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["Consider nontraumatic cord compression and promptly report the progressive neurologic and bladder changes to the responsible team.","Treat the symptoms as an expected effect of chronic back pain and arrange routine follow-up.","Exclude spinal cord injury because there was no traumatic event and continue monitoring without escalation."],"feedback":["Tumors can compress or damage the cord, and progressive weakness with bladder changes warrants prompt clinical escalation.","Progressive neurologic and bladder symptoms are not adequately explained as routine chronic pain findings.","Spinal cord injury can have nontraumatic causes, including malignancy, so absence of trauma does not exclude it."]},{"stem":"Immediately after a spinal injury, a patient has flaccid leg weakness and absent reflexes. Which reassessment interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Early spinal shock can cause flaccidity and absent reflexes, so continue serial neurologic assessment rather than infer completeness from this finding alone.","Absent reflexes prove that the injury is complete, so additional neurologic assessment will not add useful information.","Flaccidity indicates neurogenic shock, so reassessment should focus only on blood pressure and heart rate."],"feedback":["Spinal shock may temporarily produce broad neurologic dysfunction; serial examination helps characterize the injury as the patient is assessed.","Absent reflexes early after injury do not alone establish a complete lesion.","Flaccidity is a neurologic finding; neurogenic shock is a circulatory condition associated with hypotension and bradycardia."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Spinal Cord Injuries - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560721/"},{"title":"Spinal Trauma - Injuries; Poisoning","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/spinal-trauma/spinal-trauma"}],"frames":[{"label":"Fall With Neck Pain","detail":"After a fall, neck pain and disproportionate hand weakness with sensory loss suggest cervical cord injury."},{"label":"Neurogenic Shock Pattern","detail":"Hypotension, bradycardia, and warm, dry skin are consistent with neurogenic shock, though bleeding may coexist."},{"label":"Stabilize And Report","detail":"Prioritize airway, breathing, circulation, and spinal alignment under trauma protocol; promptly report neurologic findings and vital-sign pattern."},{"label":"Reassess Perfusion","detail":"Reassess breathing, perfusion, vital signs, and motor-sensory findings; escalate worsening weakness, respiratory difficulty, or persistent instability."}]},{"id":"s031","unit":"neuro","title":"Hydrocephalus and ventricular drains","objective":"Use patient findings and EVD system checks to recognize possible changes in cerebrospinal fluid drainage or intracranial pressure and respond safely within nursing practice.","concept":"Hydrocephalus can occur when cerebrospinal fluid production and absorption are no longer balanced, including when blood or another obstruction disrupts flow. An external ventricular drain can divert fluid and measure intracranial pressure. Drainage depends on the prescribed collection-system height, so its position relative to the patient’s head matters. Repositioning can alter both drainage and pressure readings, making accurate leveling, clinical assessment, and communication essential.","recognize":"Interpret neurological changes alongside the drain’s output, appearance, waveform, and setup. Reduced or absent flow with a dampened waveform or absent meniscus movement can suggest obstruction, while a kink or other system problem may impair flow. Cloudy fluid, a leak, or bright red bloody fluid raises concern for complications. These cues need clinical context and are not diagnoses on their own.","respond":"First assess the patient and check the system’s visible integrity and level, using the prescribed setup and local protocol. Relevel after position changes and promptly report neurological deterioration, unexpected drainage changes, concerning fluid appearance, or suspected obstruction to the appropriate clinician. Do not independently change the prescribed height, irrigate, or access the system outside your role and orders. Keep the drain clamped during transport as directed by practice guidance.","reassess":"After repositioning or another permitted adjustment, reassess neurological status, the displayed pressure and waveform, drainage, and system level. Confirm that the reading is obtained with the system appropriately leveled. Escalate promptly for worsening neurological findings, persistent loss of flow with concerning waveform changes, a leak, or suspicious fluid.","trap":"A low pressure reading is not automatically reassuring if the transducer is too high; it is like reading a tilted scale and trusting the number. Also, no drainage alone does not prove obstruction, because flow may stop when pressure no longer exceeds the collection-system setting.","caseStory":"A patient with an EVD becomes less alert after being repositioned for care. The displayed pressure is lower than before, the drain has produced little fluid, and the transducer is visibly above the level of the patient’s ear. The nurse must decide whether the number is reassuring or whether to check the setup and escalate.","caseReason":"The transducer’s position may produce a falsely low pressure reading and insufficient drainage, so the displayed value does not resolve the concern. The nurse should assess the patient, restore accurate leveling according to local protocol, and promptly communicate the neurological change and drain findings to the care team.","teachback":"After a patient with an EVD is repositioned, what patient and system findings would you assess before deciding whether a change in drainage or pressure is reassuring?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with an EVD becomes harder to arouse after a bed turn. The displayed pressure is lower and drainage has decreased, while the transducer is now above the patient’s ear. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these findings?","options":["The reading may be falsely low because the system is not level; assess the patient, relevel according to protocol, and notify the care team.","The lower reading confirms that intracranial pressure has improved; continue observation without changing the system position.","The reduced drainage establishes catheter obstruction; irrigate the catheter before contacting the care team."],"feedback":["Correct. A transducer above the appropriate reference level can produce a falsely low reading and insufficient drainage, especially after a position change.","This treats a potentially inaccurate reading as proof of improvement despite the patient’s worsening alertness and the visible leveling problem.","Reduced flow can accompany obstruction, but it does not establish the cause, and irrigation is not an independent nursing response."]},{"stem":"A patient with an EVD develops a worsening headache and reduced alertness. Drainage has stopped, the waveform is dampened, and the visible tubing has no obvious kink. What is the safest priority response?","options":["Promptly assess the patient and system, preserve the prescribed setup, and urgently notify the responsible clinician about the concerning changes.","Lower the collection system until fluid appears, then leave it at the new height if the headache improves.","Collect a fluid sample from the drainage bag to check whether infection explains the change."],"feedback":["Correct. Neurological deterioration combined with reduced flow and a dampened waveform warrants prompt assessment and escalation; the nurse should not independently alter the prescribed height.","Changing the collection height can alter drainage and pressure measurement without an order or protocol-based direction.","A sample from the collection bag is not an appropriate way to investigate this change, and it delays response to neurological deterioration."]},{"stem":"A patient with an EVD is being taken from the intensive care unit to imaging. Which nursing action best supports safe management during this transfer and after arrival?","options":["Clamp the drain during transport as directed by practice guidance, then ensure it is appropriately releveled for the patient’s position before drainage resumes.","Leave the drain open at its original height throughout transport, then use the displayed pressure to decide whether it needs adjustment.","Raise the collection chamber during transport to prevent drainage, then keep that height after arrival to avoid excess output."],"feedback":["Correct. Practice guidance describes clamping during transport, and position changes require the system to be releveled to maintain accurate measurement and drainage.","An unchanged height may no longer be accurate after movement, and leaving the drain open during transport does not follow the cited transport guidance.","Independently changing the prescribed height can alter drainage and pressure interpretation; it is not a substitute for correct leveling."]},{"stem":"After a patient is repositioned, an EVD is correctly leveled and shows a pulsatile waveform, but no fluid is currently draining. The patient’s neurological assessment is unchanged and the system appears intact. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["No drainage by itself may be expected when pressure is not above the collection-system setting; continue assessment and monitoring for changes.","The lack of drainage proves obstruction even though the waveform and patient assessment are unchanged; request immediate irrigation.","The pulsatile waveform proves there is no possible drain problem; stop checking output and neurological status."],"feedback":["Correct. Drainage can cease when pressure equalizes with the collection system, so absent output alone does not establish obstruction; continued patient and system assessment remains important.","Obstruction may be associated with reduced flow and a dampened waveform, but absent flow alone is not proof and does not justify independent irrigation.","A pulsatile waveform is useful information but does not eliminate the need to monitor the patient, drainage, and system for evolving problems."]}],"sources":[{"title":"External ventricular drains: Management and complications","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4450504"},{"title":"External Ventricular Drains: Development and Evaluation of a Nursing Clinical Practice Guideline","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9782641/pdf/nursrep-12-00090.pdf"}],"frames":[{"label":"Repositioned EVD","detail":"After repositioning, an EVD transducer above the prescribed head reference can distort pressure readings and affect drainage."},{"label":"Pressure-flow mismatch","detail":"Reduced output and a dampened waveform may suggest impaired flow, while neurological decline makes a low displayed pressure concerning."},{"label":"Level and escalate","detail":"Assess the patient, check visible system integrity and level, then promptly report neurological change and drain findings under local protocol."},{"label":"Post-adjustment check","detail":"After permitted releveling, reassess neurologic status, pressure, waveform, drainage, and level; escalate persistent loss of flow or worsening findings."}]},{"id":"s032","unit":"neuro","title":"Brain death and confounders","objective":"By the end of this unit, you will be able to recognize major confounders of brain death assessment and communicate findings that support a safe clinician-led evaluation.","concept":"Brain death, also called death by neurologic criteria, is permanent loss of function of the brain as a whole, including the brainstem, even when ventilation and medications maintain circulation and other body activity. Diagnosis depends on a catastrophic permanent brain injury, exclusion of reversible causes and drug effects, coma, absent brainstem reflexes, and no respiratory drive during a carefully supervised apnea test. These safeguards matter because confounders can mimic neurologic failure and assessment is a formal clinician-led determination.","recognize":"Look for a documented catastrophic brain insult with coma and absent pupil, corneal, gag or cough, and vestibular responses, alongside no spontaneous breathing during formal evaluation. Before interpreting these findings, check the record and bedside context for sedatives or other depressants, paralytics, intoxication, metabolic or acid-base disturbance, low temperature, or hypotension. Arousal, breathing, or intact brainstem reflexes argues against proceeding.","respond":"Treat this as a clinician-led determination, not a nursing diagnosis. Promptly communicate the injury history, medication and substance exposure, temperature, hemodynamic status, metabolic concerns, observed responses, and any spontaneous breaths to the responsible team. Follow local protocols and orders to support stabilization and documentation; do not initiate apnea testing, stop ventilation, or interpret isolated movements as proof of brain death.","reassess":"After reporting a possible confounder, verify that the team has acknowledged it and reassess the documented temperature, blood pressure, medication history, metabolic results, oxygenation, and observed neurologic responses as directed. Escalate promptly for spontaneous breathing, arousal, instability, or a newly identified drug or metabolic confounder; these require clinical review before conclusions.","trap":"An absent response is not a verdict if a confounder can suppress it; it is like a muted alarm, not proof that the system has failed. Likewise, a limb movement can arise from spinal reflexes, so its appearance alone neither confirms nor rules out brain death.","caseStory":"An adult with a catastrophic intracranial injury is comatose and has no observed pupillary or corneal responses during a planned neurologic evaluation. The chart shows recent sedative exposure, and the patient is hypothermic; a nurse notices brief leg flexion during care. What should the nurse communicate and prioritize before interpreting these findings?","caseReason":"Recent CNS depressants and hypothermia can confound the examination, so the findings cannot be treated as a completed determination until clinicians address them under applicable guidance. Report the timing of sedative exposure, temperature, and movement; the leg flexion may be spinal, but only the qualified team interprets it in the full assessment.","teachback":"If a patient has absent brainstem responses but possible sedative exposure, what information would you report, and why should the examination not be interpreted in isolation?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a documented catastrophic brain injury is comatose and has absent pupil and corneal responses. The medication record shows a recent CNS depressant, while the team discusses whether the examination is sufficient. Which interpretation should the bedside nurse communicate?","options":["Drug effect remains a confounder to resolve before these findings support a determination.","Absent pupil and corneal responses establish brain death despite the medication history.","An EEG should replace review of the medication exposure before the team proceeds."],"feedback":["Correct. CNS depressant effects must be excluded because they can confound neurologic findings.","This is misleading because absent responses alone do not establish brain death when a potential drug confounder remains.","An EEG may provide additional evidence in selected circumstances, but it does not remove the need to address a possible medication confounder."]},{"stem":"An adult with catastrophic brain injury is being considered for evaluation, but hypothermia and hypotension have not been corrected. No spontaneous breathing is observed. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Notify the responsible clinician and support correction of the confounding conditions under orders or protocol before testing proceeds.","Continue directly to apnea testing because absent spontaneous breathing resolves the effects of low temperature and pressure.","Request a brain perfusion study immediately because it removes the need to stabilize the patient first."],"feedback":["Correct. Hypothermia and hypotension must be addressed as potential confounders before the assessment proceeds.","This is unsafe reasoning because absent breathing does not resolve the effects of hypothermia or hypotension, and apnea testing requires careful clinical oversight.","Ancillary testing may be used in selected situations, but it does not make unresolved confounders or stabilization needs irrelevant."]},{"stem":"After a catastrophic injury, an adult remains comatose with absent assessable brainstem responses, but severe facial trauma prevents caloric testing. Which nursing communication best supports a safe evaluation?","options":["Tell the responsible clinician which component cannot be assessed; the clinician may use an accepted ancillary study when the examination is prevented.","Record the untestable response as absent because the injury explains why it cannot be elicited.","Ask the family to decide whether the missing examination can be waived."],"feedback":["Correct. When an injury prevents part of the examination, the clinician can consider an accepted ancillary study alongside the assessable findings.","This is misleading because an untestable response is not the same as a demonstrated absent response.","The family should be informed about the process and may be offered the opportunity to witness it, but the clinical determination is made by qualified clinicians."]},{"stem":"During a clinician-led evaluation of an adult with catastrophic brain injury, the patient has no arousal or brainstem reflexes, but the nurse sees a brief stereotyped limb movement. What is the best nursing interpretation and action?","options":["Describe and report the movement; it may be spinal in origin and must be interpreted within the full clinician-led assessment.","Treat any limb movement as proof of preserved integrated brain function and stop all evaluation permanently.","Treat the movement as confirmed spinal activity and omit it from documentation."],"feedback":["Correct. Some movements can be spinal reflexes, but the nurse should report what was observed for interpretation in the complete assessment.","This overinterprets the movement because spinal reflexes can occur despite brain death.","This is misleading because the nurse should not independently determine that a movement is spinal or omit it from the clinical record."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Consensus guideline for death by neurologic criteria","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10791061/"},{"title":"Brain Death - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/coma-and-impaired-consciousness/brain-death"},{"title":"Table: Guidelines for Determining Brain Death/Death by Neurologic ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/multimedia/table/guidelines-for-determining-brain-deathdeath-by-neurologic-criteria-in-patients-37-weeks-old"}],"frames":[{"label":"Neurologic evaluation","detail":"A catastrophic brain injury may prompt formal evaluation, but diagnosis requires clinicians to exclude reversible causes and confounders."},{"label":"Confounding findings","detail":"Recent sedative exposure and hypothermia can confound absent reflexes; report these alongside observed leg flexion before conclusions."},{"label":"Communicate context","detail":"Promptly tell the responsible team about medication timing, temperature, observed movement, and any spontaneous breathing, following local protocols."},{"label":"Confirm team review","detail":"Verify confounders have been acknowledged; escalate spontaneous breathing, arousal, instability, or newly identified drug and metabolic concerns for clinical review."}]},{"id":"s033","unit":"neuro","title":"Encephalopathy","objective":"Recognize possible hepatic encephalopathy, identify competing causes and triggers, and communicate and reassess changes in a safe, coordinated manner.","concept":"Hepatic encephalopathy is impaired brain function associated with liver failure or blood bypassing the liver, and it can range from subtle cognitive changes to coma. Ammonia is an important contributor, but inflammation, altered neurotransmission, and other metabolic changes also matter. Because the syndrome has multiple causes and resembles other conditions, nursing assessment must connect the mental status change with the clinical context rather than rely on one finding.","recognize":"Look for a change from the person’s usual cognition or behavior, altered sleep pattern, disorientation, somnolence, or asterixis in someone with liver disease or a portosystemic shunt. Asterixis supports but does not establish the diagnosis. Consider competing explanations such as infection, bleeding, constipation, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, renal dysfunction, hypoxia, or sedating substances; altered mental status requires evaluation for other causes.","respond":"Prioritize immediate safety and prompt team notification for new or worsening confusion, reduced responsiveness, or concern about airway protection. Share the mental status trend, relevant liver or shunt history, and possible triggers. Support assessment and correction of precipitating factors under orders and local protocols, and give prescribed therapy with monitoring. Do not treat an ammonia result alone as proof of HE or independently change medication or diet.","reassess":"After actions or prescribed treatment, reassess alertness, orientation, behavior, motor findings, and the direction of change from baseline. Monitor bowel pattern, hydration, and relevant laboratory results as directed, since diarrhea and electrolyte disturbance can worsen the picture. Escalate promptly for declining responsiveness, new neurologic findings, or failure to improve.","trap":"An elevated ammonia result is a clue, not a diagnostic stamp; it is more like one instrument on a dashboard than the whole story. Likewise, absent asterixis does not rule out advanced HE, and asterixis can occur in conditions such as uremia.","caseStory":"A patient with cirrhosis becomes disoriented and unusually sleepy after a day of poor intake and constipation. The patient has no reported sedative exposure, but an infection or metabolic problem has not yet been excluded. The nurse must decide whether to assume HE or promptly communicate the change and support a broader assessment.","caseReason":"The liver disease, altered cognition, and possible constipation make HE plausible, but none confirms it. The nurse should report the change and context, support evaluation for precipitating and alternative causes, and maintain safety rather than treating a presumed diagnosis in isolation.","teachback":"When a patient with liver disease becomes confused, which changes and possible triggers will you communicate, and how will you avoid relying on a single test or sign?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with cirrhosis who was previously oriented is now inattentive, disoriented to time, and has asterixis. The patient is also febrile. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next communication?","options":["Hepatic encephalopathy is possible, and the mental status change and possible infection should be promptly reported for evaluation of triggers and other causes.","The asterixis confirms hepatic encephalopathy, so the fever can be addressed after treatment for encephalopathy begins.","The fever makes infection the only likely explanation, so the liver history and neurologic findings need not be included in the report."],"feedback":["This fits because the findings and liver history make HE plausible, while fever may indicate a precipitating infection and other causes still need consideration.","Asterixis is not specific to HE, and the fever may be clinically important as a trigger or competing cause.","This is misleading because infection and HE can coexist, and both the neurologic change and relevant liver history matter to the assessment."]},{"stem":"A patient with advanced liver disease becomes difficult to arouse and responds inconsistently to voice. The nurse suspects worsening encephalopathy. Which action is the priority?","options":["Promptly alert the clinical team, protect the patient from immediate harm, and communicate the reduced responsiveness and need for urgent assessment.","Wait to see whether the patient becomes more oriented after the next scheduled bowel-directed treatment before notifying the team.","Focus first on obtaining an ammonia result, because the result will determine whether the change requires urgent attention."],"feedback":["This is the priority because reduced responsiveness can accompany severe HE and warrants prompt team assessment and supportive care.","Waiting may delay evaluation of a serious mental status decline and possible alternative or precipitating causes.","Ammonia may be helpful, but it does not replace urgent clinical assessment of declining responsiveness."]},{"stem":"A patient without known cirrhosis develops fluctuating confusion after a procedure that created a portosystemic shunt. The family reports a new sleep pattern, and the patient has no focal weakness. Which interpretation is most appropriate for the nurse to share?","options":["Hepatic encephalopathy remains possible because portosystemic bypass can be associated with it even without intrinsic liver disease.","Hepatic encephalopathy is unlikely without cirrhosis, so the shunt history is not relevant to the confusion.","The altered sleep pattern establishes minimal hepatic encephalopathy, so evaluation for other causes is unnecessary."],"feedback":["This fits because HE can occur with portosystemic bypass even without intrinsic liver disease, and the change still requires clinical evaluation.","This misses the recognized association between portosystemic shunting and HE in the absence of cirrhosis.","Sleep changes can occur with HE but do not establish the diagnosis or remove the need to consider other causes."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving prescribed treatment for suspected HE is more attentive than earlier, but a repeat ammonia result remains elevated. The patient has no new neurologic findings. Which nursing interpretation is best?","options":["Use the improved clinical status in the reassessment while continuing to monitor and report concerns; the ammonia result alone does not establish ongoing deterioration.","Treat the unchanged ammonia result as proof that the patient has worsened, even though the clinical examination has improved.","Consider the patient fully recovered because alertness improved, and stop monitoring for recurrence or treatment-related problems."],"feedback":["This fits because ammonia can support assessment, but clinical status and its trend matter; continued observation remains appropriate.","This overinterprets one laboratory finding and disregards the improved clinical examination.","This is misleading because improvement does not eliminate the need to monitor the mental status trend and treatment-related concerns."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Current trends in the treatment of hepatic encephalopathy - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2724191/"},{"title":"Hepatic encephalopathy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5421503/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Changing cognition","detail":"A patient with cirrhosis may develop disorientation and sleepiness, but altered mental status can have several causes."},{"label":"Possible triggers","detail":"Poor intake and constipation may contribute to hepatic encephalopathy, while infection, dehydration, or metabolic problems remain possible."},{"label":"Report and protect","detail":"Prioritize safety and promptly share the mental status change, liver history, and possible triggers with the care team."},{"label":"Track the trend","detail":"Reassess alertness, orientation, behavior, and motor findings; escalate declining responsiveness, new neurologic findings, or failure to improve."}]},{"id":"s034","unit":"neuro","title":"CNS infections","objective":"Recognize cues of possible central nervous system infection and use timely nursing assessment and escalation to support safe, coordinated evaluation and treatment.","concept":"Encephalitis is inflammation of brain tissue from direct infection or a postinfectious immune response; meningitis affects the covering membranes, and both can coexist. Fever and headache occur in either, while altered behavior or consciousness, seizures, or focal deficits point toward brain involvement. Cerebrospinal fluid testing and neuroimaging help identify causes, but early findings may be incomplete. Prompt recognition matters because treatable viral causes, especially herpes simplex, can cause severe injury if therapy is delayed.","recognize":"Suspect CNS infection when fever or a flu-like prodrome accompanies headache and new confusion, personality change, reduced alertness, seizure, or focal neurologic findings. Meningeal symptoms such as neck stiffness and photophobia may be mild or absent in encephalitis. Consider stroke, intoxication, and autoimmune encephalitis as alternatives, but do not let them dismiss a concerning infectious pattern.","respond":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician and use the facility escalation pathway, reporting symptom onset and changes in mental status, seizures, focal findings, vital signs, immune status, and relevant travel or exposure history. Support immediate assessment and ordered testing, and follow local protocols for safety and monitoring. Do not wait for test results before escalating or delay prescribed treatment while awaiting diagnostic confirmation.","reassess":"After escalation, track mental status, vital signs, seizure activity, and any new focal findings, and communicate trends rather than isolated observations. Confirm that the team is aware of deterioration and that ordered evaluation is progressing. Worsening consciousness, a seizure, new focal deficit, or signs of impaired airway protection warrant immediate escalation.","trap":"A patient with encephalitis may not have striking neck stiffness, so its absence is not a safe rule-out. Think of meningitis as inflammation around the brain and encephalitis as inflammation within it: symptoms can overlap, but altered mental status or focal brain signs should raise concern for encephalitis.","caseStory":"A 46-year-old arrives with fever and headache after a brief flu-like illness. A family member reports new confusion, and the patient describes a sudden foul smell before a short seizure. The nurse must decide whether the mild neck stiffness makes urgent escalation less necessary.","caseReason":"The new confusion and seizure indicate brain involvement, while an unusual smell before the seizure can suggest temporal lobe involvement associated with herpes simplex encephalitis. Mild neck stiffness does not make the presentation reassuring. The nurse should urgently communicate the findings and support prompt team evaluation.","teachback":"If a patient has fever and headache but little neck stiffness, what new neurologic findings would make you escalate concern for encephalitis, and what information would you report?","questions":[{"stem":"A 39-year-old has fever, headache, and new confusion. During assessment, the patient reports a brief foul-smell sensation followed by a focal seizure. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's immediate communication to the clinical team?","options":["The altered mental status and seizure suggest encephalitis, with the smell sensation raising concern for temporal lobe involvement.","The lack of marked neck stiffness makes meningitis the most likely explanation and lowers concern for brain inflammation.","The focal seizure makes a primary psychiatric or behavioral cause more likely than an acute CNS infection."],"feedback":["Correct. Altered mental status and seizures are key encephalitis cues, and an olfactory aura can suggest temporal lobe involvement associated with herpes simplex.","Mild or absent meningeal signs do not exclude encephalitis; confusion and seizure are important signs of brain involvement.","Behavioral changes can occur with CNS infection, and the fever, confusion, and seizure pattern requires urgent clinical evaluation rather than assuming a psychiatric cause."]},{"stem":"A patient admitted with suspected CNS infection becomes less responsive and develops a new focal weakness. The diagnostic tests ordered earlier have not yet been completed. What is the nurse's priority action?","options":["Escalate the deterioration immediately through the facility pathway and report the neurologic changes and timeline.","Continue routine observations until the ordered tests clarify whether the cause is infectious or vascular.","Ask the patient to rest in a quiet room and reassess after the next scheduled observation."],"feedback":["Correct. Declining consciousness and a new focal deficit require immediate communication and team reassessment; testing should not delay escalation.","These changes may reflect severe CNS disease or another urgent condition, so waiting for results risks delaying necessary care.","Rest does not address acute neurologic deterioration, which warrants prompt escalation rather than a later routine reassessment."]},{"stem":"A 58-year-old receiving treatment for advanced HIV develops fever and a gradual decline in alertness. The patient has no prominent neck stiffness. Which nursing response best supports evaluation of this new presentation?","options":["Report the immune compromise, fever, and altered alertness promptly, and support the team’s ordered evaluation for CNS infection.","Reassure the team that CNS infection is unlikely because neck stiffness is not prominent.","Treat the change as a likely medication effect and defer escalation unless a seizure occurs."],"feedback":["Correct. Immunocompromised patients may have less prominent inflammatory signs, and altered alertness with fever warrants prompt evaluation; the team may consider less common infections.","Neck stiffness can be absent or subtle, and its absence does not rule out encephalitis or another CNS infection.","Medication effects may be considered, but they should not replace escalation of fever and declining alertness in an immunocompromised patient."]},{"stem":"A patient with suspected encephalitis has an early cerebrospinal fluid sample that does not show the expected abnormalities. The patient remains febrile and confused. What is the best nursing interpretation and action?","options":["Continue to communicate the persistent neurologic concern because early cerebrospinal fluid changes may not yet be evident.","Consider encephalitis excluded and stop reporting confusion unless the patient develops neck stiffness.","Rely on the early sample as definitive and redirect attention to supportive care alone."],"feedback":["Correct. Cerebrospinal fluid abnormalities may take time to develop, so persistent fever and altered mental status still warrant communication and clinical reassessment.","Neither an early sample without expected findings nor lack of neck stiffness safely excludes encephalitis.","An early test may not be definitive; ongoing symptoms should remain part of the team’s assessment and decision-making."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Encephalitis - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/brain-infections/encephalitis"},{"title":"Infectious meningitis and encephalitis: A primer for acute care ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13053408/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Fever with confusion","detail":"After a flu-like illness, fever and headache followed by new confusion may indicate brain involvement, even with mild neck stiffness."},{"label":"Seizure warning","detail":"A brief seizure and reported foul smell before it, alongside confusion, are concerning neurologic changes requiring urgent attention."},{"label":"Urgent communication","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician through local escalation pathways, reporting symptom onset, mental status, seizure, and focal findings."},{"label":"Watch for deterioration","detail":"Track consciousness, vital signs, seizures, and focal findings; immediately escalate worsening alertness, another seizure, or impaired airway protection."}]},{"id":"s035","unit":"neuro","title":"Autonomic storming","objective":"Use episode patterns, bedside cues, and competing-cause assessment to recognize possible paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity after severe brain injury and guide timely nursing escalation.","concept":"Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity (PSH) is episodic sympathetic activation with motor overactivity after severe acquired brain injury, often traumatic. Episodes can combine raised temperature, blood pressure, heart and respiratory rates, sweating, and increased tone or dystonic posturing. Injury may disrupt descending inhibition of lower sympathetic circuits, allowing ordinary care stimuli to provoke disproportionate responses. Because no biomarker or imaging finding confirms PSH, recognition depends on the clinical pattern, structured assessment, and ongoing review of competing explanations.","recognize":"Look for several features occurring together in recurrent, abrupt episodes, sometimes after routine care or other minor stimuli, with relative improvement between episodes. Increased tone or posturing supports the pattern but may be absent. Consider sepsis, seizures, pulmonary embolism, medication effects or withdrawal, endocrine emergencies, pain, and other causes. PSH is a clinical possibility, not a diagnosis made from fever or tachycardia alone; another illness, including sepsis, can coexist.","respond":"Prioritize immediate assessment of the patient's overall stability and promptly notify the responsible clinician or response team according to local protocols. Describe the episode pattern, associated motor signs, possible triggers, and changes between episodes; use the PSH Assessment Measure if directed by the team. Help reduce avoidable stimuli and provide supportive care under orders. Do not dismiss possible infection or other emergencies as PSH, or independently start or change medication.","reassess":"After nursing actions, trend heart and respiratory rates, blood pressure, temperature, sweating, tone or posturing, episode duration, triggers, and recovery between episodes. Report recurrence, worsening instability, new findings, or symptoms that persist after the episode, since these may signal an ongoing or coexisting cause requiring reassessment.","trap":"A cluster of storm-like signs is a pattern to investigate, not a diagnostic stamp. Think of PSH as one possible channel in a busy control room: identifying it does not prove that infection or another alarm is absent.","caseStory":"A patient recovering from severe traumatic brain injury develops repeated episodes of sweating, increased tone, rapid heart rate, and raised temperature during routine repositioning, then settles between episodes. The nurse must decide whether to report a possible PSH pattern or attribute the changes to the care activity and wait.","caseReason":"The synchronized, recurrent features and possible stimulus trigger fit a pattern that warrants consideration of PSH. The nurse should document the timing and associated signs and promptly communicate them to the team, while supporting assessment for sepsis and other mimics rather than treating PSH as confirmed.","teachback":"When a patient with acquired brain injury has repeated autonomic episodes, what pattern would make you raise possible PSH with the team, and what competing cause would you still want assessed?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with severe traumatic brain injury has recurring episodes during routine care that include sweating, increased muscle tone, rapid heart rate, and raised blood pressure. The findings ease between episodes. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next communication?","options":["The episode pattern raises possible PSH, so report the synchronized features and timing while the team evaluates other causes.","The increased muscle tone makes a seizure the most likely explanation, so focus communication on seizure activity alone.","The raised temperature and heart rate establish infection as the explanation, so an episodic pattern is not important."],"feedback":["Recurrent, simultaneous autonomic and motor features after brain injury support considering PSH, but do not confirm it or remove the need to assess mimics.","Posturing can resemble seizure activity, but this pattern alone does not establish a seizure; the team should consider alternatives, including non-convulsive seizures.","Fever and tachycardia can occur with infection or PSH, and the conditions may coexist. The timing and full episode pattern remain important to report."]},{"stem":"A patient with severe brain injury develops a sudden episode of rapid breathing, sweating, increased tone, and marked changes in heart rate and blood pressure during nursing care. Which action should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Assess immediate stability, follow local escalation protocols, and promptly communicate the episode pattern and possible trigger to the responsible team.","Assume the care activity caused the episode, pause briefly, and wait to see whether the findings resolve before notifying anyone.","Treat the temperature as the main problem and defer reporting the motor and cardiovascular changes unless they continue."],"feedback":["Immediate assessment and timely escalation address potential instability while giving the team the pattern and context needed to evaluate PSH and other causes.","A care stimulus may trigger an episode, but waiting can delay assessment of PSH or an emergency. Report the event promptly while reducing avoidable stimulation when appropriate.","Temperature is only one feature. The synchronized autonomic and motor changes, their timing, and the patient's overall stability also need prompt assessment and communication."]},{"stem":"A patient recovering from cardiac arrest with hypoxic-ischemic brain injury has recurrent episodes of sweating, increased tone, rapid heart rate, and raised temperature. A nurse assumes PSH cannot apply because the injury was not traumatic. Which response is most appropriate?","options":["PSH can follow non-traumatic acquired brain injury; report the episode pattern and support evaluation of competing causes.","PSH is limited to traumatic injury, so the nurse should report the episodes only as a nonspecific fever response.","The episode pattern confirms PSH in this setting, so evaluation for infection and other causes is unnecessary."],"feedback":["PSH has been described after non-traumatic acquired brain injuries, including hypoxic-ischemic injury. The pattern warrants consideration, alongside assessment for mimics.","Traumatic brain injury is common in PSH, but the syndrome can also occur after non-traumatic acquired brain injury.","The pattern may support possible PSH but does not confirm it. Sepsis and other causes remain relevant and may coexist."]},{"stem":"A patient with severe traumatic brain injury is being assessed for possible PSH. After a care activity ends, the rapid heart rate and increased tone improve, but the temperature remains raised and later rises again. Which nursing reassessment is most appropriate?","options":["Continue trending the full episode pattern and report persistent or recurrent changes so the team can reassess for PSH and coexisting causes such as sepsis.","Record the improvement in heart rate and tone as confirmation of PSH, and consider the remaining temperature change part of the same diagnosis.","Stop tracking the episode once the heart rate improves, since a persistent temperature change is not useful for distinguishing causes."],"feedback":["Improvement in some features does not establish PSH or explain persistent fever. Trend the full pattern and report changes because a coexisting cause may need attention.","Partial improvement is not diagnostic, and persistent or recurrent fever should not automatically be attributed to PSH.","Temperature remains a relevant feature to trend, and a persistent change may warrant team reassessment rather than being ignored."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Recognising and differentiating paroxysmal sympathetic ...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42622998/"},{"title":"Paroxysmal Sympathetic Hyperactivity after Traumatic Brain Injury: Current Understanding and Therapeutic Options","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10839941/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Triggered episodes","detail":"After severe brain injury, routine repositioning may precede recurrent episodes of sweating, increased tone, rapid heart rate, and raised temperature."},{"label":"Synchronized features","detail":"Episodes that combine motor and sympathetic changes, then improve between events, fit a possible PSH pattern but do not confirm it."},{"label":"Report the pattern","detail":"Assess stability and promptly share episode timing, signs, triggers, and recovery with the responsible clinician under local protocols."},{"label":"Trend between episodes","detail":"Track vital signs, tone, triggers, duration, and recovery; report worsening instability or persistent symptoms that may indicate another cause."}]},{"id":"s036","unit":"neuro","title":"Neuromuscular respiratory failure","objective":"Recognize possible neuromuscular ventilatory failure in an adult with myasthenia gravis and escalate care using the patient’s clinical trend and team protocols.","concept":"In myasthenia gravis, fatigable weakness can involve the diaphragm, other respiratory muscles, and the muscles that protect the airway. Weak cough or impaired swallowing increases the risk of retained secretions and aspiration, while inadequate ventilation can cause carbon dioxide retention. Respiratory failure may progress without obvious distress, so examination, blood gases, and trends in bedside respiratory measures matter alongside oxygen saturation.","recognize":"Look for worsening or fatigable weakness alongside new dysphagia, nasal or weak speech, ineffective cough, head drop, or increasing work of breathing. Lethargy may accompany carbon dioxide retention even when distress is subtle. Compare serial respiratory measures and blood gases with the patient’s condition. If hypoxia occurs without other myasthenic signs or hypercapnia, consider non-myasthenic causes rather than assuming crisis.","respond":"Treat worsening bulbar or respiratory weakness as urgent: promptly notify the responsible clinician and critical-care team, communicate the symptom and measurement trends, and follow local escalation and monitoring protocols. Support airway safety and swallowing precautions as directed, and prepare to assist with ordered respiratory support. Do not wait for dramatic distress or a single threshold, and do not assume all dyspnea or hypoxia in myasthenia is caused by crisis.","reassess":"After escalation or a protocol-directed intervention, reassess alertness, speech, swallowing, cough effectiveness, breathing pattern, oxygenation, blood-gas results, and trends in respiratory measurements. Report worsening weakness, rising carbon dioxide, deteriorating ventilation, aspiration concerns, or reduced alertness promptly, even if oxygen saturation appears reassuring.","trap":"A calm appearance is not a reliable ventilation monitor: carbon dioxide retention can make a failing patient lethargic rather than visibly distressed. Think of oxygen saturation as one dashboard gauge, not the whole engine; pair it with the neurologic exam, blood gases, and respiratory trends.","caseStory":"An adult with known myasthenia gravis is admitted with a recent cough and increasing fatigue. The nurse notices nasal speech, weak neck movement, and new lethargy, although the patient is not visibly struggling to breathe; blood gases show carbon dioxide retention and bedside respiratory measurements have worsened. The decision is whether to escalate now or wait for obvious distress.","caseReason":"The combination of bulbar and neck weakness, lethargy, carbon dioxide retention, and worsening respiratory measurements supports concern for neuromuscular ventilatory failure despite the lack of dramatic distress. Promptly alerting the critical-care team and following escalation protocols is safer than waiting for visible respiratory effort to increase.","teachback":"A patient with myasthenia becomes lethargic but has little visible breathing distress. What findings would you combine to judge whether ventilation is worsening, and what change would make you escalate care?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with myasthenia has new lethargy, nasal speech, and neck weakness but no obvious tachypnea. Blood gases show carbon dioxide retention, and bedside respiratory measurements are declining. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next action?","options":["Suspect worsening neuromuscular ventilatory failure and promptly escalate the trend to the clinical team.","Consider respiratory failure unlikely because the patient lacks visible distress, and continue routine observation.","Attribute the lethargy to a nonspecific cause and focus assessment on oxygen saturation alone."],"feedback":["Correct. Carbon dioxide retention and declining respiratory measures with bulbar and neck weakness can indicate ventilatory failure even without obvious distress, so prompt escalation is warranted.","This is misleading because myasthenic respiratory failure may not produce dramatic visible distress; waiting risks missing deterioration.","This overlooks the combination of neurologic changes, carbon dioxide retention, and worsening respiratory measures; oxygen saturation alone does not assess ventilation."]},{"stem":"An adult with myasthenia develops worsening swallowing, coughing after sips, and a weaker cough. Oxygen saturation is currently stable, but respiratory measurements are trending downward. What is the nursing priority?","options":["Promptly notify the clinical and critical-care teams, communicate the trends and aspiration concerns, and follow local airway-safety and escalation protocols.","Continue oral intake with close observation because stable oxygen saturation indicates the airway is currently protected.","Wait for a low oxygen saturation or severe breathlessness before reporting a change in respiratory status."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening bulbar function raises aspiration and airway risks, and declining respiratory measures merit prompt escalation even if oxygen saturation remains stable.","This is unsafe because impaired swallowing and cough can compromise airway protection; oral intake should be managed according to the clinical team’s direction and local protocols.","This delays action until late signs may appear. Bulbar deterioration and respiratory trends are important reasons to escalate before severe hypoxia or distress."]},{"stem":"A postoperative adult with known myasthenia reports worsening breathlessness. The neurologic examination shows no new ocular, bulbar, or limb weakness, respiratory measurements are near the patient’s prior values, and blood gases do not show carbon dioxide retention; hypoxia persists. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Escalate the hypoxia and support evaluation for non-myasthenic causes rather than assuming a myasthenic crisis.","Treat the breathlessness as a myasthenic crisis because any respiratory symptom in a patient with myasthenia has the same cause.","Reassure the team that stable respiratory measurements rule out a serious respiratory or cardiac problem."],"feedback":["Correct. Isolated dyspnea or hypoxia without typical myasthenic features or hypercapnia should prompt consideration of other causes and appropriate evaluation.","This overattributes the symptom to myasthenia. Respiratory symptoms in these patients can have other pulmonary, cardiac, airway, or systemic explanations.","Stable respiratory measurements do not rule out other serious causes of hypoxia, so the finding still needs escalation and assessment."]},{"stem":"An adult with myasthenia is receiving protocol-directed respiratory support after worsening weakness. The oxygen saturation has improved, but the patient is becoming more lethargic and repeat blood gases show increasing carbon dioxide. What should the nurse do?","options":["Promptly escalate the worsening alertness and carbon dioxide trend, while continuing ordered monitoring and support.","Interpret the improved oxygen saturation as recovery and reduce concern about ventilation.","Wait for a new episode of visible respiratory distress before contacting the clinical team."],"feedback":["Correct. Increasing carbon dioxide and lethargy suggest worsening ventilation despite improved oxygen saturation and require prompt communication and reassessment.","This confuses oxygenation with ventilation. Better saturation does not negate worsening carbon dioxide retention or reduced alertness.","Visible distress may be absent in neuromuscular respiratory failure, so waiting could delay needed reassessment and intervention."]}],"sources":[{"title":"SOP myasthenic crisis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7650067/"},{"title":"Pitfalls in the Evaluation of Respiratory Failure in Myasthenia ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11561952/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Weakening respiratory muscles","detail":"Myasthenic weakness can impair the diaphragm, airway protection, and cough, allowing carbon dioxide retention and secretions to accumulate."},{"label":"Bulbar signs with lethargy","detail":"Nasal speech, weak neck movement, lethargy, rising carbon dioxide, and worsening bedside measures can signal failure without visible distress."},{"label":"Escalate respiratory decline","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician and critical-care team with symptom and measurement trends; follow local escalation and monitoring protocols."},{"label":"Trend ventilation and alertness","detail":"Recheck speech, cough, alertness, breathing, blood gases, and respiratory measurements; promptly report rising carbon dioxide or reduced alertness."}]},{"id":"s037","unit":"neuro","title":"After cranial or spinal surgery","objective":"Use comparison with a documented neurologic baseline to recognize and promptly communicate meaningful changes after cranial or spinal surgery.","concept":"Cranial and spinal procedures can be followed by neurologic complications, so postoperative monitoring is part of ongoing safety care. A documented baseline gives meaning to later findings: changes in alertness, orientation, pupils, strength, sensation, or movement may signal a change in central nervous system function. Repeated, consistent assessment and clear communication help the team recognize deterioration and evaluate it promptly.","recognize":"Compare current findings with the patient’s documented baseline, including level of consciousness, orientation, pupil response, motor function, and sensation. New weakness, sensory change, confusion, or reduced responsiveness deserves attention even if other findings appear unchanged. Recent anesthesia may affect alertness, so consider the overall pattern and trend rather than attributing a new or worsening finding to routine recovery.","respond":"Prioritize the new finding over routine tasks, remain with the patient, and promptly notify the appropriate surgical or clinical team using local protocols. Report the baseline, current assessment, timing, and trend; continue focused neurologic and general postoperative monitoring and document findings and actions. Do not assume a change is simply anesthesia wearing off or independently prescribe treatment.","reassess":"After communicating or taking an action under local protocol, repeat the same relevant neurologic checks and compare them with baseline and prior findings. Document the time, findings, and response. Escalate promptly for a new, persistent, or worsening deficit, reduced responsiveness, or other concerning change.","trap":"An unchanged pupil response does not cancel out a new motor or sensory change. Think of the neurologic assessment as several dashboard indicators: one steady reading cannot confirm that every other system is stable.","caseStory":"A fictional patient returns from cranial surgery with documented preoperative strength in both arms. During a later check, the patient is slower to answer and has less strength in one arm, while pupil responses appear unchanged. The nurse must decide whether to attribute this to routine recovery or communicate a neurologic change.","caseReason":"The reduced responsiveness and new motor difference are changes from baseline in two assessed neurologic areas. Unchanged pupils do not resolve the concern. The nurse should promptly communicate the comparison and continue focused assessment under local protocols.","teachback":"When a postoperative neurologic finding differs from baseline, what will you compare, communicate, and reassess before deciding the change is harmless?","questions":[{"stem":"A nurse assesses an adult after cranial surgery. The documented baseline notes equal arm strength and usual alertness. The patient now answers slowly and has reduced strength in one arm, while pupil responses appear unchanged. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The new changes in responsiveness and strength warrant prompt communication and continued focused assessment.","The unchanged pupil responses indicate that the neurologic status is stable.","Slower answers are expected after surgery, so the arm finding can wait for the next routine check."],"feedback":["Correct. Responsiveness and motor function are both relevant neurologic findings, and each has changed from baseline.","Unchanged pupil responses do not establish that other parts of the neurologic assessment are stable.","This assumes an explanation for the slower response and delays attention to a new motor change."]},{"stem":"An adult recovering from spinal surgery had intact strength and sensation documented earlier. The patient now reports new numbness and weakness. Airway and circulation checks are not showing a new concern. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly inform the appropriate clinical team of the change, report the baseline and current findings, and continue focused assessment under local protocols.","Assist the patient to walk so the nurse can determine whether the weakness affects mobility.","Wait until the next scheduled check because the patient is awake and other observations are stable."],"feedback":["Correct. New sensory and motor findings differ from baseline and should be communicated promptly while monitoring continues.","Walking could delay communication and is not needed to establish that the reported change deserves attention.","Stable observations in other areas do not remove the concern raised by a new neurologic change."]},{"stem":"An adult has returned to the care unit after an endovascular neurosurgical procedure. The initial postoperative assessment is documented. On a later check, the patient is less responsive than before and has new difficulty moving one arm. Which action best applies the same neurologic-monitoring skill in this setting?","options":["Compare the findings with the documented baseline, promptly communicate the changes, and continue focused assessment under local protocols.","Treat the findings as unrelated to neurologic status because the procedure was not a spinal operation.","Recheck only the patient’s pupils, since they are the most useful finding for deciding whether to report a change."],"feedback":["Correct. The cited review includes endovascular procedures, and postoperative monitoring includes attention to neurologic complications and changes from baseline.","Neurologic monitoring is relevant across the neurosurgical procedures described, not only spinal operations.","Pupils are one assessment component; changes in responsiveness and motor function also matter."]},{"stem":"After a nurse reports a new postoperative arm weakness, the patient becomes more alert during follow-up. The weakness remains different from the documented baseline, and pupil responses are unchanged. What is the best reassessment decision?","options":["Repeat and document the relevant neurologic checks, communicate the persistent weakness to the team, and follow local escalation protocols.","Conclude that the patient has returned to baseline because alertness improved.","Focus on comfort assessment and defer another neurologic check because pupil responses are unchanged."],"feedback":["Correct. Improved alertness is useful information, but persistent motor change still differs from baseline and warrants communication and continued assessment.","A change in one assessment area does not establish recovery in another area.","Comfort and pupil response do not replace reassessment of the known motor change."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Neurologic Complications in the Postoperative ...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34618765/"},{"title":"31.4 Postoperative Phase - Fundamentals of Nursing | OpenStax","url":"https://openstax.org/books/fundamentals-nursing/pages/31-4-postoperative-phase"}],"frames":[{"label":"Postoperative neurologic risk","detail":"Cranial or spinal surgery can be followed by neurologic complications, making a documented baseline essential for recognizing change."},{"label":"New unilateral arm weakness","detail":"Slower responses and reduced strength in one arm differ from documented baseline, even when pupil responses remain unchanged."},{"label":"Report baseline change","detail":"Remain with the patient and promptly notify the surgical or clinical team, giving baseline, current findings, timing, and trend."},{"label":"Repeat focused neurologic checks","detail":"Repeat the same neurologic checks against baseline, document findings and response, and escalate persistent or worsening deficits or reduced responsiveness."}]},{"id":"s038","unit":"neuro","title":"Aneurysms and subarachnoid hemorrhage","objective":"By the end of this unit, the nurse will recognize suspected aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage and prioritize escalation, stabilization, and focused reassessment within the interprofessional plan of care.","concept":"A ruptured aneurysm releases arterial blood into the space around the brain, which can abruptly raise intracranial pressure and disrupt cerebral perfusion. Blood may obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow, causing hydrocephalus, while an unsecured aneurysm can rebleed. Securing the aneurysm reduces that risk, but later delayed cerebral ischemia can still cause neurologic decline, so vigilance continues after treatment.","recognize":"A sudden, severe headache reaching maximum intensity quickly is a key warning sign, often accompanied by neck stiffness, vomiting, light sensitivity, fainting, seizure, or reduced consciousness. Presentation can be less dramatic, so a normal examination does not exclude hemorrhage. Noncontrast head CT identifies blood; when suspicion remains despite a negative scan, further evaluation may be needed rather than reassurance.","respond":"For suspected hemorrhage, prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation, activate the emergency and neurovascular teams, and facilitate urgent imaging under local protocol. Communicate symptom onset, changes in consciousness, medications, and relevant bleeding history. If hemorrhage is identified, support timely specialist evaluation and aneurysm treatment. Do not independently change blood pressure therapy or give medications outside orders and protocol; avoid overcorrection that may impair brain perfusion.","reassess":"After each nursing action, reassess consciousness, neurologic findings, pupils, headache, vomiting, oxygenation, and blood pressure trends, comparing them with the prior examination. New lethargy, focal deficit, worsening consciousness, or signs of hydrocephalus require immediate escalation to the responsible clinician and neurocritical team.","trap":"A negative early CT is not an automatic all-clear when the clinical concern remains high; further evaluation may be appropriate. Think of the scan as one reading from a warning system, not proof that the alarm was false.","caseStory":"A previously well adult develops an abrupt severe headache during activity, vomits, and briefly loses consciousness. The patient is now awake with neck stiffness, and the initial CT shows blood around the brain. The nurse must decide what to communicate and prioritize while the team evaluates the aneurysm.","caseReason":"The abrupt headache, transient loss of consciousness, and neck stiffness are concerning for subarachnoid hemorrhage, and CT-confirmed blood requires urgent specialist assessment. The nurse should promptly communicate the onset and neurologic status, support stabilization and the imaging and treatment pathway, and watch for deterioration rather than assuming that being awake means the danger has passed.","teachback":"A patient has a negative early head CT but a sudden severe headache and persistent clinical concern for aneurysmal bleeding. What would you communicate, and why should the evaluation not automatically stop?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult develops a sudden, maximal-at-onset headache, vomits, and briefly faints. The patient is now alert, but CT shows blood in the basal cisterns. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The findings are consistent with subarachnoid hemorrhage and warrant urgent aneurysm-focused specialist evaluation.","The brief loss of consciousness makes a seizure mimic more likely than hemorrhage, despite the CT finding.","An alert examination makes clinically important subarachnoid bleeding unlikely, so routine observation is sufficient."],"feedback":["Correct. Sudden severe headache, transient loss of consciousness, and blood around the brain are consistent with subarachnoid hemorrhage and require urgent evaluation for an aneurysm.","A seizure can accompany subarachnoid hemorrhage, and fainting is also described; these possibilities do not outweigh CT evidence of blood.","A patient may be awake or have a less dramatic presentation despite hemorrhage. Alertness does not make the CT finding safe to disregard."]},{"stem":"A patient with CT-confirmed subarachnoid hemorrhage becomes progressively difficult to arouse and develops abnormal breathing. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Immediately escalate the change, assess and support airway and breathing, and summon the emergency and neurocritical team.","Continue frequent neurologic checks and wait for the next scheduled imaging before notifying the team.","Focus first on reducing the reported headache, then reassess consciousness after the patient rests."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening consciousness and abnormal breathing require immediate escalation and airway, breathing, and circulation attention while the team evaluates possible complications.","Waiting for scheduled imaging risks delaying stabilization and assessment of a potentially life-threatening decline.","Pain relief does not address the immediate airway and breathing concern, and sedation or delay could complicate assessment."]},{"stem":"In a community emergency department, an adult reports a sudden severe headache that peaked rapidly. An early noncontrast CT is negative, but the clinician remains concerned for aneurysmal bleeding. Which nursing response best supports safe evaluation?","options":["Communicate the persistent concern and support the clinician-directed plan for additional evaluation rather than treating the scan as definitive.","Reassure the patient that a negative early CT excludes subarachnoid hemorrhage when the neurologic examination is normal.","Request vessel imaging as a substitute for further evaluation because the initial CT showed no blood."],"feedback":["Correct. When suspicion remains high despite a negative early CT, additional evaluation may be appropriate; the nurse should communicate the concern and support the clinician's plan.","A negative early CT does not always end the evaluation when clinical suspicion remains, and a normal examination does not exclude hemorrhage.","Vessel imaging is used to evaluate for a vascular lesion, but it is not a universal substitute for evaluating suspected hemorrhage after a negative CT."]},{"stem":"Several days after an aneurysm has been secured, a patient who was previously conversant becomes confused and develops new weakness. The patient denies a severe headache. What is the best nursing interpretation and action?","options":["Treat the new neurologic change as urgent, promptly escalate it, and support evaluation for delayed cerebral ischemia or another complication.","Consider the absence of headache reassuring and continue routine observation unless the symptoms persist.","Attribute the confusion to the recent procedure and defer notification until the next scheduled assessment."],"feedback":["Correct. Delayed cerebral ischemia can cause neurologic decline after aneurysm treatment, and new confusion or weakness warrants prompt escalation even without headache.","The absence of headache does not exclude delayed cerebral ischemia or another serious complication; the new deficit is more important than that reassuring feature.","A new neurologic change should not be presumed to be a routine procedure effect or left unreported while awaiting a scheduled check."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Stroke - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535369/"},{"title":"Aneurysmal Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/8147706"}],"frames":[{"label":"Blood raises intracranial pressure","detail":"A ruptured aneurysm can raise intracranial pressure, disrupt perfusion, obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow, and leave risk of rebleeding."},{"label":"Abrupt severe headache","detail":"Sudden maximal headache, vomiting, neck stiffness, or brief loss of consciousness with CT-confirmed blood signals urgent hemorrhage evaluation."},{"label":"Activate neurovascular response","detail":"Prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation; activate emergency and neurovascular teams, communicating onset and neurologic status under local protocol."},{"label":"Watch for neurologic decline","detail":"Reassess consciousness, neurologic findings, headache, vomiting, and blood pressure trends; immediately escalate new lethargy, focal deficit, or worsening consciousness."}]},{"id":"s039","unit":"neuro","title":"Intracranial masses","objective":"Recognize patterns that may indicate an intracranial mass, prioritize urgent nursing escalation, and reassess for evolving neurologic or airway compromise.","concept":"An intracranial mass can injure nearby tissue through invasion or compression, cause swelling or bleeding, and raise pressure within the fixed space of the skull. It may also obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow or absorption. These mechanisms can produce headache, altered mental status, seizures, or location-related deficits. Symptoms alone do not establish whether a tumor is primary or metastatic, and a benign mass can still cause serious harm.","recognize":"Be alert to a persistent, unexplained new headache that progresses or worsens when lying down, with straining, or after sleep, especially alongside vomiting. New seizures, declining alertness, personality or cognitive change, or focal neurologic or endocrine findings also warrant attention. Papilledema may support concern but can be absent. Similar findings can arise from other intracranial masses or stroke, so avoid assuming a tumor diagnosis.","respond":"Treat a new or worsening neurologic pattern as a reason for prompt communication with the responsible clinician and care team. Report the onset, progression, associated symptoms, and focused neurologic findings; prioritize airway concerns if alertness or protective reflexes are impaired. Follow local protocols and orders for monitoring and care. Do not independently prescribe treatment or dismiss symptoms because one expected sign is absent.","reassess":"After nursing actions or team evaluation, reassess alertness, airway protection, headache and vomiting, seizures, and the focal findings relevant to the patient’s symptoms. Compare with the prior assessment and report worsening consciousness, a new deficit, recurrent seizure, or concern about airway protection promptly. A normal finding in one area does not cancel a worsening pattern elsewhere.","trap":"A missing sign such as papilledema does not rule out increased intracranial pressure; it is like one quiet alarm that cannot certify the whole system is safe. Likewise, a focal deficit can be misleading about the mass’s location when pressure affects distant structures.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult reports a new headache that has become more persistent and is worse when lying down. The patient vomits and then has a first seizure, followed by slower responses than earlier in the shift. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as an isolated headache or promptly escalate a potentially evolving intracranial problem.","caseReason":"The progressive headache, vomiting, new seizure, and change in responsiveness together raise concern for an intracranial mass effect or another serious intracranial cause, though they do not identify the cause. The nurse should promptly communicate the pattern and change in status, assess airway protection and neurologic findings, and follow team direction and local protocols.","teachback":"When a patient has a concerning new headache but no papilledema, what other changes would you assess and communicate, and why would the absent sign not reassure you by itself?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult presents with a recent, persistent headache that has progressively worsened and is more intense after sleep. The patient now has unexplained vomiting but no known cancer history. Which interpretation should guide the nurse’s next communication?","options":["The pattern warrants prompt evaluation for an intracranial process, including a possible mass, without assuming a specific cause.","The absence of a cancer history makes a primary headache disorder the most likely explanation to communicate.","Vomiting without prominent nausea points away from increased intracranial pressure and lowers the need to escalate."],"feedback":["A persistent, unexplained recent-onset headache that worsens after sleep and is accompanied by vomiting is a concerning pattern. It calls for prompt evaluation, while other intracranial causes remain possible.","A cancer history is not required for an intracranial mass, and symptoms alone cannot establish the cause. The progressive pattern and vomiting should not be dismissed.","When intracranial pressure is very high, vomiting may occur with little preceding nausea. This detail does not make the pattern reassuring."]},{"stem":"A patient being evaluated for an intracranial mass becomes increasingly drowsy and responds slowly to voice. The nurse notices that the patient’s protective airway reflexes appear impaired. Which action has priority?","options":["Promptly alert the responsible clinician and team to the change, prioritize airway concern, and follow the established emergency response and orders.","Continue routine neurologic observations until a focal weakness appears, then update the team with the complete trend.","Ask the patient to rest and reassess after the next scheduled observation, since drowsiness can accompany a headache."],"feedback":["Declining mental status with impaired airway protection is urgent. Prompt team communication and action under local protocols take priority; airway management decisions belong to the responsible team.","Waiting for focal weakness could delay response to an important deterioration. Altered alertness and impaired protective reflexes already warrant escalation.","Drowsiness can reflect neurologic deterioration, and impaired airway reflexes raise an immediate safety concern. Waiting for a routine reassessment is not appropriate."]},{"stem":"An adult with no known malignancy develops new unsteadiness, nystagmus, and vomiting. The nurse is considering whether the combination could reflect a posterior fossa intracranial problem. Which interpretation best supports prompt escalation?","options":["Cerebellar-region masses can be associated with unsteadiness or nystagmus and may obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow, so the combined findings need prompt evaluation.","Unsteadiness and nystagmus localize reliably to a benign cerebellar tumor, so the nurse can communicate that diagnosis.","The absence of a known malignancy makes a brain metastasis unlikely enough that the findings can be managed as an isolated balance problem."],"feedback":["Cerebellar tumors may be associated with ataxia and nystagmus, and posterior fossa masses can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid drainage. The findings merit escalation without claiming a diagnosis.","These findings can suggest a region but do not establish tumor type or whether a mass is benign. Clinical evaluation and imaging are needed.","Brain metastases can occur without a previously known malignancy, and other intracranial causes are possible. The combination should not be reduced to an isolated balance complaint."]},{"stem":"A patient with a suspected intracranial mass has a worsening headache and increasing sleepiness, but the latest eye examination does not show papilledema. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["The absent papilledema does not exclude increased intracranial pressure; the worsening symptoms and mental status change still require prompt communication.","The absent papilledema makes a pressure-related problem unlikely, so the nurse should wait for another objective sign before escalating.","The eye finding rules out a mass effect, so reassessment can focus on whether the headache responds to routine comfort measures."],"feedback":["Papilledema may be absent even when intracranial pressure is increased. Worsening headache and alertness remain important changes to report.","Waiting for another sign could delay evaluation. No single absent finding should override a concerning clinical trend.","An eye examination without papilledema does not rule out a mass or increased pressure. The broader neurologic pattern remains relevant."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Intracranial Tumors - Neurologic Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/intracranial-and-spinal-tumors/overview-of-intracranial-tumors?ruleredirectid=749"},{"title":"Overview of Intracranial Tumors - Neurologic Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/intracranial-and-spinal-tumors/overview-of-intracranial-tumors?ruleredirectid=744"}],"frames":[{"label":"Mass effect in fixed skull","detail":"An intracranial mass may compress tissue, cause swelling or bleeding, obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow, and raise pressure."},{"label":"Progressive headache and seizure","detail":"Worsening positional headache, vomiting, a first seizure, and slower responses suggest an evolving intracranial problem without identifying its cause."},{"label":"Communicate evolving pattern","detail":"Promptly report symptom progression and focused neurologic findings, assess airway protection, and follow team direction and local protocols."},{"label":"Recheck responsiveness and airway","detail":"Reassess alertness, airway protection, headache, vomiting, seizures, and relevant focal findings; promptly report worsening consciousness, new deficit, or recurrent seizure."}]},{"id":"s040","unit":"renal","title":"Acute kidney injury","objective":"Interpret common acute kidney injury cues and prioritize nursing actions that help identify reversible causes and detect worsening complications.","concept":"Acute kidney injury is a rapid decline in kidney function that can cause waste products, fluid, and electrolyte disturbances to accumulate. Causes are grouped as reduced kidney perfusion, intrinsic kidney damage, or urinary obstruction. These mechanisms guide assessment: perfusion problems may improve when the underlying issue is corrected, while intrinsic injury or obstruction needs targeted evaluation. Urine output may change early, but its amount alone does not identify the cause.","recognize":"Look for falling urine output or rising creatinine, while considering the patient’s recent illness, surgery, blood pressure, fluid losses, heart function, and medication or contrast exposure. Low urine output with a history of fluid loss and concentrated urine may support reduced perfusion; abnormal urine sediment can suggest intrinsic disease. A palpable bladder, urinary symptoms, or a sudden catheter-output change raises concern for obstruction or catheter problems.","respond":"Prioritize assessment for immediately reversible perfusion problems and urinary obstruction, and promptly communicate urine-output and laboratory trends to the care team. Review medication and contrast exposure, assess vital signs and signs of fluid depletion or overload, and check a catheter system for obstruction according to local protocol. Support ordered tests and treatment; do not independently change medications or give fluid when the cause and volume status are unclear.","reassess":"After an action, reassess urine output, vital signs, fluid balance, edema, breathing, and ordered kidney-function and electrolyte results. Report persistent or worsening low output, rising creatinine, new respiratory findings, or concerning electrolyte or acid-base changes promptly. Increased urine output does not by itself confirm that kidney function has recovered.","trap":"Urine output is like a warning light, not a diagnosis: a low reading can reflect reduced perfusion, kidney injury, or an obstructed outlet. Likewise, a return of urine does not mean the kidney’s filtering function has already recovered; creatinine and fluid and electrolyte status still need follow-up.","caseStory":"After surgery, a patient has several episodes of vomiting, lower blood pressure than earlier, and declining urine output. The urine is concentrated, and the team is evaluating kidney-function tests. The nurse must decide whether the pattern supports reduced perfusion and what to communicate first.","caseReason":"Vomiting and lower blood pressure support possible volume depletion and reduced kidney perfusion, while concentrated urine is consistent with the kidney conserving water. These cues suggest a prerenal cause but do not prove it. The nurse should promptly report the trend and assessment findings and support evaluation and treatment under the care team’s direction.","teachback":"A patient’s urine output rises after a period of low output, but creatinine remains elevated. What would you assess next, and why would you avoid assuming that kidney function has recovered?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized patient has had substantial diarrhea and a recent drop in blood pressure. Urine output is declining, and urine testing shows concentrated urine with low urine sodium. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Reduced kidney perfusion is a likely contributor, although the findings do not establish the cause by themselves.","Intrinsic tubular injury is established because any decline in urine output indicates damaged tubules.","Urinary obstruction is most likely because concentrated urine commonly identifies an obstructed outlet."],"feedback":["Fluid loss and lower blood pressure support reduced perfusion, and concentrated urine with low urine sodium can fit that pattern. The findings support but do not prove a prerenal cause.","Low urine output can occur with several AKI mechanisms and does not alone establish tubular injury. The history and urine findings instead support considering reduced perfusion.","Concentrated urine and low urine sodium are described with prerenal physiology, not as findings that identify obstruction. Obstruction requires consideration of urinary-tract cues and evaluation."]},{"stem":"A catheterized, normotensive patient’s urine flow falls suddenly. The patient has no new edema or breathing difficulty, and the catheter tubing is visible. What is the best initial nursing action?","options":["Check the catheter system for kinking or blockage and follow local protocol to confirm patency before broadening the evaluation.","Assume the sudden decline indicates intrinsic kidney injury and wait for the next creatinine result before acting.","Give a fluid bolus under standing practice because any sudden fall in urine output is evidence of volume depletion."],"feedback":["A sudden decrease in a normotensive catheterized patient can reflect catheter occlusion or displacement. Checking the system and following protocol addresses a potentially reversible cause.","A sudden catheter-output change can be mechanical, so intrinsic injury should not be assumed before checking catheter patency. Assessment should not be delayed if the concern persists.","A low output reading does not by itself establish volume depletion, and the patient’s volume status and other causes need assessment. Fluid administration should follow orders or local protocol."]},{"stem":"A patient with one functioning kidney develops flank discomfort and markedly reduced urine output after a ureteral stone is identified. Which nursing response best reflects the risk in this context?","options":["Promptly report concern for urinary obstruction and support timely assessment, because obstruction affecting the only functioning kidney can cause AKI.","Reassure the patient that ureteral obstruction cannot significantly affect kidney function unless both ureters are blocked.","Focus only on fluid intake because reduced output with a stone is most consistent with dehydration."],"feedback":["Obstruction at one ureter can cause significant AKI when the patient has only one functioning kidney. Reporting the concern supports timely evaluation of a potentially reversible cause.","Bilateral ureter involvement is generally needed for significant AKI only when both kidneys function; the reference notes an exception for a single functioning kidney.","A stone and flank discomfort raise concern for urinary obstruction, which cannot be distinguished from dehydration by urine output alone. The obstructive possibility warrants communication and evaluation."]},{"stem":"A patient recovering from an oliguric AKI now produces substantially more urine, but creatinine remains elevated. Which interpretation and reassessment are most appropriate?","options":["Urine output may recover before creatinine falls; continue monitoring fluid balance, urine output, kidney-function trends, and electrolytes.","The rise in urine output confirms filtration has normalized, so continued monitoring for AKI-related changes is unnecessary.","Persistent creatinine elevation proves that obstruction remains and should be treated as the only possible cause."],"feedback":["In the postoliguric phase, urine output may return toward normal before creatinine and urea fall. Continued assessment is important because tubular dysfunction and fluid or electrolyte changes may persist.","Urine output alone does not confirm recovery of filtration, and the source describes a delay before creatinine and urea may fall. Ongoing monitoring is warranted.","Persistent creatinine elevation during early recovery does not prove obstruction or identify a single cause. The change should be interpreted with the broader clinical assessment and trends."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Nephrology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki"},{"title":"Oliguria - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/oliguria?"}],"frames":[{"label":"Reduced kidney perfusion","detail":"Vomiting and lower blood pressure can reduce kidney perfusion, while the kidneys may conserve water and produce concentrated urine."},{"label":"Falling urine output","detail":"Declining urine output after fluid loss, with concentrated urine and lower blood pressure, supports possible prerenal injury but does not prove it."},{"label":"Report output and pressure trend","detail":"Promptly communicate urine-output, blood-pressure, and kidney-function trends; support evaluation and treatment as directed by the care team."},{"label":"Reassess output and volume","detail":"Recheck urine output, vital signs, fluid balance, edema, breathing, and ordered results; report persistent low output or new respiratory findings."}]},{"id":"s041","unit":"renal","title":"Chronic kidney disease in the ICU","objective":"Interpret cues that may indicate chronic kidney disease or acute deterioration on chronic disease and communicate nursing priorities for team-led care.","concept":"Chronic kidney disease is lasting loss of kidney function, but remaining kidney tissue can compensate for a time, so early disease may have few symptoms. As function declines, regulation of fluid, electrolytes, acid, and phosphate becomes less reliable; anemia and bone and mineral changes may also develop. In critical care, chronic impairment can coexist with a new kidney injury, so trends and the clinical picture matter more than one result.","recognize":"Look for a history or evidence of longstanding impairment, such as prior reduced kidney function, chronic symptoms, or small kidneys on imaging. Nocturia, fatigue, itching, anemia, and mineral abnormalities may support chronic disease but do not prove it alone. A rapid creatinine rise or new oliguria suggests acute injury, possibly on CKD; normal or enlarged kidneys do not exclude CKD.","respond":"Assess and report changes in urine output, creatinine trend, fluid status, breathing, mental state, and relevant electrolyte results. Communicate the baseline and what has changed, and promptly escalate concerning findings through the care team and local protocol. Carry out ordered monitoring and treatment, including fluid or medication plans. Do not independently change medicines, restrict intake, or assume one abnormal result establishes the cause.","reassess":"After an ordered intervention, reassess urine output, breathing, edema, mental status, and the relevant laboratory trends, then document and communicate the response. Escalate promptly for worsening respiratory symptoms, persistent or increasing oliguria, concerning electrolyte changes, or uremic symptoms despite the current plan.","trap":"A normal blood sodium does not mean fluid balance is normal: the kidneys may maintain sodium concentration while fluid accumulates. Think of concentration as the mixture’s strength and volume as the size of the container; one can look normal while the other is excessive.","caseStory":"A patient admitted to intensive care has a history of CKD, chronic anemia, and prior imaging describing small kidneys. During the current admission, urine output falls and creatinine rises rapidly compared with recent results. The nurse must decide whether this is simply the expected course of CKD or a new change needing prompt team communication.","caseReason":"The chronic history and small kidneys support CKD, but a rapid creatinine rise and new oliguria point to possible acute kidney injury superimposed on it. The nurse should report the change from baseline and assess for related fluid and electrolyte problems rather than attributing the findings to chronic disease alone.","teachback":"When a patient with known CKD develops a rapid change in kidney function, which baseline details and current cues would you communicate to help the team distinguish chronic impairment from an acute change?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with known CKD has chronic anemia and previously documented small kidneys. Over the current shift, urine output falls and creatinine rises rapidly from the recent baseline. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these cues?","options":["Possible acute kidney injury on CKD; report the trend and assess for associated fluid and electrolyte changes.","Progression of CKD alone; continue routine observation because chronic anemia explains the new findings.","Uncertain chronic kidney disease; rely on the current creatinine result before contacting the care team."],"feedback":["A rapid creatinine rise and new oliguria suggest acute injury, which can occur on top of CKD. Reporting the change and assessing related problems supports timely team evaluation.","Chronic anemia can accompany CKD, but it does not explain away a new rapid change in kidney function and urine output.","The recent trend and clinical change are important context; waiting for a single result to establish the cause could delay communication of possible acute deterioration."]},{"stem":"A patient with advanced CKD develops worsening breathlessness and increasing dependent edema. The nurse notes that the patient’s sodium concentration is within the laboratory reference range. What is the priority nursing interpretation and action?","options":["Fluid overload remains possible despite normal sodium; promptly assess respiratory status and notify the care team.","Clinically important fluid accumulation is unlikely; defer escalation unless sodium becomes abnormal.","The findings most strongly establish anemia as the cause; focus communication on the chronic blood count."],"feedback":["Sodium concentration is typically maintained in CKD, while sodium and water overload can still contribute to edema and heart failure. Worsening breathlessness warrants prompt assessment and team communication.","A normal sodium concentration does not rule out excess fluid or heart failure in CKD, so this could delay attention to worsening respiratory symptoms.","Anemia may occur in CKD, but it does not account for increasing edema, and the respiratory change needs prompt evaluation rather than attribution to one chronic finding."]},{"stem":"A critically ill patient with CKD has a stable recent creatinine trend but a newly elevated potassium result. The medication record includes an ACE inhibitor. Which nursing interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["CKD and the ACE inhibitor may contribute to elevated potassium; promptly communicate the result and review the trend and medication context with the team.","Stable creatinine makes a kidney-related potassium problem unlikely; wait for a creatinine rise before reporting the result.","The ACE inhibitor is the confirmed cause; independently withhold it and reassess potassium later."],"feedback":["Potassium can rise in CKD, and ACE inhibitors are among the medicines that may contribute. Communicating the result with its medication and trend context supports team-led assessment.","Potassium abnormalities can occur without a simultaneous new creatinine rise, particularly in people with impaired kidney function and relevant medicines.","The medication is a possible contributor, not a confirmed cause. Medication changes should follow orders and local protocols rather than independent nursing decisions."]},{"stem":"A patient with CKD has fatigue and itching, but no recent baseline kidney results are available. Today’s creatinine is elevated; the patient is making urine and has no documented trend. Which approach best avoids mistaking one finding for a complete diagnosis?","options":["Gather prior kidney results and clinical history, assess current cues, and communicate that duration and cause remain uncertain.","Treat the elevated creatinine as proof of chronic disease and use the symptoms to confirm its stage.","Use preserved urine output to rule out acute kidney injury and defer further assessment."],"feedback":["An elevated creatinine and chronic-type symptoms can raise concern but do not establish duration or cause by themselves. Prior results, history, examination, and trends help distinguish chronic disease from acute or acute-on-chronic change.","One elevated result cannot establish chronicity or stage, and symptoms such as fatigue and itching are not sufficient to confirm either.","Urine output alone does not rule out acute kidney injury. The absence of a trend makes additional history and team communication especially important."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Chronic Kidney Disease - Nephrology","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/chronic-kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease"},{"title":"Chronic Kidney Disease - Chronic Kidney Disease - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-gb/professional/genitourinary-disorders/chronic-kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease"}],"frames":[{"label":"Chronic compensation","detail":"Remaining kidney tissue may compensate early, while chronic impairment can coexist with a new acute injury during critical illness."},{"label":"Change from baseline","detail":"A rapid creatinine rise and new oliguria, against prior CKD evidence, suggest acute injury rather than chronic disease alone."},{"label":"Report the trend","detail":"Communicate baseline kidney function, urine-output change, fluid status, breathing, mental state, and relevant electrolytes promptly through the care team."},{"label":"Worsening kidney signs","detail":"Reassess output, breathing, edema, mental status, and laboratory trends; promptly escalate worsening respiratory symptoms, persistent oliguria, or uremic symptoms."}]},{"id":"s042","unit":"renal","title":"Kidney infection and urosepsis","objective":"Recognize possible kidney infection with sepsis and obstructed urine flow, then prioritize timely nursing escalation and focused reassessment.","concept":"A urinary infection can ascend from the bladder to the kidneys, and infection combined with a dysregulated body response can impair organ function. When infected urine is trapped behind an obstruction, urgent drainage may be needed for source control alongside antimicrobial and supportive care directed by the clinical team. Obstruction can coexist with sepsis related kidney injury, so one explanation should not prematurely exclude another.","recognize":"Look for urinary symptoms or flank tenderness with systemic cues such as chills, marked malaise, confusion, reduced urine output, or worsening kidney function. Fever may be absent, and a reassuring white cell count or unchanged ultrasound does not reliably exclude serious infection or obstruction. Known hydronephrosis, stones, catheters, or urologic malignancy heighten concern; assess for competing infection sources too.","respond":"Treat suspected urinary sepsis or infected obstruction as time sensitive: promptly notify the responsible clinician and follow local escalation procedures. Support timely urine and blood cultures and ordered antibiotics, fluids, monitoring, and imaging; communicate obstruction concerns even when initial imaging is equivocal. Do not independently select antimicrobials, dismiss organ dysfunction, or delay escalation while waiting for a single test to settle the diagnosis.","reassess":"After nursing actions or team-directed treatment, reassess vital signs, mental status, perfusion, urine output, and relevant laboratory trends, and communicate changes promptly. Worsening confusion, falling blood pressure, persistent tachycardia, declining output, or new evidence of organ dysfunction warrants immediate escalation. After urinary drainage, watch closely for substantial output and hemodynamic change.","trap":"An unchanged or nondilated ultrasound is not a locked door proving that urine can flow freely; obstruction may be present without obvious dilation. Likewise, a normal temperature or white cell count is one quiet instrument, not proof that the whole clinical picture is safe.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult with a ureteral stent and known hydronephrosis becomes increasingly confused and lethargic, with rising creatinine and a tender flank. The patient is not febrile, and ultrasound shows no change from an earlier study. The nurse must decide whether the scan settles the concern or whether urgent team reassessment is needed.","caseReason":"The combination of altered mental status, worsening kidney function, flank findings, and known hydronephrosis raises concern for urinary infection with possible obstruction, even without fever or new ultrasound dilation. The nurse should promptly escalate the concern and communicate the full trend so the team can evaluate infection, organ dysfunction, and the need for source control.","teachback":"When a patient has possible urinary infection and worsening organ function, what findings would prompt you to escalate concern for obstruction even if the first ultrasound appears unchanged?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a history of urinary stones has chills, flank tenderness, confusion, and declining urine output. Ultrasound reports hydronephrosis that is unchanged from a prior scan. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication to the team?","options":["Obstruction with infected urine remains possible despite unchanged ultrasound findings and needs prompt team reassessment.","Stable ultrasound findings make urinary obstruction unlikely enough to focus only on dehydration.","Confusion and reduced output are more consistent with medication effects than an upper urinary infection."],"feedback":["Correct. Known hydronephrosis plus systemic illness and organ dysfunction warrants concern for infected obstruction; unchanged imaging does not rule it out.","This overweights one imaging comparison. Obstruction may be difficult to demonstrate, and dehydration could coexist rather than explain away the other cues.","Medication effects may be considered, but this conclusion underweights the flank findings, chills, known hydronephrosis, and declining urine output."]},{"stem":"A patient with urinary symptoms becomes acutely confused and tachycardic, and laboratory results show worsening kidney function. The nurse suspects a urinary source and notes a history of ureteric obstruction. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician and follow local escalation procedures, reporting the suspected infection, organ changes, and obstruction history.","Wait for the urine culture result before reporting the change, so the source can be confirmed first.","Focus on documenting the urinary symptoms and repeat the assessment at the next scheduled observation."],"feedback":["Correct. Suspected sepsis with organ dysfunction and possible obstruction is time sensitive; the team needs the findings promptly to direct evaluation and treatment.","Cultures can help identify infection, but waiting for results delays escalation of a potentially serious and treatable condition.","Documentation matters, but delayed communication and routine reassessment are insufficient for acute confusion, tachycardia, and worsening kidney function."]},{"stem":"An adult with metastatic pelvic cancer and poor oral intake has a rapid rise in creatinine, low urine output, and new confusion. The patient has no fever, and ultrasound shows no clear urinary tract dilation. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Malignancy and volume depletion can obscure urinary dilation, so obstruction remains a concern to communicate alongside other possible causes of kidney injury.","Without dilation on ultrasound, postrenal kidney injury can be set aside while sepsis-related kidney injury is assessed.","The absence of fever makes infection an unlikely contributor, so the kidney change is most likely due only to poor intake."],"feedback":["Correct. The supplied case discussion describes obstruction without clear dilation in settings including malignancy and dehydration, and multiple causes of kidney injury can coexist.","This treats ultrasound as definitive when obstruction can be nondilated, particularly in relevant clinical contexts.","Fever may be absent in serious illness, and poor intake does not exclude infection or obstruction as additional contributors."]},{"stem":"An adult with suspected urinary sepsis undergoes team-directed drainage of an obstructed collecting system. Over the next hours, urine output rises substantially, while blood pressure falls and confusion persists. What should the nurse do?","options":["Promptly report the blood pressure and mental-status changes, and closely communicate urine output and ongoing clinical trends.","Interpret the increased urine output as evidence that the infection is controlled and continue routine observations.","Focus on the urine output alone because the blood pressure change may be expected after drainage."],"feedback":["Correct. Output may increase after drainage, but falling blood pressure and persistent confusion require prompt communication and continued assessment.","Increased output alone does not establish infection control or resolution of organ dysfunction.","The case material describes hemodynamic deterioration after drainage, so blood pressure changes should not be dismissed."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Urosepsis - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482344/"},{"title":"Pyelonephritis and obstructive uropathy: a case of acute kidney injury","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4716328/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Infected obstruction","detail":"Infection behind an obstructed urinary tract can impair organ function and may require urgent drainage for source control."},{"label":"Sepsis without fever","detail":"Confusion, flank tenderness, rising creatinine, or falling urine output can signal serious urinary infection despite absent fever or unchanged ultrasound."},{"label":"Escalate source concern","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician and communicate obstruction concerns; support ordered cultures, antibiotics, fluids, monitoring, and imaging under local procedures."},{"label":"Declining perfusion","detail":"Reassess mental status, blood pressure, pulse, perfusion, and output; worsening confusion, falling pressure, or declining output needs immediate escalation."}]},{"id":"s043","unit":"renal","title":"Continuous renal replacement therapy","objective":"Apply CRRT principles to recognize when continuous therapy is appropriate, communicate priority concerns, and evaluate whether prescribed treatment is being delivered safely.","concept":"CRRT removes solutes and water gradually through a semipermeable filter, using diffusion, convection, or both. CVVHD primarily uses diffusion, CVVH uses convection, and CVVHDF combines them. This slower approach can support fluid and solute control when a patient is hemodynamically unstable or vulnerable to rapid shifts, while intermittent hemodialysis can remove potassium or toxins more rapidly when urgent clearance is needed.","recognize":"Consider CRRT when acute kidney injury is accompanied by fluid overload, acidosis, electrolyte disturbance, or uremic complications and hemodynamic instability makes rapid fluid shifts difficult to tolerate. Cerebral edema or rapid cell breakdown may also favor gradual control. Distinguish this from life-threatening hyperkalemia or acute intoxication, for which intermittent hemodialysis can provide faster removal when appropriate.","respond":"Prioritize the patient’s hemodynamic status, fluid balance, laboratory trends, access function, and the therapy’s delivered amount. Report instability, worsening abnormalities, access concerns, alarms, or treatment interruptions promptly to the responsible clinician and CRRT team. Follow local protocols and prescribed orders; do not independently change settings, fluid removal goals, anticoagulation, or solutions.","reassess":"After an action, reassess hemodynamic tolerance, fluid balance, relevant electrolyte and acid-base trends, access and circuit function, and whether interruptions have reduced delivered therapy. Escalate promptly for worsening instability, persistent or worsening abnormalities, suspected access obstruction, recurrent circuit alarms, or a gap between prescribed and delivered treatment.","trap":"Continuous does not mean automatically gentle or sufficient: interruptions can reduce the therapy actually delivered, much as a paused pump cannot provide its planned flow. CRRT is also not always the fastest option; intermittent hemodialysis may remove potassium or toxins more rapidly when urgent clearance is the priority.","caseStory":"A fictional adult in intensive care has acute kidney injury, increasing fluid overload, and blood pressure instability. The team is considering renal replacement therapy, while the bedside nurse notes that the patient is also becoming less tolerant of fluid shifts. The decision point is which treatment approach best fits the immediate clinical priorities and what the nurse should communicate.","caseReason":"The combination of fluid overload and hemodynamic instability supports consideration of CRRT because it allows more controlled fluid and solute management. The nurse should communicate the instability and fluid concerns to the team rather than select or prescribe a modality independently. If urgent rapid potassium or toxin removal were the dominant need, intermittent hemodialysis might be favored when clinically appropriate.","teachback":"When a patient is hemodynamically unstable and needs gradual fluid and solute control, what findings would you communicate to the CRRT team, and what would you reassess to determine whether the prescribed treatment is actually being delivered?","questions":[{"stem":"A fictional adult with acute kidney injury has worsening fluid overload and unstable blood pressure. The team is comparing continuous and intermittent renal replacement approaches. Which interpretation best supports the nurse’s communication to the team?","options":["CRRT may better support gradual fluid and solute control when instability makes rapid shifts harder to tolerate.","Intermittent hemodialysis is generally preferable because rapid fluid and solute removal is gentler during instability.","The modality should be selected from the kidney injury diagnosis alone, without considering hemodynamic status."],"feedback":["This fits the described instability and fluid overload: CRRT allows more controlled fluid management and gradual solute removal.","This reverses the distinction; intermittent hemodialysis can remove substances more rapidly, while CRRT is useful when gradual control is needed.","Hemodynamic status is a key factor in choosing a renal replacement approach, so the diagnosis alone is insufficient."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient receiving CRRT develops a high venous pressure alarm, and the displayed treatment has been interrupted. The patient’s condition has not yet changed. What is the nurse’s priority response?","options":["Assess the patient and visible circuit or access concerns, follow the local alarm protocol, and promptly notify the CRRT team.","Increase the fluid removal goal so the planned treatment can be recovered after the alarm clears.","Assume the alarm is harmless because the patient appears unchanged and wait for the next routine review."],"feedback":["A high venous pressure alarm can signal kinking, clotting, or flow obstruction; prompt assessment and team communication are appropriate without independently changing treatment.","Changing a prescribed fluid goal is not a nursing response to an alarm, and the cause of the interruption needs evaluation.","An unchanged appearance does not exclude a circuit-flow problem or loss of delivered therapy; the alarm and interruption warrant attention."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient with acute kidney injury and cerebral edema needs renal replacement therapy. The team is weighing gradual solute removal against a faster treatment approach. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate to communicate?","options":["CRRT can be advantageous because gradual removal may help limit rapid osmotic changes associated with increased intracranial pressure.","Intermittent hemodialysis is preferred because the fastest possible solute shift best protects against intracranial pressure increases.","The presence of cerebral edema rules out renal replacement therapy until the neurologic findings resolve."],"feedback":["The supplied material identifies gradual removal and controlled mean arterial pressure adjustment as reasons CRRT is favored in cerebral edema.","This reverses the relevant concern: rapid changes in osmotically active substances may increase intracranial pressure.","Cerebral edema is described as a setting in which CRRT may be preferred, not as a reason to rule out renal replacement therapy."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient’s CRRT prescription remains unchanged after imaging and filter-clotting interruptions. The machine is now running at the prescribed setting. Which finding should the nurse communicate when evaluating whether treatment was adequate?","options":["Delivered therapy may be below the prescribed amount because downtime can reduce the treatment actually received.","The current displayed setting establishes that the full prescribed treatment was delivered despite earlier interruptions.","Filter downtime affects fluid balance only and does not meaningfully affect delivered solute clearance."],"feedback":["The prescribed amount does not necessarily equal the delivered amount; interruptions, including imaging and filter clotting, can reduce delivered therapy.","A current setting does not account for treatment missed during prior downtime, so delivered therapy still needs consideration.","Downtime can reduce delivered dialysis dose and therefore can affect solute clearance, not only fluid balance."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556028/"},{"title":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK556028/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Gradual clearance","detail":"CRRT removes water and solutes gradually, supporting fluid control when hemodynamic instability makes rapid shifts difficult to tolerate."},{"label":"Instability favors CRRT","detail":"Acute kidney injury with fluid overload and blood-pressure instability can favor controlled CRRT over rapid fluid shifts."},{"label":"Report therapy gaps","detail":"Communicate instability, abnormal trends, access concerns, alarms, and interruptions to the responsible clinician and CRRT team; follow prescribed settings."},{"label":"Delivered therapy check","detail":"Reassess hemodynamic tolerance, fluid balance, laboratory trends, access, and circuit function; escalate recurrent alarms or worsening abnormalities promptly."}]},{"id":"s044","unit":"gi","title":"Recognizing gastrointestinal bleeding","objective":"Recognize findings that suggest gastrointestinal bleeding, identify cues of possible hemodynamic compromise, and communicate priorities promptly within nursing scope.","concept":"Gastrointestinal bleeding may arise anywhere from the mouth to the anus, and its appearance depends partly on location and transit time. Vomited red blood or coffee-ground material usually points to an upper source; black, tarry stool often does too. Red blood passed rectally commonly suggests a lower source, but brisk upper bleeding can also cause it. Recognizing these patterns alongside perfusion changes helps nurses communicate urgency without assuming that appearance alone confirms the source.","recognize":"Ask what the blood looked like, whether it was vomited or passed, and whether it was mixed with stool or only on its surface. Check for weakness, dizziness, fainting, confusion, pallor, sweating, rapid pulse, low blood pressure, and reduced urine output. Iron or bismuth can darken stool without bleeding; consider other sources such as nose, mouth, or respiratory bleeding.","respond":"Start with immediate assessment of airway, breathing, circulation, vital signs, mental status, and visible bleeding. For concerning instability or substantial ongoing bleeding, promptly notify the responsible clinician and activate the facility’s escalation process. Maintain or obtain access and prepare for ordered evaluation or treatment under local protocols. Give a clear report of observed findings, trends, medications, and relevant liver or bleeding history; do not delay escalation to identify the exact source.","reassess":"After actions or handoff, reassess vital signs, mental status, skin perfusion, urine output, and whether bleeding continues or recurs. Track ordered laboratory results and trends, remembering that early hemoglobin values may not reflect the full acute loss. Worsening hypotension, tachycardia, confusion, fainting, or ongoing bleeding warrants prompt escalation.","trap":"A normal-looking initial hemoglobin is like a snapshot taken before the whole change is visible; it does not by itself exclude acute blood loss. Black stool is also not automatically melena, just as a dark stain does not identify what caused it: iron and bismuth can darken stool without bleeding.","caseStory":"A fictional adult reports passing black, sticky, tar-like stool and feeling faint when standing. The nurse notes pallor, sweating, and a rapid pulse; the patient also takes an anticoagulant. The decision is whether to treat this as a potentially significant bleed and escalate now or wait for a test to confirm the source.","caseReason":"The stool description is consistent with melena, which commonly indicates an upper gastrointestinal source, though it does not prove one. Faintness, pallor, sweating, and a rapid pulse raise concern for compromised circulation, and anticoagulant use adds relevant risk. Prompt escalation and focused reassessment take priority over waiting for confirmation.","teachback":"When a patient has blood loss cues but the source or initial laboratory results are uncertain, what findings will you communicate first, and what changes would make you escalate again?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient vomits dark, granular material resembling coffee grounds and later passes black, sticky stool. The patient is pale and reports dizziness. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pattern suggests upper gastrointestinal bleeding, with the dizziness and pallor increasing concern about its clinical significance.","The pattern most strongly suggests bleeding from the lower bowel because stool color is more useful than vomit appearance.","The dark material is most likely caused by iron or bismuth, so the dizziness can be assessed separately from possible bleeding."],"feedback":["Coffee-ground vomit and melena commonly indicate an upper gastrointestinal source; pallor and dizziness are important associated cues to report.","Black, tarry stool commonly indicates an upper source, and coffee-ground emesis also supports that interpretation. A brisk upper bleed can cause red rectal blood, but that is not the pattern described.","Iron and bismuth can darken stool, but they do not explain coffee-ground emesis. The accompanying symptoms should not be separated from possible blood loss."]},{"stem":"A patient with ongoing rectal bleeding becomes confused, clammy, and hypotensive, with a rapid pulse. Which nursing action is the priority?","options":["Assess immediate airway and circulation, promptly activate the local escalation process, and communicate the deterioration to the responsible team.","Collect a detailed history of stool characteristics and medication use before contacting the responsible team.","Arrange routine evaluation for a lower bowel source because rectal bleeding usually originates below the upper gastrointestinal tract."],"feedback":["Confusion, clammy skin, hypotension, and tachycardia are concerning for poor perfusion. Immediate assessment and escalation take priority over locating the source.","A focused history is useful, but delaying escalation to complete it is unsafe when the patient shows signs of possible shock.","Rectal bleeding often suggests a lower source, but brisk upper bleeding can also cause it. The signs of deterioration, not presumed location, set the priority."]},{"stem":"A patient with known chronic liver disease reports vomiting red blood. The patient is awake and speaking, but appears pale. Which nursing interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["Treat the report as possible significant upper gastrointestinal bleeding, promptly notify the care team, and report the liver history and current findings.","Assume the bleeding is from a lower source unless black stool is also present, and continue routine observation.","Attribute the blood to liver disease alone and wait for diagnostic testing before communicating the concern."],"feedback":["Vomiting red blood indicates an upper source, and chronic liver disease is relevant history when communicating possible bleeding. Prompt team notification is appropriate even if the patient is speaking.","Hematemesis indicates an upper source; black stool is not required. Waiting for another sign could delay recognition and care.","Liver disease can increase bleeding risk and may be associated with varices, but it does not establish the exact cause. The observed bleeding warrants prompt communication."]},{"stem":"A patient is being monitored after a suspected acute gastrointestinal bleed. The first hemoglobin result is within the laboratory reference range, but the patient remains tachycardic and becomes increasingly weak and clammy. What should the nurse do?","options":["Reassess perfusion and vital signs, communicate the worsening findings promptly, and avoid using the single hemoglobin result to dismiss concern.","Reassure the patient that the normal hemoglobin makes significant acute blood loss unlikely, then repeat observations at the routine interval.","Focus on checking stool color because it can determine whether the current weakness is related to bleeding."],"feedback":["Hemoglobin and hematocrit can lag behind clinical findings in acute bleeding. Worsening weakness, tachycardia, and clamminess warrant reassessment and prompt escalation.","A single early hemoglobin result does not reliably exclude acute blood loss. The worsening clinical signs should not wait for routine reassessment.","Stool appearance can help characterize bleeding, but it cannot determine perfusion status or explain away worsening signs. Reassess the patient and escalate the change."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Gastrointestinal Bleeding - Gastroenterology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/overview-of-gastrointestinal-bleeding"},{"title":"Evaluation and treatment of gastrointestinal bleeding in ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11141076/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Bleeding appearance varies","detail":"Blood may appear as hematemesis, coffee-ground material, black tarry stool, or rectal red blood; brisk upper bleeding can also appear red."},{"label":"Perfusion warning signs","detail":"Black tarry stool with faintness, pallor, sweating, or rapid pulse raises concern for significant bleeding and compromised circulation."},{"label":"Escalate active bleeding","detail":"Assess airway, breathing, circulation, vital signs, and mental status; promptly notify the clinician and activate facility escalation for instability or substantial bleeding."},{"label":"Ongoing blood loss","detail":"Reassess vital signs, perfusion, mental status, urine output, and recurrent bleeding; worsening hypotension, confusion, or continued bleeding warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s045","unit":"gi","title":"Liver failure and portal hypertension","objective":"Recognize and escalate suspected variceal hemorrhage in an adult with portal hypertension while supporting stabilization and confirming the bleeding source with the care team.","concept":"Portal hypertension, most often related to cirrhosis, can create enlarged collateral veins in the lower esophagus and upper stomach. These varices may rupture and cause sudden, severe bleeding, while liver-related clotting problems can worsen blood loss. Because people with cirrhosis can also bleed from nonvariceal causes, identifying a likely variceal bleed guides urgent care, but endoscopy is needed to establish the source and provide treatment.","recognize":"Suspect variceal hemorrhage when a person with cirrhosis develops sudden, painless upper gastrointestinal bleeding, such as hematemesis, especially with signs of shock. Gastric variceal bleeding may be less abrupt. Do not assume every bleed in cirrhosis comes from varices: peptic ulcer and other nonvariceal causes remain possible, so endoscopy is important even when varices are already known.","respond":"Prioritize prompt team notification, airway and circulation support, and fluid or blood product resuscitation as directed by the treating team and local protocols. Prepare for urgent endoscopy and ordered IV octreotide; antibiotic prophylaxis may be ordered for a person with cirrhosis and gastrointestinal bleeding. Communicate bleeding, hemodynamic changes, relevant laboratory results, and current medicines. Avoid assuming the source or giving unnecessary fluid volume.","reassess":"After interventions, reassess airway protection, vital signs, perfusion, visible bleeding, mental status, and response to ordered fluids or transfusion. Report recurrent bleeding, worsening shock, or new confusion promptly; gastrointestinal bleeding can precipitate encephalopathy when hepatic function is impaired.","trap":"Known varices are a warning sign, not proof that varices caused this episode; endoscopy also checks for other sources such as ulcers. Think of the varices as one possible leak in a system with several vulnerable points, rather than assuming the first suspected source is confirmed.","caseStory":"A fictional adult with cirrhosis suddenly vomits a large amount of blood and becomes pale and light-headed. The chart notes previously identified esophageal varices, but the bleeding source has not been confirmed. The nurse must decide what to communicate and prepare for while the team evaluates the patient.","caseReason":"The abrupt, painless upper gastrointestinal bleed in a person with cirrhosis makes variceal hemorrhage an urgent concern, with possible hypovolemia requiring prompt team-led stabilization. Prior varices do not rule out another source, so endoscopy is important for diagnosis and treatment. The nurse should communicate the clinical changes and support ordered resuscitation and preparation for endoscopy.","teachback":"In a person with cirrhosis and sudden upper gastrointestinal bleeding, how would you explain why you would prepare for variceal hemorrhage while still supporting evaluation for other bleeding sources?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with cirrhosis develops sudden, painless hematemesis. The patient has known esophageal varices, but no source has been confirmed. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication with the team?","options":["Variceal hemorrhage is a serious possibility, but endoscopy is needed to confirm the source and assess for alternatives.","Known varices establish the source, so evaluation for another cause is unnecessary.","The painless presentation makes a variceal source unlikely and points away from an upper gastrointestinal bleed."],"feedback":["This interpretation recognizes the urgency of a likely variceal bleed without treating history alone as proof; endoscopy can also identify nonvariceal causes.","This is misleading because people with cirrhosis may have an upper gastrointestinal bleed from a nonvariceal source, including an ulcer.","This is misleading because sudden, painless upper gastrointestinal bleeding is a typical presentation of variceal hemorrhage."]},{"stem":"An adult with cirrhosis is actively vomiting blood, is becoming drowsy, and has signs of poor perfusion. Which nursing priority best supports immediate care?","options":["Promptly alert the team, support airway and circulation, and prepare for ordered resuscitation and urgent endoscopy.","Wait for endoscopy to identify the source before reporting changes or preparing for resuscitation.","Focus first on correcting laboratory abnormalities, postponing airway and circulation support until results are available."],"feedback":["This is the priority because active bleeding with drowsiness and poor perfusion requires prompt team-led airway and circulation support while diagnostic and endoscopic care is arranged.","This delays response to possible hemorrhagic shock; stabilization should not wait for endoscopic confirmation.","This is misleading because laboratory evaluation matters, but it should not postpone immediate attention to airway, perfusion, and active blood loss."]},{"stem":"After endoscopic treatment and ordered medication, a fictional adult with cirrhosis has another episode of substantial hematemesis and worsening hypotension. What should the nurse communicate as the next concern?","options":["Bleeding may be continuing or recurring despite initial measures, requiring urgent team escalation and consideration of further intervention.","The recurrence is expected after endoscopy, so routine observation is sufficient unless laboratory results change.","A nonvariceal source is now established, so further evaluation for portal hypertensive bleeding is unnecessary."],"feedback":["This response recognizes recurrent bleeding and worsening hemodynamics as reasons for urgent escalation; a TIPS procedure may be considered by the treating team if bleeding persists or recurs despite initial measures.","This is misleading because recurrent substantial bleeding with hypotension is not a reason to rely on routine observation.","This is misleading because recurrence does not establish a nonvariceal source or eliminate concern for variceal bleeding."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult with cirrhosis and known varices has gastrointestinal bleeding. The patient appears to stop bleeding after an episode, and a nasogastric tube has been passed without a new visible bleed. Which reassessment and interpretation are most appropriate?","options":["Continue assessing vital signs, perfusion, visible bleeding, and mental status; report deterioration or recurrence, since neither apparent cessation nor tube passage settles the source.","Consider the absence of bleeding after tube passage evidence that varices have ruptured and the hemorrhage is now controlled.","End ongoing reassessment once visible bleeding stops, because this makes a nonvariceal source unlikely."],"feedback":["This is appropriate because bleeding can recur and may be associated with shock or encephalopathy; nasogastric tube passage has not been shown to trigger bleeding, and endoscopy is still important to identify the source.","This is misleading because tube passage does not establish that a varix ruptured or that bleeding is controlled.","This is misleading because apparent cessation does not confirm the source or remove the need to monitor for recurrent bleeding and deterioration."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices"},{"title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices?ruleredirectid=746"}],"frames":[{"label":"Variceal rupture risk","detail":"Portal hypertension can enlarge esophageal or gastric collateral veins, which may rupture and cause sudden severe bleeding in cirrhosis."},{"label":"Abrupt hematemesis","detail":"Sudden painless vomiting of blood with pallor or light-headedness in cirrhosis raises concern for variceal hemorrhage, though source remains unconfirmed."},{"label":"Prepare urgent support","detail":"Notify the team, support airway and circulation, communicate changes, and prepare for urgent endoscopy and ordered therapies under local protocols."},{"label":"Recurrent bleeding","detail":"Reassess airway, perfusion, vital signs, mental status, and visible bleeding; recurrent hemorrhage, worsening shock, or new confusion needs prompt reporting."}]},{"id":"s046","unit":"gi","title":"Acute pancreatitis","objective":"Use clinical cues and trends to recognize acute pancreatitis, prioritize supportive nursing care, and escalate signs of worsening organ dysfunction.","concept":"Acute pancreatitis begins when digestive enzymes activate inside pancreatic cells, injuring the gland and triggering inflammation, edema, and sometimes necrosis. Inflammatory mediators can increase capillary leak and reduce vascular tone, shifting fluid out of circulation and affecting organs such as the lungs and kidneys. Severity depends not only on pancreatic injury but also on complications and whether organ failure is transient or persists.","recognize":"Suspect pancreatitis with steady, often severe upper abdominal pain, commonly epigastric or left-sided and sometimes radiating to the back, with nausea or vomiting. Gallstones and alcohol are common causes, but other triggers exist. Diagnosis uses at least two of compatible pain, pancreatic enzymes above three times the upper limit of normal, or characteristic imaging; enzyme elevation alone is not definitive.","respond":"Prioritize a focused assessment of breathing, circulation, pain, mental status, and evolving abdominal findings, then promptly communicate concerning changes to the treating team. Support prescribed fluid therapy, analgesia, and nutrition according to orders and local protocols, and coordinate ordered investigations. Avoid assuming the cause from history alone or treating an enzyme result as proof without the broader clinical picture.","reassess":"After team-directed supportive care, reassess vital signs, oxygenation, mental status, pain, abdominal findings, and available renal and inflammatory trends, including BUN when measured. Escalate promptly for worsening breathing, falling blood pressure, altered alertness, persistent signs of poor perfusion, or deterioration after initial stabilization.","trap":"A high pancreatic enzyme result is like one warning light on a dashboard: it matters, but it does not diagnose the whole problem by itself. Conversely, less pain after analgesia does not establish that systemic inflammation or organ dysfunction is improving.","caseStory":"A patient arrives with sudden, steady epigastric pain radiating to the back and repeated vomiting; the history includes known gallstones. Lipase is more than three times the assay upper limit, while imaging has not yet been performed. The nurse must decide whether the findings support pancreatitis and what to communicate first.","caseReason":"Compatible pain plus the marked lipase elevation meet two diagnostic features, so imaging is not required to satisfy the stated diagnostic criteria. The nurse should communicate the supported concern and continue assessing for systemic effects, rather than waiting for imaging or treating the enzyme value as a measure of severity.","teachback":"When a patient’s abdominal pain improves after analgesia but breathing or circulation worsens, what findings would you reassess and communicate, and why does pain relief alone not establish improvement?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient has steady severe epigastric pain radiating to the back and vomiting. Lipase is above three times the assay upper limit, and imaging has not been done. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pain and enzyme result together satisfy two diagnostic features of acute pancreatitis.","Acute pancreatitis cannot be supported until imaging confirms pancreatic inflammation.","The enzyme elevation alone proves pancreatitis regardless of the patient’s symptoms."],"feedback":["Compatible pain and an enzyme level above the stated threshold provide two of the diagnostic features; imaging is not needed to meet that criterion.","Imaging is one diagnostic feature, but the compatible pain and enzyme result already provide two.","An enzyme result alone is insufficient because elevations can occur in other conditions; interpretation requires the broader clinical picture."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized patient with acute pancreatitis develops increasing respiratory effort, falling blood pressure, and new confusion. Which nursing action is the priority?","options":["Promptly escalate the deterioration to the treating team while assessing and communicating current vital signs and oxygenation.","Continue routine observation because these changes can be expected with abdominal pain.","Focus on the next scheduled enzyme result before reporting the change in condition."],"feedback":["Worsening breathing and circulation with altered mental status may signal systemic effects or organ dysfunction and requires prompt team communication.","Routine observation could delay response to potentially serious deterioration.","Enzyme trends do not take priority over immediate changes in breathing, circulation, and mental status."]},{"stem":"A patient develops persistent upper abdominal pain and vomiting after ERCP. The lipase is above three times the assay upper limit, and the patient has no known gallstones or alcohol history. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Acute pancreatitis is supported by the compatible symptoms and enzyme result, and the recent procedure is a possible context.","Pancreatitis is unlikely because the patient lacks the two most common causes.","The procedure history alone establishes pancreatitis, so the symptoms and enzyme result are not relevant."],"feedback":["Compatible symptoms plus the enzyme result meet two diagnostic features, and pancreatitis can occur after ERCP.","Gallstones and alcohol are common causes, but other causes include ERCP; their absence does not rule out pancreatitis.","ERCP is a recognized context for pancreatitis, but the diagnosis still depends on the clinical criteria rather than history alone."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving team-directed supportive care reports less pain, but repeat assessment shows persistent elevation in BUN despite resuscitation. Which nursing interpretation is best?","options":["Communicate the persistent BUN elevation because it is associated with increased morbidity and mortality despite apparent pain relief.","Conclude the episode is improving because pain has decreased, and defer further assessment.","Treat the BUN result as diagnostic proof of pancreatic necrosis."],"feedback":["Persistent BUN elevation despite resuscitation is an adverse trend, and improved pain does not rule out ongoing systemic risk.","Pain relief alone does not establish improvement; the BUN trend warrants communication and continued assessment.","BUN is associated with risk and clinical course but does not by itself diagnose pancreatic necrosis."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/pancreatitis/acute-pancreatitis"},{"title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Gastrointestinal Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/pancreatitis/acute-pancreatitis?ruleredirectid=741"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pancreatic Injury","detail":"Digestive enzymes activate within pancreatic cells, causing gland injury, inflammation, edema, and sometimes necrosis."},{"label":"Diagnostic Features","detail":"Steady epigastric pain radiating to the back plus lipase above three times normal meets two diagnostic features, even before imaging."},{"label":"Communicate Concern","detail":"Promptly communicate the supported concern while assessing breathing, circulation, mental status, pain, and evolving abdominal findings."},{"label":"Track Deterioration","detail":"Recheck oxygenation, vital signs, mental status, pain, and renal trends; worsening breathing, hypotension, or poor perfusion needs prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s047","unit":"gi","title":"Obstruction, ischemia and perforation","objective":"Use bedside patterns and changes in abdominal findings to recognize possible obstruction, intestinal ischemia, or perforation and promptly communicate the need for urgent evaluation.","concept":"Obstruction is suggested by abdominal pain with vomiting and obstipation, often with distension and high-pitched or absent bowel sounds. Intestinal ischemia threatens bowel when blood flow is interrupted or reduced; early severe pain may greatly exceed examination findings, while later tissue injury can produce peritonitis. Perforation can also cause peritonitis. These conditions may progress to shock, so recognizing a changing pattern matters more than waiting for a single test result.","recognize":"With obstruction, look for pain, vomiting, inability to pass stool, distension, and altered bowel sounds. Ischemia is a concern when severe, often diffuse pain contrasts with a soft abdomen and little tenderness, especially with relevant vascular or low-flow risks. Guarding, rigidity, marked tenderness, or worsening pain suggests peritoneal irritation, which can occur with advanced ischemia or perforation and warrants urgent escalation.","respond":"Treat a concerning pattern as time-sensitive: promptly notify the responsible clinician and activate the local urgent escalation pathway, communicating onset, pain severity, bowel symptoms, risk factors, vital signs, and examination changes. Prepare to support urgent evaluation and follow orders or local protocols, including resuscitation measures if directed. Do not reassure based on a soft early abdomen or wait for lactate or imaging to become abnormal when concern is high.","reassess":"After actions taken under orders or local protocols, reassess pain, abdominal tenderness and distension, bowel sounds, stool or vomiting, vital signs, and overall appearance. Report worsening pain, new guarding or rigidity, hemodynamic deterioration, or other signs of peritonitis immediately. A normal or nonspecific early laboratory result does not rule out evolving ischemia.","trap":"A soft abdomen early in suspected ischemia is like a quiet alarm, not proof that danger is absent. The contrast between severe pain and minimal early findings can be a clue; peritoneal signs may appear later as injury advances.","caseStory":"An older adult with atrial fibrillation develops sudden, severe diffuse abdominal pain and nausea. The abdomen is soft with little tenderness, and the patient looks distressed; the initial lactate is not elevated. The nurse must decide whether to await a more abnormal test or promptly escalate concern for intestinal ischemia.","caseReason":"The severe pain with minimal early physical findings is a concerning ischemia pattern, and atrial fibrillation is a risk factor for arterial embolism. Lactate may rise later and is nonspecific, so the nurse should promptly communicate the concern and support urgent evaluation rather than wait for a laboratory change.","teachback":"When severe abdominal pain seems disproportionate to the examination, what findings and changes would you communicate to distinguish possible early ischemia from obstruction or developing peritonitis?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a history of atrial fibrillation reports abrupt, severe abdominal pain. The abdomen is soft with little tenderness, and the patient appears distressed. Which interpretation best fits this combination?","options":["Early intestinal ischemia is a concern because severe pain can precede prominent abdominal findings.","Bowel obstruction is most likely because severe pain alone establishes impaired passage.","Perforation is unlikely because the patient has not yet developed abdominal rigidity."],"feedback":["This pattern is characteristic of possible early mesenteric ischemia, and atrial fibrillation raises concern for an arterial embolic cause.","Obstruction is more strongly suggested by pain accompanied by vomiting, obstipation, or distension; pain alone does not establish it.","A soft abdomen does not exclude evolving ischemia or another urgent abdominal process; peritoneal signs may develop later."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with abdominal pain now has marked tenderness, guarding, and rigidity after earlier severe pain with few examination findings. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Escalate immediately to the responsible clinician and urgent pathway, reporting the new peritoneal findings.","Continue routine observation until bowel sounds become absent or a laboratory marker rises.","Request additional routine imaging before communicating the change in examination."],"feedback":["New peritoneal signs in a patient with a concerning abdominal course warrant immediate escalation and urgent evaluation.","Waiting for another sign or marker risks delay; clinical deterioration should be communicated promptly.","Imaging can be part of evaluation, but the new peritoneal findings call for immediate communication rather than delaying escalation."]},{"stem":"An adult recovering from cardiopulmonary bypass develops new severe abdominal pain. The abdomen remains soft, with little tenderness, and the patient has required support for a low-flow state. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Consider nonocclusive intestinal ischemia and promptly report the pain and low-flow context.","Favor uncomplicated obstruction because a soft abdomen makes impaired intestinal perfusion unlikely.","Consider perforation excluded unless the patient first develops a rigid abdomen."],"feedback":["Low-flow states, including cardiopulmonary bypass, can be associated with nonocclusive ischemia, which may initially cause severe pain with minimal findings.","A soft abdomen does not rule out ischemia, and obstruction is better supported by symptoms such as vomiting, obstipation, and distension.","Peritoneal signs may develop as injury progresses; their absence does not safely exclude an evolving emergency."]},{"stem":"An adult with abdominal pain and vomiting is being evaluated for possible obstruction. After ordered supportive care, the patient reports worse pain; the abdomen is now more tender with guarding, although an early lactate result is not elevated. What should the nurse do?","options":["Promptly report the worsening pain and new guarding, noting that the early lactate does not exclude serious progression.","Continue the current plan without escalation because the lactate is not elevated.","Attribute the change to obstruction alone and wait for bowel sounds to disappear before reporting it."],"feedback":["Worsening pain and new guarding suggest peritoneal irritation and require prompt escalation; early serum markers can be normal or nonspecific.","An early lactate result cannot reliably exclude evolving ischemia or another urgent abdominal condition.","Obstruction may cause pain and vomiting, but new guarding is a significant change that should be communicated without waiting for absent bowel sounds."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia"},{"title":"Acute Abdomen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459328/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Threatened Bowel","detail":"Reduced intestinal blood flow threatens bowel; later tissue injury or perforation can trigger peritonitis and shock."},{"label":"Severe Pain Pattern","detail":"Severe diffuse pain with a soft abdomen, especially with atrial fibrillation, raises concern for ischemia; normal early lactate does not exclude it."},{"label":"Urgent Escalation","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician through the local urgent pathway, sharing pain, bowel symptoms, risk factors, vital signs, and examination changes."},{"label":"Watch For Peritonitis","detail":"Reassess pain, tenderness, distension, vital signs, and overall appearance; new guarding, rigidity, or hemodynamic decline needs immediate reporting."}]},{"id":"s048","unit":"gi","title":"Abdominal compartment syndrome","objective":"Recognize possible adult abdominal compartment syndrome, connect pressure-related organ changes to clinical cues, and coordinate timely measurement, escalation, and reassessment within nursing scope.","concept":"Abdominal compartment syndrome occurs when sustained abdominal pressure compromises organ function. Pressure can limit venous return and cardiac output, elevate the diaphragm and impair ventilation, reduce renal and intestinal perfusion, and affect other systems. In adults, the syndrome is defined by sustained intra-abdominal pressure above 20 mm Hg together with new organ dysfunction; pressure elevation without organ dysfunction is not, by itself, the syndrome. Early recognition matters because dysfunction may improve with timely management.","recognize":"Suspect the syndrome in a critically ill adult with risk factors such as major abdominal surgery, trauma, bleeding, sepsis, or large-volume resuscitation, especially when abdominal distension accompanies worsening ventilation, hypotension, or falling urine output. Examination alone cannot reliably confirm or exclude it. Trend objective findings and distinguish pressure-related deterioration from other possible causes of organ failure; bladder pressure measurement is the accepted practical method.","respond":"Treat a concerning pattern as time-sensitive: promptly notify the responsible clinician and communicate risk factors, trends, and organ changes using the local escalation process. Assist with ordered intra-abdominal pressure monitoring according to unit protocol and role, and coordinate with the critical-care team. Avoid relying on abdominal palpation or imaging alone, delaying escalation while waiting for a dramatic examination finding, or independently initiating treatment outside nursing scope.","reassess":"After an ordered intervention, trend pressure measurements taken under standardized conditions alongside urine output, hemodynamics, oxygenation, ventilation pressures, and abdominal findings. Report persistent or worsening organ dysfunction, rising pressure, or lack of improvement promptly; a single improved measurement does not establish that the clinical problem has resolved.","trap":"A tense or distended abdomen is a warning sign, not a pressure gauge, and a less dramatic examination does not rule out the syndrome. Think of pressure monitoring as checking a constrained room's actual load: appearance alone cannot tell whether the occupants' circulation and breathing are being compromised.","caseStory":"An adult returns to intensive care after damage-control abdominal surgery and substantial fluid resuscitation. Over several hours, urine output falls, ventilator pressures rise, and the abdomen appears increasingly distended; the patient also becomes more hemodynamically unstable. The nurse must decide whether to attribute the changes to routine postoperative recovery or promptly escalate concern and support objective pressure assessment.","caseReason":"The combination of major risk factors and worsening renal, respiratory, and circulatory findings raises concern for pressure-related organ dysfunction, though other causes must also be considered. Examination cannot establish the diagnosis, so the nurse should promptly communicate the trend and facilitate ordered bladder pressure measurement under protocol. The adult diagnostic definition requires sustained pressure elevation together with new organ dysfunction.","teachback":"In a critically ill adult with abdominal distension and worsening organ function, what findings would you report, how would you support objective assessment, and what would you continue to reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in intensive care after abdominal trauma and large-volume resuscitation has repeated bladder pressure measurements above 20 mm Hg taken according to unit protocol. Urine output is falling and ventilator pressures are rising compared with earlier values. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The sustained pressure elevation with new organ changes raises concern for abdominal compartment syndrome and requires prompt team escalation.","The pressure readings establish intra-abdominal hypertension, but the organ changes should be treated as unrelated until imaging confirms a cause.","The findings are most consistent with expected effects of trauma resuscitation, so pressure should be rechecked only if the abdomen becomes more tense."],"feedback":["This is the best interpretation because the adult definition combines sustained pressure elevation above 20 mm Hg with new organ dysfunction. The pressure and clinical trends together warrant timely communication and assessment.","This separates the pressure finding from the new organ changes without support; the combination is precisely why the syndrome should be considered. Imaging is not the standard method for confirming it.","This risks delaying recognition: physical examination is unreliable, and trauma with substantial resuscitation is a risk context. Worsening organ findings should prompt escalation rather than waiting for a more dramatic abdomen."]},{"stem":"A critically ill adult with sepsis and substantial fluid resuscitation develops increasing abdominal distension, new hypotension, and declining urine output. No intra-abdominal pressure has been documented. Which nursing response is the highest priority?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician, communicate the risk factors and organ changes, and support ordered pressure measurement under local protocol.","Request abdominal imaging first, because it is needed to establish the diagnosis before contacting the critical-care team.","Continue routine intake and output monitoring and wait for abdominal palpation to show marked rigidity before escalating."],"feedback":["This response addresses a concerning pattern promptly and supports objective assessment within nursing scope. Bladder pressure monitoring is the accepted practical measurement approach when indicated.","Imaging may show findings that raise suspicion, but it is not standard practice for diagnosing the syndrome. Waiting for imaging before escalation could delay assessment of evolving organ dysfunction.","Palpation is not reliable for identifying or excluding elevated pressure. The risk factors and changes in perfusion already warrant timely communication and further assessment."]},{"stem":"An adult with severe pancreatitis has not had abdominal surgery but has received substantial fluid resuscitation during critical illness. The abdomen is distending, urine output is declining, and ventilation is becoming more difficult. Which nursing interpretation best transfers the principles of abdominal compartment syndrome to this context?","options":["A non-abdominal critical illness and fluid-related capillary leak can still raise pressure, so the organ changes warrant escalation and objective assessment.","Without abdominal surgery or trauma, the pressure problem is unlikely to be relevant, so the changes should be attributed to pancreatitis alone.","Because the fluid resuscitation was for a medical illness, abdominal pressure measurement is unnecessary unless imaging shows a large fluid collection."],"feedback":["This applies the concept to a secondary cause: critical illness and substantial fluid resuscitation can contribute to rising pressure even without abdominal surgery. The associated organ changes make timely assessment important.","The absence of surgery or trauma does not exclude the syndrome. Secondary causes include conditions outside the abdomen and fluid-related capillary leak.","Imaging is not required before considering pressure measurement, and a visible collection is not the only mechanism. The clinical risk pattern should be communicated and assessed objectively."]},{"stem":"After an ordered intervention for suspected abdominal compartment syndrome, an adult's latest bladder pressure is lower than the prior measurement. However, urine output remains low and ventilator pressures remain elevated; the abdomen feels less tense. Which reassessment is most appropriate?","options":["Continue trending pressure and organ-function findings, and promptly report persistent dysfunction despite the lower reading and softer examination.","Consider the syndrome resolved because both the pressure trend and abdominal examination have improved, then return to routine observations.","Use the softer abdominal examination as confirmation that organ function should recover without further communication unless pressure rises again."],"feedback":["This is appropriate because a single improved measurement or less tense abdomen does not establish resolution. Persistent renal and respiratory changes need continued reassessment and prompt communication.","This overinterprets partial improvement; physical examination is unreliable, and ongoing organ dysfunction remains concerning. Continued trending and team communication are warranted.","This relies on an insensitive examination finding and delays communication about persistent dysfunction. Clinical response should be judged using trends in pressure and affected organ systems."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Abdominal Compartment Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430932/"},{"title":"Intra-abdominal hypertension/abdominal compartment syndrome of ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12273658/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pressure Risk","detail":"Major abdominal surgery and substantial fluid resuscitation can precede sustained abdominal pressure that compromises organ function."},{"label":"Organ Changes","detail":"Falling urine output, rising ventilator pressures, distension, and instability suggest pressure-related dysfunction, though other causes remain possible."},{"label":"Measure And Escalate","detail":"Promptly alert the responsible clinician and facilitate ordered bladder-pressure measurement under unit protocol; examination alone cannot confirm the syndrome."},{"label":"Trend Organ Function","detail":"Trend standardized pressure with urine output, hemodynamics, and ventilation; persistent organ dysfunction or rising pressure requires prompt reporting."}]},{"id":"s049","unit":"gi","title":"Abdominal trauma","objective":"Recognize high-risk abdominal trauma cues and communicate priorities for timely team evaluation and reassessment.","concept":"Blunt force or penetrating injury can damage the abdominal wall, solid organs, hollow organs, or blood vessels. Bleeding may remain internal and cause shock, while leakage from a hollow organ can cause peritonitis. Injury severity and symptoms vary, and some injuries initially cause little pain. A systematic trauma assessment matters because visible wounds or a reassuring early examination may not reveal injuries that need intervention.","recognize":"Watch for tachycardia, narrow pulse pressure, hypotension, altered sensorium, reduced urine output, abdominal tenderness, distention, or peritoneal signs. Pain and tenderness can be mild or obscured by other injuries, and a seat belt mark is not a reliable rule-in or rule-out finding. Guarding or rebound is concerning; hematuria may point toward renal injury, while retroperitoneal injury can make abdominal findings subtle.","respond":"Support the organized trauma assessment, beginning with airway, breathing, and circulation, and promptly report concerning trends to the trauma team using local escalation pathways. Share the mechanism, examination changes, vital signs, urine findings, and limits to reliable assessment. Under orders and protocols, prepare for indicated imaging or other evaluation. Do not probe penetrating wounds or treat a negative early examination as proof that injury is absent.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess vital signs, mental status, pain and abdominal findings, urine output, and response to resuscitation, then communicate trends rather than isolated values. Escalate promptly for worsening pain, new peritoneal signs, hypotension, tachycardia, reduced urine output, or respiratory deterioration, including after an initially reassuring assessment.","trap":"A quiet abdomen is like a smoke alarm that has not sounded: it does not prove there is no fire, especially when pain is obscured or the patient cannot give a reliable history. A negative FAST also does not exclude every injury, so persistent concern after stabilization still needs team evaluation.","caseStory":"A fictional adult arrives after a vehicle collision with a seat belt mark and a painful leg injury that dominates the initial interview. The patient is initially alert with mild abdominal discomfort, but becomes more tachycardic and less attentive during observation. The nurse must decide whether the modest abdominal findings justify continued concern and escalation.","caseReason":"The mechanism and evolving vital signs raise concern for internal injury even though the initial pain and abdominal examination were modest. A distracting fracture and changing sensorium can make examination less reliable, so the nurse should promptly communicate the trend and support further trauma-team evaluation rather than relying on the early examination.","teachback":"When a trauma patient has a reassuring initial abdominal examination but later develops concerning changes, what information would you reassess and communicate, and why would the early examination not settle the question of injury?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult arrives after blunt torso trauma with a painful arm fracture and only mild abdominal discomfort. The patient becomes increasingly drowsy, and the heart rate rises, although the abdomen remains only mildly tender. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's communication?","options":["The examination may be unreliable, and evolving signs warrant continued concern for intra-abdominal injury.","Mild tenderness makes significant abdominal injury unlikely unless distention or bruising appears.","Drowsiness is more likely from the painful fracture, so abdominal reassessment can wait."],"feedback":["Correct. Other injuries and altered sensorium can obscure abdominal symptoms, while evolving vital signs merit prompt communication and further evaluation.","This overvalues tenderness and visible findings, which can be absent or equivocal despite intra-abdominal injury.","This assumes a cause for the changing sensorium and delays attention to a potentially unreliable abdominal assessment."]},{"stem":"An adult with a penetrating abdominal wound becomes hypotensive and develops guarding and rebound during the initial trauma assessment. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Immediately alert the trauma team and support resuscitation and urgent evaluation under the trauma protocol.","Arrange transport for CT before notifying the team so the injury location can guide the response.","Continue serial abdominal examinations until the patient can describe the pain more clearly."],"feedback":["Correct. Hemodynamic instability and peritoneal signs are high-risk findings that require immediate team attention while resuscitation proceeds.","This delays urgent team evaluation in an unstable patient with peritoneal signs; imaging decisions belong to the treating team.","Serial examinations do not replace urgent escalation when instability and peritoneal signs are already present."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving high-volume resuscitation after severe burns, without a primary abdominal injury, develops reduced urine output, worsening ventilation, and a tense, distended abdomen. Which concern should the nurse communicate promptly?","options":["Abdominal compartment syndrome may be causing organ dysfunction and requires urgent team assessment.","A distended abdomen is expected with fluid administration, so the changes can be observed without escalation.","The combination is most consistent with an isolated urinary problem, so abdominal concerns are secondary."],"feedback":["Correct. High-volume resuscitation and abdominal tissue edema can raise pressure and impair kidney and lung function, even when the initial condition is not abdominal trauma.","This dismisses a concerning combination of abdominal distention and organ dysfunction in a patient at risk.","Reduced urine output can reflect kidney effects of elevated abdominal pressure; the accompanying respiratory and abdominal findings argue against treating it as isolated."]},{"stem":"Several days after blunt abdominal trauma, a patient who had been stable reports sudden worsening abdominal pain and becomes tachycardic and hypotensive. Which interpretation should the nurse escalate?","options":["A delayed hemorrhage, including rupture of a solid-organ hematoma, is possible and needs prompt team assessment.","Pain after several days is more consistent with a resolving hematoma, so routine observation is appropriate.","The change most strongly indicates a late abscess, even without fever or other supporting findings."],"feedback":["Correct. Sudden worsening pain with tachycardia or hypotension after injury raises concern for delayed bleeding, including hematoma rupture.","This assumes spontaneous resolution despite acute deterioration and signs that may indicate hemorrhage.","Abscess is a possible delayed complication, but sudden pain with hypotension and tachycardia particularly warrants concern for bleeding."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Abdominal Trauma - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/abdominal-trauma/overview-of-abdominal-trauma"},{"title":"Blunt Abdominal Trauma - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431087/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Hidden Trauma","detail":"Blunt or penetrating abdominal injury can cause internal bleeding or leakage, even when early pain and examination findings are modest."},{"label":"Changing Vital Signs","detail":"After a collision, increasing tachycardia and reduced attentiveness despite mild abdominal findings raise concern for internal injury."},{"label":"Trauma Team Update","detail":"Support airway, breathing, and circulation assessment; promptly report the mechanism, examination changes, vital signs, urine findings, and assessment limits."},{"label":"Recheck For Shock","detail":"Reassess vital signs, mental status, abdominal findings, and urine output; worsening pain, hypotension, tachycardia, or reduced output needs prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s050","unit":"gi","title":"After gastrointestinal surgery","objective":"Recognize possible gastrointestinal anastomotic leakage, prioritize response by the patient’s stability, and evaluate whether the patient is improving or needs urgent escalation.","concept":"A gastrointestinal anastomosis can leak when its join fails, allowing intestinal contents and bacteria to reach surrounding tissues. The resulting infection may remain contained as inflammation or an abscess, or spread through the abdomen and cause peritonitis and sepsis. Poor blood supply, tension, and technical failure can contribute to leakage. Because a leak may appear after discharge and imaging can be negative despite a leak, assessment depends on both diagnostic findings and the patient’s clinical course.","recognize":"Be alert to fever, unexplained tachycardia, increasing abdominal pain, leukocytosis, hypotension, or signs of an acute abdomen after gastrointestinal surgery. CT may show air or fluid around the join, inflammation, or an abscess, but a negative scan does not always settle the question. A contained collection in a stable patient differs in urgency from worsening signs of diffuse peritonitis or hemodynamic instability.","respond":"First assess the patient’s stability and promptly communicate concerning findings and trends to the surgical team. Follow local protocols and orders for resuscitation, intravenous antibiotics, bowel rest, and diagnostic imaging when appropriate. If the patient remains unstable or has signs of diffuse peritonitis, do not delay escalation for imaging; the surgical team may need to proceed to urgent exploration. Do not independently prescribe or perform procedures.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess vital signs, abdominal pain and tenderness, mental status, and the overall direction of the patient’s condition. Communicate whether signs are improving or worsening. Persistent instability, increasing pain, or signs of diffuse peritonitis warrant urgent escalation even if an earlier scan or another finding seemed reassuring.","trap":"A single reassuring scan is like one still frame from a changing scene: it cannot replace attention to the patient’s course. A leak can be contained or initially hard to see, so worsening clinical signs matter even when imaging is not confirmatory.","caseStory":"On the sixth day after colorectal surgery, a patient develops fever, a rising heart rate, and increasing abdominal pain. CT shows a small collection near the anastomosis, and the patient is currently hemodynamically stable. The nurse must decide whether this is a finding to monitor routinely or one to report promptly for evaluation and a plan.","caseReason":"The symptoms and nearby collection are concerning for a possible contained leak, even though the patient is stable. The nurse should promptly report the findings and support the team’s evaluation and ordered care, while continuing to assess for deterioration that could indicate spreading infection.","teachback":"After gastrointestinal surgery, what changes in a patient’s condition would make you escalate concern for a leak even if an earlier scan did not confirm one?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient on the sixth day after colorectal surgery has a new fever, unexplained tachycardia, and increasing abdominal pain. CT shows a small collection near the anastomosis, and the patient is hemodynamically stable. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next communication?","options":["The findings could represent a contained leak and warrant prompt communication with the surgical team.","The stable blood pressure makes an anastomotic problem unlikely, so routine observation is sufficient.","The collection confirms free perforation, so the nurse should prepare for immediate exploration without further team assessment."],"feedback":["The symptoms and perianastomotic collection are concerning for a possible contained leak; stability informs urgency but does not make the findings routine.","A stable patient can still have a leak. New fever, tachycardia, pain, and a collection warrant communication and continued assessment.","A collection can accompany a contained leak, and the supplied material does not make this finding alone proof of free perforation or an independent nursing decision for surgery."]},{"stem":"A patient shortly after gastrointestinal surgery becomes hypotensive, increasingly tachycardic, and has diffuse abdominal tenderness. A team member suggests obtaining CT before notifying the surgeon. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Escalate immediately to the surgical team and support ordered resuscitation rather than delaying for imaging.","Arrange CT first because imaging is needed before the team can determine whether the patient is unstable.","Continue serial abdominal assessments and report the findings after the next routine review."],"feedback":["Instability and signs of diffuse peritonitis require urgent escalation and resuscitation; imaging should not delay that response.","The patient’s instability is already apparent clinically, and the supplied material advises against delaying exploration for imaging when instability persists.","The worsening signs call for immediate escalation, not waiting for a routine review."]},{"stem":"A patient after gastrectomy has a suspected duodenal stump leak with a localized collection. The patient is stable, and the team is considering conservative care with percutaneous drainage. Which nursing approach best applies the learning from colorectal surgery to this new context?","options":["Support the team’s ordered conservative and drainage plan while closely monitoring for worsening infection or instability.","Treat any postoperative leak as diffuse peritonitis and prioritize preparation for immediate reoperation.","Assume that a localized leak will resolve without intervention and reduce the frequency of assessment."],"feedback":["The gastrectomy review supports conservative care with drainage for stable patients with local collections; close monitoring is important because deterioration may change the plan.","The need for surgery depends on stability, peritonitis, leakage location, and response to care; a localized leak in a stable patient does not automatically require reoperation.","A localized collection may be managed conservatively with drainage, but the patient still needs monitoring and team-directed care."]},{"stem":"A patient with a suspected contained leak is receiving team-directed treatment. The fever is lower than earlier, but the heart rate is rising and abdominal pain is worsening. What is the best reassessment response?","options":["Promptly report the worsening trend because it may indicate that infection is not controlled despite the lower fever.","Continue the current observation plan because improvement in fever outweighs the other changes.","Wait for a repeat scan before reporting, since clinical changes are less reliable than imaging."],"feedback":["A rising heart rate and worsening pain are concerning changes. Reassess and communicate the overall trend rather than relying on one improved sign.","A lower fever does not negate worsening pain and tachycardia; these findings warrant prompt communication and reassessment.","Clinical deterioration should be escalated without waiting for imaging. Imaging can assist evaluation but does not replace attention to the patient’s condition."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Operative Management of Anastomotic Leaks after ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6494610/"},{"title":"Treatment and Prevention of Postoperative Leakage after ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10299500/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Anastomotic Leak","detail":"Failure of a gastrointestinal surgical join can release intestinal contents, causing a contained collection or spreading peritonitis and sepsis."},{"label":"Postoperative Warning","detail":"Fever, rising heart rate, increasing abdominal pain, and a nearby collection raise concern for a possible leak, even when stable."},{"label":"Notify Surgical Team","detail":"Assess stability and promptly communicate concerning findings to the surgical team; follow local protocols and orders for evaluation and care."},{"label":"Watch For Spread","detail":"Reassess vital signs, pain, tenderness, and overall condition; persistent instability, increasing pain, or diffuse peritonitis warrants urgent escalation."}]},{"id":"s051","unit":"gi","title":"Nutrition and refeeding risk","objective":"Recognize refeeding risk in adults receiving renewed nutrition and communicate changes that require timely team assessment.","concept":"During starvation, intracellular electrolyte and vitamin stores become depleted. When nutrition resumes, rising glucose and insulin shift phosphorus and potassium into cells, and magnesium may also fall; thiamine demand increases as glucose is metabolized. Phosphate depletion is a key clue, while fluid and sodium retention can add strain. These changes can impair cardiac, respiratory, muscular, and neurologic function, so trends and symptoms matter together.","recognize":"Consider risk with prolonged poor intake or malnutrition, low body mass, recent unintentional weight loss, alcohol use, or low baseline electrolytes. After nutrition resumes, falling phosphate, potassium, or magnesium and new weakness, breathing difficulty, confusion, edema, or rhythm changes warrant attention. Compare timing, laboratory trends, and clinical findings: fluid overload can also lower measured electrolytes, so do not interpret a result in isolation.","respond":"Before nutrition resumes, share risk factors and baseline electrolyte results with the treating team. Follow local protocols and orders for monitoring, electrolyte replacement, and gradual nutritional advancement; communicate substantial abnormalities or new symptoms promptly. Thiamine may be part of the team’s plan before carbohydrate provision. Do not independently prescribe, alter feeding, or assume that nutrition should proceed unchanged when severe abnormalities are present.","reassess":"After nursing actions, review phosphate, potassium, and magnesium trends alongside vital signs, cardiac rhythm, breathing, strength, mental status, fluid balance, and weight. Report worsening electrolyte abnormalities, new organ dysfunction, or clinical deterioration promptly; stable appearance alone does not rule out evolving risk.","trap":"Refeeding risk is not simply a low phosphate result: it is a changing metabolic response that can involve several electrolytes, thiamine, fluid balance, and organ function. Think of it like traffic shifting into a newly opened lane: a single snapshot misses the developing congestion, so follow the pattern and its effects.","caseStory":"A fictional adult in critical care has had very limited intake and substantial recent weight loss. After enteral nutrition begins, phosphate falls, potassium and magnesium trend downward, and the patient develops new weakness with more labored breathing. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as an expected isolated laboratory change or promptly raise concern about refeeding-related deterioration.","caseReason":"The history raises nutritional risk, and the electrolyte changes follow the restart of nutrition. Weakness and worsening breathing make the pattern clinically significant rather than an isolated laboratory finding. The nurse should promptly communicate the trend and symptoms, then follow team orders and local protocols for monitoring and management.","teachback":"When nutrition resumes for an adult at risk, which history, laboratory trends, and clinical changes would prompt you to contact the team, and what would you reassess afterward?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with prolonged poor intake begins enteral nutrition. Over the next several days, phosphate, potassium, and magnesium all decline, and new generalized weakness appears. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["The timing, multiple electrolyte declines, and weakness raise concern for refeeding syndrome and need prompt team communication.","The findings most likely reflect an isolated phosphate problem, so the other electrolyte trends can be reviewed later.","The weakness is more consistent with deconditioning, so the nutrition-related laboratory changes need no immediate attention."],"feedback":["This combination after nutrition resumes is concerning for refeeding-related shifts, which can affect several electrolytes and impair muscle function.","Phosphate is an important clue, but potassium and magnesium changes can also contribute to complications and should not be set aside.","Deconditioning may contribute to weakness, but it does not explain away the timing and concurrent electrolyte declines."]},{"stem":"An adult with marked weight loss and a history of alcohol use is about to restart nutrition after prolonged poor intake. The nurse sees that baseline electrolyte results are not yet available. What is the best next nursing action?","options":["Communicate the risk factors to the treating team and seek baseline electrolyte assessment and a monitored nutrition plan under local protocols.","Begin the planned nutrition and wait for symptoms before raising concern, since the patient is not yet showing deterioration.","Independently reduce or delay the nutrition plan until all laboratory results are normal."],"feedback":["The history signals increased risk; baseline assessment and coordinated monitoring help the team guide safe nutritional advancement.","Refeeding complications can develop after nutrition resumes, so waiting for symptoms misses an opportunity for prevention and early detection.","The nurse should raise the concern and follow team orders or local protocols, not independently prescribe or change the nutrition plan."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult with chronic alcohol use is receiving parenteral nutrition after a period of poor intake. The patient develops confusion and an abnormal cardiac rhythm while electrolyte results are being reviewed. Which nursing response is most appropriate?","options":["Escalate the new neurologic and cardiac findings promptly, communicate the nutritional risk, and follow orders or protocols for evaluation and monitoring.","Focus only on the rhythm because neurologic changes are unrelated to nutritional rehabilitation.","Wait for a thiamine level before notifying the team, since symptoms cannot be related to deficiency without that result."],"feedback":["Refeeding-related electrolyte changes and thiamine deficiency can affect cardiac and neurologic function; new organ-related findings warrant prompt team assessment.","Neurologic changes can be relevant, including in the setting of thiamine deficiency, and should be communicated along with the rhythm change.","The supplied guidance emphasizes clinical assessment and does not require waiting for a thiamine level before escalating concerning symptoms."]},{"stem":"An adult with recent weight loss has started enteral nutrition. The serum albumin is unchanged, but phosphate is falling and new edema is present. Which reassessment best distinguishes a misleading stable result from evolving concern?","options":["Review electrolyte trends and clinical status, including fluid balance and respiratory or cardiac findings, and report the falling phosphate with new edema.","Use the unchanged albumin as evidence that nutritional status is stable and continue routine observation.","Attribute the phosphate decline to dilution from edema and disregard it unless the patient develops an arrhythmia."],"feedback":["Albumin can be an unreliable nutrition marker, while falling phosphate and new fluid findings after feeding merit assessment in context and communication.","An unchanged albumin does not reliably exclude nutritional risk or an evolving refeeding problem.","Fluid overload can affect measured electrolytes, but it does not establish the cause or make a falling phosphate safe to ignore."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Refeeding Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564513/"},{"title":"Refeeding syndrome: a challenging entity","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10123452/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Nutrition restart","detail":"After prolonged poor intake, renewed carbohydrate intake raises insulin, shifting phosphate and potassium into cells while increasing thiamine demand."},{"label":"Falling electrolytes","detail":"New weakness and labored breathing alongside falling phosphate, potassium, and magnesium after feeding suggest clinically important deterioration."},{"label":"Raise refeeding concern","detail":"Promptly communicate nutritional risk, electrolyte trends, and symptoms; follow team orders and local protocols for monitoring and gradual advancement."},{"label":"Track organ function","detail":"Recheck electrolyte trends with breathing, strength, rhythm, mental status, fluid balance, and weight; worsening abnormalities or organ dysfunction need prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s052","unit":"heme","title":"Anemia and oxygen delivery","objective":"Recognize acute anemia as a potential threat to tissue oxygen delivery, distinguish likely blood loss from hemolysis, and prioritize nursing assessment, communication, and reassessment.","concept":"Acute anemia is a rapid reduction in circulating red cells, commonly from hemorrhage or hemolysis, that can impair oxygen delivery before the body has time to compensate. In acute bleeding, red cells and plasma are lost together, so hemoglobin and hematocrit may initially appear unchanged. As plasma volume is restored, the measured anemia may become more apparent. Clinical stability and the suspected cause therefore matter alongside laboratory results.","recognize":"Assess for tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, cool or clammy skin, altered mental status, and signs of reduced perfusion, while looking for a bleeding source. Trauma, gastrointestinal or gynecologic bleeding, and recent surgery support hemorrhage. Jaundice or dark urine can point toward hemolysis. A reassuring early hemoglobin result does not rule out acute blood loss.","respond":"Prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation, promptly alert the appropriate clinical team to instability or suspected ongoing bleeding, and follow local protocols. Monitor vital signs and mental status, assess for a likely bleeding source, and facilitate ordered blood tests and preparation for blood products when indicated. Do not delay escalation while waiting for hemoglobin results or treat a laboratory value in isolation.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess vital signs, mental status, skin temperature and appearance, and any visible or suspected bleeding; compare findings with the prior assessment and report the trend. Worsening tachycardia or hypotension, new altered mental status, increasing pallor or clamminess, or continued bleeding warrants prompt escalation.","trap":"An early normal hemoglobin after sudden bleeding is like a level reading taken before the fluid compartments have had time to show the loss; it does not establish that blood volume is safe. Judge the whole clinical picture and its trend rather than letting one laboratory result overrule signs of instability.","caseStory":"A patient arrives after a fall with pallor, a rapid pulse, cool clammy skin, and abdominal tenderness. The first hemoglobin result is within the laboratory reference range, and the patient becomes less responsive during assessment. The nurse must decide whether to wait for repeat laboratory results or escalate the concern now.","caseReason":"The findings are concerning for acute hemorrhage with impaired perfusion, and the changing mental status increases the urgency. Hemoglobin can initially appear unchanged when red cells and plasma are lost together, so the result does not justify waiting to alert the team and prioritize ABC assessment.","teachback":"When a patient has signs of poor perfusion after a possible acute blood loss but an initially reassuring hemoglobin, what cues and trends would guide your escalation rather than relying on that result alone?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient is assessed shortly after a traumatic injury. The patient is pale, tachycardic, and cool to the touch, but the first hemoglobin result is within the laboratory reference range. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Acute blood loss remains possible because red cells and plasma may be lost together before the hemoglobin change is evident.","Acute blood loss is unlikely because a normal initial hemoglobin confirms that circulating red cell volume is adequate.","Chronic anemia is the most likely explanation because acute bleeding would immediately lower the measured hemoglobin."],"feedback":["Correct. The clinical signs after trauma support possible hemorrhage, and an early hemoglobin may not yet reflect the loss of both red cells and plasma.","This overrelies on a single early laboratory result, which can appear unchanged after acute hemorrhage.","A rapid change after trauma is more consistent with an acute process than a gradual chronic decline, and the initial result does not exclude bleeding."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized patient with suspected gastrointestinal bleeding develops worsening hypotension and becomes confused. The nurse has not yet completed a detailed bleeding history, and the latest hemoglobin is still pending. What is the priority nursing response?","options":["Prioritize ABC assessment, promptly alert the clinical team, and continue close monitoring under local protocols.","Complete a detailed gastrointestinal history first so the team receives a precise account of the bleeding source.","Wait for the hemoglobin result before escalating, because it will establish whether the patient is unstable."],"feedback":["Correct. Hypotension and new confusion are signs of instability that require prompt attention; detailed history and pending laboratory results should not delay escalation.","A focused history is useful once immediate priorities are addressed, but it should not take precedence over a deteriorating patient.","Clinical instability warrants action before a pending hemoglobin result, which does not by itself establish the patient's overall status."]},{"stem":"A patient reports dark urine and appears jaundiced after starting a new medication. The patient is weak, and there is no apparent external bleeding. Which nursing interpretation and communication best fit the available cues?","options":["Hemolysis is a possible cause of acute anemia; promptly report the findings and medication history to the clinical team.","Occult hemorrhage is established by dark urine, so the medication history is not relevant to the assessment.","Chronic anemia is most likely because jaundice and dark urine are not useful cues when considering acute red cell destruction."],"feedback":["Correct. Jaundice and dark urine can occur with hemolysis, and some drugs can contribute to red cell destruction; reporting the cues and exposure supports team evaluation.","Dark urine alone does not establish hemorrhage. The jaundice and medication history are relevant when considering hemolysis.","These findings can help identify hemolysis, which may cause acute anemia; they should not be dismissed as evidence only of a chronic process."]},{"stem":"A patient being treated for acute bleeding receives intravenous fluids under the care plan. The next hemoglobin is lower, while the patient is more alert and the pulse is less rapid. Which nursing response best interprets this potentially misleading change?","options":["Recognize that fluid replacement can make anemia more apparent by dilution, then reassess perfusion and bleeding and report the trend.","Conclude that the lower hemoglobin proves the bleeding has worsened, even though the patient's clinical signs have improved.","Treat the improved alertness and pulse as proof that the anemia has resolved, so further reassessment is unnecessary."],"feedback":["Correct. Replenishing plasma volume can make a previously less apparent anemia show more clearly; the nurse should evaluate the clinical trend and ongoing bleeding as well as report the result.","A lower result after fluids does not by itself prove that bleeding has worsened; fluid dilution can contribute to the change.","Improved signs are encouraging but do not establish that anemia or bleeding has resolved. Continued assessment and communication remain important."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Anemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537232/"},{"title":"Acute Anemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK537232/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Acute blood loss","detail":"In acute bleeding, red cells and plasma are lost together, so early hemoglobin may remain unchanged despite impaired oxygen delivery."},{"label":"Perfusion warning signs","detail":"Pallor, cool clammy skin, rapid pulse, hypotension, or declining responsiveness can signal acute blood loss and reduced perfusion."},{"label":"Escalate suspected bleeding","detail":"Prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation; promptly alert the team to instability or suspected ongoing bleeding without waiting for repeat hemoglobin."},{"label":"Reassess perfusion","detail":"Compare vital signs, mental status, skin, and bleeding; worsening tachycardia, hypotension, responsiveness, or continued blood loss warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s053","unit":"heme","title":"DIC and platelet consumption","objective":"Interpret the clinical pattern of platelet and coagulation factor consumption in DIC, then prioritize timely recognition, communication, and reassessment within nursing practice.","concept":"DIC is widespread activation of clotting that generates thrombin and fibrin while consuming platelets and coagulation factors. Fibrinolysis also breaks down fibrin, producing D-dimer and other products that can further impair platelet function. The balance of these processes explains why rapidly evolving DIC can cause bleeding and microvascular organ injury, while slower DIC more often presents with venous or arterial thrombosis. Treating the underlying cause is central.","recognize":"Consider DIC when a high-risk condition accompanies unexplained bleeding or thrombosis. Rapid disease may show falling platelets and fibrinogen, prolonged PT and PTT, and high D-dimer; slower disease may have milder or near-normal clotting tests, so trends matter. A declining fibrinogen can be informative even if initially normal. No single result establishes the pattern; liver disease can resemble acute DIC.","respond":"Escalate the suspected pattern promptly and communicate the trigger, bleeding or clotting signs, and serial laboratory changes to the care team. Support urgent treatment of the underlying cause and, when ordered for severe bleeding, preparation for replacement of platelets, fibrinogen, or other clotting factors according to local processes. Do not independently initiate anticoagulation; heparin is generally not used for rapidly evolving DIC with bleeding.","reassess":"After actions or ordered replacement, reassess bleeding at puncture sites and elsewhere, signs of venous or arterial thrombosis, and evidence of worsening organ function. Review repeat platelet count, PT, PTT, fibrinogen, and D-dimer when available. Persistent or increasing bleeding, worsening trends, new thrombosis, or organ dysfunction warrants prompt escalation.","trap":"DIC is not simply a bleeding problem: think of a system making clots throughout the circulation and using up the materials needed to stop bleeding elsewhere. A relatively reassuring early clotting result does not rule out slower DIC, just as one snapshot cannot show whether a trend is worsening.","caseStory":"In a fictional critical-care case, a patient being treated for a severe infection develops persistent oozing at an arterial puncture site and new bruising. Platelets and fibrinogen are falling, PT and PTT are lengthening, and D-dimer is high. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as a local puncture problem or urgently communicate concern for a systemic process.","caseReason":"The infection is a recognized setting for DIC, and the combined bleeding and changing laboratory pattern supports rapidly evolving consumption rather than an isolated local issue. The nurse should promptly alert the team, report the trend and clinical cues, and support treatment of the underlying cause and any ordered replacement therapy.","teachback":"When a patient at risk for DIC has a single near-normal fibrinogen result but a declining trend and increasing D-dimer, what other clinical and laboratory cues would you communicate, and why?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient develops persistent bleeding from venipuncture sites after a major obstetric complication. Platelets and fibrinogen are falling, PT and PTT are prolonged, and D-dimer is markedly increased. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Rapidly evolving DIC is likely, with clotting-factor and platelet consumption accompanying bleeding.","Slowly evolving DIC is most likely, because thrombosis is the usual dominant presentation.","An isolated platelet disorder best explains the findings, since clotting tests are prolonged."],"feedback":["The high-risk context, bleeding, falling platelets and fibrinogen, prolonged clotting times, and increased D-dimer fit rapidly evolving DIC.","Slow DIC more often presents with venous thromboembolism and usually has milder laboratory abnormalities; this case shows a severe bleeding pattern.","An isolated platelet disorder would not account for the combined prolonged PT and PTT, falling fibrinogen, and high D-dimer pattern described."]},{"stem":"A critically ill patient has ongoing gastrointestinal bleeding, worsening thrombocytopenia, prolonged PT and PTT, and a rapidly declining fibrinogen level. DIC is suspected. What is the nurse's priority response?","options":["Escalate immediately, communicate the pattern, and support cause-directed care and ordered replacement therapy.","Request heparin as the first response to stop further clot formation throughout the circulation.","Focus on local bleeding control and defer escalation until the next scheduled laboratory results."],"feedback":["Severe bleeding with a rapid DIC pattern requires urgent team communication, treatment of the cause, and support for replacement therapy when ordered.","Heparin is usually not indicated in rapidly evolving DIC with bleeding; anticoagulation decisions belong with the treating team.","Local measures alone do not address systemic consumption, and waiting risks delaying treatment of a potentially life-threatening process."]},{"stem":"A patient with cancer develops a new painful, swollen leg. Testing shows mildly low platelets, increased D-dimer, and near-normal PT and PTT. Which nursing interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["Consider slowly evolving DIC, report the thrombotic signs and laboratory pattern, and anticipate team-directed management.","Exclude DIC because bleeding and markedly prolonged PT and PTT are absent, then focus only on the leg symptoms.","Assume rapidly evolving DIC and prioritize preparation for bleeding-related replacement therapy."],"feedback":["Cancer can be associated with slowly evolving DIC, which may cause venous thrombosis with mild platelet changes and near-normal clotting times.","Slow DIC may present chiefly with thrombosis rather than bleeding, and PT and PTT can be normal or only minimally prolonged.","The described pattern is not the typical severe, rapidly evolving bleeding presentation that prompts replacement therapy for bleeding."]},{"stem":"A patient with a condition that can trigger DIC has an initially normal fibrinogen result, increased D-dimer, and mildly low platelets. A repeat fibrinogen result is lower, although still not markedly reduced. Which interpretation best uses these findings?","options":["A downward fibrinogen trend may support evolving DIC, so report it with the risk context and other results.","The first normal fibrinogen result rules out DIC, so the later change is not clinically relevant.","The increased D-dimer alone confirms DIC, making platelet and fibrinogen trends unnecessary."],"feedback":["Fibrinogen can be increased by inflammation, so a declining value across measurements may help identify DIC even before it becomes markedly low.","A single normal value does not rule out evolving DIC; the trend and clinical context matter.","D-dimer indicates fibrin generation and breakdown but should be interpreted alongside the clinical setting and other laboratory trends."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) - Hematology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/coagulation-disorders/disseminated-intravascular-coagulation-dic"},{"title":"Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) - Blood ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/blood-disorders/bleeding-due-to-clotting-disorders/disseminated-intravascular-coagulation-dic"}],"frames":[{"label":"Systemic clotting consumption","detail":"DIC activates clotting and consumes platelets and factors, while fibrinolysis produces D-dimer; rapidly evolving disease can cause bleeding and organ injury."},{"label":"Changing coagulation pattern","detail":"Oozing with falling platelets and fibrinogen, lengthening PT and PTT, and high D-dimer suggests a systemic process, not only local injury."},{"label":"Report DIC concern","detail":"Promptly communicate the trigger, bleeding or clotting signs, and serial laboratory changes; support urgent treatment of the underlying cause under team direction."},{"label":"Watch bleeding and organs","detail":"Reassess puncture-site bleeding, thrombosis, organ function, and repeat results; persistent bleeding, worsening trends, new thrombosis, or dysfunction needs escalation."}]},{"id":"s054","unit":"heme","title":"HIT versus immune thrombocytopenia","objective":"Distinguish suspected heparin-induced thrombocytopenia from other causes of low platelets, including immune thrombocytopenia, and communicate the concern promptly.","concept":"In immune-mediated HIT, antibodies form against complexes of platelet factor 4 and heparin, activating platelets and driving thrombin generation. Platelets fall as they are consumed, yet the central danger is clotting rather than bleeding. Immune thrombocytopenia is another possible explanation for a low platelet count, but the supplied sources do not describe its distinguishing features. Heparin exposure, timing, platelet trend, and thrombosis therefore guide concern for HIT.","recognize":"Suspect HIT when an unexplained platelet decline follows heparin exposure, often beginning several days into treatment, especially when a new or progressive clot appears. Include every heparin source in the exposure history, such as flushes or dialysis-related use. A low platelet count alone does not distinguish HIT from immune thrombocytopenia; consider the timing, thrombosis, and other possible causes.","respond":"Promptly report the platelet trend, heparin exposure timeline, and any possible thrombosis to the responsible clinician, using local escalation protocols. Help identify all heparin sources and follow authorized orders or protocols for stopping heparin and using an alternative anticoagulant. Do not independently prescribe therapy; communicate the concern about platelet transfusion or warfarin during suspected acute HIT.","reassess":"After the team acts, continue ordered platelet monitoring and assess for new or worsening limb pain or swelling, chest symptoms, or injection-site skin changes. Report suspected thrombosis or a continued platelet decline promptly. Lack of bleeding or a stable hemoglobin does not rule out HIT.","trap":"A falling platelet count does not automatically mean a bleeding disorder: HIT can consume platelets while intensifying clot formation. Think of the platelet count as a warning light, not a diagnosis; the heparin timeline and evidence of thrombosis help distinguish HIT from other causes such as immune thrombocytopenia.","caseStory":"A fictional adult receiving unfractionated heparin has a steady platelet decline several days after treatment began. The patient reports new calf swelling, while hemoglobin remains stable and there is no bleeding. The nurse must decide whether to treat the finding as an expected laboratory change or promptly raise concern for HIT.","caseReason":"The timing after heparin exposure and the new possible venous thrombosis are important HIT cues. Stable hemoglobin and no bleeding do not exclude HIT, whose major danger is thrombosis. The nurse should promptly communicate the findings and support evaluation and action under orders or local protocols.","teachback":"When a patient has a low platelet count, what exposure, timing, and clinical findings would lead you to raise concern for HIT rather than assume another cause?","questions":[{"stem":"A fictional adult has received unfractionated heparin for several days. The platelet count has fallen steadily, and new unilateral leg swelling is reported. Hemoglobin is stable, with no bleeding. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["The timing, platelet decline, and possible new thrombosis raise concern for HIT and warrant prompt clinical communication.","The stable hemoglobin makes a heparin-related platelet reaction unlikely, so routine observation is sufficient.","The low platelet count establishes immune thrombocytopenia, making the heparin timeline less relevant."],"feedback":["HIT commonly involves a platelet fall after heparin exposure and can cause venous thrombosis; absence of bleeding does not rule it out.","HIT is primarily prothrombotic, and stable hemoglobin or lack of bleeding does not exclude it.","A low platelet count alone does not establish immune thrombocytopenia; exposure timing and possible thrombosis must be considered."]},{"stem":"A fictional inpatient has an unexplained platelet decline after heparin was started, and a new clot is suspected. The nurse sees heparin listed in the medication record and is unsure whether line flushes are also being used. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician, report the platelet trend and suspected clot, and help identify all heparin sources while following local protocols.","Wait for a confirmatory antibody test before communicating the concern or reviewing heparin sources.","Independently substitute a different anticoagulant and discontinue all prescribed therapies."],"feedback":["The clinical pattern warrants prompt team communication; all heparin sources, including flushes, should be considered, with treatment changes handled under orders or protocols.","The supplied reference advises acting on suspected HIT without waiting for confirmatory testing, because thrombosis risk is important.","Nurses should communicate and follow authorized orders or protocols, not independently prescribe or substitute anticoagulants."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient receiving heparin during dialysis develops repeated extracorporeal circuit clotting and a falling platelet count. Which nursing interpretation and response best fit these findings?","options":["Recognize possible HIT despite the dialysis context, promptly alert the clinical team, and report heparin exposure and circuit clotting.","Attribute the circuit clotting to the dialysis setting and defer reporting unless bleeding develops.","Assume immune thrombocytopenia because the platelet count is low, and disregard the heparin exposure."],"feedback":["The supplied material describes HIT in dialysis patients with circuit clotting and advises stopping heparin when HIT is suspected through appropriate clinical management.","Circuit clotting can be a manifestation of HIT, and bleeding is not required for concern or escalation.","Immune thrombocytopenia is a differential diagnosis, but a low count alone does not establish it or negate the relevance of heparin exposure."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult being evaluated for possible HIT has had heparin stopped by the clinical team and an alternative anticoagulant started under orders. The platelet count begins to recover, hemoglobin remains stable, and no bleeding is seen. Which reassessment remains most important?","options":["Continue ordered platelet monitoring and assess for new or worsening thrombosis symptoms, escalating concerning changes even without bleeding.","Conclude that HIT is excluded because the platelet count is recovering and there is no bleeding.","Focus only on bleeding checks because a stable hemoglobin shows that the prothrombotic risk has resolved."],"feedback":["HIT carries ongoing thrombotic risk; platelet trends and signs of new or progressive thrombosis remain important to reassess.","Platelet recovery does not by itself exclude HIT, and the absence of bleeding is not evidence against its prothrombotic pattern.","Bleeding assessment may be relevant during anticoagulation, but stable hemoglobin does not establish that HIT-related clotting risk has resolved."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3097589/"},{"title":"Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK482330/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Heparin antibody activation","detail":"After heparin exposure, immune complexes activate platelets and thrombin generation; platelet consumption occurs while clotting, rather than bleeding, is the central danger."},{"label":"Platelet fall with clot","detail":"A platelet decline several days after heparin begins, especially with new calf swelling, raises concern for HIT despite stable hemoglobin."},{"label":"Communicate exposure timeline","detail":"Promptly report platelet trends, all heparin sources, timing, and possible thrombosis; follow authorized orders and local protocols for heparin-related actions."},{"label":"Monitor new thrombosis","detail":"Continue ordered platelet monitoring and watch for limb swelling or pain, chest symptoms, or skin changes; report thrombosis or continued decline promptly."}]},{"id":"s055","unit":"heme","title":"Febrile neutropenia","objective":"Recognize suspected febrile neutropenia and promptly coordinate urgent assessment and treatment with the clinical team.","concept":"Neutrophils are a major defense against bacterial and fungal infection, so a low absolute neutrophil count weakens containment and can blunt local inflammation. With profound neutropenia, organisms from normal mouth or gut flora may cause invasive infection. Fever may therefore be the clearest warning, while redness, pus, leukocytosis, or urinary white cells can be subtle or absent. Risk also depends on acuity and clinical context.","recognize":"Suspect febrile neutropenia when a patient with known or possible neutropenia develops fever, especially during or after cancer treatment. Look for subtle changes and examine likely infection sites, including the mouth, lungs, abdomen, urinary tract, skin, and vascular access sites. A quiet examination does not rule out infection; consider other explanations for fever without dismissing this risk.","respond":"Treat fever in a neutropenic patient as an urgent concern and promptly notify the responsible clinician or activate the local pathway. Under orders and local protocols, help obtain cultures and other ordered tests while coordinating timely empiric intravenous antibiotics. Communicate the neutrophil trend, symptoms, treatment history, and vital signs. Do not wait for obvious inflammation or culture results before escalating.","reassess":"After escalation and ordered treatment, monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, symptoms, and the suspected infection sites. Track culture and test results with the team. Escalate deterioration, hypotension, new focal symptoms, or persistent fever despite treatment, since these findings may signal serious or unresolved infection.","trap":"Do not use a quiet examination as an all-clear: neutropenia can mute the usual inflammatory signals. Think of fever as a smoke alarm when the visible flames may be hard to see.","caseStory":"A patient receiving cancer treatment has a documented severe neutropenia and develops a fever. The patient reports feeling chilled but has no obvious redness, swelling, or productive cough. The nurse must decide whether the subtle examination supports waiting or urgent escalation.","caseReason":"Fever in a patient with severe neutropenia raises concern for infection even when focal signs are absent. The weakened inflammatory response can hide the usual clues, so the nurse should promptly escalate and coordinate cultures and empiric treatment under orders and local protocols.","teachback":"How would you explain why fever with neutropenia deserves urgent escalation even when the patient has no clear local signs of infection?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient receiving chemotherapy has a documented very low neutrophil count and a new fever. The patient is alert, and examination shows no clear skin, oral, or respiratory infection. Which interpretation should guide the nurse’s next action?","options":["Infection remains a serious concern because neutropenia can mute local inflammatory signs, so promptly escalate the fever.","The lack of a visible infection focus makes a noninfectious fever more likely, so continue routine observation first.","The patient’s alert appearance makes a serious infection unlikely, so wait for a change in vital signs before notifying the team."],"feedback":["Correct. Fever may be the main indication of infection in severe neutropenia because focal signs can be subtle or absent.","This is misleading because an absent focus does not reliably exclude infection when the inflammatory response is blunted.","This is misleading because a reassuring appearance does not remove the risk of rapidly serious infection in acute, severe neutropenia."]},{"stem":"A febrile patient with neutropenia becomes newly hypotensive. Blood cultures have not yet been collected, and empiric antibiotics are being arranged by the care team. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Immediately escalate the change and coordinate urgent cultures and empiric intravenous antibiotics under orders and local protocol.","Wait for the culture results before supporting antibiotic treatment so the team can target the identified organism.","Focus on locating a visible infection site before alerting the team, since treatment depends on finding the source."],"feedback":["Correct. Fever with hypotension in neutropenia warrants immediate concern for serious infection and urgent team-directed treatment; cultures are a key part of evaluation.","This is misleading because delaying empiric treatment until cultures result is not appropriate when serious infection is suspected.","This is misleading because infection may have no obvious focus, and source-finding should not delay urgent escalation."]},{"stem":"A patient with a marrow disorder and chronic neutropenia, rather than chemotherapy-related neutropenia, develops fever and mild abdominal discomfort. The patient has no marked abdominal tenderness. Which nursing interpretation best applies?","options":["The fever still warrants prompt escalation because infection risk applies beyond chemotherapy and abdominal signs may be muted.","The fever is less concerning because only patients with chemotherapy-related neutropenia need urgent infection assessment.","The mild discomfort makes an abdominal infection unlikely, so focus assessment on the lungs and defer escalation."],"feedback":["Correct. Neutropenia from different causes can impair infection defenses, and abdominal infection may not produce prominent findings.","This is misleading because neutropenia has multiple causes, and fever in a neutropenic patient remains an urgent concern.","This is misleading because muted inflammation can make focal findings less apparent, and the abdomen is one of the sites to assess."]},{"stem":"A neutropenic patient receiving empiric antibiotics remains febrile after several days. The patient is otherwise stable, and initial cultures have not identified a cause. Which response is most appropriate?","options":["Promptly communicate the persistent fever and support reassessment for resistant or nonbacterial infection, a second infection, inadequate drug levels, or a localized source.","Treat the negative cultures and stable appearance as evidence that infection is unlikely, and discontinue concern about the fever.","Assume the current antibiotic has failed and independently request a different regimen before further evaluation."],"feedback":["Correct. Persistent fever despite treatment calls for reassessment because several infectious and treatment-related explanations remain possible.","This is misleading because negative cultures and a stable appearance do not by themselves rule out infection in neutropenia.","This is misleading because reassessment and any treatment change are team decisions; persistent fever has several possible causes."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Neutropenia - Hematology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia"},{"title":"Neutropenia - Neutropenia - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia?ruleredirectid=746autoredirectid=37881"}],"frames":[{"label":"Muted infection signs","detail":"Severe neutropenia weakens infection containment and local inflammation, so invasive infection may develop without obvious redness, pus, or leukocytosis."},{"label":"Fever despite quiet exam","detail":"Fever and chills during severe neutropenia remain concerning even when examination shows no focal redness, swelling, or productive cough."},{"label":"Activate urgent pathway","detail":"Promptly notify the clinician or activate the local pathway; coordinate ordered cultures and timely empiric intravenous antibiotics without waiting for results."},{"label":"Track response and decline","detail":"Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, symptoms, and sites; hypotension, deterioration, focal symptoms, or persistent fever requires escalation."}]},{"id":"s056","unit":"heme","title":"Tumor lysis syndrome","objective":"Recognize the pattern and urgency of tumor lysis syndrome, then communicate and reassess nursing actions in coordination with the oncology team.","concept":"Rapid destruction of tumor cells releases potassium, phosphate, and nucleic acids, which are metabolized into uric acid. When the kidneys cannot clear this load, uric acid and calcium phosphate may injure renal tubules, while electrolyte shifts can cause hypocalcemia and dangerous cardiac rhythms. The syndrome is most associated with rapidly growing, treatment-sensitive blood cancers, but it can also arise spontaneously or after some immune therapies.","recognize":"Consider tumor lysis when a patient with a high-burden or treatment-sensitive cancer develops a changing pattern of potassium, phosphate, uric acid, calcium, or kidney-function results, especially around cancer treatment. Weakness, nausea, reduced urine output, cramps, confusion, or arrhythmias may occur but are not specific. Compare trends and timing, and do not dismiss the syndrome because treatment has not yet begun.","respond":"Treat suspected tumor lysis as urgent: promptly notify the responsible clinician and oncology team, communicate relevant trends and symptoms, and follow local escalation protocols. Support timely ordered laboratory testing, ECG monitoring, fluid management, and uric-acid-lowering treatment; track intake and urine output. Avoid initiating medications or fluid changes independently. Do not give calcium for asymptomatic hypocalcemia without direction, because calcium phosphate deposition may worsen.","reassess":"After actions are initiated, reassess symptoms, vital signs, rhythm monitoring, urine output, fluid status, and ordered laboratory trends, including kidney function and electrolytes. Escalate promptly for a new arrhythmia, seizure, worsening weakness or mental status, falling urine output, or worsening metabolic or renal results.","trap":"A normal-looking presentation before treatment does not rule out tumor lysis; spontaneous cases occur, so the laboratory pattern and clinical context matter. Think of the kidneys as a drain under a sudden surge: symptoms may lag behind the accumulating electrolyte and waste load.","caseStory":"A patient with bulky lymphoma is awaiting the first cancer treatment and reports nausea and new muscle weakness. The latest results show rising potassium, phosphate, and uric acid, falling calcium, and worsening kidney function. The nurse must decide whether to wait for treatment to start or urgently escalate the emerging pattern.","caseReason":"The combination of tumor burden, metabolic changes, and declining kidney function is concerning for tumor lysis even before therapy begins. The nurse should promptly notify the responsible team, report the trends and symptoms, and support ordered monitoring and treatment rather than waiting for a more dramatic symptom.","teachback":"When a patient with a rapidly growing cancer has concerning metabolic trends before treatment begins, what pattern would prompt you to escalate, and what would you communicate to the team?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with a rapidly growing hematologic cancer develops rising potassium, phosphate, and uric acid, falling calcium, and a creatinine increase after treatment begins. The patient reports nausea but has no new focal symptoms. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["The combined metabolic and renal changes are concerning for tumor lysis syndrome and need prompt escalation.","The nausea most likely explains the laboratory changes, so the nurse should reassess after the next meal.","The findings fit an isolated calcium problem, so the other laboratory trends are less relevant."],"feedback":["The pattern is consistent with release of intracellular contents and impaired renal clearance, with potential for serious complications even when symptoms are limited.","Nausea can occur in tumor lysis, but it does not account for the linked electrolyte, uric acid, and kidney-function changes.","Focusing on calcium alone misses the broader pattern of potassium, phosphate, uric acid, and renal changes."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving cancer treatment has laboratory trends concerning for tumor lysis and reports new palpitations. The nurse is reviewing the results while routine care is underway. Which action should take priority?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician and follow local escalation protocols, including support for ordered ECG and laboratory monitoring.","Continue routine care and repeat the assessment after the next scheduled meal because palpitations may be temporary.","Independently administer calcium and change the fluid plan to address the reported symptoms."],"feedback":["Tumor lysis can cause electrolyte-related arrhythmias, so palpitations with concerning trends warrant prompt communication and coordinated monitoring.","Waiting could delay recognition of a potentially serious rhythm or metabolic complication.","Nurses should not independently prescribe treatment or fluid changes, and calcium requires particular caution when phosphate is elevated."]},{"stem":"A patient with bulky lymphoma has not yet received cancer treatment but develops reduced urine output. Results show rising uric acid and potassium with worsening kidney function. Which nursing interpretation best transfers tumor lysis recognition to this situation?","options":["Tumor lysis remains a concern because it can occur spontaneously, and the pattern warrants prompt team notification.","Tumor lysis is unlikely before treatment, so the nurse should focus only on documenting urine output.","The changes establish a urinary obstruction, so the nurse should treat that as the sole explanation."],"feedback":["Tumor lysis can arise spontaneously, and rising metabolic markers with kidney changes in this context merit urgent escalation.","Waiting for cancer treatment to begin could delay recognition of a spontaneous syndrome.","Reduced urine output and kidney changes may have multiple causes; the supplied pattern does not establish obstruction as the sole cause."]},{"stem":"After the team initiates ordered care for suspected tumor lysis, a patient says nausea has improved. However, urine output continues to fall and repeat results show worsening kidney function and electrolyte abnormalities. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Escalate the worsening urine output and laboratory trends despite symptom improvement, and continue reassessment under the care plan.","Treat the improved nausea as evidence that the syndrome is resolving and reduce monitoring.","Focus on nausea alone because symptoms are a more reliable measure of kidney recovery than laboratory trends."],"feedback":["Improved nausea does not demonstrate resolution; falling urine output and worsening metabolic or renal findings require prompt communication and continued reassessment.","Reducing monitoring based on one improved symptom could miss progression of renal or electrolyte complications.","Nausea is nonspecific, while urine output and laboratory trends provide important information about renal and metabolic status."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Management of Adverse Effects of Cancer Therapy - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/oncology/principles-of-cancer-therapy/management-of-adverse-effects-of-cancer-therapy"},{"title":"Tumor Lysis Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518985/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Tumor-cell breakdown","detail":"Rapid tumor-cell destruction releases potassium, phosphate, and nucleic acids; spontaneous lysis can occur before treatment in high-burden cancers."},{"label":"Changing metabolic pattern","detail":"Rising potassium, phosphate, and uric acid with falling calcium and worsening kidney function signal possible tumor lysis despite absent symptoms."},{"label":"Urgent team escalation","detail":"Promptly notify oncology and the responsible clinician; communicate symptoms and trends, then support ordered testing, ECG monitoring, fluids, and uric-acid treatment."},{"label":"Renal and rhythm checks","detail":"Reassess urine output, fluid status, rhythm, kidney function, and electrolytes; urgently escalate arrhythmia, seizure, reduced output, or worsening results."}]},{"id":"s057","unit":"heme","title":"Transfusion reactions","objective":"Recognize a suspected transfusion reaction, initiate the immediate nursing response, and communicate findings that guide safe team-based evaluation.","concept":"Transfusion reactions may be immune mediated or nonimmune, and can occur during transfusion or later. Incompatibility, donor antibodies, inflammatory mediators, contamination, and excess volume can produce overlapping symptoms but different threats, including hemolysis, shock, and pulmonary edema. Because early signs may be nonspecific, nurses should treat a new concerning change as a possible reaction rather than trying to establish the diagnosis alone. Prompt interruption and coordinated evaluation can limit harm.","recognize":"Watch for new fever, chills, itching, hives, pain, dyspnea, hypotension, or red urine during or after transfusion. Fever and chills can occur with a febrile reaction, but may also signal hemolysis or contamination. Flank or back pain and hemoglobinuria raise concern for hemolysis; wheezing and hypotension suggest anaphylaxis. Crackles, jugular venous distension, and hypertension favor volume overload, while hypoxemia, fever, and hypotension may suggest TRALI.","respond":"For any suspected reaction, stop the transfusion, maintain IV access with appropriate fluid according to local protocol, and promptly notify the treating clinician and blood bank. Recheck patient and product identification, monitor and record vital signs, and send the requested blood samples and product bag or tubing for evaluation. Follow orders and emergency protocols; do not independently prescribe treatment or assume a mild-looking start rules out progression.","reassess":"Continue close observation and document vital signs, respiratory status, skin findings, pain, urine appearance, and symptom progression. Report worsening breathing, hypoxemia, hypotension, high fever, or hemoglobinuria urgently. A symptom that improves after stopping the product does not by itself establish that the reaction is resolved or identify its cause.","trap":"A transfusion reaction is not a single pattern, like one alarm with one cause; the same fever or breathlessness can point to different problems. Do not let an apparently mild symptom reassure you: it can be an early signal, so stop the transfusion and assess for progression.","caseStory":"During a red blood cell transfusion, a fictional adult patient develops chills, new flank pain, and falling blood pressure. The nurse also notices that the urine in the collection bag has become red. The decision is whether to treat this as an expected fever or as a potentially serious transfusion reaction.","caseReason":"Flank pain, hypotension, and red urine raise concern for acute hemolysis, although the bedside findings do not establish the cause. The nurse stops the transfusion, maintains IV access, alerts the clinician and blood bank, and follows local procedures for checks, monitoring, and specimen handling. Waiting to see whether the fever settles could delay evaluation of a serious reaction.","teachback":"If a patient develops a new symptom during transfusion, what immediate actions would you take, and which changes would you report as evidence of possible progression?","questions":[{"stem":"During a red blood cell transfusion, an adult patient develops chills, flank pain, and hypotension. The nurse observes red urine in the collection bag. Which interpretation best supports the immediate nursing response?","options":["Suspect a serious reaction, including possible acute hemolysis, and stop the transfusion while notifying the clinical team and blood bank.","Consider a febrile nonhemolytic reaction most likely and continue the transfusion while observing whether the chills settle.","Assume the findings indicate volume overload and independently administer a diuretic before contacting the clinical team."],"feedback":["Flank pain, hypotension, and possible hemoglobinuria are concerning for acute hemolysis, which requires stopping the transfusion and prompt team evaluation.","Fever and chills can occur in a febrile reaction, but they can also accompany serious reactions; flank pain and red urine make continuing the transfusion unsafe.","Volume overload can cause respiratory findings and hypertension, but the vignette does not establish that diagnosis, and a nurse should not independently prescribe a diuretic."]},{"stem":"Several minutes after a transfusion begins, an adult patient develops widespread hives, wheezing, and hypotension. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Stop the transfusion, maintain IV access, and urgently notify the clinician while following the emergency response protocol.","Give an antihistamine first and continue the transfusion if the hives begin to fade.","Slow the transfusion and wait for another set of vital signs before deciding whether to notify the clinician."],"feedback":["Wheezing and hypotension with hives can indicate anaphylaxis, so immediate interruption and urgent team response take priority.","An antihistamine may be used if prescribed, but continuing the transfusion or delaying the urgent response could allow a severe reaction to progress.","These are signs of a potentially life-threatening reaction; slowing the transfusion and waiting is not an appropriate substitute for stopping it and escalating."]},{"stem":"An older adult with cardiac and renal disorders becomes short of breath during a transfusion. The nurse hears crackles, sees jugular venous distension, and notes hypertension. Which response best fits the cues and nursing role?","options":["Stop the transfusion, report the findings as concerning for circulatory overload, and follow clinician direction and local protocol.","Identify TRALI as established because any new dyspnea during transfusion indicates noncardiac lung injury.","Continue the transfusion at the same rate because hypertension and crackles are expected changes in an older adult."],"feedback":["Crackles, jugular venous distension, and hypertension support concern for TACO; stopping and communicating the findings allows the team to direct further care.","TRALI can cause dyspnea and pulmonary edema, but these findings do not establish it, and the volume-overload cues favor considering TACO.","These new respiratory and cardiovascular findings are concerning, not expected reassurance; continuing without evaluation risks worsening the reaction."]},{"stem":"A patient develops hypoxemia, fever, and hypotension after a plasma-rich blood product. The transfusion is stopped and oxygen support is provided under clinical direction. Oxygenation improves, but blood pressure continues to fall. What is the best nursing reassessment and communication?","options":["Continue close respiratory and vital-sign monitoring and urgently report the worsening hypotension, since improved oxygenation does not mean the reaction has resolved.","Conclude that the reaction is resolving because oxygenation improved, and reduce observation unless dyspnea returns.","Reclassify the event as TACO solely because pulmonary edema may follow transfusion, and independently request a diuretic."],"feedback":["TRALI can involve hypoxemia, fever, and hypotension; worsening blood pressure needs urgent escalation even if oxygenation improves.","An improvement in one measure does not rule out ongoing or worsening problems, and continued monitoring is needed when another vital sign deteriorates.","Pulmonary edema can have different causes, and the vignette does not establish TACO; a nurse should report the pattern and follow clinician direction rather than independently prescribe treatment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Transfusion Reactions","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK482202/"},{"title":"Table 3.2c, [Transfusion Reactions and Related Nursing Interventions[]].","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK594497/table/ch3bloodproducts.T.transfusion_reactions/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Overlapping transfusion threats","detail":"Transfusion threats overlap; hemolysis, contamination, allergy, and volume overload may emerge during or after administration."},{"label":"Possible hemolysis cues","detail":"Chills with flank pain, falling pressure, and red urine raise concern for serious hemolysis, not an expected fever."},{"label":"Stop and notify","detail":"Stop transfusion, maintain IV access per local protocol, and promptly notify the treating clinician and blood bank."},{"label":"Watch for progression","detail":"Track vital signs, breathing, pain, urine, and symptoms; urgently report worsening hypotension, hypoxemia, fever, or hemoglobinuria."}]},{"id":"s058","unit":"heme","title":"Autoimmunity and organ injury","objective":"Recognize patterns of SLE-related organ injury and communicate changes that require timely team evaluation.","concept":"Systemic lupus erythematosus is an autoimmune inflammatory disease that can injure multiple organ systems, with activity waxing and waning. Joint and skin findings may coexist with serosal, neurologic, hematologic, or renal involvement. Kidney disease can be clinically quiet while urine shows protein or red-cell casts; complement may fall during active disease. This matters in critical care because a familiar symptom such as fatigue cannot substitute for focused surveillance of threatened organs.","recognize":"Look for patterns rather than one symptom: joint complaints, photosensitive malar erythema sparing the nasolabial folds, oral ulcers, serositis, cytopenias, or neurologic change. Renal clues include proteinuria, red-cell casts, hypertension, or edema and may occur without obvious symptoms. ANA is a sensitive screen, not proof of SLE; fatigue and widespread pain with an isolated positive result are weak evidence, and infection can mimic disease.","respond":"Prioritize new neurologic change, respiratory or cardiac compromise, bleeding or significant cytopenia concerns, and evidence of nephritis; promptly notify the responsible clinician and use local escalation pathways. Communicate onset, trends, vital signs, mental status, relevant blood counts, urinalysis, renal tests, and current therapies. Obtain ordered tests and support coordinated evaluation. Do not label a positive ANA or nonspecific pain as a flare or independently change immunosuppression.","reassess":"After ordered evaluation or intervention, trend mental status and seizure activity, respiratory and cardiac status, blood counts, blood pressure, renal chemistry, and urine protein or sediment as available. Escalate promptly for new seizure or confusion, worsening cardiopulmonary status, falling counts with bleeding, or new casts or increasing proteinuria.","trap":"ANA is like a smoke alarm that is sensitive, not a signed diagnosis: it can sound in other disorders and in healthy people. Treat the clinical pattern and organ evidence as the fire assessment, not the alarm alone.","caseStory":"A  thirty-year-old adult with SLE reports worsening fatigue and new ankle swelling. The nurse notes elevated blood pressure and a urine result with protein and red-cell casts, while the patient denies urinary discomfort. The decision is whether to treat this as nonspecific fatigue or promptly report possible renal involvement.","caseReason":"SLE kidney involvement can be asymptomatic and may present with proteinuria, red-cell casts, hypertension, or edema. The findings warrant prompt communication and team evaluation; they should not be dismissed because the patient has no urinary discomfort or because fatigue is nonspecific.","teachback":"What combination of clinical pattern and organ-specific findings would make you escalate concern for SLE-related kidney injury rather than rely on an ANA result alone?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with intermittent joint swelling has a photosensitive facial rash that spares the nasolabial folds, recurrent oral ulcers, and protein in the urine. ANA is positive. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's communication?","options":["The combined clinical and renal findings raise concern for multisystem SLE, so report the urine abnormality and support further evaluation.","The positive ANA confirms SLE, so the other findings add little and can be documented as expected.","The rash and joint symptoms suggest a skin-limited condition, so the urine finding is unlikely to be relevant."],"feedback":["Correct. The combined pattern supports concern for multisystem disease, and proteinuria is a relevant renal finding that warrants communication and evaluation.","ANA is sensitive but not specific and does not by itself establish SLE; it should be interpreted alongside clinical and organ findings.","SLE can involve the kidneys as well as skin and joints, and renal involvement may be clinically quiet, so the urine finding should not be set aside."]},{"stem":"An adult admitted with established SLE and chronic joint discomfort suddenly has a seizure. The patient is now confused. Which nursing priority is most appropriate?","options":["Promptly escalate the new neurologic change through the local pathway and communicate the timing, assessment, and relevant clinical information.","Document the event as a likely routine flare and wait to see whether the confusion resolves before notifying the team.","Focus first on the chronic joint discomfort because it is the most familiar manifestation of this patient's SLE."],"feedback":["Correct. Seizures and other neurologic changes can occur with SLE and require prompt team evaluation; do not assume the cause without assessment.","A seizure with ongoing confusion is a significant neurologic change, not a finding to attribute to a flare and observe without escalation.","Joint symptoms are common in SLE, but a new seizure and confusion indicate a more urgent potential organ threat."]},{"stem":"A pregnant adult with SLE is 21 weeks pregnant and has anti-Ro/SSA antibodies. Which nursing action best transfers the nurse's organ-surveillance thinking to this context?","options":["Communicate the antibody status and support coordination with the high-risk pregnancy team for fetal heart-block surveillance.","Reassure the patient that fetal heart-block surveillance is unnecessary unless the mother develops joint symptoms.","Treat the antibody result as proof that fetal heart block is present and communicate a confirmed complication."],"feedback":["Correct. Maternal anti-Ro/SSA antibodies are associated with congenital heart block, and the reference supports coordinated fetal surveillance during this stage of pregnancy.","Fetal heart-block risk is associated with the antibody status; maternal joint symptoms are not a reason to omit the recommended surveillance.","The antibody indicates risk, not a confirmed fetal complication; surveillance is intended to assess for heart block."]},{"stem":"An adult reports fatigue and widespread muscle pain. ANA is positive, but there are no other clinical or laboratory findings suggesting SLE. Which interpretation best avoids a misleading conclusion?","options":["The ANA alone does not establish SLE; continue to relate results to the clinical pattern and assess for organ-specific findings.","The positive ANA makes active SLE the most likely explanation, so the fatigue should be recorded as a disease flare.","The absence of kidney symptoms rules out SLE, so further clinical assessment is unnecessary."],"feedback":["Correct. ANA is useful as a sensitive screening test but is not specific, and an isolated positive result with fatigue and generalized pain is rarely significant.","A positive ANA alone does not diagnose SLE or establish that fatigue is a flare; other clinical or laboratory evidence is needed.","SLE can affect multiple organs, and renal involvement may be asymptomatic; absence of kidney symptoms neither rules out SLE nor removes the need for appropriate assessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Rheumatology and ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/musculoskeletal-and-connective-tissue-disorders/systemic-rheumatic-diseases/systemic-lupus-erythematosus-sle"},{"title":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/en-pr/professional/musculoskeletal-and-connective-tissue-disorders/autoimmune-rheumatic-disorders/systemic-lupus-erythematosus-sle"}],"frames":[{"label":"Multiorgan inflammation","detail":"SLE inflammation can affect multiple organs; kidney injury may be clinically quiet while urine abnormalities and hypertension develop."},{"label":"Renal involvement clues","detail":"Proteinuria, red-cell casts, ankle edema, and elevated blood pressure suggest renal involvement despite absent urinary discomfort."},{"label":"Report possible nephritis","detail":"Promptly report possible nephritis and communicate blood pressure, renal tests, urinalysis, trends, and current therapies for coordinated evaluation."},{"label":"Trend renal findings","detail":"Trend blood pressure, renal chemistry, and urine findings; escalate new casts, increasing proteinuria, seizure, confusion, or cardiopulmonary decline."}]},{"id":"s059","unit":"endo","title":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","objective":"Interpret the clinical and laboratory pattern of diabetic ketoacidosis and support timely, safe team-based care through focused assessment, communication, and reassessment.","concept":"Diabetic ketoacidosis develops when insulin is insufficient and counterregulatory hormones rise, driving glucose production and fat breakdown. The liver converts released fatty acids into ketones, which contribute to metabolic acidosis; high glucose also causes osmotic fluid and electrolyte losses. These linked processes explain why nurses track perfusion, mental status, glucose, ketones, acid-base status, and potassium together rather than treating a glucose value in isolation.","recognize":"Consider DKA when diabetes or hyperglycemia accompanies ketonemia and metabolic acidosis, often with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration, or deep labored breathing. Blood beta-hydroxybutyrate is more informative than urine ketones, which may lag or misrepresent ketone burden. HHS more often features profound dehydration and neurological change without significant ketonemia or acidosis; infection, fasting, alcohol use, and toxins may also produce overlapping findings.","respond":"Treat suspected DKA as an urgent, monitored emergency: promptly notify the responsible clinician, communicate the glucose, ketone, acid-base, potassium, vital-sign, and mental-status findings, and help identify possible triggers. Support ordered fluid and insulin therapy and electrolyte monitoring under local protocols. Do not initiate or continue insulin contrary to potassium-related orders or protocol; escalating hypokalemia requires prompt team attention.","reassess":"After interventions, reassess vital signs, perfusion, mental status, glucose, potassium, ketones, and acid-base trends at the ordered frequency. Report falling potassium, worsening hypotension, new confusion, declining consciousness, or persistent acidosis promptly. Do not interpret urine ketones alone as proof that DKA is worsening or resolving.","trap":"A glucose level that is not dramatically elevated does not rule out DKA, particularly with SGLT-2 inhibitor use or pregnancy. Think of glucose as one instrument on a dashboard: ketones and acidosis can signal danger even when that instrument looks less alarming.","caseStory":"An adult with type 1 diabetes arrives after vomiting and missing insulin during a respiratory illness. The patient is tachycardic, dehydrated, and breathing deeply; testing shows hyperglycemia, elevated blood beta-hydroxybutyrate, acidosis, and low potassium. An insulin infusion is ordered, and the nurse must decide what to communicate before it begins.","caseReason":"The combination of ketonemia and acidosis with hyperglycemia supports DKA, while the illness may be a precipitating stressor. The low potassium makes immediate communication essential because insulin can lower serum potassium further; the nurse should follow the local protocol and clarify the plan with the responsible clinician before insulin is initiated.","teachback":"When glucose is improving but urine ketones remain positive during treatment, what other findings and trends would you use to judge the patient’s status and decide whether to escalate?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with type 1 diabetes reports missed insulin and now has vomiting, abdominal discomfort, and deep labored breathing. Glucose is elevated, blood beta-hydroxybutyrate is markedly elevated, and testing shows metabolic acidosis. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["The findings support diabetic ketoacidosis, requiring urgent monitored team-based treatment.","The findings support hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state because dehydration accompanies elevated glucose.","The findings support starvation ketosis because vomiting can produce ketones and acidosis."],"feedback":["Elevated ketones with metabolic acidosis and hyperglycemia form the characteristic DKA pattern, and missed insulin is a common precipitant.","HHS is more consistent with extreme hyperglycemia and dehydration without significant ketonemia or metabolic acidosis.","Starvation ketosis can cause ketosis and mild acidosis, but the supplied pattern of diabetes, hyperglycemia, marked ketonemia, and acidosis supports DKA."]},{"stem":"An adult with suspected DKA has an insulin infusion ordered. The latest laboratory result shows potassium below the level at which the local protocol permits insulin initiation. The patient is alert but tachycardic. What is the nurse’s priority action?","options":["Hold initiation according to protocol and promptly contact the responsible clinician about potassium management.","Begin the ordered insulin and recheck potassium after the first scheduled monitoring interval.","Request urine ketone testing before deciding whether the insulin order should proceed."],"feedback":["Insulin can shift potassium into cells and further lower serum potassium, so the nurse should follow the protocol and urgently communicate the result before initiation.","Starting insulin despite potassium below the protocol threshold risks worsening hypokalemia, which can be life-threatening.","Urine ketones may not reflect current ketone burden reliably and do not address the immediate potassium-related safety concern."]},{"stem":"An adult with type 2 diabetes taking an SGLT-2 inhibitor presents with nausea, rapid deep breathing, and fatigue. Glucose is only mildly elevated, but blood beta-hydroxybutyrate is elevated and testing shows metabolic acidosis. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Suspect euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis and promptly escalate the findings to the care team.","Consider hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state unlikely because the glucose is not extremely elevated.","Attribute the ketones to fasting alone and defer escalation unless glucose rises substantially."],"feedback":["DKA can occur with normal or mildly elevated glucose in people taking SGLT-2 inhibitors; ketonemia and acidosis warrant urgent escalation.","The glucose pattern does not establish HHS, which is associated with severe hyperglycemia and hyperosmolality without significant ketoacidosis.","Fasting may cause ketosis, but the combination of diabetes, elevated blood ketones, acidosis, and SGLT-2 inhibitor use cannot safely be dismissed."]},{"stem":"During ordered DKA treatment, an adult’s glucose is falling and the patient is more alert. Urine ketones remain positive, while the acid-base results are improving. Which reassessment best avoids being misled by a single test?","options":["Review the ordered acid-base and blood ketone trends, continue potassium monitoring, and report deterioration or concerning results.","Treat persistent urine ketones as proof that acidosis is worsening and immediately request a higher insulin rate.","Use improved alertness and falling glucose as sufficient evidence that ketoacidosis has resolved."],"feedback":["Urine ketones can lag or overstate ketonemia during resolution; blood ketone and acid-base trends, alongside potassium and clinical status, provide a more useful reassessment.","Persistent urine ketones alone do not prove worsening acidosis, and changing insulin treatment requires team direction and relevant trend data.","Improving glucose and alertness are encouraging but do not by themselves establish resolution of ketonemia or acidosis."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Hyperglycemic Crises","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/endotext/hyperglycm-dka-hhs/"},{"title":"Acute Complications of Diabetes Mellitus - Endocrinology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/diabetes-mellitus-and-hypoglycemia/acute-complications-of-diabetes-mellitus"}],"frames":[{"label":"Insulin deficit effects","detail":"Insufficient insulin drives ketone production and acidosis, while hyperglycemia causes osmotic fluid and electrolyte losses."},{"label":"DKA pattern with hypokalemia","detail":"Vomiting, deep breathing, elevated beta-hydroxybutyrate, acidosis, and low potassium support urgent concern for DKA."},{"label":"Clarify insulin plan","detail":"Before ordered insulin begins, promptly communicate low potassium and DKA findings; clarify the potassium-related plan under local protocol."},{"label":"Recheck metabolic status","detail":"Reassess potassium, ketones, acid-base status, perfusion, and mental status; urgently report falling potassium, hypotension, or declining consciousness."}]},{"id":"s060","unit":"endo","title":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state","objective":"Recognize the pattern of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state and describe nursing priorities for urgent, protocol-guided assessment, treatment support, and reassessment.","concept":"In hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, insufficient insulin action allows severe hyperglycemia, while enough insulin often remains to limit major ketone production. Glucose in the urine draws water and electrolytes with it, causing profound dehydration and reduced kidney perfusion; this can worsen hyperglycemia and raise serum osmolality. Hypertonicity can impair brain function, so fluid status, mental status, glucose, electrolytes, and treatment pace must be considered together.","recognize":"Consider HHS when an adult has days of thirst, frequent urination, weakness, or blurred vision followed by marked dehydration and altered thinking, with severe hyperglycemia and elevated osmolality but little ketosis or acidosis. Confusion, seizures, or focal deficits may occur. Prominent ketones and metabolic acidosis favor DKA or a mixed presentation; infection or another acute illness may precipitate either.","respond":"Treat suspected HHS as an emergency: promptly notify the responsible clinician or rapid response team according to local policy, support immediate assessment, and communicate mental status, perfusion, glucose, and available laboratory findings. Facilitate ordered fluid therapy and frequent monitoring; verify potassium and follow protocol before insulin is administered. Avoid assuming a normal or high initial potassium means body stores are adequate, or allowing overly rapid correction.","reassess":"After ordered fluids or insulin, trend mental status, vital signs, urine output, glucose, potassium, renal markers, and osmolality at the prescribed frequency. Escalate worsening consciousness, unstable perfusion, poor urine output, a concerning potassium trend, or glucose or osmolality falling too rapidly.","trap":"A potassium level that looks normal or high is not a full tank gauge: HHS can involve substantial total body potassium loss despite that blood result. Insulin can shift potassium into cells, so potassium assessment and protocol-directed correction must precede insulin when indicated.","caseStory":"An older adult with type 2 diabetes arrives after several days of frequent urination and poor intake, now confused and profoundly dry. Glucose and osmolality are markedly elevated, while ketones are minimal and pH is near normal; the initial potassium is within the laboratory reference range. The nurse must decide what to communicate and verify before insulin begins.","caseReason":"The prolonged course, dehydration, altered mentation, hyperosmolality, and limited ketosis or acidosis fit HHS more than classic DKA, though overlap remains possible. The nurse should urgently communicate the presentation, support ordered fluid replacement, and verify potassium management under protocol before insulin, because a seemingly acceptable serum value does not exclude total body depletion.","teachback":"In a future adult with severe hyperglycemia and new confusion, what cues would lead you to suspect HHS, and what would you check or communicate before and after insulin is started?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with type 2 diabetes has had several days of polyuria and thirst and now presents with marked dehydration and confusion. Testing shows severe hyperglycemia and high serum osmolality, with minimal ketones and no substantial acidosis. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pattern is most consistent with HHS, while possible overlap and a precipitating illness still need evaluation.","The pattern establishes classic DKA because altered mental status is a defining sign of ketoacidosis.","The pattern is unlikely to be a hyperglycemic emergency because minimal ketones exclude serious metabolic illness."],"feedback":["Severe hyperglycemia, hyperosmolality, dehydration, and altered mentation with minimal ketosis or acidosis are characteristic of HHS; mixed presentations and precipitants still warrant consideration.","Confusion can occur in HHS from hypertonicity, while classic DKA is distinguished by significant ketosis and metabolic acidosis.","Minimal ketones do not make this benign; HHS is life-threatening and can cause profound dehydration and neurologic changes."]},{"stem":"A critically ill adult with suspected HHS has severe dehydration and an elevated glucose. The initial potassium result is below the expected range, and an insulin infusion is being prepared under the unit protocol. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Promptly alert the responsible clinician and follow the protocol to address potassium before insulin proceeds.","Proceed with insulin because lowering glucose will correct the potassium deficit.","Delay communication until a repeat glucose confirms that fluids alone are insufficient."],"feedback":["Insulin shifts potassium into cells and can worsen hypokalemia; the nurse should urgently communicate the result and follow protocol for potassium management before insulin.","Insulin does not correct total body potassium depletion and may lower serum potassium further, creating risk.","A concerning potassium result needs prompt attention; waiting for another glucose trend could delay necessary safety measures."]},{"stem":"An older adult with heart failure develops severe hyperglycemia, dehydration, and increasing confusion after an infection. The team is evaluating for HHS and orders fluid replacement. Which nursing action best transfers HHS priorities to this context?","options":["Facilitate ordered fluid therapy while closely reporting perfusion, respiratory status, urine output, and mental status for individualized reassessment.","Withhold all ordered fluid because heart failure makes volume replacement inappropriate in HHS.","Encourage rapid fluid administration without reassessment because restoring volume takes priority over cardiac history."],"feedback":["HHS requires restoration of volume, but older adults and people with heart failure may need smaller or carefully reassessed fluid administration to avoid overload.","Heart failure affects how fluids are administered, but does not automatically eliminate the need to treat dehydration; coordinate with the team.","Unmonitored rapid fluid delivery can cause overload in this context; response and tolerance should be closely observed and communicated."]},{"stem":"An adult being treated for HHS has a serum potassium result within the reference range before insulin. After treatment begins, the patient becomes weaker and the next potassium result is lower. Which interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["Total body potassium depletion may have been present despite the initial result, and insulin can lower serum potassium; promptly report the trend and follow replacement and monitoring orders.","The initial potassium result rules out depletion, so the later change is unlikely to matter unless the patient develops an arrhythmia.","The lower potassium confirms that HHS has changed into DKA, so fluid therapy should be stopped."],"feedback":["HHS can cause substantial potassium loss while serum potassium initially appears normal or elevated; insulin shifts potassium into cells, so a falling level requires prompt communication and protocol-guided management.","A single initial blood level does not reflect total body stores, and waiting for an arrhythmia risks delayed response to a potentially important trend.","A potassium decline does not diagnose DKA, and stopping fluids is not supported by this finding; assess the full metabolic picture and communicate promptly."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar Syndrome","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/endotext/hyper_synd/"},{"title":"DIAGNOSIS AND...","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK620700/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Hyperosmolar dehydration","detail":"Limited insulin action can cause severe hyperglycemia, osmotic water loss, dehydration, reduced kidney perfusion, and rising osmolality."},{"label":"HHS pattern cues","detail":"Confusion and profound dehydration with marked hyperglycemia, high osmolality, and minimal ketosis or acidosis suggest HHS."},{"label":"Urgent fluid coordination","detail":"Urgently notify the clinician or rapid response per policy; support ordered fluids and verify potassium management before insulin."},{"label":"Track correction trends","detail":"Trend mental status, urine output, potassium, renal markers, glucose, and osmolality; escalate declining consciousness, poor perfusion, or glucose or osmolality falling too rapidly."}]},{"id":"s061","unit":"endo","title":"Hypoglycemia and inpatient diabetes","objective":"Use symptoms, glucose findings, and clinical context to recognize possible inpatient hypoglycemia and take safe, timely nursing action.","concept":"Glucose is the brain’s key fuel, while insulin treatment and illness can shift the balance between intake, glucose use, and available reserves. Falling glucose triggers counter-regulatory responses, but recurrent episodes, long-standing diabetes, critical illness, or age can blunt warning symptoms. A measured value, symptoms, recent food and medication timing, and illness context must therefore be interpreted together. Individualized inpatient control aims to avoid harm from both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia.","recognize":"Look for sweating, tremor, palpitations, hunger, or anxiety, as well as confusion, drowsiness, unusual behavior, speech difficulty, poor coordination, seizure, or reduced consciousness. Check glucose promptly when cues arise, but do not rely on symptoms alone because unawareness is common. Chronic hyperglycemia may cause symptoms at comparatively higher readings, while acute neurologic changes can resemble stroke and need urgent assessment.","respond":"Stay with the patient, protect against falls, obtain a bedside glucose, and alert the responsible clinician for concerning findings or severe change. If awake and able to swallow, provide fast-acting carbohydrate under the care plan or local protocol. If unable to take it safely, seek urgent help and follow ordered or facility treatment pathways. Never give oral food or drink to an unconscious patient.","reassess":"Recheck glucose at the interval required by local protocol, and reassess alertness, symptoms, swallowing safety, and whether improvement lasts. Document the reading, cues, action, and response, and communicate likely contributors such as missed food or medication timing. Persistent or recurrent low readings, worsening neurologic status, or failure to improve warrants escalation.","trap":"A low glucose reading is not the whole story, just as a monitor alarm is not a diagnosis: symptoms, measurement, and context all matter. Conversely, absent sweating does not rule out hypoglycemia; impaired awareness can leave confusion as the first visible cue.","caseStory":"An adult with diabetes in the medical ICU receives insulin, but a planned meal is delayed. The patient becomes sweaty and then confused, and the bedside glucose is low by the hospital’s protocol. The nurse must decide whether to attribute the change to illness or promptly respond to possible hypoglycemia.","caseReason":"Insulin exposure combined with interrupted food intake makes hypoglycemia a plausible explanation, and confusion can be a neuroglycopenic cue even if symptoms change over time. The nurse should stay with the patient, follow the local response pathway, and notify the responsible clinician rather than waiting for more symptoms or assuming the change is only due to illness.","teachback":"When an inpatient has new confusion, how would you combine the glucose result, symptoms, recent intake and medication timing, and illness context to decide what to do and what to reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult inpatient with diabetes received insulin before a meal, but the meal tray was delayed. The patient is now newly confused without tremor or sweating, and the bedside glucose is low by the facility protocol. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these findings?","options":["Consider hypoglycemia with a neuroglycopenic presentation, begin the local response pathway, and do not wait for autonomic symptoms.","Favor illness-related delirium because autonomic symptoms are absent, and repeat the glucose after the next meal.","Treat the change as a possible stroke only and defer the hypoglycemia response until neurologic assessment is complete."],"feedback":["Confusion can occur when the brain is deprived of glucose, and hospitalized patients may have few or no warning symptoms. The insulin and delayed meal add relevant context, so prompt action under local protocol is appropriate.","Absence of sweating or tremor does not exclude hypoglycemia, particularly when awareness is impaired. Waiting for a meal could delay needed assessment and treatment.","Neurologic changes can resemble stroke, but checking and responding to possible hypoglycemia is also urgent. A stroke-like presentation should not cause the nurse to defer the glucose response."]},{"stem":"An adult inpatient taking a sulfonylurea is found unresponsive. The nurse has not yet checked a glucose level, and the patient cannot safely swallow. What is the best immediate nursing action?","options":["Stay with the patient, summon urgent assistance, check bedside glucose, and follow the facility’s emergency pathway without giving anything by mouth.","Place juice at the bedside and give it when the patient briefly opens their eyes, then check glucose if symptoms continue.","Wait for the responsible clinician to arrive before checking glucose or taking any action, since the cause is not yet confirmed."],"feedback":["Unresponsiveness requires urgent help and immediate assessment, including a bedside glucose. Oral intake is unsafe when swallowing cannot be established, so the nurse should follow the facility pathway.","Briefly opening the eyes does not establish safe swallowing. Oral juice could be unsafe, and delaying the glucose check could postpone appropriate care.","The nurse can check glucose and activate the emergency pathway while notifying the clinician. Waiting without assessing or seeking help is not a safe response to unresponsiveness."]},{"stem":"An adult without known diabetes is hospitalized with sepsis, renal failure, and poor intake. The patient becomes drowsy, and a glucose check confirms a low result. No glucose-lowering medication is listed. Which nursing response is most appropriate?","options":["Treat the finding as clinically important, follow the local response pathway, and promptly notify the team while communicating the critical illness and poor intake.","Assume fasting explains the result in a person without diabetes and repeat the check at the next routine observation.","Assume the bedside result is inaccurate because no diabetes medication is listed and wait for laboratory confirmation before alerting the team."],"feedback":["Critical illness and inadequate intake can contribute to hypoglycemia even without diabetes medication. Drowsiness adds a concerning clinical cue, so prompt action and team communication are warranted.","Fasting alone should not be used to dismiss a low result in a critically ill, drowsy patient. Delaying assessment could miss an important clinical problem.","Hypoglycemia can occur in critical illness without glucose-lowering medication. The nurse should respond to the confirmed finding and communicate it rather than waiting to alert the team."]},{"stem":"An adult with long-standing, poorly controlled diabetes reports shakiness. The bedside glucose is within the patient’s usual range and not low by the hospital protocol. The patient remains alert. Which approach best distinguishes a misleading symptom pattern from a confirmed low glucose finding?","options":["Recognize that symptoms may occur at a comparatively higher glucose level, verify and reassess the reading under local procedure, and report persistent or worsening symptoms.","Document confirmed hypoglycemia based on shakiness alone and treat without checking the reading or the patient’s response.","Rule out a glucose-related concern because the reading is not low, and stop assessing unless autonomic symptoms become stronger."],"feedback":["Chronic hyperglycemia can shift the level at which symptoms are noticed, so symptoms deserve assessment even when the reading is not low. Verifying the result and monitoring for persistence or worsening avoids both dismissal and overstatement.","Symptoms alone do not establish a documented low glucose finding. The nurse should check and interpret the measurement with the patient’s presentation and local procedure.","A reading that is not low does not make symptoms irrelevant, especially when chronic hyperglycemia may shift symptom thresholds. Continued assessment is appropriate."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Understanding hypoglycemia in hospitalized patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4153389/"},{"title":"Hypoglycemia (Nursing) - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelfwww.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov › books › NBK568695","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568695/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Delayed Meal, Insulin","detail":"Insulin is given while a planned meal is delayed, shifting the balance between glucose use and available intake."},{"label":"Sweating With Confusion","detail":"Sweating followed by confusion and a low bedside glucose supports hypoglycemia, even when illness could also explain behavior."},{"label":"Follow Local Pathway","detail":"Stay with the patient, protect against falls, check glucose, and alert the clinician; give oral carbohydrate only if awake and safe to swallow."},{"label":"Persistent Low Escalation","detail":"Recheck per local protocol and reassess alertness; persistent low glucose, worsening neurologic status, or no improvement requires escalation."}]},{"id":"s062","unit":"endo","title":"Water balance: DI and SIADH","objective":"Differentiate diabetes insipidus from SIADH and use assessment trends to prioritize safe nursing actions and escalation.","concept":"Antidiuretic hormone helps the kidneys retain water. Too little effective ADH activity, from deficient release or impaired renal response, can cause large volumes of dilute urine, dehydration, and rising serum sodium and osmolality. Excess ADH retains water, diluting serum sodium and osmolality and risking cerebral edema. Connecting urine pattern, fluid status, laboratory trends, and neurologic findings guides nursing surveillance and timely team communication.","recognize":"With DI, look for persistent thirst and large urine volumes, often with dehydration cues such as dry mucosa, tachycardia, or hypotension; urine osmolality may be low while serum osmolality and sodium rise. SIADH more often presents with low serum sodium and osmolality, concentrated urine, and neurologic or gastrointestinal symptoms. Use the full assessment and investigate other causes rather than relying on one finding.","respond":"Prioritize neurologic status, vital signs, intake and output, daily weight, and relevant laboratory trends. Communicate changes promptly and coordinate investigation and treatment with the clinical team. Carry out prescribed fluid management and medications only under orders and local protocols; use seizure precautions when indicated by hyponatremia risk. Avoid assuming that thirst or urine output alone establishes the diagnosis or changing fluid or medication plans independently.","reassess":"After an intervention, compare intake and output, same-time daily weight, vital signs, mental status, and sodium and osmolality trends. Assess hydration cues in suspected DI and neurologic changes in suspected SIADH. Escalate promptly for worsening confusion, seizure, reduced consciousness, hemodynamic instability, or concerning laboratory trends.","trap":"A normal-looking fluid examination does not rule out SIADH; excess retained water can dilute serum sodium even when obvious edema is absent. Think of the laboratory pattern and neurologic status as the dashboard, not a single visible sign as the whole story.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult repeatedly asks for water and produces frequent large volumes of clear urine, including overnight. The patient has dry mucosa and a rising serum sodium and osmolality, while urine osmolality is low. The nurse must decide whether this pattern suggests DI and what to communicate first.","caseReason":"The combination of thirst, high-volume dilute urine, dehydration cues, and rising serum concentration fits impaired ADH effect more than SIADH, which is associated with water retention and low serum sodium and osmolality. The nurse should promptly communicate the pattern and continue close fluid-balance, vital-sign, and laboratory monitoring while the team evaluates the cause and plan.","teachback":"In a new patient, which combination of urine findings, serum trends, and clinical cues would make you suspect DI rather than SIADH, and what change would prompt you to escalate?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult inpatient reports intense thirst and has frequent large volumes of clear urine. The nurse notes dry mucosa and tachycardia; serum sodium and osmolality are elevated, while urine osmolality is low. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["The findings support DI because water loss in dilute urine is accompanied by increasing serum concentration.","The findings support SIADH because excess ADH causes high-volume dilute urine and rising serum sodium.","The findings suggest fluid overload because thirst and tachycardia are expected with retained body water."],"feedback":["This pattern is consistent with DI: impaired ADH effect limits renal water retention, producing dilute urine and possible dehydration with increased serum sodium and osmolality.","SIADH causes excess water retention and is associated with low serum sodium and osmolality, not this combination of dilute polyuria and rising serum concentration.","Fluid overload does not explain the large dilute urine volumes, dry mucosa, or rising serum concentration; assess and communicate the complete pattern."]},{"stem":"An adult with suspected SIADH has low serum sodium and osmolality. During assessment, the patient becomes increasingly confused and then has a seizure. What is the nurse's priority response?","options":["Protect the patient from injury, initiate seizure precautions and emergency response per protocol, and promptly notify the clinical team.","Offer oral fluids and recheck the sodium later, because confusion may reflect inadequate hydration.","Wait for the next scheduled laboratory result before changing the observation plan, because the seizure has ended."],"feedback":["Seizure and worsening confusion with hyponatremia signal urgent neurologic risk. Immediate safety measures and prompt team notification are priorities while following emergency protocols.","SIADH involves excess water relative to sodium, and additional fluids may worsen dilution; this response also delays urgent safety actions.","A seizure requires immediate safety response and escalation; waiting for a scheduled result risks missing worsening cerebral effects of hyponatremia."]},{"stem":"An adult admitted with pulmonary disease has nausea, headache, and new lethargy. Laboratory results show low serum sodium and osmolality, and urine sodium is elevated; the patient has no visible edema. Which nursing interpretation and next step are most appropriate?","options":["SIADH is a concern despite absent edema; assess neurologic status closely and communicate the findings for evaluation of causes and treatment.","SIADH is unlikely without edema; focus on documenting nausea and repeat the assessment at the next routine interval.","DI is most likely because pulmonary disease and lethargy indicate deficient ADH; encourage unrestricted oral fluids."],"feedback":["SIADH can present with a normal-appearing fluid examination while excess water lowers serum sodium and osmolality. Neurologic changes warrant close assessment and communication with the team.","The absence of visible edema does not exclude SIADH, and new lethargy with low sodium and osmolality merits timely attention rather than routine delay.","The laboratory pattern and symptoms fit possible SIADH more than DI; unrestricted fluids could worsen water retention and sodium dilution."]},{"stem":"An adult with SIADH is following a prescribed fluid restriction. At reassessment, intake is lower and urine output has changed, but the patient is newly drowsy. Which approach best evaluates whether the plan is safe and effective?","options":["Review mental status, intake and output, daily weight, and sodium and osmolality trends, then promptly report the new drowsiness.","Use reduced urine output as proof that SIADH has resolved and continue the plan without reviewing laboratory trends.","Use the absence of edema as evidence of improvement and defer reassessment unless the patient reports thirst."],"feedback":["No single fluid-balance measure proves resolution. New drowsiness may signal worsening hyponatremia, so assess neurologic status and trends and communicate the change promptly.","Urine output alone cannot establish resolution, and the new neurologic finding requires reassessment rather than reliance on one measure.","SIADH may occur without visible edema, and absence of thirst does not establish safety; laboratory and neurologic trends remain important."]}],"sources":[{"title":"21.4 Pituitary Disorders - Medical-Surgical Nursing","url":"https://openstax.org/books/medical-surgical-nursing/pages/21-4-pituitary-disorders"},{"title":"Disorders of sodium balance - PMC","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1410848/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Reduced ADH Effect","detail":"Reduced effective ADH activity can cause large volumes of dilute urine, dehydration, and rising serum sodium and osmolality."},{"label":"Pattern Suggests DI","detail":"Thirst, dry mucosa, high-volume clear urine, low urine osmolality, and rising serum concentration fit DI more than SIADH."},{"label":"Communicate Fluid Pattern","detail":"Promptly communicate the pattern and continue intake-output, vital-sign, and laboratory monitoring while the team evaluates the cause and plan."},{"label":"Neurologic or Vital Changes","detail":"Reassess mental status, vital signs, fluid balance, and laboratory trends; worsening confusion, seizure, instability, or concerning results warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s063","unit":"endo","title":"Adrenal crisis","objective":"Recognize possible adrenal crisis and prioritize timely nursing escalation, team-based treatment, and reassessment.","concept":"Adrenal crisis occurs when cortisol availability cannot meet basal or stress needs, because of primary adrenal failure or disruption of the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis. Cortisol supports vascular responsiveness to catecholamines and glucose production, so deficiency can cause hypotension, shock, and hypoglycemia. In primary failure, aldosterone may also be deficient, worsening sodium loss and potassium retention; central failure usually preserves aldosterone. Illness can rapidly precipitate collapse.","recognize":"Suspect crisis when acute weakness, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, or altered alertness accompanies hypotension, especially with known adrenal insufficiency, steroid interruption, or recent illness. Electrolyte abnormalities can support concern, but normal potassium does not exclude central adrenal insufficiency. Infection may trigger crisis, so fever does not rule it out; consider adrenal crisis when shock is unexplained or poorly responsive to usual measures.","respond":"Treat suspected crisis as an emergency: promptly notify the responsible clinician and activate the local escalation pathway for instability. Communicate adrenal history, steroid exposure or interruption, symptoms, vital-sign trends, and relevant findings. Under orders and local protocols, support timely evaluation and prepare for prescribed parenteral glucocorticoid and fluid resuscitation. Do not independently prescribe treatment or delay escalation while waiting for a definitive diagnosis.","reassess":"After nursing actions, trend blood pressure, heart rate, alertness, symptoms, fluid response, and ordered glucose and electrolyte results. Report persistent or worsening hypotension, deteriorating consciousness, or signs of shock promptly. Improvement after treatment supports the concern but does not remove the need to evaluate and address a precipitating illness.","trap":"A normal potassium result is not an all-clear signal: central adrenal insufficiency often preserves aldosterone, unlike many cases of primary adrenal failure. Think of cortisol as support for the circulation's response to stress; without that support, blood pressure can fail even when potassium offers no warning.","caseStory":"A patient taking long-term glucocorticoids stopped them abruptly several days ago and now has vomiting, marked weakness, and low blood pressure during a gastrointestinal illness. The patient is less alert than earlier, but potassium is within the reported reference range. The nurse must decide whether the normal potassium makes adrenal crisis unlikely or whether urgent escalation is needed.","caseReason":"Abruptly stopping chronic glucocorticoids can suppress the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and illness can increase cortisol needs. A normal potassium does not exclude central adrenal insufficiency because aldosterone is generally preserved. The combination of steroid interruption, acute illness, hypotension, and declining alertness warrants immediate escalation for possible crisis.","teachback":"When a patient with possible adrenal insufficiency has acute illness and hypotension, which cues would you communicate, and why should a normal potassium result not end your concern?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient who has taken glucocorticoids long term stopped them without clinical advice. During a gastrointestinal illness, the patient develops vomiting, weakness, hypotension, and hyponatremia; potassium is normal. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next step?","options":["Consider adrenal crisis from suppressed cortisol production and escalate promptly despite the normal potassium.","Consider isolated gastrointestinal fluid loss most likely and defer escalation until potassium becomes abnormal.","Consider primary adrenal failure unlikely and rule out an adrenal cause because potassium is normal."],"feedback":["Correct. Chronic glucocorticoid use can suppress the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and acute illness can precipitate crisis. Aldosterone is usually preserved in central insufficiency, so normal potassium does not exclude it.","Vomiting may contribute to volume loss, but it does not account for the risk created by steroid interruption or remove concern about acute adrenal insufficiency. Hypotension in this setting calls for prompt escalation.","Normal potassium may be less expected with aldosterone deficiency in primary adrenal failure, but it cannot rule out central adrenal insufficiency. The patient's history and acute instability remain important cues."]},{"stem":"A patient with known adrenal insufficiency develops fever, repeated vomiting, falling blood pressure, and increasing confusion. Which nursing action is the highest priority?","options":["Promptly escalate the instability and communicate the adrenal history while preparing for prescribed emergency treatment under local protocols.","Continue routine observations until the infection evaluation clarifies whether the fever explains the low blood pressure.","Wait for electrolyte results before contacting the responsible clinician about possible adrenal crisis."],"feedback":["Correct. Adrenal crisis is a life-threatening emergency, and infection can precipitate it. Prompt escalation supports timely team evaluation, fluid resuscitation, and prescribed parenteral glucocorticoid treatment.","Fever and infection do not exclude adrenal crisis and may be the precipitating stressor. Waiting with worsening hypotension and confusion risks delaying emergency care.","Electrolytes can support evaluation, but the emergency is recognized from the clinical picture and history. Waiting for results can delay urgent assessment and treatment."]},{"stem":"A patient several days after pituitary surgery develops nausea, dizziness, and hypotension during a new respiratory infection. The patient has no skin darkening and potassium is normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Consider central adrenal insufficiency with possible crisis, since pituitary disruption can impair cortisol while aldosterone is preserved.","Consider adrenal crisis unlikely, since normal potassium and no skin darkening argue against a central cause.","Consider primary adrenal failure most likely, since infection alone explains the absence of pigmentation and normal potassium."],"feedback":["Correct. Pituitary surgery can lead to central adrenal insufficiency, and infection may precipitate acute deterioration. Aldosterone is generally preserved in central insufficiency, so normal potassium and absent pigmentation do not rule it out.","These findings do not exclude central adrenal insufficiency. Skin pigmentation is associated with long-standing primary adrenal insufficiency, while potassium may remain normal when aldosterone is preserved.","Infection can be a trigger, but it does not establish primary adrenal failure. The recent pituitary surgery makes central insufficiency a relevant concern, and the presentation warrants escalation."]},{"stem":"A patient with suspected adrenal crisis receives prescribed parenteral glucocorticoid and fluid resuscitation under the care plan. Blood pressure improves somewhat, potassium remains normal, but the patient becomes harder to arouse. What should the nurse do?","options":["Escalate the worsening alertness promptly and continue trending blood pressure and other ordered assessments.","Continue routine observation because a normal potassium and some blood pressure improvement indicate resolution.","Focus on the blood pressure trend alone and wait for the next scheduled assessment of alertness."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening consciousness is deterioration and warrants prompt escalation even if one measure improves. Ongoing assessment is needed because adrenal crisis can progress to encephalopathy and shock.","Normal potassium does not exclude central adrenal insufficiency, and partial blood pressure improvement does not establish resolution. Worsening alertness requires immediate communication.","Alertness is an important clinical cue, not a finding to defer when it is worsening. Assessing only blood pressure could miss progression to metabolic encephalopathy."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Adrenal Crisis - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499968/"},{"title":"Adrenal Insufficiency","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/endotext/adren-insufficiency/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Steroid Interruption, Illness","detail":"Stopping long-term glucocorticoids abruptly can suppress the stress response, while gastrointestinal illness increases concern for inadequate cortisol availability."},{"label":"Hypotension and Weakness","detail":"Vomiting, marked weakness, low blood pressure, and declining alertness after steroid interruption suggest possible adrenal crisis; normal potassium does not exclude it."},{"label":"Escalate Possible Crisis","detail":"Promptly notify the clinician and activate the local pathway; communicate steroid history, illness, symptoms, vital-sign trends, and relevant findings."},{"label":"Trend for Deterioration","detail":"Reassess blood pressure, alertness, symptoms, and fluid response; persistent hypotension, worsening consciousness, or signs of shock require prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s064","unit":"endo","title":"Severe thyroid dysfunction","objective":"Recognize patterns of life-threatening thyroid hormone excess or deficiency and promptly escalate care while supporting assessment and treatment under orders and local protocols.","concept":"Severe thyroid dysfunction can present at opposite ends of thyroid hormone activity. Thyroid storm is an acute, life-threatening worsening of severe hyperthyroidism, sometimes triggered by infection, trauma, or surgery. Myxedema coma is a life-threatening complication of longstanding hypothyroidism, often precipitated by illness, cold exposure, or medicines that suppress the central nervous system. Recognizing these patterns matters because both require rapid team evaluation and supportive care.","recognize":"Consider thyroid storm when severe hyperthyroid features abruptly worsen, with findings such as fever, marked restlessness or confusion, gastrointestinal symptoms, tachycardia, or cardiovascular collapse. Consider myxedema coma in a patient with known or suspected hypothyroidism who develops profound hypothermia, reduced responsiveness, seizures, or respiratory depression. An older adult may show confusion or weakness rather than classic hyperthyroid symptoms, so assess the whole pattern and history.","respond":"Treat either suspected crisis as an emergency: promptly notify the responsible clinician and activate the local emergency response process. Support immediate assessment of breathing, oxygenation, circulation, mental status, temperature, and cardiac rhythm, and communicate relevant thyroid history, medicines, recent illness, and observed changes. Prepare to assist with ordered testing and treatment. Do not delay escalation while seeking a single confirmatory symptom or laboratory result.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess responsiveness, breathing and oxygenation, vital signs, temperature, and cardiac rhythm, and report trends to the team. Escalate immediately for worsening confusion, seizures, respiratory depression, hemodynamic instability, or cardiovascular collapse. In suspected myxedema coma, avoid rapid rewarming unless specifically directed by the treating team.","trap":"A normal T3 does not rule out hypothyroidism; it can remain normal even when primary thyroid failure is present. Think of T3 as one dashboard indicator, not the whole engine report: TSH and free T4 are central to assessment.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult with a history of hyperthyroidism develops fever, marked agitation, vomiting, and a rapid irregular pulse during an infection. The patient becomes increasingly confused. The nurse must decide whether to wait for thyroid test results or escalate the acute change now.","caseReason":"The abrupt combination of severe hyperthyroid features and acute illness is concerning for thyroid storm, a life-threatening emergency. Infection can precipitate it, and confusion adds concern. The nurse should promptly escalate and support assessment and treatment under orders and local protocols rather than wait for a test result.","teachback":"A patient with known hypothyroidism becomes unusually cold, difficult to arouse, and slow to breathe during an acute illness. Which findings would prompt you to suspect a crisis, and what would you do first within your nursing role?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with known hyperthyroidism develops fever, severe restlessness, vomiting, confusion, and a rapid pulse after a recent infection. Which interpretation best fits this cluster and should guide the nurse’s immediate response?","options":["Suspect thyroid storm and promptly escalate this life-threatening change while supporting assessment and care under orders.","Consider an uncomplicated hyperthyroid presentation and continue routine observation until thyroid results are available.","Suspect myxedema coma because confusion and gastrointestinal symptoms can occur in severe thyroid dysfunction."],"feedback":["Correct. Severe hyperthyroid features with acute illness and confusion raise concern for thyroid storm, which requires prompt escalation.","This underestimates an acute, potentially life-threatening change; the nurse should not wait for laboratory confirmation before escalating.","Confusion and gastrointestinal symptoms alone are not specific, but fever, agitation, and a rapid pulse in a patient with hyperthyroidism better fit thyroid storm than myxedema coma."]},{"stem":"A patient with a long history of hypothyroidism is found difficult to arouse, markedly hypothermic, and breathing slowly after an acute illness. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Promptly activate the local emergency response and support immediate breathing and oxygenation assessment while notifying the treating team.","Begin routine thyroid replacement teaching and request a standard outpatient thyroid follow-up.","Warm the patient rapidly before contacting the treating team to correct the low temperature first."],"feedback":["Correct. Hypothermia, reduced responsiveness, and respiratory depression in this context are concerning for myxedema coma, which requires rapid evaluation and supportive care.","Routine follow-up is not appropriate for a possible life-threatening crisis with impaired responsiveness and breathing.","Rapid rewarming may precipitate hypotension or arrhythmias in myxedema coma; escalate urgently and follow the treating team’s direction."]},{"stem":"An adult taking a medicine that can suppress the central nervous system becomes confused, profoundly cold, and slow to breathe during a respiratory illness. There is no known thyroid history. Which nursing interpretation and action are most appropriate?","options":["Consider myxedema coma despite the absent history, and urgently escalate the change while supporting assessment of breathing and oxygenation.","Rule out myxedema coma because the patient has no documented thyroid diagnosis, and focus only on the respiratory illness.","Assume thyroid storm because acute infection and confusion are present, and defer escalation until a thyroid test distinguishes the conditions."],"feedback":["Correct. Illness and medicines that suppress the central nervous system can precipitate myxedema coma, and the clinical pattern warrants urgent escalation even without a known history.","Absence of a documented diagnosis does not outweigh the concerning combination of hypothermia, altered responsiveness, and respiratory depression.","Infection and confusion are not enough to identify thyroid storm; the marked coldness and respiratory depression with possible hypothyroidism support concern for myxedema coma, and escalation should not wait for test results."]},{"stem":"A patient being evaluated for possible primary hypothyroidism has fatigue and cold intolerance, an elevated TSH, low free T4, and a T3 result within the reference range. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate to the team?","options":["The normal T3 does not exclude primary hypothyroidism; the TSH and free T4 pattern supports it.","The normal T3 rules out hypothyroidism, so the other thyroid results are unlikely to be meaningful.","The elevated TSH with low free T4 indicates secondary hypothyroidism, so pituitary disease is established."],"feedback":["Correct. T3 often remains normal in primary hypothyroidism, so it is not a sensitive test for excluding the condition; elevated TSH with low free T4 supports primary hypothyroidism.","A normal T3 can be misleading and does not rule out hypothyroidism when TSH is elevated and free T4 is low.","That pattern supports primary, not secondary, hypothyroidism; secondary hypothyroidism is characterized by low free T4 with low or sometimes inappropriately normal TSH."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Hyperthyroidism","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/thyroid-disorders/hyperthyroidism"},{"title":"Hypothyroidism - Endocrinology - Merck Manual Professional...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/thyroid-disorders/hypothyroidism"}],"frames":[{"label":"Infection Triggers Worsening","detail":"An infection can precipitate an acute, life-threatening worsening of severe hyperthyroidism in a person with a history of thyroid disease."},{"label":"Storm Warning Pattern","detail":"Fever, marked agitation, vomiting, rapid irregular pulse, and increasing confusion are concerning for thyroid storm."},{"label":"Escalate Without Delay","detail":"Promptly notify the clinician and activate the local emergency process; support assessment of breathing, oxygenation, circulation, temperature, and rhythm."},{"label":"Track Acute Changes","detail":"Reassess responsiveness, breathing, oxygenation, vital signs, temperature, and cardiac rhythm; worsening confusion, instability, or cardiovascular collapse requires immediate escalation."}]},{"id":"s065","unit":"skin","title":"Pressure injury and skin perfusion","objective":"Use skin findings, pressure exposure, and tissue risk factors to prioritize nursing actions that prevent pressure injury and recognize when reassessment or escalation is needed.","concept":"Sustained pressure can restrict small-vessel blood flow, reducing oxygen delivery and damaging tissue; friction and shear add injury, while moisture can weaken skin. Limited mobility, impaired circulation, chronic illness, and inadequate nutrition reduce tissue tolerance. Pressure injury prevention therefore links skin assessment with pressure relief, moisture management, suitable support surfaces, mobility, and nutrition rather than relying on risk scores or equipment alone.","recognize":"Inspect bony prominences and areas beneath or near medical devices, noting skin color, integrity, temperature, firmness, and tenderness. Nonblanchable color change on intact skin suggests a pressure injury; blanchable redness is a warning to relieve pressure, not a staged injury. Purple or maroon change may signal deeper damage. Moisture-related dermatitis and traumatic skin damage have different causes and should not be mislabeled as pressure injury.","respond":"Relieve or redistribute pressure promptly, reposition according to the person’s needs, and avoid dragging, rubbing, or massaging reddened skin. Keep skin clean and dry, address incontinence, and use appropriate support surfaces without treating them as substitutes for movement. Communicate findings and risk factors to the care team, document changes, and follow local protocols for prevention and escalation.","reassess":"After pressure relief or repositioning, inspect the affected area and nearby skin for color, blanching, temperature, firmness, tenderness, and intactness. Check whether the position or device continues to create pressure and whether moisture is controlled. Persistent nonblanchable change, new discoloration, skin breakdown, or worsening pain warrants prompt team communication and escalation under local protocols.","trap":"A pressure-redistributing mattress is like a seat belt: useful protection, but it does not do the work of changing position. Likewise, blanchable redness is an early warning, not proof that tissue is unharmed if pressure continues.","caseStory":"A fictional patient recovering from hip fracture surgery has limited mobility and needs help changing position. During skin inspection, a nurse finds intact, persistent redness over the heel that does not fade with light pressure; the heel is also tender. The nurse must decide whether this is a cue for immediate pressure relief or simply a finding to document and revisit later.","caseReason":"Persistent nonblanchable redness on intact skin is consistent with an early pressure injury, and tenderness can be an early cue. The nurse should relieve heel pressure promptly, avoid friction, document the finding, and communicate it to the care team. Waiting risks continued tissue damage.","teachback":"When you find a new skin change in a patient who cannot reposition independently, how will you distinguish a warning sign from a pressure injury and decide what to do next?","questions":[{"stem":"A bedbound adult has intact skin over the sacrum with a localized red area that remains red when lightly pressed. The patient reports tenderness there. Which interpretation and nursing action best fit these findings?","options":["This is consistent with a pressure injury; relieve pressure now and report and document the finding.","This is blanchable warning redness; leave the patient in place and repeat the check later.","This is most likely moisture-associated dermatitis; cleanse the area and focus only on incontinence care."],"feedback":["Nonblanchable color change on intact skin is consistent with an early pressure injury, and tenderness supports concern. Pressure relief and communication are appropriate immediate nursing actions.","Blanchable redness is a warning sign, but the vignette specifically states the color persists with light pressure. Continuing the same position risks further tissue damage.","Moisture can cause dermatitis, but the described finding over a pressure-prone area with nonblanchable redness is more consistent with pressure injury. Moisture care alone would not relieve the pressure."]},{"stem":"An adult awaiting surgery has limited mobility. During a routine check, the nurse finds blanchable redness over a heel and damp skin after an episode of incontinence. Which action should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Offload the heel, reposition the patient, and promptly cleanse and dry the skin.","Document the redness and dampness, then wait for the next scheduled skin inspection.","Apply a support surface and leave the patient in the current position to avoid disturbing rest."],"feedback":["Blanchable redness signals pressure exposure and a need for pressure relief before injury develops. Prompt skin cleansing and drying also reduce moisture-related damage.","Documentation is useful, but delaying action leaves pressure and moisture in place despite visible risk cues.","A support surface can help redistribute pressure, but it does not replace repositioning. Leaving the patient in place also fails to address the damp skin."]},{"stem":"An adult in critical care requires oxygen by a device that rests against the ear. The nurse notices localized nonblanchable redness beneath the device, with intact skin. Which response best transfers pressure injury prevention to this situation?","options":["Recognize possible device-related pressure injury and promptly involve the care team to relieve pressure while maintaining necessary therapy.","Classify the redness as moisture-associated skin damage and manage it only with routine cleansing.","Wait for skin breakdown before changing the device position, since the skin remains intact."],"feedback":["Pressure injuries can occur beneath medical devices and may follow the device’s contact pattern. The finding warrants prompt pressure relief coordinated with the team so necessary therapy is maintained.","The location beneath a pressure-bearing device and the nonblanchable change point toward pressure injury rather than moisture damage. Cleansing alone does not address the pressure.","Intact skin can already have a pressure injury when nonblanchable redness is present. Waiting for an open wound allows avoidable tissue damage to progress."]},{"stem":"A nurse repositions an adult with darkly pigmented skin and finds a new area over the hip that appears subtly different from surrounding skin. It feels warmer and firmer, and the patient reports localized discomfort. Which next step is best?","options":["Treat the change as possible tissue injury, relieve pressure, and promptly communicate and document the findings.","Dismiss the finding because redness is difficult to see and the skin has no visible break.","Massage the area to improve circulation, then reassess whether the discomfort resolves."],"feedback":["Color changes may be less apparent in darkly pigmented skin, while altered temperature, firmness, or sensation can precede visible changes. Pressure relief and prompt communication are warranted.","Absence of an obvious color change or skin break does not exclude tissue injury. The warmth, firmness, and discomfort are meaningful assessment cues.","Massage can create friction and shear that damage vulnerable tissue. It is not an appropriate response to a possible pressure injury."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Pressure Injury Prevention and Wound Management - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK543831/"},{"title":"Pressure Injuries - Pressure Injuries - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-pt/professional/dermatologic-disorders/pressure-injury/pressure-injuries"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pressure, Limited Mobility","detail":"Limited mobility and sustained pressure can reduce tissue blood flow; friction, shear, and moisture can further damage vulnerable skin."},{"label":"Persistent Heel Redness","detail":"Intact heel skin with persistent redness that does not blanch under light pressure, especially with tenderness, suggests an early pressure injury."},{"label":"Relieve Heel Pressure","detail":"Relieve heel pressure promptly, avoid dragging or rubbing, document the finding, and communicate it to the care team."},{"label":"Check Skin Response","detail":"Reinspect the heel for color, tenderness, and intactness; persistent discoloration, new breakdown, or worsening pain warrants prompt team communication."}]},{"id":"s066","unit":"skin","title":"Infiltration and extravasation","objective":"Recognize possible infiltration or extravasation, initiate the appropriate immediate nursing response, and reassess for progression or complications.","concept":"Infiltration or extravasation occurs when infused fluid or medication leaks outside a vessel into surrounding tissue. Extravasated drugs may injure tissue, with risk influenced by the drug, its concentration, location, and duration of exposure. Vesicants can cause serious damage, while other infusates may cause less severe injury. Early recognition matters because promptly stopping delivery and following the drug-specific response process can limit further exposure.","recognize":"Suspect leakage with new pain, burning, tingling, redness, swelling, or a slowed or stopped infusion. Blood return may be poor, but no single cue establishes the diagnosis. With a central line, stinging pain or swelling near the port, chest, or insertion site also warrants concern. Pain along a vein with intact blood return and no edema or ulcer may suggest vessel irritation instead.","respond":"At the first concern, stop the infusion and disconnect the tubing from the cannula. Aspirate residual medication through the cannula, then follow local protocol for any drug-specific antidote and promptly notify the provider. Do not continue the infusion while assessing the site. Elevate the affected limb if appropriate, protect the area, document findings and actions, and involve the clinical team for further management.","reassess":"Recheck pain, sensation, color, temperature, swelling, skin integrity, mobility, and infusion-site findings, and document changes over time. Escalate promptly for worsening pain or edema, blistering, skin loss, limited movement, or concerns involving a central line. Continue follow-up according to local policy because symptoms may appear or progress later.","trap":"A functioning infusion or some blood return is not a safety certificate; leakage can still be present, like a pipe that drains partly while also leaking. Conversely, pain along a vein alone does not prove extravasation, so interpret it with swelling, skin changes, blood return, and the overall pattern.","caseStory":"During a vesicant infusion through a peripheral cannula, an adult patient reports new burning near the site. The nurse observes swelling and redness, and the infusion has slowed. The patient has no fever, and the nurse must decide whether to continue observing during infusion or treat this as possible extravasation.","caseReason":"Burning, swelling, redness, and reduced flow together raise concern for extravasation, even without fever. The nurse should stop the infusion, disconnect the tubing, aspirate residual medication through the cannula, and notify the provider while following the drug-specific local protocol.","teachback":"When a patient reports a new sensation during an infusion, which site findings and infusion changes would lead you to suspect leakage, and what would you do first?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient receiving a vesicant through a peripheral cannula reports burning. The site is swollen and red, and the infusion is running more slowly than before. Which interpretation and immediate action best fit these findings?","options":["Suspect extravasation, stop the infusion, and begin the local response process.","Suspect vessel irritation, continue the infusion, and reassess after it finishes.","Consider the findings nonspecific, adjust the infusion rate, and monitor for fever."],"feedback":["Burning, swelling, redness, and slowed flow together are concerning for leakage, particularly during vesicant administration; stop delivery and follow the response process.","Vessel irritation can cause pain along a vein, but swelling at the site and slowed flow make possible extravasation a concern that should not be managed by continuing the infusion.","Fever is not required for extravasation, and changing the rate while continuing delivery could prolong tissue exposure."]},{"stem":"An adult patient receiving an IV medication reports sudden stinging, and the nurse sees new edema around the cannula. Which action should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Stop the infusion, disconnect the tubing, and aspirate residual medication through the cannula.","Flush the cannula with saline to check patency, then decide whether to restart the medication.","Apply a compress first and wait to see whether the swelling resolves before interrupting delivery."],"feedback":["Stopping delivery, disconnecting the tubing, and aspirating residual medication are the recommended immediate nursing steps when extravasation is suspected.","Flushing or restarting could continue or increase exposure to leaked medication; assess and manage the suspected event instead.","A compress may be supportive care under local guidance, but it does not replace stopping the infusion and addressing residual medication promptly."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving medication through a central venous access device reports stinging pain and develops swelling near the chest insertion area. Blood return is absent. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Stop medication administration, notify the provider, and follow the central-line complication protocol.","Reposition the patient and continue the medication if the infusion begins to flow more freely.","Treat the finding as routine vessel irritation because pain can occur during IV medication administration."],"feedback":["Stinging pain, chest-area swelling, and absent blood return are concerning central-line findings; stop administration and promptly involve the provider and local protocol.","Improved flow after repositioning would not establish safe catheter placement or exclude leakage, so continuing medication is not appropriate.","Vessel irritation is described along a vein and typically has working blood return without edema; these central-line findings warrant escalation."]},{"stem":"During an infusion, a patient reports tightening and pain along the vein. Blood return remains present, and there is no swelling or ulceration. Which interpretation best distinguishes this pattern from extravasation?","options":["Vessel irritation is more consistent, but the nurse should continue assessment and report concerning change.","Extravasation is established by pain alone, so the nurse should document tissue injury without further assessment.","A flare reaction is most likely because it causes persistent pain and edema around the cannula."],"feedback":["Pain or tightening along the vein with working blood return and no edema or ulceration is consistent with the described pattern of vessel irritation; continue to assess for change.","Pain alone does not establish extravasation, and the absence of swelling or tissue changes makes further assessment important before drawing that conclusion.","A flare reaction may produce spots or lines along vessels, but pain, edema, and ulcer do not typically occur, and symptoms generally resolve within a short period."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Guidelines for the management of extravasation","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7431942/"},{"title":"Table 4.2c, [Complications/Unexpected Outcomes of CVADs].","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK594495/table/ch4centrallines.T.complicationsunexpecte/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Vesicant Tissue Exposure","detail":"Vesicant medication can escape a peripheral vessel, exposing surrounding tissue to injury influenced by the drug and duration of exposure."},{"label":"Burning With Slowed Flow","detail":"New burning with swelling, redness, and slowed infusion raises concern for extravasation, even without fever or another definitive cue."},{"label":"Stop And Aspirate","detail":"Stop infusion, disconnect tubing, aspirate through the cannula, and promptly notify the provider while following drug-specific local protocol."},{"label":"Track Tissue Changes","detail":"Recheck pain, sensation, color, temperature, swelling, and skin; promptly escalate worsening edema, blistering, skin loss, or limited movement."}]},{"id":"s067","unit":"skin","title":"Cellulitis or deep infection?","objective":"Identify cues that raise concern for necrotizing soft-tissue infection rather than uncomplicated cellulitis and promptly escalate them to the care team.","concept":"Cellulitis is an infection of the skin and tissue beneath it, commonly associated with streptococci or staphylococci entering through a break in the skin. It often causes localized warmth, redness, swelling, and tenderness. A necrotizing soft-tissue infection can spread through deeper tissue and fascia, impair blood supply, and cause tissue death. Because it may progress rapidly and become life-threatening, recognizing warning signs matters more than waiting for diagnostic certainty.","recognize":"Cellulitis is often unilateral, warm, tender, and swollen, with redness that may have indistinct borders; lymphangitic streaks can occur. Pain greater than the visible findings, rapid progression, bullae, skin discoloration or sloughing, numbness, crepitus, or systemic toxicity raise concern for deeper infection. Stasis dermatitis is often bilateral with scaling or other chronic skin changes; deep vein thrombosis can cause swelling but usually lacks lymphangitis and regional lymph node enlargement.","respond":"Treat suspected deep infection as an urgent escalation: promptly notify the responsible clinician and activate local emergency response pathways as indicated. Clearly report the rate of change, pain relative to visible findings, skin changes, vital signs, and relevant risks. Support timely surgical evaluation while carrying out fluid and antibiotic orders and other local protocol actions. Do not delay escalation while awaiting imaging or cultures.","reassess":"After communicating and carrying out ordered actions, reassess the affected area, pain, rate of spread, vital signs, mental status, and overall appearance. Report worsening pain, new bullae or discoloration, skin sloughing, numbness, crepitus, hypotension, confusion, or other deterioration immediately, even if earlier findings seemed reassuring.","trap":"A red, warm limb is not automatically a routine skin infection; it can be the visible edge of a deeper process. Think of disproportionate pain, rapid change, or tissue damage as a warning flare, while remembering that neuropathy can mute pain and does not rule out serious infection.","caseStory":"An adult arrives with a warm, swollen lower leg and a small skin break. Over several hours, the redness advances, pain becomes severe compared with the visible changes, and a tense blister appears; the patient is febrile and increasingly confused. The decision is whether to continue routine cellulitis monitoring or urgently escalate concern for a deep infection.","caseReason":"The initial warmth, swelling, and redness can occur with cellulitis, but rapid progression, disproportionate pain, a blister, fever, and confusion are concerning for necrotizing soft-tissue infection. Promptly alert the responsible clinician and support urgent surgical assessment and ordered treatment; diagnostic imaging should not delay this escalation.","teachback":"When a patient’s redness seems consistent with cellulitis, which changes in pain, skin appearance, progression, or systemic status would make you promptly raise concern for deeper infection?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult has unilateral leg redness, warmth, swelling, and tenderness. The border is advancing over a short interval, pain seems far greater than the visible findings, and new bullae are present. Which interpretation best fits these cues?","options":["The findings raise urgent concern for necrotizing soft-tissue infection, not just uncomplicated cellulitis.","The findings are most consistent with stasis dermatitis because redness and swelling can affect the legs.","The findings favor deep vein thrombosis because unilateral swelling is more important than skin changes."],"feedback":["Correct. Rapid progression, disproportionate pain, and bullae are warning signs of a potentially life-threatening deeper infection.","Stasis dermatitis is more often bilateral and associated with chronic features such as scaling or other eczematous changes; the rapid change and bullae are concerning for deep infection.","Unilateral swelling can occur with deep vein thrombosis, but advancing warm erythema with bullae and disproportionate pain raises concern for a serious soft-tissue infection."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with a suspected skin infection develops rapidly increasing pain, skin discoloration, and hypotension. Imaging is available but has not yet been performed. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Immediately escalate to the responsible clinician and urgent surgical evaluation pathways while supporting ordered resuscitation and treatment.","Arrange imaging first so the team can confirm the depth of infection before urgent escalation.","Continue close observation and report the change if the next assessment shows further progression."],"feedback":["Correct. These findings suggest possible necrotizing soft-tissue infection, which requires urgent team escalation and surgical assessment; treatment should not wait for imaging.","Imaging may help define disease extent, but it should not delay treatment when clinical findings raise concern for necrotizing infection.","Waiting risks delaying care in a rapidly progressive, potentially life-threatening infection; the current deterioration warrants immediate escalation."]},{"stem":"An adult with chronic liver disease develops rapidly worsening redness and swelling with bullae after an aquatic injury in a warm coastal area. The patient now appears acutely ill. Which nursing interpretation and action are most appropriate?","options":["Recognize possible severe or necrotizing infection and urgently alert the responsible clinician for assessment and treatment.","Assume this is routine nonpurulent cellulitis and continue observation because the injury began at the skin surface.","Focus on a local allergic reaction and wait to escalate unless the affected area becomes bilateral."],"feedback":["Correct. Aquatic injuries in warmer coastal areas can be associated with severe infection, and rapid worsening with bullae and acute illness warrants urgent escalation.","A skin break can precede cellulitis, but the rapid progression, bullae, and acute illness are not reassuring features of a routine uncomplicated case.","A contact reaction may be itchy and limited to an exposure area, but this case has progressive infection signs and systemic illness that require urgent assessment."]},{"stem":"An adult with diabetes and known peripheral neuropathy has a rapidly worsening, discolored area of skin with swelling and fever but reports little pain. Which reassessment interpretation is safest?","options":["Do not use minimal pain to reassure; neuropathy can blunt pain, so the rapid change and systemic signs need urgent escalation.","Treat the lack of severe pain as evidence against deep infection and reassess after routine cellulitis care.","Attribute the discoloration to venous stasis unless lymphangitic streaks appear."],"feedback":["Correct. Pain may be minimal or absent in areas affected by diabetic neuropathy; worsening discoloration and fever remain concerning and warrant escalation.","Minimal pain does not exclude necrotizing infection, especially when neuropathy is present, and waiting could delay urgent care.","Venous stasis can cause skin changes, but it does not safely explain rapid worsening with fever; absence of streaking does not remove the concern."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Cellulitis - Infectious Disease - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/infectious-diseases/bacterial-skin-infections/cellulitis"},{"title":"Necrotizing Soft-Tissue Infection - Infectious Disease","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/infectious-diseases/bacterial-skin-infections/necrotizing-soft-tissue-infection"}],"frames":[{"label":"Infection Spreading Deeply","detail":"Skin breaks can admit bacteria, while a deep infection may spread rapidly through fascia and threaten tissue viability."},{"label":"Rapid Painful Progression","detail":"Advancing redness, pain exceeding visible changes, a tense blister, fever, and confusion signal urgent concern for deep infection."},{"label":"Urgent Surgical Escalation","detail":"Promptly alert the responsible clinician, report progression and systemic changes, and support urgent surgical evaluation without awaiting imaging or cultures."},{"label":"Watch For Deterioration","detail":"Reassess spread, pain, skin, vital signs, and mental status; immediately report new bullae, discoloration, numbness, hypotension, or confusion."}]},{"id":"s068","unit":"skin","title":"Wounds and healing","objective":"Use wound appearance, healing context, and patient risks to identify impaired healing or dehiscence and choose an appropriate nursing response and reassessment.","concept":"Wound healing depends on viable tissue, adequate perfusion, a balanced moist environment, and advancing wound edges. Healing may occur with approximated edges, from the wound base when edges cannot meet, or after delayed closure. Infection, necrotic tissue, poor perfusion, smoking, diabetes, and nutritional deficits can impede progress. A systematic view of the patient and wound helps nurses recognize barriers, communicate changes, and support the plan of care.","recognize":"Compare the wound with its prior appearance and expected course. Increasing redness at incision margins, drainage, pain, or separation may signal dehiscence; visible deeper tissue or organs indicates a more serious opening. A stalled wound may reflect local barriers such as devitalized tissue, infection, excess or inadequate moisture, or edges that are rolled, undermined, or callused. Consider perfusion and comorbidities too.","respond":"Prioritize prompt assessment and communication when an incision separates or shows worsening redness, drainage, or pain. Escalate urgently for deep separation or exposed organs, and protect the wound according to emergency protocol and orders. For stalled wounds, report observed barriers and collaborate with the wound-care team; use dressings and other interventions only within orders, competence, and local policy. Do not assume drying a wound promotes healing.","reassess":"After care, inspect the wound and surrounding skin for further separation, drainage, redness, pain, tissue viability, and moisture-related maceration or dryness. Compare with documented findings and report deterioration, deep separation, or exposed organs promptly. Note whether wound edges appear to be advancing over time.","trap":"A wound is not a dry surface that heals best when all moisture is removed; appropriate moisture supports cell migration, while excess can damage the edges. Think of moisture as a narrow working range: too little stalls activity, and too much softens surrounding tissue.","caseStory":"A nurse finds that a postoperative abdominal incision has newly separated at one point, with more drainage and pain than on the previous assessment. The margins are red, but no organs are visible. The nurse must decide whether this is routine healing or a change requiring prompt escalation.","caseReason":"New separation together with increasing drainage, pain, and redness is consistent with possible wound dehiscence, not simply an unchanged healing incision. The nurse should assess and promptly notify the responsible clinician, document the findings, and follow wound-care orders and local protocol. Absence of visible organs does not remove the need to communicate the change.","teachback":"When a wound stops progressing, how would you use its tissue, moisture, infection or inflammation, and edges to identify what to report and what to reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"A nurse assesses a surgical incision that was previously approximated. A small section is now separated, with increasing drainage, margin redness, and pain. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The incision may be developing dehiscence and needs prompt assessment and communication.","The incision is healing by secondary intention, which is expected whenever drainage increases.","The redness and pain establish infection as the only relevant concern."],"feedback":["New edge separation with increased drainage, redness, and pain are concerning signs of dehiscence and warrant prompt attention.","Secondary intention describes healing when wound edges cannot be approximated; it does not make new separation and worsening findings routine.","Infection is a possible concern, but these findings do not establish it as the only explanation; the separation itself requires assessment."]},{"stem":"During an abdominal incision check, a nurse sees that the wound has opened widely and abdominal organs are visible. Which action has priority?","options":["Activate urgent clinical assistance and protect the wound with a sterile saline dressing according to emergency protocol.","Apply a dry dressing and continue routine observations before contacting the surgical team.","Pack the opening to encourage the wound to heal upward from its base."],"feedback":["Visible organs indicate evisceration, a serious complication of complete dehiscence; urgent escalation and protection with a sterile saline dressing are supported actions.","Routine observation delays response to a surgical emergency, and this situation calls for urgent escalation rather than a dry dressing.","Packing may be used for some wound features under an appropriate plan, but an open incision with visible organs requires emergency response."]},{"stem":"A patient with diabetes has a chronic foot ulcer that is not advancing. The wound has a thick callused edge that partly rolls inward, without a reported new increase in drainage. Which nursing interpretation and response are best?","options":["The wound edge may be obstructing progression; report the finding and coordinate management under the wound-care plan.","The wound needs to be dried completely because moisture prevents epithelial cells from moving.","The callused edge confirms a wound infection, so infection treatment should be initiated independently."],"feedback":["Callused or rolled edges can impede edge advancement; the nurse should communicate the finding and follow the care plan rather than independently perform debridement.","Appropriate moisture supports cell migration, while excessive moisture can macerate edges; complete drying is not the supported approach.","A callused edge alone does not confirm infection, and nurses should not independently initiate treatment outside orders and policy."]},{"stem":"After applying an ordered dressing to a shallow wound, a nurse reassesses the surrounding skin and finds that the edges are newly white and soggy. Which interpretation and next step are most appropriate?","options":["The wound edges may be macerated from excess moisture; reassess moisture management and communicate or adjust care within the plan.","The wound is too dry, so additional moisture should be applied without further assessment.","The change shows that the wound is healing because moist edges always indicate healthy epithelial migration."],"feedback":["White, soggy edges are consistent with maceration from excessive moisture; reassess the dressing and moisture balance and act within orders or local policy.","The described edges suggest excess rather than inadequate moisture, so adding moisture without reassessment may worsen the problem.","Moisture can support healing, but excessive moisture can macerate wound edges; this finding should not automatically be interpreted as progress."]}],"sources":[{"title":"28.2 Wound Healing Process - Medical-Surgical Nursing","url":"https://openstax.org/books/medical-surgical-nursing/pages/28-2-wound-healing-process"},{"title":"Wound Dehiscence - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551712/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Incision Edges Separating","detail":"Postoperative incision edges may separate when healing is disrupted, with drainage, redness, or pain marking a change in the wound."},{"label":"New Drainage And Pain","detail":"New separation with increased drainage and pain differs from an unchanged incision, even when organs remain covered."},{"label":"Notify And Protect","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician, document findings, and follow wound-care orders and local protocol; escalate urgently for deep separation."},{"label":"Compare Wound Changes","detail":"Recheck separation, drainage, redness, pain, and tissue; report worsening or exposed organs promptly and compare edge advancement over time."}]},{"id":"s069","unit":"msk","title":"Acute compartment syndrome","objective":"Recognize cues of acute compartment syndrome and promptly communicate concerns to support urgent team assessment and treatment.","concept":"Acute compartment syndrome develops when swelling or bleeding raises pressure inside a closed fascial space that cannot readily expand. Rising pressure first impairs venous outflow and then reduces tissue perfusion, creating ischemia that can worsen swelling and cause muscle and nerve injury. Fractures, especially tibial fractures, are common triggers, but the syndrome can also follow crush injury, vascular injury, or constriction. Timely recognition matters because tissue damage can become irreversible.","recognize":"Suspect the syndrome when pain is worsening or out of proportion to the injury, especially when passive muscle stretch increases it or the compartment feels tense. Tingling or altered sensation may occur early; pallor, paralysis, and absent pulses are often late, and pulses may remain present despite serious compromise. Compare with the injury context, but do not assume fracture pain alone explains a concerning pattern.","respond":"Treat a concerning pattern as time critical: promptly notify the surgical or orthopedic team and communicate the injury, pain pattern, stretch response, compartment findings, and neurovascular changes. Follow local protocols and orders for removing restrictive casts or dressings, supporting oxygenation, and keeping the limb level with the heart. Do not delay escalation to await a pressure reading or rely on a palpable pulse to rule it out.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess and document pain and its response to passive stretch, compartment tension, sensation, motor function, skin appearance, and pulses, comparing with prior findings. Escalate immediately for worsening pain, a newly tense compartment, sensory or motor change, or other deterioration. A present pulse or one normal pressure reading does not settle the concern.","trap":"A palpable pulse is not an all-clear; it is like a functioning main road while small tissue-level routes are already failing. Likewise, late findings such as pallor or paralysis are not prerequisites for escalation, so waiting for them risks missing the window to prevent irreversible injury.","caseStory":"A patient with a tibial fracture reports increasingly severe lower-leg pain despite analgesia. The compartment feels tense, and passive movement of the toes worsens the pain, while the foot remains warm with a palpable pulse. The nurse must decide whether the pulse makes continued observation reasonable or whether to escalate now.","caseReason":"The worsening pain, tense compartment, and pain with passive stretch form a concerning pattern for acute compartment syndrome. A palpable pulse does not exclude impaired tissue perfusion, so the nurse should promptly notify the surgical team rather than wait for pulselessness or further deterioration.","teachback":"After a limb injury, which changes would make you escalate concern for compartment syndrome even if a pulse is still palpable, and what would you communicate to the surgical team?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with a tibial fracture develops worsening lower-leg pain that seems greater than expected. The compartment feels firm, and passive movement of the toes increases the pain. The foot is warm and a pulse is palpable. What is the best interpretation?","options":["The pattern is concerning for acute compartment syndrome and warrants prompt surgical-team notification.","The palpable pulse makes acute compartment syndrome unlikely, so continue routine fracture monitoring.","The pain is most consistent with a vascular occlusion, so focus on pulse checks before notifying the team."],"feedback":["Worsening disproportionate pain, pain with passive stretch, and compartment firmness are concerning cues; a palpable pulse does not rule out the syndrome.","Pulses can remain present even when tissue perfusion is compromised, so this finding should not defer escalation.","The presented pattern is concerning for compartment pressure-related tissue compromise; pulse findings alone do not establish or exclude vascular causes."]},{"stem":"Several hours after a forearm fracture, a patient reports escalating pain and new tingling in the hand. The forearm is increasingly tense, but the patient is otherwise stable. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly notify the surgical team and report the pain trend, tension, and sensory change.","Wait for pallor or loss of the radial pulse before contacting the surgical team.","Request a pressure measurement first and notify the team only if the result is abnormal."],"feedback":["The symptoms and tense compartment warrant urgent team assessment; paresthesia may occur before late signs develop.","Pallor and pulselessness are generally late findings, and waiting for them may delay necessary care.","Pressure measurement can assist when needed, but it should not delay escalation when the clinical pattern is concerning."]},{"stem":"After vascular repair for an arm injury, a patient develops increasing forearm swelling, deep pain, and pain when the fingers are passively extended. The hand remains warm. Which nursing response best transfers the recognition principle to this context?","options":["Treat the findings as possible compartment syndrome and urgently communicate them to the surgical team.","Attribute the swelling to expected postoperative change and reassess at the next scheduled observation.","Focus on the warm hand as evidence that tissue perfusion is adequate and continue routine monitoring."],"feedback":["Vascular injury and repair can be associated with compartment syndrome, and the pain, stretch response, and swelling warrant urgent communication.","Expected swelling does not explain away a concerning combination of worsening pain and pain with passive stretch.","A warm hand does not rule out compartment syndrome because tissue compromise can occur while a pulse or other reassuring distal signs remain."]},{"stem":"A patient with a crush injury initially had severe limb pain, but now reports less pain. The compartment remains tense, and new numbness and weakness are present; a distal pulse is still palpable. How should the nurse interpret these reassessment findings?","options":["Escalate immediately because sensory or motor changes with persistent tension may indicate advanced compromise despite less pain and a pulse.","Consider the reduced pain reassuring and continue observation because the pulse remains palpable.","Document the numbness and weakness as expected after a crush injury, then reassess after the next analgesic dose."],"feedback":["Pain may be absent or lessen in advanced disease, while sensory or motor changes and tension remain concerning; prompt escalation is warranted.","Neither reduced pain nor a palpable pulse reliably excludes serious compartment compromise.","New neurologic changes in this context require urgent communication rather than routine documentation and delayed reassessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Compartment Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK448124/"},{"title":"Compartment Syndrome - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/fractures/compartment-syndrome"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pressure Within Compartment","detail":"Fracture-related swelling or bleeding raises pressure inside a closed fascial compartment, threatening muscle and nerve perfusion."},{"label":"Pain With Passive Stretch","detail":"Escalating pain, a tense compartment, and pain with passive toe movement are concerning despite a warm foot and palpable pulse."},{"label":"Notify Surgical Team","detail":"Promptly notify the surgical or orthopedic team with pain and neurovascular findings; follow local orders without waiting for pressure measurement."},{"label":"Recheck Limb Function","detail":"Recheck pain with stretch, tension, sensation, movement, skin, and pulses; immediately escalate worsening pain or sensory or motor change."}]},{"id":"s070","unit":"msk","title":"Fractures and fat embolism","objective":"Recognize possible fat embolism syndrome after fracture or another supported risk, and coordinate timely supportive nursing care and escalation.","concept":"Fat emboli are fat droplets in the microcirculation; fat embolism syndrome is the resulting systemic illness, with inflammation and impaired microcirculation affecting the lungs, brain, skin, and other organs. After long bone or pelvic fractures and some procedures, symptoms often emerge over the next one to three days, though onset can be fulminant. The distinction matters because emboli alone do not establish the syndrome, while evolving organ dysfunction needs prompt recognition.","recognize":"After a long bone or pelvic fracture, new breathing difficulty, hypoxemia, mental status change, or petechiae should raise concern, especially when these findings occur together. Anemia and thrombocytopenia can add support. Findings are nonspecific, and a normal early chest radiograph does not settle the diagnosis. Consider other trauma-related causes and assess for head injury, but do not delay escalation while seeking certainty.","respond":"Treat worsening breathing, oxygenation, consciousness, or circulation as urgent: notify the responsible clinician and use the local escalation pathway. Assess and communicate airway, respiratory effort, oxygen saturation, vital signs, mental status, injury timing, and skin findings. Provide oxygen or other supportive measures only under orders or local protocols, and collaborate with the team on monitoring and fracture stabilization. Do not assume a specific drug is established treatment.","reassess":"After supportive action, reassess oxygenation, respiratory effort, mental status, heart rate, blood pressure, and the trend in symptoms. Report worsening hypoxemia, new or increasing confusion, declining consciousness, hypotension, or other signs of respiratory or circulatory failure promptly, even if one measure, such as oxygen saturation, has improved.","trap":"Fat emboli and fat embolism syndrome are not interchangeable: droplets may be present without the full systemic illness. Think of a normal chest radiograph as a quiet smoke alarm, not proof that there is no fire, because imaging and other findings can be nonspecific.","caseStory":"A fictional adult with a femur fracture is awaiting fixation when, about a day later, breathing becomes rapid and oxygenation declines. The patient is newly confused and has small petechiae on the conjunctiva, with no known head injury. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as an expected fracture-related change or urgently raise concern for a systemic complication.","caseReason":"The timing and combination of respiratory impairment, cerebral change, and petechiae after a long bone fracture make fat embolism syndrome a serious possibility. The findings are not diagnostic by themselves, but waiting for a confirmatory test could delay supportive care. The nurse should promptly communicate the changes and escalate under local protocol.","teachback":"In a patient with a fracture or another recognized risk, which combination of changing findings would make you suspect fat embolism syndrome, and what would you communicate and reassess first?","questions":[{"stem":"A 31-year-old adult with a pelvic and femur fracture develops worsening oxygenation, rapid breathing, and confusion about 36 hours after injury. Small petechiae are visible on the conjunctiva, and there is no known head injury. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Fat embolism syndrome is a serious possibility because respiratory, cerebral, and skin findings have appeared after a long bone or pelvic injury.","An isolated pulmonary embolus is established because oxygenation has worsened, while the confusion and petechiae are unrelated findings.","A primary head injury is the most likely explanation because confusion after trauma makes the respiratory and skin changes less relevant."],"feedback":["Correct. The timing and combination of respiratory impairment, cerebral change, and petechiae after fracture support concern for fat embolism syndrome, although they do not prove it.","Misleading. Respiratory decline alone does not establish a thrombotic pulmonary embolus, and this interpretation disregards the accompanying neurologic and skin findings.","Misleading. Head injury must be considered after trauma, but no known head injury plus the combined respiratory and petechial findings should prompt concern for fat embolism syndrome as well."]},{"stem":"An adult admitted with a long bone fracture becomes increasingly short of breath and less responsive during the first two days after injury. Oxygenation is declining and the patient is tachycardic. Which nursing action has the highest priority?","options":["Promptly escalate the deterioration, assess airway and breathing and mental status, and provide ordered supportive care under local protocol.","Complete a detailed skin survey for petechiae before contacting the team, because the rash is needed to identify the syndrome.","Wait for chest imaging and blood results before reporting the change, because these tests are needed to confirm fat embolism syndrome."],"feedback":["Correct. Declining oxygenation and responsiveness are urgent changes; prompt escalation and focused assessment support timely respiratory care without waiting for diagnostic certainty.","Misleading. Petechiae can support recognition, but their absence or delayed discovery should not postpone escalation for worsening breathing and consciousness.","Misleading. Fat embolism syndrome has no universally accepted diagnostic test or criteria, and laboratory and imaging findings may be nonspecific."]},{"stem":"A patient being treated for acute pancreatitis, without a recent fracture, develops new breathing difficulty, confusion, and petechiae. Which nursing interpretation best transfers the fracture-related recognition principle to this situation?","options":["Fat embolism syndrome remains a possibility because acute pancreatitis is a supported nontraumatic context and the patient has multisystem findings.","Fat embolism syndrome can be set aside because it occurs only after long bone fractures or orthopedic procedures.","The findings establish a medication reaction because a nontraumatic illness cannot cause fat droplets to affect multiple organs."],"feedback":["Correct. Acute pancreatitis is a recognized nontraumatic context, and the combined respiratory, cerebral, and skin findings warrant concern and clinical escalation.","Misleading. Fractures and orthopedic procedures are common contexts, but nontraumatic causes, including acute pancreatitis, are also described.","Misleading. The vignette does not establish a medication reaction, and nontraumatic fat embolism syndrome is possible."]},{"stem":"After ordered oxygen support, an adult with a recent femur fracture has a better oxygen saturation reading but becomes more confused and hypotensive. An early chest radiograph was read as normal. What is the best reassessment and response?","options":["Treat the worsening mental status and blood pressure as deterioration, promptly escalate, and continue reassessing respiratory and circulatory status.","Consider the improved oxygen saturation and normal radiograph reassuring enough to continue routine observation without further notification.","Focus on repeating the chest radiograph before reporting the changes, because a normal early image excludes a serious pulmonary complication."],"feedback":["Correct. New confusion and hypotension may signal worsening cerebral or circulatory involvement; improvement in one measure does not offset deterioration elsewhere.","Misleading. Fat embolism syndrome can affect multiple organs, and improved oxygen saturation alone does not show that the patient is stable.","Misleading. A normal early chest radiograph does not exclude fat embolism syndrome, and repeating imaging should not delay escalation for clinical deterioration."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Fat Embolism and Fat Embolism Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK499885/"},{"title":"Fat embolism syndrome - PMC - National Center for ...","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3047848/"}],"frames":[{"label":"After Long-Bone Fracture","detail":"After a femur fracture, systemic effects of fat emboli may develop over one to three days, affecting lungs, brain, and skin."},{"label":"Breathing And Mental Changes","detail":"New hypoxemia, rapid breathing, confusion, and conjunctival petechiae together raise concern for fat embolism syndrome."},{"label":"Escalate Systemic Changes","detail":"Promptly communicate respiratory, oxygenation, mental-status, and skin changes and escalate through the local pathway; provide support under orders or protocol."},{"label":"Monitor Organ Function","detail":"Reassess oxygenation, breathing, consciousness, and circulation; promptly report worsening hypoxemia, declining consciousness, hypotension, or other deterioration."}]},{"id":"s071","unit":"msk","title":"Crush injury and rhabdomyolysis","objective":"Recognize rhabdomyolysis after muscle injury, communicate the risk of acute kidney injury and electrolyte complications, and reassess for deterioration using team-directed care.","concept":"Crush injury can damage skeletal muscle cells, releasing creatine kinase, myoglobin, and electrolytes into the circulation. Myoglobin and electrolyte disturbances can injure the kidneys, while severe rhabdomyolysis may lead to acute kidney injury and dangerous electrolyte changes. Early recognition matters because supportive care, including intravenous fluids when prescribed, and treatment of the cause and complications can help limit harm.","recognize":"Consider rhabdomyolysis after crush trauma, muscle ischemia, seizures, extreme exertion, prolonged immobilization, or relevant medication or drug exposure. Weakness, muscle pain, and tea-colored urine are clues, but the full triad is uncommon and symptoms or urine discoloration may be absent. A raised creatine kinase supports the diagnosis; urine dipstick blood with few or no red cells can support myoglobinuria.","respond":"Promptly report the muscle injury or exposure, symptoms, creatine kinase result, urine findings, urine output, and relevant electrolyte or creatinine changes to the responsible clinician. Carry out prescribed intravenous fluid therapy and ordered monitoring, and support evaluation and treatment of the inciting cause and complications under local protocols. Do not rule out rhabdomyolysis because urine color is normal or independently change treatment.","reassess":"After ordered fluids or other nursing actions, reassess urine output, fluid status, symptoms, and available trends in creatinine, creatine kinase, potassium, calcium, and phosphate. Escalate promptly for declining urine output, rising creatinine, significant electrolyte abnormalities, or worsening clinical status, including concern for a complication such as compartment syndrome.","trap":"Dark urine is a clue, not a required entry ticket to the diagnosis; myoglobin can clear from urine quickly, and many patients lack the classic symptom triad. Think of creatine kinase, urine findings, exposure history, and kidney or electrolyte trends as several dashboard indicators, rather than trusting one warning light.","caseStory":"After a building collapse, a conscious adult is brought to the emergency department with a crushed lower leg. The patient reports increasing muscle soreness, has a markedly raised creatine kinase, and the urine dipstick is positive for blood although microscopy shows few red cells. The nurse must decide what findings to report and what to monitor while carrying out the care plan.","caseReason":"The crush injury and raised creatine kinase support muscle breakdown, while dipstick blood with few red cells is consistent with myoglobin in the urine. These findings raise concern for kidney injury and electrolyte complications, so the nurse should promptly communicate them and follow ordered fluid and monitoring plans. A normal or changing urine color alone would not settle the diagnosis.","teachback":"If a patient at risk for muscle breakdown has no dark urine, what other findings and trends would you use to recognize possible rhabdomyolysis and decide when to escalate?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult arrives after a prolonged entrapment injury. The patient has muscle weakness, a substantially raised creatine kinase, and a urine dipstick positive for blood, but microscopy shows almost no red blood cells. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Muscle breakdown with possible myoglobin in the urine is likely and warrants attention to kidney function and electrolytes.","Visible or microscopic bleeding from the urinary tract is established, so muscle injury is unlikely to explain the urine result.","The urine result excludes muscle breakdown because myoglobin should produce red blood cells on microscopy."],"feedback":["A positive dipstick with few or no red blood cells can support myoglobinuria, and the injury and raised creatine kinase support rhabdomyolysis.","The dipstick result alone does not establish urinary tract bleeding; myoglobin can also cause a positive result.","Myoglobin does not need to produce red blood cells, and their absence does not exclude rhabdomyolysis."]},{"stem":"An adult with a crush injury has a rising creatinine, decreasing urine output, and a newly reported high potassium level. Intravenous fluids have been prescribed. Which nursing response is the priority?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician of the urine, creatinine, and potassium changes, and carry out the prescribed fluid plan.","Wait for dark urine to appear before reporting the changes, since urine color confirms muscle pigment injury.","Independently increase the prescribed fluid rate to reverse the creatinine rise before contacting the clinician."],"feedback":["Decreasing urine output, rising creatinine, and high potassium raise concern for kidney injury and an important electrolyte complication, requiring prompt communication and ordered care.","Urine discoloration is not required for rhabdomyolysis and may be absent even when the condition is present.","Fluid therapy is supportive, but the nurse should follow prescribed orders and promptly communicate deterioration rather than independently changing treatment."]},{"stem":"An adult is found after many hours immobilized during an episode of intoxication. The patient reports no muscle pain, and the urine appears normal, but testing shows a rising creatine kinase. Which nursing interpretation best supports safe care?","options":["Rhabdomyolysis remains possible, so communicate the exposure and laboratory trend and monitor for kidney and electrolyte complications as ordered.","Rhabdomyolysis is unlikely because muscle pain and dark urine are both required before the diagnosis can be considered.","The creatine kinase trend is not relevant unless the patient first develops reduced urine output."],"feedback":["Prolonged immobilization and intoxication can be associated with muscle injury, and the classic symptoms are absent in many cases; a rising creatine kinase merits attention.","The classic triad is present in fewer than one in ten cases, and urine discoloration may be absent.","Reduced urine output can be an important sign, but it is not a prerequisite for recognizing or communicating possible rhabdomyolysis."]},{"stem":"An adult being treated after a crush injury initially had dark urine and a high creatine kinase. The urine is now pale, but urine output has fallen and creatinine is rising. What is the best reassessment and response?","options":["Treat the urine color change as insufficient reassurance, reassess output and laboratory trends, and promptly escalate the falling output and rising creatinine.","Consider the resolving urine color proof that kidney risk has passed, and continue routine observation without reporting the trend.","Use urine color as the main indicator of recovery, and wait for it to darken again before reviewing kidney results."],"feedback":["Myoglobin is excreted rapidly, so urine color can change even while kidney injury is developing; falling output and rising creatinine warrant escalation.","Pale urine does not rule out rhabdomyolysis or acute kidney injury, and the worsening trends should not be ignored.","Urine color is not a reliable stand-alone measure of recovery; kidney function and urine output trends are important to reassess."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Rhabdomyolysis - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/rhabdomyolysis"},{"title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Nephrology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki"}],"frames":[{"label":"Crush muscle damage","detail":"A crushed lower leg can break skeletal muscle cells, releasing creatine kinase, myoglobin, and electrolytes into circulation."},{"label":"Urine dipstick clue","detail":"Markedly raised creatine kinase and dipstick blood with few red cells support muscle breakdown with possible myoglobinuria."},{"label":"Report and follow plan","detail":"Promptly report injury, findings, urine output, and relevant laboratory changes; carry out prescribed fluids and monitoring under local protocols."},{"label":"Watch kidneys and electrolytes","detail":"Reassess urine output, fluid status, creatinine, creatine kinase, and electrolytes; promptly escalate declining output or worsening clinical status."}]},{"id":"s072","unit":"msk","title":"ICU-acquired weakness","objective":"Recognize patterns consistent with ICU-acquired weakness and communicate priorities for safe, team-based assessment and rehabilitation.","concept":"ICU-acquired weakness commonly reflects critical illness myopathy, critical illness polyneuropathy, or both, and may follow critical illness and prolonged immobilization. Myopathy affects muscle; polyneuropathy affects peripheral nerves. Weakness is loss of strength, unlike fatigue or task limitation when strength is normal. Early recognition matters because structured multidisciplinary rehabilitation and individualized early active mobilization can improve short-term strength and mobility, while passive interventions alone appear insufficient.","recognize":"Look for generalized difficulty with routine movement after critical illness or prolonged bed rest, and distinguish reduced strength from reported tiredness or pain-limited movement. Diffuse wasting with preserved sensation and reflexes can fit disuse; distal-predominant weakness, sensory changes, and reduced reflexes suggest neuropathy. Sudden focal asymmetry is unlike generalized ICU weakness and warrants prompt escalation.","respond":"Prioritize safety and communicate onset, distribution, functional effect, and any sensory, swallowing, speech, or breathing concerns to the responsible clinician. Use the team's ordered assessment and local mobility and rehabilitation processes; collaborate with rehabilitation staff and support individualized active mobilization when judged safe. Do not assume fatigue explains objective loss of strength or independently prescribe an exercise plan.","reassess":"After activity or a care intervention, document functional strength, tolerance, and changes in breathing, swallowing, speech, sensation, or symmetry, comparing with baseline. Escalate promptly for worsening respiratory or bulbar function, sudden focal deficit, or rapidly progressing weakness, as these findings may signal a problem beyond routine rehabilitation needs.","trap":"Do not equate a patient's word fatigue with measured weakness: fatigue is like a low battery limiting effort, while true weakness is reduced force despite effort. Conversely, do not label all post-ICU weakness as simple deconditioning, because critical illness myopathy or polyneuropathy may contribute and warrants team assessment.","caseStory":"After a prolonged ICU stay for severe illness, an adult patient reports difficulty rising from a chair and lifting both arms. The weakness is gradual and generalized, sensation is intact, and reflexes are preserved. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as routine tiredness or communicate a pattern of possible ICU-acquired weakness and support team-directed rehabilitation.","caseReason":"The prolonged illness and immobilization, with generalized functional weakness, fit possible ICU-acquired weakness. Preserved sensation and reflexes with diffuse weakness can be consistent with disuse-related muscle wasting, but do not establish a cause. The nurse should report the findings and support individualized mobilization through the care team rather than dismissing the change as fatigue.","teachback":"When a recovering ICU patient says they feel tired, what findings would help you distinguish fatigue from true weakness, and what change would prompt you to escalate?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult recovering after prolonged ICU immobilization has gradual, symmetric difficulty rising from a chair and lifting the arms. Examination notes diffuse muscle wasting, intact sensation, and preserved reflexes. Which interpretation best fits these cues?","options":["The pattern can fit generalized muscle wasting from illness and disuse, while still requiring team assessment.","The pattern most strongly indicates a distal peripheral polyneuropathy despite preserved sensation and reflexes.","The pattern is best explained by fatigue because the patient reports difficulty with activity."],"feedback":["Diffuse wasting with normal sensation and reflexes after prolonged immobilization is described with illness- and disuse-related muscle wasting.","Peripheral polyneuropathy more often has distal-predominant weakness and reduced reflexes, sometimes with sensory deficits.","A report of tiredness does not establish fatigue as the cause; assess whether there is objective loss of strength."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving rehabilitation after critical illness has had generalized weakness for several days. During a routine assessment, the nurse notices sudden new weakness on one side. What is the priority nursing response?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician and communicate the sudden focal change.","Continue the planned mobility activity because weakness is expected after critical illness.","Document the change as likely disuse and reassess at the next scheduled session."],"feedback":["Sudden focal weakness differs from generalized ICU-acquired weakness; stroke is a common cause of unilateral weakness and needs prompt evaluation.","Continuing activity without communicating a sudden focal deficit could delay assessment of a serious cause.","Disuse-related wasting is generally diffuse and does not explain a sudden focal change."]},{"stem":"A patient in ICU recovery has weakness that becomes more apparent with repeated activity and improves after rest, along with fluctuating eyelid droop. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate to the team?","options":["Fluctuating, activity-related weakness suggests a possible neuromuscular junction disorder rather than assuming ICU-acquired weakness.","The findings establish critical illness myopathy because the patient has been in the ICU.","The findings are most consistent with peripheral polyneuropathy because weakness changes during the day."],"feedback":["Weakness that fluctuates, worsens with activity, and lessens with rest is characteristic of myasthenia gravis, a neuromuscular junction disorder.","An ICU history does not establish myopathy, and the fluctuation with activity and rest points to another possible mechanism.","Peripheral polyneuropathies more typically cause distal-predominant weakness and reduced reflexes, rather than this characteristic fluctuating pattern."]},{"stem":"During an individualized mobility session, a patient recovering from critical illness develops new difficulty swallowing and a weaker-sounding voice. What should the nurse do next?","options":["Stop and promptly communicate the new bulbar symptoms to the responsible clinician.","Record expected exertional fatigue and continue the session at a slower pace.","Focus the reassessment on leg strength because swallowing and voice changes are unrelated to weakness."],"feedback":["Bulbar weakness can occur with neurologic weakness and may be clinically important; new swallowing or speech changes warrant prompt communication.","New swallowing and voice changes should not be dismissed as ordinary fatigue or managed by continuing activity without communication.","Weakness can affect specific muscle groups involved in speech and swallowing, so these changes are relevant to the assessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Weakness - Neurology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/symptoms-of-neurologic-disorders/weakness?ruleredirectid=741ruleredirectid=450"},{"title":"Rehabilitation in ICU-Acquired Weakness: An Evidence-Based Narrative Review - PubMed","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42220872/"}],"frames":[{"label":"After critical illness","detail":"Prolonged critical illness and immobilization can precede generalized muscle weakness that limits ordinary movement."},{"label":"Strength loss pattern","detail":"Gradual difficulty rising and lifting both arms with intact sensation and preserved reflexes suggests possible ICU-acquired weakness, not simply tiredness."},{"label":"Coordinate safe mobilization","detail":"Communicate onset, distribution, and functional effect; support individualized active mobilization and rehabilitation through the care team when judged safe."},{"label":"Escalate new deficits","detail":"Reassess strength and activity tolerance; promptly report worsening breathing or swallowing, sudden focal asymmetry, or rapidly progressing weakness."}]},{"id":"s073","unit":"multi","title":"Sepsis and organ dysfunction","objective":"Recognize infection-associated organ dysfunction and communicate and reassess suspected sepsis promptly within nursing scope and local protocols.","concept":"Sepsis occurs when the body’s response to infection becomes dysregulated and causes life-threatening organ dysfunction. Inflammatory mediators can widen blood vessels and disrupt small-vessel blood flow, so tissues may receive inadequate oxygen even when cardiac output is initially increased. Poor perfusion can impair the kidneys, lungs, liver, brain, and heart. Persistent circulatory and metabolic abnormalities despite fluid resuscitation characterize septic shock and demand urgent team-based care.","recognize":"Consider sepsis when suspected or known infection accompanies systemic changes or new organ dysfunction, such as confusion, reduced urine output, breathing difficulty, or falling blood pressure. Fever, fast heart rate, and rapid breathing may occur, but cues can be subtle, especially in older, immunosuppressed, or postoperative adults. Warm skin does not exclude poor perfusion. Consider noninfectious causes and other types of shock during assessment.","respond":"Treat suspected sepsis with organ dysfunction or shock as urgent: promptly notify the responsible clinician or activate the local escalation pathway, communicate the infection concern and changing organ-function cues, and support frequent monitoring. Obtain ordered tests and cultures, and assist with prescribed fluids, antimicrobials, vasopressors, and source-control care under local protocols. Do not delay escalation while awaiting a score, culture result, or fever.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess blood pressure and its trend, mental status, urine output, breathing and oxygenation, skin perfusion, and ordered lactate results. Report persistent or worsening hypotension, confusion, oliguria, respiratory decline, or other evidence of impaired perfusion promptly, even if one observation appears reassuring.","trap":"A normal temperature or warm skin is not an all-clear; sepsis can resemble a fire that is spreading beneath a calm-looking surface. Likewise, a screening score can prompt closer evaluation, but it cannot establish or exclude sepsis on its own.","caseStory":"A postoperative adult develops a faster heart rate and breathing, becomes newly inattentive, and produces less urine. The incision is clean, and the patient’s skin feels warm. The nurse must decide whether to attribute the changes to recovery or escalate concern for infection-related organ dysfunction.","caseReason":"New confusion and reduced urine output are concerning organ-function changes, while the faster breathing and heart rate add to concern for a systemic response. A clean incision and warm skin do not rule out infection or impaired tissue perfusion. The nurse should promptly communicate the changes and follow the local escalation pathway while continuing assessment.","teachback":"When a patient with possible infection has a reassuring temperature or warm skin but develops confusion or reduced urine output, what would you communicate and reassess, and why?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult being treated for a urinary infection has a faster breathing rate, new confusion, and decreasing urine output. The blood pressure is currently within the patient’s usual range. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next step?","options":["Suspect infection-associated organ dysfunction and promptly escalate the concern for clinical evaluation.","Interpret the changes as a local urinary problem because blood pressure remains near baseline.","Attribute the confusion and reduced urine output to fatigue unless a fever develops."],"feedback":["Correct. New mental-status and urine-output changes with a suspected infection raise concern for organ dysfunction even before hypotension appears.","A near-baseline blood pressure does not rule out evolving sepsis, and the changes extend beyond a local urinary symptom.","Fever may be absent or subtle, and waiting for it could delay evaluation of concerning organ-function changes."]},{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with a suspected abdominal infection becomes hypotensive and less alert. The nurse has notified the responsible clinician, and local sepsis procedures are available. What is the best priority nursing action?","options":["Continue frequent assessment and support timely ordered evaluation and treatment through the local sepsis pathway.","Wait for culture results before seeking further action because they will identify the cause.","Focus on the abdominal findings and defer escalation until the patient develops a higher temperature."],"feedback":["Correct. Hypotension and altered alertness with suspected infection warrant urgent team-based evaluation; the nurse should monitor closely and assist with interventions as ordered.","Cultures help identify infection, but waiting for results before escalation can delay care in a deteriorating patient.","The current circulatory and neurologic changes are already concerning; fever is not required before escalation."]},{"stem":"An adult receiving treatment for a blood cancer has suspected infection but no fever. The patient becomes newly confused and has worsening blood pressure. Which action best transfers sepsis recognition to this context?","options":["Escalate promptly because immunosuppression can make infection signs less obvious, and communicate the new organ-function concerns.","Continue routine observation because the absence of fever makes sepsis unlikely in this patient.","Wait for a screening score to reach its threshold before reporting the blood-pressure change."],"feedback":["Correct. Immunosuppressed patients may have subtle signs, and new confusion with worsening blood pressure warrants prompt communication and evaluation.","An absent fever does not exclude sepsis, particularly in a vulnerable patient with new concerning changes.","Screening tools may support assessment but should not delay escalation when the clinical picture is worsening."]},{"stem":"After an ordered fluid intervention for suspected sepsis, an adult’s skin remains warm and blood pressure improves slightly. The patient is still confused and producing little urine. How should the nurse interpret these findings?","options":["Continue reassessment and promptly report persistent organ-function concerns despite the initially reassuring skin and pressure findings.","Consider perfusion restored because warm skin and a slight blood-pressure improvement outweigh the other findings.","Stop further observation for sepsis because the response to fluid makes infection less likely."],"feedback":["Correct. Persistent confusion and reduced urine output remain concerning; warm skin or a limited pressure improvement does not establish adequate perfusion.","Warm skin can occur in sepsis, and the ongoing neurologic and renal cues should not be dismissed.","A response to fluid does not exclude sepsis, and continued assessment is important when organ-function concerns persist."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Sepsis and Septic Shock - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/sepsis-and-septic-shock/sepsis-and-septic-shock"},{"title":"Early Recognition and Initial Management of Sepsis in Adult Patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK598311/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Infection disrupts perfusion","detail":"A dysregulated response to infection can impair tissue perfusion and cause life-threatening dysfunction across organs."},{"label":"New organ changes","detail":"New inattention and reduced urine output with faster breathing and heart rate raise concern for infection-related organ dysfunction."},{"label":"Urgent team escalation","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician or activate local escalation; communicate infection concern and changing organ cues without waiting for fever or test results."},{"label":"Trend perfusion cues","detail":"Reassess mental status, urine output, breathing, oxygenation, and blood pressure; promptly report persistent or worsening signs of impaired perfusion."}]},{"id":"s074","unit":"multi","title":"Comparing shock states","objective":"Compare the mechanisms and bedside cues of hypovolemic, distributive, cardiogenic, and obstructive shock to support timely nursing recognition, escalation, and reassessment.","concept":"Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion that deprives cells of oxygen and can progress to organ injury. Hypovolemia reduces circulating volume and cardiac filling; cardiogenic shock reflects a primary failure of the heart; obstructive shock impedes cardiac filling or outflow; and distributive shock involves abnormal vessel dilation and blood flow. These mechanisms can overlap, so nurses use the whole clinical picture and trends rather than blood pressure alone.","recognize":"Look for altered mentation, weak or rapid pulses, cool or clammy skin, reduced urine output, and changing vital signs as possible hypoperfusion cues. Warm or flushed skin and bounding pulses may point toward distributive shock, while cool skin can occur with hypovolemic, cardiogenic, or obstructive states. Use history and associated findings to distinguish causes, not one sign in isolation.","respond":"Use a structured airway, breathing, circulation, disability, and exposure assessment while promptly alerting the responsible clinician or emergency response team to suspected shock. Communicate the cues, relevant history, and changes over time; support monitoring and interventions under orders and local protocols. Keep the person warm, address visible external bleeding within role and protocol, and avoid oral intake or delaying escalation while seeking a perfect classification.","reassess":"After an action, reassess mental status, skin and peripheral perfusion, pulse quality, breathing, blood pressure trends, urine output, and available laboratory trends such as lactate. Report promptly if perfusion cues persist or worsen, even if blood pressure improves, or if new respiratory distress or other organ dysfunction appears.","trap":"A normal or improving blood pressure is not a reliable all-clear, just as a working dashboard light does not prove the engine is running well. Shock is about tissue perfusion, so interpret pressure alongside mentation, skin, urine output, and trends.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult with a febrile illness becomes confused and produces less urine, although the blood pressure has not fallen. The patient’s skin is warm and flushed, and the pulse feels bounding. The nurse must decide whether preserved pressure rules out shock or whether the pattern needs urgent escalation.","caseReason":"The combination of infection history, altered mentation, reduced urine output, and warm skin with a bounding pulse is consistent with distributive shock, which can impair tissue perfusion before hypotension appears. The nurse should escalate the concern and report the full pattern rather than waiting for a low blood pressure or treating one cue as definitive.","teachback":"In a new patient whose blood pressure seems reassuring, which other findings would you compare to decide whether tissue perfusion may still be inadequate, and how would the history help you distinguish the likely shock mechanism?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a suspected infection becomes newly confused and has decreasing urine output. The blood pressure remains near the patient’s usual level, while the skin is warm and the pulse is bounding. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Distributive shock is possible despite preserved blood pressure, and the perfusion changes warrant prompt escalation.","Shock is unlikely because blood pressure is preserved, so the nurse should continue routine observation.","Cardiogenic shock is most likely because confusion and decreasing urine output identify primary pump failure."],"feedback":["These cues can reflect distributive shock, in which vasodilation and abnormal microvascular flow impair perfusion even before hypotension develops.","This is misleading because early shock may occur without low blood pressure, and altered mentation and reduced urine output are concerning perfusion cues.","Confusion and reduced urine output indicate possible hypoperfusion but do not by themselves establish a primary cardiac cause; the warm, bounding pattern and infection history support considering distributive shock."]},{"stem":"An adult develops sudden severe breathing difficulty and hypotension after a chest injury. One side of the chest has markedly reduced breath sounds, and the patient is becoming less alert. What is the nurse’s best immediate action?","options":["Activate urgent clinical escalation, support airway and breathing assessment, and report the findings suggesting obstructive shock.","Wait for additional blood pressure readings before contacting the clinical team, since hypotension can have several causes.","Focus first on obtaining a detailed history of fluid intake to determine whether hypovolemia explains the change."],"feedback":["The sudden respiratory change and asymmetric breath sounds after chest injury raise concern for tension pneumothorax, an obstructive cause; urgent escalation and concurrent assessment are appropriate.","Waiting delays response to a potentially life-threatening obstructive cause, and a blood pressure trend alone cannot safely resolve the concern.","Fluid history may help characterize volume status, but it does not take priority over escalating sudden respiratory compromise and findings that suggest mechanical obstruction."]},{"stem":"An adult several days after surgery becomes pale and clammy, with a rapid weak pulse and worsening lightheadedness. The patient reports increasing abdominal discomfort, and the nurse notes a downward trend in blood pressure. Which interpretation and nursing response are best?","options":["Suspect possible concealed blood loss with hypovolemic shock and promptly escalate the changing perfusion signs and postoperative history.","Suspect distributive shock as the leading cause because any postoperative deterioration is most consistent with infection.","Suspect cardiogenic shock as the leading cause and defer escalation until a cardiac rhythm abnormality is documented."],"feedback":["Postoperative abdominal discomfort with cool, clammy skin and weak rapid pulses raises concern for concealed bleeding and reduced circulating volume; prompt escalation is appropriate.","Infection can cause distributive shock, but the supplied cues and possible concealed postoperative bleeding make it unsafe to assume infection is the cause.","A rhythm problem can cause cardiogenic shock, but no rhythm abnormality is given, and waiting for one would delay escalation of possible hemorrhage and hypoperfusion."]},{"stem":"An adult being treated under clinical orders for suspected volume depletion has a higher blood pressure on reassessment. The patient remains drowsy, the hands are cool, and urine output is still low. What should the nurse conclude and do?","options":["Perfusion may remain inadequate despite the pressure change; reassess the full pattern and promptly report persistent cues.","The improved blood pressure confirms that shock has resolved, so further perfusion assessment can wait.","Persistent cool hands establish obstructive shock, so the nurse should report that mechanism as confirmed."],"feedback":["Blood pressure improvement alone does not establish adequate tissue perfusion; persistent altered mentation, cool extremities, and low urine output warrant reassessment and escalation.","This treats one measurement as conclusive and overlooks ongoing signs of hypoperfusion that may persist despite a better pressure.","Cool extremities can occur in hypovolemic, cardiogenic, and obstructive shock, so they do not confirm obstruction; report the findings without overclaiming the mechanism."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock"},{"title":"Shock - Shock - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?ruleredirectid=741wruleredirectid=5"}],"frames":[{"label":"Shock limits tissue flow","detail":"Shock deprives cells of oxygen; hypovolemia, pump failure, obstruction, and abnormal vessel dilation can produce overlapping causes."},{"label":"Perfusion before pressure","detail":"Confusion and reduced urine output with warm flushed skin and bounding pulse can suggest distributive shock despite preserved blood pressure."},{"label":"Escalate full pattern","detail":"Use structured airway, breathing, circulation, disability, and exposure assessment; alert the responsible clinician and share history and changing cues."},{"label":"Track organ perfusion","detail":"Reassess mentation, skin perfusion, pulse, breathing, pressure trends, and urine output; report persistent or worsening hypoperfusion even if pressure improves."}]},{"id":"s075","unit":"multi","title":"Reading an arterial blood gas","objective":"Interpret an arterial blood gas by identifying the pH direction, the primary respiratory or metabolic change, expected compensation, and findings that need escalation.","concept":"An ABG directly measures pH and arterial carbon dioxide pressure; bicarbonate is usually calculated, while chemistry-panel bicarbonate is directly measured. The pH reflects the balance between carbon dioxide, influenced by ventilation, and bicarbonate, the metabolic component. Compensation moves pH toward normal but does not fully correct it or overshoot. A mismatch between measured and expected compensation suggests more than one primary acid-base process. Oxygenation and ventilation are distinct assessments.","recognize":"Begin with whether the pH indicates acidemia, alkalemia, or is near the laboratory reference range. Then see whether carbon dioxide or bicarbonate changes in the direction that explains the pH. Check whether the other value is consistent with expected compensation; an unexpected change can indicate a mixed disorder. Interpret oxygenation separately, considering supplemental oxygen and the patient's clinical condition.","respond":"Assess the patient before acting on a number, including breathing, alertness, circulation, and oxygenation. Communicate the ABG pattern and concerning clinical changes promptly to the responsible clinician, and follow local escalation procedures and orders. If a result is unexpected, check sample and treatment context with the care team. Do not independently prescribe treatment or assume a near-normal pH means the disorder is resolved.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess respiratory effort and rate, alertness, circulation, oxygenation, and trends in ordered ABGs. Compare the patient's condition and gas values with the prior findings and the intervention. Worsening work of breathing, reduced responsiveness, deteriorating circulation, or a worsening gas pattern warrants prompt escalation.","trap":"A pH near normal is not proof that acid-base balance is normal; opposing primary disorders can mask each other. Think of compensation as a stabilizer that reduces a swing, not a repair that erases the cause.","caseStory":"A fictional adult with chronic lung disease receiving supplemental oxygen becomes harder to keep awake and is breathing shallowly. The ABG shows pH 7.32, PaCO2 58 mm Hg, bicarbonate 29 mEq/L, and PaO2 82 mm Hg. The nurse must decide whether the oxygen value is reassuring or whether ventilation needs urgent attention.","caseReason":"The low pH with elevated PaCO2 supports respiratory acidosis; the elevated bicarbonate may reflect compensation, but these results alone do not establish the disorder's duration. A PaO2 measured while oxygen is being given does not rule out impaired ventilation. The nurse should reassess the patient and promptly communicate the findings and reduced alertness to the care team.","teachback":"When an ABG pH is close to normal, what would you compare next to decide whether compensation is appropriate or a second primary disorder may be present?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized adult has an ABG with pH 7.29, PaCO2 58 mm Hg, and bicarbonate 27 mEq/L. Which interpretation best identifies the primary process?","options":["Respiratory acidosis is the primary process, with bicarbonate possibly reflecting compensation.","Metabolic acidosis is the primary process, with carbon dioxide showing respiratory compensation.","Metabolic alkalosis is the primary process, with carbon dioxide showing respiratory compensation."],"feedback":["The acidemic pH and elevated PaCO2 point to respiratory acidosis as the primary process. The bicarbonate value does not change that direction; timing and degree of compensation require context.","Metabolic acidosis would be supported by a low bicarbonate, with compensatory ventilation lowering PaCO2. Here, PaCO2 is elevated.","Metabolic alkalosis would produce an alkalemic direction, not the acidemic pH shown. The elevated PaCO2 instead fits the primary respiratory process."]},{"stem":"An adult being treated for diabetic ketoacidosis has deep, rapid breathing and is becoming less responsive. The ABG shows pH 7.19, bicarbonate 10 mEq/L, and PaCO2 25 mm Hg. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Reassess breathing and responsiveness, promptly communicate the deterioration and ABG pattern, and follow the local escalation process.","Ask the patient to slow the breathing because the low PaCO2 is causing the acidemia.","Wait for a second ABG before reporting the reduced responsiveness because the low PaCO2 indicates compensation."],"feedback":["The low pH and bicarbonate indicate metabolic acidosis, while the lower PaCO2 is consistent with respiratory compensation. Reduced responsiveness is a concerning clinical change that warrants prompt assessment and escalation.","The lower PaCO2 is consistent with compensation for metabolic acidosis, not the cause of the acidemia. Slowing the patient's breathing independently could interfere with that response.","A compensatory pattern does not make reduced responsiveness safe to ignore. Assess the patient and communicate the change promptly rather than delaying escalation for another result."]},{"stem":"An adult with several days of vomiting has an ABG showing pH 7.49, bicarbonate 34 mEq/L, and PaCO2 48 mm Hg. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Metabolic alkalosis with a carbon dioxide increase consistent with respiratory compensation.","Respiratory acidosis with a bicarbonate increase consistent with metabolic compensation.","Metabolic acidosis with a carbon dioxide decrease consistent with respiratory compensation."],"feedback":["The alkalemic pH and elevated bicarbonate support metabolic alkalosis. The higher PaCO2 is in the expected compensatory direction, and the case context is compatible with acid loss from vomiting.","Primary respiratory acidosis would be expected to move pH toward acidemia, not the alkalemia present. The elevated bicarbonate and vomiting context fit a metabolic primary process.","Metabolic acidosis would be associated with a decreased bicarbonate and a compensatory decrease in PaCO2. Both the pH direction and bicarbonate here point away from that interpretation."]},{"stem":"An adult receiving supplemental oxygen has a PaO2 of 90 mm Hg, but is newly drowsy. The ABG shows pH 7.36, PaCO2 62 mm Hg, and bicarbonate 34 mEq/L. What should the nurse prioritize reassessing?","options":["Ventilation and alertness, while reviewing the ABG trend and promptly escalating the new drowsiness.","Oxygenation alone, because the PaO2 and near-normal pH indicate that respiratory function is adequate.","Whether the bicarbonate is low enough to explain the drowsiness before assessing respiratory effort."],"feedback":["A near-normal pH does not establish normal acid-base status, and elevated PaCO2 can signal impaired ventilation. Supplemental oxygen and the PaO2 value do not remove the need to assess drowsiness and communicate the change.","PaO2 describes oxygenation, not ventilation, and supplemental oxygen affects its interpretation. A near-normal pH can occur despite a significant respiratory abnormality or compensation.","The bicarbonate is elevated rather than low, and the new drowsiness makes immediate assessment of ventilation and the patient's condition important. Do not delay that assessment while focusing on bicarbonate."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Arterial Blood Gas Analysis: Fundamentals, Interpretation ... - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536919/"},{"title":"Acid-Base Disorders - Nephrology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acid-base-regulation-and-disorders/acid-base-disorders"}],"frames":[{"label":"Ventilation and pH","detail":"Carbon dioxide reflects ventilation, while bicarbonate represents the metabolic component; their balance shapes arterial pH."},{"label":"CO2-related acidemia","detail":"Low pH with elevated PaCO2 supports respiratory acidosis; elevated bicarbonate may reflect compensation, while reduced alertness is concerning."},{"label":"Report clinical context","detail":"Assess breathing and alertness, then promptly communicate the ABG pattern, supplemental oxygen context, and reduced responsiveness to the care team."},{"label":"Reassess ventilation","detail":"Follow respiratory effort, alertness, oxygenation, and ordered gas trends; worsening breathing or responsiveness warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s076","unit":"multi","title":"Fluids and electrolytes","objective":"Recognize hypokalemia cues, distinguish likely potassium loss from redistribution, and communicate priorities for safe team-based care.","concept":"Potassium is the main intracellular cation and helps establish electrical gradients needed for nerve, muscle, and cardiac activity. A low serum value may reflect total-body depletion from renal or gastrointestinal losses, or redistribution into cells after insulin or beta 2 agonist stimulation. Renal handling is influenced by aldosterone and distal sodium delivery. Magnesium deficiency can increase renal potassium loss, making correction difficult. Symptoms and ECG effects depend on severity and clinical context.","recognize":"Look for weakness, cramps, reduced bowel activity, polyuria, and, when potassium is substantially low, ECG changes such as a flattened T wave or prominent U wave. Review medication use, gastrointestinal losses, and recent insulin or beta 2 agonist exposure. When the cause is unclear, urinary potassium and acid-base findings help distinguish renal loss from gastrointestinal loss or redistribution.","respond":"Prioritize prompt communication of a low potassium result accompanied by ECG changes, significant weakness, or breathing difficulty. Follow local monitoring and escalation protocols, and share relevant losses, medications, cardiac history, and magnesium findings with the care team. Support ordered testing and replacement, but do not independently select a replacement route or amount. Avoid assuming that a shift into cells means total-body stores are adequate.","reassess":"After ordered treatment, reassess symptoms, cardiac rhythm or ECG findings as directed, and repeat laboratory results according to the care plan. Review whether losses continue and whether magnesium is low. Escalate persistent or worsening weakness, breathing difficulty, new rhythm changes, or potassium that remains low despite treatment.","trap":"A low serum potassium is like a warning light, not a complete fuel gauge: it can reflect depleted stores or potassium temporarily shifted into cells. A normal-looking ECG or few symptoms do not by themselves rule out clinically important hypokalemia.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult with several days of diarrhea reports new leg weakness, and the laboratory result shows low potassium. The ECG has a prominent U wave, while the medication history does not suggest recent insulin or beta 2 agonist exposure. The nurse must decide what to communicate first and what information could help clarify the source.","caseReason":"Diarrhea supports gastrointestinal potassium loss, while the ECG change and weakness raise concern for cardiac and neuromuscular effects. The nurse should promptly communicate these findings and follow monitoring and escalation protocols. If the cause remains uncertain, urinary potassium and magnesium results can help the team assess renal loss and a factor that may sustain hypokalemia.","teachback":"When a low potassium result occurs after gastrointestinal losses and after insulin exposure, what cues and team-directed assessments would help you distinguish potassium depletion from redistribution and decide how urgently to communicate the finding?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult admitted with persistent diarrhea has a low serum potassium result, leg weakness, and a prominent U wave on ECG. There is no recent insulin or beta 2 agonist exposure, and urinary potassium is low. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The kidneys are conserving potassium, supporting gastrointestinal loss as the likely source.","The kidneys are wasting potassium, making a renal cause the most likely source.","Potassium has most likely shifted into cells, despite the history of diarrhea."],"feedback":["Low urinary potassium is consistent with renal conservation, while the diarrhea supports gastrointestinal loss.","Renal potassium wasting is more consistent with inappropriately high urinary potassium excretion.","Insulin and beta 2 agonist exposure can shift potassium into cells, but neither is reported here and diarrhea supports loss."]},{"stem":"An adult with a low potassium result develops increasing weakness and new ECG changes during hospitalization. The patient takes digoxin and has a history of heart disease. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly communicate the findings and follow local protocols for cardiac monitoring and ordered evaluation.","Wait for another potassium result before reporting the changes because symptoms may be nonspecific.","Encourage potassium-rich food and reassess the ECG at the next routine observation."],"feedback":["ECG changes with weakness warrant prompt communication; digoxin use and heart disease increase concern about cardiac effects.","Delaying communication risks overlooking clinically important hypokalemia and associated rhythm changes.","Dietary intake does not replace timely escalation and monitoring when ECG changes and symptoms are present."]},{"stem":"An adult being treated with insulin develops a low potassium result and new muscle weakness. There is no reported vomiting or diarrhea, and the result was obtained soon after insulin administration. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication with the team?","options":["Insulin may have shifted potassium into cells, so redistribution is a possible contributor.","The finding establishes gastrointestinal potassium loss, even without gastrointestinal symptoms.","The finding establishes renal potassium wasting, regardless of urinary potassium results."],"feedback":["Insulin can promote potassium movement into cells; report the timing and symptoms so the team can evaluate redistribution and other causes.","Gastrointestinal loss is not established by these findings, and no gastrointestinal symptoms are reported.","Renal loss cannot be concluded without supporting information such as urinary potassium findings and clinical context."]},{"stem":"An adult receiving ordered treatment for low potassium has a repeat result that remains low. The patient is taking a loop diuretic, has a low magnesium result, and still reports weakness. Which reassessment and communication best address a potentially misleading assumption?","options":["Communicate the persistent weakness and low magnesium, since magnesium deficiency can contribute to ongoing renal potassium loss.","Assume the potassium result is no longer important because the patient has already received treatment.","Conclude that the loop diuretic caused redistribution of potassium into cells and no further assessment is needed."],"feedback":["Magnesium deficiency can increase renal potassium loss and make hypokalemia difficult to correct; persistent symptoms and results merit communication.","Treatment does not establish that potassium has corrected, and weakness remains relevant to reassessment.","Loop diuretics can cause renal potassium loss, while insulin and beta 2 agonists are examples of agents associated with intracellular shifts."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Hypokalemia - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/electrolyte-disorders/hypokalemia"},{"title":"Hypokalemia: a clinical update","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5881435"}],"frames":[{"label":"Diarrhea-related loss","detail":"Several days of diarrhea can deplete total-body potassium, while magnesium deficiency may sustain renal potassium loss."},{"label":"Weakness and U wave","detail":"Leg weakness and a prominent U wave distinguish clinically concerning hypokalemia from an isolated low laboratory value."},{"label":"Communicate ECG changes","detail":"Promptly communicate the low potassium, weakness, ECG change, diarrhea, medication history, and magnesium findings; follow local monitoring and escalation protocols."},{"label":"Track rhythm and potassium","detail":"Reassess symptoms, rhythm or ECG findings, and ordered potassium results; escalate worsening weakness, breathing difficulty, new rhythm changes, or persistent low potassium."}]},{"id":"s077","unit":"multi","title":"Burns and inhalation injury","objective":"Recognize evolving airway and systemic hazards after burns or smoke exposure, and prioritize timely team communication and reassessment.","concept":"Burn injury disrupts the skin barrier and can drive fluid loss, while inhalation injury may occur with or without visible burns. Hot smoke usually injures the upper airway because it cools, whereas steam can carry enough heat to injure lower airways; combustion chemicals can also damage tissue. Carbon monoxide and cyanide interfere with cellular oxygen use. Airway swelling may evolve, and lower airway problems can appear later, so serial assessment matters.","recognize":"Suspect inhalation injury after confinement in a burning space, especially with respiratory symptoms, carbonaceous sputum, perioral burns, or singed nasal hair. Stridor or posterior pharyngeal swelling raises concern for upper airway compromise. Lower airway injury may emerge later as oxygen needs rise or lung compliance falls. Headache, nausea, weakness, or confusion can reflect carbon monoxide toxicity, while unexpectedly severe obtundation may raise concern for cyanide.","respond":"Prioritize airway, breathing, and circulation. Under emergency orders and local protocols, support oxygen delivery and promptly alert the responsible clinician to concerning exposure, soot, airway swelling, altered consciousness, or respiratory distress. Facilitate timely airway assessment and preparation for possible clinician-performed intubation when indicated. Do not wait for severe obstruction to develop, since increasing edema can make airway management more difficult.","reassess":"After actions, reassess airway sounds, work of breathing, oxygen requirement, mental status, and the direction of respiratory change. Report new stridor, worsening consciousness, increasing oxygen needs, or declining respiratory status promptly. Remember that a reassuring early chest image or airway examination does not exclude injury that develops later.","trap":"A quiet airway at first assessment is like a fire hidden behind a wall: absence of obvious damage now does not prove the danger has passed. Upper airway swelling can progress, and lower airway problems may become apparent later, so repeat assessment is essential.","caseStory":"After being rescued from an enclosed house fire, a patient has facial burns, singed nasal hair, and soot in the sputum. The patient is initially answering questions but develops hoarseness and noisy breathing during assessment. The nurse must decide whether to continue routine burn assessment or urgently escalate the airway concern.","caseReason":"The enclosed smoke exposure and soot support inhalation injury, while new hoarseness and noisy breathing raise concern for evolving upper airway compromise. The nurse should promptly alert the clinician and support oxygen and airway assessment under protocol, because increasing edema can make later airway management more difficult.","teachback":"In a new patient with no visible burns but worsening oxygen needs after a smoke or steam exposure, what findings would make you escalate concern for inhalation injury, and what would you communicate to the team?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient rescued from a room filled with smoke has singed nasal hair and carbonaceous sputum but is speaking comfortably and has no stridor. Which interpretation should guide the nurse’s next action?","options":["Inhalation injury remains a concern, so promptly report the exposure and findings and support ongoing airway assessment.","A comfortable voice makes significant airway injury unlikely, so routine observation without escalation is appropriate.","The findings indicate lower airway injury alone, so airway assessment can wait until oxygenation worsens."],"feedback":["Correct. Confinement, singed nasal hair, and soot are clues to inhalation injury, and early symptoms may not reflect later airway swelling.","Misleading. A patient may initially appear comfortable even though airway edema can evolve and make later airway management harder.","Misleading. These findings do not establish injury location or exclude upper airway involvement; assessment should not be delayed."]},{"stem":"A patient with extensive burns after an enclosed fire becomes increasingly confused and has soot in the posterior pharynx. Which nursing priority is most appropriate?","options":["Promptly alert the responsible clinician, support oxygen delivery under protocol, and facilitate urgent airway assessment.","Continue wound care first because confusion is expected after a frightening event and the patient is still breathing.","Wait for a chest radiograph to show injury before escalating the airway concern or preparing the team."],"feedback":["Correct. Altered consciousness and posterior airway soot are serious findings that warrant prompt escalation and support for oxygenation and airway assessment.","Misleading. Confusion can signal hypoxia or toxic exposure, and soot in the posterior airway increases concern; it should not be dismissed or deferred.","Misleading. Airway decisions are based on clinical findings as well as tests, and imaging may not show injury early."]},{"stem":"A worker exposed to a burst of steam in an industrial space has no facial burns. The following day, the worker needs more oxygen and shows worsening respiratory status. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Delayed lower airway injury is possible, so promptly report the deterioration and support reassessment under protocol.","The absence of facial burns makes inhalation injury unlikely, so the increased oxygen need is probably unrelated.","Steam exposure affects only the upper airway, so the later respiratory change does not fit inhalation injury."],"feedback":["Correct. Steam carries substantial heat and can injure lower airways; lower airway problems may become evident later as oxygen needs rise.","Misleading. Inhalation injury can occur without visible burns, and worsening oxygen need after exposure merits reassessment.","Misleading. Unlike ordinary hot smoke, steam can carry enough heat to injure the lower airways."]},{"stem":"A patient evaluated after smoke exposure initially has a reassuring chest radiograph and airway examination. Several hours later, oxygen needs increase and new noisy breathing develops. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Escalate the new respiratory findings promptly because injury can evolve despite initially reassuring studies.","Reassure the patient because normal initial studies rule out clinically important inhalation injury.","Focus on carbon monoxide testing alone because noisy breathing is not a sign of airway change."],"feedback":["Correct. Airway injury can develop after an initially normal examination, and lower airway abnormalities may appear later; new noisy breathing and rising oxygen needs warrant escalation.","Misleading. Early normal findings do not reliably exclude evolving airway or lower airway injury.","Misleading. Carbon monoxide testing may be relevant, but it does not explain away new noisy breathing or replace airway reassessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Smoke Inhalation - Injuries; Poisoning","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/burns/smoke-inhalation"},{"title":"Burns - Injuries; Poisoning - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/burns/burns"}],"frames":[{"label":"Enclosed fire exposure","detail":"Smoke exposure in an enclosed fire can cause inhalation injury, and airway swelling may progress even when initial breathing seems stable."},{"label":"Hoarseness and noisy breathing","detail":"New hoarseness and noisy breathing after facial burns, singed nasal hair, and soot in sputum raise concern for evolving upper airway compromise."},{"label":"Escalate airway concern","detail":"Promptly alert the clinician and support oxygen delivery and airway assessment under local protocol; do not wait for severe obstruction to develop."},{"label":"Watch respiratory change","detail":"Reassess airway sounds, work of breathing, oxygen needs, and mental status; promptly report new stridor, worsening consciousness, or increasing oxygen needs."}]},{"id":"s078","unit":"multi","title":"Healthcare-associated infections","objective":"Use catheter-related infection principles to recognize concern, prioritize evidence-based nursing prevention, and reassess response in acute and critical care.","concept":"Venous catheters can provide a route for organisms from skin or contaminated access points to enter the bloodstream; risk is shaped by device choice, insertion, maintenance, and time in place. Hand hygiene, chlorhexidine skin preparation, insertion and maintenance bundles, and reliable implementation reduce risk. Because colonization risk rises with duration, regularly reviewing whether access remains necessary and arranging timely removal through the care team are central prevention practices.","recognize":"New fever, chills, or other signs of sepsis in a patient with a venous catheter warrant assessment, but do not by themselves establish the catheter as the source. Examine the insertion area and dressing, review recent line access, and look for plausible alternative infection sources. A normal-looking site does not rule out bloodstream infection; clinical diagnosis and surveillance classification are not interchangeable.","respond":"Address immediate deterioration using the unit's escalation pathway, then promptly communicate the patient's changes, catheter type, site findings, recent access, and possible alternative sources to the responsible team. Use hand hygiene, aseptic access, and prescribed hub and dressing practices under local protocol; review ongoing need daily and support prompt removal when no longer needed. Do not independently remove or routinely replace a catheter outside authorized orders or protocol.","reassess":"After action, reassess overall condition and vital-sign trends, symptoms, insertion site and dressing, line access, and whether the team has evaluated other sources. Confirm that prevention steps and the continued-need plan are documented. New or worsening signs of sepsis warrant prompt escalation, as does persistent concern without an identified source.","trap":"Think of fever as a smoke alarm, not a fingerprint: it signals a need to investigate but cannot identify the catheter as the cause. Likewise, a clean insertion site is not an all-clear, and routine scheduled line replacement is not a substitute for daily need review and sound maintenance.","caseStory":"In the ICU, a patient with a central venous catheter develops chills and a rising temperature during the shift, while the dressing is intact and the insertion site looks unremarkable. No source has yet been identified, and the patient is being monitored closely. The nurse must decide whether the normal site appearance is reassuring enough to defer escalation.","caseReason":"New systemic symptoms make catheter-associated infection a concern, but neither the catheter nor the timing proves causation, and the intact site does not exclude it. The nurse should assess the patient's condition and other possible sources, promptly notify the team through the unit pathway, maintain aseptic line care, and support a review of whether the catheter remains necessary.","teachback":"When a catheterized patient develops new systemic symptoms but the site looks normal, what will you assess, communicate, and do to reduce ongoing risk without assuming the line is the source?","questions":[{"stem":"Detect and interpret: A critically ill adult with a central venous catheter develops new fever and chills. The dressing is intact, the insertion site has no visible changes, and the team is also evaluating another possible infection source. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next steps?","options":["Treat catheter-associated infection as a concern, assess for other sources, and promptly communicate the findings to the team.","Consider bloodstream infection unlikely because the insertion site looks normal, and continue routine observation.","Assume the catheter is the source and arrange immediate removal without waiting for team assessment or authorized direction."],"feedback":["Correct. Systemic symptoms warrant assessment, but they do not prove the catheter is the source; a normal-looking site does not rule out infection.","A normal site appearance is not enough to exclude bloodstream infection, so this response could delay needed assessment and communication.","The catheter is a possible source, not a confirmed one. The nurse should escalate and support team assessment rather than independently deciding on removal."]},{"stem":"Prioritize: A stable ICU patient has a central venous catheter that the team no longer considers necessary. There are no current signs of infection. Which nursing action best supports infection prevention?","options":["Communicate the lack of ongoing need and support prompt removal through the authorized team process or local protocol.","Recommend routine replacement at a scheduled interval to reduce the time any one catheter remains in place.","Maintain the catheter until discharge because the patient is stable and the insertion site appears healthy."],"feedback":["Correct. Colonization risk increases with catheter duration, so regular need review and timely removal when access is no longer needed are important prevention measures.","Scheduled replacement has not been shown to prevent infection and may increase mechanical complications; it is not a substitute for assessing ongoing need.","A healthy-looking site does not make an unnecessary catheter risk-free. Continuing need should be reviewed and the team should be informed."]},{"stem":"Transfer to a new context: In acute care, a nurse is preparing to insert a short peripheral catheter for a patient who needs intravenous access. Which action is supported for reducing infection risk?","options":["Use chlorhexidine gluconate for skin preparation according to local protocol and allow the preparation to dry before insertion.","Skip antiseptic skin preparation when the intended site looks clean, then rely on the dressing to protect it.","Apply skin antiseptic and cover the site before it dries so the preparation remains under the dressing."],"feedback":["Correct. The reviewed evidence supports chlorhexidine gluconate for skin preparation; the central-line prevention material also specifies allowing chlorhexidine to dry before insertion.","A clean appearance does not replace evidence-supported skin preparation as part of infection prevention.","The supported insertion practice includes allowing the skin preparation to dry; covering it before it dries does not follow that practice."]},{"stem":"Reassess and distinguish misleading findings: After the nurse escalates concern about possible catheter-associated infection, a patient with a central venous catheter develops worsening signs of sepsis, although the insertion site remains unchanged. What should the nurse do?","options":["Promptly escalate the worsening condition through the unit's response pathway and communicate that the site remains unchanged.","Continue routine checks without further escalation because an unchanged insertion site makes a catheter source less likely.","Wait for visible changes at the insertion site before reporting the worsening systemic findings."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening systemic signs require prompt escalation; an unchanged site does not rule out bloodstream infection.","This gives too much weight to the appearance of the site and risks delaying a response to worsening signs of sepsis.","Visible site changes are not required before escalating systemic deterioration or communicating ongoing concern."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Effective nursing interventions for infection prevention and control in ...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41270713/"},{"title":"Prevention of Central Line-Associated Bloodstream Infections","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/5666696"}],"frames":[{"label":"Catheter infection risk","detail":"A venous catheter can provide a route for organisms into the bloodstream, and infection risk rises with time in place."},{"label":"Chills despite intact site","detail":"New chills and rising temperature warrant assessment; an intact dressing and normal-looking insertion site do not rule out bloodstream infection."},{"label":"Report and review sources","detail":"Use the unit escalation pathway and communicate symptoms, catheter details, site findings, recent access, and possible alternative infection sources to the team."},{"label":"Track systemic changes","detail":"Reassess condition and vital-sign trends, site and dressing, and source evaluation; promptly escalate new or worsening signs of sepsis."}]},{"id":"s079","unit":"multi","title":"After organ transplantation","objective":"Recognize possible central nervous system infection after solid organ transplantation and promptly communicate concerning neurologic changes to the transplant and critical-care team.","concept":"Immunosuppression after solid organ transplantation increases vulnerability to infections, including infections of the central nervous system. These infections may arise from pathogens acquired from donors, the environment, or reactivation of latent infection, and reduced immune responses can make presentation less typical or delay recognition. Fever, altered mental status, headache, seizures, and focal deficits warrant attention, while medication effects and metabolic or vascular problems remain important alternatives.","recognize":"Look for new or changing neurologic findings, including altered behavior or alertness, headache, vomiting, seizure, or focal deficit, with or without fever. In a pediatric transplant cohort, fever was common, but presentations varied and some patients had no recorded symptoms. Do not attribute a change automatically to medication toxicity, metabolic disturbance, or a vascular event; infection can resemble these problems.","respond":"Prioritize immediate focused assessment of airway, breathing, circulation, vital signs, and neurologic status, then promptly notify the responsible transplant and critical-care clinicians about the change and transplant history. Support diagnostic evaluation and antimicrobial treatment as ordered or directed by local protocol. Do not independently alter immunosuppression or delay escalation while deciding which cause is most likely.","reassess":"After notifying the team or carrying out an ordered action, trend alertness, behavior, speech, strength, seizure activity, vital signs, and the patient’s overall trajectory. Report worsening consciousness, a new focal deficit or seizure, instability, or persistent neurologic concern even if an initial test is unrevealing.","trap":"A transplant recipient’s confusion is not automatically a medication side effect; infection, metabolic problems, and vascular events can also affect the nervous system. Treat the differential like several warning lights on one dashboard: a plausible explanation for one light does not make the others safe to ignore.","caseStory":"A fictional adult several months after kidney transplantation develops a new headache, vomiting, and increasing confusion during a critical-care admission. The patient is taking maintenance immunosuppression and has no obvious focal weakness. The nurse must decide whether to continue routine observation or escalate the neurologic change.","caseReason":"The combination of new confusion and other neurologic symptoms in an immunosuppressed transplant recipient raises concern for central nervous system infection, although other causes remain possible. The nurse should promptly report the change and support team-directed evaluation rather than wait for fever or assume a medication effect.","teachback":"If a transplant recipient develops a new neurologic change, what cues would prompt you to contact the clinical team, and what would you report to help them assess the change?","questions":[{"stem":"A fictional adult heart transplant recipient develops a new headache, vomiting, and unusual confusion. The patient is receiving immunosuppression, has no known focal deficit, and is not febrile at the time of assessment. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next action?","options":["Possible central nervous system infection needs prompt team evaluation, while other causes remain in the differential.","Medication neurotoxicity is the most likely explanation, so assess again later before contacting the team.","The lack of fever and focal deficit makes an urgent neurologic cause unlikely, so continue routine observation."],"feedback":["Correct. New confusion with other neurologic symptoms in an immunosuppressed transplant recipient warrants concern for infection and timely evaluation, even though other causes are possible.","Medication neurotoxicity is a recognized alternative, but the available cues do not establish it as the cause or justify delaying escalation.","Presentations can be nonspecific, and absence of fever or a focal deficit does not rule out central nervous system infection."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult lung transplant recipient has a seizure followed by reduced responsiveness. The patient is immunosuppressed, and the cause is not yet known. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Assess immediate physiologic and neurologic status and urgently notify the responsible clinical team.","Wait for a fever or another neurologic sign to develop before contacting the transplant team.","Assume a post-transplant medication effect and independently hold the next immunosuppressive dose."],"feedback":["Correct. A seizure and reduced responsiveness require prompt assessment and escalation; infection is one potential cause among several.","Waiting for additional signs risks delaying evaluation of a serious neurologic change, and fever is not required to raise concern.","Medication effects are possible, but the cause is not established; independent changes to immunosuppression are not appropriate."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult liver transplant recipient in intensive care develops a new focal weakness and a change in behavior. The patient has no reported headache, and medication toxicity and a vascular event are also being considered. Which nursing response best transfers the same recognition skill to this presentation?","options":["Treat the new neurologic changes as urgent, report them promptly, and include central nervous system infection among possible causes.","Focus only on the vascular possibility because a focal deficit makes infection an unlikely concern.","Defer escalation unless headache or fever appears, since behavior change alone is nonspecific."],"feedback":["Correct. Focal deficits and behavior changes can occur with serious neurologic disease; infection remains a consideration in an immunosuppressed transplant recipient while the team evaluates alternatives.","A vascular event is a relevant alternative, but it does not exclude infection or remove the need to promptly report the change.","Behavior change can be nonspecific, but new neurologic changes in this context warrant communication rather than waiting for additional symptoms."]},{"stem":"A fictional adult kidney transplant recipient remains confused after an initial cerebrospinal fluid culture shows no growth. The patient continues to have neurologic symptoms, and other diagnostic findings are still being reviewed. Which reassessment conclusion is most appropriate?","options":["Continue close neurologic observation and update the team because one negative culture does not resolve persistent clinical concern.","Consider central nervous system infection excluded and return to routine neurologic checks.","Attribute the confusion to immunosuppressive medication unless a later culture becomes positive."],"feedback":["Correct. In the reviewed cohort, only a small proportion of cerebrospinal fluid cultures were positive, and diagnosis also relied on other evidence; persistent symptoms merit continued communication and reassessment.","A negative culture alone is not enough to dismiss ongoing neurologic symptoms or exclude infection.","Medication effects are possible, but the finding does not establish that explanation; ongoing symptoms need team review."]}],"sources":[{"title":"COVID-19 and solid organ transplantation: Finding the right balance","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9251959/"},{"title":"Central Nervous System Infections in Pediatric Solid Organ Transplant Recipients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13478769"}],"frames":[{"label":"Immunosuppression raises risk","detail":"Maintenance immunosuppression after transplantation increases infection vulnerability, and central nervous system infection may present atypically or resemble other problems."},{"label":"New confusion and headache","detail":"A transplant recipient with new headache, vomiting, and increasing confusion needs attention even without fever or obvious focal weakness."},{"label":"Notify transplant clinicians","detail":"Promptly report the neurologic change and transplant history after focused airway, breathing, circulation, vital-sign, and neurologic assessment."},{"label":"Trend neurologic status","detail":"Trend alertness, behavior, speech, strength, seizures, and vital signs; escalate worsening consciousness, a new focal deficit, seizure, or instability."}]},{"id":"s080","unit":"multi","title":"Maternal critical illness","objective":"Recognize evolving preeclampsia-related maternal critical illness and communicate priorities for timely team evaluation and reassessment.","concept":"Preeclampsia can emerge after 20 weeks of pregnancy or after birth, and may injure the brain, kidneys, liver, lungs, and placenta. Placental and vascular dysfunction can reduce organ perfusion and disrupt the vessel lining, producing hypertension, proteinuria, and other organ findings. Proteinuria is not essential when new hypertension accompanies end-organ damage. Seizures signal eclampsia, while HELLP can occur without hypertension or proteinuria.","recognize":"Watch for new or worsening hypertension, severe persistent headache, visual change, confusion, brisk reflexes, right upper abdominal or epigastric pain, dyspnea, or reduced urine output. Proteinuria supports preeclampsia but a negative dipstick does not exclude it. New hypertension after 20 weeks without proteinuria or organ findings may be gestational hypertension; HELLP may lack both hypertension and proteinuria.","respond":"Escalate concerning symptoms or seizure promptly to the obstetric team and follow the local emergency pathway. Communicate blood pressure trends, neurologic and respiratory findings, urine output, relevant laboratory results, and fetal status. Under orders and local protocols, prepare for magnesium sulfate when indicated and support evaluation and delivery planning after maternal stabilization. Do not independently prescribe or delay escalation while waiting for proteinuria.","reassess":"After team-directed actions, reassess blood pressure, headache or visual symptoms, mental status, reflexes, breathing, urine output, abdominal pain, and fetal status as appropriate. Review ordered platelet, kidney, and liver results. Worsening neurologic, respiratory, renal, hepatic, or fetal findings, or a seizure, warrants immediate escalation.","trap":"A negative urine dipstick is not an all-clear: preeclampsia can be recognized through new hypertension with organ injury even when protein is not detected. Think of proteinuria as one warning indicator on a wider dashboard, not the master switch; HELLP can also present without hypertension or proteinuria.","caseStory":"A patient at 31 weeks reports a severe persistent headache and right upper abdominal pain, and her blood pressure is newly elevated. A urine dipstick does not show protein, but ordered tests show a low platelet count and elevated liver enzymes. The nurse must decide whether the urine result is reassuring or whether urgent escalation is needed.","caseReason":"The negative dipstick does not rule out preeclampsia, and new hypertension with neurologic or hepatic concerns requires prompt evaluation. Low platelets and elevated liver enzymes raise concern for HELLP, which can occur without proteinuria. The nurse should promptly communicate the findings and follow the obstetric emergency pathway.","teachback":"In a pregnant or recently postpartum patient with new hypertension, what findings would make you escalate concern even if a urine dipstick is negative, and what would you communicate to the obstetric team?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient at 29 weeks has newly elevated blood pressure, persistent visual disturbance, and a negative urine dipstick. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication to the obstetric team?","options":["Preeclampsia remains a concern because new hypertension with end-organ symptoms can qualify without detected proteinuria.","Gestational hypertension is most likely because the urine dipstick is negative and no protein has been confirmed.","The visual disturbance is not relevant to hypertensive disease unless a seizure or severe swelling also occurs."],"feedback":["Correct. New hypertension with visual symptoms can indicate end-organ involvement, and a negative dipstick does not exclude preeclampsia.","This is misleading because gestational hypertension is defined by the absence of proteinuria and other end-organ findings; visual disturbance requires evaluation.","This is misleading because visual disturbance is a severe-feature warning sign and should be communicated promptly, even without seizure or swelling."]},{"stem":"A patient with preeclampsia develops a generalized seizure in the labor unit. Which nursing response is the priority?","options":["Activate the obstetric emergency response, promptly notify the team, and prepare for ordered seizure treatment and maternal stabilization.","Collect a urine specimen for protein measurement before notifying the team to clarify the diagnosis.","Begin delivery preparations as the sole immediate response, postponing seizure management until the birth plan is settled."],"feedback":["Correct. Eclampsia requires prompt treatment to prevent recurrent seizures and maternal stabilization before delivery planning proceeds.","This is misleading because the seizure is an emergency; urine testing must not delay escalation or treatment.","This is misleading because delivery is definitive treatment for preeclampsia, but maternal stabilization, including seizure control, comes first."]},{"stem":"A patient with chronic hypertension is now 27 weeks pregnant. Compared with her baseline, blood pressure has risen, and she has developed new proteinuria. Which interpretation is most appropriate for the nurse to report?","options":["The pattern raises concern for preeclampsia superimposed on chronic hypertension and warrants prompt obstetric evaluation.","The pattern is chronic hypertension alone because hypertension was present before pregnancy.","The pattern is gestational hypertension because the blood pressure increase occurred after 20 weeks."],"feedback":["Correct. New proteinuria, blood pressure elevation above baseline, or organ findings after 20 weeks can indicate superimposed preeclampsia.","This is misleading because chronic hypertension does not rule out superimposed preeclampsia when new concerning changes develop.","This is misleading because gestational hypertension describes new hypertension without proteinuria or end-organ damage, rather than worsening chronic hypertension with new proteinuria."]},{"stem":"Three days after delivery, a patient reports a severe headache and shortness of breath. Her urine dipstick is negative for protein. Which reassessment and action best address the misleading urine result?","options":["Promptly escalate the symptoms and reassess blood pressure, neurologic status, and breathing because postpartum preeclampsia remains possible.","Document the negative dipstick and continue routine observation because delivery has removed the placental cause.","Focus on urine testing alone and defer assessment of breathing unless proteinuria is later confirmed."],"feedback":["Correct. Preeclampsia can occur postpartum, and headache and dyspnea may reflect serious organ involvement; a negative dipstick does not rule it out.","This is misleading because preeclampsia can develop after delivery, and symptoms may signal urgent maternal complications.","This is misleading because respiratory and neurologic symptoms require prompt assessment and escalation regardless of dipstick findings."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Preeclampsia and Eclampsia - Gynecology and Obstetrics - Merck Manuals Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/preeclampsia-and-eclampsia/?autoredirectid=23422"},{"title":"Preeclampsia and Eclampsia - Gynecology and Obstetrics","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/preeclampsia-and-eclampsia"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pregnancy-related organ injury","detail":"Preeclampsia can emerge after 20 weeks and affect multiple organs; HELLP may occur without hypertension or proteinuria."},{"label":"Pain and abnormal labs","detail":"New hypertension, severe headache, right upper abdominal pain, low platelets, and elevated liver enzymes raise urgent concern despite a negative urine dipstick."},{"label":"Escalate obstetric findings","detail":"Promptly communicate blood pressure, neurologic and abdominal symptoms, and laboratory results to the obstetric team; follow the emergency pathway without waiting for proteinuria."},{"label":"Reassess maternal status","detail":"Reassess blood pressure, symptoms, breathing, urine output, and ordered results; immediately escalate worsening organ or fetal findings, or any seizure."}]},{"id":"s081","unit":"multi","title":"Recovery after cardiac arrest","objective":"Use nursing assessment and team communication to support careful, multimodal neurological prognostication and recovery-focused care after adult cardiac arrest.","concept":"After cardiac arrest, brain injury can develop during absent or reduced blood flow and continue after circulation returns through reperfusion and secondary injury. Hypoxemia, hypotension, fever, seizures, and metabolic disturbances can worsen injury. Because sedatives, temperature management, and other confounders can alter examination findings, prognosis should be assessed at an appropriate time using several complementary modalities rather than a single sign.","recognize":"In a patient who remains comatose, note level of consciousness, command following, motor response, pupils, corneal reflexes, and any myoclonus, while checking whether sedation, temperature management, or metabolic problems may affect findings. Bilateral absent pupillary responses later in the assessment are concerning, but corneal findings or early myoclonus alone do not establish prognosis. EEG, evoked potentials, biomarkers, and imaging add context.","respond":"Support ordered post-arrest care by observing oxygenation, ventilation, hemodynamics, temperature, glucose, and seizure activity, and promptly communicating changes to the team. Perform and document neurological observations as directed, noting medication and temperature-management context. Follow local protocols for escalation and testing; do not infer a definitive outcome from one examination, an early myoclonic movement, or a single test.","reassess":"After an intervention or change in condition, reassess and document consciousness, command following, motor response, pupils, oxygenation, hemodynamics, temperature, and seizure-like activity, including relevant medication context. Escalate new neurological changes, suspected seizures, or instability promptly, and report persistent concerning findings for team review alongside other prognostic data.","trap":"One abnormal sign is not a verdict: prognostication is more like combining independent views of a landscape than trusting one photograph. Early myoclonus, absent corneal reflexes alone, or an examination affected by sedation or temperature management can mislead; timing, confounders, and other tests matter.","caseStory":"A fictional adult remains unresponsive after resuscitation and temperature management. Around the later assessment, sedation has been stopped, but the team is checking whether drug effects and metabolic disturbances have cleared; bilateral pupillary responses are absent, while the EEG remains suppressed. The nurse must decide how to communicate these findings without presenting them as a certain outcome.","caseReason":"The absent bilateral pupillary responses and persistent EEG suppression are concerning findings, but they should be interpreted in context and combined with other modalities. The nurse should report the examination, its timing, and potential confounders, support the planned assessment, and avoid describing prognosis as settled on these findings alone.","teachback":"What would you include when reporting a concerning neurological examination after cardiac arrest so the team can interpret it in context rather than as a stand-alone prognosis?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult remains comatose after cardiac arrest. During a later assessment, the team documents absent pupillary light responses on both sides and a persistently suppressed EEG. Medication effects and temperature-management context are being reviewed. Which nursing interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["These are concerning findings to communicate and integrate with other prognostic information after confounders are considered.","Together, these findings establish an irreversible outcome, so further assessment is unnecessary.","The EEG finding should be disregarded because the neurological examination is more useful than other modalities."],"feedback":["Both findings are associated with poor neurological outcome, but prognostication should be multimodal and account for timing and confounders.","This overstates certainty. Even concerning examination and EEG findings should be considered with other assessments and clinical context.","This incorrectly dismisses a complementary modality. EEG can contribute important information alongside examination and other tests."]},{"stem":"An adult is still comatose one day after resuscitation while receiving temperature management and sedative medication. A colleague proposes documenting that the neurological prognosis is poor based on the current motor response. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Continue ordered recovery-focused care, document the examination and confounders, and communicate that prognostic assessment should wait for appropriate timing and review.","Treat the motor response as a definitive predictor and advise the family that recovery is unlikely.","Stop temperature management and sedation so that an immediate examination can determine the outcome."],"feedback":["Early examination can be affected by medication and temperature management. The nurse should support care, record context, and communicate the need for appropriately timed assessment.","A motor response alone does not reliably establish poor outcome, especially in an early assessment with potential confounders.","This proposes changing treatment outside the nurse’s independent role and does not account for the planned management or appropriate prognostic timing."]},{"stem":"A patient who remains comatose develops repeated jerking movements within two days of cardiac arrest. Pupillary responses are present, and the movements occur intermittently. Which nursing action best supports interpretation?","options":["Report the movements and request team review for EEG assessment of their origin and possible epileptic activity.","Document the movements as proof of poor neurological outcome because myoclonus is always prognostic.","Reassure the team that preserved pupillary responses rule out clinically important brain injury."],"feedback":["Myoclonus should be reported, and EEG can help determine whether it is cortical, subcortical, or associated with epileptic activity. Myoclonus alone is not a reliable poor-outcome predictor.","This treats a single sign as conclusive. The references caution that myoclonus alone does not reliably predict poor outcome.","Preserved pupillary responses do not rule out significant injury or provide a reliable favorable prognosis; assessment should remain multimodal."]},{"stem":"After sedation is stopped, a comatose adult has preserved pupillary responses, absent corneal responses, and an extensor motor response. The nurse is preparing the next handoff. Which approach best avoids a misleading conclusion?","options":["Report each finding, timing, and medication context, noting that absent corneal responses alone are not a reliable predictor and need team interpretation.","Describe the absent corneal responses as a definitive poor-outcome marker because sedation has been stopped.","Use the extensor motor response alone to determine prognosis and omit the other examination findings."],"feedback":["This preserves the full clinical picture and recognizes that corneal reflexes alone are less reliable; the team can interpret findings with other modalities and confounders.","Stopping sedation does not make an isolated corneal finding definitive. Residual medication effects and other contextual factors still require consideration.","Motor response can help assessment but is not independently definitive; omitting other findings would undermine multimodal interpretation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Neurological prognostication after cardiac arrest - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13633088/"},{"title":"Prognostication of patients after cardiopulmonary resuscitation","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7807999/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Post-arrest injury","detail":"Brain injury can evolve during low blood flow and continue after circulation returns through reperfusion and secondary injury."},{"label":"Pupillary and EEG findings","detail":"Later bilateral absent pupillary responses and persistent EEG suppression are concerning, but sedation and metabolic problems may confound interpretation."},{"label":"Report context, not certainty","detail":"Report examination timing and medication or temperature-management context, and support multimodal team assessment without presenting prognosis as settled."},{"label":"Trend neurological status","detail":"Document consciousness, motor response, pupils, oxygenation, hemodynamics, and temperature; escalate new neurological changes or suspected seizures."}]},{"id":"s082","unit":"multi","title":"Temperature emergencies","objective":"Use heat exposure, neurologic status, and response to cooling to recognize suspected heatstroke, prioritize urgent team-based care, and identify findings that need escalation.","concept":"Heat illness develops when environmental and metabolic heat gain exceed the body’s ability to lose heat. Sweating and increased skin blood flow initially support cooling, but dehydration, humidity, exertion, or impaired sweating can overwhelm these responses. Severe hyperthermia can trigger inflammation and dysfunction in the brain, muscles, kidneys, liver, lungs, heart, and coagulation system. Recognizing this progression matters because rapid cooling and supportive care can limit serious harm.","recognize":"Suspect heatstroke when heat exposure or strenuous exertion accompanies altered mental status, such as confusion, unusual behavior, ataxia, seizures, or coma; a very high core temperature strengthens concern. Sweating can be present or absent, so a moist skin surface does not rule it out. Heat exhaustion generally preserves normal central nervous system function. If the picture is unclear, consider other causes of hyperthermia and neurologic change.","respond":"Treat suspected heatstroke as an emergency: activate the local response and arrange urgent transport while the team begins rapid cooling under protocol. Cold-water immersion is preferred when feasible; evaporative cooling is another effective approach. Support resuscitation and isotonic IV fluids as directed, and communicate neurologic status, core temperature, and response. Do not delay cooling for tests, rely on ice packs alone, or use antipyretics as treatment.","reassess":"Follow core temperature continuously when available, neurologic status, vital signs, urine output, and ordered laboratory trends for muscle, kidney, liver, electrolyte, or coagulation injury. Report worsening consciousness, unstable vital signs, poor urine output, or evidence of organ dysfunction promptly. Stop active cooling near the team’s target to avoid overcooling.","trap":"A person with heatstroke can still be sweating, so a dry-skin rule is as unreliable as judging a storm by whether the pavement is wet. Heat exhaustion does not explain altered central nervous system function in the same way; neurologic change with heat exposure calls for urgent escalation.","caseStory":"During a hot outdoor training session, an adult athlete collapses and is confused, unsteady, and tachycardic. The skin is moist, and a core temperature measurement is markedly elevated. The nurse must decide whether this is uncomplicated heat exhaustion or a time-critical emergency and what to communicate first.","caseReason":"Heat exposure, a very high core temperature, and impaired mental status point toward heatstroke rather than heat exhaustion, even though the athlete is sweating. The nurse should activate the emergency response, communicate the neurologic findings and temperature, and support rapid cooling and transport under local protocol.","teachback":"When someone becomes confused after heat exposure, what findings would make you suspect heatstroke, and what would you communicate and reassess as cooling begins?","questions":[{"stem":"After working outdoors in hot weather, an adult becomes disoriented and walks unsteadily. The person is sweating, tachycardic, and has a very high core temperature. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next communication?","options":["Suspect heatstroke because heat exposure with neurologic dysfunction is more concerning than whether the skin is wet or dry.","Suspect heat exhaustion because sweating indicates that heat loss remains adequate despite the change in behavior.","Prioritize a neurologic event because a moist skin surface makes a heat-related emergency unlikely."],"feedback":["Correct. Altered mental status with heat exposure and a very high core temperature supports concern for heatstroke; sweating may still be present.","Sweating does not establish that heat dissipation is adequate, and neurologic dysfunction is a key distinction from heat exhaustion.","Other neurologic causes may need consideration, but moist skin does not exclude heatstroke in this presentation."]},{"stem":"At a sports event, an athlete collapses after intense exertion in hot weather and is now confused. A very high core temperature is recorded. Which nursing action has the highest priority while help is arranged?","options":["Activate the emergency response and support rapid cooling and transport under local protocol.","Obtain a complete set of laboratory samples before beginning cooling to clarify the cause.","Offer oral fluids and observe for improvement before escalating care."],"feedback":["Correct. Suspected heatstroke requires urgent recognition, rapid cooling, and hospital transport; assessment and resuscitation proceed alongside cooling.","Laboratory testing can assess organ dysfunction, but it should not delay effective cooling in suspected heatstroke.","Confusion signals a serious emergency, and oral fluids with observation are not an appropriate substitute for urgent team-based care."]},{"stem":"During a heat wave, an older adult is found confused in an unventilated apartment. The person is hot to the touch, and no recent exertion is reported. Which interpretation and response best fit this context?","options":["Consider classic heatstroke, activate urgent care, and communicate the heat exposure and mental-status change.","Consider exertional heatstroke, because abrupt neurologic changes are typical even without strenuous activity.","Consider heat exhaustion, because prolonged indoor exposure is less concerning than collapse during exercise."],"feedback":["Correct. Classic heatstroke can develop over days in older adults exposed to heat, and altered mental status makes the situation urgent.","Exertional heatstroke is associated with strenuous activity, whereas this history fits a different heatstroke pattern.","Heat exhaustion generally preserves normal central nervous system function; confusion after heat exposure requires urgent concern."]},{"stem":"During protocol-based cooling for suspected heatstroke, an adult’s continuously monitored core temperature reaches approximately 39°C. The person remains sweaty but is more responsive. What is the best nursing interpretation and action?","options":["Communicate the improvement and temperature, stop active cooling near the team’s target, and continue close monitoring.","Continue the same cooling until the person stops sweating, because perspiration shows that core temperature remains unsafe.","Give an antipyretic and end monitoring because improved responsiveness indicates the emergency has resolved."],"feedback":["Correct. Cooling is stopped near the target to reduce the risk of overcooling, while continued monitoring is needed for organ dysfunction.","Sweating can persist in heatstroke and is not a reliable endpoint for cooling; continuing may cause iatrogenic hypothermia.","Antipyretics do not treat the underlying heat illness, and improved responsiveness does not remove the need for close observation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Heatstroke - Injuries; Poisoning - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/heat-illness/heatstroke"},{"title":"Overview of Heat Illness - Injuries; Poisoning - MSD Manual ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/heat-illness/overview-of-heat-illness"}],"frames":[{"label":"Heat overwhelms cooling","detail":"Heat exposure or exertion can overwhelm the body’s cooling responses, causing severe hyperthermia and injury across multiple organs."},{"label":"Altered mentation with heat","detail":"Confusion, unusual behavior, ataxia, seizures, or coma with heat exposure suggests heatstroke; sweating does not rule it out."},{"label":"Activate cooling response","detail":"Activate the local emergency response and arrange urgent transport while supporting rapid cooling under protocol; communicate neurologic status and core temperature."},{"label":"Monitor for organ injury","detail":"Track core temperature, neurologic status, vital signs, urine output, and ordered organ-injury trends; report deterioration and avoid overcooling."}]},{"id":"s083","unit":"multi","title":"Toxic exposures","objective":"Use exposure history, toxidrome patterns, and immediate physiologic threats to guide safe, team-based nursing care for adults with suspected toxic exposure.","concept":"Toxicity often depends on the amount absorbed over time, the route of exposure, and patient characteristics. Toxidromes are clusters of findings that can suggest substance classes, but mixed exposures may blur the pattern. Effects can also be delayed when a metabolite is toxic or organ injury develops later. Exposure history helps the team interpret findings, while immediate physiologic instability takes priority over identifying a precise substance.","recognize":"Look for patterns such as reduced breathing, sedation, and pinpoint pupils with an opioid toxidrome; or tachycardia, agitation, and sweating with a sympathomimetic pattern. Dry, warm skin, dilated pupils, and delirium suggest an anticholinergic pattern. These clues guide suspicion, not certainty. Consider other causes of altered consciousness, and remember that symptoms may be delayed or mixed.","respond":"First assess the patient’s overall stability, especially airway, breathing, circulation, and level of consciousness, and promptly communicate concerning changes to the responsible clinician. Gather exposure details from the patient and others, including substance, route, amount, timing, and possible co-exposures. Follow local protocols and consult the poison center through the care team. Do not delay stabilization or treat a screening result as proof of the cause.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess breathing, airway protection, vital signs, mental status, and the direction of symptoms. Share worsening ventilation, new seizures, deteriorating consciousness, or unstable vital signs promptly with the team. Continue to watch for delayed effects when the suspected substance or exposure history raises concern.","trap":"A positive urine drug screen is a clue, not a verdict: it may reflect recent exposure without explaining the current symptoms, and routine screens detect only a limited range of substances. Think of it like a timestamped footprint; it shows that something may have passed through, but not that it caused the problem now.","caseStory":"An adult arrives drowsy with slow, shallow breathing and pinpoint pupils. A companion reports finding several medication containers nearby but cannot say what or when the person took anything. The nurse must decide whether to wait for a urine screen or immediately address the patient’s breathing while the team gathers exposure history.","caseReason":"The breathing pattern and other findings suggest an opioid toxidrome, but they do not identify the exact substance or rule out co-ingestion. Immediate physiologic stability comes first, so the nurse promptly communicates the respiratory concern and supports team assessment while obtaining collateral exposure details. A screening result should not delay care or replace consideration of other causes.","teachback":"If a toxicology screen is positive but the patient’s symptoms do not fit the suspected substance, what information and bedside findings would you bring to the team before concluding what caused the symptoms?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with possible ingestion is tachycardic, has dilated pupils, warm dry skin, urinary retention, and delirium. Which interpretation best fits this cluster of findings?","options":["An anticholinergic pattern is suggested, although the exposure history and broader assessment are still needed.","A sympathomimetic pattern is most likely because dilated pupils and tachycardia are present.","A cholinergic pattern is most likely because urinary symptoms and altered behavior are present."],"feedback":["Dry, warm skin and urinary retention alongside dilated pupils and delirium support an anticholinergic pattern; the pattern suggests a class but does not prove the substance.","Tachycardia and dilated pupils can occur with sympathomimetic exposure, but agitation and diaphoresis are more characteristic than dry skin and urinary retention.","Cholinergic findings commonly include secretions, urination, gastrointestinal symptoms, and often bronchial symptoms; this vignette instead highlights dryness and retention."]},{"stem":"An adult with unexplained reduced consciousness after a possible ingestion has shallow breathing and abnormal vital signs. The substance is unknown, and a urine drug screen has been ordered. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly communicate the instability and support immediate team assessment of airway, breathing, and circulation while exposure details are gathered.","Obtain the urine screen result first so the team can choose the most likely substance-specific response.","Focus first on identifying the exact amount ingested before escalating the respiratory findings."],"feedback":["Severe poisoning can require rapid action for airway or cardiopulmonary compromise, so immediate stability takes priority while the team investigates the exposure.","Routine urine screens are limited and do not establish the cause of symptoms; waiting for a result risks delaying attention to instability.","The amount and timing are useful history, but they should be gathered alongside, not before, escalation of concerning breathing and vital-sign findings."]},{"stem":"An adult develops cough and breathing difficulty after inhaling a chemical described by the response team as less water-soluble. Which concern best applies when the nurse reports the findings?","options":["Lower airway injury and noncardiogenic pulmonary edema are possible and warrant prompt communication and continued respiratory assessment.","Upper airway injury is the expected pattern, so lower airway effects are less relevant to reassessment.","A toxidrome pattern alone identifies the specific chemical, so further exposure history is unnecessary."],"feedback":["Less water-soluble inhaled toxins are associated with lower airway injury and possible noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, making respiratory assessment important.","Upper airway injury is more associated with water-soluble inhaled toxins; the stated property points toward lower airway concerns instead.","Exposure properties can guide the type of injury to consider, but they do not identify a precise substance or remove the need for history and assessment."]},{"stem":"An adult remains obtunded after a possible mixed ingestion, and a routine urine screen is positive for an opioid class. Which nursing interpretation and next assessment approach is most appropriate?","options":["Treat the result as one clue, reassess the patient’s current status, and communicate the need to consider other causes of altered consciousness.","Assume the opioid result explains the obtundation and focus assessment only on opioid-related findings.","Dismiss the result because routine urine screens cannot provide any useful information about possible exposure."],"feedback":["A positive screen can indicate exposure without proving that the drug caused the current symptoms; reassessment and consideration of other causes, including those identified in the clinical evaluation, help avoid anchoring.","The screen is qualitative and limited, and a detected drug may not explain the patient’s current condition, so this conclusion is too certain.","Routine screens have limitations, but they may still offer a clue; the appropriate response is cautious interpretation rather than automatic dismissal."]}],"sources":[{"title":"General Principles of Poisoning - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/poisoning/general-principles-of-poisoning"},{"title":"Symptoms and Treatment of Specific Poisons - Injuries","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/poisoning/symptoms-and-treatment-of-specific-poisons"}],"frames":[{"label":"Unknown medication exposure","detail":"Substance, route, amount, and timing may be unclear, while mixed exposures or delayed effects can blur toxidrome patterns."},{"label":"Slow shallow breathing","detail":"Drowsiness, slow shallow breathing, and pinpoint pupils suggest opioid toxicity, but nearby containers do not identify what was taken."},{"label":"Stabilize and gather history","detail":"Communicate breathing concerns promptly, support airway and breathing assessment, and gather exposure details with the team under local protocol."},{"label":"Watch ventilation trend","detail":"Reassess breathing, airway protection, consciousness, and vital signs; worsening ventilation or new seizures require prompt team communication."}]},{"id":"s084","unit":"multi","title":"Submersion injury","objective":"Explain how hypoxia after submersion shapes nursing recognition, immediate priorities, and reassessment for evolving respiratory or neurologic compromise.","concept":"Submersion can impair breathing through fluid aspiration, reflex airway closure, or both, making hypoxia the central threat to the brain, heart, and other organs. Aspiration and lung injury can also worsen oxygenation later through inflammation, impaired surfactant function, and poorly ventilated lung regions. Cold exposure may cause hypothermia. These processes make early ventilation support and continued observation important, even when initial symptoms seem limited.","recognize":"Consider submersion injury when a person has been rescued from water and develops cough, wheeze, vomiting, anxiety, altered consciousness, rapid or labored breathing, or cyanosis. Respiratory findings can be subtle or delayed. Also consider associated trauma, especially after a dive, and conditions that may have contributed, such as seizure, intoxication, hypoglycemia, or a cardiac problem.","respond":"Prioritize airway and breathing assessment, activate the emergency response, and provide oxygen or ventilation support within role, training, and local protocol. For an apneic drowning patient, rescue breathing is prioritized; if the patient does not respond, begin chest compressions and continue resuscitation with the team. If trauma is possible, support spinal motion restriction. Do not attempt to drain water from the lungs.","reassess":"After interventions, reassess breathing effort, oxygenation, lung sounds, mentation, and temperature, and communicate trends to the response team. Escalate worsening oxygenation, increasing work of breathing, abnormal lung findings, or persistent altered consciousness. Continue observation as directed because respiratory problems may emerge after an initially reassuring assessment.","trap":"A person who is quiet or briefly seems well after rescue is not necessarily in the clear; evolving lung injury can be like a leak that becomes visible over time. Trying to remove water from the lungs is not a useful shortcut, because it delays ventilation and can provoke vomiting.","caseStory":"A swimmer is pulled from a lake after submersion and is awake but anxious, coughing, and wheezing. Their breathing becomes more labored while a companion says the person looked normal immediately after rescue. The nurse must decide whether continued observation and escalation are needed.","caseReason":"Coughing, wheezing, and increasing breathing effort after submersion suggest respiratory injury and possible worsening hypoxia. The initially reassuring appearance does not rule out delayed complications, so the nurse should promptly communicate the change, support oxygenation under protocol, and ensure ongoing clinical observation.","teachback":"After a person seems to recover from submersion, which new respiratory or mental-status changes would make you escalate care, and why?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult rescued from a pool is awake and speaking but has persistent coughing and wheezing. The initial assessment showed no obvious trauma, and a bystander reports that the person briefly appeared well. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next steps?","options":["Aspiration-related lung injury may be impairing oxygenation and can worsen, so respiratory assessment and observation remain important.","A brief period of normal appearance makes clinically important respiratory injury unlikely, so routine discharge planning is appropriate.","The symptoms most strongly indicate a water-related electrolyte shift, so fluid replacement should be the immediate focus."],"feedback":["Cough and wheeze after submersion can reflect aspiration-related lung injury, and respiratory complications may be delayed. Continued assessment and observation are appropriate.","An initially reassuring appearance does not exclude evolving respiratory problems; symptoms may be subtle or delayed.","Freshwater and saltwater submersion are generally physiologically similar, and significant fluid or electrolyte correction is rarely needed."]},{"stem":"A person is removed from the water, is apneic, and has no evidence of a diving or impact mechanism. The nurse is trained in resuscitation and another responder is activating emergency services. Which action should be prioritized?","options":["Begin rescue breathing promptly and, if there is no response, proceed to chest compressions while continuing resuscitation.","Begin chest compressions first and defer ventilation until advanced responders arrive.","Attempt to expel water from the lungs before providing ventilation to improve air entry."],"feedback":["Drowning resuscitation prioritizes rescue breathing for an apneic patient; if there is no response, chest compressions follow.","Drowning is an exception to the usual compressions-first sequence described in the source; ventilation is prioritized.","Attempts to remove water delay ventilation and increase the risk of vomiting rather than helping oxygenation."]},{"stem":"After a vehicle enters a pond, an adult is rescued and is breathing but confused. Witnesses report that the vehicle struck the bottom before submersion. Which nursing approach best addresses the immediate risks?","options":["Support airway and oxygenation while maintaining spinal motion restriction and promptly involving the emergency response team.","Focus on the possible spinal injury first and defer respiratory support until imaging clarifies the mechanism.","Treat the confusion as proof of hypoxia alone and postpone assessment for head or other associated injury."],"feedback":["Respiratory support remains a priority, while the impact mechanism warrants spinal motion restriction and assessment for associated injury.","Resuscitation and respiratory support should not be delayed for diagnostic imaging when indicated.","Hypoxia may contribute to altered consciousness, but head injury and other associated causes also require consideration."]},{"stem":"A patient rescued from a bathtub is alert, has clear lungs on the first examination, and reports no breathing difficulty. During observation, the nurse notes new labored breathing and a change in mental status. Which interpretation and response are most appropriate?","options":["The change may signal evolving respiratory compromise; promptly escalate and reassess oxygenation, breathing, and mentation.","The initial normal examination makes a respiratory complication unlikely, so continue routine observation without escalation.","The change is best attributed to hypothermia alone, so respiratory reassessment can wait until temperature is corrected."],"feedback":["Respiratory symptoms can be delayed, and new labored breathing or altered mentation warrants prompt escalation and focused reassessment.","A normal initial examination does not rule out later respiratory deterioration after submersion.","Hypothermia can occur, but it does not explain away new respiratory or neurologic findings; both require reassessment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Drowning - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/drowning/drowning"},{"title":"Drowning - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/drowning/drowning?query=773"}],"frames":[{"label":"Submersion threatens oxygenation","detail":"Aspiration or reflex airway closure after submersion can impair breathing, threaten oxygenation, and cause respiratory problems to worsen later."},{"label":"Delayed respiratory signs","detail":"Coughing, wheezing, and increasing breathing effort after rescue suggest respiratory injury, even if the person initially appeared well."},{"label":"Support breathing and escalate","detail":"Activate the emergency response and provide oxygen or ventilation support within role and protocol; promptly communicate increasing work of breathing."},{"label":"Monitor for deterioration","detail":"Recheck breathing effort, oxygenation, lung sounds, mentation, and temperature; worsening oxygenation or labored breathing needs escalation."}]},{"id":"s085","unit":"multi","title":"Pain and sleep in critical care","objective":"Use structured pain assessment, timely team-based intervention, and sleep-supportive care to reduce avoidable discomfort and delirium risk in adult critical care.","concept":"Pain is both a sensory and emotional experience, and a patient’s own report is preferred when possible. Inability to speak does not rule out pain, so behavioral tools and family observations can help guide assessment. Painful procedures call for anticipatory comfort measures. Sleep support is part of delirium prevention: protecting familiar routines, daytime light, and a quieter, dimmer night environment helps preserve day-night patterns.","recognize":"Ask patients who can communicate to describe their pain, then use a validated behavioral tool when self-report is unavailable or unreliable. Notice changes in behavior, and ask family about the patient’s usual pain behaviors. Agitation may reflect pain, delirium, or another cause, so do not assume its meaning. Consider whether a painful procedure or disrupted nighttime routine may be contributing.","respond":"Prioritize assessment and comfort before a painful procedure, using ordered analgesia and appropriate nonpharmacologic measures such as explanation, family support, music, or distraction. Communicate the assessment and response to the interprofessional team, and follow local protocols for medication and validated pain tools. Avoid treating agitation as proof of pain or relying on sedation alone to manage discomfort. Protect nighttime rest where care needs allow.","reassess":"After an intervention, reassess pain using the patient’s report or the same appropriate behavioral tool, and note whether the targeted behaviors have changed. Reassess within the recommended interval and continue routine pain and sedation assessment. Persistent or worsening pain cues, new mental-status changes, or concern about the response warrant communication with the care team.","trap":"Quietness after medication is like a muted alarm, not proof that the underlying problem is fixed. Recheck pain with an appropriate tool rather than equating reduced movement or increased sleepiness with comfort.","caseStory":"An intubated adult becomes tense and grimaces during a planned turn, and a family member says this resembles the patient’s usual pain behavior. The patient cannot answer questions, and the night shift has also included frequent interruptions. The nurse must decide how to assess and address discomfort while supporting rest.","caseReason":"The inability to self-report does not exclude pain, and the turn is a potentially painful procedure. The nurse should use an appropriate behavioral pain tool, incorporate the family’s observation, and provide ordered or protocol-based comfort measures. The team can also consider ways to reduce avoidable nighttime disruption.","teachback":"When a critically ill patient cannot describe pain and becomes restless during care, how will you distinguish a pain cue from an assumption and choose a response that also protects rest?","questions":[{"stem":"An intubated adult grimaces, stiffens, and resists during wound care but cannot answer questions. A family member recognizes the behavior as similar to the patient’s usual response to pain. What is the best interpretation and next nursing step?","options":["Pain is plausible despite absent self-report; assess with a validated behavioral tool and use the family’s observation as context.","Pain is unlikely without a verbal report; wait until the procedure ends and ask the patient to rate discomfort.","The behavior establishes delirium; begin a delirium intervention without assessing pain or considering the procedure."],"feedback":["Correct. Self-report is preferred when possible, but inability to communicate does not rule out pain; behavioral assessment and family input can help.","This is misleading because nonverbal status does not exclude pain, and wound care may be painful.","This overinterprets behavior. Delirium should be assessed with a validated tool, while pain and the procedure are also considered."]},{"stem":"A patient who can communicate is scheduled for chest tube removal and reports substantial pain during prior care. Which nursing action best prioritizes comfort?","options":["Assess current pain and coordinate preprocedural analgesia and suitable nonpharmacologic support under the care plan.","Wait for visible distress during removal, then report the behavior and request a pain assessment afterward.","Use reassurance alone because medication before a procedure may conceal whether the patient is experiencing pain."],"feedback":["Correct. Chest tube removal is among the painful procedures identified in the reference, which supports anticipatory analgesia and nonpharmacologic measures.","This delays attention to a predictable source of pain rather than planning comfort beforehand.","This wrongly treats medication and reassurance as alternatives; preprocedural analgesia and nonpharmacologic support may both be considered."]},{"stem":"In a different ICU context, a nonverbal patient becomes restless during the night. The patient wears hearing aids at home, and staff have completed several avoidable interruptions. Which nursing approach best combines pain assessment with prevention priorities?","options":["Use an appropriate behavioral pain tool, restore hearing support if available, orient the patient, and reduce avoidable nighttime disruption.","Treat the restlessness as pain without assessment, then keep the room bright and continue routine interruptions for observation.","Assume the patient is delirious because of nighttime behavior and defer pain assessment until the next shift."],"feedback":["Correct. Behavioral assessment helps evaluate pain when self-report is unavailable, while sensory support, orientation, and protecting nighttime rest are delirium-prevention strategies.","This assumes the cause without assessment and conflicts with strategies to support nighttime sleep.","Nighttime restlessness alone does not establish delirium, and delaying pain assessment overlooks a possible contributor."]},{"stem":"After an ordered pain intervention, a previously restless patient is quieter and appears drowsy. Which nursing action best determines whether the intervention helped?","options":["Reassess pain using self-report if possible or the appropriate behavioral tool, and report persistent cues or concerning changes.","Conclude that pain has resolved because movement has decreased, and stop further pain assessment unless the patient calls out.","Interpret drowsiness as proof of adequate pain relief and avoid distinguishing sedation from the patient’s pain response."],"feedback":["Correct. Reduced activity alone does not establish comfort; reassessment with an appropriate pain measure helps determine response, and concerning findings should be communicated.","This mistakes reduced movement for evidence of pain relief and may miss persistent discomfort in a patient who cannot call out.","Drowsiness is not a pain score. Pain and sedation require appropriate assessment rather than being treated as interchangeable."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Untitled","url":"https://sccm.org/tab-pages/icu-liberation-bundle-(a-f)?PWP57f5689392c54854bccac791a7d69f2f=true"},{"title":"All SCCM Guidelines","url":"https://www.sccm.org/clinical-resources/guidelines/guidelines"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pain during repositioning","detail":"An intubated patient grimaces during a turn after a night of frequent care interruptions."},{"label":"Behavioral pain cues","detail":"Inability to speak does not exclude pain; the potentially painful turn and family-described behavior support behavioral assessment."},{"label":"Use tool and comfort measures","detail":"Use a validated behavioral pain tool and ordered or protocol-based comfort measures, then communicate findings and response to the team."},{"label":"Recheck comfort and rest","detail":"Reassess with the same appropriate tool after intervention; persistent pain cues warrant team communication, and reduce avoidable nighttime interruptions where care allows."}]},{"id":"s086","unit":"multi","title":"Frailty and nutritional decline","objective":"Use changes in nutrition, physical performance, and daily function to recognize possible frailty, communicate concerns, and support coordinated assessment and follow-up.","concept":"Frailty reflects reduced physiologic reserve and intrinsic capacity across systems, increasing vulnerability to stressors; it can coexist with sarcopenia and functional decline. Nutritional risk can contribute to muscle loss and impaired function, while frailty may make shopping, cooking, chewing, swallowing, or eating independently harder. Weight loss, poor intake, inactivity, multimorbidity, and psychosocial barriers can reinforce one another. Early recognition matters because some contributors are modifiable and care should be individualized.","recognize":"Notice unintentional weight loss, poor appetite or intake, fatigue, slower gait, weakness, reduced activity, falls, and increasing help with daily tasks, alongside cognitive, medical, mood, oral-health, or social concerns. Distinguish a pattern of reduced function and reserve from a single symptom or acute illness: these may trigger screening or explain decline, but no one cue alone establishes frailty. Ask about baseline and trajectory.","respond":"Treat a positive screen as a prompt for fuller assessment, not a diagnosis. Communicate the trajectory, intake and weight change, mobility, function, cognition, comorbidities, and barriers to the responsible clinician; use local pathways for comprehensive geriatric and nutrition assessment. Support documented nutrition and mobility plans, medication review, and social support with the interprofessional team; do not independently prescribe supplements or exercise intensity.","reassess":"After the agreed intervention, reassess actual intake, weight trend when available, fatigue, mobility or performance, ability to perform daily activities, and barriers or tolerance; compare with baseline rather than relying on one score. Escalate worsening intake or weight loss, declining function, new cognitive concern, or inability to participate to the responsible clinician for reassessment.","trap":"A frailty screen is a smoke alarm, not a full fire investigation: a positive result signals the need to look for contributors and plan care, not a definitive diagnosis. Likewise, one reassuring symptom or score cannot replace attention to the broader pattern of intake and functional change.","caseStory":"A fictional 76-year-old is being assessed before elective abdominal surgery and reports unintentional weight loss, early satiety, fatigue, and eating only one small meal most days. The person uses a cane outdoors, walks more slowly than before, and relies on a family member for grocery shopping. A screening result is positive, and the nurse must decide what it means and what to communicate next.","caseReason":"The combination of weight loss and poor intake with reduced mobility and reliance on help raises concern for frailty and nutritional risk; neither the screening result nor any single cue establishes a diagnosis. The nurse should communicate the trajectory and functional findings and support referral through local pathways for comprehensive geriatric and nutrition assessment.","teachback":"When an older adult has both declining intake and changing mobility, what information would you communicate to the team, and why should a positive frailty screen lead to fuller assessment rather than stand alone?","questions":[{"stem":"A 79-year-old medical-ward patient has had unintentional weight loss and poor appetite for several months, walks more slowly than before, and now needs help with some daily tasks. A brief frailty screen is positive. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next step?","options":["The findings raise concern for frailty and nutritional risk; communicate them and support comprehensive assessment of contributors.","The weight loss and poor appetite establish malnutrition as the sole problem; defer assessment of mobility and daily function.","The positive screen confirms frailty; document the diagnosis and proceed without further multidimensional assessment."],"feedback":["Correct. The combined nutrition, mobility, and functional changes warrant fuller assessment; a positive screen is not itself a diagnosis.","This narrows the picture too much. Frailty and nutritional risk can overlap, and functional and mobility changes are relevant to assessment.","A screen identifies concern, not a definitive diagnosis. Further assessment can identify potentially modifiable medical, functional, cognitive, nutritional, or social contributors."]},{"stem":"An older adult awaiting surgery reports eating very little and losing weight, has slowed gait, and depends on a relative for transportation. The nurse notes these concerns during preoperative assessment. Which action is the best priority?","options":["Communicate the changes and functional context to the responsible clinician, and follow local pathways for comprehensive geriatric and nutrition assessment.","Select and recommend a high-protein supplement independently, then reassess appetite at the next visit.","Repeat the same brief screen before sharing findings, because an initial positive result may not be clinically meaningful."],"feedback":["Correct. Sharing the trajectory and barriers supports coordinated assessment and individualized planning without treating a screen as a diagnosis.","Nutrition intervention may be part of a plan, but the nurse should not independently prescribe it; assessment should also consider function and other contributors.","A positive screen should prompt further assessment and communication, not delay them until the same screen is repeated."]},{"stem":"During a home visit, a community-dwelling older adult says shopping has become difficult, meals are often skipped, and walking around the home has slowed since a recent decline in health. Which response best transfers frailty recognition into this setting?","options":["Report the intake and mobility changes and explore practical or social barriers with the team as part of a fuller assessment.","Focus on meal frequency alone, because difficulty shopping is separate from frailty and functional assessment.","Reassure the person that the concern is resolved if they can still walk independently inside the home."],"feedback":["Correct. Poor intake, slower mobility, and barriers to obtaining food can be connected concerns; communicating them supports multidimensional assessment.","This overlooks how social and functional circumstances can affect nutrition and frailty. Both intake and the barriers to it matter.","Retained ability to walk inside does not rule out a broader pattern of reduced function or reserve. Assess change from baseline and other concerns."]},{"stem":"After an agreed nutrition and mobility plan begins, an older adult says appetite is better and the screening score is unchanged. The nurse observes that the person now needs help rising from a chair and requires more assistance with daily activities than at baseline. What is the best reassessment response?","options":["Escalate the new functional decline to the responsible clinician, while reporting the improved appetite and unchanged screen score.","Consider the plan successful because appetite improved, and wait for the next scheduled screening before communicating further.","Repeat only the appetite questions, because unchanged screening results make the mobility findings less informative."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening performance and increased help with daily activities matter even when appetite improves or a screen score is unchanged, and warrant communication.","Improved appetite is useful information but does not negate declining function. Waiting could overlook a meaningful change from baseline.","The functional change is clinically relevant to frailty assessment and should not be dismissed because a score is unchanged."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Frailty in older adults: detection and management in primary care. Fisterra clinical practice guideline","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13754928/"},{"title":"Nutrition and Frailty in the Older Adult: A Case-Based Approach to Assessment, Diagnosis, Intervention, Monitoring, and Evaluation in Preoperative Care","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13762823/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Reserve and nutrition","detail":"Frailty reduces physiologic reserve; poor intake and muscle loss can reinforce slower movement and growing dependence."},{"label":"Combined decline cues","detail":"Weight loss, one small meal, slower walking, and grocery help raise concern; neither a screening result nor one cue establishes frailty."},{"label":"Functional findings shared","detail":"Report weight and intake trajectory, mobility, daily function, and barriers; support local comprehensive geriatric and nutrition assessment."},{"label":"Baseline function tracked","detail":"Recheck intake, weight trend, mobility, and daily tasks; escalate worsening loss or function to the responsible clinician."}]},{"id":"s087","unit":"multi","title":"Obesity and respiratory reserve","objective":"Recognize possible obesity hypoventilation syndrome and describe nursing priorities for supporting prescribed care and monitoring ventilation.","concept":"Excess adiposity increases the mechanical load on breathing and can blunt the ventilatory response to carbon dioxide, reducing respiratory reserve. When this contributes to inadequate ventilation while awake, carbon dioxide rises; the kidneys may retain bicarbonate over time. Obstructive sleep apnea commonly coexists, but daytime hypercapnia also requires consideration of other causes. Recognizing this pattern matters because oxygen saturation alone does not establish adequate ventilation.","recognize":"Consider OHS when a person with obesity has unexplained dyspnea, daytime sleepiness, morning headaches, or hypoxemia, especially with sleep-disordered breathing. A raised serum bicarbonate can suggest chronic carbon dioxide retention, but is not diagnostic; arterial blood gas measurement assesses awake hypercapnia. Distinguish OHS from other causes by supporting evaluation for lung, medication-related, neuromuscular, or metabolic explanations.","respond":"Prioritize respiratory assessment and timely communication of concerning changes, including increasing sleepiness, hypoxemia, or signs of respiratory distress. Support ordered positive airway pressure use and coordinate with the respiratory, pulmonary, or sleep team under local protocols. Do not treat supplemental oxygen as a substitute for ventilation support or delay prescribed PAP while weight loss is pursued.","reassess":"After an intervention, reassess alertness, work and pattern of breathing, oxygenation, and tolerance and use of prescribed PAP. Review blood gas results when obtained under the care plan, since improved saturation alone may not show improved carbon dioxide clearance. Escalate promptly for worsening drowsiness, respiratory distress, or failure to improve.","trap":"A reassuring oxygen saturation is like a full fuel gauge that says nothing about whether the engine is clearing exhaust. Oxygen alone does not correct hypoventilation and may worsen carbon dioxide retention, so assess ventilation and follow the prescribed PAP plan.","caseStory":"A fictional adult with severe obesity reports morning headaches and marked daytime sleepiness, and a family member describes loud snoring and pauses in breathing. The serum bicarbonate is elevated, and an arterial blood gas shows awake hypercapnia; the team is evaluating other causes. The nurse must decide what to communicate and what the bicarbonate result does, and does not, establish.","caseReason":"The symptoms and awake hypercapnia raise concern for OHS, while the bicarbonate result supports possible chronic compensation but cannot confirm the diagnosis by itself. OHS requires obesity and awake hypoventilation with other causes excluded. The nurse should communicate the pattern and support the ordered diagnostic and respiratory care plan.","teachback":"When a patient with obesity becomes more alert and has a better oxygen saturation after an intervention, what additional findings would help you judge whether ventilation has also improved?","questions":[{"stem":"A fictional adult with obesity, loud snoring, daytime sleepiness, and unexplained dyspnea has a raised serum bicarbonate. An arterial blood gas obtained while awake shows hypercapnia. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's communication with the team?","options":["The pattern raises concern for OHS, but the blood gas and evaluation for alternative causes are needed to establish the diagnosis.","The raised bicarbonate alone confirms OHS, so evaluation for other causes is no longer necessary.","The snoring establishes obstructive sleep apnea as the cause, so awake hypercapnia does not need further attention."],"feedback":["Correct. Bicarbonate can suggest chronic carbon dioxide retention, while awake hypercapnia supports concern for OHS; other causes must still be excluded.","Bicarbonate is a screening clue and is not specific enough to confirm OHS or eliminate the need to evaluate other causes.","Obstructive sleep apnea often coexists with OHS, but does not by itself explain or exclude persistent awake hypercapnia."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient with diagnosed OHS is stable at home and has coexisting severe obstructive sleep apnea. The patient asks whether to wait on prescribed airway pressure treatment until substantial weight loss occurs. What is the nurse's best priority?","options":["Support use of the prescribed CPAP and reinforce that treatment should not be delayed while weight loss is pursued.","Recommend postponing airway pressure until weight loss is achieved, then reassess symptoms with the clinician.","Ask the patient to request bilevel ventilation as the routine first treatment for stable OHS with severe obstructive sleep apnea."],"feedback":["Correct. For stable ambulatory OHS with severe OSA, CPAP is suggested as first-line PAP, and treatment should not be delayed for weight loss.","This delays treatment of sleep-disordered breathing and hypoventilation; weight management is important but is not a reason to defer prescribed PAP.","The guideline favors CPAP rather than NIV as initial treatment for stable ambulatory OHS with severe OSA; treatment selection remains with the clinical team."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient hospitalized with respiratory failure is suspected of having OHS, but has no prior sleep study or PAP settings. The patient is improving and discharge planning has begun. Which nursing action best supports the recommended plan?","options":["Coordinate with the team to support discharge with prescribed noninvasive ventilation and arrange outpatient diagnostic evaluation and PAP titration.","Support discharge without ventilation support because the diagnosis and ideal PAP settings are not yet confirmed.","Arrange oxygen alone at discharge and defer sleep evaluation unless daytime symptoms continue."],"feedback":["Correct. For hospitalized patients with respiratory failure suspected of having OHS, NIV is suggested at discharge until outpatient evaluation and PAP titration.","The lack of a completed sleep study does not remove the recommendation to use NIV temporarily after hospitalization for respiratory failure when OHS is suspected.","Oxygen alone does not address hypoventilation, and outpatient diagnostic evaluation and PAP titration should not be deferred on this basis."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient with OHS is receiving prescribed supplemental oxygen with PAP. The oxygen saturation improves, but the patient becomes increasingly drowsy. What is the nurse's best response?","options":["Reassess breathing and alertness, promptly notify the team, and follow the care plan for evaluating ventilation, including blood gas testing if ordered.","Treat the improved oxygen saturation as evidence of adequate ventilation and continue the current plan without further assessment.","Increase oxygen under the assumption that drowsiness indicates inadequate oxygenation, then reassess saturation alone."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening drowsiness can signal deterioration; oxygen saturation does not establish carbon dioxide clearance, so prompt assessment and team communication are appropriate.","A better saturation alone does not show that ventilation or hypercapnia has improved, particularly when alertness is worsening.","Oxygen alone is not a substitute for ventilation support and can worsen carbon dioxide retention; changing therapy must follow orders and local protocols."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Treatment / Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542216/"},{"title":"Evaluation and Management of Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome. An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6680300/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Reduced breathing reserve","detail":"Excess adiposity increases breathing’s mechanical load and can blunt carbon-dioxide response, reducing respiratory reserve."},{"label":"Awake hypercapnia checked","detail":"Elevated bicarbonate may suggest chronic retention, but awake hypercapnia on blood gas and exclusion of other causes are needed."},{"label":"Respiratory pattern shared","detail":"Report sleepiness, hypoxemia, or distress promptly; support ordered positive airway pressure and coordinate with respiratory or sleep teams."},{"label":"Ventilation signs tracked","detail":"Recheck alertness, breathing effort, oxygenation, and prescribed PAP use; worsening drowsiness or distress needs prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s088","unit":"multi","title":"End-of-life symptom care","objective":"Apply comfort-focused nursing assessment and team communication to recognize and respond to common symptoms near the end of life.","concept":"As illness advances, multiple body systems may decline, reducing alertness, swallowing, intake, and the ability to clear secretions. Symptoms can have different causes and may change as physiology worsens, so comfort care depends on observing the patient and reassessing rather than relying on one sign. When goals focus on comfort, simple measures and timely team communication can reduce distress while avoiding burdensome evaluation or interventions unlikely to help.","recognize":"Assess the patient’s experience and observable cues, not sound or family distress alone. Breathlessness may accompany heart or lung illness, while noisy breathing near death can result from pooled secretions and may not discomfort the patient. Reduced intake and turning away food can occur as death approaches. Symptoms may also have treatable causes, so consider the context and care goals rather than assuming every change is inevitable.","respond":"Clarify the documented goals of care, assess comfort, and share changes with the clinical team. Under orders and local protocols, use positioning, airflow, and mouth care as appropriate; explain expected changes to loved ones and invite questions. Follow prescribed symptom-relief plans and report inadequate relief or new distress. Avoid forcing food or fluids, and do not use suction routinely for pooled secretions because it may be uncomfortable and ineffective.","reassess":"After an action, check the patient’s breathing effort, reported or observed distress, position, and response to airflow or mouth care. Ask whether family concerns have been addressed. Escalate to the team if discomfort persists, worsens, or changes, or if a new symptom suggests a potentially treatable cause consistent with the goals of care.","trap":"A disturbing sound is not a reliable measure of the patient’s distress; it is more like a noisy signal than a direct readout of comfort. Likewise, reduced eating near death does not mean that forcing food or fluids will restore comfort, and it can create aspiration risk.","caseStory":"An adult with advanced cancer is less responsive and has noisy breathing while resting. The patient shows no visible breathing struggle, but a family member is alarmed and asks for suction. The nurse must decide what to assess and what comfort-focused action to take.","caseReason":"The sound may reflect pooled secretions near the end of life, while the patient’s lack of visible distress is an important separate cue. The nurse should assess comfort, consider repositioning under the care plan, and explain the sound to the family. Routine suction may cause discomfort and often does not reach the pooled secretions.","teachback":"When a symptom or bodily change alarms a family member, how will you distinguish the patient’s comfort from the observer’s distress and choose a response consistent with the goals of care?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with advanced cancer is near the end of life and has noisy breathing while resting. The patient shows no visible breathing struggle, but a family member is frightened by the sound. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these cues?","options":["Pooled secretions may be causing a death rattle; assess comfort, reposition as appropriate, and explain the sound to the family.","The sound establishes that the patient is experiencing severe breathlessness; prioritize suction to clear the airway.","The sound proves that the patient is actively dying; stop assessing symptoms and focus only on family support."],"feedback":["Noisy breathing can result from pooled secretions and may distress family more than the patient. Assessment, repositioning, and explanation are supported comfort measures.","The sound alone does not establish patient distress, and routine suctioning may be uncomfortable and ineffective for pooled secretions.","The sound can occur near death, but it does not remove the need to assess comfort or continue symptom care."]},{"stem":"A patient with advanced illness and comfort-focused goals reports frightening breathlessness. The patient is upright but still distressed, and no new cause has been identified. What is the nurse’s best priority?","options":["Assess the patient’s distress, try airflow such as a fan if appropriate, and promptly communicate the ongoing symptom to the team for the prescribed comfort plan.","Obtain oxygen saturation and arrange imaging before offering comfort measures, even though the goals focus on comfort.","Ask the patient to lie flat and reduce airflow to avoid increasing awareness of breathing."],"feedback":["Breathlessness warrants attention to the patient’s distress. Airflow and team communication about the prescribed symptom plan are consistent with comfort-focused care.","When death is expected and goals focus on comfort, routine testing and imaging may be burdensome and are not indicated solely to evaluate the symptom.","Positioning should support comfort; lying flat and reducing airflow do not reflect the suggested comfort measures for breathlessness."]},{"stem":"In a different setting, an adult with advanced illness is increasingly drowsy and turns away from offered food and fluids. A relative fears the patient is starving and asks the nurse to keep offering spoonfuls. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Do not force intake; explain that reduced desire to eat or drink can occur near death, and offer mouth care such as moistening the mouth if appropriate.","Continue offering food and fluids until the patient accepts them, because maintaining intake is necessary for comfort.","Request artificial nutrition and fluids as the immediate response, because stopping oral intake causes immediate death."],"feedback":["Reduced intake can occur near death, and forcing food or fluids may lead to aspiration. Mouth care can address dryness and support comfort.","The patient’s turning away is a cue not to force intake; repeated offers may create distress and aspiration risk.","The supplied material does not support the claim that stopping medically supplied fluids causes immediate death; artificial nutrition and fluids may add discomfort near death."]},{"stem":"After a nurse repositions a patient with noisy breathing, the sound continues. The patient remains without visible distress, but a visitor says the patient must be suffering and requests suction. What finding should most guide the nurse’s next step?","options":["The patient’s breathing effort and observable comfort, while continuing appropriate positioning and explaining that the sound alone does not establish distress.","The persistence of the sound, because it indicates that repositioning failed and suction is required.","The visitor’s level of alarm, because family distress confirms the patient is uncomfortable."],"feedback":["The patient’s comfort and breathing effort are more informative than the sound alone. Reassess and communicate with the visitor; routine suction may be uncomfortable and ineffective.","The sound may persist despite repositioning and does not by itself establish patient distress or a need for suction.","Family concern deserves attention and explanation, but it does not independently establish that the patient is suffering."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Last Days of Life (PDQ®)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK65868.14/?report=printable"},{"title":"Symptom Relief for the Dying Patient - Special Subjects","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/the-dying-patient/symptom-relief-for-the-dying-patient"}],"frames":[{"label":"Declining body systems","detail":"Advanced illness can reduce alertness, swallowing, intake, and the ability to clear secretions."},{"label":"Sound versus distress","detail":"Noisy breathing may reflect pooled secretions; assess the patient’s breathing effort and distress rather than sound alone."},{"label":"Comfort goals guide care","detail":"Under documented comfort goals and local protocols, reposition, provide mouth care, explain secretions, and avoid routine suction."},{"label":"Comfort response checked","detail":"Recheck breathing effort and observed distress; tell the team if discomfort persists, worsens, or a new symptom appears."}]},{"id":"s089","unit":"psych","title":"Delirium or sedation?","objective":"Distinguish possible delirium from medication-related reduced arousal in critical care and communicate a focused nursing response.","concept":"Delirium is an acute change in attention and awareness with additional cognitive changes, often fluctuating over hours and becoming more apparent at night. Critical illness, medications, infection, dehydration, hypoxia, and metabolic problems can contribute, sometimes together. Sedation or coma can reduce arousal enough to prevent meaningful delirium assessment. A quiet or drowsy presentation does not by itself distinguish sedation from hypoactive delirium, so nurses use the clinical context and repeated assessment.","recognize":"Look for a recent change from baseline, especially difficulty registering or sustaining attention, disorganized thinking, altered awareness, or cognition that varies over time. Agitation and hallucinations may occur, but withdrawal, slowed responses, and lethargy can also signal delirium. Review the timing of sedatives and other medication changes, and compare with family or caregiver reports; orientation alone may not reveal impaired attention.","respond":"Prioritize immediate observation of vital signs, oxygenation, arousal, and neurologic status, then promptly communicate the acute change and relevant medication timing to the responsible team. Follow local assessment and escalation protocols, and support orientation, sleep, mobility, hydration, and sensory aids as appropriate to the care plan. Avoid assuming that quietness means comfort or that antipsychotic medication treats the underlying cause.","reassess":"After supportive measures or a team-directed change, reassess arousal, attention, thinking, and fluctuation, and document how findings compare with baseline. Recheck vital signs and oxygenation as indicated by the change. Escalate promptly for worsening responsiveness, new focal findings, physiologic instability, or persistent acute confusion needing evaluation.","trap":"Do not treat delirium and sedation as interchangeable labels: sedation describes reduced arousal, while delirium centers on an acute disturbance in attention and cognition. Think of arousal as the room's lighting and attention as the patient's ability to follow what is happening; dim lighting can limit assessment, but it does not explain every change in attention.","caseStory":"An intubated adult in the ICU is less drowsy after a team-directed sedation adjustment. Over the next shift, the patient alternates between calm periods and restlessness, repeatedly loses track of simple instructions, and gives disorganized responses. The nurse must decide whether to document this only as expected sedation or report a possible acute cognitive change.","caseReason":"The fluctuating attention and disorganized thinking after a change in arousal are concerning for delirium, not simply proof of sedation. The nurse should assess vital signs, oxygenation, arousal, and neurologic status, compare with baseline, and promptly communicate the change and medication timing so the team can evaluate contributing causes.","teachback":"When a patient becomes quieter after a medication change, what observations would help you distinguish reduced arousal from a new fluctuating disturbance in attention or cognition, and what would you communicate to the team?","questions":[{"stem":"An intubated ICU patient has become more alert after a team-directed reduction in sedation. The patient now alternates between calm and restless periods, cannot sustain attention to instructions, and gives disorganized answers. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The acute fluctuation and inattention raise concern for delirium and warrant evaluation for contributing causes.","The restlessness is sufficient evidence of inadequate sedation, so the nurse should focus only on comfort.","The disorganized answers most likely reflect baseline memory loss unless the patient is disoriented to person."],"feedback":["Acute, fluctuating inattention with cognitive change is characteristic of delirium; the nurse should communicate the change and support evaluation of possible causes.","Restlessness can occur in hyperactive delirium, and it does not by itself establish that sedation is inadequate.","Baseline comparison is important, but delirium can be present even without disorientation to person; attention and acute change are key cues."]},{"stem":"A critically ill patient develops new confusion during a shift. The patient is harder to engage, and oxygen saturation has fallen from the prior observation; the cause is not yet known. What is the nurse's priority response?","options":["Assess current vital signs, oxygenation, arousal, and neurologic status, then promptly report the acute change to the responsible team.","Begin repeated reorientation and wait to report the change unless the patient becomes physically agitated.","Assume the confusion is a medication effect and request a medication change before gathering further findings."],"feedback":["New confusion with a change in oxygenation needs prompt physiologic and neurologic assessment and team communication; hypoxia is among possible causes.","Reorientation may support care, but waiting can delay assessment of a potentially serious cause such as hypoxia or infection.","Medication effects are possible, but the nurse should not assume the cause or seek a change before assessing and communicating relevant findings."]},{"stem":"After surgery, an older adult who was previously conversational becomes quiet and withdrawn. A caregiver reports this is new, and the patient repeatedly loses track of questions; the change is more evident in the evening. Which nursing interpretation and action are most appropriate?","options":["Consider hypoactive delirium, compare findings with baseline, review recent medication changes, and communicate the acute change to the team.","Consider normal evening behavior in an older adult and defer further assessment until the patient is more alert.","Consider a primary mood disorder and focus the assessment on affect rather than attention and recent change."],"feedback":["Hypoactive delirium can appear as withdrawal and lethargy, and a new change with inattention warrants assessment and communication, including review of medication changes.","New evening deterioration should not be presumed to be routine, and quiet behavior can obscure delirium rather than rule it out.","A mood disorder is a possible alternative, but acute change and impaired attention favor evaluating for delirium and medical or medication-related causes."]},{"stem":"A patient in critical care is awake and correctly states their name and location, but repeatedly fails to register a simple instruction and gives answers that vary over the next hour. A colleague says delirium is unlikely because the patient is oriented. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Explain that orientation does not exclude delirium, assess attention and fluctuation over time, and report the acute cognitive change.","Accept orientation as evidence against delirium and continue routine observation without further assessment.","Record the findings as a memory problem because the patient can remain awake and answer some questions."],"feedback":["A patient may be oriented and still have impaired attention; the acute, fluctuating pattern supports further delirium assessment and communication.","Orientation alone is not enough to rule out delirium, which primarily affects attention and may fluctuate.","Assessing attention helps distinguish failure to register information from rapid forgetting; the observed fluctuation and acute change should not be reduced to a memory label."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Delirium - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium"},{"title":"ICU Delirium - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559280/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Arousal affects assessment","detail":"Sedation and critical illness can lower arousal, making meaningful delirium assessment difficult."},{"label":"Fluctuating attention noticed","detail":"After sedation adjustment, fluctuating attention and disorganized responses differ from drowsiness alone and raise concern for delirium."},{"label":"Acute change communicated","detail":"Check vital signs, oxygenation, arousal, and neurologic status; promptly report the acute change and medication timing under local protocols."},{"label":"Cognition compared with baseline","detail":"Reassess attention and arousal against baseline; escalate worsening responsiveness, focal findings, physiologic instability, or persistent confusion."}]},{"id":"s090","unit":"psych","title":"Agitation and de-escalation","objective":"By the end of this unit, the nurse will be able to recognize potential medical causes of acute agitation, use initial de-escalation measures, and communicate findings that guide timely evaluation.","concept":"Agitation is a nonspecific state of heightened arousal and increased verbal or motor activity, not a diagnosis. It can arise from medical illness, delirium, substance intoxication or withdrawal, or psychiatric conditions, and more than one cause may coexist. Altered attention and cognition can signal delirium from an underlying medical problem. Treating agitation only as a behavioral issue can delay recognition of reversible illness and increase risk to the patient and staff.","recognize":"Look for restlessness, hostility, confusion, disorientation, communication difficulty, or changes in vital signs. Acute onset with fluctuating attention or awareness suggests delirium; trauma, abnormal breathing or oxygenation, fever, intoxication or withdrawal signs, and metabolic concerns also raise suspicion of a medical cause. Gradual cognitive decline differs from delirium, while a new or atypical presentation in someone with psychiatric history should not be assumed to be psychiatric.","respond":"First attend to immediate safety: keep an exit accessible, maintain a safe distance, and use a calm, empathic approach. When feasible, reduce noise and stimulation and try verbal de-escalation before medication or restraint. Obtain available vital signs, oxygenation, and point-of-care glucose, and promptly communicate concerning findings to the responsible clinician. Follow local protocols and orders; do not presume a psychiatric cause or delay urgent evaluation when medical warning signs are present.","reassess":"After a calming or environmental change, reassess behavior, attention, orientation, vital signs, breathing, oxygenation, and glucose when available. Note whether attention and cognition remain altered even if the person is quieter. Escalate promptly for abnormal vital signs, suspected hypoxia or glucose disturbance, trauma, worsening confusion, or continuing safety concerns.","trap":"A quieter patient is not necessarily a medically stable patient, just as a quiet alarm does not prove the underlying fault is fixed. Agitation is a sign to investigate, so calming the behavior should make assessment easier rather than replace it.","caseStory":"An older adult in a medical unit becomes restless and suspicious over several hours, with attention that varies during conversation. A family member says this confusion is new, and the nurse notes fever and an abnormal oxygen saturation. The patient becomes less restless in a quieter space, creating a decision point about whether to continue medical evaluation.","caseReason":"The acute change, fluctuating attention, fever, and abnormal oxygenation make a medical cause, including delirium, a priority to evaluate. Reduced restlessness after environmental modification supports continuing assessment but does not resolve the concerning findings. The nurse should promptly report them and follow the clinical team's evaluation plan.","teachback":"If a patient becomes calmer after de-escalation but remains newly inattentive or disoriented, what findings would you reassess and communicate, and why does calm behavior alone not settle the cause?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized older adult becomes restless and suspicious over several hours. During conversation, attention fluctuates and the patient cannot consistently follow questions. A relative reports that this is a sudden change from the patient's usual gradual memory problems. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's next communication?","options":["The acute, fluctuating change raises concern for delirium from a medical cause and warrants prompt clinical evaluation.","The restlessness is most consistent with progression of the chronic cognitive disorder, so routine observation is sufficient.","The suspiciousness indicates a primary psychiatric episode, so medical assessment can wait until behavior settles."],"feedback":["Acute onset and fluctuating attention distinguish possible delirium from the gradual course of dementia and point toward an underlying medical cause.","Gradual cognitive decline can occur with dementia, but a sudden fluctuating change is not adequately explained by that history.","Suspiciousness can occur with different causes of agitation; new confusion and impaired attention make it unsafe to assume a primary psychiatric cause."]},{"stem":"A patient in an emergency care area is shouting and moving toward staff. The nurse observes difficult, rapid breathing and an abnormal oxygen saturation. De-escalation has not yet been attempted. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Address immediate safety, promptly alert the responsible clinician to the breathing and oxygenation findings, and follow the urgent evaluation plan.","Move the patient to a quieter space and continue verbal de-escalation before reporting the oxygenation finding.","Seek a detailed substance-use history first because intoxication commonly contributes to agitation."],"feedback":["Abnormal breathing and oxygenation may indicate a serious medical cause, so safety and timely clinical evaluation take priority over delaying escalation.","A quieter environment and de-escalation can help when feasible, but they should not postpone communication of a potentially urgent medical finding.","Substance use is one possible cause, but a detailed history should not take priority over an abnormal oxygenation finding and immediate safety."]},{"stem":"At a community mental health clinic without an onsite medical team, an adult shows mild to moderate restlessness but has no apparent medical warning signs. The person becomes calmer and cooperative after the nurse reduces noise and uses a calm, nonconfrontational approach. What is the best next step?","options":["Continue assessment in the clinic if feasible, while arranging transfer if de-escalation fails or concerning findings emerge.","Arrange immediate transfer to emergency care because any agitation in a nonmedical setting requires transfer.","End the encounter because successful calming establishes that no further assessment is needed."],"feedback":["For mild to moderate agitation without medical warning signs, successful de-escalation and environmental modification can allow further clinic evaluation; lack of response or concerning findings calls for transfer.","The setting alone does not require immediate transfer when agitation is mild to moderate, no warning signs are present, and de-escalation is successful.","Calming can support assessment but does not establish a cause or eliminate the need to evaluate the patient."]},{"stem":"A patient with a history of schizophrenia becomes less physically restless after the nurse dims the lights and speaks calmly. The patient remains inattentive and gives disoriented answers, unlike during prior episodes. Which reassessment finding should most strongly prompt the nurse to communicate concern for a medical cause?","options":["Persistent new inattention and disorientation despite reduced agitation, especially because the presentation differs from the patient's usual pattern.","The patient's reduced motor activity, because improvement in restlessness makes further medical assessment unnecessary.","The established psychiatric diagnosis, because it best explains the current behavior even when the presentation is atypical."],"feedback":["Persistent altered attention and orientation, together with an atypical presentation, are concerning for delirium or another medical cause and should be communicated.","Reduced restlessness is not proof that the underlying cause has resolved; reassessment should include cognition and attention.","A prior psychiatric diagnosis does not rule out medical illness, particularly when the current presentation differs from the patient's usual pattern."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Treatment / Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK493153/"},{"title":"Medical Evaluation and Triage of the Agitated Patient - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3298208/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Agitation has many causes","detail":"Agitation is nonspecific and may reflect delirium, medical illness, intoxication, withdrawal, or psychiatric conditions."},{"label":"Medical warning cues","detail":"New fluctuating attention with fever and abnormal oxygenation points toward a medical cause needing evaluation."},{"label":"Safe de-escalation and reporting","detail":"Keep an exit accessible, use calm de-escalation, obtain available vital signs, oxygenation, and glucose, then promptly communicate concerns."},{"label":"Behavior and physiology rechecked","detail":"Recheck attention, behavior, and physiology; escalate abnormal findings, worsening confusion, suspected hypoxia or glucose disturbance, or safety concerns."}]},{"id":"s091","unit":"psych","title":"Substance withdrawal","objective":"Recognize evolving alcohol withdrawal, distinguish it from other causes of altered mental status, and prioritize timely nursing escalation and reassessment.","concept":"Alcohol depresses central nervous system activity. With prolonged heavy use, physical dependence can develop, and sudden cessation may produce a continuum of nervous system and autonomic overactivity. Symptoms can progress from tremor, sweating, and nausea to seizures, hallucinations, and delirium tremens, which can be fatal. Recognizing this progression matters because prompt team-based treatment and monitoring can reduce harm while other causes of illness are considered.","recognize":"Early withdrawal may involve tremor, headache, sweating, weakness, nausea, and increased reflexes. Hallucinations with clear consciousness and without the marked autonomic changes of delirium tremens suggest a different withdrawal presentation than escalating confusion, disorientation, sweating, a rapid pulse, or fever. Do not assume altered mental status is withdrawal alone: injury, infection, low blood sugar, or other substances may also be involved.","respond":"Prioritize immediate assessment of breathing, circulation, mental status, and injury risk, then promptly communicate suspected withdrawal and changes to the responsible clinician or rapid response team under local protocol. Maintain a safe environment and give ordered treatment, including prescribed withdrawal medication and thiamine when directed. Do not independently prescribe, assume alcohol explains every symptom, or use home remedies to reverse intoxication.","reassess":"After nursing actions or ordered treatment, reassess mental status, orientation, vital signs, tremor, seizure activity, breathing, and ability to remain safe. Escalate promptly for worsening confusion, fever, marked autonomic changes, a seizure, reduced responsiveness, or suppressed breathing; these findings may signal severe withdrawal or another emergency.","trap":"A person who is hallucinating is not automatically in delirium tremens: hallucinosis can occur while consciousness remains clear, whereas delirium tremens includes confusion and autonomic instability. Think of the distinction as a clear window versus a fogged one, while remembering that either presentation needs clinical attention.","caseStory":"A fictional adult admitted for another condition has a history of heavy daily drinking and has had no alcohol during the hospital stay. The patient develops sweating, hand tremor, and nausea, then becomes disoriented and reports frightening visual experiences. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as a routine change or urgently escalate concern for severe withdrawal while considering other causes.","caseReason":"The progression from early withdrawal symptoms to disorientation, hallucinations, and autonomic changes raises concern for delirium tremens, a potentially fatal form of withdrawal. The nurse should promptly notify the care team, support safety, and facilitate assessment for both withdrawal and alternative or concurrent causes of altered mental status.","teachback":"When a patient who has stopped heavy alcohol use develops new symptoms, what changes would make you suspect progression to severe withdrawal, and what would you communicate and reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized adult with a history of prolonged heavy drinking reports accusatory voices after stopping alcohol. The patient remains oriented, follows conversation, and has no marked sweating or rapid pulse. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Alcohol-related hallucinosis is possible because hallucinations can occur while consciousness remains clear.","Delirium tremens is established because any hallucination after alcohol cessation indicates delirium.","Withdrawal is unlikely because the patient remains oriented and can participate in conversation."],"feedback":["Hallucinosis may involve frightening auditory or visual experiences while consciousness remains clear and marked autonomic instability is absent.","Hallucinations alone do not establish delirium tremens; confusion, disorientation, and autonomic changes are important distinguishing cues.","Withdrawal can include hallucinosis without impaired consciousness, so orientation does not rule it out."]},{"stem":"A patient with heavy long-term alcohol use becomes increasingly confused and restless after stopping alcohol. The patient is sweating heavily, has a rising pulse and temperature, and is trying to climb out of bed. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Promptly escalate to the responsible clinician or rapid response team, protect the patient from injury, and support urgent assessment and ordered treatment.","Reduce stimulation and wait to see whether the confusion resolves before notifying the clinical team.","Focus on obtaining a detailed drinking history before addressing the patient's immediate safety and changing condition."],"feedback":["Progressive confusion with autonomic changes and unsafe behavior raises concern for severe withdrawal, which can be fatal and warrants prompt team-based care.","Reducing stimulation may help support safety, but waiting delays escalation when severe withdrawal is possible.","A drinking history can inform assessment, but immediate safety and urgent escalation take priority in a rapidly worsening condition."]},{"stem":"A patient recovering from surgery has been unable to drink during the hospital stay. The patient develops tremor and sweating, then becomes disoriented and sees objects moving in a dim room. Which nursing interpretation best supports the next action?","options":["Consider severe alcohol withdrawal despite the surgical admission and promptly communicate the change while supporting assessment for other causes.","Attribute the symptoms to the postoperative setting and continue routine observation unless the patient reports pain.","Treat the visual experiences as proof of a primary psychiatric disorder and defer assessment of withdrawal."],"feedback":["Withdrawal can emerge when a person who drinks continually cannot obtain alcohol, including during hospitalization; disorientation and autonomic changes make urgent assessment important.","A postoperative context does not explain away progressive confusion, tremor, and sweating, which require prompt clinical attention.","Visual experiences can occur during withdrawal, and the presentation does not establish a primary psychiatric disorder; other medical causes also need consideration."]},{"stem":"After ordered treatment for suspected alcohol withdrawal, a patient has less visible tremor but becomes difficult to arouse and is breathing inadequately. Which reassessment and response is most appropriate?","options":["Treat reduced responsiveness and inadequate breathing as urgent deterioration and escalate immediately rather than assuming withdrawal has resolved.","Interpret the quieter tremor as adequate response and continue routine observation until the next scheduled assessment.","Focus on orientation questions first because mental status changes are expected during withdrawal."],"feedback":["Inadequate breathing and reduced responsiveness require urgent escalation and airway-focused assessment; improvement in one withdrawal sign does not establish overall safety.","A quieter tremor can be misleading when alertness and breathing worsen, so routine observation alone is not appropriate.","Orientation assessment is useful when the patient can participate, but it must not delay response to compromised breathing and reduced responsiveness."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Alcohol Use Disorder and Alcohol Withdrawal - Mental Health - Merck Manual Consumer Version","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/mental-health-disorders/alcohol-use-disorder/alcohol-use-disorder-and-alcohol-withdrawal"},{"title":"Alcohol Toxicity and Withdrawal - The Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/illicit-drugs-and-intoxicants/alcohol-toxicity-and-withdrawal?media=full"}],"frames":[{"label":"Dependence after abstinence","detail":"Prolonged heavy alcohol use can cause physical dependence; sudden hospital abstinence may trigger progressively increasing nervous-system and autonomic activity."},{"label":"Escalating withdrawal signs","detail":"Tremor, sweating, and nausea followed by disorientation, frightening visual experiences, and autonomic changes raise concern for severe withdrawal."},{"label":"Urgent team escalation","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician or response team, maintain safety, and facilitate assessment for withdrawal and other causes."},{"label":"Worsening red flags","detail":"Reassess orientation, vital signs, breathing, tremor, and seizures; escalate worsening confusion, fever, seizure, reduced responsiveness, or suppressed breathing."}]},{"id":"s092","unit":"psych","title":"Suicide risk and immediate safety","objective":"Use a structured, individualized assessment to recognize changing suicide risk, support immediate safety, and communicate findings for timely clinical decisions.","concept":"Suicide risk reflects interacting historical vulnerabilities, current changeable factors, protective supports, and the circumstances a person may face next. Prior attempts, psychiatric illness, or chronic illness can establish baseline vulnerability, while factors such as agitation, severe insomnia, substance use, recent loss, access to lethal means, or preparatory behavior can increase near-term concern. Screening identifies a need for further assessment; it does not predict an individual outcome or replace clinical judgment.","recognize":"Ask sensitively about current and past suicidal thoughts, plans, intent, access to means, preparations, and prior attempts, including the most severe past episode. A denial of current thoughts does not settle risk: passive thoughts, guarded responses, recent stress, intoxication or withdrawal, agitation, and collateral information may change the picture. Distinguish a screening result from a comprehensive assessment, and consider protective supports without treating them as a guarantee.","respond":"Prioritize immediate safety when current thoughts, plan, intent, or preparatory behavior raise acute concern. Stay with the patient as required by local policy, reduce access to dangerous objects within nursing role and protocol, and promptly alert the responsible clinician or response team. Share relevant patient statements, observed changes, records, and collateral information. Do not rely on a screening score or a promise to remain safe as the management plan.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess current thoughts, plan, intent, access, preparatory behavior, agitation, substance-related changes, and the patient's ability to engage. Confirm whether the responsible clinician has received the update and whether the safety plan remains workable. New intent, preparation, worsening agitation, or unresolved uncertainty warrants prompt escalation under local protocol.","trap":"A negative screen is like a brief weather reading, not a forecast of every approaching storm. A denial of current thoughts or the presence of family support cannot replace assessment of recent changes, past severity, access to means, and information from other sources.","caseStory":"An adult admitted to a medical unit after a recent relationship breakdown says, “I am not suicidal now,” but reports a past suicide attempt and has slept very little for several nights. A family member tells the nurse that the patient recently gave away valued belongings. The nurse must decide whether the denial is enough to defer further assessment.","caseReason":"The denial is relevant but does not resolve concern because prior attempt, acute stress, severe insomnia, and reported preparatory behavior may indicate changing risk. The nurse should promptly communicate these findings and support a comprehensive assessment and safety measures through the responsible clinician and local protocol.","teachback":"When a patient denies current suicidal thoughts, what other current, historical, and collateral findings would you gather before deciding whether further assessment and escalation are needed?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult medical patient denies current suicidal thoughts but describes a prior attempt and a recent bereavement. The nurse notices increasing withdrawal, and a relative reports the patient has been giving away possessions. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next step?","options":["The denial does not resolve concern; communicate the combined findings and support a comprehensive risk assessment.","The denial makes acute concern unlikely; document the screen and revisit the issue during routine rounds.","The relative’s report is sufficient to determine risk without asking the patient about current thoughts or circumstances."],"feedback":["Correct. Current denial does not rule out risk, and prior behavior, recent stress, observed change, and collateral information should be synthesized for further assessment.","This overweights one answer and treats screening as a complete evaluation, despite several relevant historical and changing factors.","Collateral information is valuable, but it complements rather than replaces a sensitive patient interview and clinical assessment."]},{"stem":"A patient tells the nurse they intend to end their life, describes a current plan, and says they have begun taking steps toward it. What is the priority nursing response?","options":["Maintain immediate safety within nursing role and local protocol, promptly alert the responsible clinician, and communicate the patient’s statements and actions.","Ask the patient to agree not to act, then arrange routine follow-up if they appear calmer.","Complete a screening instrument first and wait for its result before notifying the responsible clinician."],"feedback":["Correct. Current intent, a plan, and preparatory behavior signal acute concern; immediate safety actions and prompt team communication take priority.","A promise to remain safe is not an evidence-based substitute for assessment, safety measures, and an appropriate disposition decision.","A screening tool does not replace clinical assessment or justify delaying escalation when acute warning signs are already present."]},{"stem":"An older adult on a medical ward has a new diagnosis of serious illness and reports severe insomnia and feeling trapped. A brief screening tool is negative, but the patient’s partner describes a marked change in mood. Which nursing approach best transfers the assessment principles to this situation?","options":["Share the dynamic concerns and collateral report with the responsible clinician and support further individualized assessment despite the negative screen.","Accept the negative screen as the best indicator and focus only on routine medical care unless the patient later volunteers suicidal thoughts.","Treat the partner’s report as proof of imminent risk and make a disposition decision without further clinical assessment."],"feedback":["Correct. A new serious illness, insomnia, feeling trapped, and collateral-observed change merit clinical attention; a screening result alone cannot settle risk.","This treats screening as comprehensive assessment and overlooks dynamic factors and the possibility that suicidal thoughts may not be disclosed.","Collateral information informs assessment but does not by itself establish imminent risk or replace the responsible clinician’s evaluation."]},{"stem":"After the nurse alerts the clinical team and helps reduce immediate environmental hazards under local protocol, a patient who was visibly agitated appears calmer and says, “I am fine.” Which reassessment is most appropriate before the team decides next steps?","options":["Reassess current thoughts, plan, intent, access, preparatory behavior, and relevant changes, then communicate unresolved or worsening concerns to the team.","Treat the calmer appearance and reassurance as evidence that the earlier concern has resolved, and end further safety checks.","Ask only whether the patient still feels agitated, because the original statements remain the sole basis for risk decisions."],"feedback":["Correct. Appearance and reassurance do not replace reassessment of specific risk factors and response to interventions; unresolved or worsening concern warrants escalation.","A calmer presentation is not enough to conclude that risk has resolved, particularly without reassessing the factors that prompted intervention.","The initial statements remain important, but reassessment should also examine current thoughts, intent, access, behavior, and clinical changes."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Suicide: Assessment and Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK617057/?report=printable"},{"title":"Suicide Risk in Children and Adolescents: Assessment ... - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK576416/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Changing suicide risk","detail":"Suicide risk combines baseline vulnerabilities with changing stressors, including prior attempts, recent loss, insomnia, substance use, and access to lethal means."},{"label":"Denial is not decisive","detail":"A denial of current thoughts does not resolve concern when past attempts, agitation, acute stress, or reported preparations are present."},{"label":"Immediate safety steps","detail":"For acute concern, stay with the patient as local policy requires, reduce dangerous-object access within role, and promptly alert the clinician."},{"label":"Reassess acute concern","detail":"Reassess thoughts, intent, access, preparations, and agitation; new intent, preparation, or unresolved uncertainty warrants prompt escalation under local protocol."}]},{"id":"s093","unit":"psych","title":"Anxiety, depression and trauma","objective":"Recognize possible anxiety, depression, or trauma-related symptoms after critical illness and coordinate appropriate screening, support, and referral.","concept":"Post-intensive care syndrome can include new or worsened mental health difficulties after critical illness. Anxiety, depression, and post-traumatic stress symptoms may occur alongside physical or cognitive problems, and can persist after discharge. A history of anxiety or depression is a risk factor for later difficulties, but symptoms may also reveal problems that were present but unrecognized. Recognizing this connection helps nurses support assessment and recovery without assuming a diagnosis.","recognize":"Ask about mood, anxiety, distressing memories, and how symptoms affect daily function. Difficulty recalling the ICU stay can accompany anxiety or trauma symptoms and may persist well beyond discharge; it does not establish a diagnosis. Consider that symptoms may be new or pre-existing, and that physical or cognitive difficulties can also affect recovery. Use established screening approaches and communicate concerns to the care team.","respond":"Listen without minimizing the experience, document the patient’s concerns, and communicate the pattern and functional impact to the responsible clinician. Follow local processes for mental health screening and referral; anxiety or trauma symptoms may warrant psychiatric services. Support coordination with rehabilitation and available post-ICU services. Do not independently diagnose, prescribe, or treat screening results as definitive; involve the patient and team in next steps.","reassess":"After discussion, screening, or referral, reassess the patient’s reported distress, mood, recall concerns, and ability to manage daily activities. Check whether the agreed follow-up has been arranged and whether symptoms are changing. Escalate persistent or worsening concerns, substantial functional difficulty, or findings that suggest another cause for the symptoms.","trap":"A patient’s difficulty recalling the ICU stay is a clue to explore, not proof of PTSD. Think of screening as a dashboard that identifies which systems need attention, rather than a single warning light that supplies a diagnosis.","caseStory":"Several months after an ICU admission, a patient reports persistent anxiety and difficulty piecing together memories of the stay. The patient also describes low mood that is interfering with usual activities. The nurse must decide whether these concerns should be treated as unrelated complaints or raised as possible post-critical-illness difficulties.","caseReason":"Anxiety, mood symptoms, and difficulty recalling ICU events are all relevant concerns after critical illness, but they do not by themselves establish a diagnosis. The nurse should document their impact, use established screening processes, and share the findings with the responsible clinician. The team can then consider mental health referral and other recovery supports.","teachback":"When a former ICU patient reports distress or low mood, what would you assess and communicate before deciding with the team on follow-up?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient seen after an ICU stay reports persistent anxiety and says the memories of the admission are fragmented. The patient asks whether this means they have PTSD. Which nursing response best interprets these cues?","options":["Explain that these concerns can follow critical illness, assess their impact, and raise them with the clinician for appropriate screening and follow-up.","Explain that fragmented recall confirms PTSD and document the diagnosis so the patient can be referred.","Reassure the patient that anxiety is expected after ICU and defer discussion unless physical recovery also worsens."],"feedback":["Correct. Anxiety and difficulty recalling ICU events can occur after critical illness, but they do not establish PTSD; assessment and team follow-up are appropriate.","Fragmented recall can be associated with anxiety and trauma symptoms, but it is not diagnostic. Nurses should communicate concerns rather than assign a diagnosis.","This response minimizes a potentially significant post-ICU concern. Mental health difficulties can occur even when physical recovery is not worsening."]},{"stem":"A former ICU patient reports persistent low mood that is making it harder to resume everyday activities. The patient had no known mental health history, and the ICU admission was brief. What is the best nursing priority?","options":["Assess and communicate the mood symptoms and functional impact, then coordinate appropriate screening and follow-up with the care team.","Defer mental health follow-up because depression after ICU is unlikely without a prior history or prolonged admission.","Recommend a patient diary as the primary response because it is established as effective for depression after ICU."],"feedback":["Correct. Depression can occur after critical illness, and its relationship to ICU length of stay or severity is not established in the supplied material. Functional impact warrants assessment and follow-up.","A lack of prior history or a brief stay does not rule out post-ICU depression. The patient’s symptoms and their impact should be addressed.","Patient diaries are described as helpful for reducing PTSD symptoms, with less effect on depression. The reported low mood still needs assessment and coordinated follow-up."]},{"stem":"A family caregiver of an ICU survivor reports worsening mood and says they have little social support. The survivor’s illness is improving. Which nursing action best applies post-ICU mental health principles to this different context?","options":["Acknowledge the caregiver’s concerns, assess their support needs, and discuss family-based care and social support with the team.","Focus follow-up on the survivor because caregiver distress is expected to track the patient’s illness severity.","Treat the caregiver’s report as evidence that the survivor has post-traumatic stress and direct the caregiver to manage the survivor’s diary."],"feedback":["Correct. Caregivers can experience reduced quality of life and depressive symptoms; family-based care and increased social support may help.","The supplied material notes that longer-term caregiver quality of life does not appear to depend on the patient’s illness severity. The caregiver’s own needs merit attention.","Caregiver distress does not establish a diagnosis in the survivor, and the caregiver should not be assigned responsibility for the survivor’s mental health care."]},{"stem":"After a nurse discusses referral for a former ICU patient’s anxiety, the patient says the anxiety has eased but still cannot recall parts of the admission. Which reassessment approach is most appropriate?","options":["Reassess anxiety, recall concerns, and daily function separately, and communicate persistent concerns through the follow-up plan.","Conclude that the trauma concern has resolved because the patient reports less anxiety, and stop mental health follow-up.","Treat persistent memory gaps alone as confirmation of PTSD and bypass further screening or clinician assessment."],"feedback":["Correct. Mental health and cognitive concerns can coexist, so improvement in one reported symptom does not establish that all concerns have resolved. Reassess and communicate each relevant domain.","Reduced anxiety is useful information but does not resolve the patient’s continuing recall concern or remove the need for planned follow-up.","Recall difficulty can accompany anxiety or trauma symptoms but is not diagnostic by itself. Screening and clinician assessment remain appropriate."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Care of ICU survivors in the community: a guide for GPs","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5604827/"},{"title":"Post–intensive Care Syndrome - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10772956/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Post-ICU difficulties","detail":"After critical illness, anxiety, depression, trauma symptoms, and physical or cognitive difficulties may emerge or worsen and persist after discharge."},{"label":"Distress affects function","detail":"Persistent anxiety, low mood, or difficulty recalling ICU events that disrupts daily function warrants attention but does not establish diagnosis."},{"label":"Share recovery concerns","detail":"Listen without minimizing, document distress and functional impact, and communicate concerns for established screening, referral, and recovery support."},{"label":"Check ongoing impact","detail":"Reassess distress, recall concerns, and daily functioning; worsening symptoms, substantial impairment, or signs of another cause need team follow-up."}]},{"id":"s094","unit":"psych","title":"When a patient declines care","objective":"Use a structured, noncoercive nursing response to an adult’s refusal by supporting informed discussion, escalating uncertainty, and reassessing understanding and safety.","concept":"Consent is a prerequisite to intervention, but a patient may decline treatment. For a meaningful choice, discussion should cover the current condition and likely course without treatment, helpful options and their harms, benefits, and burdens, the clinician’s view of the best alternative, and uncertainties. Legal and clinical capacity are needed for informed consent; refusal alone does not show impaired capacity. Distinguishing informed choice from unresolved confusion guides discussion and team follow-up without overriding autonomy.","recognize":"Listen for whether the patient understands the condition, likely course without treatment, available options, and relevant uncertainties. Identify unanswered questions, inconsistent understanding, or communication barriers that may hinder the discussion. Distinguish disagreement or refusal from evidence that capacity is impaired; a puzzling refusal calls for further discussion, not an automatic conclusion about capacity.","respond":"Acknowledge the refusal without pressure, then promptly inform the treating clinician and support a discussion of the patient’s condition, options, benefits, harms, burdens, and uncertainties. In the United States, take reasonable steps to communicate with patients who have language or other communication barriers. Follow local processes for documenting the discussion; do not equate refusal with incapacity or proceed as though consent was given.","reassess":"After the discussion, check what the patient understands about the condition, likely course without treatment, options, and uncertainties, and whether questions or communication barriers remain. Report unresolved confusion or concerns that capacity may be questionable to the treating clinician. If refusal may harm a minor child or another dependent, seek ethics and legal consultation through the appropriate team.","trap":"A refusal is not a capacity test: it is more like a decision point that should lead to a conversation, not an automatic label. Do not mistake disagreement with the clinician for proof of impaired capacity, or treat a signed form as a substitute for informed discussion.","caseStory":"A hospitalized adult with a serious respiratory illness declines a proposed intervention. The patient explains the clinician’s account of the condition and likely course without treatment, but says the burdens of the intervention do not fit their priorities. The nurse must decide whether to challenge the refusal or support a further discussion with the care team.","caseReason":"The refusal itself does not establish impaired capacity, and the patient’s stated priorities warrant respectful discussion rather than pressure. The nurse should inform the treating clinician, help ensure the patient’s questions and understanding are addressed, and follow local documentation processes. If capacity seems questionable, raise that concern rather than treating refusal alone as evidence.","teachback":"A patient continues to decline after discussion. What would you check and communicate before concluding that the refusal reflects impaired capacity?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in a critical-care unit declines a proposed intervention and accurately describes the clinician’s explanation of the condition, likely course without treatment, and available options. The patient says the burdens outweigh the potential benefits for them. What is the best nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Respect the stated choice, inform the treating clinician, and support any remaining questions or discussion.","Treat the refusal as evidence of impaired capacity and request a capacity evaluation solely because the patient declined.","Proceed with the intervention because the patient’s presence in the unit implies consent to treatment."],"feedback":["This response respects the patient’s right to refuse while keeping the treating clinician involved and supporting informed discussion.","Refusal alone is not evidence of diminished capacity; further discussion is appropriate if the refusal is puzzling or capacity seems questionable.","Being in the unit does not imply consent to a more invasive or risky intervention; express informed consent is required for such interventions."]},{"stem":"A patient in a US hospital declines a recommended treatment but speaks little English. The patient appears uncertain about what may happen without treatment. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Support reasonable communication steps so the qualified clinician can explain the condition, options, and uncertainties in a way the patient can understand.","Ask a family member to decide which treatment is best and present that choice to the patient as the recommendation.","Record the refusal immediately and avoid further discussion so the patient is not pressured."],"feedback":["Reasonable communication steps are needed when language barriers may prevent adequate understanding; the discussion should cover the patient’s condition, options, and uncertainties.","The patient is the decision-maker when capable; the clinician should explain options and answer questions rather than shifting the decision to family.","A refusal should not end a needed discussion when the patient appears uncertain, and documentation does not replace informed communication."]},{"stem":"A capable adult receiving hospital care declines treatment and tells the nurse that a dependent minor child may be left without necessary care if the illness worsens. What is the most appropriate nursing action?","options":["Bring the potential effect on the dependent to the care team and seek ethics and legal consultation through appropriate channels.","Treat the possible effect on the child as proof that the patient lacks capacity and disregard the refusal.","Keep the concern private because a patient’s refusal can never warrant consultation with others."],"feedback":["When refusal may harm a minor child or another dependent, ethics and legal consultation should be sought; involving the appropriate team supports that response.","The possible effect on a dependent warrants consultation, but it does not by itself establish impaired capacity or authorize disregarding the refusal.","The guidance specifically supports ethics and legal consultation when refusal may harm a dependent."]},{"stem":"After discussing a proposed treatment, a patient refuses and then gives conflicting descriptions of the likely course without treatment. The patient also says they did not understand part of the explanation. What should the nurse do next?","options":["Arrange further discussion with the treating clinician, address the communication gap, and report why capacity may need assessment.","Accept the refusal without follow-up because questioning it would always violate the patient’s rights.","Request a capacity assessment only because the patient refused, without first clarifying the misunderstanding."],"feedback":["Conflicting understanding and an unexplained communication gap call for further discussion; if capacity seems questionable, report that concern to the treating clinician.","A puzzling refusal should prompt further discussion, particularly when the patient says the explanation was not understood.","Capacity should not be assessed or sought solely because a patient refuses; first address the stated misunderstanding and report any remaining concern."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Informed Consent - Special Subjects - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/medicolegal-issues/informed-consent?media=full"},{"title":"Consentimiento informado - Temas especiales - Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/es-us/professional/temas-especiales/cuestiones-medicolegales/consentimiento-informado"}],"frames":[{"label":"Informed refusal context","detail":"A patient may refuse an intervention after considering the condition, likely course without treatment, options, burdens, benefits, and uncertainties."},{"label":"Understanding may vary","detail":"A reasoned refusal is not itself incapacity; unanswered questions, inconsistent understanding, or communication barriers may leave the decision unresolved."},{"label":"Support patient discussion","detail":"Acknowledge refusal without pressure, inform the treating clinician, and support discussion of options and communication needs under local processes."},{"label":"Check decision understanding","detail":"Check understanding of the condition, untreated course, options, and uncertainty; report persistent confusion or capacity concerns to the treating clinician."}]},{"id":"s095","unit":"ethics","title":"Advocacy and moral distress","objective":"Identify moral distress in critical care and use team-based advocacy to raise ethical concerns while staying within nursing authority and local processes.","concept":"Moral distress occurs when a clinician recognizes an ethically appropriate course but cannot carry it out because of constraints such as orders, communication failures, or limited resources. Repeated unresolved episodes can leave moral residue and contribute to chronic workplace stress and burnout. In intensive and palliative care, concerns about nonbeneficial treatment and conflict around goals of care are signals to examine team and system conditions, not merely individual coping.","recognize":"Look for a specific ethical conflict: the nurse can name a patient-centered obligation yet feels prevented from acting, often with powerlessness, dissonance, or lingering distress. This differs from workload strain alone, which may be exhausting without a values conflict. Repeated distress around treatment perceived as nonbeneficial, poor communication, or resource limits suggests a moral and organizational concern; it does not by itself settle the treatment decision.","respond":"First clarify the ethical concern and connect it to the patient’s expressed goals and available facts. Communicate respectfully with the responsible clinician and interprofessional team, request a structured discussion or ethics consultation, and follow organizational escalation pathways. Do not independently change or stop an ordered treatment, substitute personal judgment for shared decision-making, or treat resilience training as a fix for a system barrier.","reassess":"After speaking up or an ethics discussion, check whether the concern was heard, the plan and rationale were revisited with relevant team members, and communication reflects the patient’s goals. Ask whether distress remains unresolved and support is available. Escalate through local pathways if the ethical concern or communication gap persists.","trap":"Moral resilience is not a thicker coat that makes clinicians tolerate any ethical problem; it is the capacity to preserve or restore integrity while engaging with the problem. Like a warning light, moral distress can signal a condition that needs team or system attention, not simply an individual reset.","caseStory":"In a fictional ICU case, a patient has expressed a preference for comfort-focused care, but aggressive treatment continues after family requests and clinician orders. The bedside nurse believes the treatment may not benefit the patient and feels powerless because the team has not discussed the conflict. The nurse must decide how to raise the concern without unilaterally changing the plan.","caseReason":"The nurse’s distress is tied to a perceived ethical conflict and inability to act, rather than workload alone. The nurse can respectfully bring the patient’s expressed preference and concern to the responsible clinician and team, and request structured ethical discussion or consultation. The nurse should not independently stop ordered treatment.","teachback":"When you feel distressed about care that seems inconsistent with a patient’s expressed goals, what specific concern would you raise with the team, and what local support or escalation pathway could help address it?","questions":[{"stem":"An ICU nurse is caring for a patient receiving treatment the nurse believes is nonbeneficial. The nurse feels unable to act because of the current plan and reports lingering ethical dissonance after repeated discussions fail to resolve the concern. Which interpretation best fits?","options":["Moral distress linked to a perceived ethical conflict and constraints on action","General workload strain because intensive care is demanding","Moral resilience because the nurse continues providing care despite discomfort"],"feedback":["The nurse identifies an ethical concern and feels constrained from acting, which is characteristic of moral distress.","Workload can cause stress, but the specific conflict between ethical judgment and constrained action points beyond workload alone.","Continuing to work while distressed does not establish moral resilience or resolve the ethical conflict."]},{"stem":"A critical care nurse believes the current treatment plan conflicts with a patient’s expressed goals. The plan remains in place under clinician orders, and the nurse has not yet discussed the concern with the team. What is the best next nursing action?","options":["State the concern respectfully to the responsible clinician and request team-based ethical discussion","Stop the treatment the nurse believes is nonbeneficial and explain the decision afterward","Use personal reflection to manage the distress without raising the concern with others"],"feedback":["Team communication and structured ethical discussion address the concern while keeping the nurse within role and organizational processes.","Unilaterally stopping an ordered treatment exceeds the appropriate advocacy response described here.","Reflection may help process distress, but handling it privately does not address the team or system issue."]},{"stem":"In a palliative care setting, a patient has documented a preference for comfort-focused care, while family members ask the team to continue aggressive treatment. A nurse is concerned that the patient’s preference is not guiding discussions. Which action best transfers the advocacy skill to this situation?","options":["Bring the documented preference and ethical concern to the interprofessional team for structured discussion","Ask the family to choose the plan without involving the clinical team","Use mindfulness to reduce distress and defer further discussion"],"feedback":["Sharing the patient’s expressed preference and concern in a team discussion supports ethical reflection and communication.","This bypasses the team and does not ensure the patient’s preference is considered in shared discussion.","Mindfulness may support personal coping, but it does not address the ethical concern or communication gap."]},{"stem":"After an ICU nurse raises a concern, the team meets and revises the plan toward symptom management. The nurse reports feeling calmer, but it is unclear whether the revised plan and rationale were communicated consistently. What should the nurse reassess?","options":["Whether the plan and communication reflect the patient’s goals and whether any ethical concern remains unresolved","Whether the nurse feels calm, treating that as sufficient evidence that the concern is resolved","Whether the unit workload has changed, without revisiting the plan or team communication"],"feedback":["Rechecking alignment with the patient’s goals, communication, and any remaining concern shows whether the ethical issue was actually addressed.","Feeling calmer is relevant but does not establish that the plan or communication now reflects the patient’s goals.","Workload may matter, but it does not answer whether the specific ethical concern and communication gap were resolved."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Moral Distress, Burnout, and the Intensive Care Unit Environment - PubMed","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43003538/"},{"title":"Facing Moral Distress: Why We Need to Enhance ... - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12670416/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Ethical conflict emerges","detail":"Moral distress arises when a clinician recognizes a patient-centered ethical course but feels blocked by orders, communication failures, or resources."},{"label":"Powerlessness signals conflict","detail":"Powerlessness tied to perceived nonbeneficial treatment or goals-of-care conflict signals ethical concern, not necessarily workload strain alone."},{"label":"Raise concerns collaboratively","detail":"Clarify the concern against patient goals, communicate respectfully with the team, and request structured discussion or ethics consultation through local pathways."},{"label":"Check team response","detail":"Check whether the concern and patient goals were heard and the plan revisited; escalate persistent ethical or communication gaps locally."}]},{"id":"s096","unit":"ethics","title":"Goals of care and family meetings","objective":"Use shared decision-making principles to support and communicate about goals-of-care discussions with patients, surrogates, and the critical-care team.","concept":"Goals-of-care decisions in intensive care combine clinical evidence about options, benefits, harms, and uncertainty with what matters to the patient. Shared decision-making is a collaborative process, not simply handing choices to family or having clinicians decide alone. Information exchange, deliberation, and a decision help join medical expertise with patient values. Because capacity and preferred decision-making roles may vary, clinicians should adapt the partnership while aiming for medically appropriate, value-concordant care.","recognize":"A goals-of-care discussion may involve decisions about life-prolonging treatment or other major choices shaped by personal values. Listen for whether options, likely benefits and harms, and uncertainty are explained; whether the patient’s values are elicited; and whether the surrogate’s understanding and preferred role are explored. Providing information alone, or asking family to decide without support, does not establish a collaborative process.","respond":"Communicate the patient’s capacity, the surrogate’s questions, and any stated patient values to the responsible clinician and care team. Support information exchange and deliberation by inviting questions and clarifying what the family understands, within your role and local practice. Avoid steering the decision toward your own preferences or leaving the surrogate to carry the decision without clinical guidance.","reassess":"After the discussion, listen for the surrogate’s understanding of the options and uncertainty, the patient values informing the choice, and whether the family’s preferred decision-making role was addressed. Report unresolved misunderstanding, new questions, or inability to reach an acceptable plan to the responsible clinician for follow-up.","trap":"A family meeting is not a ballot in which clinicians list options and leave relatives to choose alone. Think of it as joining two kinds of expertise: clinicians contribute medical knowledge, while patients or surrogates contribute knowledge of the patient’s values.","caseStory":"An incapacitated patient with severe neurologic injury has an uncertain recovery, and the team is meeting with the patient’s sister about ongoing life support. She asks what the options are, then says the patient had previously described what quality of life mattered to them. The team must decide how to include her knowledge while explaining clinical uncertainty.","caseReason":"This is a preference-sensitive goals-of-care discussion, so shared decision-making is appropriate. The team should explain options and uncertainty, explore the patient’s values, check the sister’s understanding, and deliberate with her rather than decide without her or transfer the full burden to her.","teachback":"In a future family meeting, what would you listen for to tell whether the team is combining clinical information with the patient’s values rather than simply presenting options?","questions":[{"stem":"A surrogate for an incapacitated ICU patient hears a clinician explain two treatment options and their possible outcomes. The clinician then asks what the patient had said about acceptable quality of life, checks the surrogate’s understanding, and discusses which option best fits those preferences. Which interpretation best describes this conversation?","options":["It uses shared decision-making by combining clinical information with the patient’s values and deliberation.","It is informed choice because the surrogate alone selects after receiving clinical information.","It is clinician-directed decision-making because the clinician explains the treatment options."],"feedback":["Correct. The conversation includes information exchange, elicitation of patient values, checking understanding, and deliberation about a decision.","This is misleading because the clinician and surrogate discuss the choice together rather than leaving the surrogate to decide alone.","Explaining options is part of information exchange, but the clinician also elicits the patient’s preferences and deliberates with the surrogate."]},{"stem":"During a goals-of-care meeting, a patient’s spouse says, “I trust the team; please tell me what you think is best.” The patient is incapacitated, and the spouse has not yet described the patient’s values or preferred role for family in decisions. What should the nurse prioritize communicating to the team?","options":["Explore the spouse’s preferred role and the patient’s values while the team provides supported clinical guidance.","Ask the spouse to make the treatment choice independently so the patient’s autonomy is respected.","Treat the spouse’s request as permission for the team to decide without further discussion."],"feedback":["Correct. Decision-making roles can vary, and the team should adapt while learning the patient’s values and offering clinical expertise.","This misreads autonomy as placing the full decision burden on the surrogate; the recommended approach supports surrogates in deliberation.","The spouse’s request may indicate a preference for clinician guidance, but it does not remove the value of discussing the patient’s preferences and role."]},{"stem":"An incapacitated adult with advanced COPD has had repeated unsuccessful ventilator-weaning attempts. The team is considering further weaning efforts or a transition to palliative therapy, and the family is unsure what the patient would value. Which nursing contribution best transfers shared decision-making to this context?","options":["Tell the team this is a preference-sensitive choice and help surface the patient’s values for discussion alongside the clinical options.","Recommend further weaning because repeated attempts show that recovery remains the family’s preferred goal.","Ask the family to choose between the options before the team discusses their risks, benefits, or uncertainty."],"feedback":["Correct. This type of major treatment choice may depend on personal values, so clinical information should be considered with the patient’s preferences.","This infers the patient’s goals from the treatment history, which does not establish what the patient would value.","This asks the family to decide before receiving the information needed for supported deliberation."]},{"stem":"After a family meeting about life-prolonging treatment, a surrogate nods throughout and says the team has been helpful. When asked to describe the options, the surrogate cannot distinguish them and does not know what uncertainty the clinicians discussed. Which reassessment finding should prompt the nurse to report a need for follow-up?","options":["The surrogate cannot explain the options or uncertainty despite appearing engaged during the meeting.","The surrogate expresses gratitude and says the clinician answered several questions.","The surrogate asks to take time before stating which option fits the patient’s values."],"feedback":["Correct. Apparent agreement or engagement does not establish understanding; the team should address the gap in understanding.","Gratitude and questions can accompany a useful discussion, but they do not by themselves show whether the surrogate understands the options.","Taking time to consider the patient’s values is not itself evidence of misunderstanding; deliberation is part of the decision process."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Shared Decision Making in Intensive Care Units: An American College of Critical Care Medicine and American Thoracic Society Policy Statement","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/4788386"},{"title":"Prevalence and predictors of shared decision-making in goals ...","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10590503/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Uncertain recovery","detail":"Severe neurologic injury leaves recovery uncertain while the incapacitated patient’s sister considers ongoing life support."},{"label":"Values guide choices","detail":"The sister recalls the patient’s quality-of-life priorities, signaling that personal values should inform this preference-sensitive decision."},{"label":"Explain and deliberate","detail":"The team explains options, likely benefits, harms, and uncertainty, then explores the patient’s values and the sister’s preferred role."},{"label":"Check shared understanding","detail":"Listen for the sister’s understanding and unresolved questions; report misunderstanding or no acceptable plan to the responsible clinician."}]},{"id":"s097","unit":"ethics","title":"Clinical inquiry","objective":"By the end of this unit, the nurse will be able to turn a critical-care practice concern into a focused PICOT question and identify how to evaluate an evidence-informed change.","concept":"Clinical inquiry connects a practice concern to the best available evidence, nursing expertise, client needs, and the care environment. A PICOT question makes the population, intervention, comparison when relevant, measurable outcome, and time frame explicit, helping guide an evidence search. Evidence-based practice includes asking, searching, appraising, integrating, evaluating, and sharing. Quality improvement and research have related but distinct purposes, so clarify the project aim before choosing an approach.","recognize":"Look for recurring variation in practice, outcome patterns, safety concerns, or uncertainty about whether current care aligns with evidence. Separate a measurable pattern from a single impression, and check whether records provide meaningful, reliable information. A request to improve a local process may signal quality improvement; a question seeking to apply broader evidence calls for an evidence-based practice approach.","respond":"Clarify the concern with the interprofessional team and, where appropriate, the client; define a measurable outcome and time frame before proposing a practice change. Use PICOT terms to guide a search of reliable, preferably peer-reviewed sources, then critically appraise the evidence with appropriate support. Follow organizational processes, orders, and local protocols. Do not treat one study, a documentation change, or personal preference alone as sufficient evidence.","reassess":"After an approved change, assess the defined process and client outcomes using relevant, meaningful data over the planned time frame. Review whether the intervention was implemented as intended and whether results fit the original question. Escalate concerns about safety, unexpected outcomes, unreliable data, or a lack of organizational support to the appropriate team or leader.","trap":"A well-completed record is like a carefully filled-in map: it shows what was documented, not necessarily whether the journey reached the intended destination. Do not confuse a process measure or local improvement project with proof that an intervention is effective across settings.","caseStory":"An adult critical-care unit reviews records and finds inconsistent use of a nursing process, while staff describe uncertainty about which approach best supports client outcomes. The nurse is asked to recommend a change, but the available records mainly show whether the process was documented. The decision is whether to implement a preferred approach now or first frame an inquiry and identify suitable evidence and outcomes.","caseReason":"The variation and uncertainty support a focused practice question, not an immediate conclusion about which approach is best. The nurse can use PICOT to define the population, approaches for comparison, measurable outcome, and time frame, then search and appraise evidence with the team. Documentation can inform evaluation, but it should not substitute for assessing the outcome of interest.","teachback":"In a different clinical setting, what population, intervention, comparison if relevant, measurable outcome, and time frame would you specify to turn a recurring practice concern into a searchable PICOT question?","questions":[{"stem":"In an adult critical-care unit, nurses report varying approaches to a recurring care process, and the team wants to know whether one approach is associated with better client outcomes. Which next step best turns this concern into a focused clinical inquiry?","options":["Define the population, approaches to compare, measurable outcome, and time frame in a PICOT question before searching for evidence.","Ask the most experienced nurse to select a preferred approach, then review documentation to see whether staff adopt it.","Begin a unit-wide practice change and use staff agreement as the main indicator that the approach improves care."],"feedback":["This identifies the PICOT elements that make the concern searchable and the outcome evaluable.","Experience may contribute to decisions, but it does not replace a focused question and appraisal of evidence.","Adoption or agreement alone does not establish whether a change improves the defined client outcome."]},{"stem":"A critical-care team has written a PICOT question about two approaches to a nursing process and has selected a measurable outcome and review period. Before recommending a practice change, what should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Search reliable sources using the question terms and critically appraise the relevant evidence with appropriate team support.","Select the approach used most often in the unit and treat its frequency as evidence of effectiveness.","Implement both approaches in different shifts and choose the one staff members find easier to document."],"feedback":["A focused search followed by critical appraisal is the appropriate next step before integrating evidence into practice.","Frequency of use does not establish that an approach is effective or consistent with current evidence.","Ease of documentation may be relevant to feasibility, but comparing preferences alone does not appraise evidence or client outcomes."]},{"stem":"A nurse in an adult rehabilitation service notices that clients receive different approaches to discharge teaching and wants to examine whether a structured teaching approach improves a measurable client outcome within a defined follow-up period. Which question best transfers the clinical inquiry skill to this new setting?","options":["For adult rehabilitation clients, does structured discharge teaching compared with usual teaching improve a specified measurable outcome during a defined follow-up period?","Do rehabilitation nurses prefer structured discharge teaching, and can the service increase the number of teaching records completed?","Should every rehabilitation service adopt structured discharge teaching because it is likely to improve outcomes for all clients?"],"feedback":["This specifies a population, intervention, comparison, measurable outcome, and time frame, making it a focused PICOT-style question.","This emphasizes staff preference and documentation volume rather than the stated client outcome and comparison.","This presumes effectiveness and universal applicability before evidence has been searched and appraised."]},{"stem":"After an approved change in an adult critical-care unit, electronic records show that documentation of the new process is more complete. The team has not yet reviewed the client outcome named in its original question. What is the best nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Treat documentation completion as process information, then evaluate the defined client outcome and implementation over the planned period before judging the change.","Conclude that the change improved care because more complete documentation demonstrates better client outcomes.","Disregard all record data and conclude that the change failed unless every client outcome improves immediately."],"feedback":["Documentation can show whether a process was recorded, but the defined outcome and implementation also need evaluation before drawing conclusions.","More complete records do not by themselves demonstrate improvement in the client outcome.","The records may provide useful process information, and the outcome should be evaluated over the planned period rather than presumed to fail immediately."]}],"sources":[{"title":"QUALITY AND EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICE","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/openrnnmpc2e/ch9/"},{"title":"Nursing Professional Development Evidence-Based Practice","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK589676/?report=printable"}],"frames":[{"label":"Practice variation","detail":"Unit records show inconsistent use of a nursing process, while staff remain uncertain which approach supports client outcomes."},{"label":"Documentation gap","detail":"Records mainly show documentation, not whether the nursing approach improves the client outcome of interest."},{"label":"Frame PICOT inquiry","detail":"Work with the team to define population, approaches, measurable outcome, and time frame, then search and appraise reliable evidence."},{"label":"Review meaningful results","detail":"After an approved change, compare relevant process and client outcomes over the planned period; escalate safety concerns or unreliable data."}]},{"id":"s098","unit":"ethics","title":"Collaboration and handoff","objective":"Use structured communication and closed-loop handoff practices to transfer critical-care information, responsibility, and accountability clearly between team members.","concept":"Critical-care teamwork depends on information being clearly and accurately exchanged, not merely sent. A handoff transfers information together with authority and responsibility, so the sender remains accountable until the receiver understands and accepts the transfer. SBAR organizes information as situation, background, assessment, and recommendation or request. Verbal confirmation, questions, and receiver acknowledgment close the communication loop and help expose ambiguity before it affects care.","recognize":"A handoff is in progress when care responsibility moves between people or settings. Look for whether the receiver has the relevant situation, background, assessment, and requested actions, and whether responsibility is explicitly accepted. A chart entry or message marked sent is not proof of understanding. Unanswered questions, unclear ownership, or an unacknowledged transfer indicate an incomplete handoff, rather than a completed exchange.","respond":"Prioritize clear, timely communication using SBAR when critical information needs attention. State the request and identify who is responsible for the next action. Give the handoff verbally through the appropriate channel, invite questions, clarify uncertainty, and use check-back to confirm key information. Follow orders and local protocols; do not assume that an electronic message was read or that responsibility transferred without acknowledgment.","reassess":"After communicating, verify that the receiver can restate the key information and requested actions, and explicitly accepts responsibility. Resolve any remaining ambiguity before ending the exchange. Escalate through the appropriate team channel if urgent information is not understood, the handoff is not accepted, or responsibility remains unclear.","trap":"A handoff is not like dropping a note into a mailbox and assuming it arrived; the sender remains accountable until the receiver acknowledges the transfer. Concision helps, but omitting relevant information or leaving uncertainty unresolved is not effective brevity.","caseStory":"During an adult ICU shift change, the outgoing nurse reports a recent change in the patient’s status and says a follow-up assessment is pending. The incoming nurse is interrupted before confirming who will complete it, and the outgoing nurse is preparing to leave. The decision is whether to rely on the written handoff or close the verbal transfer first.","caseReason":"The pending assessment has unclear ownership, so the handoff is incomplete. The outgoing nurse should clarify the requested action, confirm the incoming nurse understands it, and obtain acknowledgment that responsibility is accepted before relinquishing accountability.","teachback":"When handing off a patient with an unresolved task, what will you say and confirm before considering responsibility transferred?","questions":[{"stem":"At ICU shift change, a nurse gives a concise verbal update about an adult patient and mentions a pending task. The receiving nurse turns to another conversation without confirming the task or accepting responsibility. How should the outgoing nurse interpret this exchange?","options":["The handoff is incomplete, so the outgoing nurse remains accountable until the receiver understands and accepts the transfer.","The handoff is complete because the key information was stated aloud, even though the task was not acknowledged.","The handoff is complete if the pending task also appears in the written record, regardless of the receiver’s response."],"feedback":["Correct. A handoff requires receiver acknowledgment and clear transfer of responsibility; until then, the sender cannot relinquish accountability.","Stating information aloud is important, but it does not establish that the receiver understood or accepted the task.","A written record does not substitute for confirmation that the receiver understands and accepts responsibility."]},{"stem":"An ICU nurse needs to alert a colleague about a critical change and request follow-up. The colleague is available, but the request and ownership are not yet clear. What is the best communication priority?","options":["Organize the message with SBAR, state the request and responsible person, then confirm understanding and acceptance.","Give a brief summary, send the same information electronically, and assume the colleague will identify the next action.","Wait to communicate until every detail is known, then provide a complete update without requesting confirmation."],"feedback":["Correct. SBAR structures critical information, while a clear request and closed-loop confirmation help establish understanding and ownership.","A message marked sent does not verify receipt or clarify who is responsible for the action.","Timely communication matters, and relevant uncertainty should be clarified with the team rather than used to postpone an urgent exchange."]},{"stem":"An adult ICU patient is being transferred to another care area. The transport team has arrived, but the receiving clinician has not yet heard the relevant update or accepted responsibility. What should the nurse do?","options":["Provide a verbal handoff through the appropriate channel, clarify uncertainties, and confirm the receiver understands and accepts responsibility.","Allow the transfer to proceed because the written summary accompanies the patient, then contact the receiving clinician if questions arise.","Give the receiving clinician only the immediate task, since background information can be reviewed after the transfer."],"feedback":["Correct. A transition requires clear information transfer, an opportunity to clarify, and acknowledgment by the receiver before responsibility is relinquished.","An accompanying document does not establish that the information was understood or that responsibility was accepted.","A handoff should include relevant information, not only an immediate task; delaying context can leave important uncertainty unresolved."]},{"stem":"After an electronic ICU handoff is sent, the system displays a sent status. The receiving nurse has not replied, and the sender cannot tell whether the key action was understood. What is the best reassessment of the handoff?","options":["Verify verbally that the receiver understood the key information and accepts responsibility; the sent status alone is insufficient.","Treat the handoff as complete because the system confirms delivery, and wait for the receiver to request clarification.","Repeat the full written message in the record and consider responsibility transferred without further contact."],"feedback":["Correct. Electronic transmission alone does not confirm understanding or acceptance; the sender remains accountable until the receiver acknowledges the transfer.","Delivery does not prove that the message was understood, and the sender should not wait passively when responsibility is unclear.","Repeating a message in the record still does not provide verbal confirmation or receiver acknowledgment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"TeamSTEPPS for Office-Based Care: Communication","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps/officebasedcare/module3/office_comm.html"},{"title":"Section 1: Overview of Key Concepts and Tools","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/communication/overview/index.html"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pending assessment handoff","detail":"At ICU shift change, an outgoing nurse reports a status change and a follow-up assessment remains pending."},{"label":"Ownership unclear","detail":"The incoming nurse is interrupted before confirming who will complete the assessment, leaving responsibility unaccepted."},{"label":"Close verbal transfer","detail":"Use clear handoff communication to state the requested action, invite questions, and obtain acknowledgment before relinquishing accountability."},{"label":"Confirm accepted responsibility","detail":"Verify the receiver understands the action and accepts responsibility; escalate if ownership remains unclear or urgent information is not understood."}]},{"id":"s099","unit":"ethics","title":"Teaching with teach-back","objective":"Use teach-back and, when relevant, show-me to check and strengthen a patient’s understanding of health instructions.","concept":"Teach-back checks whether a clinician has explained information clearly by asking patients or family members to describe in their own words what they need to know or do. It supports engagement, safety, adherence, and quality because clinicians may overestimate how much patients understand or remember. Use it especially for new diagnoses, medicines, devices, or home-care instructions. Show-me adds a practical check when a patient needs to demonstrate a task.","recognize":"Consider teach-back when sharing a new concept or instruction, including medication directions, device use, or care at home. A yes-or-no reply does not establish understanding, and repeating the clinician’s words verbatim may not show comprehension. If a patient cannot explain the plan in their own words, treat that as a signal to improve the explanation, not as a test failure or a judgment about the patient.","respond":"Explain a small segment in clear language, then use a non-shaming prompt that makes clear you are checking your explanation. Ask the patient to describe the plan in their own words; use show-me when a task such as device use needs demonstration. If understanding is incomplete, explain it differently and check again. Share unresolved confusion with the appropriate clinician or team, following local protocols.","reassess":"Listen for an accurate explanation in the patient’s own words, rather than a simple yes or a verbatim repeat. For a task, observe whether the patient can demonstrate the instruction. If either check reveals a gap, re-explain using a different approach and check again. Involve the appropriate clinician or team if the concern remains unresolved.","trap":"Teach-back is not a quiz that measures a patient’s memory; it is more like checking whether a message arrived intact. A confident yes or a perfect-sounding repeat can still conceal misunderstanding, so ask for the person’s own explanation and clarify when needed.","caseStory":"A nurse explains a newly prescribed inhaler to a fictional patient, who replies, “Yes, I understand.” When invited to show how they will use it, the patient cannot follow the instructions. The nurse must decide whether to accept the verbal assurance or revisit the explanation and check again.","caseReason":"The yes-or-no response does not establish understanding, and show-me is useful for checking a device task. The nurse should explain the instructions differently, then invite another demonstration. If the gap remains, the nurse should communicate it to the appropriate clinician or team according to local practice.","teachback":"A patient says they understand a new care instruction but repeats your wording exactly. How would you check understanding without making the patient feel tested, and what would you do if their explanation reveals a gap?","questions":[{"stem":"A nurse explains new home-care instructions to a fictional patient. Asked to explain the plan, the patient repeats the nurse’s sentences word for word but cannot describe what they will do at home. Which interpretation and next step best fit teach-back?","options":["Understanding is not yet clear; explain the plan differently and ask the patient to describe it again in their own words.","Understanding is established because the patient repeated the instructions accurately; document the response and end the discussion.","Understanding is likely adequate; ask whether the instructions make sense and accept a yes as confirmation."],"feedback":["Correct. Verbatim repetition may not show comprehension, so reteach using a different approach and check again in the patient’s own words.","This is misleading because repeating the clinician’s wording is not the same as explaining what the patient needs to know or do.","A yes-or-no reply does not show what the patient understood and may conceal a gap in the explanation."]},{"stem":"During a fictional visit, a nurse needs to discuss a new diagnosis, related instructions, and home care. The patient is attentive, but the amount of information is substantial. Which approach best prioritizes teach-back during the discussion?","options":["Explain one small segment, ask the patient to teach it back, then proceed to the next segment and check again.","Explain all of the information first, then ask the patient to summarize everything at the end of the visit.","Provide written material during the discussion and ask whether the patient understands before moving on."],"feedback":["Correct. Chunking information and checking understanding throughout the visit can reveal gaps before more information is added.","Waiting until the end may make it harder to identify which part was unclear; teach-back need not be delayed until the visit concludes.","Written material may support learning, but asking whether the patient understands is not a teach-back check."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient calls an appointment desk and receives instructions about arrival time, required documents, and bringing current medicines to the next visit. Which action best applies teach-back in this nonclinical interaction?","options":["Ask the patient to describe in their own words what they will need to do before and during the next visit.","Read the instructions a second time and ask the patient to confirm that they heard each item.","Ask the patient whether the instructions are clear and repeat them only if the patient says no."],"feedback":["Correct. Staff who interact with patients, including appointment staff, can use teach-back to check whether visit requirements are understood.","Hearing the instructions again does not confirm that the patient can explain what they need to do.","A yes-or-no question can miss misunderstanding; an explanation in the patient’s own words provides a more useful check."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient correctly describes when and how much of a newly prescribed medicine they will take, but when asked to demonstrate using the pills, the demonstration does not match the instructions. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Recognize the mismatch, clarify the instructions, and ask the patient to demonstrate again using the show-me approach.","Rely on the patient’s correct verbal explanation and conclude that the medicine instructions were understood.","Repeat the same explanation and ask whether the patient is now confident about following it."],"feedback":["Correct. A verbal explanation can differ from what a patient can demonstrate, so clarify and check the task again.","This overlooks the discrepancy. Show-me can reveal problems that a correct verbal response does not uncover.","Confidence is not a demonstration of the task, and repeating the same explanation may not resolve the misunderstanding."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Use the Teach-Back Method: Tool #5","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/improve/precautions/tool5.html"},{"title":"Teach-Back","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/patient-safety/reports/engage/teachback.html"}],"frames":[{"label":"Inhaler instruction","detail":"A nurse explains a newly prescribed inhaler, and the patient answers, “Yes, I understand.”"},{"label":"Demonstration gap","detail":"When invited to show inhaler use, the patient cannot follow the instructions, so verbal assurance is insufficient."},{"label":"Explain then retry","detail":"Re-explain the instructions differently with a non-shaming prompt, then invite another demonstration of the device task."},{"label":"Observe accurate use","detail":"Look for an accurate demonstration; if difficulty persists, communicate the unresolved gap to the appropriate clinician or team."}]},{"id":"s100","unit":"ethics","title":"Language access and equitable care","objective":"Use language-access strategies to support clear, equitable communication with adult patients who have limited English proficiency.","concept":"Linguistic competence means making culturally appropriate oral and written language services readily available to patients with limited English proficiency. Nurses should use the patient’s preferred language, clear wording, trained medical interpreters for spoken communication, and qualified translated materials when needed. These supports reduce communication barriers and help patients participate in care. Relying on family members can compromise accuracy or privacy, while appropriate interpreter use is associated with fewer communication errors and better patient experiences.","recognize":"Notice when a patient has difficulty speaking, reading, writing, or understanding English, even if they can manage brief conversation. Ask which language they prefer for health information rather than assuming from appearance or family presence. A language barrier differs from a hearing or vision barrier, which may require accommodations such as captioning, sign-language interpretation, large print, or audio support.","respond":"Prioritize access to a qualified medical interpreter when discussing care, instructions, or consent information with a patient who has limited English proficiency. Follow the facility process, speak clearly, and provide written materials in the patient’s preferred language using simple wording. Coordinate with the care team. Avoid asking relatives to interpret because information may be changed or withheld, and medical terms may be misunderstood.","reassess":"Check that communication occurred in the patient’s preferred language and invite the patient, through the interpreter, to raise questions or state preferences. Confirm that needed instructions and written materials were accessible. If the interpreter is unavailable or the patient still cannot communicate questions or preferences, clarify the barrier and escalate through the facility process.","trap":"A family member who speaks both languages is not automatically an equivalent substitute for a trained medical interpreter. Think of interpretation as a safety-critical relay: each message must arrive accurately, without being edited or lost through unfamiliar medical terminology.","caseStory":"In an adult critical-care unit, a patient can exchange greetings in English but struggles to discuss the ordered care plan and asks to use Vietnamese. A relative offers to interpret, and the patient nods during the conversation. The nurse must decide how to support accurate communication before continuing the discussion.","caseReason":"Brief conversation and nodding do not establish that the patient can understand detailed health information in English. The nurse should arrange a qualified medical interpreter through the facility and communicate in the patient’s preferred language. The relative should not be used as the interpreter because information could be altered or misunderstood.","teachback":"In a future care discussion, what would you do to determine the patient’s preferred language, obtain appropriate language support, and recognize when communication remains inadequate?","questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized adult answers greetings in English but cannot explain the ordered care plan and requests Vietnamese for health information. No hearing or vision difficulty is apparent. What is the best interpretation of these cues?","options":["The patient needs language support for health communication, so arrange a qualified medical interpreter.","The patient’s brief English responses show adequate understanding, so repeat the plan more slowly.","The difficulty explaining the plan suggests cognitive impairment, so defer language support."],"feedback":["Correct. The patient’s stated language preference and difficulty discussing health information indicate a language-access need, which should be addressed with a qualified medical interpreter.","Brief conversation does not show that detailed health information is understood. Slower English does not replace communication in the patient’s preferred language.","The cues do not establish cognitive impairment. Address the stated language barrier before drawing conclusions about the patient’s understanding."]},{"stem":"In an adult critical-care unit, a patient with limited English proficiency needs an explanation of ordered care. A relative offers to interpret, while the facility can arrange a qualified medical interpreter. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Use the facility process to arrange a qualified medical interpreter for the discussion.","Ask the relative to interpret and check afterward whether the patient has questions.","Postpone all discussion until a bilingual member of the usual care team is available."],"feedback":["Correct. A qualified medical interpreter supports accurate communication, and the facility can arrange one. The nurse should use the patient’s preferred language for the discussion.","A relative may edit or withhold information, and unfamiliarity with medical terminology can lead to misunderstanding. A relative should not replace a trained interpreter.","The available qualified interpreter provides a way to communicate without waiting for a particular care-team member. Follow the facility process rather than postponing needed communication for that reason."]},{"stem":"At a community clinic, an adult with limited English proficiency is preparing to leave with written health instructions. The patient identifies a preferred language, and the clinic has access to an interpreter and translated materials. Which approach best transfers language-access principles to this setting?","options":["Use a qualified interpreter for spoken communication and provide simple written materials in the preferred language.","Give the patient English instructions and ask a family member to explain them at home.","Use an interpreter for the conversation but provide only English written materials."],"feedback":["Correct. Linguistically competent care includes appropriate oral and written language services, with materials in the patient’s preferred language and clear wording.","This places the burden on a family member and risks changed or misunderstood information. It also fails to provide accessible written instructions.","An interpreter supports spoken communication, but English-only written materials leave a communication barrier in place. Written information should be accessible in the preferred language."]},{"stem":"After a qualified interpreter assists with a care discussion, an adult patient nods but does not ask questions. The patient has limited English proficiency. What is the most useful nursing reassessment?","options":["Continue through the interpreter and invite the patient to raise questions or state preferences, clarifying any remaining barrier.","Document effective communication because the patient nodded throughout the interpreted discussion.","Ask the accompanying relative whether the patient understood the information."],"feedback":["Correct. Nodding alone does not establish that communication was adequate. Using the interpreter to invite questions and preferences helps identify whether clarification or escalation is needed.","A nod is not enough to determine whether the patient could communicate questions or preferences. Reassess communication rather than relying on this cue alone.","The relative is not a substitute for the qualified interpreter, and asking them does not directly establish whether the patient can communicate questions or preferences."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Factors Preventing...","url":"https://openstax.org/books/clinical-nursing-skills/pages/5-4-diversity-equity-and-inclusion"},{"title":"Chapter 3 Diverse Patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/openrnnf/ch3diversepatients/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Preferred language","detail":"The patient speaks some English but struggles with the ordered care plan and asks to use Vietnamese."},{"label":"Nods do not confirm","detail":"A relative offers to interpret, while the patient’s nodding does not establish understanding of detailed health information."},{"label":"Arrange qualified interpreter","detail":"Use the facility process to obtain a qualified medical interpreter and communicate in the patient’s preferred language."},{"label":"Confirm accessible communication","detail":"Invite questions and preferences through the interpreter; escalate if the patient still cannot communicate or needed materials remain inaccessible."}]},{"id":"s101","unit":"ethics","title":"Systems thinking and safety","objective":"Use a systems approach to interpret a safety event, identify contributing conditions, and choose a nurse-level response that reduces risk without defaulting to blame.","concept":"Health care depends on people working within complex processes, so lapses are foreseeable rather than proof that a clinician is inherently unsafe. An active error occurs at the point of care; a slip is an attention lapse, while a mistake reflects a wrong choice. Latent weaknesses in staffing, workflow, equipment layout, policy, or team culture can let an active error reach a patient. Safety improves by finding and redesigning these weak points, not by expecting flawless humans.","recognize":"When an event or near miss occurs, separate the visible action from the conditions around it. Distraction, fatigue, or stress may point to a slip; a knowledge or judgment gap may suggest a mistake. Then look for latent contributors such as workload, schedule pressure, team changes, communication barriers, or equipment placement. Several aligned weaknesses favor a system explanation over a lone-person account.","respond":"First address the patient's immediate needs and communicate promptly with appropriate clinical and safety leaders under local processes. Support the patient, family, and involved staff, and preserve time-sensitive records or equipment when relevant. Share a factual account focused on what happened and the work conditions, not fault. Avoid making discipline or retraining the default before contributing factors are understood; use system redesign where indicated.","reassess":"After actions, check whether immediate patient and family needs are addressed, relevant records and equipment have been secured, and staff can describe the workflow and contributing conditions. Escalate promptly through local channels if harm or an unresolved hazard remains, or if needed resources to reduce recurrence are unavailable.","trap":"A safety event is not automatically evidence of incompetence, just as one missed turn does not explain why a route repeatedly sends drivers into a hazard. Support normal human error, coach at-risk behavior, and reserve sanctions for deliberate reckless conduct after fair review, while maintaining appropriate accountability.","caseStory":"In a fictional outpatient operating room, a patient receives a different hand procedure than scheduled after a delayed list and a last-minute team change. The surgeon had been interrupted, no interpreter was available for the patient's language, and the monitor position made it hard for nurses to watch the field. As the event reviewer, the nurse must decide whether to focus on the surgeon alone or examine the conditions around the event.","caseReason":"The surgeon's action is an active error, and the interruption may have contributed to a slip. Delays, team turnover, communication barriers, the absent time-out, and monitor layout are potential latent conditions that could align. A systems review should gather facts and consider workflow, communication, and room-layout changes rather than assuming individual retraining will prevent recurrence.","teachback":"In a near miss on your unit, what observable workflow, staffing, communication, or equipment conditions would you examine before recommending an individual-focused correction, and what would you do first to protect people and preserve useful facts?","questions":[{"stem":"During a fictional outpatient procedure, the wrong hand procedure is performed. The surgeon had been interrupted, the schedule was delayed, the operating-room team had changed, no interpreter was available, and nurses had limited visibility of the monitor. Which interpretation best applies systems thinking?","options":["The procedure is an active error, while the interruptions and work conditions suggest latent contributors that should be investigated together.","The procedure most likely reflects a knowledge mistake by the surgeon, so the review should focus on assessing that clinician's competence.","The procedure is an active error, but the schedule and room setup are background details unless another person reports the same event."],"feedback":["This distinguishes the action at the point of care from system conditions that may have made an error more likely or harder to catch.","A mistake can involve an incorrect choice, but the scenario includes distraction and system weaknesses that warrant broader investigation before drawing that conclusion.","Latent hazards matter even when they have not yet produced repeated events; a systems review seeks conditions that could allow future errors."]},{"stem":"A medication-dispensing near miss occurs on a hospital ward, and the team is beginning a prospective failure modes and effects analysis. Which next step best follows a systems approach?","options":["Map the process steps, identify how each could fail, and consider failure likelihood, detectability, and impact to prioritize improvement.","Interview the staff member involved, determine which policy was missed, and begin retraining before reviewing the rest of the dispensing process.","List the process steps and rank them only by how often errors are reported, then focus on the most frequently reported step."],"feedback":["FMEA examines process steps and possible failures, considering likelihood, whether a failure can be detected, and its impact to help prioritize hazards.","This centers the individual before understanding the process and does not follow the prospective, step-by-step risk assessment described for FMEA.","Frequency alone omits detectability and impact, which are part of the FMEA assessment and prioritization."]},{"stem":"After a safety event involving poor visibility of an operating-room monitor, the team changes the monitor position. Which observation would best help the nurse reassess whether this change addresses the identified system weakness?","options":["Observe whether nurses can view the monitor while maintaining visibility of the patient and procedure without turning away.","Confirm that the involved surgeon completed a refresher session about checking the scheduled procedure before starting.","Confirm that staff signed a revised policy acknowledging their responsibility to monitor the procedure."],"feedback":["This checks the work environment against the specific barrier identified in the event review: monitor placement limiting nurses' ability to observe.","Training may be relevant in some situations, but completion alone does not show whether the visibility problem has been corrected.","Policy acknowledgement does not demonstrate that the monitor layout now supports observation or that the identified system weakness has changed."]},{"stem":"A nurse is supporting the initial review of an event involving an infusion device. The patient and family need support, and the device and tubing may contain time-sensitive information. Which response best fits the nurse's role in a systems-focused review?","options":["Use the local event process to address immediate patient and family needs, support caregivers, and preserve the device, tubing, and relevant records for review.","Ask the involved caregiver to explain the error first, then decide whether to report it after identifying whether a policy was followed.","Prioritize documenting a likely cause in the event record, leaving patient and family communication and device collection to the later review meeting."],"feedback":["The initial response includes immediate support and timely collection of potentially important information, without substituting blame-finding for fact-finding.","This makes reporting contingent on a judgment about the caregiver and risks discouraging open reporting of events and hazards.","A later in-depth review does not replace the prompt response needed for immediate needs and time-sensitive records or equipment."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Systems Approach","url":"https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/systems-approach"},{"title":"System-Focused Event Investigation and Analysis Guide - AHRQ","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/patient-safety/settings/hospital/candor/modules/guide4.html"}],"frames":[{"label":"Delayed procedure and handoff","detail":"A delayed operating-room list and last-minute team change create conditions around the wrong-hand procedure, beyond the surgeon’s visible action."},{"label":"Multiple contributing conditions","detail":"Interruption, absent interpreter, missing time-out, and monitor placement point to possible system weaknesses rather than a lone-person explanation."},{"label":"Share factual account","detail":"Address immediate patient needs and promptly communicate what happened and the work conditions to appropriate clinical and safety leaders."},{"label":"Check safety actions","detail":"Confirm patient and family needs are addressed and relevant records secured; escalate if harm or an unresolved hazard remains."}]},{"id":"s102","unit":"cardio","title":"Arterial waveform quality","objective":"Distinguish waveform distortion from a true change in blood pressure and communicate findings that could affect hemodynamic decisions.","concept":"An arterial line transmits pressure through fluid-filled tubing to a transducer, which displays the pressure waveform. A recognizable waveform has a systolic upstroke, a decline, a dicrotic notch, and diastolic runoff. Excessive damping can make systolic pressure appear low and obscure the notch, while insufficient damping can make systolic pressure appear high and add spikes. Mean pressure is less affected by damping, but waveform distortion still warrants assessment.","recognize":"Look at the shape as well as the displayed numbers. A blunted waveform with a missing dicrotic notch suggests excessive damping; a sharply spiked tracing suggests insufficient damping. Air, clot, or a kink can impair pressure transmission. Also consider whether transducer level or zeroing, patient movement, or a difference between limbs could explain a reading that does not fit the patient or another measurement.","respond":"Prioritize the patient’s perfusion and the reliability of the signal. Check the tracing, visible tubing and connections, transducer level, and zeroing status according to local protocol, and compare with the patient’s condition and a cuff measurement when appropriate. Report the waveform pattern, pressure trend, and relevant assessment promptly to the responsible clinician. Do not independently change vasoactive therapy based on a questionable reading.","reassess":"After checks or actions under protocol, reassess waveform shape, displayed pressures, transducer level, and the patient’s perfusion. Compare measurements obtained on the same limb when feasible. Escalate promptly if the abnormal tracing persists, readings remain discordant, or signs such as cool mottled skin, reduced urine output, altered mental status, or rising lactate suggest hypoperfusion.","trap":"A dramatic systolic number is not automatically a reliable pressure, just as a loud radio signal is not necessarily clear audio. Damping can distort systolic and diastolic values even when the patient’s underlying pressure has not changed; assess the waveform and clinical context before interpreting the number.","caseStory":"A patient receiving vasopressor support has a radial arterial tracing with a flattened contour and no visible dicrotic notch, while the displayed systolic pressure falls. The patient is awake with warm extremities, and a cuff reading is higher. The nurse must decide whether to treat the displayed change as confirmed deterioration or first verify the signal and report the discrepancy.","caseReason":"The flattened contour and absent notch are cues to possible excessive damping, which can make systolic pressure appear falsely low. The patient’s examination and cuff reading do not confirm the apparent change, but they do not by themselves prove the arterial reading is wrong. The nurse should check the setup under protocol, assess perfusion, and promptly communicate the discrepancy rather than independently changing therapy.","teachback":"When a displayed arterial systolic pressure changes but the waveform becomes distorted, what findings would you assess and communicate before treating the number as a true blood pressure change?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient’s arterial tracing becomes blunted, the dicrotic notch is no longer visible, and the displayed systolic pressure falls. The patient’s appearance is unchanged. Which interpretation best fits these cues?","options":["The tracing suggests excessive damping, which can make systolic pressure appear low; verify signal quality and assess the patient.","The tracing suggests insufficient damping, which typically raises displayed systolic pressure and creates additional spikes.","The tracing confirms a true fall in blood pressure because a lower systolic value outweighs waveform shape."],"feedback":["A blunted tracing with a missing notch is consistent with excessive damping, which can lower the displayed systolic pressure. The signal and the patient both need assessment.","Insufficient damping is associated with falsely high systolic pressure and additional artifactual spikes, not this blunted pattern.","Waveform distortion can affect the displayed pressure, so a lower value alone does not confirm a true physiologic decline."]},{"stem":"A patient on vasoactive medication develops a sharply spiked arterial waveform and a sudden rise in displayed systolic pressure. The patient’s mental status and skin appearance are unchanged. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Assess the patient and signal, check the setup under local protocol, and promptly report the changed waveform and pressure before any treatment change.","Treat the displayed systolic rise as a confirmed pressure increase and independently reduce the vasoactive medication.","Use the unchanged patient appearance to conclude the reading is harmless and defer further assessment."],"feedback":["Insufficient damping can produce a falsely high systolic value and artifactual spikes. Assessing the patient and signal and communicating before a treatment change is appropriate.","A distorted waveform makes the systolic value uncertain, and the nurse should not independently adjust vasoactive therapy based on it.","An unchanged examination does not establish that the signal is reliable or that the pressure is adequate; the discrepancy needs assessment and communication."]},{"stem":"After a patient is repositioned, the arterial pressure appears lower while the waveform shape is otherwise recognizable. The transducer is now above the level of the right atrium. Which explanation and action are most appropriate?","options":["The elevated transducer can produce a falsely low reading; reposition it to the appropriate level under local protocol and reassess the measurement.","The elevated transducer adds hydrostatic pressure and can produce a falsely high reading; leave it in place and compare later.","The recognizable waveform establishes that transducer position cannot explain the lower reading; accept the value as physiologic."],"feedback":["A transducer above the right atrium can produce a falsely low pressure reading. Correcting its level under protocol and reassessing addresses a plausible technical cause.","A transducer below the heart, not above it, can produce a falsely high reading because of hydrostatic pressure.","A recognizable waveform does not rule out a leveling error; transducer position can affect the displayed pressure even when the contour is recognizable."]},{"stem":"A patient’s previously blunted arterial waveform improves after the team addresses a visible tubing kink under local protocol. The displayed systolic pressure rises, the patient remains alert with warm skin, and a cuff measurement differs. What is the best reassessment?","options":["Recheck waveform shape, transducer level and zeroing status, compare pressures obtained on the same limb when feasible, and assess perfusion before drawing conclusions.","Accept the higher systolic pressure as definitive because the waveform improved, and stop comparing it with the patient’s condition.","Assume the cuff is definitive because it differs from the arterial reading, and disregard the arterial waveform."],"feedback":["A kink can impair pressure transmission, so improvement in waveform quality is relevant, but the remaining discrepancy still calls for signal checks and assessment of perfusion.","A better waveform improves confidence but does not make one systolic value definitive; the patient and measurement conditions still matter.","Neither method is automatically correct when measurements disagree. Checking technical factors and comparing measurements on the same limb when feasible helps interpret the difference."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Arterial Lines - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499989/"},{"title":"Should I Target the Blood Pressure from the Arterial Line or ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12733910/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Arterial pressure transmission","detail":"A fluid-filled arterial line transmits pressure to a transducer, so tubing or setup problems can distort the displayed waveform."},{"label":"Flattened waveform","detail":"A blunted contour and missing dicrotic notch suggest excessive damping, especially when the displayed systolic pressure conflicts with the patient’s condition."},{"label":"Verify and communicate","detail":"Check the tracing, visible tubing, connections, transducer level, and zeroing under local protocol; report the pressure trend and discrepancy promptly."},{"label":"Reassess signal and perfusion","detail":"Recheck waveform and patient perfusion; escalate if distortion or discordant readings persist, or signs of hypoperfusion appear."}]},{"id":"s103","unit":"cardio","title":"Vasoactive infusions and response","objective":"Recognize possible complications during peripheral vasoactive infusion and use bedside findings to guide timely nursing communication and reassessment under local protocols.","concept":"Vasoactive agents support blood pressure through effects on vascular tone, but leakage from a peripheral catheter can expose nearby tissue to their vasoconstrictive effects and cause injury. Peripheral administration can allow prompt treatment while avoiding or deferring some central-line risks, but it requires suitable access, a safety protocol, and vigilant observation. The nurse’s role is to detect local changes, communicate promptly, and reassess both the infusion site and the patient’s response.","recognize":"Look for new swelling, blanching, coolness, discoloration, leakage, pain, skin injury, or vesicular changes around the catheter; inspect the area proximal and distal to it. These local findings raise concern for leakage or tissue injury even if the infusion appears to run or the patient’s blood pressure improves. Systemic instability also needs prompt attention, but does not explain away a changing catheter site.","respond":"Check the site and infusion, promptly notify the responsible clinician of concerning changes, and follow the local protocol for peripheral vasoactive infusions or suspected extravasation. Continue close observation and communicate the patient’s hemodynamic response. Do not independently alter the dose, concentration, or access plan. Do not delay a protocol-approved, ordered peripheral infusion solely because central access is being arranged.","reassess":"After nursing action, reassess the site for evolving swelling, color or skin changes, leakage, and discomfort, as well as infusion continuity and the patient’s hemodynamic response. Escalate promptly if local findings worsen, tissue injury appears, or instability persists or develops, following local protocols.","trap":"A reassuring blood pressure is not a site check: the patient can respond systemically while local tissue is being injured. Think of peripheral infusion as a bridge that can support timely treatment, not as a line that needs no surveillance or is automatically unsuitable.","caseStory":"A fictional adult in intensive care is receiving an ordered vasoactive infusion through a forearm catheter while the team evaluates longer-term access. The patient’s blood pressure has improved, but the nurse notices new swelling and cool, pale skin near the catheter. The nurse must decide whether the reassuring pressure changes the urgency of the local finding.","caseReason":"The new localized swelling, coolness, and blanching are concerning for leakage and possible tissue injury, regardless of the improved blood pressure. The nurse should promptly alert the responsible clinician and follow the local extravasation protocol, then reassess the site and the patient’s response.","teachback":"If a patient’s blood pressure improves during a peripheral vasoactive infusion but the catheter site changes, what would you assess, communicate, and reassess?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in intensive care is receiving an ordered vasoactive infusion through a peripheral catheter. The pump appears to be running and the patient’s blood pressure is improving, but the skin near the catheter is newly pale, cool, and swollen. What is the best interpretation and nursing response?","options":["Suspect local leakage with possible tissue injury, promptly notify the responsible clinician, and follow the local extravasation protocol.","Treat the improved blood pressure as evidence that the infusion is reaching the circulation and continue without changing the site checks.","Interpret the cool, pale skin as proof of worsening systemic shock and focus on that rather than the catheter site."],"feedback":["Correct. New localized pallor, coolness, and swelling are concerning for extravasation even when systemic pressure improves; prompt communication and protocol-guided action are appropriate.","Misleading. A systemic response does not rule out local leakage or tissue injury, so the site still requires attention and reassessment.","Misleading. Systemic instability warrants assessment, but localized changes at the catheter site are a distinct warning that should not be dismissed."]},{"stem":"An adult with shock is receiving an ordered vasoactive infusion through a peripheral catheter. The nurse finds new swelling and discoloration at the site while the patient remains hypotensive. Which action should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly notify the responsible clinician and follow the local suspected-extravasation protocol while the team addresses the patient’s instability.","Increase the infusion rate independently to address the low blood pressure, then inspect the site again after the pressure responds.","Wait for central access before reporting the site change, because the patient is already receiving treatment through a peripheral line."],"feedback":["Correct. The local changes may indicate extravasation and need prompt protocol-guided attention; the patient’s ongoing instability also requires team communication and response.","Misleading. Independent dose changes are outside the described nursing role, and increasing the infusion does not address possible local injury.","Misleading. A suspected site complication should be communicated promptly; waiting for central access may delay appropriate action."]},{"stem":"In a postoperative recovery unit, an adult has persistent hypotension and a clinician has ordered a vasoactive infusion through a suitable peripheral catheter under the hospital’s protocol. Central access is not yet available, and the site currently has no visible changes. What is the most appropriate nursing approach?","options":["Proceed with the ordered, protocol-approved peripheral infusion and closely monitor the site and the patient while the team evaluates access needs.","Hold the ordered infusion until central access is available, even though the peripheral plan meets the local protocol.","Proceed with the infusion but rely on pump alarms and blood pressure readings instead of inspecting the catheter site."],"feedback":["Correct. The supplied evidence supports prompt peripheral administration under a safety protocol rather than waiting solely for central access, with vigilant site and patient monitoring.","Misleading. Delaying an ordered, protocol-approved infusion solely for central access can postpone treatment; the access decision remains with the responsible team.","Misleading. Pump function and blood pressure do not replace direct inspection for local swelling, discoloration, leakage, or skin injury."]},{"stem":"After a suspected peripheral infusion complication, an adult’s blood pressure is stable and the patient reports no pain. The nurse’s earlier assessment documented swelling and discoloration near the catheter. Which reassessment best avoids being misled by the current findings?","options":["Reinspect the site and nearby tissue for evolving swelling, discoloration, leakage, or skin injury, and escalate changes under the local protocol.","Use the stable blood pressure and lack of pain to conclude that the local complication has resolved.","Check only whether the pump is running, since a functioning infusion is the main sign of safe peripheral access."],"feedback":["Correct. Local injury can remain a concern despite stable pressure or absent pain, so direct site reassessment and protocol-guided escalation are appropriate.","Misleading. Stable pressure and no pain do not establish that localized swelling or discoloration has resolved.","Misleading. Pump operation does not show whether fluid is leaking into tissue; direct inspection is essential."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Navigating the veins: A comprehensive review of vasoactive ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13248048/"},{"title":"Feasibility and Safety of Peripheral Intravenous Administration of ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7648440/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Peripheral infusion site","detail":"An ordered vasoactive infusion through a forearm catheter may injure nearby tissue if medication leaks from the access site."},{"label":"New pale swelling","detail":"New swelling, coolness, and pale skin near the catheter suggest leakage or tissue injury, even as blood pressure improves."},{"label":"Follow local protocol","detail":"Promptly notify the responsible clinician about the site changes and follow the local protocol for suspected extravasation."},{"label":"Track site and response","detail":"Reassess swelling, color, skin changes, discomfort, infusion continuity, and hemodynamic response; escalate if findings worsen or instability develops."}]},{"id":"s104","unit":"cardio","title":"Ventricular assist devices","objective":"Recognize common LVAD warning signs and communicate focused assessment findings promptly to guide team-led emergency evaluation.","concept":"An LVAD moves blood from the left ventricle to the aorta through an implanted pump, with a driveline connecting it to an external controller and power source. Continuous-flow devices may produce little or no palpable pulse, although a patient’s own heart can contribute pulsatility. Pump flow depends on adequate blood reaching the left ventricle, so volume depletion, right ventricular failure, rhythm problems, or mechanical obstruction can all impair circulation.","recognize":"Assess the patient as well as the device: check mental status, skin and extremity perfusion, symptoms, heart rhythm, expected pump hum, controller display, alarms, driveline, and power connections. Low-flow alarms can reflect low circulating volume or right ventricular failure, among other causes. Congestion may suggest right-sided failure; bleeding, infection signs, or new neurologic changes point toward other urgent complications.","respond":"Prioritize immediate clinical assessment and escalate instability using local emergency protocols. Contact the VAD coordinator or advanced heart failure team early, and report symptoms, perfusion findings, alarms, displayed parameters, and recent events. Check power and connections without changing settings, and obtain blood pressure using the approved local method. Do not assume absent pulse means cardiac arrest or silence an alarm without evaluation.","reassess":"After actions under protocol, reassess mental status, perfusion, symptoms, rhythm, blood pressure or MAP, alarm status, and displayed pump parameters. Report persistent low flow, worsening perfusion, new neurologic findings, significant bleeding, or loss of the expected pump hum promptly to the VAD team and emergency response team.","trap":"A continuous-flow LVAD is more like a steady stream than a hand pump, so a weak or absent palpable pulse alone does not establish arrest or poor perfusion. Conversely, a reassuring device display does not replace assessment of the person, because low output can arise from problems such as bleeding or right ventricular failure.","caseStory":"A patient with an LVAD arrives after several episodes of diarrhea and reports weakness. The controller shows a low-flow alarm, the patient appears dry, and there is no new edema or breathlessness. The nurse must decide whether to treat the alarm as a device failure or promptly assess and communicate possible causes of reduced pump filling.","caseReason":"Diarrhea and a dry appearance support possible volume depletion, which can reduce blood reaching the pump, but the alarm does not identify the cause by itself. The nurse should assess perfusion and device connections, report the alarm and findings, and contact the VAD team promptly rather than adjusting pump settings independently.","teachback":"If an LVAD patient has no palpable pulse but is awake, what additional findings would you assess before deciding whether the patient is poorly perfused, and whom would you contact?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with an LVAD reports weakness after repeated vomiting. The controller displays a low-flow alarm, the patient has dry mucous membranes, and there is no new edema or breathlessness. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit these findings?","options":["Reduced circulating volume may be limiting pump filling; assess perfusion and device connections, then promptly report the findings and alarm to the VAD team.","The low-flow alarm establishes pump thrombosis; prepare to change pump settings while waiting for the device team.","The absence of edema rules out a serious cause; document the alarm and reassess at the next routine observation."],"feedback":["Vomiting and signs of dryness support possible reduced volume and preload, which can lower pump flow. The alarm requires assessment and team communication because it does not establish a single cause.","Pump thrombosis is one possible complication, but the alarm alone does not diagnose it. Nurses should not independently change device settings.","Lack of edema does not rule out hypovolemia or other urgent causes of low flow. Delaying communication could postpone evaluation of impaired perfusion."]},{"stem":"A patient with an LVAD becomes confused and diaphoretic, and the controller displays a critical alarm. The patient’s perfusion appears worse than on arrival. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Initiate the local emergency response, assess immediate clinical status, and promptly notify the VAD coordinator or advanced heart failure team while reporting the alarm and findings.","Focus first on obtaining a complete history of the alarm pattern before notifying the response team.","Silence the alarm and observe whether the patient’s mental status returns to baseline."],"feedback":["New confusion and diaphoresis with worsening perfusion indicate possible hemodynamic compromise. Immediate response and early VAD team communication support timely evaluation while the nurse continues assessment.","A detailed history may help later, but it should not delay escalation when the patient has signs of deterioration and a critical alarm.","Silencing the alarm does not address the patient’s worsening condition and may obscure an important warning. Reassessment and team escalation are needed."]},{"stem":"A patient with an LVAD presents after a fall and now has a severe headache and new difficulty following commands. The patient takes anticoagulation, and a family member says the pump sounds normal. Which nursing action is most appropriate?","options":["Treat the neurologic changes as urgent, activate local emergency procedures, and promptly communicate the findings and anticoagulation history to the treating and VAD teams.","Defer escalation because a normal pump sound makes a serious LVAD-associated complication unlikely.","Prioritize a detailed driveline-site inspection before reporting the new neurologic findings."],"feedback":["Headache and altered mental status in an anticoagulated LVAD patient require urgent evaluation for a cerebrovascular event, including possible intracranial bleeding. A normal pump sound does not exclude this complication.","The expected pump hum is useful when assessing device function but does not rule out stroke or intracranial hemorrhage. The neurologic changes warrant prompt escalation.","Driveline inspection can be part of assessment, but it should not delay reporting acute neurologic changes after a fall in an anticoagulated patient."]},{"stem":"A patient with an LVAD is awake, conversing normally, and has warm extremities with prompt capillary refill, but no radial pulse is palpable. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s next assessment?","options":["Minimal pulsatility can occur with continuous-flow support; assess perfusion and obtain blood pressure or MAP by the approved method rather than treating the absent pulse alone as arrest.","An absent radial pulse confirms that the pump has stopped, even though the patient is alert and well perfused.","Normal conversation and warm extremities make further blood pressure and device assessment unnecessary."],"feedback":["Continuous-flow LVADs may produce little or no palpable pulse, and native cardiac contribution varies. The patient’s perfusion and an appropriate blood pressure or MAP assessment provide more useful information.","An absent pulse alone does not establish pump stoppage or cardiac arrest in a patient with continuous-flow support. The patient still needs assessment of perfusion and device status.","These findings are reassuring but do not replace blood pressure or MAP measurement and device assessment. Continue evaluation and follow local procedures."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Left Ventricular Assist Device Management in the Emergency ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6123099/"},{"title":"Continuous&#x02010;flow left ventricular assist devices: Management in the emergency department","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7493560/pdf/EMP2-1-362.pdf"}],"frames":[{"label":"Low flow after diarrhea","detail":"Diarrhea and a dry appearance may reduce blood reaching the left ventricle and impair filling of the LVAD pump."},{"label":"Alarm with dry appearance","detail":"A low-flow alarm with weakness and apparent dryness raises concern for reduced filling, but the alarm alone does not identify its cause."},{"label":"Contact the VAD team","detail":"Assess perfusion and device connections, report the alarm and findings, and contact the VAD coordinator or advanced heart failure team promptly."},{"label":"Recheck flow and perfusion","detail":"Reassess mental status, perfusion, alarm status, and displayed parameters; promptly report persistent low flow or worsening perfusion."}]},{"id":"s105","unit":"resp","title":"Tracheostomy emergencies","objective":"Recognize a possible tracheostomy emergency and prioritize oxygenation, early team activation, and reassessment within the local emergency pathway.","concept":"A tracheostomy provides a direct route into the trachea, but airflow depends on a correctly positioned, patent tube and an open route for ventilation. A blocked inner cannula or displaced tube can interrupt oxygen delivery; displacement may also direct air into surrounding tissue. Emergency care therefore prioritizes oxygenation, early waveform capnography, and distinguishing a patient with a usable upper airway from a laryngectomy patient, whose airway is through the stoma.","recognize":"Suspect an emergency when a patient with a tracheostomy develops new difficulty with breathing or ventilation, or when tube displacement or blockage is suspected. A blocked inner cannula is one possible cause; a displaced tube may no longer provide a reliable tracheal route. Check the bedside airway information and clarify whether the patient has a patent upper airway or a laryngectomy.","respond":"Activate the emergency response and airway team, prioritize oxygenation, and use the displayed local pathway. When an upper airway may be patent, oxygen is applied to both potential airways; for a laryngectomy, follow its separate pathway. Waveform capnography is an early assessment tool. Do not trial ventilation through a potentially displaced tube; suction is attempted only after a potentially blocked inner cannula is removed, according to the pathway.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess oxygenation, breathing, waveform capnography, and whether the tube or inner cannula remains obstructed or displaced. Communicate changes and the response to oxygenation measures. Persistent or worsening respiratory compromise, or continuing concern that the tube is blocked or displaced, warrants immediate escalation to the airway team.","trap":"A visible tracheostomy tube is not automatically a dependable airway. Think of it as a route that still needs checking: capnography helps confirm tracheal placement but does not prove ideal position, and attempting ventilation through a suspected displaced tube can cause harm.","caseStory":"An adult with a tracheostomy becomes acutely difficult to ventilate shortly after being moved in bed. The tube appears to have shifted, and the waveform capnography changes. The nurse must decide whether to keep trying ventilation through the tube or activate the emergency pathway and prioritize oxygenation.","caseReason":"Displacement is a recognized tracheostomy emergency, and movement is a common context for tube-related incidents. The changed waveform supports urgent assessment but does not alone establish the tube's exact position. The nurse should activate the team, prioritize oxygenation under the local pathway, and avoid trial ventilation through a potentially displaced tube.","teachback":"If a tracheostomy patient deteriorates after a move, what would you assess and communicate first, and what would you avoid doing while the airway team responds?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult with a tracheostomy develops sudden respiratory difficulty during a bed transfer. The tube appears to have shifted outward, and the waveform capnography changes. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's immediate response?","options":["The tube may be displaced or obstructed, so activate the emergency pathway and prioritize oxygenation rather than trialing ventilation through it.","The change most likely reflects a harmless monitor variation, so continue routine care while observing the patient.","The tube is confirmed to be correctly positioned because it remains visible, so focus only on checking the ventilator settings."],"feedback":["Correct. Displacement is a common tracheostomy-related emergency, and capnography is an early assessment tool but does not establish ideal tube position. The response should prioritize oxygenation and avoid trial ventilation through a potentially displaced tube.","A waveform change during acute respiratory difficulty should not be dismissed as a monitor issue without assessment. The scenario includes possible displacement, which warrants urgent team activation and oxygenation priorities.","A visible tube does not confirm that it remains in the trachea or is correctly positioned. Capnography also cannot, by itself, verify ideal position."]},{"stem":"A patient with a tracheostomy has increasing difficulty breathing, and the team suspects a blocked inner cannula. The patient has a potentially patent upper airway. Which nursing response best prioritizes care while following the emergency pathway?","options":["Activate the airway response, prioritize oxygenation to both potential airways, and follow the pathway before suctioning.","Begin suctioning through the inner cannula immediately, then call for help if the obstruction remains.","Attempt ventilation through the tracheostomy tube to test whether it is patent, then decide whether oxygen is needed."],"feedback":["Correct. The referenced emergency principles prioritize oxygenation and applying oxygen to both potential airways when the upper airway may be patent. Suction is attempted only after a potentially blocked inner cannula is removed, in line with the pathway.","Suction should only be attempted after removing a potentially blocked inner cannula. The airway response and oxygenation priorities should not be delayed.","The emergency principles advise against trials of ventilation through a potentially displaced or obstructed tube. Oxygenation and team activation take priority."]},{"stem":"A patient with a laryngectomy becomes acutely breathless during recovery. The patient has a tube at the neck, and the nurse is preparing to follow the usual tracheostomy response for a patient with a patent upper airway. What is the best adjustment?","options":["Activate the laryngectomy-specific pathway and prioritize oxygenation through the stoma, recognizing that conventional upper-airway steps do not apply.","Use the usual patent-upper-airway pathway and apply oxygen to the mouth, nose, and stoma in the same way.","Wait to clarify the patient's airway history before activating a response or starting oxygenation measures."],"feedback":["Correct. A laryngectomy patient has a separate emergency pathway without the conventional upper-airway steps. The response should prioritize oxygenation and use the stoma route.","The general pathway for a potentially patent upper airway is not interchangeable with the laryngectomy pathway. Laryngectomy patients do not have the conventional upper-airway route.","Airway emergency response and oxygenation should not be delayed while awaiting clarification. The laryngectomy-specific pathway is the relevant one in this scenario."]},{"stem":"A patient with a recently placed tracheostomy has a waveform capnography trace, but the nurse notices an intermittent cuff leak and persistent concern about tube position. Which interpretation is safest?","options":["The trace does not prove ideal tube position; report the leak and concern promptly for assessment by the airway team.","The trace confirms correct tube position, so the intermittent leak can be treated as unrelated to the airway.","The leak alone proves the tube is displaced, so the nurse should independently reposition it before reassessment."],"feedback":["Correct. Capnography helps confirm that the tube is in the trachea, but it does not ensure appropriate position. An intermittent cuff leak can indicate a poorly sized or malpositioned tube and warrants team assessment.","A capnography trace does not establish that the tube is appropriately positioned. An intermittent cuff leak is a reason to consider malposition or tube sizing.","An intermittent leak may suggest a problem but does not, by itself, prove displacement. Tube position assessment and corrective procedures belong with the airway team under the relevant protocol."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Airway Establishment and Control - Critical Care Medicine ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-arrest/airway-establishment-and-control?qt=tracheostomy"},{"title":"Update on management of tracheostomy","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7807977/pdf/main.pdf"}],"frames":[{"label":"Tube shifts after movement","detail":"A tracheostomy tube that shifts during repositioning may no longer provide a reliable route for ventilation."},{"label":"Changed capnography waveform","detail":"New difficulty ventilating with suspected displacement and a changed waveform capnography tracing signals an airway emergency."},{"label":"Activate airway pathway","detail":"Activate the emergency response and airway team, prioritize oxygenation, and follow the local pathway rather than trialing ventilation through a potentially displaced tube."},{"label":"Reassess oxygenation","detail":"Reassess oxygenation, breathing, and waveform capnography; persistent respiratory compromise or concern about displacement warrants immediate airway-team escalation."}]},{"id":"s106","unit":"resp","title":"ARDS proning and pressure protection","objective":"Explain how prone positioning can protect the lungs in ARDS and identify nursing actions and findings that support safe team-based care.","concept":"In ARDS, prone positioning can make lung inflation and ventilation more even, improve ventilation-perfusion matching, and reduce regional stress and strain. These effects help explain why early, prolonged proning is an important lung-protective strategy for selected intubated patients with moderate-to-severe ARDS, not simply a way to raise oxygen levels. Oxygenation often improves, but the broader aim is to limit ventilator-related lung injury.","recognize":"Consider proning in an intubated patient with moderate-to-severe ARDS despite optimized ventilation, in line with the care plan and local guidance. Better oxygenation after turning is a common response, but does not by itself prove lung protection or exclude worsening illness. Notice trends in oxygenation, respiratory mechanics, hemodynamics, skin, and facial swelling; distinguish these from a single reassuring saturation.","respond":"Coordinate with the interprofessional team and follow orders and local turning protocols; repositioning requires trained caregivers. Before and during the maneuver, communicate concerns about the airway, lines, stability, and pressure areas, and help protect exposed skin and the face. Do not independently change ventilator settings or treat improved oxygenation as a reason to stop prescribed monitoring or support.","reassess":"After positioning, reassess oxygenation alongside respiratory mechanics, hemodynamics, airway and line security, skin pressure areas, and facial swelling. Report promptly any worsening oxygenation, unstable hemodynamics, airway or line concern, or new pressure injury; an improved saturation alone should not end surveillance.","trap":"A better oxygen number is a window into gas exchange, not a full report on lung stress or overall safety. Think of proning as evening out uneven load across the lung, rather than simply inflating every collapsed region without any tradeoff.","caseStory":"An intubated adult with ARDS remains hypoxemic despite optimized ventilation, and the team plans a prone session. After turning, oxygenation improves, but the nurse notices new facial swelling and an area of persistent pressure on the cheek. The decision is whether the oxygen response is enough to continue routine observation or whether the new findings need attention.","caseReason":"The oxygenation improvement is consistent with a common effect of proning, but it does not resolve the pressure concerns. Pressure sores and facial edema are recognized adverse effects, so the nurse should promptly communicate the findings and follow the team protocol to protect the affected areas while continuing broader reassessment.","teachback":"After a patient is placed prone and oxygenation improves, what other findings would you reassess before concluding that the intervention is going well?","questions":[{"stem":"An intubated adult with moderate-to-severe ARDS is placed prone as part of the care plan. Oxygenation improves, while the team notes a change in airway pressure. Which interpretation best supports pressure-protective nursing care?","options":["Proning can improve gas exchange and make lung stress and strain more even, so assess the full clinical picture rather than interpreting either change alone.","Improved oxygenation establishes that regional lung stress has fallen, so the pressure change is unlikely to need further review.","A change in airway pressure means the lungs are necessarily more overdistended, so the prone session should be stopped."],"feedback":["Correct. Proning commonly improves oxygenation and can reduce uneven stress and strain, but a single gas-exchange or pressure finding does not establish the whole response.","This overreads oxygenation. Better oxygen levels are common, but do not alone prove reduced injury or make other findings irrelevant.","This treats airway pressure as a direct measure of lung stress in every position. Chest wall mechanics can change when prone, so report and assess the finding in context rather than assuming its meaning."]},{"stem":"A team is preparing to prone an intubated adult with ARDS. The patient has an endotracheal tube, vascular lines, and several areas at risk of pressure injury. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Coordinate with the trained team under the turning protocol, communicate airway and line concerns, and plan ongoing pressure-area checks.","Proceed with the turn as soon as oxygenation is reviewed, then assess the airway and pressure areas after the patient is positioned.","Ask one available colleague to assist with the turn, since improved oxygenation is the main safety measure during repositioning."],"feedback":["Correct. Turning requires a skilled team, and airway, line, and pressure-area concerns need coordinated attention before and during the maneuver.","This delays attention to airway, line, and pressure risks until after the turn, rather than coordinating safeguards with the team.","This underestimates the staffing and monitoring needs of a complex turn and mistakes oxygenation for a complete safety assessment."]},{"stem":"A non-intubated adult receiving high-flow nasal oxygen is awake and willing to try prone positioning under the unit protocol. Oxygenation improves, but the patient reports increasing difficulty sustaining the position. Which nursing response is most appropriate?","options":["Assess respiratory effort and tolerance, communicate the change, and follow the team plan without letting the oxygen response delay needed escalation.","Encourage the patient to remain prone because improved oxygenation shows the intervention is preventing respiratory deterioration.","Treat awake proning as equivalent to intubated proning and continue it regardless of the patient's ability to tolerate the position."],"feedback":["Correct. Awake proning may improve oxygenation, but its effect on respiratory effort and clinical outcomes is uncertain; tolerance and the overall clinical course matter.","This assumes oxygenation improvement guarantees prevention of deterioration. It also overlooks the reported difficulty and the need for reassessment.","This incorrectly equates evidence and application in awake and intubated patients. Awake proning depends on patient tolerance and should remain part of team-guided care."]},{"stem":"During a prone session, an intubated adult's oxygen saturation rises. On reassessment, the nurse finds persistent pressure on the face and new facial swelling, while other observations are unchanged. What is the best next action?","options":["Report the pressure and swelling, follow the local protocol to protect the affected areas, and continue assessing the patient beyond oxygen saturation.","Continue routine observation without reporting the findings because stable observations and improved oxygenation outweigh localized changes.","Conclude that the prone position has caused worsening lung injury and request that the team end the session immediately."],"feedback":["Correct. Facial edema and pressure injury are recognized concerns during proning; communicate them and follow the protocol while continuing comprehensive reassessment.","This dismisses recognized prone-position complications and gives too much weight to a reassuring oxygen reading.","This draws a lung-injury conclusion from skin and facial findings that do not establish that outcome. The findings warrant communication and protocol-based action, not an unsupported inference."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Prone positioning in ARDS - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13562319/"},{"title":"Prone position in ARDS patients: why, when, how and for whom","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7652705/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Evening Lung Inflation","detail":"Proning can distribute lung inflation and ventilation more evenly, improving ventilation-perfusion matching and reducing regional stress beyond simply raising oxygen levels."},{"label":"Persistent Cheek Pressure","detail":"Even after oxygenation improves, new facial swelling and persistent cheek pressure remain concerning adverse effects, not reassurance from saturation alone."},{"label":"Protect During Proning","detail":"Promptly communicate pressure findings to the team, protect the face and skin, and follow trained-caregiver turning protocols and prescribed monitoring."},{"label":"Broader Reassessment","detail":"Continue checking oxygenation, respiratory mechanics, hemodynamics, airway and line security, skin pressure areas, and facial swelling; report worsening findings promptly."}]},{"id":"s107","unit":"resp","title":"Pleural drainage after thoracic surgery","objective":"Use patient assessment and drainage-system cues to recognize concerns and prioritize safe nursing care after thoracic surgery.","concept":"After thoracic surgery, pleural drainage removes air or fluid so the lung can re-expand and normal negative-pressure relationships can recover. The closed drainage unit collects output, while its water seal lets air leave without flowing back into the pleural space; prescribed suction may support drainage. Nurses connect system behavior with respiratory status rather than interpreting a chamber in isolation, because changes in the patient and equipment can produce similar signs.","recognize":"Compare breathing, oxygen saturation, blood pressure, pain, and lung sounds with baseline, and inspect the insertion dressing, tubing, and unit. Water-seal movement with respiration can reflect patency; continuous bubbling there may signal an air leak. New dyspnea, worsening oxygenation, hypotension, altered tracheal alignment, bleeding, or crepitus merits prompt assessment and team communication. A quiet chamber alone does not establish obstruction.","respond":"Prioritize the patient: assess respiratory status and vital signs, and promptly notify the responsible clinician of deterioration or a suspected air leak. Check that the drainage unit remains upright below chest level, the tubing and dressing appear intact, and suction matches the current order. Use local policy and team direction for troubleshooting; do not independently change suction or perform invasive correction.","reassess":"After an authorized adjustment or other nursing intervention, repeat the respiratory assessment and vital signs, compare oxygenation and symptoms with baseline, and inspect the dressing, tubing, water seal, suction setup, and drainage trend. Escalate promptly for persistent or worsening dyspnea, deteriorating oxygenation or blood pressure, new crepitus or bleeding, or a continuing suspected leak.","trap":"Do not treat every movement or bubble as the same message: water-seal tidaling with breaths can indicate patency, whereas continuous bubbling there can suggest an air leak. Think of the water seal as a one-way gate, not a dashboard that diagnoses the patient; interpret it alongside respiratory findings and the entire system.","caseStory":"An adult returns from thoracic surgery with a chest tube connected to a closed drainage unit. The unit is upright below the chest and ordered suction is present; the nurse notices continuous water-seal bubbling, new shortness of breath, and lower oxygen saturation than at baseline. The decision is whether to simply document the chamber or promptly assess and notify the clinical team.","caseReason":"Continuous water-seal bubbling can indicate an air leak, while new dyspnea and declining oxygenation make a patient change more concerning than an isolated chamber observation. The nurse should promptly reassess respiratory status and system integrity, maintain the ordered setup, and communicate findings for team-directed action rather than independently adjusting suction.","teachback":"When a drainage chamber changes, how will you use the patient’s respiratory assessment and the specific chamber behavior together to decide whether to continue monitoring or escalate?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult recovering from thoracic surgery is comfortable, has vital signs and oxygen saturation consistent with baseline, and has water-seal movement that rises and falls with breathing. There is no continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["Respiratory-linked water-seal movement can indicate a patent tube, and the stable assessment supports continued monitoring.","Any water-seal movement indicates an air leak, so the nurse should urgently change the suction setting.","The absence of continuous water-seal bubbling indicates that the tube is obstructed and needs immediate correction."],"feedback":["Tidaling with respiration can indicate patency, and the stable patient findings support continued assessment rather than an urgent intervention.","Water-seal tidaling is not itself an air-leak finding; continuous bubbling in that chamber may suggest a leak.","The supplied material does not establish that the absence of continuous bubbling means obstruction; interpret the system with the patient’s condition."]},{"stem":"An adult with a chest tube after thoracic surgery develops new dyspnea and a lower oxygen saturation than at baseline. The nurse observes continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber. What is the best immediate nursing response?","options":["Reassess respiratory status and vital signs, check the visible system setup, and promptly notify the responsible clinician.","Increase the suction setting independently, then wait to see whether the bubbling and dyspnea resolve.","Document the bubbling and repeat the assessment at the next scheduled observation because bubbling is expected."],"feedback":["The patient’s deterioration and continuous water-seal bubbling warrant prompt assessment, a check of the setup, and team communication.","Suction should match the current order; the nurse should not independently change it, especially when the patient is deteriorating.","Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber can indicate an air leak, and new dyspnea with lower oxygenation should not wait for routine reassessment."]},{"stem":"A stable adult has mediastinal drainage after cardiac surgery. The closed drainage unit is found tilted on the bed at chest level, and the tubing remains connected. Which nursing action best supports safe drainage-system function?","options":["Place the unit upright on a stable support below the patient’s chest, then reassess the patient and drainage system.","Leave the unit where it is because an intact connection is sufficient to support drainage.","Raise the unit above the chest to increase drainage, then reassess the patient’s breathing."],"feedback":["The drainage system should remain upright and below the chest to facilitate drainage; reassessment helps identify any effect of the change.","Connection alone is not enough; the system’s position is also important for safe drainage.","The supplied material supports placing the unit below the chest, not above it."]},{"stem":"After confirming that an adult’s wet-suction drainage unit is set according to the current order, the nurse sees gentle bubbling in the suction-control chamber. The patient is comfortable and stable, with no continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber. Which interpretation and next step are most appropriate?","options":["Gentle bubbling in the suction-control chamber can be expected; continue monitoring the patient and system.","Bubbling in any chamber confirms a pleural air leak; notify the clinician and prepare to change suction.","The absence of water-seal bubbling indicates failed suction; independently increase the wall suction."],"feedback":["Gentle bubbling is expected in a wet-suction control chamber; the stable assessment and water-seal finding support continued monitoring.","The chamber matters: continuous bubbling in the water seal may indicate an air leak, but gentle suction-control bubbling can be expected.","The suction should remain at the prescribed setting, and absence of water-seal bubbling does not establish failed suction."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Care of a Chest Tube - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556088/"},{"title":"Chapter 6 Manage Chest Tube Drainage Systems - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594490/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Closed Pleural Drainage","detail":"A chest tube removes air or fluid, while its water seal lets air leave without flowing back into the pleural space."},{"label":"Bubbling With Dyspnea","detail":"Continuous water-seal bubbling with new shortness of breath and lower-than-baseline oxygen saturation raises concern for an air leak."},{"label":"Notify And Preserve Setup","detail":"Promptly assess respiratory status and notify the clinical team; keep the unit below chest level and suction matched to the order."},{"label":"Trend Patient And System","detail":"Recheck breathing, vital signs, oxygenation, tubing, water seal, and drainage; escalate persistent dyspnea, worsening oxygenation, hypotension, bleeding, or crepitus."}]},{"id":"s108","unit":"resp","title":"ECMO support and surveillance","objective":"Use ECMO configuration and bedside trends to recognize respiratory or circulatory support concerns, communicate priorities, and reassess within nursing scope and local protocols.","concept":"ECMO temporarily moves blood through a pump and oxygenator to support gas exchange, circulation, or both while the heart or lungs recover. Venovenous support returns blood to the venous circulation and provides respiratory support, so it depends on the heart to circulate blood. Venoarterial support returns blood to the arterial circulation and supports both cardiac and respiratory function. Knowing the configuration helps nurses interpret oxygenation, perfusion, and circuit changes.","recognize":"Interpret findings in relation to the ECMO mode and the patient’s condition. With femoral arterial venoarterial support, a cool or pale cannulated-side limb raises concern for impaired limb perfusion. In venoarterial support, better oxygenation in the lower body than in the head or upper extremities can suggest differential hypoxia. Falling post-oxygenator oxygen, rising pressure gradient, or increasing gas flow can signal oxygenator problems.","respond":"Prioritize the patient, then promptly communicate the ECMO mode, observed changes, trends, and relevant circuit or cannulation findings to the ECMO and critical-care team. Perform focused assessments and follow local protocols and orders, including ordered monitoring of perfusion and anticoagulation. Do not independently alter pump settings, gas flow, anticoagulation, or cannula position; escalate urgent changes without waiting for routine rounds.","reassess":"After a nursing action, reassess the patient’s oxygenation, work of breathing, hemodynamic status, and relevant limb perfusion, and compare circuit flow and oxygenator observations with prior trends. Confirm whether the change is improving or persists. Escalate promptly for worsening oxygenation or perfusion, concerning circuit trends, or new neurologic changes.","trap":"A good oxygenator reading does not guarantee that every part of the patient is receiving well-oxygenated blood, especially with venoarterial support. Think of ECMO as a support route that joins the patient’s own circulation: where the streams meet can affect which regions receive more oxygenated blood.","caseStory":"A fictional adult receiving femoral venoarterial ECMO has a newly cool, pale leg on the cannulated side, while the other leg remains warm. The patient’s overall blood pressure has not changed. The nurse must decide whether this is a routine temperature variation or a perfusion concern requiring prompt communication.","caseReason":"The change is localized to the limb on the side of arterial cannulation, which raises concern for impaired limb perfusion, a recognized complication of femoral venoarterial ECMO. A stable overall blood pressure does not rule out a local problem. The nurse should promptly report the finding and assess perfusion according to local protocol rather than adjust the circuit independently.","teachback":"When oxygenation differs between the upper and lower body during femoral venoarterial ECMO, what would you assess and communicate before deciding that support is adequate?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult on venovenous ECMO for severe respiratory failure has a falling post-oxygenator oxygen level, a rising pressure gradient across the oxygenator, and an increasing need for fresh gas flow. Which interpretation and nursing response best fit this pattern?","options":["Suspect oxygenator dysfunction, report the trend promptly to the ECMO team, and continue close observation under local protocol.","Assume worsening native lung disease, defer circuit communication, and focus only on the ventilator trend.","Treat the pressure change as proof of inadequate anticoagulation and independently change the anticoagulation plan."],"feedback":["These combined trends are described as warning signs of oxygenator failure, often related to clot formation. Prompt team communication is appropriate; the nurse should not independently change circuit or anticoagulation settings.","Native lung disease can affect patient oxygenation, but it does not explain away concerning post-oxygenator and pressure-gradient trends. Circuit concerns warrant communication as well as patient assessment.","Circuit thrombosis can be associated with inadequate anticoagulation, but these findings do not establish its cause or authorize an independent treatment change. Report the observations for team assessment."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving femoral venoarterial ECMO develops a cool, pale foot on the cannulated side, and the pulse there is weaker than on the other side. The patient’s blood pressure is unchanged. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly report possible impaired limb perfusion and assess the extremity according to local protocol.","Reassure the team that stable blood pressure rules out a cannulation-related perfusion problem.","Increase ECMO flow independently to improve blood supply to the affected limb."],"feedback":["Femoral arterial cannulation carries a risk of limb ischemia, and the localized findings warrant prompt assessment and communication. A stable overall blood pressure does not exclude a regional perfusion problem.","Systemic blood pressure does not establish that perfusion is adequate in a particular limb. The unilateral change should be reported and assessed.","Changing ECMO flow is not an independent nursing action and may not address the cause of localized ischemia. Escalate the finding for assessment by the ECMO team."]},{"stem":"A patient on femoral venoarterial ECMO for cardiac failure has better oxygenation readings in the lower body than at the right hand, while the native heart continues to eject blood and lung function remains poor. Which concern should the nurse communicate?","options":["Possible differential hypoxia, with less oxygenated native blood reaching the upper body than ECMO blood reaching the lower body.","Likely venovenous recirculation, because oxygenated blood is returning to the venous drainage cannula.","Definite oxygenator membrane failure, because any difference between upper- and lower-body readings proves the membrane is not working."],"feedback":["In femoral venoarterial ECMO, poorly oxygenated blood ejected by the native heart can supply the brain and upper extremities while ECMO blood supplies the lower body. The regional difference should be communicated for team assessment.","Recirculation is a concern with venovenous cannulation when returned oxygenated blood is drawn back into the drainage cannula. This case describes venoarterial support and a regional oxygenation pattern.","A regional difference does not by itself prove oxygenator failure. The described pattern is consistent with differential hypoxia, so communicate the findings rather than conclude a specific circuit failure."]},{"stem":"After a team-directed repositioning of a patient on venovenous ECMO, oxygen saturation falls and circuit flow fluctuates. Post-oxygenator oxygen remains similar to the prior reading. Which reassessment is most useful?","options":["Reassess the patient’s oxygenation and circuit flow, observe cannula position without manipulating it, and promptly report persistent changes.","Conclude that the oxygenator is failing from the patient’s saturation alone and request an immediate circuit change.","Treat the stable post-oxygenator oxygen as proof that ECMO-related causes are excluded and stop monitoring the flow change."],"feedback":["Movement can affect flow, and cannula malposition can contribute to recirculation during venovenous support. Reassessing the patient and circuit while reporting persistent changes helps the team evaluate the cause without unsafe cannula manipulation.","A lower patient saturation with a similar post-oxygenator reading does not establish oxygenator failure. The flow change and possible movement-related or cannula-related issues still require assessment and communication.","A stable post-oxygenator reading does not rule out all ECMO-related causes of reduced patient oxygenation. Continue to assess the patient and circuit and report persistent flow or oxygenation changes."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation in Adults - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576426/"},{"title":"Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO): What We Need to Know","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9363689/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Femoral VA Support","detail":"Venoarterial ECMO returns blood to the arterial circulation, supporting heart and lungs; femoral arterial cannulation can threaten perfusion in that limb."},{"label":"Cool Cannulated Leg","detail":"A newly cool, pale leg on the cannulated side suggests impaired limb perfusion, even when overall blood pressure remains unchanged."},{"label":"Escalate Perfusion Change","detail":"Promptly communicate the limb change to the ECMO and critical-care team, and assess perfusion under local protocols without altering the circuit."},{"label":"Recheck Limb And Support","detail":"Reassess limb perfusion and patient status against prior findings; promptly escalate persistent or worsening perfusion changes or other concerning trends."}]},{"id":"s109","unit":"neuro","title":"Spinal surgery: new neurologic deficit","objective":"Recognize a possible postoperative spinal epidural hematoma when a new neurologic deficit develops and promptly escalate for urgent evaluation.","concept":"A postoperative spinal epidural hematoma can compress the spinal cord or nerve roots, causing pain and progressive neurologic dysfunction. Small amounts of blood at a surgical site may be clinically insignificant, so symptoms and examination changes matter; imaging helps determine whether a compressive hematoma is present. Neurologic recovery may be poorer when decompression is delayed, making early recognition and communication important.","recognize":"Be alert for new or worsening severe pain near the operative level, radiating pain, weakness, sensory loss, or bladder dysfunction. Weakness or sensory change may follow pain, but presentations vary and deterioration can occur after the immediate postoperative period. Other causes of postoperative dysfunction are possible, so do not assume the cause without urgent clinical evaluation and imaging.","respond":"Treat a new deficit or concerning progression as urgent: promptly notify the responsible surgical team, communicate the time and pattern of change, and follow local escalation and monitoring protocols. Perform and document focused neurologic observations within your role, including motor, sensory, pain, and bladder findings. Support timely imaging and treatment decisions by the team; do not dismiss symptoms as routine postoperative pain or wait for a complete paralysis.","reassess":"After escalation, repeat and document focused neurologic observations as directed, noting changes in strength, sensation, pain, and bladder function. Confirm that the responsible team has received the update and that evaluation is progressing. Any new or worsening weakness, sensory loss, severe pain, or bladder dysfunction warrants immediate escalation through the local pathway.","trap":"A postoperative scan may show blood that is not causing symptoms, but that does not make a new deficit safe to ignore. Think of symptoms as an alarm in a building: imaging helps locate the problem, while silencing the alarm without investigating risks missing a developing emergency.","caseStory":"A patient several hours after lumbar surgery reports suddenly worsening back and leg pain, then has weaker ankle movement than on the previous assessment. The nurse must decide whether to continue routine observation or promptly alert the surgical team and seek urgent evaluation.","caseReason":"The new pain and motor change are concerning for a compressive postoperative complication, including an epidural hematoma. The nurse should communicate the change promptly and support urgent evaluation and imaging; the team determines the diagnosis and need for decompression.","teachback":"If a postoperative patient has worsening pain followed by a new motor, sensory, or bladder change, what will you communicate and do first?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient returns from thoracic spine surgery with unchanged strength on the first check. Two hours later, the patient reports sharp pain at the surgical level and new tingling in both legs. What is the most appropriate interpretation and nursing action?","options":["The new symptoms may signal a compressive complication; promptly alert the surgical team and report the change for urgent evaluation.","The tingling is likely an expected anesthetic effect; continue routine checks and report it only if weakness appears.","The pain is most consistent with routine incisional discomfort; provide usual comfort measures and reassess at the next scheduled check."],"feedback":["Correct. New pain with a sensory change after spine surgery warrants prompt escalation because a postoperative epidural hematoma can cause neurologic deterioration.","This could delay recognition. The new sensory symptom is a change from baseline and should be communicated without waiting for motor weakness.","Postoperative pain can occur, but new pain paired with a neurologic change should not be treated as routine discomfort without urgent evaluation."]},{"stem":"After cervical decompression, a patient develops rapidly worsening arm weakness and reports that the neck pain has intensified. The patient is hemodynamically stable and asks to rest before another examination. What should the nurse prioritize?","options":["Promptly escalate the new deficit to the surgical team and follow the local urgent evaluation pathway.","Allow uninterrupted rest, then repeat the neurologic examination when the next routine assessment is due.","Give the prescribed comfort measure first and contact the team only if the weakness remains afterward."],"feedback":["Correct. New or worsening weakness after spine surgery is urgent even when the patient is otherwise stable; escalation should not wait for a routine check.","Waiting risks delaying evaluation of a potentially compressive cause of the deficit.","Comfort measures do not establish the cause of new weakness, and reassessment after treatment should not delay notifying the team."]},{"stem":"On the fifth day after a minimally invasive lumbar procedure, a patient develops new leg weakness and difficulty initiating urination. The patient had been walking earlier that day. Which response best applies the same safety principle in this different postoperative setting?","options":["Treat the new motor and bladder changes as urgent, notify the surgical team, and support prompt evaluation.","Continue observation because the patient was recently walking and the procedure was minimally invasive.","Encourage walking and fluids, then reassess before deciding whether the changes need to be reported."],"feedback":["Correct. Deterioration can be delayed, and new weakness with bladder dysfunction warrants prompt escalation regardless of earlier function or procedure type.","Earlier walking does not rule out a new complication, and minimally invasive surgery does not make new deficits safe to ignore.","These interventions do not evaluate the cause of the new neurologic and bladder changes and could delay needed assessment."]},{"stem":"A patient with new leg weakness after spine surgery receives an ordered analgesic. The pain score falls, but the nurse's repeat examination shows further decline in ankle movement. What is the best reassessment-based action?","options":["Escalate the worsening motor finding promptly; pain relief does not resolve the concern raised by progressive weakness.","Continue routine monitoring because improved pain suggests the underlying problem is resolving.","Document the lower pain score and defer another neurologic update unless bladder symptoms develop."],"feedback":["Correct. Progressive weakness is a concerning change even if pain improves, and it warrants immediate communication to the team.","Pain relief alone does not show that a compressive cause has resolved; the worsening motor finding requires escalation.","Bladder symptoms are not required before reporting worsening weakness, and documentation should accompany, not replace, communication."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Postoperative Spinal Epidural Hematoma: Risk Factor and ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2688151/"},{"title":"Perspective: Postoperative spinal epidural hematomas (pSEH ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10629307/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Possible Neural Compression","detail":"A postoperative epidural hematoma can compress the spinal cord or nerve roots, causing pain and progressive neurologic dysfunction."},{"label":"Pain Then Weakness","detail":"Suddenly worsening back and leg pain followed by weaker ankle movement is a concerning change from the previous neurologic assessment."},{"label":"Urgent Surgical Communication","detail":"Promptly notify the surgical team of the timing and pattern, document focused neurologic findings, and follow local escalation and monitoring protocols."},{"label":"Track Deficit Progression","detail":"Repeat focused observations as directed and confirm evaluation is progressing; immediately escalate worsening weakness, sensory loss, severe pain, or bladder dysfunction."}]},{"id":"s110","unit":"neuro","title":"ALS and ventilatory weakness","objective":"Recognize signs of ventilatory weakness in ALS, distinguish potential acute complications, and communicate changes promptly to the care team.","concept":"ALS progressively weakens the muscles used for breathing and coughing. Diaphragm weakness can make breathing harder during activity, at night, or while lying down, while a weaker cough impairs airway clearance. These changes can leave a person vulnerable to rapid respiratory deterioration. Aspiration and infection may also affect breathing, so a new respiratory change should be assessed rather than presumed to be ordinary disease progression.","recognize":"Watch for new or worsening shortness of breath, increased work of breathing, difficulty breathing when lying down or at night, and a cough that is less effective. Fever, crackles, or hypoxia can raise concern for aspiration pneumonia or infection. Acute breathlessness with limb swelling, chest pain, or a change in vital capacity beyond expected weakness warrants prompt assessment for thromboembolism rather than automatic attribution to ALS.","respond":"Prioritize a focused respiratory assessment and promptly report acute or worsening changes to the responsible clinician and respiratory care team. Follow local protocols and orders for positioning, monitoring, and respiratory support, and communicate baseline function, cough effectiveness, swallowing concerns, and the timing of change. Do not assume weakness alone explains deterioration or delay escalation while waiting for a witnessed choking event.","reassess":"After an intervention, reassess breathing effort, dyspnea, ability to cough and clear secretions, position-related symptoms, and relevant observations under local protocol. Compare findings with the person’s baseline and report persistent or worsening distress, impaired airway clearance, or new signs suggesting aspiration, infection, or thromboembolism.","trap":"A feeding tube is not an airtight barrier against aspiration; secretions or reflux can still enter the airway. Think of it as rerouting food, not closing every route to the lungs.","caseStory":"A person with ALS who usually sleeps comfortably reports new breathlessness when lying flat and has a weaker cough than usual. They have no fever and do not recall choking. The nurse must decide whether to treat this as expected weakness or promptly report a possible respiratory change.","caseReason":"Breathlessness when lying down and a weaker cough fit possible progression of ventilatory and airway-clearance weakness. The absence of fever or a witnessed choking episode does not establish the cause or rule out an urgent problem. The nurse should assess, compare with baseline, and promptly communicate the change for clinical evaluation.","teachback":"When a person with ALS develops new breathing difficulty, which findings would you assess and communicate to help the team distinguish ventilatory weakness from another acute complication?","questions":[{"stem":"A person with ALS reports that breathing has become harder at night and while lying flat. The nurse also notes a weaker cough than at the last assessment. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The pattern is concerning for respiratory muscle weakness with reduced airway clearance and needs prompt communication.","The pattern most strongly indicates aspiration pneumonia, even without other signs of infection or aspiration.","The pattern is most consistent with anxiety because symptoms occur at night and while lying down."],"feedback":["Correct. ALS-related diaphragm weakness can make breathing harder at night or when lying down, and a weaker cough can impair airway clearance.","Aspiration is possible in ALS, but these findings alone do not establish pneumonia. Assess for additional cues such as fever, crackles, or hypoxia and report the respiratory change.","Anxiety should not be presumed to explain new breathing difficulty in a person with ALS. The change in breathing and cough warrants assessment and communication."]},{"stem":"A person with ALS arrives with increasing work of breathing and difficulty clearing secretions. The nurse is considering whether to focus first on meal planning, mobility equipment, or the breathing change. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Perform a focused respiratory assessment and promptly alert the responsible clinician and respiratory care team.","Arrange a feeding tube discussion first because bulbar symptoms can lead to aspiration.","Request a mobility assessment first because weakness and falls are common in ALS."],"feedback":["Correct. Increasing work of breathing and poor secretion clearance require prompt respiratory assessment and team communication.","Feeding tube planning may be relevant to ongoing care, but it does not address the immediate respiratory change or eliminate aspiration risk.","Mobility assessment can help reduce falls, but the current breathing difficulty and impaired cough take priority."]},{"stem":"A person with ALS develops acute breathlessness and pleuritic chest pain. One lower leg is newly swollen and warm, and the breathing change seems greater than expected from their usual weakness. What should the nurse communicate as an important concern for evaluation?","options":["Possible thromboembolism, given the combination of acute respiratory symptoms and unilateral leg changes.","Expected ALS progression, because respiratory and limb weakness commonly worsen together.","Aspiration as the most likely explanation, even without swallowing symptoms or other supporting cues."],"feedback":["Correct. Limb swelling, warmth, and pain with acute breathlessness or pleuritic chest pain should prompt evaluation for DVT or PE in ALS.","ALS progression should not be used to explain away an acute change with possible thromboembolic cues. Communicate the pattern for prompt evaluation.","Aspiration can cause respiratory illness in ALS, but this presentation also contains specific cues that warrant consideration of thromboembolism."]},{"stem":"A person with ALS has a feeding tube and no witnessed choking episode, but now has increased work of breathing, fever, and crackles. Which reassessment and interpretation is most appropriate after notifying the care team?","options":["Continue assessing breathing and airway clearance; aspiration remains possible despite the feeding tube and no observed choking.","Treat the feeding tube and lack of witnessed choking as evidence that aspiration is unlikely, and focus reassessment on nutrition.","Interpret the fever and crackles as expected ventilatory muscle weakness and reassess only the person’s cough."],"feedback":["Correct. A feeding tube does not prevent aspiration of secretions or reflux, and the respiratory findings warrant continued assessment and clinical evaluation.","A feeding tube does not eliminate aspiration risk, and aspiration may occur without a witnessed event. The new respiratory findings should not be set aside.","Fever and crackles are not explained simply by ventilatory muscle weakness and can raise concern for aspiration pneumonia or infection."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Emergencies in Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13749253/"},{"title":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) | Health","url":"https://health.nih.gov/health-topics-a-z/als"}],"frames":[{"label":"Progressive Respiratory Weakness","detail":"ALS can weaken breathing and cough muscles, making breathing harder when lying down and reducing the ability to clear secretions."},{"label":"New Positional Breathlessness","detail":"New breathlessness when lying flat and a weaker-than-usual cough signal a respiratory change, even without fever or witnessed choking."},{"label":"Report Change Promptly","detail":"Assess breathing and cough, compare with baseline, and promptly report acute or worsening changes to the clinician and respiratory care team."},{"label":"Recheck Breathing And Cough","detail":"Reassess breathing effort, position-related symptoms, and secretion clearance; report persistent distress, impaired cough, or new signs of aspiration or infection."}]},{"id":"s111","unit":"renal","title":"CRRT interruptions and fluid balance","objective":"Explain how CRRT interruptions can make delivered therapy and fluid balance differ from the prescription, and communicate the discrepancy promptly to the care team.","concept":"CRRT removes solutes and fluid continuously, but the treatment actually delivered can differ from the prescribed plan when therapy is interrupted for procedures, circuit or filter changes, or equipment problems. The effluent prescription represents treatment intensity, while net fluid removal is a separate goal. Tracking both delivered therapy and the patient’s fluid balance helps the team judge whether solute and fluid goals are being met.","recognize":"Compare the prescribed plan with documented treatment time, interruptions, fluid removal, and cumulative intake and output. A positive balance despite a goal for net fluid removal may reflect missed treatment time or a change in the patient’s fluid inputs, not necessarily a failure of the prescription itself. Do not infer delivered therapy from the prescribed settings alone.","respond":"First assess the patient and verify the current treatment status and fluid balance in the record. Report the interruption, its duration if known, the prescribed goals, and the observed balance to the appropriate team members under local protocols. Follow unit procedures for notifications and documentation. Do not independently change CRRT settings or fluid removal targets.","reassess":"After the team responds, reassess treatment status, interruptions, intake and output, cumulative balance, and the patient’s clinical condition. Escalate if therapy remains interrupted, fluid balance continues to diverge from the plan, or the patient’s condition worsens.","trap":"A prescription is a plan, not proof that the treatment was delivered. Think of the prescribed CRRT settings as a route on a map: interruptions can change the journey, so verify the actual treatment and fluid balance rather than assuming the destination was reached.","caseStory":"During a shift, a patient receiving CRRT has a prescribed goal for net fluid removal, but the cumulative balance is positive. The record shows that treatment was paused for a procedure and later for a filter change. The patient’s condition is being monitored, and the nurse must decide what information to bring to the team.","caseReason":"The positive balance does not by itself show whether the prescription was inappropriate or whether the delivered treatment differed from the plan. The documented interruptions are relevant because downtime can reduce delivered CRRT. The nurse should verify treatment and fluid-balance records, assess the patient, and communicate the discrepancy without independently changing settings.","teachback":"When the prescribed fluid-removal goal and the patient’s recorded cumulative balance do not agree, what information would you verify and communicate before assuming the plan was delivered?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient on CRRT has a prescribed goal for net fluid removal. At handoff, the cumulative balance is positive, and the record shows two treatment interruptions. Which interpretation best fits these findings?","options":["The delivered treatment may have differed from the prescription because interruptions reduced treatment time, so verify the record and report the discrepancy.","The positive balance confirms that the prescribed fluid-removal goal was not appropriate for the patient.","The prescribed CRRT settings show that the fluid-removal goal was achieved despite the recorded balance."],"feedback":["Correct. CRRT downtime can make delivered therapy differ from the prescribed plan, so the interruptions and actual fluid balance both need review.","This conclusion is not established by a positive balance alone. The team needs to consider delivered treatment and fluid inputs before evaluating the plan.","A prescription does not establish what was actually delivered or whether the intended fluid balance was reached."]},{"stem":"A patient’s CRRT circuit is temporarily unavailable while the team addresses a filter problem. The patient remains under close observation, and the current fluid balance is already different from the planned goal. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Assess the patient, verify and document the interruption and current balance, and promptly notify the appropriate team members under local protocol.","Independently increase the fluid-removal setting after CRRT resumes to compensate for the time off treatment.","Wait until the end of the shift to report the interruption, since the prescribed settings remain documented."],"feedback":["Correct. The team needs timely information about the interruption, actual treatment status, and fluid balance to reassess the plan.","Changing the setting independently is outside the nursing role described here and may not account for the patient’s current condition or treatment goals.","Delaying communication can obscure how downtime affected delivered therapy and the patient’s balance."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving CRRT is taken for diagnostic imaging, and treatment is paused during transport. On return, the prescribed treatment plan is unchanged, but the patient’s intake and output have also changed. What is the best next step?","options":["Reconcile the treatment interruption with the updated intake and output, assess the patient, and communicate the actual balance and treatment status to the team.","Assume the original fluid-removal goal remains achieved because the prescription was not changed during imaging.","Treat the interruption as the only explanation for the balance and disregard the patient’s recorded fluid inputs."],"feedback":["Correct. Both CRRT downtime and changing fluid inputs can affect the balance, so the nurse should verify and communicate the complete picture.","The unchanged prescription does not show that therapy was delivered during the interruption or that the goal was achieved.","The interruption matters, but fluid balance also reflects fluid inputs and outputs; ignoring them could mislead the team."]},{"stem":"A flowsheet shows the prescribed effluent treatment, while the patient’s cumulative fluid balance remains positive. The chart also shows periods when CRRT was off. Which finding best helps distinguish the treatment plan from the fluid-removal outcome?","options":["Review actual treatment time and net fluid removal alongside intake and output, because the effluent prescription alone does not establish the fluid balance achieved.","Use the effluent prescription as evidence that the patient should have a negative cumulative balance.","Use the positive balance alone to conclude that CRRT did not provide solute clearance."],"feedback":["Correct. Effluent treatment intensity and net fluid removal are distinct considerations, and interruptions can affect delivered therapy; intake and output also inform the balance.","The effluent prescription does not by itself establish net fluid removal or the resulting cumulative balance.","A positive fluid balance does not establish whether solute clearance occurred. The record of actual treatment and interruptions is needed to interpret delivery."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Drug Dosing During Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy in Critically Ill Patients: A Practical Narrative Review","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13708363/"},{"title":"How To Prescribe And Troubleshoot Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy: A Case-Based Review","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8741005"}],"frames":[{"label":"Treatment downtime","detail":"CRRT is prescribed continuously, but procedures and filter changes can reduce delivered treatment time."},{"label":"Positive fluid balance","detail":"Despite a net-removal goal, cumulative balance is positive after pauses for a procedure and filter change."},{"label":"Communicate discrepancy","detail":"Verify current treatment and intake-output records, then report downtime, prescribed goals, and observed balance under local protocol."},{"label":"Recheck delivered course","detail":"Reassess treatment status, cumulative balance, and clinical condition; escalate if interruption persists or balance diverges."}]},{"id":"s112","unit":"gi","title":"Mesenteric ischemia","objective":"Recognize early acute mesenteric ischemia cues and promptly escalate concern while supporting team-directed evaluation and care.","concept":"Acute mesenteric ischemia occurs when arterial inflow, venous outflow, or overall splanchnic perfusion is disrupted, causing intestinal oxygen deprivation. Mucosal injury weakens the barrier and releases bacteria, toxins, and vasoactive mediators, potentially driving systemic inflammation and organ failure before bowel is fully infarcted. Because early injury can progress rapidly and tests may lag behind clinical deterioration, recognizing the pattern and communicating concern promptly are central nursing contributions.","recognize":"Suspect it when severe, often abrupt abdominal pain contrasts with a soft abdomen and little tenderness, especially in an older adult or someone with atrial fibrillation, atherosclerosis, heart failure, or low-flow illness. Gradual pain may occur with venous thrombosis, while prior post-meal discomfort can precede arterial thrombosis. Guarding, rigidity, absent bowel sounds, blood in stool, or shock suggest later injury; normal early tests do not exclude it.","respond":"Treat the presentation as time-critical and promptly alert the responsible clinician and surgical team under local escalation pathways. Support ordered resuscitation, maintain access and monitoring, and prepare for urgent CT angiography or angiography when directed; clear peritoneal signs require immediate surgical evaluation. Do not delay escalation while waiting for lactate, other serum markers, or late imaging signs, and do not initiate medications or procedures independently.","reassess":"After ordered fluids and other team-directed measures, trend pain, abdominal tenderness and distention, bowel sounds, vital signs, perfusion, mental status, and available laboratory results. Escalate immediately for new guarding, rigidity, worsening pain, hemodynamic deterioration, or other peritoneal signs, even if earlier tests were unrevealing.","trap":"Do not use a soft abdomen or an initially normal lactate as a safety signal; early mesenteric ischemia can be like a fire behind a closed door, with severe pain before visible abdominal damage. Waiting for peritonitis or late radiographic signs can mean waiting until infarction has advanced.","caseStory":"A 76-year-old with atrial fibrillation develops sudden severe central abdominal pain; the abdomen is soft with minimal tenderness, and the initial lactate is not elevated. While awaiting review, the patient reports worsening pain, and the nurse must decide whether to continue routine observation or urgently escalate concern for bowel ischemia.","caseReason":"Sudden pain with few examination findings and atrial fibrillation fits possible arterial embolism, while an unrevealing early lactate cannot rule it out. The nurse should promptly alert the responsible team and support urgent vascular imaging or surgical assessment as directed rather than wait for peritoneal signs.","teachback":"When severe abdominal pain seems out of proportion to the examination, what cues would prompt you to escalate concern even if early tests are unrevealing?","questions":[{"stem":"An older adult with atrial fibrillation reports sudden severe abdominal pain, yet the abdomen is soft and minimally tender; the first lactate is within the laboratory reference range. Which interpretation should guide the nurse's communication?","options":["Arterial embolic ischemia remains a concern despite the quiet examination and early lactate result.","The soft abdomen and normal lactate make intestinal ischemia unlikely until tenderness develops.","The presentation best fits venous thrombosis because sudden pain is its typical onset pattern."],"feedback":["Atrial fibrillation is a risk factor for arterial embolism, and early ischemia may cause severe pain with few physical findings. Lactate can rise later and is not a reliable early exclusion.","This choice could delay recognition because minimal tenderness and an unrevealing early marker do not rule out ischemia.","A more gradual onset is typical of venous thrombosis; sudden pain suggests, but does not prove, arterial embolism."]},{"stem":"A patient with generalized atherosclerosis describes months of abdominal discomfort after meals and now has severe abdominal pain with little tenderness. What is the nurse's priority?","options":["Promptly alert the responsible team to possible acute arterial thrombosis and support urgent evaluation as directed.","Continue routine observation because the earlier post-meal discomfort suggests a chronic problem rather than an emergency.","Wait for blood in the stool or a rising lactate before communicating concern for bowel ischemia."],"feedback":["Prior post-meal discomfort can suggest intestinal angina and may precede arterial thrombosis. New severe pain warrants prompt escalation even before later signs appear.","A prior pattern does not make the new severe pain safe to observe routinely; arterial thrombosis can cause acute ischemia.","Blood in stool and rising lactate are more likely as ischemia progresses, so waiting for them risks delayed escalation."]},{"stem":"Following cardiopulmonary bypass, a patient with reduced cardiac output develops severe abdominal pain but has only mild tenderness. Which interpretation best transfers the early-recognition principle to this setting?","options":["Low-flow nonocclusive ischemia is possible, so the nurse should promptly communicate the concern to the care team.","Without an abrupt arterial cutoff, ischemia is unlikely and the pain can be assessed at the next routine interval.","Mild tenderness indicates that bowel injury is limited to the mucosa and can be managed with observation."],"feedback":["Heart failure and cardiopulmonary bypass can create low-flow states associated with nonocclusive ischemia. Severe pain with few findings merits prompt communication.","Ischemia can result from low flow without an occluding clot, and early examination findings may be minimal.","Mild tenderness does not establish the depth or extent of injury; worsening ischemia can progress to infarction and systemic complications."]},{"stem":"A patient being evaluated for mesenteric ischemia has received ordered fluids. The lactate is unchanged, but the patient develops guarding, rigidity, worsening pain, and falling blood pressure. What should the nurse do next?","options":["Escalate immediately for urgent surgical evaluation because new peritoneal signs and deterioration override the unchanged lactate.","Continue close observation and request another lactate before contacting the responsible team.","Document the stable lactate and focus reassessment on bowel sounds before deciding whether to escalate."],"feedback":["Guarding, rigidity, and hemodynamic deterioration suggest progression and require immediate escalation for surgical evaluation. An unchanged marker should not delay action.","Waiting for another test can delay care when peritoneal signs have developed; serum markers may be nonspecific or lag behind injury.","Bowel sounds are relevant to reassessment, but documenting the lactate without urgent escalation misses the significance of peritoneal signs."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia"},{"title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia - Gastrointestinal Disorders ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia?qt=Intestinal%20Ischemia"}],"frames":[{"label":"Sudden abdominal pain","detail":"An older adult with atrial fibrillation develops abrupt severe pain despite a soft abdomen and minimal tenderness."},{"label":"Early ischemia concern","detail":"An initially normal lactate does not exclude mesenteric ischemia, and worsening pain can precede peritoneal signs."},{"label":"Urgent team escalation","detail":"Promptly alert the responsible clinician and surgical team; support ordered resuscitation and prepare for directed urgent imaging."},{"label":"Watch for deterioration","detail":"Trend pain, tenderness, vital signs, and perfusion; immediately escalate new guarding, rigidity, or hemodynamic decline."}]},{"id":"s113","unit":"gi","title":"Variceal bleeding and cirrhosis","objective":"Recognize suspected variceal hemorrhage in adults with portal hypertension and coordinate timely, protocol-guided nursing priorities, communication, and reassessment.","concept":"Portal hypertension, most often caused by cirrhosis, raises pressure in the portal circulation and redirects blood through collateral vessels. Over time, these vessels can enlarge into fragile veins in the distal esophagus or stomach and rupture, causing severe bleeding. The risk is higher with larger varices, advanced liver disease, and red markings seen during endoscopy. Because cirrhosis can also cause nonvariceal bleeding, the source still needs evaluation.","recognize":"Suspect acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding when a patient has sudden hematemesis or melena, especially with known liver disease or signs of portal hypertension. Brisk upper bleeding can also appear as hematochezia, and hypotension or tachycardia signals instability. Varices are not the only possible source: ulcers, erosions, or a Mallory-Weiss tear may also bleed, so endoscopy helps distinguish causes.","respond":"Treat suspected variceal bleeding as time-critical. Follow local emergency protocols, support airway and circulation, establish IV access, monitor closely, and promptly notify the responsible clinician and endoscopy team. Prepare for ordered resuscitation, laboratory testing, blood availability, vasoactive therapy, and infection prophylaxis. Avoid excessive fluid or blood administration, and promptly clarify beta-blocker use during active bleeding, particularly if hypotension is present.","reassess":"After each intervention, reassess blood pressure, pulse, oxygenation, mental status, visible bleeding, and response to resuscitation; track ordered laboratory results and urine output as available. Escalate promptly for worsening instability, ongoing hematemesis, altered mental status, or signs of organ dysfunction. An initially normal hemoglobin does not rule out acute blood loss.","trap":"Known cirrhosis makes variceal bleeding important to consider, but it does not prove the source; think of cirrhosis as a warning sign, not a diagnosis of the bleeding site. Endoscopy is like checking the actual leak rather than assuming which pipe failed.","caseStory":"A patient with known cirrhosis suddenly vomits blood and becomes tachycardic, with falling blood pressure. The patient is pale but still responsive, and the initial hemoglobin result is within the reported reference range. The nurse must decide whether that result supports waiting or whether urgent escalation and stabilization are still needed.","caseReason":"The presentation suggests severe upper gastrointestinal bleeding in a patient at risk for varices, but endoscopy is needed to confirm the source and exclude alternatives. An early hemoglobin can remain normal before blood values equilibrate, so it does not outweigh the bleeding and hemodynamic cues. Escalate, support stabilization under protocol, and prepare for urgent team evaluation.","teachback":"In a patient with cirrhosis and acute gastrointestinal bleeding, which cues would make you escalate immediately, and why would an early normal hemoglobin not reassure you?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with cirrhosis develops sudden, painless hematemesis and melena. The patient is tachycardic, and endoscopy has not yet been performed. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next communication with the team?","options":["Suspect variceal hemorrhage, while recognizing that endoscopy must identify the source because other causes remain possible.","Treat the bleeding as confirmed variceal rupture because cirrhosis makes other upper gastrointestinal sources unlikely.","Favor a lower gastrointestinal source because melena and hematemesis do not reliably indicate upper bleeding."],"feedback":["Cirrhosis and sudden upper gastrointestinal bleeding make variceal hemorrhage an important concern, but endoscopy is needed to confirm the source and assess alternatives.","Cirrhosis increases the risk of varices, but patients with cirrhosis can also have nonvariceal bleeding, so the source is not confirmed by history alone.","Hematemesis and melena are compatible with upper gastrointestinal bleeding; brisk upper bleeding can also sometimes present as hematochezia."]},{"stem":"A patient with known portal hypertension has ongoing hematemesis, hypotension, and decreasing alertness. Which nursing response is the priority?","options":["Activate the local emergency response, support airway and circulation, establish IV access under protocol, and alert the responsible and endoscopy teams.","Wait for the next hemoglobin result before escalating, since the bleeding source has not yet been confirmed by endoscopy.","Continue the patient's usual nonselective beta-blocker and defer resuscitation until the endoscopy team arrives."],"feedback":["Ongoing bleeding with hypotension and altered alertness requires immediate escalation and stabilization while the team arranges urgent evaluation and treatment.","A laboratory result should not delay escalation when the patient is unstable; an early hemoglobin may not yet reflect acute blood loss.","During active hemorrhage, beta-blockers may worsen hypotension; medication management should be promptly clarified with the responsible clinician while stabilization proceeds."]},{"stem":"A patient with portal vein thrombosis but no known cirrhosis suddenly passes a large amount of blood rectally and becomes hypotensive. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate?","options":["Portal vein obstruction can cause portal hypertension, and brisk upper gastrointestinal bleeding can present as hematochezia, so urgent evaluation is needed.","Without cirrhosis, portal hypertension is not a plausible cause, so the presentation should be managed as a lower gastrointestinal bleed only.","The rectal bleeding establishes a lower gastrointestinal source, making assessment for upper gastrointestinal bleeding unnecessary."],"feedback":["Portal vein obstruction is a possible prehepatic cause of portal hypertension, and rapid upper gastrointestinal bleeding can appear as rectal bleeding.","Portal hypertension can result from causes other than cirrhosis, including portal vein obstruction, so the absence of cirrhosis does not exclude varices.","Hematochezia may occur with rapid upper gastrointestinal bleeding, so the observed route of blood does not by itself establish a lower source."]},{"stem":"A patient with cirrhosis has had a recent episode of hematemesis. The initial hemoglobin is within the laboratory reference range, but the patient remains tachycardic and lightheaded. Which reassessment is most appropriate?","options":["Continue close hemodynamic and mental-status assessment, report the concerning findings, and do not use the early hemoglobin to rule out significant bleeding.","Reduce observation because a normal hemoglobin makes major acute blood loss unlikely at this point.","Wait for melena to develop before reporting concern, since hematemesis alone does not indicate ongoing risk."],"feedback":["Hemoglobin can remain normal early in active bleeding before equilibration; persistent tachycardia and lightheadedness warrant continued assessment and escalation.","An early normal result does not reliably exclude acute blood loss, particularly when clinical signs remain concerning.","Hemateme sis is itself a concerning sign of upper gastrointestinal bleeding, and waiting for another sign could delay needed evaluation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Esophageal Varices - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448078/"},{"title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices"}],"frames":[{"label":"Portal hypertension","detail":"With cirrhosis, portal pressure can enlarge fragile esophageal or gastric collateral veins that may rupture."},{"label":"Bleeding with instability","detail":"Hematemesis, tachycardia, and falling pressure indicate urgent bleeding despite an initially normal hemoglobin."},{"label":"Stabilize and notify","detail":"Follow emergency protocol, support airway and circulation, and promptly notify the clinician and endoscopy team."},{"label":"Track response","detail":"Reassess pressure, pulse, oxygenation, mental status, and ongoing bleeding; escalate worsening instability or continued hematemesis."}]},{"id":"s114","unit":"heme","title":"Transfusion lung injury or overload?","objective":"Distinguish possible transfusion related acute lung injury from transfusion associated circulatory overload using the clinical pattern, then initiate prompt team notification and focused reassessment.","concept":"Transfusion related acute lung injury and transfusion associated circulatory overload can both cause acute hypoxemia and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates after transfusion. TRALI involves permeability lung injury, often linked to neutrophil activation; TACO involves hydrostatic pulmonary edema in the setting of volume stress. Their shared respiratory symptoms make the surrounding clinical pattern important: the distinction guides team evaluation and supportive care, but no single bedside cue establishes the diagnosis.","recognize":"Both conditions can arise during or within hours of transfusion and cause dyspnea, tachypnea, hypoxemia, and bilateral infiltrates. Relative hypotension, fever, or transient leukopenia can support TRALI, while hypertension, neck vein distention, an S3, peripheral edema, and cardiac or renal failure support TACO. Fluid overload signs and response to prescribed diuresis may help distinguish TACO; neither pattern is definitive alone.","respond":"For new respiratory distress or suspected reaction, stop the transfusion and promptly notify the treating clinician and blood bank according to local protocol. Assess and report oxygenation, vital signs, respiratory effort, timing, and volume status cues. Support breathing and prepare for further evaluation under orders and protocol. Do not restart the transfusion or independently prescribe diuretics or other treatment.","reassess":"After stopping the transfusion and notifying the team, reassess oxygenation, respiratory effort, vital signs, and signs of volume overload, and report trends promptly. Escalate worsening hypoxemia, increasing distress, hypotension, or other instability. If treatment is ordered, document the response, including whether pulmonary symptoms improve.","trap":"Bilateral infiltrates are like a shared alarm, not a label: they can occur with either TRALI or TACO. Fever does not rule out TACO, and the absence of a classic volume overload sign does not by itself prove TRALI.","caseStory":"During a fresh frozen plasma transfusion, a patient develops abrupt dyspnea and worsening oxygenation. The patient has a new fever and relative hypotension, with bilateral infiltrates on imaging, but no apparent neck vein distention or peripheral edema. The nurse must decide which reaction pattern to report while initiating the immediate response.","caseReason":"Both TRALI and TACO remain concerns because the respiratory symptoms and infiltrates overlap. Fever and relative hypotension, without evident volume overload cues, make possible TRALI an important interpretation, but they do not confirm it. Stop the transfusion, promptly notify the clinician and blood bank, and report the full assessment for team evaluation.","teachback":"When new respiratory distress begins during a plasma transfusion, which findings would make you consider TACO rather than TRALI, and what immediate nursing actions apply whichever cause seems more likely?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient develops dyspnea and worsening oxygenation during a plasma transfusion. Imaging shows new bilateral infiltrates, and the patient has fever and relative hypotension without evident neck vein distention or peripheral edema. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Possible TRALI is a key concern; stop the transfusion and report the findings promptly.","TACO is the leading pattern because bilateral infiltrates alone indicate hydrostatic edema.","An isolated febrile reaction is most likely because fever explains the respiratory deterioration."],"feedback":["Fever and relative hypotension, alongside acute lung injury after transfusion and no evident volume overload signs, support concern for TRALI. The findings still require prompt team evaluation rather than a definitive bedside diagnosis.","Bilateral infiltrates occur in both TRALI and TACO, so they do not alone establish hydrostatic edema. Volume overload cues such as neck vein distention or peripheral edema would make TACO more compelling.","Fever can accompany transfusion reactions, but it does not explain away new hypoxemia and pulmonary infiltrates. The respiratory deterioration requires urgent evaluation for serious pulmonary complications."]},{"stem":"A patient receiving fresh frozen plasma develops acute respiratory distress and falling oxygenation. The nurse notes new bilateral infiltrates but has not yet determined whether the cause is TRALI or TACO. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Stop the transfusion and promptly notify the treating clinician and blood bank under local protocol.","Continue the transfusion while collecting more assessment data because the cause is uncertain.","Treat presumed TACO independently before contacting the treating clinician or blood bank."],"feedback":["A suspected transfusion reaction requires the transfusion to be stopped immediately, with prompt notification of the clinician and blood bank. Further assessment and treatment proceed with the team and under protocol.","Diagnostic uncertainty does not justify continuing a transfusion during acute respiratory distress. Stop it first, then promptly communicate and assess.","TACO may be possible, but the presentation does not establish that diagnosis or authorize independent treatment. Stop the transfusion and notify the team so management can be directed appropriately."]},{"stem":"After plasma transfusion during postoperative care, a patient with renal failure and a positive fluid balance develops dyspnea, hypertension, neck vein distention, and bilateral infiltrates. Which interpretation should the nurse communicate as the leading concern?","options":["Possible TACO, given the volume risk factors and signs consistent with circulatory overload.","Possible TRALI, because any bilateral infiltrates after transfusion indicate permeability injury.","An allergic reaction, because acute respiratory symptoms after plasma are usually caused by bronchospasm."],"feedback":["Renal failure and positive fluid balance increase TACO risk, while hypertension and neck vein distention are volume overload cues. Communicate concern for possible TACO without treating the pattern as conclusive.","TRALI can cause bilateral infiltrates, but the infiltrates are not specific to TRALI. The patient’s volume risk factors and overload signs make TACO a stronger concern.","Anaphylaxis can involve airway symptoms, but this vignette emphasizes fluid balance, hypertension, neck vein distention, and pulmonary infiltrates. Those cues better support concern for TACO."]},{"stem":"A patient develops pulmonary edema during a transfusion. After the team stops the transfusion and provides prescribed treatment to reduce pulmonary pressures, respiratory findings improve. The patient also had elevated neck veins and an S3. Which conclusion best uses these reassessment findings?","options":["The improvement and volume overload signs support TACO, though the team must interpret the full clinical picture.","The improvement confirms TRALI because respiratory symptoms began during a transfusion.","The S3 and neck vein findings are misleading because fever is the only useful distinction."],"feedback":["Rapid improvement after pulmonary pressures are reduced, together with an S3 and elevated neck veins, supports TACO. These findings inform but do not independently confirm the diagnosis.","Timing after transfusion is relevant to both conditions and does not distinguish them by itself. Improvement with reduced pulmonary pressures and volume overload signs favor TACO.","Neck vein distention and an S3 are useful clinical cues for volume overload. Fever may occur in transfusion reactions and is not the sole distinguishing feature."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Managing Fresh-Frozen Plasma Transfusion Adverse Effects: Allergic Reactions, TACO, and TRALI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK597370/"},{"title":"Pulmonary Complications of Transfused Blood Components","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3727894/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Acute transfusion reaction","detail":"During plasma transfusion, abrupt dyspnea and worsening oxygenation arise with bilateral infiltrates."},{"label":"TRALI pattern cues","detail":"Fever and relative hypotension without neck vein distention or edema support possible TRALI, though TACO remains a concern."},{"label":"Stop and report","detail":"Stop transfusion and notify the treating clinician and blood bank under local protocol, reporting timing and volume-status cues."},{"label":"Reassess breathing","detail":"Trend oxygenation, respiratory effort, and vital signs; escalate worsening hypoxemia, distress, hypotension, or instability."}]},{"id":"s115","unit":"endo","title":"Sodium and neurologic change","objective":"Recognize when neurologic change may be related to hyponatremia, distinguish key patterns that alter interpretation, and escalate concerns using team protocols.","concept":"Hyponatremia reflects excess body water relative to body solute, not necessarily a loss of total body sodium. Vasopressin can reduce the kidneys’ ability to excrete free water, diluting serum sodium. When the blood is hypotonic, water shifts into brain cells; rapid-onset changes can cause cerebral swelling and serious neurologic symptoms. Chronic changes may be less obvious, so the sodium trend and clinical picture both matter.","recognize":"Watch for new confusion, gait change, headache, nausea, vomiting, seizure, unusual drowsiness, or impaired breathing, and relate these findings to sodium trends and timing. Check the measured serum osmolality, glucose, urine findings, volume assessment, medication history, and kidney, thyroid, and adrenal evaluation when available. High glucose or normal measured osmolality can point away from true hypotonic hyponatremia.","respond":"Treat seizure, declining consciousness, or respiratory compromise as urgent and activate the appropriate response pathway. Protect the patient from injury, promptly notify the responsible clinician, and report symptom onset, sodium trend, osmolality, volume cues, medications, and fluid intake. Obtain or monitor tests and administer fluids only as ordered or directed by local protocol; do not independently alter fluids or sodium therapy.","reassess":"After an ordered intervention, reassess mental status, headache, nausea, seizure activity, respiratory status, intake and output, and ordered sodium and osmolality trends. Document timing and response. Worsening neurologic or respiratory findings, a seizure, or an unexpected sodium trend warrants prompt escalation through the care team or emergency pathway.","trap":"A low sodium result is a clue about the balance between water and solute, not proof that the patient has simply lost salt. Think of measured osmolality as a second instrument panel: glucose, other osmoles, or a measurement artifact can change what the sodium result means.","caseStory":"A postoperative adult becomes nauseated and inattentive, with a newly low sodium result and low measured serum osmolality. The patient appears neither edematous nor dehydrated, has concentrated urine, and recently began an antidepressant. The nurse must decide whether this pattern is concerning for water retention and what should be communicated first.","caseReason":"The low measured osmolality supports true hypotonic hyponatremia rather than a sodium result explained by high glucose or pseudohyponatremia. Concentrated urine is consistent with ongoing vasopressin effect, and the medication is a relevant clue, but these findings do not alone establish SIADH. The nurse should report the neurologic change and laboratory pattern promptly and support further evaluation under the care team’s plan.","teachback":"When a patient with a low sodium develops a new neurologic change, what findings would you check and communicate before assuming the cause is SIADH?","questions":[{"stem":"A postoperative adult develops nausea and new confusion. The sodium result is low, measured serum osmolality is also low, urine is concentrated, and the patient has no clear signs of fluid overload or dehydration. The patient recently started an antidepressant. Which interpretation best fits these cues?","options":["The pattern supports hypotonic hyponatremia with ongoing vasopressin effect; SIADH is a consideration, but other causes still require evaluation.","The concentrated urine establishes dehydration as the cause, so the low sodium is not a neurologic concern.","The new antidepressant confirms SIADH, so other laboratory and clinical findings need not be considered."],"feedback":["Low measured osmolality supports hypotonic hyponatremia, and concentrated urine is consistent with reduced free-water excretion. Medication exposure is relevant, but SIADH requires consideration of other causes and the full clinical assessment.","Concentrated urine can reflect vasopressin activity and does not by itself establish dehydration. The neurologic change with hypotonic hyponatremia merits prompt attention.","The medication is a possible contributor, not proof of SIADH. Other causes and the patient’s clinical and laboratory findings still need evaluation."]},{"stem":"An adult with a documented history of chronic hyponatremia suddenly has a seizure and becomes difficult to arouse. A nurse is preparing to wait for the next scheduled laboratory draw before contacting the clinician. What is the best priority action?","options":["Activate the urgent response pathway, protect the patient from injury, and promptly communicate the neurologic change and sodium history.","Wait for the next sodium result because chronic hyponatremia usually causes few symptoms.","Independently change the patient’s fluid intake while monitoring for improvement."],"feedback":["A seizure and reduced responsiveness are urgent neurologic changes. Escalation and injury prevention should not wait for a scheduled test; communicate the history and current findings.","Chronic hyponatremia can be subtle, but a new seizure or reduced responsiveness is a major change that needs prompt escalation.","Fluid changes can affect sodium and should follow clinician orders or local protocols. Independent changes are not an appropriate substitute for urgent escalation."]},{"stem":"After a long-distance race, an adult who drank large amounts of water becomes confused and nauseated. Testing shows low sodium and low measured serum osmolality. Which interpretation best transfers the approach to this new context?","options":["Excess water intake combined with exercise-related vasopressin release can contribute to hypotonic hyponatremia and neurologic change.","The symptoms most likely show isolated salt loss, so the low serum osmolality does not affect interpretation.","Confusion after exercise is unrelated to sodium unless the patient has visible edema."],"feedback":["The supplied material describes exercise, increased water intake, solute loss, and sympathetic stimulation of vasopressin as contributors to hyponatremia. Low measured osmolality supports a hypotonic pattern.","Salt loss may contribute, but the low measured osmolality and large water intake are important to interpretation; the pattern should not be reduced to isolated salt loss.","Neurologic symptoms can accompany hyponatremia without visible edema. The patient’s symptoms and laboratory pattern warrant prompt communication and assessment."]},{"stem":"An adult with a low sodium result has marked hyperglycemia and an elevated measured serum osmolality. The nurse is asked whether this establishes SIADH. Which response best distinguishes a misleading finding?","options":["No; hyperglycemia can lower the measured sodium while raising osmolality, so the result does not establish hypotonic hyponatremia or SIADH.","Yes; any low sodium result with neurologic symptoms establishes SIADH, regardless of measured osmolality.","The elevated osmolality proves pseudohyponatremia, so the sodium result can be disregarded."],"feedback":["Hyperglycemia can produce a low measured sodium while increasing serum osmolality. The result needs interpretation in context and does not by itself establish hypotonic hyponatremia or SIADH.","A low sodium and symptoms require assessment, but SIADH is not established without considering osmolality and other causes.","Elevated osmolality with hyperglycemia can reflect a real osmotic effect, not necessarily pseudohyponatremia. The finding should be reported and interpreted with the full laboratory picture."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Checking your browser - reCAPTCHA","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279055/"},{"title":"Hyponatremia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/portal/utils/pageresolver.fcgi?recordid=6aaf58fb42ea1349fbeb305c"}],"frames":[{"label":"Water retention context","detail":"After surgery, a new antidepressant accompanies low sodium, low measured osmolality, concentrated urine, and inattention."},{"label":"Neurologic change","detail":"Low osmolality supports hypotonic hyponatremia; nausea and inattention are concerning even without obvious volume depletion."},{"label":"Report pattern promptly","detail":"Notify the responsible clinician with symptom onset, sodium trend, osmolality, urine findings, medication, and fluid intake."},{"label":"Follow neurologic status","detail":"Reassess mental and respiratory status and ordered sodium trends; escalate seizure, worsening consciousness, or unexpected sodium change."}]},{"id":"s116","unit":"skin","title":"Skin failure and pressure injury","objective":"Interpret skin changes in adults with sepsis, distinguish signs of hypoperfusion from a diagnosis of acute skin failure or pressure injury, and communicate findings for timely team assessment.","concept":"Acute skin failure is loss of skin barrier and thermoregulatory function associated with severe circulatory stress and reduced skin perfusion, potentially progressing to tissue necrosis. Mottling can signal cutaneous hypoperfusion, but it is not itself proof of skin failure. Pressure injury is a distinct condition, even though skin failure may involve pressure-bearing areas. Higher lactate and severity scores, and lower mean arterial pressure, were associated with skin failure in the reviewed studies; they are not diagnostic tests.","recognize":"Look for mottling, cool or clammy skin, delayed capillary refill, reduced skin sensation, or other evolving tissue changes, and consider them alongside the overall perfusion picture. Findings used to identify skin failure varied across studies, and some used mottling as a surrogate. Do not equate mottling with skin failure or classify a lesion as a pressure injury solely because it is over a pressure-bearing area.","respond":"Assess and document the distribution and evolution of skin changes along with relevant perfusion findings, such as capillary refill, mean arterial pressure, lactate results, and illness severity information available to the team. Promptly communicate concerning or changing findings to the responsible clinician and wound-care colleagues according to local processes. Continue care under orders and protocols; do not independently diagnose skin failure or initiate treatments outside your role.","reassess":"After communicating findings or completing ordered care, reassess skin appearance, temperature, capillary refill, sensation, and the extent of any tissue changes, and compare them with documented observations. Review available perfusion trends with the team. Worsening changes, emerging tissue damage, or persistent concern despite improved circulation warrant prompt escalation rather than reassurance from one improved measure.","trap":"Mottling is more like a warning light than a name for the whole problem: it can signal poor skin perfusion, but it does not establish skin failure. A pressure-bearing location is also not a diagnosis; skin failure and pressure injury are distinct conditions that require careful assessment.","caseStory":"A fictional adult admitted with sepsis develops mottling and cool, clammy skin while the team is monitoring low blood pressure and elevated lactate. A new discolored area is also noted over a pressure-bearing region, but the available observations do not establish its cause. The nurse must decide what to assess and communicate without treating mottling as a definitive diagnosis.","caseReason":"The mottling and cool skin are concerning cues of cutaneous hypoperfusion, and the perfusion context increases concern. However, mottling is not equivalent to acute skin failure, and the location alone cannot distinguish skin failure from pressure injury. The nurse should document the findings, assess their distribution and evolution, and promptly share them with the responsible team for evaluation.","teachback":"If a patient with sepsis develops mottling over a pressure-bearing area, what additional observations would you make, and how would you explain to the team why neither skin failure nor pressure injury should be assumed from that finding alone?","questions":[{"stem":"A fictional adult receiving care for sepsis has newly mottled, cool skin and delayed capillary refill. The team is also reviewing an elevated lactate result. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's next step?","options":["These findings raise concern for cutaneous hypoperfusion and possible skin failure, so assess and communicate them without treating mottling as diagnostic.","The mottling establishes acute skin failure, so document that diagnosis and focus subsequent assessment on the affected area.","The lactate result explains the skin appearance, so further skin assessment is unnecessary unless tissue loss becomes visible."],"feedback":["Correct. Mottling and delayed refill can indicate cutaneous hypoperfusion, while elevated lactate is associated with skin failure in the review; neither establishes the diagnosis alone.","Mottling is a sign that may precede or accompany skin failure, not a definitive diagnosis. The nurse should assess and communicate the broader findings.","The association between lactate and skin failure does not make lactate a diagnostic substitute for skin assessment. Continued observation and communication are appropriate."]},{"stem":"A fictional patient with sepsis has worsening mottling, cool clammy skin, and concerning perfusion observations documented during routine care. Which nursing action is the priority?","options":["Document the distribution and changes, then promptly communicate the perfusion and skin findings to the responsible team.","Label the affected area as a pressure injury and wait for routine wound review before reporting the perfusion changes.","Continue routine observation because skin changes are secondary to sepsis and do not require separate communication."],"feedback":["Correct. Worsening skin findings in the context of sepsis warrant assessment and timely communication; documentation helps the team evaluate change over time.","The site alone does not establish pressure injury, and delaying communication could obscure the significance of evolving perfusion concerns.","Skin changes can provide visible evidence of cutaneous hypoperfusion. They should be assessed and communicated rather than dismissed as incidental."]},{"stem":"In a fictional septic patient, a discolored area appears over a pressure-bearing region, while the rest of the skin is warm and no mottling is observed. Which approach best transfers the skin-assessment principles to this situation?","options":["Describe the area and surrounding skin, assess available perfusion cues and changes, and report it for team evaluation without assuming its cause.","Classify the area as acute skin failure because it is discolored, then limit the assessment to whether the color expands.","Exclude skin failure because the remaining skin is warm, and document the area only if it becomes an open wound."],"feedback":["Correct. Pressure injury and skin failure are distinct, and location or discoloration alone does not establish the cause. A focused assessment and team communication are appropriate.","Discoloration alone does not establish acute skin failure. The nurse should consider the wider skin and perfusion picture and report findings.","Warm surrounding skin and absent mottling do not independently rule out skin failure or another skin condition. Assessment and communication should not wait for an open wound."]},{"stem":"After ordered care, a fictional septic patient's mean arterial pressure improves, but mottling and delayed capillary refill remain. Which reassessment response is most appropriate?","options":["Reassess and document the persistent skin findings and communicate them, since one improved circulation measure does not establish skin recovery.","Record that skin perfusion has normalized because the mean arterial pressure improved, and resume routine checks.","Conclude that acute skin failure is present because mottling remains, and stop considering other explanations."],"feedback":["Correct. Persistent skin cues remain relevant even when one measure improves; reassessment and communication help the team interpret the full clinical picture.","Mean arterial pressure is associated with skin failure risk, but an improved value alone does not show that skin perfusion has recovered.","Persistent mottling warrants attention but is not by itself diagnostic of acute skin failure. The nurse should report findings without prematurely assigning a cause."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Incidence and risk factors of acute skin failure in patients with ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13713861/"},{"title":"Skin Failure: Results of a Think Tank Hosted by the National Pressure ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12537035/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Perfusion stress","detail":"Severe circulatory stress can reduce skin perfusion and impair barrier and thermoregulatory function, potentially progressing to tissue necrosis."},{"label":"Changing skin cues","detail":"Mottling, cool clammy skin, delayed refill, or evolving tissue changes raise concern but do not prove skin failure."},{"label":"Share evolving findings","detail":"Document distribution and progression alongside available perfusion trends, then promptly communicate concerns to clinicians and wound-care colleagues per local process."},{"label":"Persistent tissue changes","detail":"Reassess appearance, temperature, refill, sensation, and extent; worsening damage or ongoing concern needs prompt escalation despite one improved measure."}]},{"id":"s117","unit":"msk","title":"Early mobility and safety","objective":"Apply a team-based safety screen to recognize when an adult ICU patient may be ready for mobilization, when activity should pause, and what to reassess.","concept":"Critical illness, sedation, and immobility can contribute to muscle weakness, reduced physical function, respiratory complications, and delirium. Mobilization can help limit these effects, but activity also increases circulatory and respiratory demands and can put lines or other devices at risk. Readiness is therefore a changing clinical judgment based on the patient’s stability, neurological status, cooperation, and individual risks, coordinated with the ICU team and local protocols.","recognize":"Before activity, consider whether the patient is hemodynamically and respiratorily stable, sufficiently alert and cooperative, and free of concerning issues such as active bleeding, unstable intracranial pressure, or an unstable fracture. Distinguish limited mobility from current instability: weakness alone does not establish that mobilization is unsafe, while new desaturation, altered consciousness, or cardiovascular instability warrants a pause and team review.","respond":"Review the current plan and local safety process, then share relevant changes with the bedside team before activity. If the patient appears suitable, coordinate the planned level of mobilization with the responsible clinicians and help protect lines and devices. If instability or a contraindication is present, do not initiate or continue activity; support the patient safely and promptly report the concern rather than deciding independently to alter treatment.","reassess":"After activity, assess respiratory status, hemodynamic response, consciousness and cooperation, symptoms, and the security of lines and devices. Compare findings with the patient’s status before mobilization and the team’s criteria. New desaturation, cardiovascular deterioration, reduced consciousness, or device displacement warrants stopping activity as appropriate and promptly escalating to the responsible team.","trap":"A ventilator or an indwelling line is not automatically a stop sign, just as a clear path does not guarantee a safe journey. Readiness depends on the whole clinical picture, and new instability can change the decision even when the patient appeared suitable moments earlier.","caseStory":"A mechanically ventilated ICU patient is awake, follows instructions, and has had stable respiratory and cardiovascular status during the shift. During preparation for a planned transfer to a chair, the nurse notices new desaturation and increased difficulty engaging with instructions. The nurse must decide whether to proceed or pause and involve the team.","caseReason":"The new respiratory and neurological changes differ from the patient’s prior status and raise safety concerns, even though the patient was previously cooperative. The nurse should pause the planned activity, support the patient, and promptly communicate the changes to the team for reassessment under local protocol.","teachback":"What changes in respiratory status, hemodynamic stability, or cooperation would lead you to pause a planned mobilization and contact the ICU team?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult ICU patient is scheduled for assisted sitting at the bedside. The patient had been cooperative, but now appears less alert and has newly worsening oxygenation compared with the recent assessment. Which interpretation best supports a safe nursing decision?","options":["The change in respiratory status and alertness requires pausing activity and informing the team.","The patient’s earlier cooperation is the strongest readiness cue, so sitting can proceed with closer observation.","Reduced alertness is expected during critical illness, so the nurse can continue if the patient remains in bed."],"feedback":["Correct. New respiratory and neurological changes can signal reduced readiness, so activity should pause and the team should reassess.","Earlier cooperation does not outweigh a new change in oxygenation; proceeding could add respiratory and circulatory demands.","Critical illness may affect alertness, but a change from the patient’s status still needs assessment rather than being assumed expected."]},{"stem":"A patient in a surgical ICU is being considered for mobilization after a procedure. The patient is awake and follows instructions, but the nurse identifies active bleeding during the pre-activity review. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Hold the planned activity and promptly communicate the active bleeding to the responsible team.","Proceed with a brief in-bed activity because the patient is alert and can follow instructions.","Ask the patient to stand only if the bleeding appears unchanged during the first movement."],"feedback":["Correct. Active bleeding is a cited contraindication to mobilization, so the nurse should not initiate activity and should alert the team.","Cooperation is relevant, but it does not make mobilization appropriate in the presence of active bleeding.","Waiting for bleeding to worsen during movement is unsafe; the concern should be addressed before activity begins."]},{"stem":"An adult recovering from cardiac surgery is alert and has no new respiratory or cardiovascular concerns during the current assessment. The care team has discussed early mobility, but the nurse has not yet confirmed the day’s activity plan. What is the best next step?","options":["Coordinate with the team to confirm the planned activity and carry it out within local protocol.","Independently select a more intensive activity because early mobility can reduce postoperative complications.","Defer all mobility until discharge because cardiac surgery makes early activity unsafe."],"feedback":["Correct. Early mobility may benefit cardiac surgery patients, but the activity should be planned with the team and guided by the patient’s status and local protocol.","Potential benefit does not authorize the nurse to independently increase activity intensity or bypass team planning.","The supplied evidence describes benefits of early mobilization after cardiac surgery; surgery alone does not establish that all mobility must be deferred."]},{"stem":"An ICU patient completes a planned assisted transfer with staff support. Immediately afterward, the nurse notices new cardiovascular instability, although the patient says they feel comfortable and the lines appear secure. Which reassessment finding should guide the next action?","options":["The cardiovascular change warrants stopping further activity and promptly escalating to the team.","The patient’s comfort and secure lines support continuing with the next planned activity.","The absence of line displacement is the most important safety finding, so routine observation is sufficient."],"feedback":["Correct. Cardiovascular deterioration after activity is a safety concern even when the patient reports comfort and devices remain secure.","Comfort does not rule out physiological instability, and continuing activity could increase the patient’s demands.","Device security matters, but it does not replace reassessment of cardiovascular and respiratory status or escalation of a new change."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Effectiveness, Safety, and Barriers to Early Mobilization in the Intensive Care Unit","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7714600/"},{"title":"Protocolized strategies to encourage early mobilization of critical ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11805459/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Immobility burden","detail":"Critical illness, sedation, and immobility contribute to weakness, impaired function, respiratory complications, and delirium, while activity adds physiologic and device risks."},{"label":"New instability","detail":"New desaturation or difficulty engaging during transfer preparation differs from prior stability and warrants pausing for team review."},{"label":"Coordinate safe activity","detail":"Review the local safety process and share changes with the bedside team; coordinate mobilization only when suitability is established."},{"label":"Post-activity response","detail":"Reassess breathing, circulation, consciousness, symptoms, and device security; deterioration or displacement calls for stopping as appropriate and escalating."}]},{"id":"s118","unit":"multi","title":"Obstetric hemorrhage","objective":"Recognize suspected placental abruption from maternal and fetal cues and coordinate timely, protocol-based escalation while distinguishing it from placenta previa.","concept":"Placental abruption is premature separation of the placenta, allowing maternal blood to collect behind it or exit vaginally. Separation reduces the placental surface available for fetal oxygen exchange, so maternal blood loss and fetal compromise can evolve together. Visible bleeding may underestimate concealed loss; chronic separation may instead impair fetal growth or reduce amniotic fluid. Clinical findings guide concern because ultrasound may appear normal.","recognize":"Suspect abruption after midpregnancy bleeding, abdominal or uterine pain, tenderness, uterine irritability, shock, coagulopathy, or a nonreassuring fetal heart pattern; bleeding can be slight or absent when concealed. Pain and tenderness with bleeding favor abruption over the often painless bleeding of previa, but symptoms overlap. A normal ultrasound does not exclude abruption, and previa must be ruled out before pelvic examination.","respond":"Treat this as time-sensitive: promptly notify the obstetric team, support assessment, and follow local protocols. Anticipate fetal monitoring, blood count, blood typing, coagulation studies, and ultrasound as ordered; report bleeding, pain, vital-sign trends, and fetal changes. Prepare for blood-product support or delivery if directed. Do not infer safety from scant bleeding or a normal ultrasound, and avoid pelvic examination until previa is excluded.","reassess":"After each intervention, trend maternal blood pressure and overall stability, describe bleeding, reassess uterine pain or tenderness, and review fetal heart monitoring with the team. Escalate promptly for worsening maternal status, ongoing or increasing bleeding, a nonreassuring fetal pattern, or signs of coagulopathy; improvement in visible bleeding alone is not enough.","trap":"Do not treat the ultrasound as a fire alarm that must sound before abruption is real: imaging can be normal despite clinical separation. Think of concealed bleeding as water behind a wall; a small visible leak does not measure what is hidden, so pain, tenderness, maternal status, and fetal monitoring matter.","caseStory":"At 32 weeks, a patient arrives after abdominal trauma with continuous uterine pain and tenderness, scant dark bleeding, and a newly nonreassuring fetal heart pattern. Ultrasound does not show a collection. The nurse must decide whether to continue routine observation or escalate concern for abruption.","caseReason":"Pain, tenderness, trauma, and fetal change support suspected abruption, while scant bleeding may reflect concealed hemorrhage. A normal ultrasound does not rule it out. The nurse should promptly communicate the combined findings and support urgent team evaluation rather than use imaging or bleeding volume to reassure.","teachback":"When visible bleeding is minimal or ultrasound is unrevealing, which maternal and fetal cues would keep you concerned for abruption, and how would you communicate them?","questions":[{"stem":"At 29 weeks, a patient reports sudden abdominal pain; the uterus is tender, vaginal bleeding is scant, and fetal monitoring is nonreassuring. Ultrasound is read as normal. Which interpretation best integrates these findings?","options":["Suspect placental abruption with possible concealed hemorrhage; normal ultrasound does not exclude it.","Favor placenta previa because bleeding in pregnancy most strongly indicates placental location over the cervix.","Consider abruption unlikely because ultrasound did not show separation and visible blood is scant."],"feedback":["Pain, uterine tenderness, and a nonreassuring fetal pattern support concern for abruption, and bleeding may be concealed. Normal ultrasound findings do not rule it out.","Previa can cause bleeding, but it is often painless; the pain and tenderness here make abruption an important concern.","This reasoning over-relies on imaging and visible blood. Both may underrepresent the extent of an abruption."]},{"stem":"At 35 weeks after a fall, a patient has increasing uterine tenderness, ongoing bleeding, worsening maternal hemodynamic status, and a nonreassuring fetal heart pattern. What is the nurse’s priority?","options":["Promptly escalate to the obstetric team, support ordered maternal and fetal care, and prepare for delivery or blood-product support if directed.","Continue inpatient observation because the pregnancy is preterm, and repeat the assessment at the next scheduled interval.","Wait for ultrasound confirmation before reporting the findings, since trauma-related bleeding can have other causes."],"feedback":["Maternal instability and a nonreassuring fetal pattern warrant prompt team escalation; delivery and hemodynamic support may be needed.","Observation is appropriate only when maternal and fetal status are stable and bleeding is not threatening them.","Abruption is primarily a clinical diagnosis, and ultrasound can be normal. Waiting for confirmation could delay response to instability."]},{"stem":"A patient at 31 weeks is stable but reports intermittent dark spotting. Assessment also notes concern about restricted fetal growth and reduced amniotic fluid. Which nursing interpretation and action best fit this pattern?","options":["Communicate concern for possible chronic abruption and support close maternal and fetal observation as directed by the team.","Treat abruption as unlikely because there is no acute pain, heavy bleeding, or current maternal instability.","Assume acute severe abruption and prepare for immediate delivery regardless of the stable assessment."],"feedback":["Chronic abruption may cause intermittent dark spotting and can be associated with restricted growth or reduced amniotic fluid; close observation is appropriate when status is stable.","A chronic abruption can have minimal or intermittent symptoms, so the absence of acute pain or heavy bleeding does not exclude it.","The described pattern warrants concern and monitoring, but the references do not support assuming that every stable preterm case requires immediate delivery."]},{"stem":"A patient at 33 weeks is hospitalized for suspected abruption. Visible bleeding stops, and ultrasound appears normal, but uterine tenderness persists and fetal monitoring becomes nonreassuring. What should the nurse do?","options":["Escalate the persistent tenderness and new fetal change promptly to the obstetric team despite the stopped bleeding and normal ultrasound.","Continue the current observation plan without reporting a change because bleeding has stopped and imaging is reassuring.","Wait for bleeding to recur before reassessing, since visible blood is the clearest measure of ongoing separation."],"feedback":["Persistent tenderness and a new nonreassuring fetal pattern require prompt communication; stopped bleeding and normal ultrasound do not rule out abruption.","This dismisses clinically important changes. Maternal and fetal status, not ultrasound alone, guide concern.","Bleeding can be concealed, so recurrence of visible blood is not required for deterioration to be present."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - Gynecology and ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae"},{"title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - Women's Health ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/women-s-health-issues/complications-of-pregnancy/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae"}],"frames":[{"label":"Concealed separation","detail":"Placental separation can reduce fetal oxygen exchange, while blood may remain concealed and visible bleeding may underestimate maternal loss."},{"label":"Pain and fetal change","detail":"Uterine tenderness, trauma, scant bleeding, and a nonreassuring fetal pattern support concern even when ultrasound shows no collection."},{"label":"Urgent obstetric review","detail":"Promptly notify the obstetric team and report bleeding, pain, maternal trends, and fetal changes; avoid pelvic examination until previa is excluded."},{"label":"Maternal-fetal trend","detail":"Reassess stability, bleeding, uterine tenderness, and fetal monitoring; worsening status or a nonreassuring pattern needs prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s119","unit":"multi","title":"Accidental hypothermia","objective":"Use observed consciousness, circulation, and exposure history to recognize cardiac arrest risk in accidental hypothermia and communicate priorities for safe, protocol-based care.","concept":"Cooling can reduce the brain’s oxygen demand, offering some resilience when circulation is poor, but this protection is limited. A cold, unstable myocardium may deteriorate with movement, and rescue collapse can involve afterdrop and other physiologic changes. In isolated accidental hypothermia, impaired consciousness, including confusion, is directly associated with greater cardiac arrest risk. Treat it as a clinical warning, not as a precise estimate of core temperature.","recognize":"Look for cold exposure alongside changes in cognition or responsiveness, from unusual behavior and confusion to response only to voice, pain, or no stimulus. The reviewed cases link greater impairment with greater arrest risk, while shivering and clinical estimates of core temperature are unreliable. Interpret altered consciousness in context, since trauma, drowning, substances, and illness can also affect it.","respond":"Notify the team promptly about exposure, the patient’s exact responsiveness, circulation, rhythm, and any reliable temperature reading. Follow local hypothermia and transport protocols, support ongoing circulation, and handle the patient gently, keeping them horizontal during movement when feasible. Under the team’s direction, prepare for escalation when circulation is unstable or arrest occurs; avoid unnecessary movement or treating an uncertain temperature estimate as reassurance.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess responsiveness and cognition, pulse and rhythm, blood pressure, breathing, and any reliable core temperature available. Report a decline in consciousness, new arrhythmia, falling blood pressure, or loss of signs of circulation immediately. A change in shivering alone does not establish that risk has resolved.","trap":"Being awake does not automatically mean a cold patient is low risk: confusion may signal meaningful impairment even when the patient can speak. Think of consciousness as a warning light, not a thermometer; it can guide concern, but it does not give an exact core temperature.","caseStory":"An adult is brought in after prolonged cold exposure outdoors. The patient answers questions and has a palpable pulse, but gives an unusual response and cannot follow a simple instruction; a reliable low-reading thermometer is not immediately available. The nurse must decide whether apparent wakefulness is enough to support routine care or whether to escalate concern.","caseReason":"The patient is alert but confused, which is not equivalent to normal cognition and was associated with cardiac arrest risk in the reviewed cases. The nurse should promptly communicate the change, continue close assessment, and follow the local hypothermia pathway while minimizing unnecessary movement. Lack of a reliable temperature reading should not be used to dismiss the clinical warning.","teachback":"In a cold patient who can speak, what change in cognition would make you raise concern, and what specific findings would you communicate to the team?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult found after cold exposure is awake and speaking, but uses inappropriate words and cannot follow a simple instruction for a physical reason. The patient has a palpable pulse. Which interpretation best supports nursing triage?","options":["Treat the confusion as impaired consciousness that raises concern for arrest risk, and promptly communicate it to the team.","Treat the patient as low risk because speech and a palpable pulse establish adequate neurologic and cardiac stability.","Use the patient’s shivering to estimate core temperature and defer escalation unless shivering stops."],"feedback":["Confusion is distinct from normal alertness and was associated with subsequent arrest in the reviewed cases, so it warrants prompt communication and continued assessment.","Speaking and having a pulse do not rule out risk; the evidence identifies altered cognition as a relevant warning even before arrest.","Shivering is an unreliable indicator of core temperature and should not be used to defer assessment or escalation."]},{"stem":"During transfer from a cold outdoor scene, an adult with suspected hypothermia is conscious but confused and has a pulse. The team is preparing to move the patient to a warmer area. Which nursing action best reflects the priorities in the reviewed material?","options":["Communicate the mental-status change, support gentle horizontal movement when feasible, and follow the team’s monitoring and transport plan.","Ask the patient to walk briskly to the warmer area, using exertion to demonstrate that circulation is adequate.","Focus on obtaining a temperature before communicating the confusion or assisting with transfer."],"feedback":["Confusion is a meaningful risk cue, and careful, horizontal handling during movement can help avoid movement-related deterioration; actions should follow the team’s plan.","Unnecessary exertion and movement may be hazardous in a cold, unstable patient, and walking ability does not establish low risk.","Temperature measurement may be useful when reliable and available, but it should not delay communication of concerning consciousness changes."]},{"stem":"At a remote worksite, an adult exposed to cold has no reliable core-temperature measurement available. The patient opens their eyes and responds only when spoken to. What is the most appropriate nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Recognize impaired responsiveness as a risk cue despite the missing temperature, and promptly relay the finding for protocol-based assessment and transfer planning.","Assume cardiac arrest risk is low until a measured temperature confirms severe cooling, and continue routine observation.","Estimate the core temperature from the response and decide independently whether the patient needs specialist transport."],"feedback":["Responsiveness to voice reflects impaired consciousness, which is associated with increased arrest risk; a missing temperature does not remove the need to communicate and follow protocol.","Field temperature assessment may be unavailable or unreliable, and waiting for confirmation can discount a clinically useful warning sign.","Consciousness does not provide a precise temperature estimate, and transport decisions belong within the team’s pathway rather than independent nursing judgment."]},{"stem":"After warming measures begin, a cold patient stops shivering but changes from answering questions to responding only to voice. A pulse remains palpable. Which reassessment finding should prompt immediate escalation?","options":["The decline in responsiveness, which is concerning even though shivering has stopped and a pulse remains.","The cessation of shivering as proof that warming is complete, with no further assessment needed while a pulse is present.","The continued presence of a pulse as evidence that cardiac arrest risk has passed, unless the patient becomes unresponsive."],"feedback":["Worsening responsiveness is a direct concern because greater impairment is associated with greater arrest risk; communicate it promptly and continue circulation assessment.","Shivering is an unreliable indicator of core temperature or recovery, and its cessation does not establish that the patient is stable.","A palpable pulse does not eliminate the risk of deterioration; ongoing changes in consciousness and circulation require reassessment and escalation as indicated."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Accidental hypothermia–an update: The content of this review is endorsed by the International Commission for Mountain Emergency Medicine (ICAR MEDCOM)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/5025630"},{"title":"Accidental hypothermia: direct evidence for consciousness as a marker of cardiac arrest risk in the acute assessment of cold patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/8895778"}],"frames":[{"label":"Cold cardiac stress","detail":"Cold exposure can leave myocardium unstable; impaired consciousness in isolated accidental hypothermia is associated with greater cardiac arrest risk."},{"label":"Confusion despite pulse","detail":"Unusual responses or inability to follow instructions signal impaired cognition, even when the patient answers and has a palpable pulse."},{"label":"Gentle monitored care","detail":"Promptly report responsiveness, circulation, rhythm, and reliable temperature; follow the local pathway and minimize unnecessary movement."},{"label":"Declining responsiveness","detail":"Reassess cognition, pulse, rhythm, blood pressure, and breathing; worsening consciousness, arrhythmia, or falling pressure requires immediate report."}]},{"id":"s120","unit":"multi","title":"Graft dysfunction and rejection","objective":"Distinguish patterns of transplant rejection from other causes of post-transplant change and communicate graft-specific findings promptly to the transplant team.","concept":"An allograft can be attacked by the recipient’s immune system, while immunosuppressive treatment reduces rejection risk but also weakens defenses against infection. Rejection patterns reflect different mechanisms and timing: hyperacute rejection involves preexisting antibodies, acute rejection can be T-cell-mediated or antibody-mediated, and chronic rejection develops through progressive graft injury and vascular change. These distinctions help nurses interpret trends without treating timing or one symptom as diagnostic.","recognize":"Look for new graft-specific changes, such as rising creatinine, reduced urine output, hypertension, weight gain, or graft tenderness after kidney transplant. Fever can occur with rejection, but it is not specific; infection is also a major concern. Acute rejection may appear days after transplant, whereas chronic dysfunction often develops months or years later. Interpret symptoms alongside organ function and the clinical timeline.","respond":"Prioritize a focused assessment of the graft and overall status, compare findings with recent trends, and promptly communicate new dysfunction to the transplant team. Report fever, symptoms, relevant laboratory changes, and the timing after transplant. Follow local protocols and prescribed orders for evaluation. Do not independently alter immunosuppression or assume that fever alone confirms rejection rather than infection.","reassess":"After nursing actions or team-directed treatment, reassess the patient’s symptoms, vital signs, and organ-specific function, comparing results with the prior trend. For a kidney graft, note urine output and creatinine; for a lung graft, note breathing and available pulmonary function data. Worsening graft function, new instability, or concerning symptoms warrants prompt escalation.","trap":"Fever is a warning light, not a label: it can accompany rejection, but infection and other causes remain possible. A rejection pattern is more like a trend in the graft’s function than a single symptom, so pair timing and symptoms with organ-specific evidence.","caseStory":"A patient is one week after kidney transplantation and develops fever, rising creatinine, reduced urine output, and graft tenderness. The nurse must decide whether to treat the fever as proof of infection or communicate a possible graft complication using the full pattern.","caseReason":"The timing and new kidney-specific dysfunction are concerning for acute rejection, which can occur days after transplant and may include fever and increased creatinine. Infection remains a competing concern, so the nurse reports the complete assessment promptly rather than diagnosing rejection or changing immunosuppression independently.","teachback":"When a transplant recipient develops fever, what graft-specific changes and timing would you include in your communication to help the team distinguish possible rejection from other causes?","questions":[{"stem":"A kidney transplant recipient develops fever several days after surgery, with a rising creatinine, reduced urine output, hypertension, and tenderness over the graft. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?","options":["Possible acute rejection, because new graft dysfunction appears days after transplant and fever can accompany it","Possible chronic rejection, because hypertension and altered kidney function indicate a slowly progressive process","Possible hyperacute rejection, because fever and graft tenderness are typical features of a late antibody response"],"feedback":["This pattern is concerning for acute rejection: it can occur days after transplant and may involve fever and kidney graft dysfunction. The findings require team evaluation rather than a nurse-made diagnosis.","Chronic rejection generally develops months to years later and is often a gradual graft dysfunction pattern. The timing here makes this interpretation less fitting.","Hyperacute rejection occurs within 48 hours and is associated with preexisting complement-fixing antibodies. The timing described does not fit that pattern."]},{"stem":"A recent liver transplant recipient develops fever, jaundice, and worsening liver function results. The patient is stable, but the cause is not yet known. What is the nurse’s priority action?","options":["Promptly report the fever and graft-specific changes to the transplant team and follow the ordered evaluation plan","Document the fever and wait for another assessment because fever alone does not establish rejection","Treat the presentation as rejection and independently increase the patient’s immunosuppressive medication"],"feedback":["Fever with new graft-specific dysfunction warrants prompt team communication. Rejection and infection are both relevant possibilities, and the team directs further evaluation and treatment.","Fever alone is not diagnostic, but the accompanying jaundice and worsening liver function are important graft changes that should not be left for routine follow-up.","The presentation may raise concern for rejection, but nurses should not independently diagnose it or change immunosuppressive treatment."]},{"stem":"A lung transplant recipient, months after surgery, reports gradually worsening exertional dyspnea and cough, and pulmonary function has declined. Which interpretation best guides the nurse’s communication?","options":["Possible chronic rejection, because progressive respiratory symptoms and declining function can occur with late graft injury","Possible hyperacute rejection, because respiratory symptoms indicate an immediate antibody-mediated graft event","Possible accelerated rejection, because a gradual decline months later is characteristic of early preexisting antibodies"],"feedback":["Chronic lung rejection can develop months or years after transplant and may involve cough, dyspnea, and declining lung function. Report the trend to the transplant team.","Hyperacute rejection occurs within 48 hours of transplant, not months later. The gradual course is inconsistent with that timing.","Accelerated rejection occurs several days after transplant and is associated with preexisting non-complement-fixing antibodies. A gradual decline months later is less consistent."]},{"stem":"A kidney transplant recipient develops fever, but urine output and creatinine remain near the patient’s recent baseline, with no new graft tenderness. The nurse has notified the team and is reassessing. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","options":["Fever alone does not establish rejection; continue to assess for changes in graft function and other causes with the team","The fever confirms acute rejection, even without a change in kidney graft function","Stable kidney findings rule out rejection, so further assessment for graft changes is unnecessary"],"feedback":["Fever can occur with rejection but is not specific, and infection is also a concern. Continued assessment of graft function and communication of changes are appropriate.","Fever alone cannot confirm rejection. The absence of new kidney-specific dysfunction makes it especially important not to conclude that rejection is the cause.","Stable findings at this assessment do not guarantee that graft dysfunction will not develop. Continue reassessment and follow the team’s evaluation plan."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Acute transplantation rejection","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535410/"},{"title":"Overview of Transplantation - Immunology; Allergic Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/transplantation/overview-of-transplantation?ruleredirectid=743"},{"title":"Overview of Transplantation - Overview of Transplantation - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-gb/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/transplantation/overview-of-transplantation"}],"frames":[{"label":"Immune graft injury","detail":"Recipient immune responses can injure an allograft, while immunosuppression lowers rejection risk but weakens defenses against infection."},{"label":"Kidney dysfunction pattern","detail":"One week after transplant, fever with rising creatinine, reduced urine output, and graft tenderness suggests a graft complication, not a definitive diagnosis."},{"label":"Transplant team communication","detail":"Compare findings with recent trends and promptly report dysfunction, fever, laboratory changes, and transplant timing; do not independently alter immunosuppression."},{"label":"Track graft function","detail":"Reassess symptoms, vital signs, urine output, and creatinine against prior trends; worsening function or instability warrants prompt escalation."}]},{"id":"s121","unit":"multi","title":"SIRS and evolving organ failure","objective":"Use SIRS cues, suspected causes, and changing organ function to recognize deterioration and communicate the need for timely team assessment.","concept":"SIRS is a systemic inflammatory response to infection or sterile stress such as trauma, surgery, or pancreatitis. Pathogen or tissue-damage signals activate immune pathways; inflammation can disrupt endothelial function, coagulation, and microcirculation, compromising organ function. SIRS alone does not establish infection or predict organ failure. Sepsis involves life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated response to infection, while MODS involves dysfunction in two or more organ systems.","recognize":"Look for a pattern or trend in temperature, pulse, breathing, and white blood cell findings, while considering the clinical context. Infection is one possible cause, but tissue injury and other noninfectious stressors can produce similar signs. New confusion, increasing oxygen needs, impaired perfusion, or declining urine output may signal organ dysfunction; neither meeting nor missing SIRS criteria settles the diagnosis.","respond":"Prioritize the whole clinical picture and promptly communicate concerning trends, suspected triggers, and any new organ-function changes to the responsible clinician or rapid response team, following local escalation protocols. Support assessment and ordered care for the underlying cause and affected organs. Do not independently prescribe treatment, equate SIRS with sepsis, or delay escalation while waiting for every SIRS finding to appear.","reassess":"After nursing actions or team-directed treatment, reassess vital-sign trends, mental status, oxygenation, perfusion, and urine output, comparing them with the patient’s prior condition. Report promptly if organ function worsens or new dysfunction appears, even if temperature or pulse improves; changing findings may indicate progression despite partial improvement.","trap":"Think of SIRS as a smoke alarm, not proof of a fire: sterile injury can trigger it, and the alarm alone cannot identify the cause. A quiet alarm does not guarantee safety either, so assess for evolving organ dysfunction even when the pattern is incomplete.","caseStory":"An adult admitted with acute pancreatitis develops a rising pulse and breathing rate, a temperature change, and increasing oxygen needs. No infection has been confirmed, and the nurse notices the patient is newly confused. The decision is whether to wait for confirmation of infection or escalate the change in condition now.","caseReason":"Pancreatitis can trigger a noninfectious systemic inflammatory response, so the findings do not by themselves establish sepsis. New confusion and increased oxygen needs suggest possible evolving organ dysfunction, warranting prompt team escalation and continued assessment rather than waiting for infection confirmation.","teachback":"How would you explain why a patient with signs of SIRS needs assessment for both the trigger and changing organ function, even when infection has not been confirmed?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult several hours after surgery has a temperature change, a fast pulse, rapid breathing, and an elevated white blood cell count. The patient’s oxygen needs, mental status, and perfusion are unchanged, and no infectious source is identified. Which interpretation should guide the nurse’s next communication?","options":["The findings fit a systemic inflammatory response, but do not establish infection; communicate the pattern and continue assessing for a cause and organ dysfunction.","The findings establish sepsis because several inflammatory signs are present, so communicate a confirmed infectious diagnosis.","The findings are expected after surgery and need no further attention unless the patient develops a confirmed infection."],"feedback":["Correct. Surgery can trigger a noninfectious inflammatory response, and SIRS findings alone do not establish infection; continued assessment for cause and organ dysfunction is appropriate.","SIRS signs can occur with infection or sterile stress, so they do not independently confirm sepsis.","Postoperative stress may contribute, but dismissing the pattern could miss evolving illness; trends and organ function still need attention."]},{"stem":"An adult being evaluated for a possible infection becomes newly confused, needs more oxygen, and has worsening blood pressure compared with earlier assessments. Which nursing action has the highest priority?","options":["Promptly escalate the change to the responsible clinician or rapid response team, report the trends, and support assessment and care under local protocols.","Continue routine monitoring until the patient meets every SIRS criterion, then notify the responsible clinician.","Focus on repeating the temperature and pulse, and defer escalation unless those findings continue to worsen."],"feedback":["Correct. New mental-status, respiratory, and circulatory changes may indicate evolving organ dysfunction, so prompt team communication should not wait for a complete SIRS pattern.","Waiting for all criteria can delay recognition; SIRS criteria are not required before escalating concerning organ-function changes.","Temperature and pulse matter, but focusing only on them overlooks the more urgent changes in oxygenation, mentation, and circulation."]},{"stem":"An adult is brought to critical care after exertional heat exposure with marked temperature elevation, confusion, and signs of impaired circulation. No infection is suspected. Which nursing interpretation and response best applies the principles of SIRS and evolving organ failure?","options":["Recognize heat illness as a possible noninfectious inflammatory trigger, promptly escalate the organ-function changes, and support team-directed care under local protocols.","Treat the inflammatory pattern as evidence of occult sepsis and direct the team toward infection treatment before evaluating other causes.","Wait to escalate until infection testing is completed, because noninfectious stress cannot cause systemic inflammation or organ dysfunction."],"feedback":["Correct. Heat stress is a recognized noninfectious trigger, and confusion with impaired circulation warrants prompt assessment for organ dysfunction and team-directed care.","Inflammatory findings do not identify infection as the cause; assuming sepsis could obscure the relevant trigger and assessment.","Noninfectious insults can produce systemic inflammation and organ injury, so waiting for infection testing risks delaying escalation."]},{"stem":"After team-directed treatment, an adult with systemic inflammatory findings has a lower temperature and slower pulse. The nurse now observes less urine output and new confusion. Which reassessment conclusion is most appropriate?","options":["The improved temperature and pulse do not rule out deterioration; promptly report the new findings as possible evolving organ dysfunction.","The systemic response is resolving because two vital signs improved, so continue the current plan without additional communication.","The urine output and confusion should be interpreted separately from the inflammatory response unless a new fever develops."],"feedback":["Correct. Improvement in some signs does not outweigh new changes that may indicate kidney or neurologic dysfunction; communicate them promptly.","Partial improvement does not establish recovery, especially when new organ-function concerns have appeared.","Organ dysfunction can evolve without a new fever, and these findings need consideration in the overall clinical picture."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome: molecular mechanisms and ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13365620/"},{"title":"Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547669/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Pancreatic inflammation","detail":"Pancreatitis can trigger systemic inflammation without infection, while inflammation may disrupt circulation and compromise organ function."},{"label":"Emerging organ changes","detail":"Rising pulse and breathing rate with new confusion and increased oxygen needs suggest possible evolving organ dysfunction."},{"label":"Escalate the change","detail":"Promptly communicate suspected triggers and new organ-function changes to the responsible clinician or rapid response team under local protocols."},{"label":"Track organ function","detail":"Reassess mental status, oxygenation, perfusion, and urine output; report worsening function even if temperature or pulse improves."}]},{"id":"s122","unit":"multi","title":"Recognizing a deteriorating airway","objective":"Recognize evolving airway compromise from anaphylaxis or angioedema and promptly coordinate emergency response within nursing role and local protocols.","concept":"Anaphylaxis releases mast cell mediators that can cause bronchoconstriction, tissue swelling, and vasodilation with fluid leakage. Angioedema is deeper tissue swelling that may obstruct the upper airway. Histamine-mediated swelling often occurs with itching or hives, while bradykinin-mediated swelling, including that associated with ACE inhibitors, usually lacks them. Either mechanism can threaten the airway, so worsening breathing or throat symptoms require urgent attention.","recognize":"Listen for stridor, a sense of throat closure, voice change, or increasing breathing difficulty, and look for tongue or facial swelling. Wheezing can reflect lower-airway narrowing, while stridor suggests upper-airway involvement. Rapid hives or itching with respiratory or circulatory symptoms supports anaphylaxis. Slower swelling without hives, especially during ACE inhibitor use, suggests bradykinin-mediated angioedema, but does not make airway risk less urgent.","respond":"Treat suspected anaphylaxis as an emergency: activate the response team, notify the responsible clinician, and promptly facilitate epinephrine under standing orders or local protocol. Prioritize airway assessment and preparation for team-led airway management if swelling or obstruction is progressing. Follow directions for oxygen, monitoring, and other care. Do not wait for laboratory confirmation or rely on antihistamines to control a threatened airway.","reassess":"After action, repeatedly assess voice, stridor, wheezing, breathing effort, swelling, blood pressure, and level of responsiveness. Report worsening throat symptoms, respiratory distress, hypotension, collapse, or lack of improvement immediately. A reduction in hives alone does not show that airway danger has resolved.","trap":"Do not use the presence of hives as a gatekeeper for recognizing a dangerous reaction. Think of hives as one possible warning light, not the airway alarm itself: ACE inhibitor-related swelling can threaten breathing without itching or hives.","caseStory":"A patient receiving an antibiotic develops itchy hives, lip and tongue swelling, and new noisy breathing. The nurse notes that the patient is becoming hoarse, although the hives are the most visible finding. The decision is whether to treat this as a skin reaction or an evolving emergency involving the airway.","caseReason":"Rapid hives and swelling with breathing or voice changes after a likely trigger fit anaphylaxis with possible upper-airway involvement. The nurse should promptly activate emergency response and facilitate epinephrine under local protocol rather than treating the hives alone or waiting for testing.","teachback":"When could angioedema threaten a patient's airway even without hives, and what cues would prompt you to escalate?","questions":[{"stem":"Shortly after a patient receives an antibiotic, the patient develops generalized itching, hives, tongue swelling, and new noisy breathing. Which interpretation best guides the nurse's immediate response?","options":["This is suspected anaphylaxis with possible airway involvement; activate emergency response and facilitate epinephrine under local protocol.","This is an isolated skin reaction because hives are prominent; give an antihistamine and reassess before escalating.","This is most consistent with bradykinin-mediated swelling because the tongue is involved; prioritize medication review over emergency response."],"feedback":["The rapid combination of skin findings, tongue swelling, and breathing symptoms after a likely trigger supports anaphylaxis and possible airway compromise.","Hives do not make the breathing symptoms less urgent, and antihistamines should not delay emergency treatment for suspected anaphylaxis.","Tongue swelling can occur with different mechanisms, but itching and hives with rapid onset after a drug exposure support a mast cell-mediated reaction rather than an isolated bradykinin pattern."]},{"stem":"A patient taking an ACE inhibitor develops progressive tongue swelling and stridor over several hours, without itching or hives. Which nursing action is the priority?","options":["Activate emergency response and urgently alert the team to the threatened airway so airway management can be assessed and prepared.","Give an antihistamine under the routine allergy protocol and wait to see whether the swelling begins to recede.","Treat the presentation as anaphylaxis with epinephrine as the only immediate action, postponing airway escalation unless the patient collapses."],"feedback":["Progressive tongue swelling and stridor threaten the airway. The absence of hives with ACE inhibitor use suggests bradykinin-mediated angioedema, for which urgent airway management is the priority.","Antihistamines have not been shown to be effective for bradykinin-mediated angioedema, and waiting risks delay while airway swelling progresses.","Epinephrine is used for suspected anaphylaxis, but it is not a substitute for urgent airway assessment when the presentation is obviously bradykinin-mediated and stridor is progressing."]},{"stem":"During a radiocontrast procedure, a patient suddenly develops hoarseness, wheezing, and hypotension but has no visible hives. What is the best interpretation and nursing response?","options":["A severe anaphylactic-like reaction is possible despite the absent hives; activate emergency response and facilitate immediate epinephrine under local protocol.","A nonallergic contrast effect is more likely because hives are absent; continue observation and escalate only if a rash appears.","An isolated lower-airway reaction is most likely; focus on inhaled treatment and defer emergency response while assessing for skin changes."],"feedback":["Radiocontrast can trigger anaphylactic-like reactions, and these can be clinically indistinguishable from anaphylaxis. Respiratory compromise and hypotension warrant immediate emergency action even without skin findings.","The absence of hives does not exclude a severe reaction, and waiting for a rash could delay treatment of respiratory and circulatory compromise.","Wheezing may reflect bronchoconstriction, but hoarseness and hypotension make an isolated airway problem an unsafe assumption; inhaled treatment does not replace emergency response."]},{"stem":"After treatment for a rapid allergic reaction, a patient's hives begin to fade, but the patient becomes more hoarse and stridor increases. Which reassessment finding should drive the nurse's next action?","options":["Worsening hoarseness and stridor indicate possible progressive upper-airway obstruction; escalate immediately to the emergency team.","Fading hives indicate overall improvement; continue routine observation unless the skin findings return.","The absence of new hypotension suggests the airway is stable; reassess after the remaining rash has cleared."],"feedback":["Increasing hoarseness and stridor are concerning for worsening upper-airway edema, even if skin symptoms are improving, and require urgent escalation.","Skin improvement does not establish that airway swelling has resolved. Airway signs must guide reassessment and escalation.","Anaphylaxis can involve respiratory compromise without ongoing hypotension, so stable blood pressure does not make increasing stridor safe to observe without escalation."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Anaphylaxis - Allergy and Immunology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/allergic-autoimmune-and-other-hypersensitivity-disorders/anaphylaxis?qt=anaphylaxis"},{"title":"Angioedema - Allergy and Immunology - MSD Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/allergic-autoimmune-and-other-hypersensitivity-disorders/angioedema?autoredirectid=24802"}],"frames":[{"label":"Mediator-driven swelling","detail":"Anaphylaxis can cause bronchoconstriction, swelling, and vasodilation; angioedema can threaten the airway through deeper tissue swelling."},{"label":"Airway warning signs","detail":"New noisy breathing, hoarseness, and tongue swelling after an antibiotic suggest possible upper-airway involvement in anaphylaxis."},{"label":"Activate emergency response","detail":"Activate the response team, notify the clinician, and facilitate epinephrine under standing orders or local protocol; prioritize airway assessment."},{"label":"Recheck airway status","detail":"Repeatedly assess voice, breathing effort, swelling, blood pressure, and responsiveness; report worsening throat symptoms or respiratory distress immediately."}]},{"id":"s123","unit":"psych","title":"Supporting recovery after an ICU stay","objective":"Use a structured, three-domain assessment to recognize possible post-intensive care syndrome and coordinate appropriate recovery support.","concept":"Post-intensive care syndrome describes new or worsened physical, cognitive, or mental health impairment after critical illness. Assess how function compares with baseline and consider other causes rather than assuming every symptom is PICS. Physical problems often involve ICU-acquired weakness related to immobility, illness, and ventilation; cognitive changes can affect memory, attention, or processing; and anxiety, depression, or post-traumatic stress may follow a frightening, disorienting ICU experience. These problems can overlap, persist after discharge, and affect family members too.","recognize":"Ask what has changed from the person’s pre-illness function, including daily activities, mobility, thinking, mood, and ability to manage usual responsibilities. Generalized weakness, memory or attention problems, anxiety, depression, and traumatic memories may fit PICS, especially in combination. Do not assume every new deficit is PICS: consider other causes, including stroke or another acute medical problem, and communicate concerning changes for evaluation.","respond":"Prioritize a respectful assessment across physical, cognitive, and mental health domains, using family observations when helpful and appropriate. Share the patient’s reported changes and functional concerns with the primary care or critical care team, and support referrals for rehabilitation or mental health care as indicated by the care plan. In the ICU, follow local protocols that support pain management, light sedation, delirium care, mobility, and family engagement; do not independently change treatment.","reassess":"After nursing actions, reassess the patient’s reported symptoms, mobility and daily function, attention or memory, and mood, comparing them with baseline and prior observations. Note whether needs are limiting recovery or participation in care. Escalate new or worsening deficits, concerning mental health symptoms, or functional decline to the responsible clinician for further evaluation.","trap":"Improving strength does not prove that recovery is complete; PICS is more like a dashboard with several indicators than a single engine light. A survivor may still have cognitive or emotional impairment, so assess each domain rather than letting one reassuring finding stand in for the whole picture.","caseStory":"Several weeks after discharge following severe sepsis, a patient reports difficulty climbing stairs and keeping track of appointments. The patient’s family describes forgetfulness, while the patient also reports feeling persistently anxious. The nurse must decide whether to focus only on physical rehabilitation or assess and communicate concerns across recovery domains.","caseReason":"The combination of new weakness, cognitive difficulty, and anxiety after critical illness is consistent with possible PICS, but it does not establish a diagnosis or rule out other causes. The nurse should compare function with the patient’s baseline, gather relevant observations, and communicate the concerns so the care team can evaluate needs across domains.","teachback":"When one aspect of an ICU survivor’s recovery is improving, what other domains would you assess before concluding that the person’s recovery is complete?","questions":[{"stem":"A patient seen in follow-up several weeks after an ICU stay for sepsis reports new generalized weakness and difficulty concentrating. The family says the patient managed daily activities and appointments independently before the illness. Which interpretation and nursing action are most appropriate?","options":["Consider possible impairment in more than one PICS domain, compare current function with baseline, and communicate the findings for evaluation.","Treat the weakness as the main recovery problem and defer cognitive questions until physical strength has returned.","Attribute the concentration problem to a psychiatric condition and direct the assessment only toward mood."],"feedback":["New physical and cognitive difficulties after critical illness may occur together, so baseline comparison and communication about both domains are appropriate.","Physical impairment does not exclude cognitive impairment, and waiting to assess it could leave a recovery need unrecognized.","Difficulty concentrating is not enough to identify a psychiatric cause; PICS assessment considers cognition as well as mental health and physical function."]},{"stem":"A recently discharged ICU survivor reports nightmares, anxiety, and reduced ability to manage usual household tasks. The patient is medically stable during the visit and has no reported new focal neurologic symptoms. Which nursing response should take priority?","options":["Assess and document mental health symptoms and functional changes, compare them with baseline, and communicate them to the care team.","Focus the visit on the household task difficulty and postpone discussion of nightmares unless the patient raises them again.","Reassure the patient that these symptoms are expected after intensive care and wait for the next routine visit."],"feedback":["Anxiety and traumatic memories can accompany functional impairment after an ICU stay; assessing these changes and communicating them supports evaluation across domains.","Nightmares and anxiety are relevant recovery concerns and should not be set aside while assessing function.","Symptoms may persist and affect recovery, so reassurance alone could delay recognition and appropriate follow-up."]},{"stem":"A home health nurse visits an adult who survived prolonged mechanical ventilation. The patient walks farther than at the prior visit but says memory problems still interfere with managing personal affairs. What is the best nursing interpretation and next step?","options":["Recognize that physical improvement can coexist with cognitive impairment, assess the ongoing functional concern, and communicate it to the care team.","Conclude that recovery is progressing adequately because mobility has improved, and discontinue cognitive follow-up.","Focus on increasing walking activity because the memory concern is outside post-ICU recovery assessment."],"feedback":["PICS can affect multiple domains, and improvement in mobility does not resolve or rule out cognitive impairment that interferes with daily function.","A single improving domain cannot establish that the patient is free of other persistent impairments.","Cognitive changes are part of post-ICU assessment, and difficulty managing affairs is a relevant functional concern to communicate."]},{"stem":"After a nurse supports a patient’s participation in an activity permitted by the care plan, the patient appears less physically fatigued. The patient still reports poor concentration and low mood, while a family member notes difficulty with daily activities. Which reassessment is most useful?","options":["Reassess physical function, cognition, mood, and daily activities against baseline, and communicate persistent or worsening concerns.","Record the improved fatigue as evidence that post-ICU impairment has resolved and end further assessment.","Repeat only the physical assessment because the family member’s report is less useful than the patient’s current appearance."],"feedback":["Reassessment across domains and comparison with baseline can identify persistent needs; patient and family observations can contribute to understanding function.","Reduced fatigue is a limited finding and does not establish recovery in cognitive, mental health, or daily functioning domains.","Cognitive and mood symptoms remain relevant, and appropriate family observations may help assess function, particularly when cognitive difficulties are present."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Post–intensive care syndrome","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10772956/"},{"title":"Epidemiology","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558964/"},{"title":"Post-intensive Care Syndrome: an Overview - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5506407/"}],"frames":[{"label":"After critical illness","detail":"Recovery after severe illness may involve overlapping physical, cognitive, and mental health problems that persist after discharge."},{"label":"Changes across domains","detail":"New stair-climbing difficulty, forgetfulness, and persistent anxiety indicate concerns across physical, cognitive, and emotional recovery."},{"label":"Share recovery concerns","detail":"Compare changes with pre-illness function, gather family observations when appropriate, and communicate concerns to the primary care or critical care team."},{"label":"Track daily function","detail":"Reassess mobility, daily activities, memory, attention, and mood; escalate new or worsening deficits or functional decline for evaluation."}]},{"id":"s124","unit":"ethics","title":"Culturally responsive care","objective":"By the end of this unit, the nurse will use patient-led assessment to identify and support an adult patient's spiritual or cultural preferences without making assumptions or imposing personal beliefs.","concept":"Spirituality is a broader search for meaning, purpose, and connection than formal religion alone; it may be expressed through faith, relationships, nature, art, or other sources. Serious illness can unsettle meaning and connectedness, creating spiritual distress and affecting coping and quality of life. Holistic nursing care therefore attends to emotional and spiritual concerns alongside physical needs. Patients' beliefs and needs can evolve, so ask rather than infer, and involve chaplains as specialists when useful.","recognize":"In critical care, notice existential questions, expressed loss of hope or meaning, tearfulness, and requests for prayer, quiet, clergy, or connection. Ask what the cue means to this person; tears alongside reassurance invite gentle clarification, not a conclusion about belief. Religious affiliation or its absence alone does not specify preferred support, and distress should not be presumed from identity.","respond":"Maintain the clinical plan while asking what provides the patient with strength or hope and what support they want. Listen, acknowledge expressed feelings, and accommodate requests such as quiet time or contact with clergy when feasible. A chaplain can support people of any belief or none, and the nurse may refer without a provider order. Do not pressure disclosure or introduce your own beliefs.","reassess":"After offering support, ask whether it helped and whether the patient wants continued presence, a chaplain, clergy, or a different approach. Attend to the patient's words and emotional cues rather than assuming calm means the concern is resolved. Escalate an unmet spiritual need or continuing distress to the appropriate chaplain or care team.","trap":"Treating religion and spirituality as synonyms is like assuming everyone reaches comfort by the same road: people may find meaning through faith, relationships, nature, or other sources. Prayer, referral, and other support should follow the patient's preference, not the nurse's assumption.","caseStory":"An adult in critical care says, “I don't understand why this is happening,” then becomes tearful while saying they are not religious. During a quiet interval, the nurse must decide whether to end the conversation, offer personal reassurance, or ask what kind of support would feel helpful.","caseReason":"The patient's words and tears are cues to clarify, not proof of a particular belief or request. The nurse can acknowledge the emotion and ask what provides strength or hope; if the patient wants support, the nurse can explore options such as quiet presence or a chaplain, who serves people of any belief or none.","teachback":"In a future critical-care conversation, what question could you ask to learn what gives a patient strength or hope without assuming their beliefs?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in an intensive care unit says, “I know there is no point in asking why this happened,” but becomes tearful. Which response best interprets the cue and supports culturally responsive care?","options":["“I noticed you became tearful. Would you like to tell me what is coming up for you, or what might help right now?”","“Your tears suggest that you may want me to contact a religious leader, so I will arrange that.”","“I have faced difficult times too, and my own beliefs helped me through them.”"],"feedback":["This gently identifies an observed cue, invites the patient to explain it, and lets the patient define the support they want.","Tearfulness may signal distress, but it does not establish a religious preference or consent to a referral.","The nurse's personal story shifts attention away from the patient's experience and may impose a perspective."]},{"stem":"During a quiet interval in critical care, a patient asks for time to pray without interruption. The patient is not requesting a change to the care plan. What is the best nursing response?","options":["Ask whether any practical support is wanted, then facilitate quiet time as feasible while continuing the care plan.","Explain that prayer is a private matter and avoid discussing it so the nurse remains neutral.","Offer to lead a prayer that reflects the nurse's beliefs to make the patient feel supported."],"feedback":["This respects the patient's expressed practice, checks what support is wanted, and accommodates the request when feasible.","Neutrality does not require ignoring a patient's expressed spiritual need; the nurse can ask how to support it without imposing beliefs.","The patient asked for time to pray, not for the nurse to lead prayer; support must follow the patient's preferences."]},{"stem":"In a rehabilitation setting, an adult patient says they do not belong to a religion but feel grounded by being outdoors and connected with family. They ask whom they could speak with about coping with illness. What is the best response?","options":["Ask what kind of support they want and offer a chaplain referral, explaining that chaplains support people with or without religious beliefs.","Suggest that a chaplain would not be appropriate because the patient does not identify with a religion.","Recommend contacting a religious congregation because illness-related coping is primarily a religious concern."],"feedback":["This recognizes the patient's own sources of meaning and offers a chaplain as an option without requiring religious affiliation.","Chaplains can support people regardless of whether they believe in a higher power or identify with a faith.","Spirituality is broader than religion, and the nurse should not redirect the patient toward a religious community without asking their preference."]},{"stem":"After sitting quietly with a patient who had expressed spiritual distress, the nurse notices that the patient appears calmer. Which reassessment best avoids a misleading conclusion?","options":["Ask whether the quiet presence was helpful and whether the patient wants any further support.","Document that the distress has resolved because the patient now appears calm.","Assume the patient no longer wants support because they have stopped speaking."],"feedback":["The patient's own response clarifies whether the intervention helped and whether further support is wanted.","A calmer appearance alone does not establish that spiritual distress has ended; reassess with the patient.","Silence does not establish refusal or resolution; ask rather than infer the patient's preference."]}],"sources":[{"title":"Chapter 18 Spirituality","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK591821/"},{"title":"35.2 Applications of Spirituality to Health Care ... - OpenStax","url":"https://openstax.org/books/fundamentals-nursing/pages/35-2-applications-of-spirituality-to-health-care"}],"frames":[{"label":"Meaning under strain","detail":"Serious illness can unsettle meaning or connection; spirituality may involve sources beyond formal religion or religious belief."},{"label":"Tears invite clarification","detail":"A patient who questions why illness is happening and becomes tearful is showing cues to explore, not proof of a specific belief."},{"label":"Ask what helps","detail":"Acknowledge the emotion and ask what provides strength or hope; offer quiet presence or a chaplain if the patient wants."},{"label":"Check support preferences","detail":"Ask whether the support helped and whether the patient wants continued presence, a chaplain, clergy, or a different approach."}]},{"id":"s125","unit":"ethics","title":"Comfort, dignity and caring practices","objective":"Use individualized nursing assessment and team-based support to promote comfort, dignity, and spiritual well-being for adults with advanced serious illness.","concept":"Palliative care can support quality of life alongside disease-directed treatment by addressing symptoms and the person’s psychological, social, and spiritual needs. Spirituality may involve religion, relationships, nature, art, meaning, or other sources of connection; it is not limited to belief in a higher power. Person-centered care asks what matters to the individual and coordinates appropriate support with the family or other people important to them.","recognize":"Notice physical symptoms such as pain or breathlessness and emotional cues such as anxiety, sadness, or questions about meaning. A patient’s expression of spiritual distress may include feeling disconnected or struggling to find purpose, but do not infer beliefs from a diagnosis or cultural identity. Ask what provides strength or hope and what support the patient wants; distinguish expressed preferences from assumptions.","respond":"First attend to reported discomfort and urgent concerns within nursing scope, orders, and local protocols. Listen without imposing beliefs, ask what would help, and support requested cultural or spiritual practices when feasible. With the patient’s agreement, communicate needs to the care team and involve a chaplain or the patient’s chosen spiritual support; a nurse may request a chaplain referral without a provider order.","reassess":"Ask whether the specific discomfort has eased and whether the support offered matches the patient’s stated preferences. Recheck for ongoing distress, unmet requests, and effects on family or other chosen supports. Escalate persistent or worsening symptoms, distress, or barriers to the requested support through the care team and relevant protocols.","trap":"Spiritual care is not a religious script that nurses apply to everyone; it is more like asking which source of support the person wants within reach. A patient’s silence or refusal of prayer does not establish either spiritual well-being or distress, so ask rather than interpret.","caseStory":"An adult in critical care with advanced illness reports pain and says, “I do not know what gives me strength anymore.” The patient’s daughter asks the nurse to arrange a prayer, but the patient has not expressed a religious preference. The nurse must decide what to address first and how to explore support without assuming what the patient wants.","caseReason":"The reported pain calls for prompt assessment and action within orders and protocols. The statement about strength suggests possible spiritual distress, so the nurse should listen and ask what support matters to the patient rather than accept the daughter’s request as the patient’s preference. If desired, the nurse can coordinate chaplain or other support with the team.","teachback":"When a seriously ill patient shows distress, how will you identify the patient’s own sources of meaning or support and determine whether your response has helped?","questions":[{"stem":"An adult in critical care with advanced illness becomes quiet and says, “I cannot see a reason to keep going.” The patient has not discussed religion, and physical discomfort has been addressed. Which interpretation and next nursing action best fit the cues?","options":["Recognize possible spiritual distress and ask what provides strength or hope and what support the patient wants.","Interpret the statement as evidence of a specific religious crisis and ask the family to arrange a religious ritual.","Treat the quietness as evidence that the patient wants privacy and avoid further discussion unless the patient raises it again."],"feedback":["Correct. The statement may signal difficulty finding meaning or purpose, so an open question can clarify the patient’s experience and preferences without assuming a belief.","This assumes a religious cause and transfers the decision to family before establishing what the patient wants.","Quietness alone does not show that the patient wants no support, and the expressed concern warrants a respectful invitation to talk."]},{"stem":"A patient with advanced serious illness in an intensive care unit reports pain and says that being disconnected from family has made the situation feel unbearable. Which nursing action is the best priority?","options":["Assess the pain and act within orders and protocols, then ask what connection or support the patient wants and coordinate it.","Arrange a family visit first and defer pain assessment because the patient identified separation as the main concern.","Request a chaplain visit immediately and postpone discussion of family contact until the chaplain assesses the patient."],"feedback":["Correct. This addresses a reported physical symptom while exploring the patient’s stated social and emotional concern and coordinating individualized support.","The patient’s concern matters, but it does not justify deferring assessment and care for reported pain.","A chaplain may be helpful if the patient wants one, but the nurse should not assume that this is the preferred support or delay other needs."]},{"stem":"In a different setting, a patient receiving care for a serious illness says that time outdoors and listening to music help them feel connected, but they do not identify with a religion. What is the most appropriate nursing response?","options":["Acknowledge those sources of meaning and ask what practical support would help the patient connect with them during care.","Explain that spiritual care is usually provided through religious practice and offer to contact the patient’s family instead.","Refer the patient to a chaplain only if the patient first identifies a belief in a higher power."],"feedback":["Correct. Spirituality can include connection with nature and art, and asking what would help respects the patient’s own understanding and preferences.","This wrongly limits spirituality to religion and substitutes family support without asking what the patient wants.","Chaplains can support people regardless of belief in a higher power, and the patient’s stated preferences should guide whether a referral is wanted."]},{"stem":"After a patient with advanced illness accepts a quiet conversation but declines a chaplain visit, the patient appears calmer. Which reassessment best determines whether the nursing response met the patient’s needs?","options":["Ask whether the conversation helped, whether distress remains, and whether any other support is wanted.","Conclude that spiritual distress has resolved because the patient appears calmer and declined a chaplain.","Ask the family whether the patient seems more spiritually settled and use their impression as the main measure."],"feedback":["Correct. The patient’s own report can clarify whether distress persists and whether further support is desired; declining a chaplain does not preclude other needs.","A calmer appearance and refusal of one form of support do not establish that distress has resolved.","Family observations may provide context, but they do not replace asking the patient about their experience and preferences."]}],"sources":[{"title":"The Delivery of Person-Centered, Family-Oriented End-of-Life Care","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK285676/"},{"title":"Chapter 18 Spirituality - Nursing Fundamentals - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591821/table/"}],"frames":[{"label":"Comfort amid illness","detail":"Palliative care can address symptoms and psychological, social, and spiritual needs alongside disease-directed treatment."},{"label":"Explore personal meaning","detail":"Pain and uncertainty about sources of strength call for attention; do not assume the patient shares the daughter’s religious preference."},{"label":"Address pain and ask","detail":"Assess and address pain within orders and protocols, listen, and ask what support matters to the patient before coordinating assistance."},{"label":"Reassess comfort and support","detail":"Ask whether pain has eased and support matches the patient’s preferences; escalate persistent distress, symptoms, or barriers to requested support."}]}];
const CONCEPTS=[{"id":"abdominal-veins","definition":"Portal-systemic collaterals are enlarged pathways that connect portal veins with veins of the general circulation. When these pathways become enlarged in the distal esophagus or stomach, they are called varices and can bleed.","structure":"The portal venous system carries blood from abdominal organs toward the liver, while systemic veins return blood toward the heart. With sustained portal pressure, collateral pathways can enlarge; gastroesophageal collaterals form tortuous veins beneath the lining of the distal esophagus or stomach.","normal":"Normally, portal blood flows toward and through the liver before joining the systemic circulation. When portal pressure is persistently elevated, collateral channels can develop between portal and systemic veins. These channels divert some blood around the portal system and can partially decompress it.","change":"As collateral pathways enlarge, submucosal veins in the distal esophagus or stomach may become tortuous and congested, forming varices. They provide an alternate route for blood but are vulnerable to rupture. A rupture can produce severe gastrointestinal bleeding; collateral flow also bypasses some liver processing.","cues":"Vomiting blood or dark, coffee-ground-like material can signal upper gastrointestinal bleeding, while visible abdominal-wall veins may be a clue to collateral circulation. Neither finding alone establishes varices or portal hypertension; assessment must consider the full clinical context.","distinction":"Varices are enlarged collateral veins associated with portal hypertension, not simply any visible vein or any episode of gastrointestinal bleeding. Collaterals may partly decompress portal pressure, but they can also create fragile vessels that bleed.","related":["liver","gut","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can sustained elevation of portal pressure lead to enlarged veins in the distal esophagus?","options":["Collateral connections form between portal and systemic veins and can enlarge over time.","The esophageal veins normally carry portal blood directly through the liver.","Portal pressure causes the esophageal lining to produce new arteries."],"feedback":["Correct: portal hypertension promotes portosystemic collateral pathways, including gastroesophageal veins that may become enlarged.","The described collateral route connects portal and systemic circulations; it does not carry blood through the liver as stated.","The mechanism concerns venous collateral enlargement, not new arterial production."]},{"stem":"A patient vomits blood. What is the most accurate inference from this cue alone?","options":["It signals possible gastrointestinal bleeding, but does not by itself identify varices as the cause.","It confirms rupture of an esophageal varix from portal hypertension.","It rules out bleeding from other gastrointestinal sources."],"feedback":["Correct: vomiting blood is a concerning bleeding cue, but the cause cannot be determined from that sign alone.","The cue does not confirm a particular source or diagnosis; varices are one possible cause in the relevant context.","The cue does not exclude other causes of gastrointestinal bleeding."]}],"title":"Portal-systemic collaterals and varices","unit":"gi","aliases":["esophageal varices","variceal bleeding","varices","collaterals"],"model":"portal","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448078/","title":"Esophageal Varices - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices","title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hepatic-and-biliary-disorders/approach-to-the-patient-with-liver-disease/portal-hypertension","title":"Portal Hypertension"}]},{"id":"adh","definition":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) helps the kidneys conserve water by changing how much water is reabsorbed into the body. Its effect depends on both hormone release and the kidney’s response.","structure":"ADH acts on V2 receptors on principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct of the nephron. The signaling pathway moves aquaporin-2 water channels into the urine-facing membrane, where water can enter the cells.","normal":"Normally, signals such as increased plasma osmolality or reduced circulating volume promote ADH release. ADH activates V2-receptor signaling in collecting-duct cells, placing aquaporin-2 channels in the apical membrane so water is reabsorbed and urine becomes more concentrated.","change":"With excessive, unregulated ADH effect, more water is reabsorbed and urine becomes concentrated, expanding total-body water and potentially diluting plasma sodium. With deficient ADH or renal resistance, collecting ducts conserve less water, producing greater volumes of dilute urine.","cues":"A pattern of concentrated urine with low plasma sodium may fit excess ADH effect, while large volumes of dilute urine may fit deficient hormone action or renal resistance. These findings are clues, not proof of a specific cause.","distinction":"A low sodium value alone does not establish SIADH; other causes of hyponatremia must be considered. Likewise, dilute urine alone does not distinguish deficient ADH release from kidney resistance.","related":["nephron","sodium","fluid"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient has excessive ADH activity. Which change most directly explains why the urine becomes more concentrated?","options":["More aquaporin-2 channels enter the collecting-duct apical membrane, increasing water reabsorption.","The glomerulus filters more water because ADH opens aquaporin-2 channels there.","The collecting duct becomes less permeable to water, so more water remains in urine."],"feedback":["Correct: ADH signaling promotes aquaporin-2 insertion into the urine-facing membrane, allowing water reabsorption.","Aquaporin-2 action described here occurs in collecting-duct cells, not in the glomerulus.","Reduced water permeability would leave more water in the urine and make it less concentrated, the opposite of the described ADH effect."]},{"stem":"A person produces large volumes of dilute urine despite normal or elevated ADH levels. Which mechanism best fits this pattern?","options":["The kidney is not responding adequately to ADH.","ADH is causing excessive water reabsorption in the collecting duct.","Insulin is moving glucose transporters into muscle-cell membranes."],"feedback":["Correct: Renal resistance to ADH limits aquaporin-2-mediated water reabsorption even when hormone levels are present.","Excessive ADH effect would conserve water and tend to concentrate urine, rather than produce large volumes of dilute urine.","Insulin affects glucose uptake in insulin-sensitive tissues; it does not explain this renal water-conservation pattern."]}],"title":"Antidiuretic hormone","unit":"endo","aliases":["antidiuretic hormone","vasopressin secretion","ADH","water reabsorption"],"model":"osmosis","sources":[{"url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/endotext/endocrin-test-siadh/","title":"Endocrine Testing for the Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone Secretion (SIADH)"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526069/","title":"Physiology, Vasopressin - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7996474/","title":"Diabetes Insipidus: Pathogenesis, Diagnosis, and Clinical ..."}]},{"id":"afterload","title":"Afterload and vascular resistance","unit":"cardio","model":"pump","aliases":["afterload","vascular resistance","systemic vascular resistance","vasoconstriction"],"definition":"Afterload is the load a ventricle must overcome to eject blood. Arterial pressure and resistance in blood vessels influence it.","structure":"The left ventricle is the heart’s main pumping chamber for the body. Blood leaves it through the aortic valve and enters the aorta, then the systemic arteries. Pressure and resistance in this arterial system oppose the ventricle’s ejection. The right ventricle faces a similar load from the pulmonary vessels.","normal":"During filling, blood enters the ventricles. When the left ventricle contracts, it builds enough pressure to open the aortic valve and push blood into the arteries. Arterial pressure and vessel resistance provide some opposition to ejection; in a healthy system, the heart and vessels work together to sustain blood flow and pressure.","change":"When systemic vascular resistance or arterial pressure rises, the left ventricle must generate more pressure to eject blood. Greater opposition can limit muscle shortening, leave more blood in the ventricle after contraction, and reduce stroke volume. If the pressure load persists, the ventricle may thicken and become less able to relax and fill.","cues":"A high measured systolic blood pressure can be a clue to increased left ventricular afterload, but it does not measure afterload directly. If forward output falls, low blood pressure or signs of poor tissue perfusion may occur. These observations have many possible causes and do not establish a diagnosis on their own.","distinction":"Arterial pressure is a practical estimate of afterload, not afterload itself. One high blood-pressure reading does not prove that the ventricle is dysfunctional.","related":["arterial","left-ventricle","output"],"questions":[{"stem":"If systemic vascular resistance rises while other factors are initially unchanged, what is the expected effect on left ventricular ejection?","options":["Afterload rises, so stroke volume tends to fall and end-systolic volume tends to rise.","Afterload falls, so stroke volume tends to rise and end-systolic volume tends to fall.","Preload rises directly, so stroke volume must increase."],"feedback":["Correct: greater resistance opposes ejection and can leave more blood in the ventricle after contraction.","A rise in systemic vascular resistance increases, rather than decreases, the load opposing ejection.","Resistance does not directly increase preload; preload describes ventricular stretch at the end of filling."]},{"stem":"Why can stiff arteries increase the load faced by the left ventricle?","options":["Reduced arterial compliance can make pressure rise more during ejection, increasing the opposing load.","Stiff arteries make the ventricle fill more during diastole, directly increasing preload.","Stiff arteries lower arterial pressure, reducing the pressure the ventricle must overcome."],"feedback":["Correct: less compliant arteries show a greater pressure rise during ejection, which can increase afterload.","Arterial stiffness does not directly increase ventricular filling; filling relates to preload and diastolic function.","Reduced arterial compliance tends to increase, not lower, arterial pressure and afterload."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547686/","title":"Physiology, Stroke Volume - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-17279/","title":"Physiology, Afterload Reduction"}]},{"id":"airway","title":"Conducting airways","unit":"resp","model":"airway","aliases":["bronchi","bronchioles","airway resistance","conducting airways"],"definition":"Conducting airways are the passages that carry air toward the lungs. They move air but do not themselves perform gas exchange.","structure":"The conducting pathway starts at the trachea below the larynx, which connects the throat to the chest. The esophagus lies behind the trachea. In the chest, the trachea divides into right and left main bronchi; these branch into lobar and segmental bronchi, then smaller bronchioles, ending at terminal bronchioles.","normal":"During a breath, air passes from the trachea through progressively branching bronchi and bronchioles. C-shaped cartilage supports the trachea, while smaller bronchioles lack cartilage. Cilia and mucus in the trachea and bronchi help move trapped material toward the throat; the airway also warms and humidifies air. Terminal bronchioles pass airflow onward to respiratory bronchioles and alveoli.","change":"When a conducting airway narrows, becomes blocked, or collapses, air has greater difficulty reaching the distal lung. Less airflow may reach respiratory bronchioles and alveoli, reducing ventilation of the gas-exchange region. Loss of airway patency can therefore affect how well air reaches the structures where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged.","cues":"At the bedside, reduced or noisy airflow, altered breathing effort, or cough can be clues that airflow or airway protection is affected. These findings are not specific: cough is a protective reflex, and one observation alone cannot identify the site or cause. The overall pattern matters.","distinction":"A cough does not by itself prove that a conducting airway is obstructed. Terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways; respiratory bronchioles begin the respiratory region.","related":["ventilation","alveoli","oxygenation"],"questions":[{"stem":"An inhaled object enters a main bronchus. Which side is more prone to receive it, based on the usual anatomy?","options":["The right, because its main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical","The left, because its main bronchus is wider and more vertical","Either side equally, because the main bronchi have the same shape"],"feedback":["Correct: the right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left.","The left main bronchus is longer and less vertical than the right, so it is less prone to aspiration.","The main bronchi differ in shape, which makes the right more prone to aspiration."]},{"stem":"Which structure normally lacks cartilage and carries air toward the terminal bronchioles?","options":["Bronchioles","Trachea","Main bronchi"],"feedback":["Correct: bronchioles lack cartilage and conduct airflow toward the terminal bronchioles.","The trachea is supported by C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings.","The main bronchi branch from the trachea and precede the smaller bronchioles in the airway tree."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459258/","title":"Anatomy, Airway - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK459258/","title":"Anatomy, Airway - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"alveoli","title":"Alveoli and gas exchange","unit":"resp","model":"alveolar","aliases":["alveolar-capillary membrane","alveolar membrane","alveoli","alveolus","gas exchange"],"definition":"Alveoli are tiny air spaces in the lungs. Oxygen moves from their air into the blood, while carbon dioxide moves from the blood into the air to be breathed out.","structure":"Alveoli sit at the ends of the lung’s smallest airways. Each air space is surrounded by capillaries, tiny blood vessels. Their walls lie close together, with a very thin layer of cells between air and blood. This close arrangement allows gases to cross between the lungs and bloodstream.","normal":"When you breathe in, air reaches the alveoli. Oxygen crosses the thin air-blood barrier into nearby capillaries, while carbon dioxide crosses the other way and leaves when you breathe out. This crossing is called diffusion: the natural movement of gases across a boundary. Blood flow carries oxygen away and brings more carbon dioxide to the lungs.","change":"If the barrier becomes thicker or the area available for exchange becomes smaller, less oxygen may cross into the blood in the same time. Poor matching between air reaching alveoli and blood reaching their capillaries can also reduce exchange. The result can be lower oxygen in blood traveling from the lungs to the body.","cues":"A low measured blood oxygen level can occur when less oxygen enters capillary blood, but it does not identify the cause. Breathing pattern or symptoms alone cannot show whether the issue is airflow, blood flow, or gas movement across the barrier. Observations need context; an isolated sign does not establish a diagnosis.","distinction":"Air moving into and out of the lungs does not by itself guarantee effective gas exchange. Oxygen transfer also depends on movement across the barrier and blood flow through nearby capillaries.","related":["ventilation","gas-gradient","oxygenation"],"questions":[{"stem":"Air reaches an alveolus, but little blood flows through its nearby capillaries. Which part of gas exchange is most directly limited?","options":["Delivery of blood to the gas-exchange surface","Movement of air into the alveolus","Thickness of the air-blood barrier"],"feedback":["Correct: reduced blood flow limits perfusion, the delivery of blood to alveolar capillaries.","Airflow describes ventilation; the question says air reaches the alveolus, while blood flow is limited.","Barrier thickness affects diffusion, but no change to the barrier is described."]},{"stem":"If the alveolar wall thickens while airflow and blood flow continue, what is the likely effect on oxygen transfer?","options":["Oxygen crosses into the blood less readily","More oxygen crosses because the barrier is thicker","Oxygen transfer is unchanged because airflow continues"],"feedback":["Correct: a thicker barrier makes diffusion less effective, so less oxygen may cross in the same time.","A thicker barrier impedes rather than increases gas movement across it.","Airflow is important, but effective exchange also depends on diffusion across the barrier."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/lung-and-airway-disorders/biology-of-the-lungs-and-airways/exchanging-oxygen-and-carbon-dioxide","title":"Exchanging Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide - Lung and Airway ..."},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539907/","title":"Physiology, Pulmonary Ventilation and Perfusion - StatPearls - NCBI"}]},{"id":"anastomosis","definition":"An anastomosis is a surgically joined connection between two segments of the gastrointestinal tract. A leak occurs when the join breaks down and contents pass through an unintended defect.","structure":"The anastomosis is the joined area of bowel or other gastrointestinal segments after surgery. Its healing depends in part on a well-perfused, tension-free connection; a defect can create communication between the inside of the tract and surrounding tissues.","normal":"Normally, the joined tissue remains intact as it heals, keeping gastrointestinal contents within the tract. Adequate local blood supply and a tension-free connection support healing, while fibrin forms a network in which fibroblastic cells can grow and contribute to scarring.","change":"If the anastomotic site breaks down, a defect allows communication between the intestinal interior and the surrounding compartment. Poor blood supply, tension, infection, or impaired tissue viability can undermine healing and contribute to leakage.","cues":"Fever, pain, respiratory symptoms, abnormal drainage, or changes in vital signs may prompt concern for a leak, but presentations vary and can be nonspecific. Findings such as gas, food, feces, or bile in local drainage are more suggestive, yet require clinical interpretation.","distinction":"Postoperative fever or pain alone does not diagnose an anastomotic leak. A leak specifically involves a defect at the joined site that permits unintended communication.","related":["gut","perfusion","inflammation"],"questions":[{"stem":"What feature defines an anastomotic leak?","options":["A defect at the joined site that allows unintended communication with the surrounding compartment.","Any fever that occurs after gastrointestinal surgery.","A normally healing connection with an intact intestinal wall."],"feedback":["Correct: the leak is defined by breakdown at the join that creates unintended communication.","Fever may raise concern, but it is nonspecific and does not define a leak.","An intact connection describes normal healing, not leakage."]},{"stem":"Why can poor local perfusion increase the risk of anastomotic breakdown?","options":["Healing tissue depends in part on adequate blood supply, so impaired perfusion can undermine healing.","Reduced blood supply guarantees that gastrointestinal contents will leave the tract.","Perfusion determines whether maternal and fetal blood mix at the surgical site."],"feedback":["Correct: adequate blood supply supports tissue healing, and impaired perfusion is a risk factor for breakdown.","Poor perfusion can contribute to risk, but it does not guarantee a leak.","Maternal-fetal blood exchange is unrelated to gastrointestinal anastomotic healing."]}],"title":"Anastomosis and tissue healing","unit":"gi","aliases":["anastomotic leak","anastomosis","wound healing","dehiscence"],"model":"gut","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5527167/","title":"Anastomotic Leakage after Upper Gastrointestinal Surgery: Endoscopic Treatment"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12077910/","title":"Endoscopic Treatment of Anastomotic Leakage in the Upper ..."},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10700696/","title":"Different Types of Management for Anastomotic Leak Post ... - NIH"}]},{"id":"aorta","title":"Aortic wall and dissection","unit":"cardio","model":"coronary","aliases":["aortic dissection","aortic wall","aorta"],"definition":"The aorta is the body’s largest artery, carrying blood away from the heart. Aortic dissection occurs when blood enters a tear or split in its wall and creates a second passage within the wall.","structure":"The aorta begins where it leaves the heart, passes upward and curves into an arch, then travels down through the chest and abdomen. Its inner lining is the intima, its middle layer contains muscle and elastic tissue, and its outer layer provides support. Branch arteries leave the aorta to supply the body.","normal":"With each heartbeat, the left ventricle pushes oxygen-rich blood into the aorta. The aorta carries this blood away from the heart, and its elastic wall stretches as blood enters and recoils between beats. Branch arteries then carry blood to organs and tissues, supporting their ongoing need for oxygen and nutrients.","change":"A tear in the inner lining, or bleeding within the middle layer, can let blood separate layers of the aortic wall. This creates a false lumen, meaning an abnormal passage alongside the normal channel. The split may extend along the aorta and interfere with blood flow to branch arteries; near the heart, it may also affect the aortic valve.","cues":"A dissection may cause sudden, severe chest or back pain that can move as the split extends. Unequal limb pulses or blood pressures may occur, and reduced blood supply, called malperfusion, can affect an organ or limb. A new aortic-regurgitation murmur may occur with proximal dissection, but no single sign confirms the diagnosis.","distinction":"A normal ECG does not rule out aortic dissection. Unequal pulses can raise concern, but do not prove that dissection is present.","related":["arterial","perfusion","ischemia"],"questions":[{"stem":"A dissection involves the ascending aorta. Which Stanford type describes it?","options":["Type A","Type B","Neither type, because the system classifies only the aortic arch"],"feedback":["Correct: Stanford Type A includes any dissection involving the ascending aorta.","In the Stanford system, type B spares the ascending aorta. A descending dissection can extend into the abdominal aorta.","The Stanford system classifies dissections by whether the ascending aorta is involved."]},{"stem":"A dissection affects blood flow through a renal artery. Which change could result?","options":["Reduced urine output","A new aortic-regurgitation murmur","Fluid in the left pleural space"],"feedback":["Correct: reduced blood supply from renal artery involvement can lead to low or absent urine output.","An aortic-regurgitation murmur is associated with involvement near the aortic valve, not specifically a renal artery.","Fluid in the left pleural space can occur when blood or inflammatory fluid leaks into that space, rather than as a direct sign of renal artery involvement."]}],"sources":[{"title":"ACC/AHA guideline: classification of aortic dissection","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9860464/"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/diseases-of-the-aorta-and-its-branches/aortic-dissection","title":"Aortic Dissection"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/diseases-of-the-aorta-and-its-branches/aortic-dissection?langselector=1","title":"Aortic Dissection - Aortic Dissection - MSD Manual ..."}]},{"id":"arousal","definition":"Arousal is the state of being awake and responsive to the environment. It depends on functioning cerebral hemispheres and brain-stem arousal mechanisms.","structure":"The ascending arousal system consists of interconnected nuclei in the upper pons, midbrain, basal forebrain, and hypothalamus. These nuclei project to the thalamus and cerebral cortex, supporting alertness alongside the function of both cerebral hemispheres.","normal":"In ordinary wakefulness, the cerebral hemispheres and brain-stem arousal system work together to support alertness and responsiveness to external stimuli. Interconnected nuclei send signals toward the thalamus and cortex, helping sustain the level of consciousness needed for attention and interaction.","change":"When both cerebral hemispheres or important parts of the brain-stem arousal system are impaired, alertness and responsiveness can decrease. Diffuse toxic or metabolic disturbances and focal brain injury are among the possible mechanisms; the specific cause cannot be inferred from reduced responsiveness alone.","cues":"Reduced responsiveness to external stimuli can signal impaired alertness, while fluctuating attention and cognition may suggest delirium in an acute clinical context. These observations are not diagnoses by themselves: the pattern, timing, and accompanying clinical circumstances help distinguish possible causes.","distinction":"Delirium is characterized mainly by an acute, fluctuating disturbance in attention and cognition, rather than simply reduced wakefulness. Coma describes unresponsiveness from which a person cannot be aroused, with the eyes remaining closed.","related":["neurons","delirium","sleep"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which structures must work together to maintain ordinary alertness?","options":["The cerebral hemispheres and the brain-stem arousal system","The arachnoid granulations and the ventricular system","The postsynaptic muscle receptors and the neuromuscular junction"],"feedback":["Alertness depends on intact cerebral hemispheres and a functioning brain-stem arousal system.","Arachnoid granulations and ventricles are involved in CSF circulation, not the core arousal mechanism.","Muscle receptors and the neuromuscular junction support nerve-to-muscle signaling, not alertness."]},{"stem":"A person has an acute, fluctuating disturbance in attention and cognition. Which interpretation best fits this pattern without overclaiming?","options":["It is consistent with delirium, although the observation alone does not identify its cause.","It establishes coma because any attention disturbance means the person cannot be aroused.","It proves a focal brain-stem injury because attention is fluctuating."],"feedback":["Acute fluctuation in attention and cognition is characteristic of delirium, but many medical conditions or drug exposures can contribute.","Coma is defined by unresponsiveness from which the person cannot be aroused, not by attention disturbance alone.","Fluctuating attention does not by itself localize a problem to the brain stem or prove a specific injury."]}],"title":"Attention and arousal","unit":"neuro","aliases":["arousal","attention","alertness","level of consciousness"],"model":"synapse","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium","title":"Delirium - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/coma-and-impaired-consciousness/overview-of-coma-and-impaired-consciousness","title":"Overview of Coma and Impaired Consciousness - Neurology"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium?ruleredirectid=749","title":"Delirium - Delirium - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"arterial","definition":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart, while veins return blood toward it. Their wall structure and pressure differ along this route.","structure":"Arteries and arterioles carry blood away from the heart and have relatively thick, muscular walls that withstand higher pressure. Veins and venules return blood after capillary passage; their thinner walls and larger lumens accommodate blood at lower pressure.","normal":"During normal circulation, ventricular contraction sends blood into arteries, where elastic walls expand and recoil to help sustain the pressure gradient. Blood then passes through capillaries to venules and veins, whose lower-pressure, more compliant channels return it toward the heart.","change":"If arterial walls become rigid and cannot expand and recoil normally, resistance to blood flow can increase and blood pressure can rise, requiring the heart to pump harder. In veins, increased blood volume can widen the compliant vessel rather than produce the same arterial pressure pattern.","cues":"A palpable arterial pulse reflects pressure waves from ventricular ejection, but its presence alone does not establish adequate tissue flow or a particular blood pressure. Veins may visibly widen as volume increases; appearance alone cannot establish the cause.","distinction":"A palpable pulse is not proof that every tissue is receiving adequate flow, and a widened vein alone does not identify a disease. Arteries and veins have different pressure and wall characteristics.","related":["exchange","perfusion","map"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which vessel feature helps maintain arterial pressure between heartbeats?","options":["Elastic arterial walls recoil after expanding.","Thin venous walls accommodate increased blood volume.","Capillary walls allow exchange of gases and nutrients."],"feedback":["Elastic recoil helps sustain the pressure gradient after ventricular ejection.","Venous compliance helps veins accommodate volume, but it is not the arterial recoil mechanism.","Capillary exchange serves tissue transfer rather than maintaining arterial pressure."]},{"stem":"A rigid arterial wall cannot expand and recoil effectively. Which change is most consistent with the described mechanism?","options":["Resistance to blood flow may rise, increasing pressure and the heart's pumping work.","Venous pressure must fall because arterial walls are less elastic.","Capillary exchange stops because all vessels lose their muscular walls."],"feedback":["The evidence links loss of arterial expansion and recoil with greater resistance, higher pressure, and more cardiac work.","The described arterial change does not establish a fall in venous pressure.","The change concerns arterial elasticity and does not imply that all vessels lose their walls or capillary exchange stops."]}],"title":"Arteries, veins and pressure","unit":"cardio","aliases":["arterial pressure","blood pressure","arterial line","arteries","veins"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/biology-of-the-heart-and-blood-vessels/biology-of-the-blood-vessels","title":"Biology of the Blood Vessels - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders"},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-1-structure-and-function-of-blood-vessels","title":"20.1 Structure and Function of Blood Vessels - Anatomy and ..."},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-2-blood-flow-blood-pressure-and-resistance","title":"20.2Blood Flow, Blood Pressure, and Resistance - OpenStax"}]},{"id":"barrier","definition":"Protective barriers are the skin and mucous membranes that help keep microbes from entering body tissues. They are part of innate defenses present from birth.","structure":"Skin provides an external barrier, while mucous membranes line sites such as the respiratory and digestive tracts. Their epithelial cells form a continuous surface; skin’s outer epidermal cells contain keratin, and mucus coats and protects mucosal surfaces.","normal":"With an intact skin surface, tightly packed, keratin-rich epidermal cells resist mechanical damage and microbial entry. Mucosal epithelial cells joined by tight junctions maintain a continuous lining; their mucus traps debris and microbes, while secretions can contain antimicrobial substances.","change":"When the skin is broken, microbes can enter and infect nearby tissue or spread deeper. If a pathogen breaches the mucosal lining, the physical barrier is less effective; the usual trapping and protective separation no longer fully prevent contact with underlying tissue.","cues":"At the bedside, a visible wound, incision, burn, or disrupted mucosal surface signals that a physical barrier may be compromised. This finding raises concern for microbial entry, but alone does not establish infection.","distinction":"A break in a barrier is a risk for microbial entry, not proof that infection is present. Inflammation can follow either tissue injury or successful pathogen entry.","related":["skin","inflammation","neutrophils"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient has a new break in the skin. Which mechanism best explains why this can increase infection risk?","options":["The break can let microbes enter tissue that the intact skin would help protect.","The break causes mucosal mucus to stop trapping debris.","The break proves that microbes have already caused an infection."],"feedback":["Correct: intact skin is a physical barrier, and a break can provide an entry point for microbes.","Mucus is a protective secretion on mucosal surfaces, not the mechanism described for a skin break.","A barrier break increases risk but does not by itself establish that infection is present."]},{"stem":"What is one way mucus helps protect the mucous membrane lining of the respiratory tract?","options":["It traps debris, including microbes, on the surface.","It makes epidermal cells mechanically tough by supplying keratin.","It seals a wound in the skin with tightly packed cells."],"feedback":["Correct: mucus covers the mucosal surface and traps debris, including microbes.","Keratin in the epidermis helps make skin mechanically tough; it is not mucus’s function.","Tightly packed skin cells contribute to the skin barrier, whereas mucus protects mucosal surfaces."]}],"title":"Protective barriers and infection","unit":"skin","aliases":["skin barrier","mucosal barrier","barrier function"],"model":"skin","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK610837/","title":"INFECTION"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/infections/biology-of-infectious-disease/defenses-against-infection","title":"Defenses Against Infection - Infections - Merck Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/infectious-diseases/biology-of-infectious-disease/host-defense-mechanisms-against-infection?ruleredirectid=741","title":"Host Defense Mechanisms Against Infection - Infectious Disease - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"bladder","definition":"Urinary outflow obstruction is a structural or functional hindrance to normal urine flow. Urine may back up and impair kidney function.","structure":"The relevant context is the urinary tract, which carries urine from the kidneys through the ureters to the bladder and out through the urethra. Obstruction can occur at different levels along this route.","normal":"Normally, urine flows through the urinary tract and drains from the bladder. An open pathway allows urine to leave the body without backing up toward the kidneys, supporting normal urinary drainage.","change":"When an obstruction hinders urine flow, urine can back up and raise pressure within the urinary tract. Increased intratubular pressure, local ischemia, and sometimes infection can contribute to kidney dysfunction. The effects depend on the obstruction's location and course.","cues":"Difficulty starting or maintaining urination, a weaker stream, dribbling, incomplete emptying, or reduced urine amount can be bedside signals of impaired outflow. Flank pain may also occur, but these cues alone do not establish obstruction.","distinction":"Reduced urine output alone does not prove urinary obstruction; other causes can also reduce output. Obstruction may occur at different urinary tract levels, so symptoms alone do not locate it.","related":["nephron","filtrate","oliguria"],"questions":[{"stem":"What is the central effect of an obstruction in the urinary tract?","options":["It hinders normal urine flow and can cause urine to back up.","It increases oxygen diffusion across the alveolar-capillary membrane.","It directly increases sensory information carried by peripheral nerves."],"feedback":["Correct: obstruction impedes urine flow, and backflow can increase pressure and affect kidney function.","Incorrect: oxygen diffusion is a respiratory process and is not the central effect of urinary obstruction.","Incorrect: sensory signaling is a nervous system function and does not describe the effect of urinary outflow obstruction."]},{"stem":"A patient reports a weak urinary stream. Which interpretation is most accurate?","options":["It is a possible signal of impaired outflow, but it does not establish obstruction by itself.","It confirms that both kidneys are obstructed.","It proves that kidney function is normal."],"feedback":["Correct: a weak stream can accompany impaired outflow, but one symptom does not confirm the cause or location.","Incorrect: the symptom alone cannot show whether obstruction exists or whether one or both kidneys are involved.","Incorrect: a weak stream does not establish that kidney function is normal."]}],"title":"Urinary outflow and obstruction","unit":"renal","aliases":["urinary obstruction","urinary retention","postrenal","bladder"],"model":"nephron","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/genitourinary-disorders/obstructive-uropathy/obstructive-uropathy?qt=hydronephrosis","title":"Obstructive Uropathy - Genitourinary Disorders - Merck Manuals ..."},{"url":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000507.htm","title":"Obstructive uropathy: MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK558921/","title":"Obstructive Uropathy - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"buffer","definition":"Acid-base balance is the regulation of acidity in body fluids. Chemical buffers, the lungs, and the kidneys work together to limit changes in blood pH.","structure":"The main blood buffer system involves carbon dioxide, carbonic acid, and bicarbonate. The lungs influence carbon dioxide through ventilation, while the kidneys regulate bicarbonate and excrete acid through the nephron; chemical buffers act in body fluids as changes occur.","normal":"In normal acid-base regulation, chemical buffers resist abrupt pH shifts. The lungs adjust carbon dioxide exhalation through breathing, and the kidneys more slowly adjust bicarbonate reabsorption and acid excretion. Together, these mechanisms help keep blood acidity within a narrow physiologic range.","change":"When a metabolic disturbance lowers pH, increased ventilation can remove more carbon dioxide and shift pH upward toward normal. When ventilation retains carbon dioxide, acidity can rise; the kidneys can respond more slowly by conserving bicarbonate and excreting acid. Compensation may be limited if an organ system is impaired.","cues":"A change in breathing depth or rate may signal respiratory compensation for an acid-base disturbance, while blood pH, carbon dioxide, and bicarbonate measurements help characterize the pattern. Breathing changes alone do not identify the primary disturbance or establish its cause.","distinction":"A breathing change that moves pH toward normal is compensation, not proof that the underlying disturbance has resolved. Interpret ventilation alongside pH, carbon dioxide, and bicarbonate rather than labeling the condition from breathing alone.","related":["ventilation","nephron","ketones"],"questions":[{"stem":"A metabolic process lowers blood pH. Which response can the lungs make relatively quickly to help move pH toward normal?","options":["Increase ventilation to exhale more carbon dioxide.","Retain more carbon dioxide by reducing ventilation.","Increase renal bicarbonate reabsorption immediately."],"feedback":["Correct: increased ventilation removes carbon dioxide, reducing the acid contribution of the carbon dioxide–carbonic acid system.","Incorrect: retaining carbon dioxide tends to increase acidity rather than compensate for low pH.","Incorrect: bicarbonate regulation is a kidney function and generally changes more slowly than respiratory compensation."]},{"stem":"A patient is breathing rapidly, and a blood gas shows a pH disturbance. What does the rapid breathing establish by itself?","options":["It may reflect compensation, but it does not identify the primary disturbance on its own.","It proves that the primary problem is metabolic acidosis.","It shows that kidney compensation has already corrected the disturbance."],"feedback":["Correct: ventilation can change in response to a pH disturbance, but breathing alone does not reveal the primary cause or full acid-base pattern.","Incorrect: rapid breathing can have several causes and does not independently prove metabolic acidosis.","Incorrect: kidney compensation is slower, and rapid breathing does not show that the disturbance is corrected."]}],"title":"Acid-base balance","unit":"multi","aliases":["acid-base balance","acidemia","alkalemia","bicarbonate","acidosis","alkalosis"],"model":"airway","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acid-base-regulation-and-disorders/acid-base-regulation","title":"Acid-Base Regulation - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/kidney-disorders/acid-base-balance/overview-of-acid-base-balance","title":"Overview of Acid-Base Balance - Kidney Disorders - Merck ..."},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/26-5-disorders-of-acid-base-balance","title":"26.5 Disorders of Acid-Base Balance - Anatomy and Physiology 2e | OpenStax"}]},{"id":"cells","title":"Cells and oxygen","unit":"multi","model":"delivery","aliases":["cellular respiration","oxygen demand","cellular oxygen use"],"definition":"Cells need oxygen to make usable energy. Oxygen travels in blood, then moves into cells for energy-making reactions.","structure":"Cells sit within tissues, surrounded by interstitial fluid, the thin layer of fluid between cells. Small blood vessels called capillaries run nearby, bringing oxygen-rich blood close to the cells. Inside cells, mitochondria are structures that use oxygen to help make ATP, a molecule cells use as energy.","normal":"Oxygen enters the blood in the lungs and is carried mostly attached to hemoglobin, a protein in red blood cells. Blood flow brings it to capillaries near tissues. Oxygen then moves from blood through the surrounding fluid into cells and mitochondria, where it helps release energy from nutrients and make ATP for cell functions.","change":"When oxygen delivery or movement into tissue is inadequate, cells have less oxygen available for making ATP. Their energy production may fall, and if oxygen is absent, mitochondria stop producing ATP. Nearby tissue functions can then fail; if the shortage is severe or lasts, cells may be damaged. Causes can include low hemoglobin, impaired oxygen unloading, or restricted blood flow.","cues":"A pulse oximeter estimates the share of hemoglobin carrying oxygen, but it does not measure total hemoglobin or oxygen use by cells. Thus, a reassuring reading does not by itself prove that tissue delivery is adequate. Fatigue or bluish skin can be clues to a problem, but neither sign identifies the cause on its own.","distinction":"Hemoglobin saturation is not the same as the total amount of oxygen carried in blood. A normal pulse-oximeter reading does not rule out anemia or carbon monoxide bound to hemoglobin.","related":["alveoli","hemoglobin","exchange"],"questions":[{"stem":"A person has reduced hemoglobin but a normal pulse-oximeter saturation. What can still be true?","options":["The blood's total oxygen content may be low.","The blood's total oxygen content must be normal.","The tissues are necessarily receiving enough oxygen."],"feedback":["Correct: saturation is a proportion, so less hemoglobin can mean less total oxygen carried despite a normal proportion.","Not necessarily: saturation alone does not show how much hemoglobin is present.","Not necessarily: tissue delivery also depends on blood flow and oxygen content."]},{"stem":"If arterial oxygen content stays the same while cardiac output falls, what happens to oxygen delivery to tissues?","options":["It falls.","It stays the same because oxygen content is unchanged.","It rises because the blood moves more slowly."],"feedback":["Correct: oxygen delivery depends on both arterial oxygen content and cardiac output.","Incorrect: unchanged oxygen content cannot offset a fall in cardiac output in the delivery relationship.","Incorrect: slower flow does not make total oxygen delivery rise when cardiac output falls."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-26494/","title":"Physiology, Oxygen Transport"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK54110/","title":"Tissue Gas Transport - Regulation of Tissue Oxygenation - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"cerebral","definition":"Cerebral perfusion is the delivery of blood through the brain's vessels. It provides the pressure gradient that supports oxygen delivery to brain tissue.","structure":"The relevant structures are the brain's arteries and arterioles within the skull. Cerebral perfusion pressure reflects the difference between mean arterial pressure and intracranial pressure, while changes in arteriolar diameter alter resistance to blood flow.","normal":"Cerebral blood flow is normally regulated so it remains relatively steady as blood pressure varies. Cerebral arterioles can constrict or dilate, adjusting vascular resistance; this autoregulation helps match flow to pressure and supports oxygen delivery to active brain tissue.","change":"When cerebral perfusion pressure falls, cerebral vessels may dilate to help maintain flow; when pressure rises, they may constrict. If autoregulation cannot compensate, or intracranial pressure rises, the pressure gradient and brain blood flow can decrease, risking inadequate oxygen delivery.","cues":"A change in neurological function or a change in measured mean arterial or intracranial pressure can raise concern about cerebral perfusion. These signals do not establish reduced brain blood flow on their own; context and the combined pressure relationship matter.","distinction":"Mean arterial pressure alone is not the same as cerebral perfusion pressure, which also depends on intracranial pressure. Autoregulation can buffer pressure changes, but it does not guarantee stable flow in every circumstance.","related":["icp","map","neurons"],"questions":[{"stem":"If mean arterial pressure remains unchanged while intracranial pressure rises, what happens to the cerebral perfusion pressure relationship?","options":["The pressure gradient for cerebral perfusion decreases.","The pressure gradient increases because intracranial pressure supports arterial inflow.","It stays unchanged because cerebral perfusion depends only on mean arterial pressure."],"feedback":["Correct: cerebral perfusion pressure is mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, so a rise in intracranial pressure reduces the gradient when mean arterial pressure is unchanged.","Intracranial pressure opposes the pressure gradient rather than supporting arterial inflow.","Mean arterial pressure is only one part of the relationship; intracranial pressure also matters."]},{"stem":"A rise in systemic blood pressure occurs while cerebral autoregulation is functioning. Which response is consistent with the described mechanism?","options":["Cerebral arterioles constrict, increasing resistance and helping limit a rise in cerebral blood flow.","Cerebral arterioles dilate, lowering resistance and ensuring that flow rises in proportion to pressure.","Cerebral vessels have no role in adjusting flow when systemic pressure changes."],"feedback":["Correct: increased pressure can prompt arteriolar constriction, which raises resistance and helps return flow toward its usual level.","Dilation is associated with reduced pressure in the described autoregulatory response, not the response to a pressure rise.","Cerebral autoregulation involves changes in arteriolar diameter and vascular resistance."]}],"title":"Cerebral perfusion","unit":"neuro","aliases":["cerebral perfusion pressure","cerebral perfusion","CPP"],"model":"cranium","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507811/","title":"Intracranial Hypertension - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK538134/","title":"Anatomy, Head and Neck: Cerebral Blood Flow - StatPearls - NCBI"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537271/","title":"Cerebral Perfusion Pressure - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"circulation","title":"Heart chambers and circulation","unit":"cardio","model":"pump","aliases":["heart chambers","pulmonary circulation","systemic circulation","right atrium","left atrium"],"definition":"The heart is a muscular pump with four chambers, or spaces that hold blood. It sends blood to the lungs to collect oxygen, then sends oxygen-rich blood to the rest of the body.","structure":"The heart lies near the center of the chest, slightly left, behind the breastbone. A wall called the septum divides its right and left sides. Each side has an upper atrium and a lower ventricle. Veins bring blood into the atria; arteries carry blood away from the ventricles, with valves between chambers and at the exits.","normal":"Blood returning from the body enters the right atrium, passes into the right ventricle, and is pumped through the pulmonary artery to the lungs. It returns oxygen-rich through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, then enters the left ventricle. The left ventricle pumps it through the aorta to the body.","change":"If a valve opening becomes narrow, less blood can pass through it; if a valve leaks, some blood flows backward. Either change can disrupt the forward movement between chambers or out of the heart. For example, a leaky valve between an atrium and ventricle allows blood to move back toward the atrium when the ventricle contracts.","cues":"A heartbeat can be heard as two sounds: the first as the mitral and tricuspid valves close, the second as the aortic and pulmonary valves close. Pulse rate also changes with the body's need for oxygen, such as during activity. These observations alone do not establish that a valve or chamber is abnormal.","distinction":"The right side sends blood to the lungs; it does not send oxygen-rich blood directly to the body. The left side sends blood to the body, while its own muscle receives blood through the coronary arteries.","related":["valves","pulmonary-artery","coronary"],"questions":[{"stem":"Blood returning from the body is low in oxygen. Which route takes it to the lungs?","options":["Right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery","Left atrium → left ventricle → aorta","Right atrium → left atrium → pulmonary veins"],"feedback":["Correct: the right ventricle pumps returning blood through the pulmonary artery to the lungs.","This route carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body.","Blood returns from the lungs through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium; it does not travel from the right atrium to the left atrium."]},{"stem":"If a heart valve opening narrows, what is the direct effect on blood passing through it?","options":["Less blood flows through the narrowed opening.","Blood is redirected into the coronary arteries.","The valve necessarily allows blood to flow backward."],"feedback":["Correct: narrowing reduces blood flow through that valve.","Coronary arteries supply the heart muscle; narrowing a valve does not redirect blood into them.","Backward leakage is called regurgitation and is distinct from narrowing, though both can occur in one valve."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/biology-of-the-heart-and-blood-vessels/biology-of-the-heart","title":"Biology of the Heart - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK279249/?report=classic","title":"In brief: How does the heart work?"}]},{"id":"compartment","definition":"Compartment syndrome is increased pressure within a closed fascial space that impairs local circulation. Reduced blood flow can deprive the enclosed tissues of oxygen and lead to injury.","structure":"A fascial compartment is a confined space containing muscle, nerves, and blood vessels, commonly in an arm or leg. Its surrounding fascia does not expand readily, so changes in tissue volume can affect pressure and circulation within that space.","normal":"Within a closed osteofascial space, fascia encloses muscle, nerves, and vessels; under ordinary conditions, arterial inflow and venous outflow are in equilibrium. Because fascia expands little, the available space constrains how much additional tissue volume can be accommodated.","change":"When swelling or added fluid raises pressure inside this noncompliant space, venous outflow falls and venous capillary pressure rises. With further pressure, arterial inflow can also decline, reducing tissue oxygenation; ischemia can intensify swelling and progress to muscle or nerve injury.","cues":"A tense, firm compartment and severe pain, especially pain with passive stretch, are concerning signals; tingling or reduced sensation may occur. These findings require interpretation in context, and a palpable pulse may persist despite serious local compromise.","distinction":"A palpable pulse does not rule out impaired local circulation in a confined compartment. Pain alone is not confirmation, although disproportionate pain is an important early cue.","related":["interstitium","perfusion","muscle"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can increased volume inside a fascial compartment impair tissue circulation?","options":["The surrounding fascia expands little, so rising pressure can reduce venous outflow and later arterial inflow.","The fascia expands readily, increasing the space available for blood vessels.","Increased compartment pressure improves venous drainage from the enclosed tissues."],"feedback":["Correct: limited expansion allows pressure to rise, which can impede venous outflow and reduce arterial inflow.","Incorrect: the surrounding fascia is described as noncompliant and does not readily expand.","Incorrect: rising compartment pressure reduces venous outflow rather than improving it."]},{"stem":"A patient has a palpable pulse in an injured limb with a tense compartment. What does the pulse establish?","options":["It does not rule out serious impairment of local tissue circulation.","It proves that tissue oxygenation is normal throughout the compartment.","It confirms that the compartment pressure is not affecting venous outflow."],"feedback":["Correct: a pulse may remain palpable even when local tissue circulation is seriously compromised.","Incorrect: a palpable pulse does not establish normal oxygenation in all enclosed tissues.","Incorrect: a pulse does not show that venous outflow is unaffected."]}],"title":"Fascia and compartment pressure","unit":"msk","aliases":["fascia","compartment pressure","compartment syndrome"],"model":"compartment","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448124/","title":"Acute Compartment Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001224.htm","title":"Compartment syndrome: MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK448124/","title":"Acute Compartment Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"conduction","title":"Cardiac conduction","unit":"cardio","model":"conduction","aliases":["conduction system","cardiac conduction","atrioventricular node","depolarization","repolarization"],"definition":"Cardiac conduction is the movement of electrical signals through the heart. These signals coordinate when heart muscle contracts.","structure":"The SA node lies high in the right atrium near the superior vena cava. Signals pass through the atria to the AV node on the right side of the interatrial septum, then enter the His bundle in the upper interventricular septum. The His bundle divides into right and left branches that extend into Purkinje fibers.","normal":"A normal beat begins when the SA node generates an impulse. The signal spreads across both atria, then reaches the AV node, where conduction slows briefly. It continues through the His bundle and its right and left branches into Purkinje fibers. The ventricles are then activated and contract in a coordinated sequence.","change":"When impulse formation or conduction is abnormal, signals may travel slowly, fail to pass onward, or circulate repeatedly through pathways. The timing or rate of ventricular activation can change: a block may leave fewer signals reaching the ventricles, while reentry can sustain a rapid rhythm. These changes can affect coordinated contraction and blood flow.","cues":"Conduction or rhythm changes may be associated with an irregular or slow pulse, palpitations, breathlessness, chest discomfort, lightheadedness, or fainting; some cause no symptoms. An ECG can show how P waves relate to QRS complexes and help locate a rhythm problem. Any one symptom or pulse finding alone does not establish its cause.","distinction":"A slow pulse alone does not show where conduction is impaired. A wide QRS does not prove a ventricular origin; a supraventricular signal can also widen because of a conduction defect or preexcitation.","related":["potassium","ischemia","output"],"questions":[{"stem":"An ECG shows every P wave followed by a QRS, with a PR interval longer than 0.2 seconds. Which conduction pattern does this describe?","options":["First-degree AV block","Mobitz type I second-degree AV block","Third-degree AV block"],"feedback":["Correct: first-degree AV block has a prolonged PR interval, with each P wave followed by a QRS.","Mobitz type I includes progressively lengthening PR intervals before a P wave is not conducted.","Third-degree AV block has no consistent relationship between P waves and QRS complexes."]},{"stem":"A rhythm has a wide QRS complex. Which interpretation is most accurate?","options":["It may be ventricular in origin or a supraventricular rhythm with a conduction defect or preexcitation.","It must arise above the His bundle bifurcation.","It proves that atrial impulses are not reaching the ventricles."],"feedback":["Correct: a wide QRS can indicate a ventricular origin, but some supraventricular rhythms also produce a wide QRS.","A wide QRS is not the usual indicator of a supraventricular origin; narrow QRS complexes more often indicate one.","A wide QRS alone does not establish whether atrial impulses reach the ventricles."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias-and-conduction-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias","title":"Overview of Arrhythmias - Cardiology - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias-and-conduction-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias?ruleredirectid=745","title":"Overview of Arrhythmias - MSD Manuals"}]},{"id":"cord","definition":"Spinal cord pathways help the autonomic nervous system regulate blood-vessel tone and heart activity. Injury that interrupts sympathetic pathways can disrupt this cardiovascular regulation.","structure":"Sympathetic pathways descend through the spinal cord and influence blood-vessel tone and cardiac regulation. Injury to cervical or upper thoracic pathways can interrupt this autonomic input, affecting circulation even when circulating blood volume has not been lost.","normal":"Normally, sympathetic signals help maintain vascular tone and support heart rate and cardiac output as the body responds to falling pressure. When spinal injury interrupts these pathways, vessels dilate and vagal influence on the heart is relatively unopposed, so pressure may fall with a slower heart rate.","change":"With disrupted sympathetic control, arteries and veins lose some of their normal constricting tone, which can lower blood pressure and reduce tissue perfusion. The heart may also lack its usual sympathetic stimulation, leaving vagal influence relatively unopposed and contributing to a slower heart rate.","cues":"After a high spinal cord injury, hypotension together with bradycardia can suggest disrupted sympathetic control; hypothermia may also occur. These findings are not specific by themselves, and other injuries or causes of instability must be considered.","distinction":"Do not assume hypotension necessarily means blood loss: neurogenic shock can lower pressure through loss of vascular tone. Spinal shock describes neurologic findings such as flaccidity and absent reflexes, not the same hemodynamic process.","related":["shock","hypotension","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"A person with a high spinal cord injury develops low blood pressure and a slow heart rate. Which mechanism best connects these findings?","options":["Loss of sympathetic cardiovascular influence, with reduced vascular tone and relatively unopposed vagal influence","Loss of circulating blood volume causing a compensatory increase in sympathetic activity","Urinary outflow obstruction reducing the amount of urine reaching the bladder"],"feedback":["Correct: disrupted sympathetic pathways can dilate vessels and leave vagal influence on the heart relatively unopposed.","This describes a possible volume-loss pattern, but it does not explain the characteristic loss of sympathetic tone after spinal injury.","Urinary obstruction can reduce urine output, but it does not explain this cardiovascular pattern."]},{"stem":"Which finding best distinguishes neurogenic shock from spinal shock as described here?","options":["Neurogenic shock involves hemodynamic changes such as hypotension and bradycardia; spinal shock involves neurologic findings such as flaccidity and loss of reflexes","Both terms refer only to loss of reflexes after spinal cord injury","Neurogenic shock is defined by urinary obstruction, while spinal shock is defined by low blood pressure"],"feedback":["Correct: the terms describe different processes—one hemodynamic and the other neurologic.","Loss of reflexes can be a feature of spinal shock, but it does not define neurogenic shock.","Neither description is accurate; urinary obstruction is not the defining feature of either term."]}],"title":"Spinal cord and sympathetic tone","unit":"neuro","aliases":["spinal cord","sympathetic tone","neurogenic shock"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/medical-surgical-nursing/pages/23-4-neurogenic-shock","title":"23.4 Neurogenic Shock - Medical-Surgical Nursing"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448163/","title":"Spinal Shock - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459361/","title":"Neurogenic Shock - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"coronary","title":"Coronary arteries and myocardium","unit":"cardio","model":"coronary","aliases":["coronary arteries","coronary artery","myocardium","myocardial"],"definition":"Coronary arteries are blood vessels that bring oxygen-rich blood to the heart muscle. Coronary artery disease (CAD) reduces blood flow through them, most often because fatty plaque narrows them.","structure":"At the aortic root, just above the aortic valve, the right and left coronary arteries begin and run along the heart’s surface. Their larger branches divide into smaller vessels that enter the myocardium, the heart’s muscular wall. The left main artery branches into the left anterior descending and circumflex arteries.","normal":"Blood leaving the heart enters the aorta, and the right and left coronary arteries branch from its root to carry oxygen-rich blood to the myocardium. The heart muscle uses oxygen to keep contracting and pumping blood. Cardiac veins then collect blood carrying waste products and drain it into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium.","change":"Most CAD develops as atherosclerotic plaque, fatty material in an artery wall, grows and narrows a coronary artery. Blood flow may then fail to meet the myocardium’s oxygen needs, especially when activity raises demand, causing ischemia (insufficient blood flow). If plaque ruptures, a clot may further narrow or block the vessel; prolonged severe ischemia can kill supplied muscle, an infarction, and impair pumping.","cues":"Reduced coronary flow can produce chest discomfort called angina, particularly when exertion increases the heart’s need for oxygen; some ischemia causes no obvious symptoms. An ECG, a recording of the heart’s electrical activity, may show changes in some settings. Chest discomfort or an ECG finding alone does not establish CAD, and presentations vary.","distinction":"Chest discomfort by itself does not prove CAD. A plaque causing only modest narrowing can still rupture and trigger a clot.","related":["arterial","perfusion","ischemia"],"questions":[{"stem":"During exertion, why can a narrowed coronary artery cause chest discomfort?","options":["The heart muscle needs more oxygen-rich blood than the narrowed artery can deliver.","Coronary blood flow normally falls to reduce the heart’s workload during exercise.","The heart muscle needs less oxygen, so waste products build up."],"feedback":["Correct: increased demand can exceed the supply through a narrowed artery, causing ischemia and sometimes angina.","Not quite: exertion raises the heart muscle’s need for oxygen; a narrowing can limit the increased supply.","Not quite: the concern is inadequate oxygen-rich blood flow, not reduced oxygen need."]},{"stem":"A coronary plaque ruptures and a clot forms. What may happen next?","options":["The clot can further narrow or block flow, suddenly depriving the supplied heart muscle of oxygen.","The plaque dissolves and widens the artery, increasing oxygen delivery.","Blood flow shifts into coronary veins, bypassing the affected heart muscle."],"feedback":["Correct: a clot after plaque rupture can interrupt coronary blood flow and cause acute ischemia.","Not quite: plaque rupture can promote clot formation, which narrows or blocks the artery rather than opening it.","Not quite: coronary veins collect blood after it passes through the heart muscle; they do not provide a bypass route around a blocked artery."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/overview-of-coronary-artery-disease-cad","title":"Overview of Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/overview-of-coronary-artery-disease","title":"Overview of Coronary Artery Disease - Cardiology"}]},{"id":"cortisol","definition":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid hormone made by the adrenal cortex. It helps the body meet changing demands, including physical stress.","structure":"The adrenal cortex sits on top of each kidney and produces cortisol as well as other hormones. Cortisol is part of the body’s stress response, while mineralocorticoids from the cortex help regulate sodium, potassium, and fluid balance.","normal":"When the body is not under unusual strain, cortisol supports normal body functions. During illness, injury, or other stress, the adrenal cortex normally increases glucocorticoid secretion, helping the body respond to the increased demand and maintain blood pressure and energy availability.","change":"With adrenal insufficiency, the cortex may not produce enough cortisol, especially when stress raises the body’s needs. Reduced cortisol can weaken vascular responsiveness to constricting signals and contribute to low blood pressure; associated mineralocorticoid deficiency can also cause sodium loss and reduced blood volume.","cues":"A person with inadequate cortisol response may develop weakness, low blood pressure, or, during severe illness or injury, signs of circulatory collapse. These findings have many possible causes; their presence alone does not establish adrenal insufficiency or an adrenal crisis.","distinction":"Low blood pressure alone does not show that cortisol deficiency is the cause. The concern is the relationship between impaired adrenal hormone production and the body’s difficulty responding to stress.","related":["hypotension","sodium","shock"],"questions":[{"stem":"During a major illness, a patient with impaired adrenal reserve becomes hypotensive. Which mechanism best links inadequate cortisol response to this finding?","options":["Reduced vascular responsiveness to constricting hormones can lower vascular tone.","Excess cortisol directly causes sodium loss and dehydration.","Cortisol deficiency prevents carbon dioxide from being exhaled."],"feedback":["Correct: inadequate glucocorticoid activity can reduce vascular responsiveness to signals such as norepinephrine, contributing to low vascular tone.","Incorrect: sodium loss and reduced blood volume are more directly associated with mineralocorticoid deficiency, not excess cortisol.","Incorrect: carbon dioxide exhalation is primarily regulated through ventilation, not cortisol secretion."]},{"stem":"A patient has weakness and low blood pressure during an acute stressor. What is the most careful interpretation of these cues?","options":["They may fit an inadequate adrenal stress response, but they are not diagnostic by themselves.","They establish adrenal insufficiency because both findings are specific to cortisol deficiency.","They indicate that the adrenal cortex is producing too much cortisol."],"feedback":["Correct: these findings can occur with adrenal insufficiency, but they are nonspecific and must be interpreted in context.","Incorrect: weakness and low blood pressure have many potential causes and do not independently establish the diagnosis.","Incorrect: the cues do not demonstrate excess cortisol production."]}],"title":"Cortisol and stress response","unit":"endo","aliases":["cortisol","adrenal insufficiency","adrenal crisis"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/hormonal-and-metabolic-disorders/adrenal-gland-disorders/adrenal-insufficiency","title":"Adrenal Insufficiency - Hormonal and Metabolic Disorders"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279122/","title":"Diagnosis and Management of Adrenal Insufficiency - Endotext - NCBI"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/hormonal-and-metabolic-disorders/adrenal-gland-disorders/adrenal-insufficiency?autoredirectid=18692","title":"Adrenal Insufficiency - Hormonal and Metabolic Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"}]},{"id":"csf","definition":"Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is a clear fluid that surrounds the brain and spinal cord and fills the brain’s ventricles. Its movement and reabsorption help maintain a stable fluid balance in the central nervous system.","structure":"The ventricular system is a set of connected spaces within the brain. CSF moves from the lateral ventricles through the third and fourth ventricles, then enters the subarachnoid space through openings before circulating around the brain and spinal cord.","normal":"CSF is produced mostly by the choroid plexus and ependymal cells, with much of its production in the lateral ventricles. It flows through connected ventricles into the subarachnoid space, where it surrounds the central nervous system and is reabsorbed, including through arachnoid granulations.","change":"When CSF production and reabsorption become unbalanced, or its flow is obstructed, CSF can accumulate and raise intracranial pressure. A blockage within the ventricular pathway differs from impaired reabsorption outside that pathway; either can disrupt the usual circulation and enlarge the ventricles.","cues":"CSF accumulation or altered flow may be associated with ventricular enlargement and raised intracranial pressure. These findings can prompt consideration of hydrocephalus, but a single imaging or pressure finding does not establish its cause or subtype; context and the pattern of flow matter.","distinction":"Ventricular enlargement does not always mean that CSF pressure is elevated; normal pressure hydrocephalus is an example. Obstructive and communicating hydrocephalus differ by where CSF flow or reabsorption is impaired.","related":["icp","neurons","cerebral"],"questions":[{"stem":"CSF is produced in the lateral ventricles. Which pathway describes its usual route onward?","options":["Through the foramina of Monro to the third ventricle, then through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle","Directly from the lateral ventricles into the dural venous sinuses","From the lateral ventricles into the spinal canal before reaching the third ventricle"],"feedback":["This follows the normal ventricular sequence: lateral ventricles, third ventricle, then cerebral aqueduct and fourth ventricle.","Arachnoid granulations contribute to reabsorption into dural venous sinuses; this is not the route from the lateral ventricles.","CSF reaches the spinal subarachnoid space after passing through the ventricular system, not before the third ventricle."]},{"stem":"A flow abnormality occurs at the arachnoid granulations, without a discrete blockage inside the ventricles. Which description best fits this mechanism?","options":["Impaired CSF reabsorption outside the ventricular pathway, consistent with communicating hydrocephalus","A physical obstruction within the ventricular pathway, consistent with obstructive hydrocephalus","Normal CSF circulation, because reabsorption does not affect CSF balance"],"feedback":["Communicating hydrocephalus involves impaired reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations without a discrete ventricular obstruction.","Obstructive hydrocephalus involves a physical blockage along the ventricular flow pathway, unlike the mechanism described.","Reabsorption is part of CSF balance; impaired reabsorption can lead to CSF accumulation."]}],"title":"Ventricles and cerebrospinal fluid","unit":"neuro","aliases":["cerebrospinal fluid","CSF","hydrocephalus","ventricular drain"],"model":"cranium","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459286/?report=reader","title":"Anatomy, Head and Neck: Cerebrospinal Fluid"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470578/?report=printable","title":"Neuroanatomy, Cerebrospinal Fluid - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK470578/","title":"Neuroanatomy, Cerebrospinal Fluid - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH"}]},{"id":"delirium","definition":"Delirium is a sudden or short-term disturbance in attention, awareness, and cognition that can fluctuate over time. It is commonly associated with an acute medical condition or drug exposure.","structure":"Delirium concerns coordinated brain function, especially attention, awareness, cognition, and level of consciousness. It can occur in a vulnerable person when acute illness, medication effects, or other physiological stressors disturb the brain’s usual functioning.","normal":"When brain function is stable, a person can direct attention, remain aware of surroundings, and think with a relatively consistent level of alertness. These abilities support coherent interaction and are interpreted in relation to the person’s usual baseline.","change":"With delirium, attention, awareness, cognition, or consciousness changes over a short period and may vary during the day. Many different stressors can contribute, including infection, dehydration, medication effects, hypoxia, pain, or sleep deprivation; the syndrome is not explained by one cause alone.","cues":"A bedside pattern of newly impaired attention, disorientation, unclear thinking, or alertness that shifts between periods may raise concern for delirium. These cues are not specific, so the person’s baseline and possible medical or medication-related causes matter.","distinction":"Fluctuating attention is a key feature, but one episode of inattention does not alone establish delirium. Delirium is an acute change in a broader pattern of mental function, and its underlying cause can vary.","related":["sleep","arousal","neurons"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient is newly inattentive and alternates between drowsiness and alertness over the day. Which interpretation best fits the described pattern?","options":["An acute, fluctuating disturbance in attention and awareness may be present.","A stable long-term cognitive pattern is established by this observation alone.","The pattern identifies one specific medication as the cause."],"feedback":["Correct: acute change and fluctuation in attention or alertness are characteristic features, though the cause still needs consideration.","A newly changing pattern is not, by itself, evidence of a stable long-term condition.","Many medical conditions and drug exposures can contribute, so the pattern does not identify one cause."]},{"stem":"Which additional information is most useful when deciding whether a change in attention is acute?","options":["The person’s usual cognitive and functional baseline.","Whether the patient was awakened once overnight.","Whether the patient has a particular sleep stage."],"feedback":["Correct: comparing current function with baseline helps establish whether there has been an acute change.","A single awakening is nonspecific and does not establish a change in cognition.","Sleep stage alone does not establish whether attention has changed from baseline."]}],"title":"Delirium and fluctuating attention","unit":"psych","aliases":["delirium","hypoactive delirium"],"model":"synapse","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium","title":"Delirium - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/brain-spinal-cord-and-nerve-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium","title":"Delirium - Brain, Spinal Cord, and Nerve Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium?qt=stroke","title":"Delirium - Neurologic Disorders - Merck Manuals Professional ..."}]},{"id":"diaphragm","definition":"The diaphragm is the main muscle that generates force for quiet breathing. Its effort contributes to the work needed to move air into and out of the lungs.","structure":"The diaphragm separates the chest and abdominal cavities and is the predominant muscle generating force during ordinary inspiration. Intercostal and accessory respiratory muscles can also contribute, particularly when breathing effort increases or diaphragm function is impaired.","normal":"During quiet breathing, the diaphragm is the primary driver of inspiration, generating pressure that moves air into the lungs. Respiratory muscles work against the elastic properties of the lungs and chest wall and against resistance to airflow.","change":"When respiratory muscle strength or coordination is impaired, ventilation can become inadequate, with shallow or slow breathing and possible carbon dioxide retention. Increased resistance or reduced compliance can also raise the work required of the diaphragm and other respiratory muscles.","cues":"Low tidal volumes with a high breathing frequency can suggest increased workload during prolonged fatiguing loads. Inward abdominal movement during inspiration, called abdominal paradox, may indicate greater accessory-muscle effort, but neither pattern alone measures effort or confirms diaphragm fatigue.","distinction":"A rapid, shallow breathing pattern may suggest increased respiratory workload, but it does not quantify effort or prove diaphragm fatigue. Abdominal paradox can reflect increased accessory-muscle force and is not, by itself, a definitive diagnosis.","related":["ventilation","airway","nmj"],"questions":[{"stem":"During quiet tidal breathing in a healthy adult, which structure is the main inspiratory muscle?","options":["The diaphragm","The abdominal muscles","The accessory inspiratory muscles alone"],"feedback":["Correct: the diaphragm is the predominant muscle driving inspiration during quiet breathing.","The abdominal muscles are not identified as the primary driver of quiet inspiration.","Accessory muscles can contribute, especially with vigorous effort or impaired diaphragm function, but they are not the main driver in ordinary quiet breathing."]},{"stem":"A person has prolonged respiratory effort and develops low tidal volumes with a high breathing frequency. What is the most appropriate interpretation?","options":["The pattern suggests increased workload but does not quantify effort or prove fatigue.","The pattern confirms diaphragm fatigue.","The pattern demonstrates that respiratory muscle effort is normal."],"feedback":["Correct: this breathing pattern can suggest increased workload and impending fatigue, but it does not quantify effort or establish a diagnosis.","The pattern may raise concern for fatigue, but the evidence does not make it definitive on its own.","Low tidal volumes with a high frequency during prolonged loads suggest increased workload rather than demonstrating normal effort."]}],"title":"Diaphragm and work of breathing","unit":"resp","aliases":["diaphragm","work of breathing","respiratory muscles"],"model":"airway","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6212364/","title":"Assessing breathing effort in mechanical ventilation: physiology and clinical implications - PMC"},{"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32863842/?dopt=Abstract","title":"Diaphragm Muscle Adaptations in Health and Disease - PubMed"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK470309/","title":"Abnormal Respirations - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"edema","definition":"Pulmonary edema is excess fluid in the lung’s interstitial spaces or alveoli. The fluid can interfere with gas exchange and make breathing less effective.","structure":"The affected structures are the pulmonary capillaries, surrounding lung tissue, and alveoli. Fluid balance in this lung region depends on pressure forces, capillary-barrier permeability, and removal through venules and lymphatics.","normal":"Normally, low pressure in pulmonary capillaries and the balance of fluid-moving forces limit leakage into lung tissue. Venules and lymphatics remove fluid that leaves the vessels, helping keep the alveolar membrane available for oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange.","change":"Two important routes lead to pulmonary edema: increased pressure can push fluid out of lung capillaries, and an injured barrier can let fluid and proteins escape more readily. If outward movement exceeds clearance, fluid collects between cells and may flood air spaces. Reduced plasma protein concentration or impaired lymph drainage can contribute.","cues":"A person may have dyspnea, hypoxemia, or diffuse pulmonary infiltrates on imaging, but these findings do not establish the cause of edema. Cardiogenic and noncardiogenic forms can look similar; context and further assessment help distinguish their mechanisms.","distinction":"Fluid in the lungs does not by itself prove left-sided heart failure: increased permeability and other imbalances can also cause edema. Cardiogenic edema is associated with elevated pulmonary venous pressure, whereas noncardiogenic edema involves increased capillary permeability.","related":["alveoli","exchange","vq"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which mechanism can cause pulmonary edema without elevated hydrostatic pressure?","options":["Increased permeability of the alveolar-capillary barrier","Improved lymphatic removal of lung fluid","Reduced fluid movement out of pulmonary capillaries"],"feedback":["Correct: barrier injury can increase permeability and allow fluid to enter lung tissue and alveoli.","Improved lymphatic removal would help clear excess fluid rather than cause its accumulation.","Reduced outward fluid movement would not explain edema; increased leakage or inadequate clearance can promote it."]},{"stem":"Two people have similar pulmonary infiltrates and hypoxemia. What is the best inference from these findings alone?","options":["The findings indicate impaired gas exchange but do not establish the edema’s cause.","The findings prove that both people have cardiogenic edema.","The findings establish increased capillary permeability as the cause."],"feedback":["Correct: these cues can accompany pulmonary edema but do not distinguish cardiogenic from noncardiogenic causes.","Similar imaging and oxygenation findings do not prove elevated pulmonary venous pressure or a cardiac cause.","The same findings can arise through more than one mechanism, so permeability injury cannot be concluded from them alone."]}],"title":"Pulmonary edema","unit":"resp","aliases":["pulmonary edema","alveolar flooding","lung water"],"model":"alveolar","sources":[{"title":"Pulmonary edema: mechanisms of fluid filtration and clearance","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-80517/"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK525948/","title":"Physiology, Pulmonary Circulatory System - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK542230/","title":"Treatment / Management"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13090562/","title":"Heart–Lung Interactions in Gas Exchange: From Physiology to ..."}]},{"id":"embolus","definition":"A pulmonary arterial obstruction occurs when material, often a clot, blocks blood flow through part of the pulmonary circulation. The affected lung region may receive air without enough blood flow for normal gas exchange.","structure":"Pulmonary arteries carry blood from the right side of the heart toward lung capillaries, where blood normally meets ventilated alveoli. An obstruction in these vessels interrupts that route and alters blood flow through the lung.","normal":"Normally, blood travels from the right ventricle through pulmonary arteries to capillaries beside ventilated alveoli. This pairing of blood flow and air movement supports exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide before blood returns to the left side of the heart.","change":"When an embolus blocks a pulmonary artery, the affected alveoli may remain ventilated but receive less blood, creating a high ventilation-perfusion relationship and impaired gas exchange. A substantial obstruction can also raise pulmonary vascular resistance, increase right ventricular strain, and impair left ventricular filling.","cues":"Possible signals include breathlessness, rapid breathing, low oxygenation, or evidence of right-heart strain. These findings can have other causes, and an isolated symptom, oxygen measurement, or echocardiographic sign does not establish a pulmonary embolism.","distinction":"Ventilated areas with reduced perfusion are consistent with a ventilation-perfusion mismatch, but that pattern alone does not identify its cause. A sign of right ventricular strain may inform concern and severity without proving an embolus.","related":["vq","pulmonary-artery","gas-gradient"],"questions":[{"stem":"A lung region receives air but has reduced blood flow because of a pulmonary arterial blockage. What change in ventilation-perfusion matching is expected there?","options":["The ventilation-perfusion relationship increases because ventilation is present while perfusion falls.","The ventilation-perfusion relationship decreases because the blockage prevents air from reaching the alveoli.","Ventilation and perfusion remain matched because blood is redirected through the blocked artery."],"feedback":["Correct. An obstructed pulmonary vessel can leave alveoli ventilated but underperfused, increasing the ventilation-perfusion relationship in that region.","The described mechanism is reduced blood flow, not reduced ventilation; therefore, a low ventilation-perfusion relationship is not the expected local change.","A blockage interrupts blood flow through the affected vessel, so matching is disrupted rather than preserved by flow through the obstruction."]},{"stem":"An adult has rapid breathing and an echocardiographic finding that may indicate right ventricular strain. What is the most appropriate interpretation of these cues?","options":["They can support concern about pulmonary vascular stress but do not independently diagnose an embolus.","Together, they prove a pulmonary embolism because right ventricular strain has only one cause.","Rapid breathing rules out a pulmonary arterial obstruction if oxygenation is not known."],"feedback":["Correct. These findings may occur with pulmonary embolism, but they are not specific enough to establish the diagnosis on their own.","Right ventricular strain and rapid breathing can have other explanations; an isolated sign or combination of cues is not definitive proof.","Rapid breathing is a possible response to pulmonary embolism, and the absence of an oxygen measurement does not rule out an obstruction."]}],"title":"Pulmonary arterial obstruction","unit":"resp","aliases":["pulmonary embolism","pulmonary embolus","embolus"],"model":"embolus","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-embolism/pulmonary-embolism-pe","title":"Pulmonary Embolism (PE) - Pulmonology"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539907/","title":"Physiology, Pulmonary Ventilation and Perfusion - StatPearls - NCBI"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-embolism/pulmonary-embolism-pe?autoredirectid=25470&ItemId=v915299","title":"Pulmonary Embolism (PE) - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"exchange","title":"Capillary exchange","unit":"cardio","model":"perfusion","aliases":["capillary bed","capillaries","capillary exchange","microcirculation"],"definition":"Capillary exchange is the movement of water and dissolved substances between tiny blood vessels and nearby tissues. It helps deliver needed materials and carry away waste.","structure":"Capillaries are the smallest blood vessels, linking arterial-side arterioles to venous-side venules within tissues. Their thin wall is mainly a single layer of endothelial cells, next to the interstitial space around tissue cells. A surface coat called the glycocalyx covers the blood-facing side and helps regulate what crosses.","normal":"Blood pressure pushes water outward, while plasma proteins exert an inward osmotic pull; together, these forces influence filtration across the vessel wall. Water and small dissolved substances move through exchange pathways, supporting tissue cells and carrying wastes away. Filtered fluid flows through the interstitium, and lymphatic vessels return much of the fluid and escaped proteins to the blood.","change":"If outward filtration rises or the endothelial barrier becomes leakier, more water and proteins enter the interstitial space. Escaped proteins increase its osmotic pull, favoring further fluid entry; lymph flow may not keep pace. Fluid then accumulates as edema, increasing the distance for oxygen and nutrient movement and potentially disturbing tissue-cell function.","cues":"Excess interstitial fluid can appear as localized or more widespread swelling, sometimes with tissue fullness or tightness. These findings can fit altered capillary exchange, but they are not specific: pressure forces, barrier leak, protein levels, and lymph drainage all affect fluid balance. A sign alone cannot identify the cause.","distinction":"Swelling does not automatically mean that capillaries are leaking; altered pressure or reduced lymph return can also contribute. Do not assume all filtered fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end; lymphatics are important for returning fluid and proteins.","related":["perfusion","interstitium","edema"],"questions":[{"stem":"If pressure inside a capillary increases, which change is generally favored?","options":["More fluid filtration out into the tissue","More inward pull from plasma proteins","Less movement of fluid across the vessel wall"],"feedback":["Correct: higher capillary hydrostatic pressure favors outward fluid filtration.","Plasma protein concentration, rather than capillary pressure, is the main source of this inward osmotic pull.","Higher pressure tends to increase outward filtration, not reduce fluid movement."]},{"stem":"If more plasma proteins cross into the interstitial space, what may happen to nearby tissue fluid?","options":["The stronger interstitial osmotic pull may favor fluid accumulation","The plasma's inward osmotic pull must become stronger","Lymphatic return must immediately stop"],"feedback":["Correct: proteins in the interstitial space can increase its osmotic pull and favor fluid entry into tissue.","Protein escaping the blood does not strengthen the plasma protein pull; it can reduce the difference between the compartments.","Protein leakage does not mean lymph flow stops, though lymph drainage may be unable to keep pace with increased filtration."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK53447/","title":"Capillary Fluid Exchange - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK54123/","title":"Structure and Function of Exchange Microvessels"}]},{"id":"filtrate","definition":"The kidneys form filtrate by filtering blood at the glomeruli, then adjust it as fluid and solutes move through the tubules. Reabsorption returns selected substances from the tubules to the blood.","structure":"The glomeruli sit within the kidneys and begin urine formation by filtering blood. The tubules continue the process by reabsorbing water and solutes, while changes in filtration or tubular flow can alter the amount and composition of urine.","normal":"Normally, glomerular filtration moves water and small solutes into the tubules, and tubular reabsorption returns needed water and solutes to the blood. Together, these processes support fluid balance and produce urine while the kidneys remove substances from circulation.","change":"When kidney perfusion falls, functioning tubules may reabsorb more sodium and water, contributing to reduced urine output. If tubular or downstream obstruction raises pressure in the urinary space, filtration can fall; intrinsic injury can also disrupt kidney structures involved in filtration or reabsorption.","cues":"Reduced urine output may accompany kidney hypoperfusion, often with concentrated urine and low urine sodium. These findings are bedside signals rather than proof of a specific cause: intrinsic injury and urinary obstruction can also affect urine formation.","distinction":"Low urine output alone does not establish intrinsic kidney damage. Hypoperfusion, intrinsic kidney disease, and urinary obstruction are different mechanisms that can all reduce urine formation.","related":["nephron","oliguria","bladder"],"questions":[{"stem":"When kidney perfusion is reduced but the tubules remain functional, what response can contribute to lower urine output?","options":["The tubules reabsorb more sodium and water.","The tubules stop filtering all blood cells.","The kidneys release more filtrate into the bladder."],"feedback":["Greater sodium and water reabsorption can conserve fluid and contribute to reduced urine output during hypoperfusion.","This choice describes neither the stated tubular response nor the mechanism of low urine output.","Filtrate enters the urinary tract after glomerular filtration; increasing its release would not describe the compensatory reabsorption response."]},{"stem":"How can urinary obstruction reduce glomerular filtration?","options":["It raises pressure in the urinary space and reduces filtration.","It increases tubular reabsorption, which directly raises glomerular pressure.","It improves renal blood flow enough to prevent filtration changes."],"feedback":["Obstructed ultrafiltrate can raise pressure in the urinary space of the glomerulus, reducing the filtration rate.","Tubular reabsorption is a different process and does not explain the pressure effect of obstruction.","Obstruction can affect renal blood flow; the evidence describes a later reduction rather than protection from filtration changes."]}],"title":"Filtration and tubular reabsorption","unit":"renal","aliases":["reabsorption","tubular injury","filtrate","filtration rate"],"model":"nephron","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki?autoredirectid=37348","title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12285995/","title":"Understanding Renal Tubular Function: Key Mechanisms, Clinical ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/genitourinary-disorders/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki?ruleredirectid=747","title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Genitourinary Disorders - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"fluid-loss","definition":"Hypovolemia is a decrease in the volume of fluid within the blood vessels. When the reduction is substantial, less blood returns to the heart and circulation may become inadequate to maintain tissue perfusion.","structure":"The relevant context is the intravascular space and its connection to the heart through the veins. Venous return determines ventricular filling, or preload; filling supports stroke volume through the Frank–Starling relationship, linking circulating volume with cardiac output and perfusion.","normal":"With adequate intravascular volume, venous return supports ventricular filling. The filled ventricle ejects a stroke volume, and heart rate combines with stroke volume to determine cardiac output. This circulation helps sustain arterial pressure and deliver blood to vital organs.","change":"When intravascular volume falls critically, venous return and preload decline, reducing ventricular filling and stroke volume. Cardiac output can then decrease unless heart rate compensates. A pressure drop can trigger sympathetic responses, including faster heart rate, increased contractility, and peripheral vasoconstriction.","cues":"A falling arterial pressure together with a faster heart rate or peripheral vasoconstriction may fit a compensatory response to reduced circulating volume. These cues are not specific to hypovolemia, and compensation can help preserve perfusion, so no single finding establishes the diagnosis.","distinction":"A low arterial pressure is not proof of hypovolemia; several mechanisms can lower pressure. Hypovolemia specifically involves reduced intravascular volume, which can lower venous return and stroke volume.","related":["preload","stroke-volume","shock"],"questions":[{"stem":"How does a substantial reduction in intravascular volume tend to reduce stroke volume?","options":["It reduces venous return and ventricular filling, lowering stroke volume through the Frank–Starling relationship.","It increases preload, causing the ventricle to eject less blood.","It raises afterload while leaving ventricular filling unchanged."],"feedback":["Correct: less circulating volume can reduce venous return and filling, which lowers stroke volume.","The described mechanism is reduced, not increased, preload and ventricular filling.","The evidence links reduced volume to lower venous return and filling, not primarily to increased afterload."]},{"stem":"A patient has a low arterial pressure and a faster heart rate. Which conclusion is best supported by these findings alone?","options":["They may reflect a compensatory response, but they do not establish hypovolemia.","They confirm that circulating volume is critically reduced.","They show that cardiac output and tissue perfusion are adequate."],"feedback":["Correct: tachycardia and a pressure change can occur in a compensatory response, but the pattern is not specific enough to diagnose hypovolemia.","The findings alone do not confirm the cause of low pressure or establish a critical volume reduction.","Compensatory signs do not prove that cardiac output or tissue perfusion is adequate."]}],"title":"Hypovolemia","unit":"multi","aliases":["hypovolemia","hypovolemic","volume depletion","blood loss"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/multimedia/image/compensatory-mechanisms-of-hypovolemic-shock","title":"Compensatory Mechanisms of Hypovolemic Shock - MSD Manuals"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/en-ca/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?content=summary","title":"Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"fluid","definition":"Fluid compartments are the body spaces that contain water, including fluid inside cells and fluid outside them. The extracellular fluid includes plasma and interstitial fluid.","structure":"The relevant spaces are inside cells, within blood vessels as plasma, and in the interstitium between vessels and cells. Capillary walls, the surrounding extracellular matrix, and lymphatic vessels influence fluid movement among these spaces.","normal":"Normally, water and dissolved substances move between plasma and interstitial fluid across exchange-vessel walls, while lymphatics return interstitial fluid to the circulation. Hydrostatic and oncotic forces, barrier properties, and the surrounding matrix shape this movement and help preserve compartment balance.","change":"With reduced intravascular volume, such as after hemorrhage, exchange forces can favor movement of interstitial fluid back into vessels. In inflammation or capillary hypertension, altered forces or barrier properties favor filtration into tissues; excess interstitial fluid can accumulate as edema and compress capillaries.","cues":"Visible or palpable tissue swelling can indicate excess interstitial fluid, but its presence alone does not identify the cause or establish the state of circulating volume. In swollen, confined tissues, capillary compression may also impair nutritive perfusion.","distinction":"Edema is excess interstitial fluid, not proof that the vascular compartment is adequately filled. Fluid can shift out of vessels while tissue swelling increases.","related":["exchange","interstitium","edema"],"questions":[{"stem":"After blood loss, which fluid shift can help support the vascular compartment?","options":["Interstitial fluid moves toward the vascular compartment as exchange forces readjust.","Plasma is drawn into cells, restoring vascular volume directly.","Lymphatic flow stops, trapping fluid in the interstitium."],"feedback":["Correct: reduced intravascular volume can favor interstitial fluid reabsorption into vessels.","Incorrect: the evidence describes movement from interstitium to vascular space, not into cells.","Incorrect: lymphatics normally drain interstitial fluid; stopping that flow would not restore vascular volume."]},{"stem":"A patient has tissue swelling. Which conclusion is best supported by this finding alone?","options":["There is excess interstitial fluid, but the underlying cause is not established.","The patient must have increased circulating blood volume.","Capillary permeability is necessarily normal."],"feedback":["Correct: edema denotes excess interstitial fluid, while several mechanisms can produce it.","Incorrect: fluid may accumulate in tissues even when effective intravascular volume is reduced.","Incorrect: altered permeability is one possible contributor to edema, so it cannot be assumed normal."]}],"title":"Fluid compartments","unit":"multi","aliases":["intravascular volume","interstitial fluid","intravascular fluid","fluid compartments","edema"],"model":"leak","sources":[{"url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/c00006isp003/ch1/","title":"Fluid Movement Across the Endothelial Barrier"},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/26-1-body-fluids-and-fluid-compartments","title":"26.1 Body Fluids and Fluid Compartments - Anatomy and ..."},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK348/","title":"Edema"}]},{"id":"gas-gradient","definition":"Partial pressure is the share of total gas pressure contributed by one gas. Differences in partial pressure drive oxygen and carbon dioxide across the alveolar-capillary membrane.","structure":"The relevant structure is the alveolar-capillary respiratory membrane, where air in the alveoli lies next to pulmonary capillary blood. This thin interface provides a large area for oxygen and carbon dioxide diffusion.","normal":"Normally, oxygen moves from alveolar air, where its partial pressure is higher, into capillary blood, where its partial pressure is lower. Carbon dioxide moves in the opposite direction. A larger pressure difference supports faster gas movement across the membrane.","change":"When the pressure difference narrows, oxygen diffuses less efficiently from alveoli into blood. Transfer can also be affected by membrane surface area, thickness, and gas properties. Arterial oxygen tension reflects not only diffusion but also alveolar oxygen and ventilation-perfusion matching.","cues":"A lower-than-expected arterial oxygen tension or oxygen saturation can signal impaired oxygen transfer, but neither identifies diffusion limitation by itself. Alveolar ventilation, membrane transfer, and ventilation-perfusion matching also shape arterial oxygen.","distinction":"An abnormal arterial oxygen value does not by itself establish a membrane diffusion problem, because ventilation and ventilation-perfusion matching also affect it. Dissolved oxygen tension, rather than total blood oxygen content, drives hemoglobin saturation.","related":["alveoli","vq","oxygenation"],"questions":[{"stem":"What drives oxygen movement from alveolar air into pulmonary capillary blood?","options":["A higher oxygen partial pressure in the alveoli than in the blood","Active transport of oxygen across the respiratory membrane","A higher oxygen partial pressure in the blood than in the alveoli"],"feedback":["Correct: oxygen diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient from alveolar air into blood.","Incorrect: oxygen crosses this membrane by passive diffusion, not energy-requiring active transport.","Incorrect: oxygen moves from higher to lower partial pressure, so this gradient would favor movement in the opposite direction."]},{"stem":"A patient has a low arterial oxygen tension. Which conclusion is best supported by this finding alone?","options":["It signals impaired oxygenation but does not identify the mechanism by itself.","It proves that the alveolar-capillary membrane is thickened.","It proves that ventilation-perfusion matching is normal."],"feedback":["Correct: arterial oxygen tension can be affected by alveolar oxygen, membrane transfer, and ventilation-perfusion matching.","Incorrect: the finding alone cannot establish membrane thickening as the cause.","Incorrect: a low arterial oxygen tension does not show that ventilation-perfusion matching is normal."]}],"title":"Diffusion and partial pressure","unit":"resp","aliases":["diffusion","partial pressure","diffusion gradient","oxygen gradient"],"model":"alveolar","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493219/","title":"Partial Pressure of Oxygen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/22-4-gas-exchange","title":"22.4 Gas Exchange - Anatomy and Physiology 2e | OpenStax"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK493219/","title":"Partial Pressure of Oxygen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"gut","definition":"The intestinal mucosa is the inner lining of the bowel, and mesenteric blood flow supplies it with oxygen and nutrients. Because the mucosa has high metabolic needs, it is sensitive to reduced perfusion.","structure":"The intestinal mucosa lines the bowel and depends on mesenteric circulation for blood flow. The gut has collateral vessels that can support flow when it falls, but sustained inadequate supply can injure the mucosal lining and its protective barrier.","normal":"The intestinal mucosa has a high metabolic rate and normally receives substantial blood flow. Collateral circulation and increased oxygen extraction can help the gut tolerate a decrease in mesenteric flow for a time, supporting the tissue while supply is reduced.","change":"When mesenteric flow is inadequate, mucosal cells can become ischemic and the barrier can be disrupted. Bacteria, toxins, and vasoactive mediators may then be released, contributing to inflammation and wider organ effects; reperfusion can also add tissue injury.","cues":"Reduced mesenteric flow may be partly compensated by collateral circulation, so early injury may not be obvious from a single bedside observation. A concerning pattern should be interpreted in context; no isolated finding establishes intestinal ischemia.","distinction":"A reduction in intestinal blood flow is not synonymous with established bowel infarction; injury can progress over time. Conversely, a lack of obvious early injury does not prove that perfusion is adequate.","related":["perfusion","ischemia","inflammation"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why is the intestinal mucosa especially sensitive to reduced mesenteric blood flow?","options":["It has a high metabolic rate and therefore a high blood-flow requirement.","It normally has no collateral circulation to support it.","It has a low metabolic rate and depends mainly on stored oxygen."],"feedback":["Correct: the mucosa's high metabolic rate makes it particularly dependent on adequate blood flow.","The gut has collateral circulation, which can help compensate for reduced flow for a time.","The mucosa has a high, not low, metabolic rate; the evidence does not describe it as relying mainly on stored oxygen."]},{"stem":"What can follow disruption of the intestinal mucosal barrier during ischemia?","options":["Bacteria, toxins, and vasoactive mediators may be released, contributing to systemic effects.","The barrier becomes stronger and prevents inflammatory signaling.","Mucosal injury guarantees that all intestinal blood flow has stopped."],"feedback":["Correct: barrier disruption can allow release of bacteria, toxins, and vasoactive mediators, with possible wider consequences.","Barrier disruption is associated with release of these substances, not with a strengthened barrier.","Mucosal injury reflects inadequate supply and can progress, but it does not establish that all intestinal blood flow has ceased."]}],"title":"Intestinal mucosa and mesenteric flow","unit":"gi","aliases":["intestinal mucosa","mesenteric perfusion","mesenteric ischemia","bowel ischemia"],"model":"gut","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia?ruleredirectid=745","title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554527/","title":"Bowel Ischemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4753178/","title":"Mesenteric ischemia: Pathogenesis and challenging diagnostic and therapeutic modalities"}]},{"id":"hemoglobin","title":"Hemoglobin and oxygen delivery","unit":"heme","model":"delivery","aliases":["hemoglobin","oxygen-carrying capacity","oxygen delivery"],"definition":"Hemoglobin is an oxygen-carrying protein inside red blood cells. It picks up oxygen in the lungs and carries most of the blood’s oxygen to body tissues.","structure":"Hemoglobin is packed inside red blood cells, which travel through the heart and blood vessels. In lung capillaries—tiny vessels beside air sacs—oxygen moves from the air into blood and binds to hemoglobin. The cells then pass through arterial vessels toward tissue capillaries, where oxygen can leave the blood near body cells.","normal":"With each breath, oxygen reaches the lung air sacs and moves into nearby capillary blood. It binds to hemoglobin in red blood cells; a small amount remains dissolved in plasma, the liquid part of blood. The heart circulates this oxygen-rich blood to tissues, where oxygen leaves hemoglobin and enters cells for energy-producing processes.","change":"When hemoglobin concentration falls, or fewer binding sites carry oxygen, blood may carry less oxygen even if saturation seems reassuring. Low cardiac output can also reduce how much oxygen reaches tissues per minute. Cells may then receive less oxygen for metabolism; saturation alone cannot represent total oxygen delivery or prove that tissue oxygen is adequate.","cues":"A pulse oximeter estimates saturation, not hemoglobin concentration or blood flow. A person can have a normal reading yet impaired oxygen delivery with severe anemia or poor circulation. Respiratory distress, altered alertness, or a rapid heart rate may be relevant observations, but none alone establishes the cause or tissue oxygen status.","distinction":"A normal SpO₂ does not mean total oxygen delivery is normal. Oxygen saturation does not measure ventilation, blood flow, or tissue oxygen use.","related":["alveoli","oxygenation","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"A person has a normal SpO₂ but a substantially reduced hemoglobin concentration. Which conclusion is best?","options":["Saturation may be normal while the blood carries less oxygen overall.","Oxygen delivery is necessarily normal because saturation is normal.","The pulse oximeter directly measures hemoglobin concentration."],"feedback":["Correct: saturation describes the proportion of hemoglobin binding sites occupied by oxygen, not how much hemoglobin is present.","A normal saturation does not rule out reduced oxygen carriage when hemoglobin concentration is low.","A pulse oximeter estimates oxygen saturation; it does not directly measure hemoglobin concentration."]},{"stem":"If arterial oxygen content stays the same but cardiac output falls, what happens to oxygen delivery?","options":["It falls because less oxygenated blood reaches tissues per unit time.","It stays the same because arterial oxygen content has not changed.","It rises because tissues extract more oxygen from each unit of blood."],"feedback":["Correct: oxygen delivery depends on both arterial oxygen content and cardiac output.","Unchanged oxygen content does not keep delivery constant if cardiac output decreases.","Greater extraction may affect the difference between arterial and venous oxygen content, but it does not make delivery rise when cardiac output falls."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK525974/","title":"Oxygen Saturation: Monitoring and Interpretation - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-26491/?report=reader","title":"Oxygen Saturation: Monitoring and Interpretation - StatPearls"}]},{"id":"hypoperfusion","title":"Poor perfusion","unit":"cardio","model":"perfusion","aliases":["poor perfusion","hypoperfusion","inadequate perfusion","impaired perfusion","impaired tissue perfusion","poor tissue perfusion","end-organ hypoperfusion"],"definition":"Poor perfusion means too little blood reaches a tissue to meet its needs. This can limit the oxygen and other materials available to its cells.","structure":"Perfusion happens throughout the body’s tissues. Small arteries branch into capillaries, tiny vessels whose thin walls lie beside tissue fluid and cells; small veins carry blood away. These capillary beds connect the larger circulation to the spaces where oxygen and nutrients leave blood and cellular waste enters it.","normal":"In healthy circulation, the heart propels blood through arteries into capillary beds. Blood flow brings oxygen to the capillaries, where it moves into nearby tissue fluid and cells. Cells use oxygen for aerobic metabolism, making energy; blood then carries some waste products away through veins and returns toward the heart.","change":"When circulation falls or blood is unevenly distributed, capillary flow may not supply enough oxygen to cells, even if blood pressure is not low. If the deficit persists, organ function can deteriorate. Lactate may rise, but increased production or reduced clearance can also raise it for reasons other than oxygen shortage.","cues":"Reduced tissue flow may be reflected by confusion, cool or clammy skin, weak pulses, or low urine output, but these findings vary with the cause and the body’s compensation. Some distributive states can produce warm skin and normal capillary refill. A single sign alone does not establish poor perfusion.","distinction":"Low blood pressure is common in shock, but it is not required. Conversely, a low reading alone does not prove that tissues are poorly perfused.","related":["shock","oxygen-debt","lactate"],"questions":[{"stem":"A person has signs that may suggest reduced tissue blood flow, but their blood pressure is normal. Which conclusion fits the concept of poor perfusion?","options":["Normal blood pressure does not rule out poor tissue perfusion.","Normal blood pressure means tissue perfusion must be adequate.","Normal blood pressure identifies a heart-related cause."],"feedback":["Correct. Poor perfusion can occur before blood pressure falls or in distributive states.","Blood pressure alone cannot establish whether tissues are receiving enough blood and oxygen.","A normal blood pressure does not identify the cause of possible poor perfusion."]},{"stem":"In a distributive state, which skin finding can still be consistent with poor perfusion?","options":["Warm or flushed skin, because vasodilation can alter blood flow.","Cool skin, because it is required whenever perfusion is poor.","Normal skin temperature, because it rules out poor perfusion."],"feedback":["Correct. Vasodilation can make skin warm or flushed even when tissue perfusion is impaired.","Cool skin can occur, but it is not required in every type of shock.","Normal skin temperature does not rule out poor perfusion."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/es-us/professional/cuidados-cr%C3%ADticos/shock-y-reanimaci%C3%B3n-con-l%C3%ADquidos/shock","title":"Shock - Cuidados críticos - Manual Merck versión para ..."}]},{"id":"hypotension","title":"Hypotension","unit":"cardio","model":"perfusion","aliases":["hypotension","hypotensive","low blood pressure"],"definition":"Hypotension is lower-than-usual pressure in the arteries. A low reading alone does not show that tissues are being injured.","structure":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart and branch into smaller arteries and arterioles, which lead to capillary beds. Arterial pressure is the force of blood against vessel walls. Beyond capillaries, blood enters small veins and returns toward the heart.","normal":"The heart ejects blood into the arteries, creating pressure that drives blood through progressively smaller vessels. Arterial walls and the heart’s continuing activity help sustain flow between beats. Blood then passes through capillaries, where oxygen and other materials reach tissues, before returning through veins to the heart.","change":"Pressure can fall when circulating volume is low, the heart pumps less effectively, or blood vessels widen. These changes reduce the pressure gradient that supports forward flow. The body may compensate by increasing heart rate and tightening vessels, so pressure can remain measurable even while organ perfusion is threatened; the effect depends on compensation and underlying health.","cues":"A low blood-pressure reading may occur without obvious symptoms, and some people in shock initially have normal pressure. If tissue perfusion is inadequate, clues can include confusion, cool or clammy skin, weak rapid pulses, or reduced urine output. Widened vessels may instead leave skin warm and pulses bounding, so no single sign settles the cause.","distinction":"A low reading alone does not establish shock; shock involves inadequate tissue perfusion. Conversely, shock can be present before blood pressure falls.","related":["map","perfusion","shock"],"questions":[{"stem":"A person has a low blood-pressure reading but no other evidence of poor perfusion. What conclusion is justified?","options":["The reading alone does not establish shock.","Shock is confirmed because the pressure is low.","Shock is ruled out because there are no other signs."],"feedback":["Correct: shock is assessed from evidence of tissue hypoperfusion, not blood pressure alone.","A low reading can occur without shock, so it does not confirm the condition by itself.","Shock can occur without obvious signs at first, so their absence does not rule it out."]},{"stem":"If major fluid loss lowers blood pressure, which change helps explain the effect on cardiac output?","options":["Reduced venous return lowers ventricular filling and stroke volume.","Increased venous return lowers ventricular filling and stroke volume.","Peripheral vasoconstriction directly prevents the ventricles from filling."],"feedback":["Correct: less blood returning to the heart reduces filling and can lower stroke volume and cardiac output.","Increased venous return would not explain reduced filling; fluid loss tends to diminish venous return.","Vasoconstriction is a compensatory response and does not directly prevent ventricular filling."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?ruleredirectid=741wruleredirectid=5","title":"Shock - Shock - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"icp","definition":"Intracranial pressure is the pressure within the skull. It reflects the combined contents of a confined space: brain tissue, blood, and cerebrospinal fluid.","structure":"The cranium is a rigid, nonexpandable space containing brain tissue, intracranial blood, and cerebrospinal fluid. Because these contents share a fixed compartment, a volume change in one affects the room available for the others.","normal":"With the skull acting as a fixed-volume container, small changes in one intracranial component can be buffered by shifts in blood or cerebrospinal fluid. These adjustments help limit pressure change while preserving the balance among brain tissue, blood, and fluid.","change":"When an intracranial component expands, blood or cerebrospinal fluid may shift to compensate. This reserve is limited; if added volume cannot be offset, intracranial pressure rises. The higher pressure can reduce cerebral perfusion pressure and threaten blood flow to brain tissue.","cues":"A rising intracranial pressure measurement, particularly alongside worsening neurological function, is a signal of possible pressure-related change. Neither the measurement nor a neurological finding alone identifies the cause or tells how much compensation remains.","distinction":"A change in intracranial pressure is not simply a change in brain tissue volume; blood and cerebrospinal fluid also occupy the skull. A pressure reading alone does not specify which component changed.","related":["cerebral","csf","fluid"],"questions":[{"stem":"A new mass increases volume inside the rigid skull. Which explanation best describes the initial compensatory principle?","options":["Blood or cerebrospinal fluid may shift to make room for the added volume.","The skull expands to accommodate the mass without affecting other contents.","Brain tissue is compressed out of the skull so the total intracranial volume stays fixed."],"feedback":["Correct: under the Monro-Kellie principle, blood and cerebrospinal fluid can shift to offset added volume within the fixed cranial space.","The cranium is described as rigid and nonexpandable, so skull expansion is not the compensatory mechanism.","The brain remains within the skull; compensation involves shifts in blood or cerebrospinal fluid rather than expelling tissue."]},{"stem":"Why can rising intracranial pressure threaten cerebral blood flow even when mean arterial pressure has not changed?","options":["Higher intracranial pressure reduces the pressure gradient represented by cerebral perfusion pressure.","Higher intracranial pressure increases cerebral perfusion pressure by adding pressure to the arterial side.","Cerebral perfusion pressure is independent of intracranial pressure."],"feedback":["Correct: cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, so a rise in the latter reduces the gradient if the former is unchanged.","Intracranial pressure is not added to arterial pressure in this relationship; it opposes the perfusion gradient.","Intracranial pressure is a component of the cerebral perfusion pressure relationship."]}],"title":"Intracranial pressure","unit":"neuro","aliases":["intracranial pressure","intracranial hypertension","ICP"],"model":"cranium","sources":[{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/medical-surgical-nursing/pages/17-1-intracranial-pressure-changes","title":"17.1 Intracranial Pressure Changes - OpenStax"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK542298/","title":"Intracranial Pressure Monitoring"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542298/","title":"Intracranial Pressure Monitoring - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"inflammation","definition":"Inflammation and capillary leak describe inflammatory changes that weaken the vascular barrier and allow fluid to move into tissues. In sepsis, these changes can occur alongside altered vascular tone and microcirculatory function.","structure":"The main structure is the vascular endothelium lining small vessels, including capillaries and venules. Its barrier, together with vascular regulation and the microcirculation, affects fluid retention, blood flow, leukocyte movement, and coagulation.","normal":"Normally, endothelial cells help regulate the passage of fluid and solutes between blood and tissues and participate in control of vascular tone, leukocyte trafficking, and coagulation. These functions allow exchange while maintaining a barrier between the vascular and interstitial spaces.","change":"In sepsis, inflammatory and vascular stress can disrupt endothelial barrier function, allowing fluid and proteins to move into tissues. Vasoregulatory dysfunction and microvascular thrombosis may also develop, contributing to edema, reduced effective circulating volume, and impaired tissue perfusion.","cues":"Progressive tissue edema and impaired fluid tolerance despite resuscitation can suggest barrier dysfunction in sepsis, but neither finding alone establishes capillary leak. Microcirculatory hypoperfusion and organ dysfunction may accompany endothelial injury and have multiple contributing mechanisms.","distinction":"Inflammation does not mean that every vessel is simply more permeable or that edema has one cause. In sepsis, barrier disruption can coexist with vasoregulatory and microcirculatory dysfunction.","related":["fluid","interstitium","shock"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which change most directly explains tissue edema when the endothelial barrier is disrupted?","options":["More intravascular fluid and proteins move into the interstitial space.","Lymphatic return increases enough to remove all tissue fluid.","Blood flow bypasses capillaries without any change in barrier function."],"feedback":["Correct: barrier disruption permits fluid and proteins to leave the vascular space and accumulate in tissues.","Incorrect: increased lymphatic return would oppose accumulation rather than explain it.","Incorrect: capillary bypass can impair tissue perfusion, but it does not by itself explain edema from barrier disruption."]},{"stem":"In a patient with sepsis and progressive edema, what is the most careful interpretation?","options":["The finding may suggest endothelial barrier dysfunction, but it does not identify the entire mechanism by itself.","The edema proves that cardiac output is high.","The edema rules out microcirculatory hypoperfusion."],"feedback":["Correct: edema can be a clinical clue to barrier dysfunction, while several vascular mechanisms may coexist.","Incorrect: the evidence does not make edema proof of high cardiac output.","Incorrect: endothelial dysfunction and edema can occur alongside microcirculatory hypoperfusion."]}],"title":"Inflammation and capillary leak","unit":"multi","aliases":["capillary leak","inflammation","inflammatory response","endothelial injury"],"model":"leak","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13668622/?tool=EBI","title":"Sepsis-associated endothelial dysfunction: clinical phenotypes, biomarkers, bedside evidence, and translational implications"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13397701/","title":"Sepsis-related immunosuppression: a comprehensive review of current ... - PMC"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/6429642","title":"Sepsis: The evolution in definition, pathophysiology, and management"}]},{"id":"insulin","definition":"Insulin is a hormone that helps regulate blood glucose and the body’s use and storage of nutrients. In muscle and adipose tissue, it promotes glucose uptake by increasing glucose transporter availability at the cell surface.","structure":"Insulin acts on target tissues including skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissue. In muscle and adipose cells, it promotes movement of GLUT4 transporters from intracellular storage vesicles to the plasma membrane, allowing glucose to enter cells.","normal":"Normally, insulin signaling moves GLUT4 transporters from intracellular vesicles to the surface of muscle and adipose cells. This increases cellular glucose entry and helps lower circulating glucose; insulin also supports glycogen synthesis and reduces glucose production by the liver.","change":"When insulin is deficient or target tissues are insulin-resistant, insulin-mediated glucose uptake is impaired. In resistance, cells respond less effectively; with deficiency, the signal is inadequate. Both can contribute to elevated blood glucose, while the underlying mechanisms are not identical.","cues":"Elevated blood glucose can be consistent with impaired insulin secretion or reduced tissue responsiveness, among other mechanisms. A glucose result alone does not reveal which mechanism is present or establish a specific diabetes type.","distinction":"High blood glucose does not by itself prove that insulin is absent; insulin resistance can also impair glucose regulation. Reduced glucose uptake in muscle is one part of a broader metabolic process.","related":["cells","pancreas","ketones"],"questions":[{"stem":"After insulin signaling in skeletal muscle, what cellular change most directly supports glucose uptake?","options":["GLUT4 transporters move from intracellular vesicles to the cell membrane.","Aquaporin-2 channels move into the collecting-duct membrane.","Glucose is converted into ketone bodies inside muscle cells."],"feedback":["Correct: Insulin promotes GLUT4 movement to the muscle-cell surface, where glucose can enter.","Aquaporin-2 is involved in kidney water reabsorption, not insulin-mediated glucose uptake in muscle.","Ketone production is associated with hepatic metabolism of fatty acids during insulin deficiency, not the direct muscle response described."]},{"stem":"Two people have elevated blood glucose. Which statement best reflects the possible mechanisms described here?","options":["One may have inadequate insulin secretion, while another may have reduced tissue response to insulin.","Both must have complete absence of insulin because elevated glucose has one cause.","Both must have excess ADH because water regulation controls GLUT4 movement."],"feedback":["Correct: Insulin deficiency and insulin resistance can each impair glucose regulation through different mechanisms.","Elevated glucose does not establish complete insulin absence; reduced responsiveness can also contribute.","ADH regulates renal water handling, whereas GLUT4 movement is associated with insulin signaling in target tissues."]}],"title":"Insulin and glucose uptake","unit":"endo","aliases":["insulin","glucose uptake","insulin resistance"],"model":"glucose","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK430900","title":"Hyperglycemia"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-22310/","title":"Physiology, Glucose"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11990519/","title":"Achievements in the Pathophysiology and Treatment of Insulin ..."}]},{"id":"interstitium","definition":"The interstitial space is the tissue space surrounding cells outside blood vessels. Extravasation occurs when an infused solution leaves a vessel and enters surrounding tissue, where it may cause injury.","structure":"The interstitial space lies around tissue cells, outside the blood vessels. In an infusion-related extravasation, fluid leaves its intended pathway within the vessel and enters this surrounding tissue compartment, where its volume and properties can affect local structures.","normal":"In the usual course of an intravenous infusion, the solution remains in the vessel and follows its intended pathway. The interstitial space is the tissue compartment around cells; it is not the planned route for infused fluid.","change":"If solution escapes the vessel, it enters surrounding tissue and may cause injury according to its volume and properties, including pH and osmolarity. Tissue pressure, direct cellular toxicity, or local ischemia from vasoconstriction can contribute; some substances cause progressively expanding damage.","cues":"Swelling, discomfort, or local tissue change around an infusion site may be consistent with leakage, but appearance can initially underestimate the eventual area of injury. The type and amount of solution and the evolving findings matter; appearance alone cannot identify the agent.","distinction":"Not every leak has the same tissue effect: injury depends on both the amount and properties of the solution. An infusion-site change alone does not establish whether the fluid is irritating or vesicant.","related":["fluid","inflammation","exchange"],"questions":[{"stem":"What best explains why two infusion leaks may cause different degrees of tissue injury?","options":["The volume and physicochemical properties of the leaked solutions can differ.","All leaked solutions produce the same tissue effect if the visible swelling is similar.","Only the location of the leak determines the tissue effect."],"feedback":["Correct: injury depends in part on the volume and properties, such as pH and osmolarity, of the solution.","Incorrect: leaked solutions can vary in their potential for cellular toxicity, osmotic effects, and other injury mechanisms.","Incorrect: location matters, but the solution's volume and properties also affect injury."]},{"stem":"A small-looking infusion-site change is observed. What is the most accurate interpretation?","options":["The visible area may not show the full eventual extent of tissue injury.","The visible area reliably identifies the final size of injury.","A small visible change proves the leaked solution was harmless."],"feedback":["Correct: damage can extend beyond the initial visible appearance, depending on the substance and other factors.","Incorrect: the initial appearance may underestimate the eventual area of injury.","Incorrect: a small visible change does not establish that the substance was harmless."]}],"title":"Interstitial space and extravasation","unit":"skin","aliases":["interstitial space","extravasation","infiltration"],"model":"leak","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3664495/","title":"Extravasation Injuries in Adults"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8579427/pdf/Dtsch_Arztebl_Int-118_0547.pdf","title":"Ausgabe_A_S00001P547_S00018P564"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/openrnas/ch4centrallines/","title":"Chapter 4 Manage Central Lines"}]},{"id":"ischemia","title":"Ischemia","unit":"cardio","model":"coronary","aliases":["ischemia","ischaemia","ischemic injury","ischemic"],"definition":"Ischemia happens when a tissue does not get enough blood flow to meet its needs for oxygen and glucose. It can impair function or, if severe or prolonged, lead to tissue injury.","structure":"In the heart, ischemia affects the myocardium, the muscle wall that pumps blood. Coronary arteries travel over the heart’s surface and branch into smaller vessels that enter this muscle. The heart’s chambers lie inside the muscular wall; coronary vessels supply the myocardium rather than carrying blood through the chambers.","normal":"Blood leaves the aorta and enters the right and left coronary arteries, which branch into smaller vessels supplying the heart muscle. This flow delivers oxygen and glucose used to make energy. As the myocardium contracts, its energy demand rises; healthy supply keeps pace so the muscle can continue pumping.","change":"When coronary flow falls, delivery of oxygen and glucose may no longer meet the myocardium’s energy demand. A gradually narrowing plaque can limit flow and cause ischemia, especially when demand rises. If a plaque ruptures, a clot may abruptly obstruct flow, producing acute ischemia and potentially myocardial injury or infarction; severity depends partly on obstruction and location.","cues":"Ischemia may cause no obvious symptom, or it may appear as stable angina or an acute coronary syndrome; severe events can trigger rhythm problems, heart failure, or sudden death. Chest symptoms alone do not prove ischemia. Clinicians interpret symptoms alongside ECG and, when needed, stress testing or coronary angiography.","distinction":"Reduced blood flow is not the only possible cause of ischemia; supply can also fail to meet unusually high demand. Chest discomfort by itself does not establish coronary ischemia.","related":["perfusion","coronary","oxygen-debt"],"questions":[{"stem":"A tissue’s oxygen and glucose delivery stays the same while its energy demand rises. What is the immediate risk?","options":["Supply may no longer meet demand, causing ischemia.","Blood flow necessarily increases enough to match the demand.","Ischemia can occur only after an artery is fully blocked."],"feedback":["Correct: ischemia can occur when energy demand exceeds the available supply.","Not necessarily: unchanged delivery may fail to meet the increased demand.","A complete blockage is not required; reduced flow can cause ischemia, depending on demand."]},{"stem":"Why might a growing coronary plaque cause ischemia when the heart’s demand for energy rises?","options":["The narrowing can limit blood flow, so supply may not meet the higher demand.","The plaque increases delivery of oxygen and glucose to the heart muscle.","Ischemia requires a clot to completely block the coronary artery."],"feedback":["Correct: narrowing can restrict supply, and the degree of narrowing that causes ischemia varies with demand.","A plaque that narrows the artery can restrict flow rather than increase delivery.","Complete blockage is not required; narrowing can limit supply enough to cause ischemia."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/overview-of-coronary-artery-disease","title":"Overview of Coronary Artery Disease - Cardiology"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC381293/","title":"Series Introduction: Tissue ischemia: pathophysiology and therapeutics"}]},{"id":"ketones","definition":"Ketones are substances produced when the liver uses fatty acids as fuel. When ketone production is excessive, acidic ketone bodies can contribute to metabolic acidosis, as occurs in diabetic ketoacidosis.","structure":"Ketone production occurs mainly in the liver when free fatty acids are converted into ketone bodies. In insulin deficiency with increased counterregulatory hormone activity, lipolysis supplies more fatty acids for this process; the resulting acids enter the circulation.","normal":"Normally, insulin restrains lipolysis and limits the availability of free fatty acids for ketone production. When the body needs alternative fuel, the liver can produce ketones, but insulin action helps keep their production from becoming excessive.","change":"With severe insulin deficiency and increased counterregulatory hormones, lipolysis releases more free fatty acids. The liver converts these fatty acids into ketone bodies, including acidic compounds; accumulation can produce ketonemia and metabolic acidosis. Hyperglycemia may also cause water and electrolyte loss.","cues":"Ketones in blood or urine can signal increased ketone production, and signs of dehydration or altered metabolic status may accompany severe illness. Ketones alone do not establish diabetic ketoacidosis or show its full severity.","distinction":"Ketones alone do not establish diabetic ketoacidosis; metabolic acidosis is also part of the picture. Glucose is often elevated, but DKA can occur without marked hyperglycemia.","related":["insulin","buffer","potassium"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can severe insulin deficiency increase ketone production?","options":["It permits increased lipolysis, supplying free fatty acids that the liver converts into ketones.","It moves GLUT4 to the cell surface, increasing muscle glucose uptake and ketone production.","It increases ADH action, which directly converts water into ketone bodies."],"feedback":["Correct: Insulin deficiency and counterregulatory hormones promote lipolysis; the liver then uses released fatty acids to make ketones.","Insulin promotes GLUT4 movement, and this does not explain the increased fatty-acid supply driving ketogenesis.","ADH regulates kidney water reabsorption and does not convert water into ketones."]},{"stem":"A patient has detectable ketones. Which additional concept is needed before interpreting the finding as diabetic ketoacidosis?","options":["Whether metabolic acidosis is also present, alongside the clinical context.","Whether the patient has low ADH activity, because that alone defines ketoacidosis.","Whether GLUT4 is absent from every tissue, because that is required for ketones."],"feedback":["Correct: DKA involves ketone accumulation together with metabolic acidosis; ketones alone are not enough to establish it.","ADH activity affects water handling and is not the defining additional feature of DKA.","The evidence links DKA to insulin deficiency and impaired glucose use, but does not require complete absence of GLUT4 from tissues."]}],"title":"Ketones and metabolic acidosis","unit":"endo","aliases":["ketones","ketonemia","ketoacidosis"],"model":"glucose","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK620701","title":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-sg/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/diabetes-mellitus-and-disorders-of-carbohydrate-metabolism/diabetic-ketoacidosis-dka","title":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) - Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279146/","title":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Endotext"}]},{"id":"lactate","definition":"Lactate is a substance produced and used by cells as part of metabolism. Its level can rise when production increases, clearance decreases, or both occur.","structure":"Lactate is produced in tissues and circulates in blood; the liver is the primary organ responsible for clearing it. The blood level reflects the balance between production, use, and clearance across the body.","normal":"Cells normally produce lactate, and the body uses and clears it. The liver is the primary site of clearance, so a measured level reflects not just tissue production but also how effectively the body is removing lactate.","change":"With reduced tissue oxygen delivery, cells may produce more lactate. But adrenergic stimulation, inflammation, altered cellular metabolism, and impaired clearance can also raise it, sometimes despite adequate oxygen delivery. These mechanisms can coexist, making an elevated level nonspecific.","cues":"An elevated lactate level can signal tissue hypoperfusion and may be useful for following physiologic stress, but it does not identify the cause. Interpret it alongside the clinical picture, since production and clearance can change independently of oxygen delivery.","distinction":"An elevated lactate is not proof of tissue hypoxia or shock. Consider both increased production and reduced clearance rather than attributing the result to one mechanism alone.","related":["hypoperfusion","oxygen-debt","shock"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient has an elevated lactate level despite preserved oxygenation. Which explanation is consistent with the evidence?","options":["Adrenergic stimulation or inflammation may increase lactate production without tissue hypoxia.","Preserved oxygenation proves that the lactate result is inaccurate.","The liver must be producing all of the excess lactate."],"feedback":["Correct: adrenergic stimulation and immune activation can increase lactate production even when oxygen delivery is adequate.","Preserved oxygenation does not make the result inaccurate; lactate can rise through oxygen-independent mechanisms.","The liver is primarily responsible for clearance, though an acutely injured liver may also contribute to production; it is not the only possible source."]},{"stem":"Why can impaired liver function contribute to a higher blood lactate level?","options":["The liver is the primary organ responsible for lactate clearance.","The liver is the only tissue capable of producing lactate.","Liver dysfunction always means that tissue oxygen delivery is inadequate."],"feedback":["Correct: impaired hepatic clearance can allow lactate to accumulate, and liver injury may also contribute to production.","Lactate can be produced in multiple tissues, so the liver is not its only possible source.","Liver dysfunction may affect lactate levels, but it does not by itself establish inadequate tissue oxygen delivery."]}],"title":"Lactate and physiologic stress","unit":"multi","aliases":["lactate","lactic acid"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/3975915","title":"Etiology and therapeutic approach to elevated lactate"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13617900/","title":"Hyperlactatemia in sepsis and shock: a renal metabolic ... - PMC"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8239768/","title":"The role of lactate in sepsis and COVID‐19 - PubMed Central - NIH"}]},{"id":"left-ventricle","definition":"The left ventricle is the heart chamber that ejects blood into the systemic circulation. Its output per beat depends on filling, the pressure it ejects against, and contraction strength.","structure":"The left ventricle receives blood during diastole and ejects it into the systemic arteries during systole. Its end-diastolic volume reflects filling, while end-systolic volume is the blood remaining after contraction; their difference is stroke volume.","normal":"During normal function, the left ventricle fills during diastole and contracts during systole to eject blood into systemic circulation. Stroke volume is the difference between end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes, and cardiac output depends on stroke volume multiplied by heart rate.","change":"When left-ventricular contractility is impaired, the chamber may empty less effectively, leaving more blood after contraction and reducing stroke volume. Reduced filling or increased afterload can also reduce ejection; impaired performance may mean less blood enters systemic circulation to meet bodily needs.","cues":"An ejection fraction estimates the proportion of end-diastolic volume ejected with each contraction and can inform assessment of systolic function. It is an index, not a direct measurement of cardiac output, and one value alone does not explain the mechanism.","distinction":"Ejection fraction is the fraction of filled ventricular volume ejected, not the total volume pumped per minute. A reduced value can reflect impaired systolic function but does not alone explain the cause or the patient’s overall state.","related":["output","stroke-volume","preload"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which expression defines left-ventricular stroke volume?","options":["End-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume.","Heart rate multiplied by end-diastolic volume.","End-systolic volume divided by heart rate."],"feedback":["The volume ejected per beat is the amount present at end-diastole minus the amount remaining at end-systole.","Heart rate multiplied by stroke volume gives cardiac output; this option does not define stroke volume.","End-systolic volume divided by heart rate is not the stated stroke-volume relationship."]},{"stem":"A patient’s ejection fraction is being used to describe left-ventricular function. What does it represent?","options":["The proportion of end-diastolic volume ejected during systole.","The total volume of blood pumped by the heart in one minute.","The pressure the ventricle must overcome during contraction."],"feedback":["Ejection fraction is stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, expressed as a proportion or percentage.","Cardiac output is blood pumped per unit time and depends on heart rate and stroke volume.","The pressure the ventricle ejects against is afterload, not ejection fraction."]}],"title":"Left ventricle and ejection","unit":"cardio","aliases":["left ventricle","left ventricular dysfunction","ejection fraction","contractility"],"model":"pump","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541098/","title":"Physiology, Left Ventricular Function - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/overview-of-heart-failure","title":"Overview of Heart Failure - Cardiology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553115/","title":"Heart Failure and Ejection Fraction - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"liver","definition":"The liver receives blood from the portal vein and hepatic artery and processes substances carried in that blood. Portal hypertension is elevated pressure in the portal vein, usually because blood meets increased resistance on its way through or beyond the liver.","structure":"The portal vein, formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins, carries blood from the abdominal gastrointestinal tract, spleen, and pancreas to the liver. Within the liver, this blood passes through sinusoids and joins blood from the hepatic artery before leaving through hepatic veins.","normal":"Normally, portal blood flows into the liver’s sinusoids, where it joins hepatic arterial blood. Blood then leaves through the hepatic veins and enters the inferior vena cava. This route brings absorbed nutrients to the liver for processing before blood returns to general circulation.","change":"When resistance to portal flow rises, as fibrosis does in cirrhosis, pressure builds upstream. Portosystemic collateral vessels can form, diverting some blood around the liver. These vessels may reduce portal pressure somewhat, but less portal blood reaches the liver for processing, and fragile esophageal or gastric varices may develop.","cues":"Possible bedside signals include findings associated with varices or portosystemic encephalopathy, but neither a single observation nor altered liver-related findings establish portal hypertension by themselves. The cause and location of increased resistance vary, so cues need interpretation alongside the broader clinical picture.","distinction":"Collateral vessels are bypass routes, not proof that portal pressure has normalized or that liver processing is unchanged. Varices can occur with portal hypertension, but their presence or absence alone does not identify its cause.","related":["abdominal-veins","gut","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which route best describes normal portal blood flow through the liver?","options":["Portal vein to liver sinusoids, where it joins hepatic arterial blood, then through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava.","Portal vein directly to the inferior vena cava, bypassing the liver sinusoids.","Hepatic veins into the sinusoids, then out through the portal vein."],"feedback":["Correct: Portal blood enters liver sinusoids, mixes with hepatic arterial blood, and leaves through hepatic veins.","The portal vein normally carries blood to the liver; it does not ordinarily bypass the sinusoids on its way to the vena cava.","This reverses the usual direction: portal blood enters the liver, while hepatic veins carry blood away from it."]},{"stem":"Why can portal hypertension lead to esophageal varices?","options":["Increased resistance to portal flow can promote collateral vessels, including enlarged veins near the lower esophagus.","Portal hypertension directly increases digestive enzyme activation in the esophagus.","Portal hypertension makes hepatic veins carry blood into the portal vein."],"feedback":["Correct: Collateral pathways can enlarge into fragile varices in the distal esophagus when portal pressure is elevated.","Digestive enzyme activation is associated with pancreatic injury, not the formation of esophageal varices in this mechanism.","Hepatic veins drain blood from the liver toward the inferior vena cava; reversing that flow does not explain varices."]}],"title":"Liver function and portal flow","unit":"gi","aliases":["portal hypertension","portal vein","liver function","hepatic failure"],"model":"portal","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hepatic-and-biliary-disorders/approach-to-the-patient-with-liver-disease/portal-hypertension","title":"Portal Hypertension"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/liver-and-gallbladder-disorders/manifestations-of-liver-disease/portal-hypertension","title":"Portal Hypertension - Liver and Gallbladder Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hepatic-and-biliary-disorders/approach-to-the-patient-with-liver-disease/portal-hypertension?media=full","title":"Portal Hypertension - Portal Hypertension - Merck Manual ..."}]},{"id":"map","title":"Mean arterial pressure","unit":"cardio","model":"perfusion","aliases":["mean arterial pressure","MAP"],"definition":"Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the average pressure pushing blood through the arteries over a heartbeat. It helps describe the pressure available to move blood toward body tissues.","structure":"The left ventricle, the heart’s main pumping chamber for the body, sends blood into the aorta and systemic arteries. These arteries branch into arterioles, the small vessels that lead to capillaries, where blood and tissues exchange materials. MAP refers to pressure in this arterial pathway.","normal":"The left ventricle contracts and sends blood into the aorta. Blood flows through large arteries, then reaches arterioles, where vessel narrowing or widening strongly influences resistance to flow. Together, the amount the heart pumps and this resistance shape arterial pressure. MAP summarizes that pressure across the heartbeat.","change":"If cardiac output, the amount of blood the heart pumps each minute, falls, or if vascular resistance falls, MAP may decrease. Less pressure can mean less blood reaches organs, especially if the change is substantial or sustained. Conversely, increased output or resistance can raise MAP and increase pressure on arterial walls.","cues":"A blood pressure cuff gives systolic pressure during heart contraction and diastolic pressure between contractions; MAP can be estimated from these values. A low reading may occur with reduced organ blood flow, while prolonged high pressure can harm organs. Readings and signs of organ function need context; one finding alone does not establish a cause.","distinction":"MAP is not the same as systolic pressure, which is the peak arterial pressure during a heartbeat. A single blood pressure reading does not by itself prove that an organ is poorly perfused.","related":["output","afterload","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which pair most directly shapes systemic arterial pressure?","options":["Cardiac output and systemic vascular resistance","Heart chamber size and blood oxygen content","Venous pressure and capillary exchange rate"],"feedback":["Correct: pressure depends on how much blood the heart pumps and the resistance to its flow.","These can matter to cardiovascular function, but they are not the pair identified as directly determining systemic arterial pressure.","Venous pressure and capillary exchange are not the two main factors in the pressure relationship described here."]},{"stem":"If an arteriole widens while other factors stay the same, what happens to resistance?","options":["It decreases, making blood flow less resisted.","It increases because a wider vessel holds more blood.","It stays the same because only vessel length affects resistance."],"feedback":["Correct: a larger vessel radius substantially reduces resistance to blood flow.","A wider vessel does not increase resistance; radius has a strong inverse effect on resistance.","Vessel length matters, but radius also strongly affects resistance."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538308/","title":"Pathophysiology"},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/pharmacology/pages/16-1-introduction-to-the-heart-circulation-and-blood-flow","title":"16.1Introduction to the Heart, Circulation, and Blood Flow"}]},{"id":"marrow","definition":"Bone marrow is the tissue where blood-forming cells develop. Its production of blood cells supports oxygen transport, host defense, and clot formation.","structure":"Bone marrow is the blood-forming tissue inside bones. Stem cells there develop into immature red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelet-producing cells, which mature and enter the circulation to support the body's changing needs.","normal":"Normally, marrow stem cells produce blood cell precursors that mature into red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. Production responds to the body's needs: low tissue oxygen can stimulate red cell production, while infection or bleeding can prompt increased white cell or platelet production.","change":"When marrow production is impaired, one or more blood cell lines may be reduced or ineffective. This can contribute to anemia, neutropenia, or thrombocytopenia; marrow disorders or infiltration can interfere with production, and examination may help distinguish reduced production from other causes.","cues":"Fatigue or pallor, increased infections or fever, and bleeding or bruising may reflect changes in different blood cell lines. These cues are not specific to marrow dysfunction; patterns in blood counts and the broader clinical context matter.","distinction":"A low blood cell count does not by itself prove that marrow production is impaired; cells may also be lost, destroyed, or sequestered. Findings across cell lines and clinical context help guide interpretation.","related":["platelets","neutrophils","cells"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which statement best describes the normal role of bone marrow in blood cell formation?","options":["It develops stem cells into red cells, white cells, and platelet-producing cells.","It stores mature blood cells and releases them only after bleeding.","It produces only red blood cells in response to low oxygen."],"feedback":["Correct: marrow stem cells develop into precursors of multiple blood cell types that mature and enter circulation.","Marrow is described as a site of blood cell production, not merely storage and release.","Low oxygen can stimulate red cell production, but marrow also produces white cells and platelets."]},{"stem":"Several blood cell lines are reduced, and marrow production is being considered. Which principle best supports further interpretation?","options":["Marrow examination can help assess whether reduced production is contributing to the changes.","Any reduction in blood cells proves marrow failure.","A normal or increased marrow cell production always confirms a specific marrow disorder."],"feedback":["Correct: marrow examination can help distinguish decreased production from other causes and may indicate an underlying disorder.","Reduced blood cell counts can have causes other than impaired marrow production.","Marrow findings provide information, but this statement overstates what a single pattern proves."]}],"title":"Bone marrow and blood cells","unit":"heme","aliases":["bone marrow","myelosuppression","anemia","thrombocytopenia"],"model":"delivery","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia","title":"Neutropenia - Hematology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542208/","title":"Thrombocytopenia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/blood-disorders/biology-of-blood/formation-of-blood-cells","title":"Formation of Blood Cells - Blood Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"}]},{"id":"muscle","definition":"Skeletal muscle produces force for movement and posture. During critical illness, weakness and muscle loss may develop as part of acquired neuromuscular dysfunction.","structure":"Skeletal muscle is the contractile tissue attached to the skeleton. In critical illness, the muscle fibers and their interaction with peripheral nerves may be affected, while immobility can reduce the muscle’s usual activity and loading.","normal":"Normally, skeletal muscle fibers contract to generate movement and help maintain posture. Repeated use supports their size and strength. In critical illness, muscle can be affected by systemic illness and reduced activity, so its capacity to generate force may decline.","change":"With prolonged immobility, muscle disuse is associated with changes in muscle size, length, and contractile strength. Critical illness may add catabolic stress and neuromuscular dysfunction. The resulting weakness can involve muscle atrophy, myopathy, peripheral nerve dysfunction, or a combination.","cues":"A patient may show new difficulty moving or maintaining posture, or generalized weakness during care. Such findings can accompany critical illness and immobility, but weakness alone does not identify its cause; other explanations may need consideration.","distinction":"Weakness in a critically ill patient is not, by itself, proof of muscle disease or ICU-acquired weakness. The syndrome is identified when another cause is not found, and neuromuscular dysfunction may involve nerves as well as muscle.","related":["nerve","cortisol","phosphate"],"questions":[{"stem":"A critically ill patient has been immobile and now has reduced strength. Which mechanism most directly links immobility with muscle loss?","options":["Reduced muscle use is associated with atrophy and declining contractile strength.","Immobility increases myoglobin storage in muscle fibers.","Reduced muscle use directly proves a peripheral nerve injury."],"feedback":["Correct: prolonged disuse is associated with muscle atrophy and reduced strength.","Myoglobin is an oxygen-binding protein in muscle, not the mechanism linking disuse to atrophy.","Weakness after immobility does not establish nerve injury; critical illness weakness can have several causes."]},{"stem":"A patient with critical illness has weakness. Which conclusion best avoids overinterpreting this finding?","options":["Weakness can have muscle, nerve, or other causes and does not identify one mechanism by itself.","Weakness confirms that muscle fibers have been destroyed.","Weakness proves that immobility is the sole cause."],"feedback":["Correct: critical illness weakness may involve myopathy, neuropathy, atrophy, or other causes, so the sign alone is not diagnostic.","Weakness does not by itself demonstrate muscle fiber destruction.","Immobility is a risk factor, but illness severity and other factors may also contribute."]}],"title":"Skeletal muscle and deconditioning","unit":"msk","aliases":["skeletal muscle","deconditioning","muscle weakness","muscle atrophy"],"model":"muscle","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5103015/","title":"ICU-Acquired Weakness"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12987148/","title":"Intensive Care Unit Acquired Weakness as a Modifiable Organ ..."},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13206770/","title":"ICU-Acquired Weakness: An Unsolved Clinical Problem - PMC"}]},{"id":"myoglobin","definition":"Myoglobin is an oxygen-binding protein found in skeletal muscle cells. When muscle cells are damaged, myoglobin can enter the bloodstream and be filtered by the kidneys.","structure":"Myoglobin is located inside skeletal muscle cells, where it binds and stores oxygen. After muscle injury, it can circulate in blood and pass through the kidney’s filtering system into urine; high circulating amounts can harm the kidneys.","normal":"In healthy muscle, myoglobin binds and stores oxygen within muscle cells. Small amounts that enter the blood can be filtered and excreted by the kidneys. When skeletal muscle is damaged, myoglobin is released along with other cell contents.","change":"A large release of myoglobin can exceed the kidney’s ability to handle it and contribute to acute kidney injury. Myoglobin in urine may make it red or brown, and its pigment can cause a urine dipstick to register blood.","cues":"Reddish-brown or tea-colored urine, especially after muscle injury, can be a signal of myoglobin in urine. A dipstick positive for blood may also occur, but neither urine color nor that result alone establishes the cause.","distinction":"Dark urine or a dipstick result positive for blood does not, by itself, diagnose muscle breakdown or kidney injury. Myoglobin is one possible source of the urine finding, and clinical context matters.","related":["muscle","nephron","potassium"],"questions":[{"stem":"After skeletal muscle injury, what explains how myoglobin can contribute to kidney injury?","options":["Released myoglobin enters the blood, is filtered by the kidneys, and in large amounts can harm them.","Myoglobin remains inside muscle cells and blocks kidney filtration directly.","The kidneys release myoglobin into blood when they cannot conserve potassium."],"feedback":["Correct: damaged muscle releases myoglobin, which reaches the kidneys; a large amount can overwhelm and injure them.","Muscle damage allows myoglobin to leave the cells, so it does not remain exclusively inside muscle.","Myoglobin is released from damaged muscle cells, not produced by the kidneys in response to potassium."]},{"stem":"A urine dipstick is positive for blood and the urine looks brown after muscle injury. What is the most careful interpretation?","options":["Myoglobin may be present, but these findings alone do not establish the cause or diagnose kidney injury.","The dipstick proves that intact red blood cells are present in the urine.","Brown urine confirms that the kidneys have failed."],"feedback":["Correct: myoglobin can produce these findings, but they are signals that require context rather than a diagnosis by themselves.","A dipstick positive for blood can reflect myoglobin pigment and does not prove intact red blood cells are present.","Urine color alone cannot confirm kidney failure."]}],"title":"Muscle breakdown and myoglobin","unit":"msk","aliases":["myoglobin","rhabdomyolysis","crush injury"],"model":"muscle","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/rhabdomyolysis","title":"Rhabdomyolysis - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/kidney-disorders/kidney-failure/rhabdomyolysis","title":"Rhabdomyolysis - Kidney Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-28509/","title":"Rhabdomyolysis"}]},{"id":"nephron","definition":"A nephron is the kidney’s microscopic functional unit. It filters blood and then adjusts the filtered fluid as it becomes urine.","structure":"Each kidney contains many nephrons, microscopic functional units. A nephron begins with the glomerulus inside the renal corpuscle, where filtrate enters Bowman space, then continues through the proximal tubule, loop of Henle, and distal tubule; collecting ducts receive fluid from tubules.","normal":"Normally, glomerular capillaries filter water and small solutes into Bowman space while retaining blood cells and most large proteins. Tubules then selectively reabsorb needed water and substances, secrete others, and deliver the remaining fluid toward collecting ducts for urine formation.","change":"When filtration or tubular handling is impaired, the amount and composition of fluid reaching the collecting ducts can change; reduced renal blood flow can also prompt increased sodium and water reabsorption. Thus urine output reflects several nephron processes, not filtration alone.","cues":"Urine output and laboratory assessment can provide clues about kidney function, but a change in output alone does not locate the problem. Alterations may arise from reduced blood flow, intrinsic nephron injury, or obstruction downstream.","distinction":"A nephron is not just the glomerulus: tubular segments modify the filtrate after filtration. Also, low urine output does not by itself prove intrinsic kidney damage, because perfusion and outflow obstruction can produce it.","related":["filtrate","fluid","oliguria"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which sequence best describes the route of fluid through a nephron?","options":["Filtration into Bowman space, followed by tubular modification and delivery toward collecting ducts","Tubular filtration first, followed by blood entering Bowman space for reabsorption","Collection in the bladder, followed by filtration in the glomerulus"],"feedback":["Correct: filtrate enters Bowman space after glomerular filtration, then passes through tubules where it is modified before reaching collecting ducts.","This reverses the sequence: filtration occurs at the glomerulus before fluid travels through the tubules.","The bladder stores urine after it has left the kidneys; it is not where nephron filtration begins."]},{"stem":"Why can urine output change even when the initial glomerular filtration step is not the only process affected?","options":["Tubular reabsorption and secretion also determine how much fluid and which substances remain in the forming urine","Only the bladder controls how much fluid is filtered by the kidneys","The glomerulus produces finished urine without further modification"],"feedback":["Correct: tubules reabsorb and secrete substances, so their handling affects the amount and composition of urine.","The bladder stores and releases urine but does not control glomerular filtration or nephron processing.","The filtrate is modified along the tubules before urine reaches the collecting ducts."]}],"title":"The nephron","unit":"renal","aliases":["nephron","nephrons","renal tubules","glomerulus","glomerular filtration"],"model":"nephron","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/kidney-disorders/diagnosis-of-kidney-disorders/kidneys","title":"Kidney Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://openstax.org/books/pharmacology/pages/33-1-introduction-to-the-renal-system","title":"33.1 Introduction to the Renal System - Pharmacology for Nurses"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/en-ca/home/kidney-disorders/diagnosis-of-kidney-disorders/kidneys?autoredirectid=45564","title":"Kidneys - Kidney Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"}]},{"id":"nerve","definition":"Peripheral nerves carry sensory information toward the central nervous system and motor signals toward muscles. Injury or dysfunction can alter sensation, movement, or both.","structure":"The relevant structures are peripheral nerves outside the brain and spinal cord, along with the sensory and motor pathways they contain. Sensory endings in the skin detect information such as pain and temperature.","normal":"Normally, sensory nerves carry information about pain, temperature, and vibration toward the central nervous system. Motor nerves convey signals that control muscle movement. These pathways allow sensation and voluntary movement to be experienced and expressed.","change":"When a peripheral nerve is injured or dysfunctional, signaling may be disrupted. Sensory changes can include abnormal or lost sensation, while motor pathway dysfunction can cause weakness or paralysis. Compression, trauma, ischemia, and other causes can affect nerve function.","cues":"Numbness, tingling, burning, altered temperature or pain sensation, and weakness can be bedside signals of nerve dysfunction. These findings are not specific to one cause or nerve location, and an isolated symptom does not establish a diagnosis.","distinction":"Weakness does not by itself prove a peripheral nerve problem, because motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, or muscle dysfunction can also affect movement. Abnormal sensation points to sensory pathway involvement but does not identify its cause.","related":["nmj","muscle","pain"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which finding most directly reflects sensory nerve dysfunction?","options":["Reduced ability to feel temperature in an affected area","Difficulty producing movement despite intact sensation","Reduced urine flow"],"feedback":["Correct: sensory nerves carry information about temperature, so altered temperature sensation can reflect sensory pathway dysfunction.","Incorrect: difficulty moving with intact sensation is primarily a motor finding, not a sensory one.","Incorrect: reduced urine flow concerns urinary outflow and does not directly indicate sensory nerve dysfunction."]},{"stem":"A patient reports tingling in one hand. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this cue?","options":["It may reflect altered sensory signaling, but does not establish the cause.","It confirms a specific peripheral nerve injury.","It proves that motor signaling is also impaired."],"feedback":["Correct: tingling is an abnormal sensory experience, but the symptom alone does not identify its cause or extent.","Incorrect: tingling can occur with different forms of nerve dysfunction and does not confirm a specific injury.","Incorrect: a sensory symptom alone does not show that motor pathways are impaired."]}],"title":"Peripheral nerves and sensation","unit":"msk","aliases":["peripheral nerves","paresthesia","peripheral nerve","sensory changes"],"model":"compartment","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/peripheral-nervous-system-and-motor-unit-disorders/overview-of-peripheral-nervous-system-disorders?ruleredirectid=744","title":"Overview of Peripheral Nervous System Disorders - MSD Manuals"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/brain-spinal-cord-and-nerve-disorders/peripheral-nerve-and-related-disorders/overview-of-the-peripheral-nervous-system?ruleredirectid=746","title":"Overview of the Peripheral Nervous System - Brain, Spinal Cord, and Nerve Disorders - MSD Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/peripheral-nervous-system-and-motor-unit-disorders/overview-of-peripheral-nervous-system-disorders?ruleredirectid=749","title":"Overview of Peripheral Nervous System Disorders - MSD Manuals"}]},{"id":"neurons","definition":"Neurons are electrically excitable cells that carry information through electrical signals and communication at connection points called synapses. Their signaling helps link incoming information to other neurons and to body tissues.","structure":"A neuron has a cell body, receiving branches called dendrites, and an axon that carries signals toward its terminal. At a synapse, the terminal communicates with a neighboring cell across a small gap or through a direct electrical connection.","normal":"At rest, ion gradients help maintain the neuron's membrane potential. When input brings the membrane to threshold, voltage-gated sodium channels open and an action potential begins. It travels along the axon; at the terminal, signaling can trigger neurotransmitter release or pass through an electrical connection.","change":"If input does not bring the membrane to threshold, an action potential is not generated. When an action potential does arrive at a chemical synapse, calcium entry into the terminal supports neurotransmitter release. The receiving cell's receptors and integration of incoming signals influence what happens next.","cues":"Changes in movement, sensation, or responsiveness may be bedside clues that neural communication is altered. These findings are nonspecific: they can arise through different pathways, and a single observed change does not identify which neuron or synapse is involved.","distinction":"A neural symptom does not by itself locate a lesion or establish a specific cause. Neurons use both electrical signaling along their processes and communication at synapses.","related":["arousal","nmj","nerve"],"questions":[{"stem":"A signal reaches a neuron's axon terminal at a chemical synapse. What event most directly supports release of neurotransmitter from that terminal?","options":["Calcium enters the presynaptic terminal through voltage-dependent channels.","The postsynaptic cell releases sodium into the synaptic cleft.","The axon terminal passes the signal directly as an electrical current without a transmitter."],"feedback":["Correct: arrival of the action potential opens voltage-dependent calcium channels, and calcium entry supports vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","The postsynaptic cell has receptors, but its sodium release is not the trigger described for presynaptic transmitter release.","Direct electrical transmission can occur at electrical synapses, but this choice does not describe chemical transmitter release."]},{"stem":"A postsynaptic neuron receives several inputs. Which statement best explains why one input may not determine whether it fires?","options":["The postsynaptic neuron integrates incoming signals before determining whether to generate its own action potential.","Every neurotransmitter causes the same response in every postsynaptic cell.","An action potential in the presynaptic neuron always guarantees an action potential in the postsynaptic neuron."],"feedback":["Correct: the receiving neuron integrates its inputs, and the combined signaling influences whether it generates an action potential.","Responses depend on the transmitter and its receptors, so neurotransmitters do not all produce the same effect.","Presynaptic activity can communicate information without guaranteeing that the postsynaptic neuron reaches threshold."]}],"title":"Neurons and neural signaling","unit":"neuro","aliases":["neurons","neuron","neural signaling","action potential"],"model":"synapse","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK546639/","title":"Neuroanatomy, Neuron Action Potential - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK526047/","title":"Physiology, Synapse - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441977/","title":"Neuroanatomy, Neurons - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"neutrophils","definition":"Neutrophils are white blood cells that provide a major defense against bacterial and fungal infections. When their numbers are reduced, infection risk can rise and signs of inflammation may be less apparent.","structure":"Neutrophils are circulating white blood cells and a major part of the body's defense against bacterial and fungal infections. Their presence supports an inflammatory response at sites where infection develops.","normal":"Normally, neutrophils help defend the body against bacterial and fungal infections, and inflammatory findings can accompany a local infection. Their circulating number reflects both production and use, so changes in either can alter this defense and the visibility of infection signs.","change":"When neutrophil numbers are reduced, the risk and severity of bacterial and fungal infections increase, while the inflammatory response may be blunted. With severe reduction, usual focal findings such as redness, swelling, or pain may be muted or absent, even when infection is present.","cues":"Fever may be the only indication of infection when neutrophils are substantially reduced, and local redness, swelling, or pain can be subtle or absent. These bedside patterns warrant attention but do not, by themselves, establish neutropenia or infection.","distinction":"Muted local inflammation does not rule out infection, especially when neutrophil defenses are reduced. Fever alone also does not identify neutropenia or a specific infection.","related":["marrow","inflammation","barrier"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can a local infection be difficult to recognize when neutrophil numbers are reduced?","options":["The inflammatory response may be blunted, making focal signs less apparent.","Neutrophils normally prevent fever from occurring during infection.","Reduced neutrophils make all infection signs more prominent."],"feedback":["Correct: a blunted inflammatory response can mute typical focal findings such as redness, swelling, or pain.","Fever may still occur and can be an important indication of infection.","The evidence describes focal findings as potentially muted, not more prominent."]},{"stem":"A patient has fever but little visible redness or swelling at a suspected infection site. What is the most appropriate interpretation of these cues?","options":["The pattern can occur when inflammatory signs are muted, but it does not establish the cause.","The absence of redness and swelling rules out infection.","Fever with muted local signs proves a reduced neutrophil count."],"feedback":["Correct: fever may be present while focal signs are muted, but these findings alone do not establish a diagnosis.","Focal inflammatory findings can be muted or absent even when infection is present.","The bedside pattern does not by itself prove neutropenia; other clinical information is needed."]}],"title":"Neutrophils and host defense","unit":"heme","aliases":["neutrophils","neutropenia","neutropenic","leukocytes"],"model":"leak","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia","title":"Neutropenia - Hematology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia?autoredirectid=37881","title":"Neutropenia"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia?autoredirectid=37881&qt=agranulocytosis","title":"Neutropenia - Neutropenia - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"nmj","definition":"The neuromuscular junction is the connection where a motor nerve communicates with a muscle. Signaling at this junction enables the muscle to contract.","structure":"The neuromuscular junction is where a motor nerve ending meets the muscle’s postsynaptic membrane. Acetylcholine released from the nerve interacts with muscle receptors; signaling can also be affected by presynaptic release or by acetylcholine breakdown.","normal":"Normally, a motor nerve releases acetylcholine at the junction, where it interacts with receptors on the muscle side and supports contraction. Effective communication depends on coordinated nerve release, postsynaptic receptor function, and appropriate breakdown of acetylcholine within the synapse.","change":"Transmission can be disrupted at different points: acetylcholine release may be reduced, postsynaptic receptors may be affected, or acetylcholine breakdown may be altered. These mechanisms can cause muscle weakness; weakness of respiratory or swallowing muscles can have important functional consequences.","cues":"Fluctuating muscle weakness without sensory deficits can be a bedside signal of impaired neuromuscular transmission. Difficulty swallowing or respiratory muscle weakness may also occur in some disorders, but weakness alone does not identify the junctional mechanism or establish a specific diagnosis.","distinction":"Weakness without sensory changes can point toward a neuromuscular transmission problem, but it does not prove one. Different disorders can affect presynaptic release, postsynaptic receptors, or acetylcholine breakdown.","related":["nerve","muscle","diaphragm"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which event normally enables a motor nerve signal to support muscle contraction at the neuromuscular junction?","options":["Nerve-released acetylcholine interacts with receptors on the muscle side","CSF passes from the fourth ventricle into the subarachnoid space","Arachnoid granulations reabsorb fluid into the dural venous sinuses"],"feedback":["Acetylcholine released by the nerve interacts with postsynaptic muscle receptors to support contraction.","This describes CSF circulation, not signaling between a motor nerve and muscle.","This describes CSF reabsorption, which is separate from neuromuscular transmission."]},{"stem":"Weakness is present without sensory deficits, and the concern is impaired nerve-to-muscle communication. Which set includes possible sites of disruption?","options":["Presynaptic acetylcholine release, postsynaptic receptors, or acetylcholine breakdown","CSF production, ventricular flow, or arachnoid granulation reabsorption","Cerebral hemisphere function, thalamic signaling, or brain-stem arousal"],"feedback":["Neuromuscular transmission can be affected at presynaptic release, postsynaptic receptors, or acetylcholine breakdown.","These are mechanisms of CSF circulation and do not describe the neuromuscular junction.","These structures contribute to alertness, rather than the nerve-to-muscle signaling mechanism."]}],"title":"Neuromuscular junction","unit":"neuro","aliases":["neuromuscular junction","bulbar weakness","neuromuscular transmission"],"model":"synapse","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/peripheral-nervous-system-and-motor-unit-disorders/disorders-of-neuromuscular-transmission","title":"Disorders of Neuromuscular Transmission - Neurology - Merck ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/peripheral-nervous-system-and-motor-unit-disorders/myasthenia-gravis","title":"Myasthenia Gravis - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10132430","title":"Myasthenia gravis, respiratory function, and respiratory tract disease"}]},{"id":"oliguria","definition":"Oliguria means producing less urine than expected over time. It is a bedside finding that can result from reduced kidney blood flow, intrinsic kidney problems, or urinary outflow obstruction.","structure":"Urine output reflects filtration in the kidney glomeruli, subsequent handling along nephron tubules, and drainage through collecting ducts, ureters, bladder, and urethra; reduced output can therefore arise at several points.","normal":"Normally, kidneys filter plasma and tubules reabsorb much of the filtered water and solutes, while the remaining fluid drains as urine. With reduced renal perfusion, an otherwise functioning kidney may conserve sodium and water, lowering urine output; intrinsic injury or obstruction can also reduce it.","change":"When kidney perfusion falls, an otherwise functioning kidney can increase sodium and water reabsorption, leaving less fluid to become urine. Intrinsic kidney injury may impair filtration or tubular function, while urinary obstruction can impede drainage; each mechanism can therefore lower measured output.","cues":"A downward trend in measured urine output can signal reduced renal perfusion, kidney injury, or impaired drainage. Its timing and context help frame possibilities, but output alone cannot distinguish these mechanisms or establish a diagnosis.","distinction":"Do not equate oliguria automatically with intrinsic kidney failure: low perfusion and urinary obstruction are distinct possibilities. Conversely, a seemingly normal blood pressure does not exclude a renal or drainage problem.","related":["nephron","perfusion","bladder"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient’s urine output decreases. Which interpretation best accounts for the possible mechanisms?","options":["Reduced renal blood flow, intrinsic kidney dysfunction, and urinary outflow obstruction are all possible","The finding proves that the glomeruli have sustained intrinsic damage","The finding can only result from inadequate fluid intake"],"feedback":["Correct: reduced output can arise from prerenal, intrinsic renal, or postrenal mechanisms, so context is needed to distinguish them.","Low output alone does not establish intrinsic kidney damage; reduced perfusion or obstruction may also explain it.","Inadequate intake may contribute, but it is not the only possible cause of reduced output."]},{"stem":"Which additional context would help distinguish a drainage problem from reduced kidney perfusion when urine output falls?","options":["Whether there is evidence of urinary outflow obstruction, considered alongside the patient’s perfusion context","Whether the patient’s urine output is low, without considering any other information","Whether oliguria is automatically labeled as intrinsic kidney failure"],"feedback":["Correct: assessing for obstruction and considering perfusion helps compare distinct possible mechanisms.","Low output is the finding itself; alone it does not identify where the problem lies.","Labeling the finding as intrinsic failure assumes a diagnosis that the output alone cannot establish."]}],"title":"Oliguria","unit":"renal","aliases":["oliguria","oliguric","falling urine output","reduced urine output","low urine output"],"model":"nephron","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki","title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Nephrology - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/oliguria?query=oliguria","title":"Oliguria - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/oliguria","title":"Oliguria - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional ..."}]},{"id":"output","title":"Cardiac output","unit":"cardio","model":"pump","aliases":["cardiac output","forward flow"],"definition":"Cardiac output is the amount of blood one ventricle pumps each minute. It equals the blood ejected per beat, called stroke volume, multiplied by heart rate, or beats per minute.","structure":"The heart sits in the chest. Its right and left ventricles are the lower pumping chambers. The right ventricle sends blood into the pulmonary artery toward the lungs; the left sends blood into the aorta toward the body. The atria, or upper chambers, lie above and deliver blood into the ventricles.","normal":"Blood returning to the heart fills the ventricles while they relax. Each contraction then ejects a stroke volume into the pulmonary artery or aorta. Multiplying the amount ejected per beat by the number of beats per minute gives cardiac output. The two ventricles normally pump similar amounts over time, supporting blood flow through the lungs and body.","change":"Changes in filling, pumping force, resistance to ejection, or heart rate can change cardiac output. For example, low circulating blood volume can reduce the blood returning to the heart, lowering ventricular filling and stroke volume. If output falls, less blood may reach body tissues, reducing oxygen delivery and impairing tissue function.","cues":"When cardiac output is inadequate, signs of poor tissue perfusion may include confusion, cool or clammy skin, weak pulses, or reduced urine output. A fast heart rate or low blood pressure may also occur, but these findings have other possible causes. Some people in early shock remain normotensive, so no single sign confirms or rules it out.","distinction":"Low blood pressure alone does not prove that a person is in shock. Normal blood pressure does not guarantee that tissue perfusion is adequate.","related":["stroke-volume","preload","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why might cardiac output fail to rise during a very rapid heart rate?","options":["The shortened filling time may lower stroke volume enough to offset the faster rate.","The faster rate always increases ventricular filling and stroke volume.","The faster rate removes the resistance the ventricle must overcome."],"feedback":["Correct: a very rapid rate can shorten filling time, reducing stroke volume and limiting the rise in output.","Not quite: faster rates shorten diastole, and filling may decrease, especially at very high rates.","Not quite: heart rate does not remove the load the ventricle must overcome to eject blood."]},{"stem":"If blood loss reduces the amount of blood returning to the heart, what is a likely direct effect on cardiac output?","options":["Less ventricular filling can reduce stroke volume and, unless compensated for, cardiac output.","More ventricular filling raises stroke volume and necessarily increases cardiac output.","The heart’s output stays unchanged because heart rate is the only determinant."],"feedback":["Correct: reduced venous return can lower filling and stroke volume, which can lower output unless another response compensates.","Not quite: blood loss generally reduces venous return and filling rather than increasing them.","Not quite: cardiac output depends on both stroke volume and heart rate."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547686/","title":"Physiology, Stroke Volume - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"oxygen-debt","definition":"Tissue-level hypoxia occurs when oxygen delivery is insufficient for the cells’ needs. It can occur even when low blood oxygen is not present.","structure":"The relevant context is the route from the lungs and blood to body tissues: oxygen is carried in blood and delivered by circulation, then moves from capillaries into cells. Tissue oxygen supply depends on both blood oxygen content and effective blood flow.","normal":"Normally, circulation delivers oxygen-containing blood to tissues, and oxygen diffuses from capillaries toward cells. This delivery supports cellular aerobic metabolism when the oxygen supplied meets the tissue’s metabolic demand.","change":"Tissue hypoxia develops when oxygen delivery falls short of demand. The imbalance can arise from low blood oxygen content, reduced blood flow or cardiac output, anemia, or impaired cellular use; low arterial oxygen is one possible cause, not the only one.","cues":"A bedside finding may suggest impaired oxygen delivery, but no single sign establishes tissue hypoxia or its cause. Interpretation considers the patient’s overall condition and whether oxygen supply, circulation, blood oxygen-carrying capacity, or cellular use may be limiting.","distinction":"Hypoxia refers to insufficient oxygen available to tissues relative to their needs; hypoxemia refers to low oxygen in arterial blood. Hypoxemia can cause hypoxia, but the terms are not interchangeable.","related":["perfusion","hemoglobin","oxygenation"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient has adequate arterial oxygenation but markedly reduced tissue blood flow. Which concept best describes the possible tissue problem?","options":["Tissue hypoxia from inadequate oxygen delivery relative to demand.","Hypoxemia, because all tissue oxygen problems mean low arterial oxygen.","Normal oxygen delivery, because arterial oxygenation is adequate."],"feedback":["Correct: reduced flow can limit tissue oxygen delivery even when arterial oxygenation is adequate.","Hypoxemia is low arterial blood oxygen; it is not the only cause of tissue hypoxia.","Adequate arterial oxygenation does not ensure adequate delivery when blood flow is reduced."]},{"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes hypoxemia from tissue hypoxia?","options":["Hypoxemia concerns low oxygen in arterial blood; hypoxia concerns insufficient oxygen for tissue needs.","Hypoxemia means reduced tissue blood flow; hypoxia means low hemoglobin only.","The terms are interchangeable whenever a patient has respiratory symptoms."],"feedback":["Correct: one describes arterial blood oxygen, while the other describes oxygen inadequacy at the tissue level.","Reduced flow and low hemoglobin can contribute to tissue hypoxia, but these descriptions do not define the two terms.","Respiratory symptoms alone do not make the terms equivalent or establish either condition."]}],"title":"Hypoxia at the tissue level","unit":"multi","aliases":["tissue hypoxia","hypoxia","hypoxic"],"model":"delivery","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493219/","title":"Partial Pressure of Oxygen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482316/?hl=en-US","title":"Hypoxia and Hypoxemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK493219/","title":"Partial Pressure of Oxygen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"oxygenation","definition":"Oxygenation describes oxygen entering arterial blood from the lungs. Hypoxemia means low oxygen in arterial blood, whereas tissue hypoxia means oxygen delivery is insufficient for tissue needs.","structure":"Oxygen moves from alveolar gas across the thin alveolar-capillary interface into pulmonary capillary blood, then travels in arterial blood. Most arterial oxygen is bound to hemoglobin; a smaller amount remains dissolved, and its partial pressure reflects dissolved oxygen tension.","normal":"Normally, oxygen diffuses from alveoli into pulmonary capillary blood and is carried mainly bound to hemoglobin. Arterial oxygen partial pressure reflects dissolved oxygen and helps drive hemoglobin saturation, while arterial oxygen content depends substantially on hemoglobin concentration and saturation.","change":"When oxygen transfer into arterial blood is impaired, arterial oxygen partial pressure or saturation may fall, producing hypoxemia. Tissue oxygen availability can also be inadequate despite an apparently normal saturation, such as when hemoglobin is very low or oxygen delivery or extraction is impaired.","cues":"A low pulse-oximeter reading may signal reduced arterial hemoglobin saturation, while arterial blood gas measurement assesses arterial oxygen partial pressure. Neither a reading nor a single observed sign identifies the cause, and saturation alone does not measure ventilation, tissue delivery, or cellular oxygen use.","distinction":"Hypoxemia is not the same as tissue hypoxia: one concerns oxygen in arterial blood, while the other concerns whether tissues receive enough oxygen for their needs. A normal saturation alone does not rule out tissue hypoxia.","related":["alveoli","hemoglobin","oxygen-debt"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes oxygen saturation from oxygen delivery to tissues?","options":["Saturation reflects the proportion of hemoglobin binding sites occupied by oxygen, but does not measure total tissue delivery.","Saturation directly measures how much oxygen cells consume.","Saturation measures ventilation and carbon dioxide removal."],"feedback":["Correct: saturation describes hemoglobin oxygen occupancy, not the full amount of oxygen transported to or used by tissues.","Cellular oxygen consumption is a distinct process and cannot be directly inferred from saturation alone.","Ventilation and carbon dioxide removal are not measured by oxygen saturation."]},{"stem":"A patient has a normal oxygen saturation but severe anemia. Which inference is most appropriate?","options":["Tissue oxygen delivery may still be inadequate because saturation does not show how much hemoglobin is available.","Normal saturation proves that oxygen delivery to tissues is adequate.","Anemia must cause a low arterial oxygen partial pressure."],"feedback":["Correct: saturation may be normal even when low hemoglobin limits arterial oxygen content and potential delivery.","Saturation alone cannot establish adequate delivery; hemoglobin concentration and circulation also matter.","Anemia can reduce oxygen content without necessarily lowering dissolved oxygen partial pressure."]}],"title":"Oxygenation and hypoxemia","unit":"resp","aliases":["hypoxemia","hypoxaemia","oxygenation","oxygen saturation"],"model":"alveolar","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493219/","title":"Partial Pressure of Oxygen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK525974/","title":"Oxygen Saturation: Monitoring and Interpretation - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482316/?hl=en-US","title":"Hypoxia and Hypoxemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"pain","definition":"Pain is a conscious sensory and emotional experience shaped by the brain. Nociception is the nervous system’s processing of potentially harmful stimuli and can occur without conscious pain.","structure":"Nociceptors in peripheral tissues detect noxious stimuli and send signals through sensory fibers to the spinal cord. Signals travel onward to the brain, where processing involving the thalamus and cortical regions contributes to conscious perception.","normal":"Normally, a noxious stimulus activates a nociceptor, and the signal travels through sensory fibers to the spinal cord and then to the brain. Modulation can increase or diminish signal transmission; brain processing contributes to the conscious, sensory, and emotional experience of pain.","change":"When tissue is exposed to a noxious stimulus, nociceptors can transduce it into a neural signal. The signal may be transmitted and modulated along the pathway, but nociceptive activity is not identical to conscious pain, which depends on higher brain processing.","cues":"A patient’s report of pain is an important bedside signal and should be respected; verbal description is not the only way to communicate it. Observed behavior or a stimulus alone cannot fully establish another person’s conscious pain experience.","distinction":"Nociception is neural processing of noxious information; pain is the conscious experience produced by higher brain processing. Nociception can occur without pain, so a signal or stimulus alone does not establish conscious experience.","related":["neurons","cord","nerve"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which statement best distinguishes nociception from pain?","options":["Nociception is nervous-system processing of noxious information, while pain is the conscious experience.","Nociception and pain are interchangeable terms for a tissue injury.","Pain is a peripheral signal that occurs before nociception."],"feedback":["Correct: nociception describes neural processing, whereas pain is the sensory and emotional experience shaped by higher brain centers.","Nociception and pain are related but distinct, and neither term simply means tissue injury.","Pain is the conscious experience arising from processing, not a peripheral signal that precedes nociception."]},{"stem":"A patient cannot give a verbal description, but the nurse needs to consider whether pain is present. Which principle is most appropriate?","options":["Verbal description is only one way to communicate pain, so the patient’s experience should not be dismissed for lack of words.","Without a verbal report, pain is impossible because nociception requires speech.","A noxious stimulus proves that the patient consciously experiences pain."],"feedback":["Correct: pain can be communicated in ways other than verbal description, and an individual’s report should be respected.","Nociception is neural processing and does not require speech; pain communication is not limited to words.","A noxious stimulus can activate nociception, but that alone does not prove conscious pain."]}],"title":"Pain pathways and perception","unit":"psych","aliases":["nociception","pain perception","pain pathways","pain"],"model":"synapse","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/pain/overview-of-pain","title":"Overview of Pain - Neurology"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK32659/","title":"Mechanisms of Pain - Recognition and Alleviation of Pain in ..."},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539789/","title":"Physiology, Pain - StatPearls"}]},{"id":"pancreas","definition":"The exocrine pancreas makes digestive enzymes that normally help break down food in the intestine. In acute pancreatitis, enzymes become active too early and injure pancreatic tissue.","structure":"The pancreas lies behind the stomach. Groups of acinar cells release digestive secretions into small ducts. These ducts join and carry pancreatic juice to the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine.","normal":"The pancreas releases protein-digesting enzymes as inactive precursors. In the duodenum, trypsinogen becomes trypsin, which activates other precursors. Delaying activation helps protect the pancreas from digesting its own proteins. Not every pancreatic enzyme follows this rule: amylase and lipase are secreted in active forms.","change":"In acute pancreatitis, enzymes can activate prematurely within acinar cells. The active enzymes injure pancreatic tissue and trigger inflammatory signaling, which can cause edema and sometimes necrosis. Enzymes and inflammatory mediators entering the peritoneal cavity may cause chemical injury and fluid third spacing; systemic effects can include increased capillary permeability and reduced vascular tone.","cues":"Abdominal illness may be accompanied by signs of inflammation, fluid third spacing, or systemic effects, but any one bedside cue is nonspecific and does not establish pancreatitis. The mechanism can extend beyond the gland, so assess signals in context rather than assuming local symptoms reflect the full process.","distinction":"Pancreatitis is not simply inflammation caused by enzymes arriving in the intestine; the key mechanism is premature enzyme activation within the pancreas. Systemic effects may occur, but they do not by themselves identify the pancreas as their source.","related":["inflammation","gut","fluid"],"questions":[{"stem":"What normally limits pancreatic autodigestion?","options":["Protein-digesting enzymes are released as inactive precursors and activated after reaching the duodenum.","Digestive enzymes are fully activated inside acinar cells before entering the ducts.","The pancreas normally prevents digestive enzymes from reaching the intestine."],"feedback":["Correct: delayed activation of pancreatic proteases helps keep protein digestion in the intestine rather than in the gland.","Activation within acinar cells is the abnormal early event that can initiate pancreatic injury.","Pancreatic enzymes normally contribute to digestion in the intestine; preventing their arrival there is not normal function."]},{"stem":"Which sequence best explains how acute pancreatitis can affect the circulation?","options":["Premature enzyme activation injures tissue, inflammatory mediators are released, and increased capillary permeability can contribute to fluid shifts and reduced vascular tone.","Enzyme activation in the duodenum directly raises portal pressure and forms esophageal varices.","Pancreatic enzymes remain inactive, so reduced vascular tone occurs without tissue injury or inflammation."],"feedback":["Correct: Pancreatic injury can activate inflammatory pathways, whose systemic effects include increased capillary permeability and decreased vascular tone.","This describes features associated with portal hypertension, not the cited systemic pathway from acute pancreatitis.","Premature enzyme activation and tissue injury are central to the inflammatory cascade described for pancreatitis."]}],"title":"Exocrine pancreas","unit":"gi","aliases":["pancreatic enzymes","pancreas","pancreatitis"],"model":"leak","sources":[{"title":"The Exocrine Pancreas: digestive enzymes and zymogen activation","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK54127/"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/pancreatitis/acute-pancreatitis?qt=Vomiting","title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/pancreatitis/acute-pancreatitis?ruleredirectid=743","title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Gastrointestinal Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482468/","title":"Acute Pancreatitis - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"peep","definition":"Positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) is pressure maintained in the lungs at the end of exhalation. It can keep alveoli open, but it also changes pressure around the heart and great veins.","structure":"PEEP acts on the airways and alveoli, while its pressure can also be transmitted through the chest to the right atrium and nearby veins. Its effects therefore involve both lung expansion and blood return to the heart.","normal":"During unassisted breathing, inhalation creates negative pressure in the chest that draws air into the lungs. This pressure also helps lower right atrial pressure, supporting venous return and filling of the heart before it pumps.","change":"With PEEP, positive pressure is maintained in the lungs and can raise pressure within the chest. This may keep alveoli open and improve ventilation-perfusion matching, while also raising right atrial pressure, reducing venous return and preload, and potentially lowering cardiac output.","cues":"A change in oxygenation may reflect improved alveolar recruitment, while falling blood pressure or cardiac output may reflect reduced venous return. These signals are not specific to PEEP; their meaning depends on the patient’s overall respiratory and circulatory picture.","distinction":"Improved oxygenation does not by itself show that PEEP has improved circulation. Its net effect depends on the balance between alveolar recruitment, overdistension, and the pressure-related effects on venous return.","related":["alveoli","vq","preload"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient’s oxygenation improves after positive pressure is applied, while venous return may fall. Which mechanism best accounts for both effects?","options":["Alveolar recruitment can improve gas exchange while increased chest pressure reduces venous return.","Positive pressure increases surfactant production, which directly raises cardiac output.","Improved oxygenation means pulmonary vascular resistance and right atrial pressure must both decrease."],"feedback":["Correct. PEEP can keep alveoli open and improve ventilation-perfusion matching, while increased intrathoracic pressure can raise right atrial pressure and reduce venous return.","Surfactant helps stabilize alveoli, but the provided evidence does not establish increased surfactant production as the explanation for these effects.","Oxygenation improvement does not establish that pulmonary vascular resistance or right atrial pressure decreased; PEEP can raise right atrial pressure and may affect pulmonary vascular resistance."]},{"stem":"Why might cardiac output decrease when PEEP is increased, even if lung expansion improves?","options":["Higher intrathoracic pressure can reduce venous return and ventricular preload.","PEEP directly prevents the heart from contracting by lowering alveolar surface tension.","Improved alveolar recruitment necessarily reduces blood flow through the right heart."],"feedback":["Correct. Increased intrathoracic and right atrial pressure can reduce the pressure gradient for venous return, lowering preload and potentially cardiac output.","The evidence describes pressure-related effects on venous return and possible overdistension, not direct prevention of heart contraction through surface tension.","Alveolar recruitment can improve lung function, but it does not necessarily reduce right-heart blood flow; the pressure effect on venous return is the relevant mechanism."]}],"title":"PEEP and alveolar recruitment","unit":"resp","aliases":["positive end-expiratory pressure","PEEP","alveolar recruitment"],"model":"alveolar","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-27458/","title":"Positive End-Expiratory Pressure"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441904/","title":"Positive End-Expiratory Pressure - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK441904/?report=printable","title":"Positive End-Expiratory Pressure"}]},{"id":"perfusion","title":"Tissue perfusion","unit":"cardio","model":"perfusion","aliases":["tissue perfusion","organ perfusion","perfusion"],"definition":"Tissue perfusion is the movement of blood through tiny vessels in an organ or tissue. It brings oxygen and other needed materials to cells and carries some waste away.","structure":"Tissue perfusion takes place in the microcirculation, the network of small vessels within tissues. Blood flows from small arteries called arterioles through capillaries, where the vessel walls lie close to surrounding cells and the interstitial space between them. It then leaves through small veins called venules.","normal":"The heart pumps blood into arteries, which carry it toward tissues. Blood enters capillaries, where oxygen and other materials move from the blood toward nearby cells. Cells use oxygen to support their functions. Blood then collects some waste and flows into venules and veins, returning toward the heart.","change":"When circulation fails to deliver enough oxygen to tissues, cells may not get what they need for normal function. They can shift toward making energy without oxygen, which increases lactate. If inadequate delivery continues, cellular damage may develop and organs such as the kidneys, lungs, or heart may function poorly.","cues":"Poor tissue perfusion may be accompanied by confusion, cool or clammy skin, delayed capillary refill, or reduced urine output. These signs reflect possible effects on the brain, skin circulation, or kidneys, but none proves poor perfusion alone. In distributive shock, skin can instead be warm and capillary refill may be normal.","distinction":"A normal blood pressure does not rule out poor tissue perfusion. Low blood pressure alone does not establish shock.","related":["circulation","exchange","oxygen-debt"],"questions":[{"stem":"A person has a normal blood pressure, but there is concern that cells are not receiving enough oxygen. Which idea best explains why these can occur together?","options":["Blood pressure can be normal even when tissue oxygen delivery is inadequate.","Normal blood pressure means capillary exchange must be adequate.","Inadequate tissue oxygen delivery always causes low blood pressure first."],"feedback":["Correct. Blood pressure is one measure of circulation, but it does not by itself show whether tissues are receiving or using enough oxygen.","Not necessarily. Normal blood pressure does not guarantee adequate exchange or oxygen delivery at the cellular level.","Not always. Some people with shock have normal blood pressure, especially early in its course or in distributive states."]},{"stem":"Which observation can fit distributive shock even though it differs from the cool, clammy skin often seen in other forms of shock?","options":["Warm or flushed skin with capillary refill that may be normal.","Cool skin with delayed capillary refill in every case.","Reduced urine output as the only possible sign."],"feedback":["Correct. Vasodilation can make the skin warm or flushed, and capillary refill may remain normal in distributive shock.","This can occur in hypovolemic, cardiogenic, or obstructive shock, but it is not the only possible skin pattern.","Reduced urine output can be a sign of hypoperfusion, but it is not the only possible sign and does not identify a shock type by itself."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?query=shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"pericardium","title":"Pericardium and tamponade","unit":"cardio","model":"tamponade","aliases":["pericardium","pericardial effusion","tamponade"],"definition":"The pericardium is a two-layered sac around the heart. Cardiac tamponade happens when pressure from fluid in this sac keeps the heart from filling normally.","structure":"The pericardium surrounds the heart and the first portions of the large vessels leaving it. Its inner, visceral layer lies on the heart muscle; its outer, parietal layer forms the tougher enclosing wall. A narrow space between the layers normally contains a small amount of lubricating fluid.","normal":"With each heartbeat, the heart chambers fill with blood and then contract to send it onward. A thin layer of fluid lets the heart's outer surface move with little friction. The enclosing sac also limits sudden overexpansion and helps coordinate how neighboring chambers interact as they fill and pump.","change":"When fluid, especially blood, collects in the sac, pressure can rise and squeeze the heart. Rapid buildup is more dangerous because the sac cannot stretch quickly; slower buildup may be accommodated as it stretches. Squeezing limits chamber filling, reducing the blood pumped with each beat and potentially lowering blood pressure and tissue blood flow.","cues":"Reduced filling can cause low blood pressure, quiet or muffled heart sounds, and enlarged neck veins, but this combination is not always present. A breathing-related fall in systolic pressure, called pulsus paradoxus, may also occur. These findings are not specific on their own; bedside ultrasound can help assess suspected tamponade.","distinction":"A pericardial effusion is fluid collection, and it does not by itself establish impaired heart filling. Beck's triad—the combination of low blood pressure, muffled heart sounds, and distended neck veins—may be incomplete or absent in tamponade.","related":["preload","output","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can a smaller, rapidly developing fluid collection impair the heart more than a larger, slowly developing one?","options":["The sac has less time to stretch around a rapidly growing collection.","Slowly developing fluid always leaves the heart chambers unaffected.","Rapid fluid buildup increases the heart's ability to fill between beats."],"feedback":["Correct: the sac cannot stretch quickly, so pressure can rise and restrict filling even with a smaller amount.","Slow accumulation may be accommodated as the sac stretches, but it does not guarantee that filling is unaffected.","Rapid buildup can restrict filling; it does not improve the heart's filling ability."]},{"stem":"A person has low blood pressure and distended neck veins. What is the most accurate interpretation?","options":["These findings can occur with tamponade, but they do not prove it by themselves.","Together, these findings confirm tamponade even without other evidence.","These findings rule out tamponade if heart sounds are not muffled."],"feedback":["Correct: the findings may raise concern, but signs can be incomplete and have other possible causes.","The signs alone do not confirm tamponade; the classic combination can be difficult to recognize or absent.","Muffled heart sounds may be hard to detect, and the full triad is not always present."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/cardiac-tamponade","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/myocarditis-and-pericarditis/pericarditis","title":"Pericarditis - Cardiology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"peritoneum","definition":"The peritoneum lines the abdominal cavity and surrounds many abdominal organs. When pressure within the abdomen rises, it can interfere with organ perfusion and affect systems beyond the abdomen.","structure":"The peritoneal cavity is the abdominal space containing the viscera. Pressure in this relatively confined space can affect abdominal vessels and organs; when it rises, it can also push the diaphragm upward, alter chest pressure, and influence venous return to the heart.","normal":"At baseline, abdominal organs and vessels function without the compressive effects of markedly elevated intra-abdominal pressure. Venous return supports cardiac filling, while blood flow supplies abdominal organs. The diaphragm can move with less pressure-related restriction, supporting lung expansion and ventilation.","change":"As intra-abdominal pressure rises, abdominal vessels and organs may be compressed and organ perfusion can fall. Pressure can also elevate the diaphragm and increase intrathoracic pressure, impairing venous return and cardiac output. Reduced renal blood flow may contribute to decreased urine output, while gut and liver perfusion may also be impaired.","cues":"Signals may include reduced urine output, respiratory restriction, or impaired abdominal-organ function, but each can have other explanations and none alone diagnoses a pressure-related syndrome. Consider whether several organ systems show changes consistent with elevated abdominal pressure, in the full clinical context.","distinction":"A single change such as reduced urine output does not prove elevated intra-abdominal pressure or abdominal compartment syndrome. The cited syndrome involves elevated pressure associated with organ dysfunction, not pressure or an isolated sign considered alone.","related":["fluid","preload","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"How can rising intra-abdominal pressure reduce cardiac output?","options":["It can compress the inferior vena cava, impair venous return, and reduce cardiac filling.","It increases venous return by opening a direct pathway from the portal vein to the heart.","It increases cardiac output by lowering pressure around abdominal organs."],"feedback":["Correct: Compression of the inferior vena cava can reduce venous return, contributing to lower cardiac output.","The evidence describes impaired, not enhanced, venous return; the portal vein does not provide this direct route to the heart.","Rising abdominal pressure can impair venous return and organ perfusion rather than reliably increasing cardiac output."]},{"stem":"Which pattern is most consistent with the possible effects of elevated intra-abdominal pressure?","options":["Reduced urine output together with respiratory restriction and impaired abdominal-organ perfusion.","An isolated change in urine output that necessarily proves abdominal compartment syndrome.","Improved lung expansion and increased renal blood flow as abdominal pressure rises."],"feedback":["Correct: Elevated pressure can affect renal blood flow, diaphragm movement, and perfusion of abdominal organs.","Reduced urine output has multiple possible causes; by itself it does not establish a pressure-related syndrome.","The described effects include restricted respiration and reduced renal blood flow, not improvement in both."]}],"title":"Peritoneum and abdominal pressure","unit":"gi","aliases":["peritoneum","peritonitis","intra-abdominal pressure"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC137242/","title":"Abdominal compartment syndrome"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3925290/","title":"Management of intra-abdominal hypertension and abdominal ..."},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC137249/","title":"Abdominal compartment syndrome"}]},{"id":"phosphate","definition":"Phosphate is a mineral used by cells to make ATP, a key form of stored energy. When phosphate is severely depleted, muscle, heart, and respiratory function can be affected.","structure":"Phosphate is present in body fluids and within cells, where it contributes to ATP and other cellular processes. Its availability also supports production of 2,3-DPG in red blood cells, which influences how readily hemoglobin releases oxygen to tissues.","normal":"With adequate phosphate, cells can make ATP to support energy-dependent work, including muscle contraction. Phosphate also supports 2,3-DPG production, which helps hemoglobin release oxygen to tissues. These functions connect phosphate availability with cellular energy use and oxygen delivery.","change":"When phosphate stores become depleted, ATP production can fall and muscle function may weaken, including respiratory muscle function. Reduced 2,3-DPG can increase hemoglobin’s oxygen affinity, limiting oxygen release to tissues. Cardiac contractility and conduction may also be affected, so several organ systems can be involved.","cues":"Weakness, reduced respiratory muscle function, or cardiac rhythm and contractility changes may be seen with profound phosphate depletion. These findings are not specific to phosphate deficiency, and their presence alone cannot establish the cause; interpretation depends on the broader clinical picture and laboratory assessment.","distinction":"Muscle weakness or respiratory difficulty does not by itself diagnose phosphate depletion. The mechanism is a measured phosphate deficiency that can affect energy production and oxygen release, not a single symptom.","related":["oxygen-debt","muscle","conduction"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient with prolonged undernutrition develops weakness after nutrition is restarted. Which mechanism best links low phosphate to impaired muscle function?","options":["Reduced phosphate can limit ATP production needed for cellular work.","Low phosphate directly blocks oxygen from entering the lungs.","Low phosphate increases venous return and therefore overloads skeletal muscle."],"feedback":["Correct: phosphate is required for ATP production, and reduced energy availability can impair muscle function.","Lung oxygen entry is not the mechanism described; phosphate depletion can affect energy use and oxygen release to tissues.","The supplied mechanism connects low phosphate with impaired energy production, not increased venous return."]},{"stem":"Why can reduced phosphate availability affect oxygen delivery even when hemoglobin is present?","options":["Lower 2,3-DPG can increase hemoglobin’s oxygen affinity and reduce oxygen release to tissues.","Lower phosphate causes hemoglobin to disappear from the circulation.","Lower phosphate makes the alveoli stop ventilating."],"feedback":["Correct: reduced 2,3-DPG shifts hemoglobin toward holding oxygen more tightly, so less may be released to tissues.","The evidence describes altered oxygen binding, not loss of hemoglobin.","Alveolar ventilation is not the mechanism given for phosphate-related changes in oxygen delivery."]}],"title":"Phosphate and cellular energy","unit":"endo","aliases":["phosphate","hypophosphatemia","phosphorus"],"model":"muscle","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/electrolyte-disorders/hypophosphatemia","title":"Hypophosphatemia - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564513/","title":"Refeeding Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2738315/","title":"Refeeding syndrome - PMC - National Center for Biotechnology ..."}]},{"id":"placenta","definition":"The placenta is the interface where maternal and fetal blood support exchange, including oxygen transfer. Placental separation can disrupt that exchange.","structure":"The placenta attaches to the uterine wall and contains closely positioned maternal and fetal circulations. Their blood normally remains separate while oxygen and other substances cross the exchange surface; separation from the uterine wall can compromise fetal oxygen exchange.","normal":"Normally, maternal blood reaches the placental exchange surface, where oxygen can pass to fetal blood without the two blood supplies mixing. This supports oxygen delivery to the fetus while the placenta remains attached to the uterine wall.","change":"With placental abruption, the placenta separates partly or completely from the uterine wall. The disrupted contact compromises fetal oxygen exchange, while bleeding arises from the maternal circulation and may cause maternal blood loss and hemodynamic instability.","cues":"Maternal bleeding or signs of circulatory instability and a nonreassuring fetal heart rate pattern can raise concern for abruption and its effects. These findings are not individually diagnostic; assessment considers the overall clinical picture and fetal monitoring.","distinction":"Maternal bleeding does not mean the blood is coming from the fetus. A nonreassuring fetal heart rate can indicate compromise but does not, by itself, establish placental abruption.","related":["exchange","oxygenation","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which description best explains why placental separation can affect the fetus even when the bleeding source is maternal?","options":["Separation disrupts the placental interface needed for fetal oxygen exchange.","Maternal blood normally mixes directly with fetal blood in the placenta.","The fetal heart rate pattern identifies the source of maternal bleeding."],"feedback":["Correct: separation compromises the exchange surface through which oxygen reaches fetal blood.","Maternal and fetal blood normally remain separate; direct mixing is not the usual exchange mechanism.","Fetal monitoring can show compromise, but it does not identify the source of bleeding."]},{"stem":"A finding raises concern for fetal compromise but does not independently prove abruption. Which finding fits this description?","options":["A nonreassuring fetal heart rate pattern.","Maternal blood loss as the source of bleeding.","Placental attachment to the uterine wall."],"feedback":["Correct: a nonreassuring pattern may indicate fetal compromise, but it is not by itself diagnostic of abruption.","Maternal bleeding is described as the source of blood loss in abruption, not as a fetal monitoring finding.","Attachment is part of the normal placental relationship, not a sign of fetal compromise."]}],"title":"Placental circulation","unit":"multi","aliases":["placental abruption","placenta","uteroplacental perfusion"],"model":"placenta","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae","title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - Gynecology and ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/en-ca/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/abnormalities-of-pregnancy/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae","title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - Gynecology and Obstetrics - Merck Manuals Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae?ruleredirectid=747autoredirectid=23514","title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - The Merck Manuals"}]},{"id":"platelets","definition":"Platelets are blood components that help stop bleeding when a vessel is injured. They form an initial plug and work with fibrin to strengthen it.","structure":"Platelets circulate in the blood and interact with a damaged vessel lining. At the injury site, they adhere and aggregate, and their surface supports coagulation reactions that generate thrombin and fibrin.","normal":"Normally, vessel injury prompts platelets to adhere to the damaged area and gather into a plug. Coagulation reactions generate thrombin, which converts fibrinogen into fibrin; fibrin strands bind the platelet aggregate and strengthen the seal to help limit bleeding.","change":"When platelet adhesion or aggregation is impaired, the initial plug may be less effective. Since platelets also support coagulation reactions, disruption can affect fibrin formation as well; however, hemostasis involves vascular factors and plasma coagulation factors in addition to platelets.","cues":"Bleeding from an injured site or easy bruising can be a bedside signal that hemostasis is not working effectively, but neither finding identifies the cause. Vessel factors, platelets, and plasma coagulation factors all contribute, so assessment requires broader context.","distinction":"Bleeding or bruising alone does not establish a platelet problem. Hemostasis depends on vessel factors, platelets, and plasma coagulation factors working together.","related":["marrow","inflammation","cells"],"questions":[{"stem":"After a vessel is injured, what is the main contribution of platelets to the early hemostatic response?","options":["They adhere and aggregate at the injury to form a plug.","They convert fibrinogen directly into fibrin strands.","They constrict the vessel as the sole hemostatic response."],"feedback":["Correct: platelet adhesion and aggregation create the initial plug at the damaged site.","Fibrinogen is converted to fibrin by thrombin, not directly by platelets.","Vessel constriction contributes to hemostasis, but it is not the sole platelet function."]},{"stem":"What best explains how fibrin strengthens a platelet plug?","options":["Fibrin strands bind the platelet aggregate and help form a stronger clot.","Fibrin replaces the need for platelets at the injury site.","Fibrin prevents coagulation reactions from occurring on platelet surfaces."],"feedback":["Correct: fibrin binds aggregated platelets into a stronger hemostatic plug.","Fibrin works with the platelet aggregate rather than replacing platelets.","Platelets provide surfaces for coagulation reactions; fibrin is a product that strengthens the clot."]}],"title":"Platelets and the clotting cascade","unit":"heme","aliases":["platelets","platelet count","fibrin","coagulation cascade","hemostasis"],"model":"clot","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/hematology/hemostasis/overview-of-hemostasis","title":"Overview of Hemostasis - Hematology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/blood-disorders/blood-clotting-process/how-blood-clots","title":"How Blood Clots - Blood Disorders - Merck Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK545263/","title":"Physiology, Hemostasis - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"pleura","definition":"The pleural space is the thin space between the lung and chest wall. Air entering this space can reduce the pressure that helps keep the lung expanded.","structure":"The pleural space lies between the lung surface and the inner chest wall. Its pressure reflects the opposing inward recoil of the lung and outward recoil of the chest wall, supporting lung expansion during breathing.","normal":"Normally, intrapleural pressure is below atmospheric pressure because the lung recoils inward while the chest wall recoils outward. This pressure relationship helps maintain lung volume as the chest moves during breathing.","change":"When air enters the pleural space, intrapleural pressure rises and lung volume decreases, potentially causing partial or complete collapse. In tension pneumothorax, pressure rises progressively and can shift the mediastinum and impair venous return.","cues":"A pneumothorax may be associated with partial or complete lung collapse. If pressure progressively rises, impaired venous return can contribute to systemic hypotension; neither hypotension nor respiratory distress alone identifies the cause.","distinction":"A pneumothorax is air in the pleural space, not simply low oxygen or breathlessness. Systemic hypotension can occur in tension pneumothorax when venous return is impaired, but hypotension alone does not establish that diagnosis.","related":["ventilation","diaphragm","peep"],"questions":[{"stem":"What normally creates the below-atmospheric intrapleural pressure that supports lung expansion?","options":["Opposing inward lung recoil and outward chest-wall recoil","Equal inward recoil of the lung and chest wall","Air entering the pleural space and raising its pressure"],"feedback":["Correct: the lung and chest wall recoil in opposite directions, creating normally negative intrapleural pressure.","If the recoils were equal and directed inward, they would not create the described opposing forces.","Air entering the pleural space raises intrapleural pressure and reduces lung volume rather than creating the normal relationship."]},{"stem":"In tension pneumothorax, which mechanism can contribute to systemic hypotension?","options":["Rising pleural pressure impairs venous return to the heart.","Fluid accumulation in alveoli increases pulmonary gas exchange.","Outward chest-wall recoil increases blood return to the heart."],"feedback":["Correct: progressive pleural pressure can shift the mediastinum and impede venous return, reducing systemic circulation.","Alveolar fluid impairs gas exchange; it does not describe the venous-return mechanism of tension pneumothorax.","Outward chest-wall recoil is part of normal mechanics and does not explain pressure-related impairment of venous return."]}],"title":"Pleural space and lung recoil","unit":"resp","aliases":["pleural space","pleura","pneumothorax","pleural pressure"],"model":"pleura","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pneumothorax","title":"Pneumothorax - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pneumothorax?qt=Pneumothorax","title":"Pneumothorax - Pulmonary Disorders - Merck Manuals ..."},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pneumothorax?ruleredirectid=741","title":"Pneumothorax - Pulmonary Disorders - MSD Manual Professional ..."}]},{"id":"potassium","definition":"Potassium helps maintain electrical conditions across cell membranes. Those conditions support signaling in nerves and the activity of skeletal, smooth, and cardiac muscle.","structure":"The relevant structures are cell membranes in excitable tissues, including neurons and skeletal and cardiac muscle. Potassium movement across these membranes helps establish resting electrical potential, which shapes how cells respond to stimulation.","normal":"Normally, potassium distribution across cell membranes helps maintain resting membrane potential. Excitable cells can then respond to a stimulus with an action potential; in muscle, that electrical event supports contraction, while cardiac electrical activity depends on coordinated membrane changes.","change":"When blood potassium is low, the concentration gradient favors potassium leaving cells, making their resting potential more negative. A stronger stimulus may then be needed to trigger an action potential. In cardiac muscle, low potassium can also delay repolarization and predispose to rhythm disturbances.","cues":"Weakness, impaired muscle contraction, cramps, or a cardiac rhythm disturbance may be compatible with altered potassium-related excitability. These signals are not specific to potassium levels, so an isolated symptom does not establish an electrolyte cause.","distinction":"A muscle symptom or rhythm disturbance alone does not diagnose low potassium. Potassium abnormalities can affect several excitable tissues, and the observed effect depends on the degree and context of the change.","related":["neurons","conduction","muscle"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can low potassium make an excitable cell harder to activate?","options":["It can make the resting membrane potential more negative, requiring a greater stimulus.","It removes the need for an action potential in muscle.","It makes every cell membrane equally permeable to all ions."],"feedback":["A more negative resting potential means a stronger stimulus may be needed to reach the conditions for an action potential.","Muscle contraction relies on electrical activation; low potassium does not remove the need for it.","The mechanism described concerns potassium gradients and membrane potential, not equal permeability to all ions."]},{"stem":"Which cardiac electrical effect is associated with low potassium in the provided evidence?","options":["Delayed ventricular repolarization that can predispose to arrhythmias.","Permanent prevention of cardiac electrical activity.","Faster repolarization that rules out rhythm disturbance."],"feedback":["Low potassium can delay ventricular repolarization and contribute to conditions that favor arrhythmias.","The evidence describes altered electrical activity and arrhythmia risk, not permanent cessation of cardiac activity.","The described effect is delayed rather than faster repolarization, and it does not rule out rhythm disturbance."]}],"title":"Potassium and electrical excitability","unit":"renal","aliases":["potassium","hyperkalemia","hypokalemia"],"model":"conduction","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK482465/","title":"Hypokalemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538338/","title":"Physiology, Resting Potential - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482465/?report=printable","title":"Hypokalemia"}]},{"id":"preload","title":"Preload and venous return","unit":"cardio","model":"pump","aliases":["preload","venous return","filling pressure"],"definition":"Preload is the stretch of a heart chamber just before it contracts, linked to how much blood fills it. Venous return is blood flowing through veins back to the heart.","structure":"The right atrium, the upper right chamber of the heart, receives blood returning from the body through two large veins called the venae cavae. It sits beside the right ventricle, which sends blood into the pulmonary artery and lungs. The left heart receives blood from the lungs and pumps it onward to the body.","normal":"Blood returning from the body enters the right atrium, passes into the right ventricle, and is pumped to the lungs. Blood then returns to the left heart and is ejected to the body. As a ventricle fills, its muscle stretches; within a normal range, more filling can increase stroke volume, the amount ejected per beat, supporting cardiac output, the amount pumped per minute.","change":"When venous return drops, a ventricle may fill and stretch less, lowering stroke volume and potentially cardiac output; reduced flow can then weaken tissue perfusion. Positive-pressure breathing can temporarily reduce return by raising chest pressure. At the other extreme, once extra filling produces little additional output, further fluid may add little benefit and can contribute to lung swelling or ventricular dysfunction.","cues":"At the bedside, central venous pressure (CVP)—pressure in the large veins near the heart—changes with breathing and reflects pressure, not blood volume directly. Variation in the strength of an arterial pulse waveform or low urine output may accompany circulatory problems, but each has other causes. These findings need interpretation alongside the overall cardiovascular picture.","distinction":"A filling pressure is not the same as ventricular volume, so one CVP value cannot establish how much blood is present. Static filling pressures alone also do not reliably show whether fluid will raise cardiac output.","related":["circulation","output","fluid"],"questions":[{"stem":"With positive-pressure ventilation, why can inspiration reduce right-heart filling?","options":["Chest pressure rises, which can reduce blood returning through the veins to the heart.","Chest pressure falls, which pushes more blood out of the right ventricle.","The left ventricle immediately stops sending blood to the body."],"feedback":["Correct: rising chest pressure can reduce venous return and right-ventricular filling.","A pressure rise during positive-pressure inspiration, rather than a fall, can reduce venous return.","The mechanism begins with changes in venous return and right-heart filling; it does not mean the left ventricle immediately stops pumping."]},{"stem":"A single central venous pressure reading is being considered as a way to predict whether fluid will increase cardiac output. Which principle is most accurate?","options":["It is a pressure measurement and, by itself, does not reliably predict fluid responsiveness.","It directly measures the total volume of blood in the circulation.","It reliably shows whether cardiac output will rise after fluid is given."],"feedback":["Correct: central venous pressure reflects pressure, not blood volume directly, and static pressures alone poorly predict fluid responsiveness.","Central venous pressure does not directly measure total circulating blood volume.","A single central venous pressure value cannot reliably establish whether cardiac output will increase with fluid."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12519902/","title":"Foundations and Advancements in Hemodynamic Monitoring"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK6895/","title":"Hemodynamic monitoring - Surgical Treatment - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"pulmonary-artery","definition":"Pulmonary vessels carry blood through the lungs, and the right ventricle ejects blood into this circulation. The load on that ventricle depends in part on resistance in the pulmonary vessels.","structure":"The pulmonary artery and its branches carry blood from the right ventricle into the lung circulation. This vascular bed is normally relatively low-pressure and compliant, but its resistance and pressure affect the effort required for right-ventricular ejection.","normal":"Normally, the right ventricle sends the blood it receives into the pulmonary circulation, which can accommodate changing blood volume through vessel distension and recruitment. This relatively compliant, low-pressure circuit places a load on the right ventricle, but ordinarily permits forward flow without excessive pressure.","change":"When pulmonary vascular resistance rises through constriction, loss, or obstruction of vessels, pressure and the right ventricle’s ejection load can increase. The ventricle may adapt by thickening; persistent strain can lead to dilation and failure, limiting cardiac output.","cues":"A pattern of right-ventricular strain or reduced output may be consistent with increased pulmonary vascular load, but neither finding alone identifies its cause. Pulmonary pressure and vascular resistance require clinical context rather than inference from one bedside sign.","distinction":"Increased pulmonary pressure is not by itself proof of a primary right-ventricular muscle problem; vascular load can be the cause of strain. A single sign of right-heart strain does not establish pulmonary hypertension.","related":["circulation","afterload","output"],"questions":[{"stem":"What does increased pulmonary vascular resistance do to the right ventricle’s ejection?","options":["It increases the load the right ventricle must eject against.","It reduces the left ventricle’s afterload directly by widening pulmonary vessels.","It eliminates the need for the right ventricle to fill."],"feedback":["Greater pulmonary vascular resistance increases right-ventricular afterload and can make ejection more difficult.","The stated mechanism is increased, not reduced, pulmonary vascular resistance and does not directly describe left-ventricular afterload.","The right ventricle still receives and ejects blood; increased resistance does not eliminate filling."]},{"stem":"Why can persistent pulmonary vascular pressure be associated with reduced cardiac output?","options":["Chronic load can impair right-ventricular function, limiting forward output.","Pulmonary vessels normally return blood directly to the systemic arteries without the heart.","Higher pulmonary pressure always means the left ventricle ejects more blood."],"feedback":["Persistent pressure and resistance can strain the right ventricle, and right-ventricular failure can limit cardiac output.","Blood in the pulmonary circulation returns to the heart; it does not bypass the heart to enter systemic arteries.","Higher pulmonary pressure does not guarantee increased left-ventricular ejection; the evidence describes possible right-ventricular overload and failure."]}],"title":"Pulmonary vessels and the right heart","unit":"resp","aliases":["right ventricle","right ventricular strain","pulmonary artery","pulmonary hypertension"],"model":"embolus","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-hypertension/pulmonary-hypertension","title":"Pulmonary Hypertension - Pulmonology - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5018168/","title":"The right ventricle: interaction with the pulmonary circulation"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499876/","title":"Right Ventricular Hypertrophy - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"id":"shock","definition":"Circulatory shock is acute circulatory failure that causes inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery. The resulting cellular dysfunction can damage tissues and organs.","structure":"The relevant structures include the heart, blood vessels, and tissue microcirculation. Together, cardiac filling and output, vascular tone, and capillary blood flow determine whether oxygenated blood reaches tissues effectively.","normal":"Normally, venous return fills the ventricles, and cardiac output moves blood through vessels to tissue capillaries. Adequate perfusion supports oxygen delivery and aerobic cellular metabolism; vascular responses can help preserve arterial pressure when it falls.","change":"In shock, inadequate circulation reduces tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery. Depending on the mechanism, intravascular volume may be critically low, vascular tone may be abnormal, cardiac output may fall, or mechanical obstruction may impede filling or flow; cells may shift toward anaerobic metabolism.","cues":"A fall in arterial pressure, signs of reduced tissue perfusion, or elevated lactate can be concerning in context, but no single cue establishes shock or its type. Findings must be interpreted with the broader circulatory and clinical picture.","distinction":"Shock is tissue hypoperfusion from acute circulatory failure, not simply a low blood-pressure reading. Different mechanisms can impair perfusion, and distributive shock may involve cellular hypoperfusion even when cardiac output is high.","related":["perfusion","output","hypoperfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which mechanism best describes hypovolemic shock?","options":["Reduced intravascular volume lowers venous return and ventricular filling, which can reduce stroke volume and output.","A primary cardiac disorder necessarily increases ventricular filling and output.","A mechanical obstruction causes blood volume to increase within the circulation."],"feedback":["Correct: reduced volume can lower preload, filling, stroke volume, and cardiac output.","Incorrect: cardiogenic shock involves reduced output from a primary cardiac disorder, not necessarily increased filling or output.","Incorrect: obstruction interferes with filling or emptying; it does not define an increase in blood volume."]},{"stem":"Why can tissue hypoperfusion occur in distributive shock even if cardiac output is high?","options":["Blood may bypass capillary beds through arteriovenous shunts, and cellular oxygen transport may be uncoupled.","High cardiac output guarantees that oxygen reaches and is used by every tissue cell.","Elevated lactate proves that cardiac output is low."],"feedback":["Correct: capillary bypass and uncoupled cellular oxygen transport can impair tissue oxygen use despite high output.","Incorrect: high output does not guarantee effective capillary delivery or cellular oxygen use.","Incorrect: lactate can rise with inadequate tissue oxygen delivery, but it does not by itself establish low cardiac output."]}],"title":"Circulatory shock","unit":"multi","aliases":["shock states","shock"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/es-us/professional/cuidados-cr%C3%ADticos/shock-y-reanimaci%C3%B3n-con-l%C3%ADquidos/shock","title":"Shock - Cuidados críticos - Manual Merck versión para profesionales"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/en-ca/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?content=summary","title":"Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"id":"skin","definition":"A pressure injury is localized damage to skin and/or underlying tissue, usually where soft tissue is compressed over a bony prominence. Sustained pressure, often with shear, can impair local blood flow and injure tissue.","structure":"The relevant structures are the skin and deeper soft tissues between a bony prominence and an external surface or device. Injury may involve the epidermis, dermis, subcutaneous tissue, and deeper muscle, with damage sometimes beginning below visibly intact skin.","normal":"Normally, skin and underlying tissues remain viable when local microcirculation supplies oxygen and nutrients and mechanical loads do not persistently overwhelm tissue tolerance. The layers also provide a continuous covering over deeper structures; their condition reflects both surface stress and deeper tissue integrity.","change":"With prolonged compression, often combined with shear or friction, small vessels can be occluded and local oxygen delivery falls. Ischemia, inflammation, and edema may follow; deep muscle can be injured before surface breakdown, and continued damage may progress through skin and underlying layers.","cues":"A localized non-blanching color change, blister, or open area over a pressure-bearing site may signal injury, while deep damage can exist beneath intact skin. Appearance alone does not establish depth or cause; consider the context and tissue changes over time.","distinction":"Intact skin does not rule out deep tissue injury, and visible discoloration alone does not establish a particular stage or prove pressure was the only contributing force.","related":["interstitium","perfusion","inflammation"],"questions":[{"stem":"Why can a pressure injury involve deeper tissue before the skin surface visibly breaks down?","options":["Compression can impair local microcirculation and injure deeper muscle before surface changes appear.","The epidermis always develops an open wound before deeper tissues are affected.","A visible skin change is required before pressure can reduce blood flow to muscle."],"feedback":["Correct: sustained compression can reduce local blood flow, and deeper muscle may become ischemic before the surface breaks down.","Incorrect: deep tissue injury can precede visible surface breakdown.","Incorrect: pressure-related impairment of local circulation can occur before visible skin changes."]},{"stem":"A patient has intact skin over a pressure-bearing area. Which inference is most appropriate?","options":["Intact skin does not exclude injury to deeper tissue.","The tissue beneath the skin must be uninjured.","The area cannot be affected by shear."],"feedback":["Correct: deeper tissue damage may occur before visible breakdown of the skin.","Incorrect: deep muscle injury can exist beneath intact skin.","Incorrect: shear can contribute to pressure injury even when the surface remains intact."]}],"title":"Skin layers and pressure","unit":"skin","aliases":["skin integrity","skin perfusion","epidermis","dermis","pressure injury"],"model":"skin","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/dermatologic-disorders/pressure-injury/pressure-injuries","title":"Pressure Injuries - Dermatology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553107/","title":"Pressure Injury - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK553107/","title":"Pressure Injury - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"sleep","definition":"Sleep is a recurring state of reduced awareness that supports a regular sleep–wake pattern. Its timing reflects both the body’s accumulated need for sleep and its internal circadian rhythm.","structure":"Sleep–wake regulation involves homeostatic processes that build sleep need and a circadian pacemaker, or biological clock, that helps time sleep and wakefulness. These processes interact, while light, noise, illness, and medications can influence their expression.","normal":"In a typical sleep–wake cycle, homeostatic sleep need and circadian timing work together to organize periods of sleep and alertness. Sleep includes deeper and REM stages, and the timing of sleep generally follows a day–night pattern shaped by the internal clock.","change":"In critical illness, sleep may become fragmented, with frequent arousals and less deep or REM sleep. ICU light, noise, procedures, ventilation, medications, and illness can disrupt the day–night pattern; circadian timing may also be altered or become uncoupled from the observed sleep–wake cycle.","cues":"At the bedside, repeated awakenings, daytime sleeping, nighttime wakefulness, or difficulty maintaining alertness may suggest disrupted sleep or circadian organization. These observations are not specific to one cause, and illness, medications, and the care environment can all contribute.","distinction":"Poor or fragmented sleep does not by itself establish delirium. Sleep disruption and delirium may be related, but the direction and cause of that relationship are not settled.","related":["delirium","arousal","neurons"],"questions":[{"stem":"A hospitalized patient sleeps in short periods during the day and is frequently awakened overnight. Which foundation best explains how both sleep need and timing shape the sleep–wake cycle?","options":["Homeostatic sleep processes interact with the circadian clock.","The circadian clock alone determines all sleep and wakefulness.","A single awakening proves that the patient has delirium."],"feedback":["Correct: sleep–wake organization reflects interaction between accumulated sleep need and circadian timing.","The circadian rhythm contributes to timing, but it interacts with homeostatic sleep processes rather than acting alone.","An awakening can occur for many reasons and is not enough to establish delirium."]},{"stem":"Which observation most directly describes the sleep-stage pattern reported in many critically ill patients?","options":["Frequent arousals with less deep and REM sleep.","A consistent increase in uninterrupted deep sleep.","A preserved day–night sleep pattern despite ICU disruption."],"feedback":["Correct: critical illness is commonly associated with fragmented sleep and reduced deep and REM sleep.","This reverses the reported pattern, which includes frequent arousals and less deep sleep.","The ICU environment commonly disrupts normal day–night organization, although individual patterns vary."]}],"title":"Sleep and circadian rhythm","unit":"psych","aliases":["sleep disruption","sleep deprivation","circadian rhythm","sleep"],"model":"synapse","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5447310/","title":"Sleep in the Intensive Care Unit"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7530631/","title":"Abnormal Sleep, Circadian Rhythm Disruption, and Delirium in the ICU: Are They Related?"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3397807/","title":"Sleep, Circadian Rhythms, and Critical Illness"}]},{"id":"sodium","definition":"Serum sodium concentration is closely linked to the balance of water and solute outside cells. Because water can cross cell membranes, changes in this balance can shift water into or out of cells.","structure":"The relevant context is extracellular fluid surrounding cells, including brain cells behind the blood-brain barrier. Cell membranes allow water to move across them, while extracellular sodium is a major determinant of plasma osmolality and water-shift direction.","normal":"Normally, extracellular and intracellular fluid reach an osmotic balance, so there is no net water shift across cell membranes. Serum sodium is a major determinant of plasma osmolality, and water can move through cell membranes as compartments adjust toward equilibrium.","change":"When extracellular osmolality falls with hyponatremia, water can move from extracellular fluid into cells, causing swelling. Brain cells are especially important clinically; with rapid-onset changes, they may not have time to adapt by removing intracellular osmolytes, increasing the risk of cerebral edema.","cues":"Headache, confusion, stupor, seizures, or coma may occur with hyponatremia, particularly when it develops rapidly. These neurologic findings are not specific to sodium imbalance, and a single symptom cannot establish the cause.","distinction":"A low serum sodium concentration reflects excess water relative to solute, not necessarily a simple loss of total-body sodium. The direction and clinical effect of water shifts depend on the extracellular osmotic context.","related":["fluid","adh","cerebral"],"questions":[{"stem":"When extracellular osmolality falls, which direction can water move across cell membranes?","options":["From extracellular fluid into cells, causing them to swell.","From cells into extracellular fluid, causing them to shrink.","There is always no water movement across the membrane."],"feedback":["Lower extracellular osmolality can draw water into cells, increasing cell volume.","Water moving out of cells is the expected direction in a relatively hypertonic extracellular environment, not the situation described.","Water crosses cell membranes; when osmolality differs, there can be a net shift toward equilibrium."]},{"stem":"Why can rapid-onset hyponatremia be especially concerning for the brain?","options":["Brain cells may not have time to remove intracellular osmolytes, so water entry can cause swelling.","Brain cells immediately remove all extracellular water, preventing swelling.","Rapid changes prevent water from crossing cell membranes."],"feedback":["With rapid onset, brain cells may not adapt quickly by removing osmolytes, allowing water entry and possible cerebral edema.","The evidence describes water moving into brain cells, not immediate removal of extracellular water that prevents swelling.","Cell membranes are permeable to water, so rapid changes can produce consequential fluid shifts."]}],"title":"Sodium and water shifts","unit":"endo","aliases":["sodium","hyponatremia","hypernatremia","osmolality"],"model":"osmosis","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/electrolyte-disorders/hyponatremia","title":"Hyponatremia - Nephrology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5762960/","title":"Hyponatremia and the Brain"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK544365/","title":"Physiology, Plasma Osmolality and Oncotic Pressure - NCBI - NIH"}]},{"id":"stroke-volume","title":"Stroke volume","unit":"cardio","model":"pump","aliases":["stroke volume"],"definition":"Stroke volume is the amount of blood one ventricle pushes out with each beat. It is one part of the amount of blood the heart pumps each minute.","structure":"The heart has two lower pumping chambers, the right and left ventricles. Each fills from an atrium, the chamber above it, then ejects blood through an outlet valve: the right ventricle to the pulmonary artery and the left to the aorta. Stroke volume describes each ventricle’s ejected amount.","normal":"During relaxation, each ventricle fills; the blood present just before contraction is end-diastolic volume. During contraction, some blood remains as end-systolic volume and the rest is ejected; stroke volume is the difference. Filling, resistance to ejection, and muscle force shape that amount. Heart rate combines with stroke volume to determine blood pumped per minute.","change":"If filling falls, or the heart muscle contracts less forcefully, less blood may be ejected. Greater afterload—the opposing load the ventricle must overcome—can also limit ejection, leaving more blood behind after contraction. A smaller stroke volume can reduce cardiac output, the amount pumped per minute, and may lessen blood flow reaching body tissues.","cues":"A change in stroke volume is not directly identified from one bedside observation. Lower output may be accompanied by lower arterial pressure or signs of reduced tissue perfusion, while heart rate can also change and partly compensate. These findings have many causes, so they do not establish stroke volume or a diagnosis on their own.","distinction":"Stroke volume is blood per beat, not blood per minute; heart rate also determines cardiac output. Total ejected volume may not equal forward blood flow to the body when a valve leaks.","related":["preload","afterload","output"],"questions":[{"stem":"If end-diastolic volume stays the same but end-systolic volume rises, what happens to stroke volume?","options":["It decreases because more blood remains after contraction.","It increases because more blood remains after contraction.","It stays the same because filling did not change."],"feedback":["Correct: stroke volume is end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume, so a larger end-systolic volume reduces it.","More blood left behind means less was ejected, so stroke volume decreases rather than increases.","Stroke volume depends on both filling and the amount left after contraction, not filling alone."]},{"stem":"Why might a very rapid heart rate lower stroke volume?","options":["It shortens filling time, which can reduce the amount entering the ventricle before contraction.","It gives the ventricle more time to fill between contractions.","It directly increases the amount ejected with every beat."],"feedback":["Correct: a faster rate can shorten relaxation and filling time, lowering end-diastolic volume and stroke volume.","A faster rate generally shortens, rather than lengthens, the time available for filling.","A faster rate does not necessarily increase blood ejected per beat; stroke volume may fall if filling is reduced."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK493174/","title":"Checking your browser - reCAPTCHA"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK547686/","title":"Physiology, Stroke Volume - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"surfactant","definition":"Surfactant is a substance in the lungs that lowers surface tension at the air-liquid lining of alveoli. This helps stabilize small air spaces and makes them less likely to collapse.","structure":"Pulmonary surfactant acts at the thin air-liquid interface lining alveoli and small airways. It is secreted by type II alveolar cells and helps counter the inward collapsing force generated by surface tension.","normal":"As alveoli expand and shrink with breathing, surface tension at their lining tends to reduce the air-liquid surface area. Surfactant lowers that tension, helping alveoli remain stable, supporting small-airway patency, and reducing the work needed to breathe.","change":"When surfactant is deficient or dysfunctional, surface tension rises and alveoli become more prone to collapse. This can contribute to atelectasis, uneven lung expansion, impaired gas exchange, and greater work of breathing; lung injury or edema can also disrupt surfactant function.","cues":"Atelectasis, impaired oxygenation, or increased work of breathing may be compatible with surfactant dysfunction, but none is specific to it. Other lung problems can produce similar signals, so an isolated bedside finding cannot identify the mechanism.","distinction":"Alveolar collapse is not proof of surfactant deficiency; other forces and conditions can also cause atelectasis. Surfactant dysfunction can contribute to collapse, and collapse can in turn worsen surfactant function.","related":["alveoli","peep","pleura"],"questions":[{"stem":"If surfactant function is impaired, what change most directly makes alveolar collapse more likely?","options":["Surface tension at the alveolar lining increases.","Surface tension falls, making alveoli more stable.","Blood flow through pulmonary arteries increases and stretches alveoli open."],"feedback":["Correct. Surfactant normally lowers surface tension; when its function is impaired, the increased collapsing force makes alveolar collapse more likely.","Lower surface tension is the normal stabilizing effect of surfactant, not the expected change when surfactant is impaired.","The described mechanism concerns surface tension at the alveolar lining; increased pulmonary arterial blood flow is not the direct explanation."]},{"stem":"A patient has atelectasis and impaired oxygenation. Which conclusion best reflects the relationship between these findings and surfactant?","options":["Surfactant dysfunction could contribute, but these findings alone do not establish it as the cause.","Atelectasis proves surfactant is deficient because no other factors can collapse alveoli.","Impaired oxygenation rules out surfactant dysfunction because surfactant affects only breathing effort."],"feedback":["Correct. Surfactant dysfunction can promote alveolar collapse and impaired gas exchange, but other causes of atelectasis and oxygenation problems are possible.","Atelectasis has multiple possible contributors, including factors other than surfactant deficiency or dysfunction.","Surfactant helps stabilize alveoli and support gas exchange as well as reducing work of breathing; impaired oxygenation does not rule out dysfunction."]}],"title":"Surfactant and alveolar stability","unit":"resp","aliases":["surfactant","atelectasis","alveolar collapse"],"model":"alveolar","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/bronchiectasis-and-atelectasis/atelectasis","title":"Atelectasis - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7152382/","title":"Alveolar Epithelium and Pulmonary Surfactant"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539825/&lang=en/","title":"Physiology, Alveolar Tension - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"swallow","definition":"Swallowing moves material from the mouth through the pharynx and esophagus toward the stomach. The coordinated swallow also helps keep food, liquid, and secretions out of the lower respiratory tract.","structure":"The oral and pharyngeal stages share space with the respiratory tract, so airway protection depends on coordinated tongue movement, pharyngeal contraction, laryngeal elevation, vocal-fold closure, epiglottic movement, and cough. The esophagus then carries the swallowed material toward the stomach.","normal":"During normal swallowing, the oral and pharyngeal phases coordinate to form and propel a bolus toward the esophagus. Laryngeal elevation, vocal-fold closure, epiglottic inversion, and a brief pause in breathing help protect the airway, while an effective cough can clear material that enters it.","change":"If swallowing coordination, consciousness, or cough is impaired, material may enter the larynx or lower respiratory tract instead of following the intended route. Neurologic disease, weakness, sedatives, or impaired alertness can disrupt protection, and aspiration may occur without an obvious cough.","cues":"Difficulty coordinating a swallow, reduced alertness, weak cough, or known neurologic or neuromuscular impairment may signal increased aspiration risk. These cues do not prove that aspiration has occurred, and aspiration can be silent when sensation or cough is reduced.","distinction":"Aspiration risk is not the same as confirmed aspiration. The absence of coughing does not rule out aspiration, because impaired sensation or cough can allow material to enter the airway without an obvious response.","related":["airway","nmj","arousal"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which sequence best describes how the pharyngeal swallow normally protects the airway?","options":["The larynx elevates, vocal folds close, and breathing briefly pauses as the bolus moves toward the esophagus.","The vocal folds remain open while the bolus is directed into the lower respiratory tract.","The cough reflex alone directs every bolus into the esophagus."],"feedback":["Correct: coordinated laryngeal movement, vocal-fold closure, and swallowing apnea help protect the airway during bolus passage.","An open airway during bolus passage would not describe the protective closure mechanisms.","Cough can help clear material, but it is not the sole mechanism directing a normal swallow."]},{"stem":"A patient with reduced alertness has no observed coughing during oral intake. What is the most accurate inference?","options":["The lack of cough does not exclude aspiration, especially if protective reflexes are impaired.","The lack of cough proves that swallowing and airway protection are normal.","Aspiration is confirmed because the patient is less alert."],"feedback":["Correct: diminished sensation or ineffective cough can make aspiration silent, so no cough does not rule it out.","No observed cough cannot establish normal swallowing or intact airway protection.","Reduced alertness can increase risk, but risk alone does not confirm that aspiration occurred."]}],"title":"Swallowing and airway protection","unit":"resp","aliases":["airway protection","aspiration","swallowing"],"model":"airway","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470169","title":"Aspiration Risk"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK554405/","title":"Anatomy, Head and Neck, Swallowing"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-29785/","title":"Physiology, Swallowing"}]},{"id":"temperature","definition":"Thermoregulation is the body's process for keeping core temperature within a range that supports normal metabolism. It balances heat production with heat loss.","structure":"Temperature sensing occurs through peripheral receptors in the skin and central receptors in the viscera, spinal cord, and hypothalamus. The preoptic area of the hypothalamus integrates these signals and coordinates responses that conserve, produce, or dissipate heat.","normal":"Thermoreceptors send information about surface and core temperature to the hypothalamus. The hypothalamus compares these inputs with its set point and coordinates responses such as skin blood flow, sweating, shivering, and changes in metabolic activity to maintain core temperature.","change":"When the body needs to lose heat, sweating and increased skin blood flow promote heat loss, while shivering and heat production are inhibited. When it needs to conserve or generate heat, skin blood vessels constrict and shivering and metabolic heat production can increase.","cues":"A measured temperature reflects the body's current heat balance, but by itself it does not reveal why that balance changed. Consider the hypothalamic control system, heat production, heat loss, and the context in which the reading was obtained.","distinction":"A temperature reading alone does not identify a cause or establish a diagnosis. Thermoregulation is a coordinated response involving sensing, hypothalamic control, and changes in heat production or loss.","related":["skin","thyroid","neurons"],"questions":[{"stem":"Which sequence best describes the basic control of body temperature?","options":["Thermoreceptors sense temperature, the hypothalamus integrates the signals, and the body produces responses that alter heat loss or production.","The skin sets the temperature target, and the hypothalamus only detects changes after sweating begins.","The hypothalamus produces heat directly, while peripheral and central thermoreceptors remove it."],"feedback":["Correct: temperature sensing provides input to the hypothalamus, which coordinates efferent responses that change heat balance.","The skin contains peripheral sensors, but the hypothalamus is the primary integrating control center.","The hypothalamus coordinates responses rather than directly producing heat; thermoreceptors sense temperature rather than removing it."]},{"stem":"When the body needs to dissipate heat, which response fits normal thermoregulation?","options":["Sweating increases and skin blood flow rises, promoting heat loss.","Skin blood vessels constrict and shivering increases to conserve and generate heat.","Catecholamine release increases metabolic heat production while sweating is inhibited."],"feedback":["Correct: sweating and increased skin blood flow promote heat loss when the body needs to cool.","Vasoconstriction and shivering are heat-conserving or heat-generating responses, not heat-dissipating ones.","Increased metabolic heat production would oppose the goal of dissipating heat."]}],"title":"Thermoregulation","unit":"multi","aliases":["thermoregulation","core temperature","hypothermia","hyperthermia"],"model":"perfusion","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK507838/","title":"Physiology, Temperature Regulation - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499843/","title":"Physiology, Thermal Regulation - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2844235/","title":"Thermoregulation: Physiological and Clinical Considerations ..."}]},{"id":"thyroid","definition":"Thyroid hormones help regulate how quickly the body uses energy and produces heat. They also influence heart activity and oxygen use.","structure":"The thyroid gland produces hormones that act in many tissues, including the heart, blood vessels, and cells that use energy. Their effects include changes in heart rate and contraction, metabolic activity, heat production, and oxygen consumption.","normal":"With adequate thyroid hormone activity, tissues use energy at a level suited to the body’s needs. The hormones support baseline heat production and metabolism, while also influencing heart rate, contraction strength, blood flow, and oxygen use across the body.","change":"With excess thyroid hormone, metabolic activity and heat production rise, and the heart may beat faster and contract more strongly. With insufficient hormone, metabolism and heat production can fall, and heart activity may slow. These patterns describe mechanisms, not conclusions from one isolated sign.","cues":"Heat intolerance, weight loss, tremor, or palpitations can be cues of increased thyroid activity; cold intolerance, fatigue, or a slow heart rate can fit reduced activity. Each cue has other possible explanations and should not be treated as a diagnosis alone.","distinction":"A fast heart rate by itself does not establish hyperthyroidism, and fatigue alone does not establish hypothyroidism. Consider the broader pattern of metabolic, temperature, and cardiovascular findings.","related":["output","temperature","oxygenation"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient has increased thyroid hormone activity. Which paired physiologic changes are most consistent with its effects?","options":["Higher metabolic heat production and increased cardiac stimulation.","Lower oxygen use and reduced heat production.","Reduced heart contraction with increased metabolic slowing."],"feedback":["Correct: excess thyroid activity can increase metabolism and thermogenesis while stimulating heart rate and contraction.","Incorrect: lower oxygen use and reduced heat production are more consistent with reduced thyroid activity.","Incorrect: this pairing describes slowing rather than the typical effects of excess thyroid hormone."]},{"stem":"A patient reports palpitations. What is the best inference about thyroid function from this cue alone?","options":["It may be consistent with increased thyroid activity, but it does not establish that cause.","It proves that thyroid hormone levels are excessive.","It rules out reduced thyroid activity."],"feedback":["Correct: palpitations can occur with increased thyroid activity, but the cue is not specific and needs broader context.","Incorrect: palpitations have multiple possible causes and do not prove excess thyroid hormone.","Incorrect: one symptom cannot rule out a thyroid pattern or other explanation."]}],"title":"Thyroid hormone and metabolism","unit":"endo","aliases":["thyroid hormone","thyrotoxicosis","myxedema"],"model":"delivery","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537039/","title":"Physiology, Thyroid Function - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/thyroid-disorders/hyperthyroidism","title":"Hyperthyroidism"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7238803/","title":"The role of thyroid hormone in metabolism and metabolic syndrome - PMC"}]},{"id":"valves","title":"Cardiac valves","unit":"cardio","model":"pump","aliases":["heart valves","valve regurgitation","valvular disease","valves"],"definition":"Heart valves are flaps that direct blood through the heart. They open to let blood pass and close to limit backward flow.","structure":"The heart has four valves between its chambers and the large vessels leaving it. The tricuspid valve lies between the right atrium and right ventricle, and the pulmonary valve is at the exit to the pulmonary artery. On the left, the mitral valve lies between the left atrium and ventricle, and the aortic valve is at the exit to the aorta. Fibrous cords and small muscles anchor the mitral and tricuspid valves.","normal":"Blood returning to the heart passes from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, then through the pulmonary valve toward the lungs. It returns to the left atrium, passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and is ejected through the aortic valve into the aorta. The valves open and close in sequence to guide this flow.","change":"A valve may become narrowed, called stenosis, or may fail to close fully, called regurgitation. Narrowing obstructs forward flow; leakage allows some blood to move backward. Either can change pressure and blood flow through the heart, making its pumping work less effective. These effects may develop before a person notices symptoms, and one valve can have both problems.","cues":"Faulty valves often produce a heart murmur, an unusual sound heard with a stethoscope, but a murmur alone does not establish the cause or severity. Possible symptoms include breathlessness, fatigue, swelling, palpitations, chest pain, or fainting; some people have none. Echocardiography, an ultrasound of the heart, can assess valve structure and blood flow.","distinction":"A murmur does not by itself prove that a valve is seriously abnormal. The absence of noticeable symptoms does not rule out a valve problem.","related":["circulation","output","perfusion"],"questions":[{"stem":"During relaxation of the left ventricle, a narrowed mitral valve would most directly impede which flow?","options":["Blood moving from the left atrium into the left ventricle","Blood moving from the left ventricle into the aorta","Blood moving from the left ventricle back into the left atrium"],"feedback":["Correct: the mitral valve is the inlet to the left ventricle, and blood normally passes through it during relaxation.","That flow passes through the aortic valve when the left ventricle contracts.","Backward flow into the left atrium describes mitral regurgitation during contraction, not the effect of a narrowed opening."]},{"stem":"When the left ventricle contracts, a mitral valve that does not close fully allows blood to move where?","options":["Backward into the left atrium","Forward from the left atrium into the left ventricle","Forward from the left ventricle into the aorta only"],"feedback":["Correct: an incompletely closed mitral valve can let blood leak from the contracting left ventricle into the left atrium.","The mitral valve normally opens for this flow while the left ventricle relaxes and fills.","The aortic valve opens during contraction, but mitral leakage means some blood can also flow backward into the left atrium."]}],"sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/heart-valve-disorders/overview-of-heart-valve-disorders","title":"Overview of Heart Valve Disorders - Heart and Blood Vessel ..."},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/valvular-disorders/overview-of-cardiac-valvular-disorders","title":"Overview of Cardiac Valvular Disorders - Cardiology"}]},{"id":"ventilation","definition":"Ventilation is the movement of air into and out of the lungs. Alveolar ventilation is the portion that reaches gas-exchanging air sacs and supports carbon dioxide removal.","structure":"Alveolar ventilation occurs in the gas-exchanging air sacs, while carbon dioxide arrives in pulmonary capillary blood from tissues. The respiratory pump—including central drive, nerves, muscles, chest wall, and lungs—moves air; effective ventilation depends on fresh air reaching functioning alveoli after accounting for dead space.","normal":"Normally, fresh air reaches alveoli and carbon dioxide diffuses from pulmonary capillary blood into alveolar gas for exhalation. Alveolar ventilation adjusts to carbon dioxide production, helping keep arterial carbon dioxide in balance with the body's ongoing metabolic activity.","change":"When alveolar ventilation falls, or dead space increases without enough compensatory breathing, less carbon dioxide is removed for a given minute ventilation. Arterial carbon dioxide can then rise. Reduced ventilation can also lower alveolar oxygen, so ventilation problems may affect oxygenation as well.","cues":"A raised arterial carbon dioxide level can signal inadequate alveolar ventilation, and a blood gas can help assess it. The finding does not identify the cause by itself: reduced drive, respiratory muscle weakness, airway resistance, lung or chest-wall load, and increased dead space are possible contributors.","distinction":"Hypercapnia does not by itself prove that respiratory drive is suppressed; mechanical load, muscle weakness, or increased dead space can also impair carbon dioxide removal.","related":["airway","diaphragm","gas-gradient"],"questions":[{"stem":"A patient has rising arterial carbon dioxide. Which mechanism most directly explains this change?","options":["Alveolar ventilation is insufficient to remove carbon dioxide at the rate it enters the blood.","Hemoglobin is carrying less oxygen despite unchanged ventilation.","Pulmonary blood flow is increased while alveolar ventilation remains adequate."],"feedback":["Correct: when alveolar ventilation falls or fails to compensate for dead space, carbon dioxide elimination becomes inadequate.","Hemoglobin oxygen carriage concerns oxygen content and does not directly explain reduced carbon dioxide elimination.","Increased blood flow alone does not establish inadequate ventilation; the key mechanism is insufficient effective alveolar ventilation relative to carbon dioxide load."]},{"stem":"Two patients have the same minute ventilation, but one has more physiologic dead space. What is expected if the patient does not compensate?","options":["Less of the ventilation reaches gas-exchanging alveoli, so carbon dioxide removal is poorer.","More of the ventilation reaches gas-exchanging alveoli, so carbon dioxide removal improves.","Dead space has no effect on carbon dioxide removal when minute ventilation is unchanged."],"feedback":["Correct: with more dead space, a smaller share of the same minute ventilation supports alveolar gas exchange.","Dead space reduces, rather than increases, the effective portion of ventilation reaching gas-exchanging alveoli.","Dead space matters because it reduces effective alveolar ventilation for a given minute ventilation."]}],"title":"Ventilation and carbon dioxide","unit":"resp","aliases":["hypoventilation","ventilation","hypercapnia","carbon dioxide retention"],"model":"airway","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/ventilatory-failure","title":"Ventilatory Failure"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-28416/","title":"Respiratory Failure in Adults"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/ventilatory-failure?qt=Fever","title":"Ventilatory Failure - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manuals ..."}]},{"id":"vq","definition":"Ventilation-perfusion matching describes how well air reaching lung alveoli corresponds to blood flowing through nearby pulmonary capillaries. Matching supports effective oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange.","structure":"The relevant structures are regional lung units: alveoli receive ventilation, and adjacent pulmonary capillaries receive perfusion. Their relationship varies across the lungs; arterial blood leaving the lungs reflects the combined gas-exchange contribution of these differently matched regions.","normal":"Normally, alveolar ventilation and capillary perfusion are sufficiently matched for oxygen to enter blood and carbon dioxide to leave it. Well-ventilated regions receive blood flow, and well-perfused regions receive fresh gas, supporting effective overall pulmonary gas exchange.","change":"With low ventilation relative to perfusion, blood passes alveoli that receive too little fresh air, lowering oxygen uptake; a shunt is an extreme form. With high ventilation relative to perfusion, airflow reaches poorly perfused alveoli, creating dead space and reducing effective gas exchange.","cues":"Hypoxemia can be a signal of impaired matching, but it is not specific to this mechanism. A blood gas and the clinical context help assess oxygenation; a low-oxygen reading alone does not distinguish mismatch from shunt, diffusion impairment, or other causes.","distinction":"A shunt is not the same as dead space: shunted blood reaches poorly ventilated or unventilated regions, whereas dead-space ventilation reaches alveoli with little or no perfusion.","related":["alveoli","pulmonary-artery","gas-gradient"],"questions":[{"stem":"A lung region receives blood flow but very little fresh air. Which effect best describes this low ventilation-perfusion relationship?","options":["Blood leaving the region gains less oxygen because ventilation is inadequate relative to perfusion.","The region is dead space because ventilation greatly exceeds perfusion.","Gas exchange improves because blood flow compensates for reduced ventilation."],"feedback":["Correct: with too little ventilation for the blood flow, oxygen uptake is limited; an extreme form is shunt.","Dead space describes the opposite imbalance: ventilation with little or no perfusion.","Blood flow cannot fully compensate for inadequate fresh air reaching the alveoli."]},{"stem":"Which description best identifies dead-space ventilation?","options":["Alveoli receive air but have little or no blood flow available for gas exchange.","Blood flows through alveoli that receive little or no air.","Alveolar ventilation and perfusion are well matched."],"feedback":["Correct: ventilation without adequate perfusion means that some air does not participate effectively in gas exchange.","This describes a shunt-like, low ventilation-perfusion region rather than dead space.","Well-matched ventilation and perfusion support effective gas exchange rather than dead space."]}],"title":"Ventilation and perfusion matching","unit":"resp","aliases":["ventilation-perfusion mismatch","V/Q mismatch","dead space","shunt"],"model":"embolus","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539907/","title":"Physiology, Pulmonary Ventilation and Perfusion - StatPearls - NCBI"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/8274320","title":"Ventilation/Perfusion Relationships and Gas Exchange: Measurement Approaches"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/8315793","title":"Pulmonary Gas Exchange and Acid-Base Balance During Exercise"}]},{"definition":"Hemodynamic monitoring brings together bedside observations and measurements to assess circulation and tissue perfusion. It uses trends and the patient’s overall condition because pressure readings do not directly measure blood volume.","structure":"Focus: left ventricular filling. The left ventricle is the heart chamber that fills before pumping blood to the body. Bedside ultrasound can show its size and contraction; the inferior vena cava, a large vein returning blood to the heart, is another observed structure. Positive-pressure ventilation can affect pressure interpretation.","normal":"With adequate filling, blood enters the left ventricle during relaxation, and the chamber then pumps blood forward. Monitoring combines findings such as blood pressure, heart rhythm, oxygen saturation, urine output, fluid balance, and weight with the patient’s condition. Changes over time generally provide more context than a single reading.","change":"When less blood reaches the left ventricle, the chamber may appear to contract vigorously while containing little blood at the end of systole. This pattern can support possible hypovolemia, but does not establish it alone. In a ventilated patient, positive pressure can make the inferior vena cava look full despite reduced effective filling.","cues":"A hyperdynamic-looking left ventricle with little visible blood at the end of systole may occur alongside a full-looking inferior vena cava. Hypotension and reduced urine output are additional concerns because they may accompany impaired perfusion. Interpret these observations together and in context, not as proof of a single cause.","distinction":"A full-looking inferior vena cava does not by itself establish adequate circulating volume, particularly during positive-pressure ventilation. Central venous pressure and pulmonary artery occlusion pressure are pressure measures, not direct measurements of volume, and their interpretation depends on conditions.","title":"Left Ventricular Filling","aliases":["central venous pressure","pulmonary artery occlusion pressure","tissue perfusion","fluid balance"],"prerequisite":"preload","related":["preload","perfusion","hypotension","oliguria","stroke-volume","hypoperfusion"],"clinicalBridge":"Preload describes the heart’s filling before contraction. In this case, the left ventricle’s vigorous contraction with little visible blood at end-systole can support possible low filling, while hypotension and reduced urine output raise concern about perfusion. A full-looking vena cava during ventilation does not settle the question; the combined pattern and its trend matter.","readingAim":"If the inferior vena cava looks full during ventilation, what other bedside finding could suggest reduced left ventricular filling?","id":"focus-s001","skill":"s001","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/monitoring-and-testing-the-critical-care-patient","title":"Monitoring and Testing the Critical Care Patient"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK6895/","title":"Hemodynamic monitoring - Surgical Treatment - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"definition":"Acute coronary syndromes are urgent conditions in which blood flow to heart muscle suddenly becomes inadequate, often after a coronary artery event. A myocardial infarction (MI) occurs when ischemia—too little oxygen-rich blood—causes heart-muscle cells to die.","structure":"The coronary arteries run over the heart and branch into smaller vessels that supply the myocardium, the heart’s muscular wall. Their blood carries oxygen to working heart cells. In acute coronary syndromes, the key site is the coronary artery–myocardium supply connection; electrocardiogram (ECG) changes reflect altered electrical activity in affected muscle.","normal":"Normally, open coronary vessels deliver oxygen-rich blood to the myocardium as it contracts and relaxes. Heart cells use that oxygen to make energy and maintain orderly electrical activation and pumping. When supply meets demand, the tissue remains viable and ECG patterns and cardiac troponin—a heart-cell protein measured in blood—show no evolving injury.","change":"An abrupt reduction in coronary flow deprives downstream myocardium of oxygen. Ischemia first disrupts cell function and electrical behavior; if severe or prolonged, cells die and release troponin into blood. ST-segment elevation or other evolving ECG changes may appear before troponin rises, so a single early normal result cannot rule out evolving infarction.","cues":"Pressure, breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or unusual weakness may reflect ischemia, but symptoms can be mild, atypical, or absent. Repeat ECGs may show ST elevation or depression, or inverted T waves (the recovery-wave pattern); troponin may rise later. Concerning symptoms and ECG changes call for urgent team evaluation.","distinction":"STEMI and NSTEMI are infarction categories, not simply two ECG patterns. NSTEMI involves acute ischemic myocardial injury without persistent ST elevation; the ECG can be nondiagnostic. An initially normal troponin does not exclude an evolving MI.","title":"Acute Coronary Syndromes","aliases":["acute coronary syndrome","ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction","non–ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction","myocardial infarction"],"prerequisite":"coronary","related":["coronary","ischemia","perfusion","conduction"],"clinicalBridge":"Link symptoms and repeat ECG findings to the Coronary arteries and myocardium: reduced flow can impair electrical activity before cell death is measurable in blood. A concerning pattern therefore calls for prompt communication and team evaluation under local protocols; biomarker trends add evidence but should not be treated as a reason to wait.","readingAim":"If coronary flow remains reduced while the first troponin is normal, what bedside change might appear next?","id":"focus-s002","skill":"s002","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/acute-myocardial-infarction-mi","title":"Acute Myocardial Infarction (MI) - Cardiology"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/coronary-artery-disease/acute-myocardial-infarction-mi?media=print","title":"Acute Myocardial Infarction (MI) - The Merck Manuals"}]},{"definition":"Cardiogenic shock is dangerously poor tissue blood flow because a primary heart problem reduces effective cardiac output. The heart cannot send enough oxygen-carrying blood to meet tissues’ needs.","structure":"Focus: Cardiogenic shock. The heart’s ventricles pump blood forward; the myocardium is the muscle that creates this push. Electrical signals travel through the conduction system to coordinate contraction, while valves guide blood forward. Injury, rhythm disruption, or acute valve damage can weaken effective output.","normal":"Normally, heart muscle contracts in a coordinated rhythm, and the ventricles eject an adequate volume with each beat (stroke volume). Valves open and close to guide blood forward. This maintains cardiac output—the blood pumped each minute—and oxygen delivery to the brain, kidneys, and other tissues.","change":"If the myocardium receives too little blood (ischemia), it may contract poorly; an abnormal rhythm can disrupt filling and coordinated ejection, and acute valve damage can send blood backward. Any of these problems can reduce effective cardiac output, lowering tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery. Cells then function poorly, and lactate may rise.","cues":"Reduced brain and kidney perfusion may appear as new confusion and declining urine output; compensatory narrowing of skin vessels can make hands cool and clammy, with weak rapid pulses and faster breathing. Elevated lactate may accompany poor perfusion but does not measure tissue oxygen use directly; production and clearance both matter. Near-usual blood pressure does not rule out shock; interpret findings with the suspected cardiac trigger.","distinction":"Do not equate shock with hypotension alone: perfusion failure can be present while blood pressure remains near baseline. Distinguish a primary heart-muscle, rhythm, or valve problem from mechanical obstruction to cardiac filling or emptying, which is obstructive shock.","title":"Cardiogenic Shock","aliases":["cardiogenic shock","primary cardiac disorder","reduction in cardiac output due to a primary cardiac disorder"],"prerequisite":"output","related":["output","perfusion","ischemia","conduction"],"clinicalBridge":"For bedside reasoning, connect impaired forward flow from the heart to the organs that depend on it: reduced cardiac output may appear as confusion, cool clammy hands, weak rapid pulses, falling urine output, or elevated lactate. Chest pressure and a new rhythm abnormality alongside these changes make urgent team communication and an ABCDE assessment important, even without new hypotension.","readingAim":"If cardiac output falls while blood pressure stays near baseline, which early signs of reduced organ perfusion might appear?","id":"focus-s003","skill":"s003","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"title":"Lactate monitoring in intensive care","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11379417/"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?ruleredirectid=748","title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"definition":"Acute heart failure is a sudden onset or worsening of the heart’s inability to meet the body’s needs. It can cause poor forward blood flow, raised pressure behind the heart, or both.","structure":"The left ventricle is the heart chamber that pumps blood into the aorta. When its filling pressure rises, pressure is transmitted backward through the left atrium and pulmonary veins to lung capillaries, the tiny vessels beside the alveoli, where oxygen passes into the blood.","normal":"Normally, the left ventricle fills and ejects blood effectively, keeping its filling pressure low enough for blood to flow through lung capillaries without flooding lung tissue. The alveoli remain mostly air-filled, supporting oxygen transfer. Cardiac assessment helps identify impaired function and possible causes, including cardiomyopathy, a disease of the heart muscle.","change":"In acute heart failure, impaired pumping or filling raises pressure within the left ventricle. That pressure backs up into lung vessels, pushing fluid into the interstitial tissue and sometimes the alveoli. Fluid-filled lung tissue makes oxygen transfer harder and increases breathing effort. This can occur even when the ejection fraction—the proportion of blood ejected with each beat—is preserved.","cues":"Breathlessness, rapid breathing, and crackles may reflect fluid in or around the alveoli; wheezing can also occur with pulmonary edema. Restlessness, cyanosis, or frothy sputum may signal severe respiratory compromise. Jugular venous distention, leg swelling, or an S3 may add evidence of congestion, but no single finding establishes the cause.","distinction":"Wheezing does not rule out pulmonary edema or prove COPD; cardiac congestion can narrow airways. Likewise, a normal ejection fraction or BNP result does not by itself exclude heart failure.","title":"Acute Heart Failure","aliases":["acute decompensated heart failure","new-onset heart failure","pulmonary edema","heart failure with preserved ejection fraction"],"prerequisite":"left-ventricle","related":["left-ventricle","edema","alveoli","oxygenation"],"clinicalBridge":"When left ventricular filling pressure rises, fluid can move from lung capillaries into surrounding tissue and the alveoli, disrupting Alveoli and gas exchange. Breathlessness and crackles are bedside cues of this possible change; wheezing does not exclude a cardiac cause. These observations support prompt escalation while the care team evaluates the cause and response.","readingAim":"If pressure rises in the left ventricle, what changes first at the lung capillary–alveolar interface, and what might the nurse observe?","id":"focus-s004","skill":"s004","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/acute-heart-failure","title":"Acute Heart Failure - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/pulmonary-edema","title":"Pulmonary Edema - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"definition":"Atrial fibrillation is an abnormal rhythm in which the atria activate without an orderly repeating pattern. Its bedside importance depends partly on whether the resulting heartbeat affects the person’s perfusion, or delivery of blood to tissues.","structure":"The sinoatrial node normally starts an impulse that spreads through the atrial muscle, pauses briefly at the atrioventricular node, then travels through the His bundle and ventricular conduction pathways. An ECG records this electrical activity at the body surface; the P wave reflects atrial activation and the QRS complex reflects ventricular activation.","normal":"Normally, an impulse spreads in an organized way from the sinoatrial node through the atria to the atrioventricular node, then to the ventricles. The atria and ventricles contract in sequence. On the ECG, atrial activation appears as a P wave before each related QRS complex, with a consistent pattern.","change":"In atrial fibrillation, many disorganized electrical activations occur in the atria instead of one coordinated wave, so distinct repeating P waves are absent. The atrioventricular node conducts some impulses to the ventricles at irregular intervals, often producing an irregular rhythm with narrow QRS complexes. Less coordinated atrial contraction and an irregular ventricular response may affect cardiac output.","cues":"The monitor may show an irregular rhythm, narrow QRS complexes, and no distinct repeating P waves. The person may report palpitations, dyspnea, chest discomfort, lightheadedness, or fainting. These findings do not by themselves establish impaired perfusion; consider the rhythm alongside the person’s symptoms and observed condition.","distinction":"A monitor pattern helps describe electrical activity, but it does not show by itself whether perfusion is impaired. Likewise, lightheadedness alone does not prove that an arrhythmia is causing poor perfusion.","title":"Atrial Fibrillation and Perfusion","aliases":["atrial fibrillation","irregular with narrow QRS complexes","no distinct, repeating P waves"],"prerequisite":"conduction","related":["conduction","perfusion","output","hypoperfusion"],"clinicalBridge":"The atrioventricular node is the gate between atrial signals and ventricular activation; during atrial fibrillation, irregular signals reaching the ventricles can make the heartbeat irregular. Because this may affect cardiac output, bedside learning pairs ECG interpretation with attention to symptoms and the person’s observed condition, rather than relying on the monitor label alone.","readingAim":"If atrial signals become disorganized, what changes might you expect to see on the ECG and notice in the person?","id":"focus-s005","skill":"s005","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias-and-conduction-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias","title":"Overview of Arrhythmias - Cardiology - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias-and-conduction-disorders/overview-of-arrhythmias?mredirectid=1662&autoredirectid=20568&qt=arrhythmias&alt=sh","title":"Overview of Arrhythmias - Cardiovascular Disorders - Merck Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"definition":"Aortic emergencies are dangerous changes in the aorta, the body’s largest artery. An aneurysm is a weak area that bulges outward; a dissection is a tear that lets blood split the wall’s layers.","structure":"The aorta carries blood from the heart through the chest and abdomen. Its wall has an inner lining, a middle layer, and an outer layer. The wall contains and directs blood under pressure; branches from the aorta carry blood to organs and limbs.","normal":"Normally, the aortic wall stays intact as blood flows through the vessel and into its branches. The layers remain joined, and the aorta does not balloon outward. Blood reaches downstream tissues through open branches, supporting their oxygen supply and function.","change":"In an aneurysm, a weak section of the aortic wall stretches outward under blood pressure; a rupture is a break that allows blood to escape into surrounding tissues. In a dissection, a tear in the inner lining allows blood to separate wall layers and form a false channel. A clot in an aneurysm may also travel and block an artery.","cues":"Sudden, severe pain with findings concerning for shock in someone with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm raises concern for rupture, but no single sign proves it. Shock means the circulation is not delivering enough blood to tissues. Sudden, excruciating tearing or ripping pain is a recognized dissection cue; blocked branches can affect the tissues they supply.","distinction":"An aneurysm is a bulge; a dissection is blood separating layers of the wall. They are different processes, and pain alone does not establish which one is present.","title":"Aortic Wall Emergencies","aliases":["aortic aneurysm","abdominal aortic aneurysm","aortic dissection","aneurysm rupture"],"prerequisite":"aorta","related":["aorta","shock","perfusion","hypotension"],"clinicalBridge":"In the case, a weak abdominal aortic wall may rupture, allowing blood to escape and reducing the blood available to perfuse tissues. This links the vessel’s physical change to shock. Sudden deterioration with severe pain is a reason for urgent clinical escalation in this educational scenario, rather than routine observation; the pattern raises concern but does not confirm rupture.","readingAim":"If blood escapes from a weakened aortic wall, what change would you expect in tissue perfusion?","id":"focus-s006","skill":"s006","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/aneurysms-and-aortic-dissection/overview-of-aortic-aneurysms-and-aortic-dissection?ruleredirectid=745","title":"Overview of Aortic Aneurysms and Aortic Dissection - Overview of Aortic Aneurysms and Aortic Dissection - MSD Manual Consumer Version"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/diseases-of-the-aorta-and-its-branches/overview-of-aortic-aneurysms-and-aortic-dissection?ruleredirectid=742autoredirectid=25832","title":"Overview of Aortic Aneurysms and Aortic Dissection - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders - MSD Manual Consumer Version"}]},{"definition":"Cardiac tamponade is pressure on the heart caused by blood or other fluid collecting in the pericardial sac. The pressure limits heart filling, lowers cardiac output, and may lead to shock.","structure":"The heart sits inside the pericardial sac, a two-layered covering. In cardiac tamponade, traumatic blood collects in the space around the heart, where pressure can compress the cardiac chambers. Those chambers normally fill with blood before each contraction; compression limits their expansion.","normal":"Normally, blood fills the heart’s chambers before each contraction, and the heart pumps blood onward to support tissue perfusion. The pericardial sac surrounds the heart without preventing chamber expansion, allowing filling and cardiac output to be maintained.","change":"With traumatic bleeding, blood can collect rapidly in the pericardial sac. Rising pressure compresses the heart and restricts chamber filling, so less blood is pumped with each beat. Cardiac output can fall, causing worsening circulation and potentially shock; rapid accumulation can be dangerous even when the fluid volume is relatively small.","cues":"After a penetrating wound near the heart, worsening hypotension and pallor raise concern, especially when circulation is deteriorating. Muffled heart tones or distended neck veins may occur, but the full triad is often absent; hypovolemia can hide neck vein distention. Markedly reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance suggest tension pneumothorax as another possibility.","distinction":"Do not rule out tamponade because one or more parts of Beck’s triad are absent, or diagnose it from a single sign. Consider the injury context and the overall pattern; bedside imaging can help clarify the cause.","title":"Traumatic Cardiac Tamponade","aliases":["cardiac tamponade","pericardial tamponade"],"prerequisite":"pericardium","related":["pericardium","preload","output","shock"],"clinicalBridge":"In penetrating chest trauma, blood can build pressure around the heart and restrict chamber filling. This reduces preload (the filling of the heart before contraction), which can lower stroke volume and cardiac output and contribute to worsening hypotension. Connecting this pattern to Preload and venous return helps explain why absent neck vein distention does not rule out tamponade.","readingAim":"If the heart’s chambers are compressed by rapidly collecting blood, what bedside change might follow before every classic sign appears?","id":"focus-s007","skill":"s007","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/cardiac-tamponade","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/injuries-and-poisoning/chest-injuries/cardiac-tamponade","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries and Poisoning - Merck Manual ..."}]},{"definition":"Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid under pressure in the sac around the heart restricts the heart’s filling. Reduced filling can lower blood flow from the heart and lead to shock.","structure":"The pericardium is the membrane-lined sac around the heart. Its space can hold fluid, while the heart chambers must relax and fill during diastole, the resting phase between beats. After surgery, blood or other fluid may collect in this sac.","normal":"Normally, a small amount of fluid in the pericardial sac allows the heart to move with little friction. During diastole, the chambers expand and receive blood. Adequate filling supports stroke volume, the blood ejected per beat, and cardiac output, the blood pumped each minute.","change":"When blood or fluid builds up under pressure, the sac restricts chamber expansion. Less blood enters during diastole, reducing preload, the filling and stretch before contraction, then stroke volume and cardiac output. Rapid accumulation after surgery can become dangerous with much less fluid than slow accumulation, before the sac can stretch.","cues":"Falling blood pressure or worsening perfusion alongside quiet, or muffled, heart sounds raises concern; trends matter more than an isolated reading. Neck-vein distention may occur but can be absent, particularly with low circulating volume. The classic three findings are often incomplete or hard to hear in a noisy unit.","distinction":"A visible pericardial effusion alone is not equivalent to tamponade; the key issue is impaired filling and its effect on circulation. The absence of neck-vein distention or a complete classic triad does not rule it out.","title":"Cardiac Tamponade After Surgery","aliases":["cardiac tamponade","pericardial effusion with impaired filling","Beck's triad","bedside echocardiography"],"prerequisite":"preload","related":["preload","output","hypotension","perfusion"],"clinicalBridge":"Connect this bedside pattern to Preload and venous return: restricted filling reduces preload, which can lower Cardiac output and then tissue perfusion. In postoperative assessment, interpret changing vital signs and examination findings together rather than treating a quiet heart sound or an image of fluid as proof by itself. These observations support prompt team evaluation, not a stand-alone diagnosis.","readingAim":"If pressure builds around the heart and chamber filling falls, what changes might follow in blood pressure and tissue perfusion?","id":"focus-s008","skill":"s008","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/cardiac-tamponade","title":"Cardiac Tamponade - Injuries; Poisoning"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/monitoring-and-testing-the-critical-care-patient?ruleredirectid=749","title":"Monitoring and Testing the Critical Care Patient - Critical Care Medicine - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"definition":"Retroperitoneal bleeding is blood loss into the space behind the abdominal lining after femoral catheter access. It can become serious before blood is visible at the groin.","structure":"The femoral artery is a large vessel in the upper thigh, beneath the inguinal ligament, which separates the abdomen from the groin. A puncture above this ligament can allow blood to enter the retroperitoneal space, a deep area behind the abdominal lining where blood may collect without obvious groin swelling.","normal":"After femoral access, the puncture site normally seals as hemostasis—the stopping of bleeding—occurs. Bedside assessment compares findings with the postprocedure baseline and follows trends in vital signs, the groin and dressing, distal perfusion, and urine output. These observations can reveal a change even when the skin looks unchanged.","change":"If the femoral artery wall is disrupted or hemostasis is impaired, blood may escape from the vessel. With a high puncture, it can track into the retroperitoneal space instead of collecting visibly at the groin. Continued hidden blood loss can reduce circulating volume and destabilize blood pressure.","cues":"A falling blood pressure, pallor, or lightheadedness after femoral access—especially together with new back discomfort—raises concern for hidden bleeding. A clean dressing and little or no groin swelling do not exclude it. Interpret these findings as a pattern and change from baseline, not as proof from one sign alone.","distinction":"Do not assume that a clean groin dressing rules out bleeding; blood can collect internally. Back pain alone does not establish the cause, so consider it alongside access history, vital-sign trends, and other findings.","title":"Retroperitoneal Bleeding","aliases":["retroperitoneal bleeding","retroperitoneal bleed","femoral access"],"prerequisite":"arterial","related":["arterial","platelets","hypotension","perfusion"],"clinicalBridge":"After catheterization, compare findings with baseline and follow trends. A pulsatile mass with a bruit suggests pseudoaneurysm; a continuous bruit or thrill suggests an arteriovenous connection. A cool, painful, discolored hand after radial access raises concern about Tissue perfusion. Falling pressure with back pain after femoral access can signal hidden bleeding and warrants prompt escalation through local procedures.","readingAim":"How could femoral access lead to falling blood pressure while the groin still looks normal?","id":"focus-s009","skill":"s009","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531461/","title":"Cardiac Catheterization Risks and Complications - StatPearls ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/cardiovascular-tests-and-procedures/cardiac-catheterization","title":"Cardiac Catheterization - Cardiology - Merck Manual ..."}]},{"definition":"Ventricular pre-excitation occurs when an extra electrical connection between an atrium and a ventricle activates part of the ventricle earlier than the usual route. In Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW), this pathway can also support a looping signal that reactivates the heart and causes a rapid rhythm.","structure":"The focus is the accessory atrioventricular connection, a strand of conducting tissue joining an atrium to ventricular muscle outside the usual route through the atrioventricular node. It enters the ventricular myocardium, the heart’s contracting tissue, and can carry impulses toward or back from the ventricle.","normal":"Normally, an electrical impulse begins in the heart’s natural pacemaker, spreads across the atria, and pauses briefly at the atrioventricular node. It then travels through the His-Purkinje conduction network to activate the ventricles in an organized sequence, supporting coordinated pumping.","change":"When the extra connection carries an impulse toward the ventricle, it bypasses the usual delay and activates part of the ventricular muscle early. The pathway may also form a reentry loop, in which an impulse repeatedly travels around a circuit. If atrial fibrillation reaches the ventricles rapidly through the pathway, ventricular fibrillation may result.","cues":"An ECG may show a short PR interval, widened QRS, and delta wave, an early slur at the start of the QRS. Sudden, regular palpitations can accompany reentry; near-fainting adds concern. An irregular, very rapid, wide-complex rhythm is a particularly dangerous change to recognize and communicate.","distinction":"Do not confuse WPW with long QT syndrome: WPW involves an extra atrioventricular route and early ventricular activation, while long QT involves prolonged ventricular repolarization. ECG clues and risk contexts differ, and a single finding alone does not establish either condition.","title":"Ventricular Pre-excitation","aliases":["Wolff-Parkinson-White","WPW","accessory atrioventricular connection","manifest WPW"],"prerequisite":"conduction","related":["conduction","potassium","perfusion","output"],"clinicalBridge":"Cardiac conduction links the ECG pattern to the tissue change: the delta wave reflects early ventricular activation through the accessory pathway. At the bedside, connecting that pattern with abrupt palpitations or near-fainting helps frame clear clinician communication; a new irregular, very rapid, wide-complex rhythm signals a more urgent risk than the baseline pattern alone.","readingAim":"If an accessory pathway activates ventricular muscle early, what ECG pattern and rhythm change would you expect?","id":"focus-s010","skill":"s010","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/arrhythmogenic-cardiac-disorders/long-qt-interval-syndromes","title":"Long QT Interval Syndromes - Cardiology"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/specific-cardiac-arrhythmias/wolff-parkinson-white-syndrome-wpw-syndrome","title":"Wolff-Parkinson-White Syndrome (WPW Syndrome) - The Merck Manuals"}]},{"definition":"Infective endocarditis is an infection of the heart’s inner lining that often involves a valve. It can damage the valve and send infected material into the bloodstream.","structure":"The focus is the prosthetic heart valve and the tissue around its attachment, called the valve ring. The valve’s leaflets open to let blood pass and close to limit backward flow. The valve surface and adjacent endocardium, the heart’s inner lining, meet the moving blood.","normal":"Normally, the prosthetic valve stays securely attached, its leaflets open and close to support one-way blood flow, and the surrounding endocardium remains uninfected. Bloodstream microbes are generally cleared rather than settling and multiplying on the valve. Comparing new observations with the patient’s documented murmur and baseline status can reveal change.","change":"If microbes circulate in the blood, damaged or artificial valve surfaces are more likely than healthy tissue to let them adhere. They can multiply with clot material into vegetations, which are growths on the valve. Vegetations may impair closure or obstruct flow, damage nearby tissue, or break away as emboli—traveling material that can block a blood vessel.","cues":"A gradual course may include mild fever, fatigue, sweating, and weight loss; a new or changed murmur suggests altered valve function. Small skin or eye spots and sudden neurologic, abdominal, or limb symptoms may reflect emboli. Breathing difficulty or weakness can accompany valve-related heart failure. No single cue confirms infection.","distinction":"A mild or gradual presentation does not make endocarditis routine, especially with a prosthetic valve. A changed murmur or fever raises concern but does not establish the diagnosis by itself.","title":"Prosthetic Valve Endocarditis","aliases":["infective endocarditis","prosthetic valve endocarditis","subacute infective endocarditis"],"prerequisite":"valves","related":["valves","conduction","perfusion","inflammation"],"clinicalBridge":"Cardiac valves direct blood forward, so infection that disrupts a prosthetic valve can cause leakage and strain the heart. Nearby spread may affect the conduction system, which coordinates the heartbeat. Emboli can reduce perfusion in distant organs. In this lesson, gradual symptoms plus a changed murmur in a person with a prosthetic valve warrant prompt clinical attention, not assumption of a confirmed diagnosis.","readingAim":"How might a vegetation on a prosthetic valve change blood flow or produce findings beyond the heart?","id":"focus-s011","skill":"s011","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/endocarditis/infective-endocarditis","title":"Infective Endocarditis - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/endocarditis/infective-endocarditis","title":"Infective Endocarditis - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"definition":"Pericarditis is inflammation of the sac around the heart, sometimes accompanied by fluid buildup. If fluid pressure becomes high enough to impair heart filling, cardiac tamponade can reduce the amount of blood pumped with each beat.","structure":"The pericardium is a two-layered sac around the heart. Its inner layer lies against the heart muscle, and its outer layer forms a protective envelope. A small space between the layers contains fluid that helps the heart move smoothly as it beats.","normal":"Normally, a small amount of fluid between the pericardial layers reduces friction as the heart moves. The sac accommodates the heart’s usual movement while the chambers relax and fill, then contract to pump blood. The heart muscle itself generates the force for that pumping.","change":"Inflammation irritates the pericardial layers and may lead to fluid accumulation in the space between them. If fluid collects rapidly or becomes substantial, pressure can restrict the heart’s expansion—especially during filling. Less filling can mean less blood is pumped with each beat; myocarditis can separately weaken the heart muscle or disturb its rhythm.","cues":"Sharp or dull chest pain that worsens with breathing, coughing, or movement and eases when sitting forward can fit a pericarditis pattern. A scratchy friction rub may come and go. Increasing breathlessness, tachycardia, hypotension, or distended neck veins raise concern for deterioration but do not establish its cause alone.","distinction":"A positional pain pattern or a single friction rub does not prove pericarditis or show that the illness is mild. Myocarditis affects the heart muscle, while pericarditis affects the surrounding sac; both may occur together.","title":"Cardiac Tamponade","aliases":["pericarditis","myocarditis","pericardial effusion","pericardial friction rub","cardiac tamponade"],"prerequisite":"pericardium","related":["pericardium","output","perfusion","conduction"],"clinicalBridge":"At the bedside, connect changes in breathing, blood pressure, pulse, and neck veins with the possibility that pressure around the heart is limiting chamber filling—not with a diagnosis based on one sign. Reduced filling can lower Cardiac output. Myocarditis adds a different concern: injured heart muscle may pump less effectively or produce rhythm changes.","readingAim":"If fluid pressure around the heart rises, what change in chamber filling would you expect to affect the patient’s circulation?","id":"focus-s012","skill":"s012","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/myocarditis-and-pericarditis/pericarditis","title":"Pericarditis - Cardiology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/heart-and-blood-vessel-disorders/pericardial-disease-and-myocarditis/myocarditis","title":"Myocarditis - Heart and Blood Vessel Disorders - Merck Manual ..."}]},{"definition":"A hypertensive emergency is markedly raised blood pressure with acute injury to an organ such as the brain, heart, kidneys, eyes, or aorta. The organ injury—not the reading alone—makes it an emergency.","structure":"Focus: cerebral autoregulation. Small brain arteries, called arterioles, adjust their width to help steady blood flow. Their inner lining helps regulate what passes from blood into brain tissue. The optic disc, where the optic nerve meets the back of the eye, can show swelling associated with increased pressure inside the skull.","normal":"Normally, brain arterioles narrow or widen as blood pressure changes, helping maintain cerebral perfusion—the flow of blood through brain tissue. This regulation supports a steady supply of oxygen while limiting excessive pressure on delicate vessels. The vessel lining also helps keep fluid from moving freely from the bloodstream into brain tissue.","change":"When pressure rises sharply beyond the brain’s ability to regulate flow, stress can injure vessel linings and make them more permeable. Fluid may then leak into brain tissue, causing swelling and impaired function. Longstanding hypertension can shift the range the brain tolerates, so abruptly lowering pressure may also reduce blood flow to vulnerable tissue.","cues":"Confusion and blurred vision with papilledema—swelling of the optic disc—are concerning for acute brain-related injury, but do not prove its cause. Assessments may include neurologic and eye examinations and tests for organ involvement. Focal weakness or other new focal deficits raise concern for stroke and need evaluation as a distinct possibility.","distinction":"A markedly elevated reading or anxiety alone does not establish a hypertensive emergency; acute target-organ injury is central to the definition. Confusion and papilledema are concerning findings, not proof of one specific diagnosis, so stroke and other causes also need consideration.","title":"Hypertensive Emergency","aliases":["acute severe hypertension","hypertensive crisis","hypertensive encephalopathy"],"prerequisite":"cerebral","related":["cerebral","map","icp","hypoperfusion"],"clinicalBridge":"In the case, confusion and blurred vision alongside papilledema raise concern, but no single finding proves the cause. Connect these observations to Cerebral perfusion: brain tissue needs steady blood flow, and excess pressure can disrupt vessel function and contribute to swelling. Prompt escalation and monitored evaluation support assessment for this injury and alternatives such as stroke.","readingAim":"Before reading, what bedside changes might follow if brain vessels can no longer steady blood flow?","id":"focus-s013","skill":"s013","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/hypertension/hypertensive-emergencies","title":"Hypertensive Emergencies - Cardiology - Merck Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470371/","title":"Hypertensive Emergency - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH"}]},{"definition":"Post-revascularization limb surveillance means observing a limb after a procedure restores blood flow to tissue that had too little. It also includes watching for local muscle injury and effects on the rest of the body.","structure":"The calf contains skeletal muscles enclosed in groups by fascia, tough connective tissue. Each group forms a compartment that allows muscles to move the ankle and toes. Small blood vessels supply these muscles; nearby nerves carry sensation and help control movement.","normal":"After flow is restored, the limb should be assessed against its documented baseline and the opposite side. Observations include pain, color, temperature, swelling, sensation, movement, and pulses or Doppler signals. A Doppler is an ultrasound tool used to detect blood flow; repeated comparisons help reveal change.","change":"Restored flow can expose injured muscle to circulation, carrying potassium, myoglobin—a muscle protein—and other substances into the blood. This may disturb body chemistry and affect organs. Locally, fluid can leak into the calf and raise pressure inside a compartment, squeezing vessels and nerves and impairing muscle function.","cues":"A developing pattern of increasing pain, pain when the limb is gently stretched, tense swelling, tingling, or weakness raises concern for compartment syndrome. A worsening pulse or Doppler signal may indicate impaired flow. Systemic decline or urine changes can accompany muscle injury; no single finding confirms its cause.","distinction":"An audible Doppler signal does not rule out compartment syndrome. Interpret changing findings together and in relation to baseline rather than treating one sign as proof of a diagnosis.","title":"Post-Revascularization Limb Surveillance","aliases":["limb perfusion after revascularization","ischemia–reperfusion injury","compartment syndrome","myonephropathic metabolic syndrome"],"prerequisite":"perfusion","related":["perfusion","compartment","myoglobin","potassium"],"clinicalBridge":"Fascia and compartment pressure help connect the anatomy to bedside observation: swelling within the calf’s firm muscle groups can compress small vessels and nerves even while a Doppler signal remains audible. Comparing pain, stretch response, sensation, movement, and swelling over time helps identify a concerning pattern for prompt team review.","readingAim":"If blood flow returns to injured calf muscle, what local and whole-body changes might become visible at the bedside?","id":"focus-s014","skill":"s014","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13212160/","title":"Revascularization for Acute Limb Ischemia - PMC"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/peripheral-artery-disorders/acute-limb-ischemia","title":"Acute Limb Ischemia - Cardiology - Merck Manual Professional ..."}]},{"definition":"IABP access-side limb perfusion concern is a possible reduction in blood supply to the leg associated with the catheter’s arterial access route. It warrants attention even when the pump tracing shows diastolic augmentation.","structure":"The IABP catheter enters an artery, often the femoral artery in the groin, and its balloon sits in the aorta. The femoral artery carries oxygen-rich blood into the leg; the access site and vessels downstream toward the foot are the device–body interface being assessed.","normal":"With adequate limb circulation, the femoral artery and its branches deliver oxygenated blood to the foot, supporting warmth, color, sensation, and palpable pulses. The balloon inflates after aortic valve closure in diastole and deflates before systole. Waveform augmentation reflects timed pressure support, while limb findings are assessed separately.","change":"A catheter at the arterial access route can be associated with impaired flow to the limb, and vascular complications may occur even when the IABP tracing shows diastolic augmentation. The tracing describes pressure timing, not proof that blood reaches the foot adequately. Individual responses to support vary, so limb and whole-patient perfusion cues remain meaningful.","cues":"A foot cooler or paler than the other, a weaker pulse on the access side, or altered sensation can signal reduced distal circulation, especially when findings change or occur together; none alone proves a cause. Access-site bleeding is another complication cue. Signs of hypoperfusion may persist despite an augmented tracing.","distinction":"Do not treat an augmented diastolic waveform as proof of adequate coronary or limb blood flow. Interpret it alongside the patient’s findings and device signals; isolated limb changes raise concern but do not establish the cause.","title":"IABP Access-Side Perfusion","aliases":["access-side vascular compromise","vascular compromise","IABP support"],"prerequisite":"perfusion","related":["perfusion","arterial","hypoperfusion","afterload"],"clinicalBridge":"IABP support changes aortic pressure timing and can reduce left ventricular afterload, but the access-side femoral artery is also a route to the leg. Relate the tracing to cardiac support, then compare distal limb color, temperature, pulse, and sensation: this is tissue perfusion at the device–body interface, not something a waveform alone can verify.","readingAim":"If diastolic augmentation is visible but the access-side foot becomes cooler and paler, what might that suggest about blood flow to the limb?","id":"focus-s015","skill":"s015","kind":"clinical","unit":"cardio","sources":[{"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11307388/","title":"The Intra-aortic Balloon Pump: A Focused Review of ... - PMC"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK542233/?report=reader","title":"Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump - StatPearls"}]},{"definition":"Oxygenation moves oxygen from the lungs into arterial blood. Ventilation moves carbon dioxide out of the body through breathing.","structure":"Focus: Oxygenation versus ventilation. In the lungs, alveoli are tiny air sacs bordered by capillaries, the small blood vessels where gas exchange occurs. Air reaches the alveoli through the airways; oxygen crosses into blood, while carbon dioxide crosses from blood into alveolar air to be breathed out.","normal":"With healthy, open alveoli, each breath brings fresh air close to blood flowing through surrounding capillaries. Oxygen enters the blood and is carried in arteries to the body. At the same time, carbon dioxide moves from blood into the alveoli and leaves with exhalation. These functions are related but distinct.","change":"In pneumonia, inflamed alveoli may fill with fluid or material, and some may collapse. If a region still receives some air but too little for its blood flow, it has a low ventilation-to-perfusion ratio. Blood passing an entirely unventilated region represents shunt. Extra oxygen may not fully correct this mismatch. Ventilatory failure, by contrast, means carbon dioxide removal is inadequate; either problem, or both, may occur.","cues":"Low oxygen saturation, especially with airspace opacities on imaging, raises concern for impaired oxygenation. Blood gas measurements help assess both arterial oxygen and carbon dioxide. Breathlessness, rapid breathing, accessory muscle use, restlessness, or confusion signal concern, but none alone proves whether oxygenation, ventilation, or both are impaired.","distinction":"Do not treat oxygen saturation as a complete picture of breathing: it reflects oxygen status, not carbon dioxide elimination. Clinical signs may signal deterioration without identifying which function is affected.","title":"Oxygenation versus Ventilation","aliases":["acute hypoxemic respiratory failure","ventilatory failure","intrapulmonary shunting","carbon dioxide elimination"],"prerequisite":"vq","related":["vq","alveoli","oxygenation","ventilation","gas-gradient","cells"],"clinicalBridge":"In the pneumonia case, airspace filling interferes with gas exchange at the alveoli, so low saturation and opacities point toward an oxygenation concern. Restlessness and increased breathing effort indicate worsening distress but do not establish carbon dioxide retention. Blood gas results add information about both oxygen and carbon dioxide.","readingAim":"If blood still flows past fluid-filled alveoli, what might happen to oxygen transfer and the response to added oxygen?","id":"focus-s016","skill":"s016","kind":"clinical","unit":"resp","sources":[{"title":"Physiology of pulmonary ventilation and perfusion","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539907/"},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/acute-hypoxemic-respiratory-failure-ahrf-including-acute-respiratory-distress-syndrome-ards","title":"Acute Hypoxemic Respiratory Failure (AHRF), Including ..."},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/overview-of-respiratory-failure","title":"Overview of Respiratory Failure - Critical Care Medicine"}]},{"definition":"Mechanical ventilation pushes gas into the lungs with positive pressure, and airway pressure reflects both resistance to airflow and the stretch of the lungs and chest wall. Comparing peak pressure with plateau pressure, measured when airflow briefly stops, helps distinguish these contributions.","structure":"Focus: Ventilator pressure mechanics. The endotracheal tube and conducting airways carry delivered gas toward the alveoli; lung tissue and the chest wall stretch as they fill and recoil during exhalation. The ventilator pressure display and flow tracing show how this device–body pathway responds during each breath.","normal":"During a breath, gas flows through the tube and airways into the lungs, so peak pressure includes pressure needed to overcome airflow resistance as well as pressure to stretch the lungs and chest wall. In a passive patient, briefly stopping airflow during an inspiratory hold gives a plateau pressure that reflects the elastic component. Expiration normally finishes before the next breath.","change":"With obstructed or narrowed airways, resistance to incoming flow can rise, increasing peak pressure while plateau pressure remains relatively unchanged. If exhalation is incomplete, air remains trapped in the lungs, creating intrinsic PEEP, or pressure left behind at the end of expiration. This can increase breathing effort and intrathoracic pressure, potentially reducing venous return and cardiac output.","cues":"A rising peak pressure with little change in plateau pressure suggests a greater resistive contribution, but does not identify its cause by itself. Expiratory flow that has not returned to baseline before the next breath suggests incomplete emptying. Visible increased effort adds a patient cue; airway, tube, and circuit findings provide context.","distinction":"A high peak pressure alone does not prove that lung compliance has worsened. Compare it with plateau pressure, and remember that patient effort can affect the interpretation of pressure measurements.","title":"Ventilator Pressure Mechanics","aliases":["peak airway pressure","plateau pressure","intrinsic PEEP","expiratory flow tracing"],"prerequisite":"airway","related":["airway","ventilation","peep","preload"],"clinicalBridge":"At the bedside, pressure values are most useful when interpreted alongside the patient’s visible effort and the ventilator’s flow tracing. For example, incomplete exhalation can leave intrinsic PEEP and increase pressure within the chest, which may affect Preload and venous return. Clear communication of the combined observations supports shared clinical assessment.","readingAim":"If exhalation remains incomplete before the next breath, what might happen to breathing effort and venous return?","id":"focus-s017","skill":"s017","kind":"clinical","unit":"resp","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/overview-of-mechanical-ventilation","title":"Overview of Mechanical Ventilation - Critical Care Medicine"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/ventilatory-failure","title":"Ventilatory Failure - Critical Care Medicine"}]},{"definition":"Prone positioning places a person face down to support breathing in acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), a sudden inflammatory lung injury. It can help distribute air more evenly among alveoli, the tiny air sacs where gas exchange occurs, and reduce damaging mechanical stress.","structure":"Within the lungs, alveoli sit beside capillaries, tiny blood vessels where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged. In ARDS, some alveoli fill with fluid or collapse. When a person lies on their back, this injury can be prominent in the posterior (back) lung regions; prone positioning changes the pressures and support around these regions.","normal":"Normally, air reaches alveoli while blood flows through nearby capillaries, allowing oxygen and carbon dioxide to be exchanged. For a ventilated patient with ARDS, care aims to support this exchange while limiting excessive lung stretch and repeated alveolar opening and collapse. Prone positioning involves coordinated turning, with attention to alignment, skin, the airway tube, and lines.","change":"In ARDS, injury to the barrier between alveoli and capillaries lets fluid enter air spaces, while uneven collapse leaves less lung available for ventilation. When prone, posterior lung tissue may be less compressed and air can spread more evenly, reopening some alveoli. This can improve ventilation-perfusion matching—the match between air reaching alveoli and blood flow nearby—and reduce regional overstretch and collapse.","cues":"The clinical pattern may include worsening oxygenation and bilateral lung opacities after sepsis or another acute illness, without a cardiac explanation for the edema. During prone care, the team observes oxygenation, breathing effort, ventilator pressures, blood pressure, skin pressure, and whether the airway tube and lines remain secure.","distinction":"A better oxygen reading after turning is not, by itself, proof of lung protection or improved survival; more even distribution of mechanical stress is an important proposed benefit. Hypoxemia or bilateral opacities alone do not establish ARDS.","title":"Prone Positioning in ARDS","aliases":["prone positioning","prone position","proning"],"prerequisite":"alveoli","related":["alveoli","vq","oxygenation","peep"],"clinicalBridge":"Prone positioning changes the pressure and shape around injured alveoli, which may allow air to reach more of the back regions while blood flow remains largely directed there. This can improve ventilation and perfusion matching—the alignment of airflow with blood flow—but the team still observes breathing, oxygenation, and device security; an improved oxygen reading alone does not show that lung stress is protected.","readingAim":"When a ventilated patient with ARDS is turned prone, what might happen to airflow distribution and lung stress?","id":"focus-s018","skill":"s018","kind":"clinical","unit":"resp","sources":[{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10965391","title":"Prone Positioning in Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK436002/","title":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"definition":"A pulmonary embolism is a blockage in a pulmonary artery, usually caused by a clot that traveled from a deep vein. It can strain the right side of the heart and interfere with oxygen exchange.","structure":"Focus: Pulmonary embolism. A clot travels through the veins and right heart into one or more pulmonary arteries, which carry blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. These vessels bring blood near alveoli, the tiny air sacs where oxygen enters blood and carbon dioxide leaves it.","normal":"Normally, blood returning from the body enters the right heart and is pumped by the right ventricle through open pulmonary arteries toward the lungs. Blood flows through small vessels beside ventilated alveoli, allowing oxygen and carbon dioxide to exchange; oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left heart for delivery to the body.","change":"When a clot lodges in a pulmonary artery, it blocks some blood flow to ventilated lung regions. The obstruction and vessel narrowing can raise resistance against the right ventricle, increasing its workload and sometimes reducing blood flow to the left heart. The resulting effects on oxygen exchange and circulation vary with clot burden and cardiopulmonary reserve.","cues":"Possible cues include sudden unexplained shortness of breath, sharp pain with breathing, rapid breathing, or a fast heart rate. More severe effects may include fainting or low blood pressure. A normal oxygen reading or no visible leg symptoms does not rule out PE; these findings also have other possible causes.","distinction":"Do not judge severity from clot location alone: right-heart strain and the person’s cardiopulmonary reserve also matter. A normal oxygen reading or lack of leg swelling does not exclude PE.","title":"Pulmonary Embolism","aliases":["PE","pulmonary arterial obstruction","right ventricular dysfunction"],"prerequisite":"pulmonary-artery","related":["pulmonary-artery","vq","output","oxygenation"],"clinicalBridge":"At the bedside, sudden dyspnea and tachycardia after reduced mobility can raise concern even when oxygen saturation is currently expected and leg symptoms are absent. A blockage can disrupt ventilation and perfusion matching, while added right-ventricle workload may affect circulation. These observations support communicating concern and continuing focused assessment, not diagnosing PE from one cue.","readingAim":"If a pulmonary artery is blocked, what change might you expect in right-ventricle workload and oxygen exchange?","id":"focus-s019","skill":"s019","kind":"clinical","unit":"resp","sources":[{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-embolism/pulmonary-embolism-pe","title":"Pulmonary Embolism (PE) - Pulmonology - MSD Manual ..."},{"url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-embolism/pulmonary-embolism-pe?autoredirectid=25470&ruleredirectid=382","title":"Pulmonary Embolism (PE) - Pulmonology - MSD Manual Professional Edition"}]},{"definition":"Severe asthma ventilatory failure occurs when narrowed airways make breathing so difficult that the respiratory muscles cannot maintain enough airflow. Carbon dioxide can then build up, and alertness may decline.","structure":"Focus: severe asthma ventilatory failure. The bronchi are branching airways within the lungs; their smooth muscle can tighten and narrow the path for airflow. The diaphragm beneath the lungs and the muscles between the ribs help move air. When air remains trapped, these muscles work from a less favorable position.","normal":"Normally, air moves through open bronchi, and breathing out empties enough air before the next breath. The diaphragm and rib muscles can sustain this exchange without excessive effort, and ventilation removes carbon dioxide. Bedside assessment considers breathing effort, speech, and alertness together, rather than relying on one sound or finding.","change":"In severe asthma, airway narrowing and air trapping—air left in the lungs because exhalation is incomplete—can progressively increase the effort needed for each breath. The diaphragm and other breathing muscles face a greater load. If their capacity cannot keep pace, ventilation may fall, carbon dioxide may rise, and blood acidity may increase.","cues":"A concerning trend may include increasing accessory-muscle use, rapid or shallow breaths, smaller breaths, difficulty speaking more than a few words, irregular breathing, or declining alertness. Wheezing that becomes quieter can accompany worsening airflow; interpreted alone, it does not establish that breathing has improved.","distinction":"Do not treat quieter wheezing as proof of recovery: less sound can also mean less air is moving. Drowsiness and other findings require assessment in context, since no isolated sign proves ventilatory failure.","title":"Severe Asthma Ventilatory Failure","aliases":["ventilatory failure","dynamic hyperinflation","air trapping","work of breathing"],"prerequisite":"ventilation","related":["ventilation","airway","diaphragm","arousal"],"clinicalBridge":"The course connects airway narrowing to changes in Ventilation and carbon dioxide, then to the breathing muscles’ rising workload and possible changes in arousal. This body-to-bedside link helps learners interpret speech, breathing effort, breath size, and alertness as a developing pattern, not as isolated clues.","readingAim":"Before reading, what bedside changes might occur if narrowed airways and trapped air make breathing effort exceed muscle capacity?","id":"focus-s020","skill":"s020","kind":"clinical","unit":"resp","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/asthma-and-related-disorders/asthma","title":"Asthma - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional Edition"},{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/ventilatory-failure","title":"Ventilatory Failure - Critical Care Medicine"}]},{"definition":"Aspiration-related lung injury occurs when material enters the lower airways and lungs. It can cause blockage, chemical inflammation, bacterial infection, or a combination of these.","structure":"Material passes the larynx into the trachea and bronchi, which conduct air toward the lungs. It may reach the alveoli, tiny air sacs where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged with blood. Gravity and body position influence which lung segments are most affected.","normal":"During normal swallowing, coordinated throat movement and closure at the larynx help keep food and liquid out of the lower airway. If tiny amounts of secretions enter, cough and lung defenses usually clear them. Open bronchi and intact alveoli support effective air movement and gas exchange.","change":"A large aspiration may physically block a bronchus or trigger reflex airway spasm. Acidic stomach contents can rapidly inflame airway and alveolar tissue, causing bronchoconstriction, swelling, and sometimes collapse of air sacs. Bacteria in aspirated material can instead cause infection. Chemical injury and infection may coexist.","cues":"Abrupt cough, wheeze, rapid breathing, or low oxygen soon after a witnessed event can favor chemical injury or blockage; progressive fever, productive cough, and breathlessness over hours to days can favor infection. These patterns overlap. A dependent lung infiltrate may occur in either condition and does not distinguish them.","distinction":"A chest image alone cannot reliably separate chemical pneumonitis from aspiration pneumonia. Do not infer infection—or its absence—from one symptom; onset and clinical trajectory provide useful context for team evaluation.","title":"Aspiration-Related Lung Injury","aliases":["chemical pneumonitis","aspiration pneumonia","airway obstruction","dependent infiltrate"],"prerequisite":"swallow","related":["swallow","airway","alveoli","oxygenation"],"clinicalBridge":"Aspiration can narrow or obstruct a bronchus and inflame alveoli, reducing the air reaching exchange surfaces and disrupting oxygenation. That link helps explain why cough, wheeze, breathing effort, and oxygen trends matter together. Nurses can communicate the aspiration context and changing trajectory; these observations support evaluation but do not establish a diagnosis alone.","readingAim":"How might the timing and progression of respiratory changes help you anticipate what is happening in the lungs?","id":"focus-s021","skill":"s021","kind":"clinical","unit":"resp","sources":[{"url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pneumonia/chemical-pneumonitis-and-aspiration-pneumonia","title":"Chemical Pneumonitis and Aspiration Pneumonia - The Merck Manuals"},{"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK470169/","title":"Aspiration Risk - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf"}]},{"id":"focus-s022","definition":"The pleural space is the thin space between the lung and chest wall. Air or excess fluid in this space can limit lung movement, and trapped air can also impair circulation.","structure":"The pleural space is the potential space between the visceral and parietal pleura surrounding each lung. It normally contains a thin lubricating fluid layer; air or excess fluid changes how the lung moves against the chest wall and may affect nearby circulation.","normal":"Normally, the thin pleural-fluid layer lets the lung surfaces slide as the chest wall moves, while pressure relationships support lung expansion. In pneumothorax, entering air separates the surfaces and can collapse lung tissue; if air is trapped under tension, rising pressure can also impede venous return.","change":"With an effusion, fluid accumulates in the pleural space and may limit lung expansion; systemic pressure changes often produce transudate, whereas local inflammation or cancer commonly produces exudate. With tension pneumothorax, trapped air progressively raises pressure, reducing venous return and threatening circulation.","cues":"Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, reduced breath sounds on one side, or hyperresonance can support concern for pneumothorax but do not prove it alone. In a ventilated patient, new hypotension with these changes heightens concern for tension physiology and warrants urgent team escalation.","distinction":"A large pneumothorax is not automatically a tension pneumothorax: tension refers to trapped air impairing circulation. In a deteriorating patient, escalation should not wait for radiographic confirmation.","related":["pleura","ventilation","perfusion"],"title":"Pleural Space Emergencies","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Pleural space and lung recoil helps connect an air leak to reduced lung expansion and altered chest findings. In this case, positive-pressure ventilation plus unilateral changes and hypotension makes the circulation effect of trapped pleural air especially important to recognize.","readingAim":"What combination of changes would raise concern for tension physiology before imaging?","prerequisite":"pleura","skill":"s022","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Pleural Space Emergencies","Pleural emergencies"],"sources":[{"title":"Pneumothorax - Pulmonology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pneumothorax"},{"title":"Pleural Effusion - Pulmonology - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/mediastinal-and-pleural-disorders/pleural-effusion"}]},{"id":"focus-s023","definition":"Hemoptysis is blood coughed from below the vocal cords. Blood from the nose or mouth, or blood vomited from the stomach, can look similar but has a different source.","structure":"The lower respiratory tract below the vocal cords includes the conducting airways and lung tissue. Blood entering this route can narrow airflow, obstruct it with clots, or pass into the opposite lung, so airway function is central to immediate risk.","normal":"Normally, conducting airways provide an open route for air to reach the lungs. When bleeding begins below the vocal cords, blood may mix with sputum; accumulating blood or clots can obstruct that route or contaminate the unaffected lung.","change":"Blood from the respiratory tract may threaten breathing before its volume is fully estimated. If blood or clots obstruct airflow or enter the unaffected lung, oxygenation can worsen; the immediate effect on breathing and circulation matters more than a precise count of blood loss.","cues":"Bright blood mixed with sputum after coughing supports a respiratory source, while falling oxygenation, tachypnea, agitation, or difficulty speaking signals possible immediate respiratory danger. These cues do not establish the cause, but worsening breathing calls for urgent escalation.","distinction":"Blood coughed from the airway is not the same as blood vomited from the stomach or bleeding from the nose or mouth. Apparent volume alone does not determine urgency, because airway obstruction can occur before much blood is counted.","related":["airway","oxygenation","ventilation"],"title":"Airway Risk With Hemoptysis","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Conducting airways links blood and clots in the respiratory tract to restricted airflow and possible loss of oxygenation. In this case, coughing bright blood followed by falling saturation and difficulty speaking makes the airway and breathing changes more urgent than estimating blood volume.","readingAim":"Which changes would make coughing blood an immediate airway concern?","prerequisite":"airway","skill":"s023","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Airway Risk With Hemoptysis","Airway bleeding"],"sources":[{"title":"Pulmonary Hemorrhage","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/statpearls/article-28032/"},{"title":"Hemoptysis - Pulmonology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/symptoms-of-pulmonary-disorders/hemoptysis"}]},{"id":"focus-s024","definition":"Pulmonary hypertension means pressure is elevated in the blood vessels that carry blood through the lungs. Persistent pressure overload can strain the right ventricle and reduce its ability to support output.","structure":"The pulmonary circulation runs from the right ventricle through vessels in the lungs and returns blood to the left heart. Increased resistance, pressure transmitted from the left heart, or increased blood flow can elevate pressure in this circuit and strain the right ventricle.","normal":"Normally, the right ventricle moves blood through the pulmonary circulation toward the lungs. When pressure in that circulation remains elevated, the right ventricle faces persistent extra workload; over time it can enlarge and weaken, limiting output during exertion and contributing to systemic venous congestion.","change":"Pulmonary pressure may rise from increased vascular resistance, pressure transmitted from the left heart, or increased pulmonary blood flow. The resulting right-ventricular strain can limit output and lead to venous congestion, but hemodynamic assessment is needed to distinguish pre-capillary, post-capillary, and combined patterns.","cues":"Progressive exertional dyspnea or fatigue, light-headedness, a loud pulmonic component of S2, jugular venous distention, or edema can raise concern for pulmonary hypertension and right-heart strain. None of these findings alone confirms the cause or the hemodynamic pattern.","distinction":"Unchanged or normal lung sounds do not rule out pulmonary hypertension or right-heart strain. Do not assume that pulmonary hypertension from lung disease has the same cause or treatment implications as pressure transmitted from left-heart disease.","related":["pulmonary-artery","output","perfusion"],"title":"Pulmonary Pressure and Right Heart","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Pulmonary vessels and the right heart connects elevated pulmonary pressure with increased right-ventricular workload and possible venous congestion. Here, exertional light-headedness and new jugular venous distention or edema deserve attention even though lung auscultation is unchanged.","readingAim":"Why might new venous congestion matter when lung sounds are unchanged?","prerequisite":"pulmonary-artery","skill":"s024","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Pulmonary Pressure and Right Heart","Pulmonary hypertension"],"sources":[{"title":"Pulmonary Hypertension - Pulmonology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/pulmonary-disorders/pulmonary-hypertension/pulmonary-hypertension"},{"title":"Right Heart Failure and Cor Pulmonale - Cardiology - MSD ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/heart-failure/right-heart-failure-and-cor-pulmonale"}]},{"id":"focus-s025","definition":"Thoracic injury can affect breathing by damaging the lungs or changing chest-wall movement, and can affect circulation through bleeding or reduced venous return. Lung contusion may worsen after the initial assessment.","structure":"The chest wall and underlying lungs work together during breathing. Rib fractures can disconnect a segment of chest wall, while associated lung contusion injures lung tissue; trauma may also affect circulation through bleeding, reduced venous return, or cardiac injury.","normal":"Normally, coordinated chest-wall movement supports lung expansion and airflow. After thoracic injury, a flail segment can move opposite the rest of the chest, disrupting that coordination; an underlying lung contusion can also impair breathing and may become more significant after the first assessment.","change":"Thoracic trauma can impair breathing through lung injury or altered chest-wall mechanics and can impair circulation through bleeding, reduced venous return, or cardiac injury. A modest early radiograph does not exclude a worsening lung contusion, so the clinical trend remains important.","cues":"Paradoxical chest-wall movement, chest pain, increasing work of breathing, hypoxemia, or worsening oxygenation after trauma raises concern for serious chest-wall or lung injury. Hypotension with distress and markedly reduced breath sounds on one side also heightens concern, but no single cue confirms the cause.","distinction":"Visible flail movement is not necessarily the whole respiratory problem; an associated lung contusion may drive deterioration. An early radiograph is a snapshot and does not ensure that the injury will remain stable.","related":["pleura","ventilation","oxygenation"],"title":"Thoracic Injury and Chest Wall","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Oxygenation and hypoxemia helps connect injured lung tissue or altered chest-wall movement to a worsening oxygenation trend. In this case, paradoxical movement with increasing breathing difficulty matters even when the first chest image shows limited lung changes.","readingAim":"What would make you reassess concern despite a modest early chest image?","prerequisite":"pleura","skill":"s025","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Thoracic Injury and Chest Wall","Thoracic injury and surgery"],"sources":[{"title":"Thoracic Trauma","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534843/"},{"title":"Flail Chest - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/thoracic-trauma/flail-chest"}]},{"id":"focus-s026","definition":"Liberation from ventilation is the process of determining whether a patient can breathe without substantial ventilator assistance. Extubation is a separate decision because the patient must also be able to protect and maintain the airway.","structure":"The relevant structures are the lungs and conducting airway, together with the endotracheal tube and ventilator interface. Readiness is assessed in the context of respiratory recovery, breathing effort, alertness, cough, secretions, and the ability to maintain airway patency.","normal":"As respiratory illness improves, the lungs can exchange gases while respiratory muscles sustain breathing; an endotracheal tube provides a route for assisted ventilation. During a spontaneous breathing trial, the patient must maintain breathing with little or no ventilator assistance.","change":"If the underlying problem has improved, breathing may remain adequate without substantial support, but this tests only breathing capacity. A weak cough, heavy secretion burden, impaired alertness, or an unsafe airway can still prevent reliable airway protection after tube removal.","cues":"A patient who completes a breathing trial without visible distress may be able to sustain breathing with little assistance. Weak cough and frequent suctioning raise concern about airway clearance, but neither finding alone determines whether extubation is appropriate.","distinction":"Passing a spontaneous breathing trial does not by itself establish readiness for extubation. Breathing ability and airway protection require separate assessment.","related":["ventilation","oxygenation","airway"],"title":"Breathing Trial and Extubation","clinicalBridge":"Understanding ventilation helps distinguish the patient's ability to breathe with minimal support from the separate ability to protect the airway. In this case, a successful trial is encouraging, while weak cough and frequent suctioning remain important findings to communicate before the team decides about extubation.","readingAim":"What airway-protection concerns remain after a patient passes a breathing trial?","prerequisite":"ventilation","skill":"s026","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Breathing Trial and Extubation","Liberation from ventilation"],"sources":[{"title":"Liberation from Mechanical Ventilation - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-failure-and-mechanical-ventilation/liberation-from-mechanical-ventilation?ruleredirectid=743ruleredirectid=450"},{"title":"Extubation - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK539804/"}]},{"id":"focus-s027","definition":"Brain injury can damage tissue immediately or cause secondary injury as swelling or bleeding increases pressure inside the skull. Serial neurologic assessment helps identify changes that may signal deterioration.","structure":"The relevant structures are the brain and the confined space inside the skull. Because the skull cannot expand, swelling or bleeding can raise intracranial pressure and affect cerebral perfusion; changes in alertness, pupils, and motor responses help track neurologic status.","normal":"Within the skull, brain tissue, blood, and cerebrospinal fluid share a confined space. When their balance is maintained, blood can continue to reach the brain and neurologic function can be supported; repeated examinations establish a baseline for comparing later findings.","change":"After injury, swelling or bleeding can increase pressure in the confined skull and reduce cerebral perfusion, progressing to ischemia or herniation. An expanding bleed may produce a temporary period of apparent improvement before alertness, pupils, or motor responses worsen.","cues":"Increasing difficulty waking, new one-sided weakness, or a newly enlarged and poorly reactive pupil are concerning changes in this patient after head injury. No single finding proves the cause, but the pattern and change from prior examinations warrant urgent communication.","distinction":"A period of improved alertness after head injury does not rule out an evolving bleed. Compare serial findings rather than treating temporary improvement as proof that the danger has passed.","related":["icp","cerebral","neurons"],"title":"Serial Changes After Brain Injury","clinicalBridge":"Understanding intracranial pressure helps connect the confined space inside the skull with this patient's changing examination. The earlier improvement does not exclude ongoing injury; later reduced arousal, new weakness, and pupil change together mark a concerning shift that should be communicated promptly.","readingAim":"How would you compare the later neurologic findings with the earlier examination?","prerequisite":"icp","skill":"s027","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Serial Changes After Brain Injury","Neurologic assessment and brain injury"],"sources":[{"title":"Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/traumatic-brain-injury-tbi/traumatic-brain-injury-tbi"},{"title":"Neuroimaging Update on Traumatic Brain Injury - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608606/"}]},{"id":"focus-s028","definition":"An ischemic stroke occurs when an obstruction interrupts blood flow to part of the brain. The affected brain territory shapes the pattern of lost function, while nearby threatened tissue may remain viable for a time.","structure":"The relevant structures are the brain's arterial territories and the tissue they supply. A blocked vessel can deprive a region of blood; the core may be severely injured while surrounding penumbra remains threatened but potentially viable through collateral flow.","normal":"Arteries deliver blood to brain regions that support specific functions. With adequate flow, those regions can sustain their roles; neighboring collateral vessels may also help maintain blood supply when a major route is impaired.","change":"When an obstruction interrupts flow, the deprived core may be irreversibly injured and surrounding penumbra remains at risk. The deficit pattern depends on the affected territory: middle cerebral artery involvement often emphasizes face and arm, while anterior cerebral artery involvement often emphasizes the opposite leg.","cues":"Sudden right face and arm weakness, reduced sensation, and difficulty finding words are focal changes consistent with stroke. A less affected leg does not rule it out; the observed pattern suggests a territory but does not confirm the diagnosis by itself.","distinction":"A less affected leg does not exclude stroke when face and arm findings predominate. Slurred speech alone neither establishes aphasia nor precisely localizes a lesion.","related":["cerebral","perfusion","ischemia"],"title":"Recognizing Focal Stroke Patterns","clinicalBridge":"Understanding cerebral perfusion helps explain why sudden focal deficits are time-sensitive: interrupted flow can threaten both the injured core and surrounding penumbra. This patient's face-arm predominance and difficulty finding words should be reported with when symptoms began or were first noticed, if known.","readingAim":"What territory-related pattern would you predict from sudden face and arm weakness with language difficulty?","prerequisite":"cerebral","skill":"s028","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Focal Stroke Patterns","Stroke recognition"],"sources":[{"title":"Treatment / Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499997/"},{"title":"Ischemic Stroke - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK499997/"}]},{"id":"focus-s029","definition":"A seizure is abnormal electrical activity in the brain that temporarily disrupts function. Status epilepticus includes prolonged seizure activity or repeated seizures without recovery between them, and may occur without obvious convulsions.","structure":"The relevant structure is the brain's neuronal networks, where electrical signaling supports awareness, movement, and other functions. Seizure activity may involve visible motor pathways or persist in networks without obvious convulsions, so bedside appearance does not reveal all electrical activity.","normal":"Neurons communicate through electrical signaling that supports awareness and coordinated function. After a seizure ends, a person may remain confused or temporarily weak during recovery; observing the course over time helps distinguish this from continued or repeated activity.","change":"Excessive excitation or inadequate inhibition can sustain or repeat seizure activity. Electrical activity may continue after visible convulsions stop, or occur without dramatic movement; persistent impaired awareness can therefore raise concern for nonconvulsive seizure activity.","cues":"The patient remains unresponsive after convulsions stop and intermittently turns the eyes to one side, without returning to usual awareness. These findings may reflect postictal recovery or ongoing seizure activity; bedside observation alone cannot determine which.","distinction":"The end of visible convulsions does not prove that seizure activity has ended. Persistent altered awareness can have more than one cause, and EEG is needed to diagnose nonconvulsive activity.","related":["neurons","arousal","conduction"],"title":"Seizure Activity Without Convulsions","clinicalBridge":"Understanding neurons as communicating electrical networks helps explain why ongoing seizure activity may not produce visible convulsions. In this case, persistent unresponsiveness and intermittent eye deviation after movements stop are reasons to communicate concern and support evaluation for continued activity, including consideration of EEG.","readingAim":"What changes after visible convulsions stop would raise concern for ongoing seizure activity?","prerequisite":"neurons","skill":"s029","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Seizure Activity Without Convulsions","Seizures and status epilepticus"],"sources":[{"title":"Status Epilepticus - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430686/"},{"title":"Seizure Disorders - Neurology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/seizure-disorders/seizure-disorders?ruleredirectid=743"}]},{"id":"focus-s030","definition":"A spinal cord injury disrupts pathways that carry movement, sensation, and autonomic signals. Depending on its level and extent, it may also impair breathing or circulation.","structure":"The spinal cord runs within the vertebral canal and carries motor, sensory, and autonomic pathways between the brain and body. A lesion’s level and extent shape which functions are disrupted; cervical injury can also affect breathing.","normal":"Normally, descending pathways help coordinate movement, sensation, and autonomic control, while ascending pathways carry sensory information toward the brain. Mechanical injury can interrupt these routes; subsequent swelling, ischemia, and inflammation may add secondary damage and broaden dysfunction.","change":"Normally, sympathetic pathways help maintain vascular tone and heart-rate responses. When a cord injury above the mid-thoracic level interrupts that output, vessels may lose constrictor tone and the heart rate may slow, producing hypotension with bradycardia; other injury causes can coexist.","cues":"After trauma, weakness, sensory changes, or bowel or bladder dysfunction may indicate cord involvement. Hypotension with bradycardia and warm, dry skin can fit neurogenic shock, but bleeding may coexist, and these findings alone do not establish the cause.","distinction":"Spinal shock describes temporary loss of reflexes and flaccid function; neurogenic shock is circulatory instability from lost sympathetic tone. Early flaccidity does not by itself prove a complete lesion or neurogenic shock.","related":["cord","perfusion","hypotension"],"title":"Cord Injury and Neurogenic Shock","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Spinal cord and sympathetic tone links cord pathways to autonomic control. A sufficiently high injury can interrupt sympathetic output, helping explain hypotension with bradycardia; this connection supports recognition, while concurrent bleeding and neurologic findings still require broader team assessment.","readingAim":"Which findings suggest impaired sympathetic tone, and what else could contribute to hypotension?","prerequisite":"cord","skill":"s030","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Cord Injury and Neurogenic Shock","Spinal cord injury and shock"],"sources":[{"title":"Spinal Cord Injuries - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560721/"},{"title":"Spinal Trauma - Injuries; Poisoning","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/spinal-trauma/spinal-trauma"}]},{"id":"focus-s031","definition":"Hydrocephalus occurs when cerebrospinal fluid production, flow, and absorption are no longer balanced. An external ventricular drain can divert fluid and measure intracranial pressure.","structure":"The brain’s ventricles contain cerebrospinal fluid, which flows through connected spaces. An external ventricular drain enters a ventricle and connects to a collection and measurement system; its prescribed height relative to the patient’s head affects drainage and pressure readings.","normal":"Normally, cerebrospinal fluid moves through the ventricles and is absorbed, helping maintain a stable fluid balance. An external ventricular drain provides an alternate route for fluid and a way to measure pressure; its position and level are part of interpreting both functions.","change":"When blood or another obstruction disrupts cerebrospinal fluid flow, hydrocephalus can develop. Drain flow also depends on the collection-system height, so repositioning can change drainage and pressure readings; a kink or obstruction may impair flow, but reduced flow alone does not prove either.","cues":"Reduced or absent drainage, a dampened waveform, or absent meniscus movement can raise concern about flow or setup. A leak, cloudy fluid, or bright red blood is also concerning; interpret these signs with the patient’s neurologic status and system configuration.","distinction":"A low displayed pressure is not automatically reassuring if the transducer is incorrectly positioned. Little or no drainage alone does not establish obstruction, because flow may stop when pressure no longer exceeds the collection-system setting.","related":["csf","icp","arousal"],"title":"Ventricular Drain and Fluid Flow","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Ventricles and cerebrospinal fluid explains why flow depends on connected spaces and drainage setup. An external ventricular drain adds a height-dependent route and pressure measurement, so patient position, system leveling, observed flow, and neurologic status must be interpreted together.","readingAim":"After repositioning, how could drain setup affect the pressure reading and observed flow?","prerequisite":"csf","skill":"s031","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Ventricular Drain and Fluid Flow","Hydrocephalus and ventricular drains"],"sources":[{"title":"External ventricular drains: Management and complications","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4450504"},{"title":"External Ventricular Drains: Development and Evaluation of a Nursing Clinical Practice Guideline","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9782641/pdf/nursrep-12-00090.pdf"}]},{"id":"focus-s032","definition":"Brain death, also called death by neurologic criteria, is permanent loss of function of the brain as a whole, including the brainstem. Its determination is a formal clinician-led process that requires attention to reversible confounders.","structure":"The brain and brainstem support consciousness, brainstem reflexes, and respiratory drive. During a formal evaluation, clinicians assess these functions in the context of a catastrophic permanent brain injury and consider whether medications, temperature, or other reversible factors could affect responses.","normal":"Normally, brain activity supports arousal, brainstem reflexes, and spontaneous breathing. A catastrophic permanent injury may abolish these functions, but sedatives, other depressants, paralytics, intoxication, metabolic or acid-base disturbance, low temperature, and hypotension can also alter examination findings.","change":"In a formal evaluation, clinicians assess for coma, absent brainstem reflexes, and no respiratory drive while excluding reversible causes and drug effects. An observed movement may arise from a spinal reflex, so it must be interpreted in context rather than treated as a definitive sign.","cues":"Coma and absent observed pupil, corneal, gag, or cough responses warrant careful reporting in the evaluation context. Recent sedative exposure or hypothermia can confound findings, while spontaneous breathing or intact brainstem reflexes argues against proceeding; isolated movement needs interpretation.","distinction":"Absent responses do not establish brain death when confounders remain unaddressed. A limb movement may be a spinal reflex and does not, by itself, confirm or rule out the determination.","related":["arousal","neurons","cord"],"title":"Brain Death and Confounders","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Attention and arousal helps frame why observed responsiveness is clinically meaningful but not self-interpreting. In a brain-death evaluation, medication exposure, temperature, and other confounders can alter responses, so findings must be communicated and considered within the formal clinician-led assessment.","readingAim":"Which confounders should be reported before absent responses are interpreted?","prerequisite":"arousal","skill":"s032","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Brain Death and Confounders","Brain death and confounders"],"sources":[{"title":"Consensus guideline for death by neurologic criteria","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10791061/"},{"title":"Brain Death - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/coma-and-impaired-consciousness/brain-death"},{"title":"Table: Guidelines for Determining Brain Death/Death by Neurologic ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/multimedia/table/guidelines-for-determining-brain-deathdeath-by-neurologic-criteria-in-patients-37-weeks-old"}]},{"id":"focus-s033","definition":"Hepatic encephalopathy is impaired brain function associated with liver failure or blood bypassing the liver. It can range from subtle changes in thinking to coma.","structure":"The relevant context is the liver and the route blood takes through it. Liver failure or a portosystemic shunt can let blood bypass the liver; the clinical concern is the resulting effect on brain function and cognition.","normal":"Normally, a person’s cognition and behavior provide a baseline for comparison, and blood does not bypass the liver through a portosystemic shunt. This context helps distinguish a new change in brain function from the person’s usual state.","change":"With liver failure or blood bypassing the liver, brain function may become impaired. Ammonia can contribute, alongside inflammation, altered neurotransmission, and other metabolic changes; the resulting cognitive change can vary from subtle symptoms to reduced responsiveness or coma.","cues":"Compare behavior and cognition with the person’s usual state. New disorientation, altered sleep, somnolence, or asterixis in someone with liver disease or a shunt may be relevant, but these findings are not specific; consider other causes such as infection, dehydration, or hypoxia.","distinction":"An elevated ammonia result or asterixis may support consideration of hepatic encephalopathy, but neither establishes the diagnosis. Their absence also does not rule it out.","related":["liver","delirium","arousal"],"title":"Hepatic Encephalopathy","clinicalBridge":"Liver function and portal flow provide context for interpreting a cognitive change: liver disease or a shunt may make hepatic encephalopathy plausible. In the case of confusion after poor intake and constipation, communicate the change and context while supporting evaluation for other causes.","readingAim":"What changes and competing causes would you communicate?","prerequisite":"liver","skill":"s033","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Hepatic Encephalopathy","Encephalopathy"],"sources":[{"title":"Current trends in the treatment of hepatic encephalopathy - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2724191/"},{"title":"Hepatic encephalopathy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5421503/"}]},{"id":"focus-s034","definition":"CNS infection can inflame brain tissue, the membranes covering it, or both. Inflammation within brain tissue may disrupt behavior, awareness, or neurologic function.","structure":"The brain’s tissue contains networks responsible for awareness, behavior, and movement; the meninges are membranes around the brain and spinal cord. Cerebrospinal fluid surrounds these structures and can be tested to help identify infection.","normal":"When brain tissue and its neural networks are functioning normally, a person can maintain their usual awareness, behavior, and neurologic abilities. The meninges cover the central nervous system, while cerebrospinal fluid provides a surrounding fluid space that can be sampled during evaluation.","change":"With encephalitis, infection or a postinfectious immune response inflames brain tissue and may disturb the networks supporting awareness, behavior, or movement. Meningitis inflames the covering membranes instead; the processes can coexist, so symptoms may overlap and early findings may be incomplete.","cues":"Fever or a flu-like illness together with headache and new confusion, personality change, reduced alertness, seizure, or focal findings raises concern for brain involvement. Neck stiffness and light sensitivity may occur, but their absence does not exclude encephalitis.","distinction":"Mild or absent neck stiffness does not make encephalitis unlikely when new confusion, seizure, or focal neurologic findings are present. These signs raise concern but do not, by themselves, establish the cause.","related":["cerebral","neurons","csf","arousal"],"title":"Brain Inflammation and Neurologic Change","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article on Cerebral perfusion links blood delivery to brain function. This lesson adds a different route to neurologic change: inflammation within brain tissue or its coverings can affect awareness and behavior, so compare the patient’s neurologic findings with the whole clinical pattern.","readingAim":"Which new neurologic findings alongside fever and headache would make you suspect brain involvement?","prerequisite":"cerebral","skill":"s034","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Brain Inflammation and Neurologic Change","CNS infections"],"sources":[{"title":"Encephalitis - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/brain-infections/encephalitis"},{"title":"Infectious meningitis and encephalitis: A primer for acute care ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13053408/"}]},{"id":"focus-s035","definition":"Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity is a possible pattern of repeated episodes of sympathetic activation after severe acquired brain injury. Episodes may combine changes in vital signs with sweating or increased muscle tone.","structure":"Descending brain pathways normally influence sympathetic circuits lower in the nervous system, including pathways involving the spinal cord. Severe acquired brain injury can disrupt this influence, providing a context for episodic autonomic and motor changes.","normal":"In ordinary regulation, descending influence from the brain helps modulate lower sympathetic circuits. This contributes to coordinated autonomic responses rather than repeated, disproportionate episodes of changes in temperature, blood pressure, heart rate, breathing, sweating, and muscle tone.","change":"After severe acquired brain injury, disrupted descending inhibition may allow lower sympathetic circuits to become excessively responsive. Routine care or another minor stimulus may precede an episode combining autonomic changes and increased tone, with relative improvement between episodes.","cues":"Repeated, abrupt episodes with several changes together—such as sweating, raised temperature, faster heart rate, and increased tone—especially with settling between episodes, suggest a pattern worth reporting. A trigger may be present, but the pattern is not diagnostic by itself.","distinction":"Fever or a fast heart rate alone does not establish PSH. Sepsis and other causes can mimic or coexist with it, so a possible PSH pattern should not end assessment for competing explanations.","related":["cord","temperature","neurons"],"title":"Episodic Sympathetic Activation","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article on Spinal cord and sympathetic tone describes how neural pathways influence autonomic activity. This lesson applies that relationship after severe brain injury: disrupted descending influence may contribute to recurrent episodes, while the observed pattern still requires review for sepsis and other causes.","readingAim":"What combination and timing of findings would make you report possible PSH rather than a single abnormal vital sign?","prerequisite":"cord","skill":"s035","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Episodic Sympathetic Activation","Autonomic storming"],"sources":[{"title":"Recognising and differentiating paroxysmal sympathetic ...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42622998/"},{"title":"Paroxysmal Sympathetic Hyperactivity after Traumatic Brain Injury: Current Understanding and Therapeutic Options","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10839941/"}]},{"id":"focus-s036","definition":"Neuromuscular respiratory failure occurs when weakness limits effective breathing or airway protection. In myasthenia gravis, weakness may be fatigable and involve respiratory and swallowing muscles.","structure":"The respiratory muscles, including the diaphragm, move air into and out of the lungs. Bulbar muscles support swallowing and airway protection, while a weak cough can make it harder to clear secretions.","normal":"With effective respiratory muscle function, the diaphragm and other muscles support ventilation and carbon dioxide removal. Adequate bulbar strength, swallowing, and cough also help protect the airway and clear secretions during ordinary breathing and eating.","change":"In myasthenia gravis, fatigable weakness can involve respiratory muscles and the muscles that protect the airway. Ventilation may become inadequate, allowing carbon dioxide retention; weak cough or impaired swallowing can increase the risk of retained secretions and aspiration.","cues":"Worsening weakness with nasal or weak speech, dysphagia, ineffective cough, head drop, or increasing work of breathing raises concern. Lethargy and worsening respiratory measures or blood gases also matter; a calm appearance or oxygen saturation alone may not show the full picture.","distinction":"Little visible breathing distress does not rule out worsening ventilation, and oxygen saturation alone is not a complete assessment. Hypoxia or dyspnea without other myasthenic signs or carbon dioxide retention may have another cause.","related":["nmj","airway","ventilation"],"title":"Weakness and Ventilatory Failure","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article on Neuromuscular junction explains how signaling supports muscle activity. This lesson connects fatigable weakness to the diaphragm, other respiratory muscles, and airway-protective muscles, showing why speech, cough, swallowing, alertness, blood gases, and respiratory trends belong in the assessment.","readingAim":"Which combination of weakness, airway-protection findings, and respiratory trends would make you escalate concern?","prerequisite":"nmj","skill":"s036","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Weakness and Ventilatory Failure","Neuromuscular respiratory failure"],"sources":[{"title":"SOP myasthenic crisis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7650067/"},{"title":"Pitfalls in the Evaluation of Respiratory Failure in Myasthenia ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11561952/"}]},{"id":"focus-s037","definition":"After cranial or spinal surgery, neurologic monitoring helps identify changes from the patient’s documented baseline. A new or worsening finding needs attention even when other examination findings remain unchanged.","structure":"The relevant context is the patient’s central nervous system after cranial or spinal surgery, assessed against a documented baseline. Checks may include alertness, orientation, pupil response, strength, movement, and sensation over time.","normal":"A documented baseline records the patient’s neurologic function before or around surgery. Repeated, consistent checks of alertness, orientation, pupils, strength, movement, and sensation make it possible to recognize whether later findings are stable or have changed.","change":"A postoperative change in central nervous system function may appear as reduced responsiveness, confusion, new weakness, or sensory change. Comparing each finding and its timing with baseline helps the team recognize deterioration; recent anesthesia may affect alertness but does not explain every change.","cues":"Slower responses or reduced strength in one arm compared with baseline are meaningful bedside changes even if pupil responses are unchanged. Note the timing and trend, communicate the comparison, and continue focused monitoring under local protocols.","distinction":"One unchanged finding, such as pupil response, does not cancel a new motor, sensory, or alertness change. Do not assume a new or worsening finding is simply routine recovery from anesthesia.","related":["neurons","arousal","cerebral"],"title":"Postoperative Neurologic Change","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article on Neurons and neural signaling introduces the nervous system functions assessed after surgery. This lesson uses a documented baseline to interpret changes in alertness, strength, movement, pupils, and sensation, emphasizing that one stable finding cannot establish that all neurologic function is unchanged.","readingAim":"How would you compare a new postoperative finding with baseline and communicate its significance?","prerequisite":"neurons","skill":"s037","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Postoperative Neurologic Change","After cranial or spinal surgery"],"sources":[{"title":"Neurologic Complications in the Postoperative ...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34618765/"},{"title":"31.4 Postoperative Phase - Fundamentals of Nursing | OpenStax","url":"https://openstax.org/books/fundamentals-nursing/pages/31-4-postoperative-phase"}]},{"id":"focus-s038","definition":"A subarachnoid hemorrhage occurs when blood from a ruptured brain aneurysm enters the space surrounding the brain. The bleeding can rapidly affect brain pressure and blood flow.","structure":"The relevant structures are the arterial wall of a brain aneurysm and the subarachnoid space around the brain. Blood entering this space can affect nearby brain tissue and cerebrospinal fluid pathways.","normal":"A cerebral artery normally carries blood within an intact wall, while the subarachnoid space contains cerebrospinal fluid around the brain. This arrangement supports circulation and cushions the brain without exposing it to arterial bleeding.","change":"When an aneurysm ruptures, arterial blood spills into the subarachnoid space. The added blood can abruptly raise intracranial pressure, disrupt cerebral perfusion, obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow, and leave the unsecured aneurysm at risk of rebleeding.","cues":"A sudden severe headache that peaks quickly, especially with vomiting, neck stiffness, fainting, seizure, or reduced consciousness, signals possible bleeding around the brain. These findings warrant urgent attention but do not establish the cause; a normal examination does not exclude hemorrhage.","distinction":"A negative early CT does not automatically exclude hemorrhage when clinical concern remains high; further evaluation may be needed. Being awake or having a normal examination does not prove the danger has passed.","related":["cerebral","icp","csf"],"title":"Aneurysm Rupture and Brain Perfusion","clinicalBridge":"Cerebral perfusion depends on blood reaching brain tissue. In this case, arterial blood entering the subarachnoid space can raise pressure and disrupt that flow, so the abrupt headache and brief loss of consciousness are concerning signals. Communicating symptom onset and neurologic changes helps the team assess the evolving risk.","readingAim":"How could bleeding around the brain affect perfusion even if the patient is now awake?","prerequisite":"cerebral","skill":"s038","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Aneurysm Rupture and Brain Perfusion","Aneurysms and subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Stroke - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535369/"},{"title":"Aneurysmal Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/8147706"}]},{"id":"focus-s039","definition":"An intracranial mass is an abnormal growth or other space-occupying process within the skull. It can harm nearby tissue or raise pressure, even when it is not cancerous.","structure":"The relevant structures are the brain and cerebrospinal fluid pathways within the skull, a fixed space. A mass may press on nearby tissue, cause swelling or bleeding, or obstruct fluid flow and absorption.","normal":"Within the skull, brain tissue and cerebrospinal fluid occupy a confined space, and fluid normally flows through and is absorbed from its pathways. This arrangement allows the brain to function without harmful compression or pressure buildup.","change":"A mass can invade or compress nearby tissue, cause swelling or bleeding, and consume space within the skull. If it obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow or absorption, pressure can rise and contribute to headache, vomiting, seizures, or altered responsiveness.","cues":"A persistent or worsening headache, particularly with vomiting, a new seizure, declining alertness, or focal changes, can signal an evolving intracranial problem. These findings do not identify a tumor or its location; report the pattern and change from the patient’s earlier status.","distinction":"The absence of papilledema does not rule out increased intracranial pressure. A focal deficit or symptom pattern alone cannot establish that a mass is present or pinpoint its location.","related":["icp","csf","cerebral"],"title":"Intracranial Mass Effect","clinicalBridge":"Intracranial pressure can rise when a mass, swelling, or blocked cerebrospinal fluid pathway occupies space inside the skull. Here, worsening headache followed by vomiting, seizure, and slower responses forms a concerning change in pattern, but those cues do not establish a tumor as the cause.","readingAim":"Why does the combination of worsening headache and slower responses matter even without papilledema?","prerequisite":"icp","skill":"s039","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Intracranial Mass Effect","Intracranial masses"],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Intracranial Tumors - Neurologic Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/intracranial-and-spinal-tumors/overview-of-intracranial-tumors?ruleredirectid=749"},{"title":"Overview of Intracranial Tumors - Neurologic Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/intracranial-and-spinal-tumors/overview-of-intracranial-tumors?ruleredirectid=744"}]},{"id":"focus-s040","definition":"Acute kidney injury is a rapid decline in kidney function. It can impair the kidneys’ handling of waste, fluid, and electrolytes.","structure":"The relevant structures are the kidney nephrons, which filter blood and form urine, and the urinary outflow pathway. Kidney function can decline when perfusion is reduced, kidney tissue is injured, or urine flow is obstructed.","normal":"Nephrons receive blood, filter it, and adjust water and dissolved substances as the filtrate passes through the tubules. Urine then drains through an open outflow pathway, helping the body regulate waste, fluid, and electrolytes.","change":"With reduced kidney perfusion, nephrons may conserve water and produce concentrated urine while urine output falls. Intrinsic tissue injury or an obstruction can also reduce output, so low urine volume alone does not identify which mechanism is operating.","cues":"After vomiting, lower blood pressure and declining urine output, especially with concentrated urine, support concern for reduced kidney perfusion. They do not prove volume depletion or exclude intrinsic injury and obstruction; communicate the trend alongside kidney-function results and the broader assessment.","distinction":"Low urine output is a warning signal, not a diagnosis: reduced perfusion, intrinsic kidney injury, and obstruction can all contribute. A later rise in output does not by itself establish that filtering function has recovered.","related":["nephron","oliguria","filtrate"],"title":"Acute Kidney Function Decline","clinicalBridge":"The nephron filters blood and adjusts water as urine forms. In this case, vomiting and lower blood pressure may reduce kidney perfusion, prompting water conservation and concentrated urine while output declines. These cues support a possible perfusion-related mechanism but need assessment alongside other causes and kidney-function trends.","readingAim":"Why can concentrated urine support reduced perfusion without proving the cause of low output?","prerequisite":"nephron","skill":"s040","unit":"renal","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Acute Kidney Function Decline","Acute kidney injury"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Nephrology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki"},{"title":"Oliguria - Critical Care Medicine - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/approach-to-the-critically-ill-patient/oliguria?"}]},{"id":"focus-s041","definition":"Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is lasting loss of kidney function. Remaining kidney tissue can compensate for a time, so early disease may cause few symptoms.","structure":"The kidneys are the organ context for chronic impairment: the amount of functioning tissue may be reduced, while remaining tissue continues to regulate fluid, electrolytes, acid, and phosphate.","normal":"When kidney tissue is functioning, the kidneys regulate fluid, electrolytes, acid, and phosphate. Remaining tissue may compensate for loss of function, so early chronic impairment can have few obvious symptoms.","change":"As chronic kidney function declines, regulation of fluid, electrolytes, acid, and phosphate becomes less reliable. Fluid and electrolyte problems may emerge, along with anemia and bone and mineral changes. A new rapid decline may add acute injury.","cues":"Prior reduced kidney function or small kidneys on imaging can support chronic impairment; fatigue, nocturia, itching, anemia, or mineral abnormalities may also occur. New oliguria or a rapid creatinine rise signals change but needs context.","distinction":"A normal blood sodium does not rule out excess fluid, because concentration and total volume can differ. One abnormal result alone does not establish whether impairment is chronic or acute.","related":["nephron","oliguria","fluid"],"title":"Chronic Kidney Disease in Critical Care","clinicalBridge":"Oliguria is a change in urine output, not proof of its cause. In this case, compare it with baseline kidney function and creatinine trend, then communicate the rapid change and assess fluid status, breathing, mental state, and electrolytes with the care team.","readingAim":"Which new findings would suggest an acute change on top of CKD rather than stable chronic impairment?","prerequisite":"nephron","skill":"s041","unit":"renal","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Chronic Kidney Disease in Critical Care","Chronic kidney disease in the ICU"],"sources":[{"title":"Chronic Kidney Disease - Nephrology","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/chronic-kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease"},{"title":"Chronic Kidney Disease - Chronic Kidney Disease - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-gb/professional/genitourinary-disorders/chronic-kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease"}]},{"id":"focus-s042","definition":"A kidney infection is an infection involving the kidneys, and urosepsis is a serious body response to infection that can impair organ function. Urine trapped behind an obstruction may require urgent drainage as part of source control.","structure":"The urinary tract carries urine from the kidneys through the ureters to the bladder and out of the body. Infection may ascend along this pathway, while a stone or other blockage can impede urine flow from a kidney.","normal":"Normally, urine flows from each kidney through its ureter to the bladder, helping carry waste out of the body. The urinary tract also helps limit the spread of microbes; when infection ascends, kidney tissue can become involved and the body may mount a dysregulated response.","change":"When urine is obstructed, infected urine may remain trapped above the blockage rather than draining freely. Infection and impaired flow can coexist with worsening kidney function and systemic illness, so obstruction and sepsis-related injury are both possible and need clinical evaluation.","cues":"Flank tenderness, urinary symptoms, confusion, malaise, reduced urine output, or worsening kidney function can raise concern, especially with hydronephrosis, stones, or a stent. Fever may be absent, and unchanged imaging or a reassuring white count does not settle the question.","distinction":"An unchanged or nondilated ultrasound does not prove that urine is flowing freely. Likewise, no fever or a normal white cell count alone cannot rule out serious infection or obstruction.","related":["bladder","nephron","oliguria"],"title":"Kidney Infection and Obstruction","clinicalBridge":"The nephron filters blood and contributes to urine formation, so infection affecting the kidney can accompany changes in kidney function. In a patient with flank findings and rising creatinine, consider both systemic illness and impaired drainage; neither an unchanged scan nor absent fever resolves the concern.","readingAim":"What combination of findings would keep obstruction on your concern list despite an unchanged ultrasound?","prerequisite":"bladder","skill":"s042","unit":"renal","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Kidney Infection and Obstruction","Kidney infection and urosepsis"],"sources":[{"title":"Urosepsis - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482344/"},{"title":"Pyelonephritis and obstructive uropathy: a case of acute kidney injury","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4716328/"}]},{"id":"focus-s043","definition":"Continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) removes water and solutes gradually through a filter. It can support fluid and solute control when a patient is unstable or vulnerable to rapid shifts.","structure":"CRRT uses an extracorporeal circuit and a semipermeable filter to process blood continuously or over extended periods. Diffusion, convection, or both move solutes across the filter, while prescribed fluid removal helps manage the patient’s fluid balance.","normal":"The kidneys normally filter blood, regulate water and solutes, and help maintain the body’s internal balance. When kidney function is impaired, CRRT can gradually remove water and solutes; diffusion, convection, or a combination determines how substances cross the filter.","change":"In acute kidney injury, fluid overload, acidosis, electrolyte disturbance, or uremic complications may prompt consideration of renal replacement therapy. CRRT’s gradual approach may suit hemodynamic instability, but interruptions can reduce the treatment actually delivered and make monitoring essential.","cues":"Instability, fluid balance, laboratory trends, access function, alarms, and interruptions help show how the patient and therapy are progressing. Worsening abnormalities or access concerns warrant communication with the responsible team; a prescribed treatment plan does not guarantee full delivery.","distinction":"Continuous therapy is not automatically sufficient or uninterrupted. Intermittent hemodialysis may remove potassium or toxins more rapidly when urgent clearance is the priority and that approach is clinically appropriate.","related":["nephron","filtrate","fluid"],"title":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy","clinicalBridge":"Filtration and tubular reabsorption normally help the kidneys regulate water and solutes. When acute kidney injury and fluid overload coexist with blood pressure instability, gradual extracorporeal removal may be considered; report instability, fluid trends, access concerns, and interruptions so the team can assess delivered therapy.","readingAim":"What patient and circuit changes would you communicate when gradual fluid and solute control is being considered?","prerequisite":"nephron","skill":"s043","unit":"renal","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy","Continuous renal replacement therapy"],"sources":[{"title":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556028/"},{"title":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy - StatPearls - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK556028/"}]},{"id":"focus-s044","definition":"Gastrointestinal bleeding is blood loss from somewhere along the digestive tract. The appearance of blood can suggest a location, but it does not establish the source by itself.","structure":"The gastrointestinal tract extends from the mouth through the esophagus, stomach, and intestines to the anus. Blood may be vomited or passed in stool, and its appearance can change with the site of bleeding and the time it takes to travel through the tract.","normal":"Normally, the gastrointestinal tract moves food and waste through the body without visible blood loss. When bleeding occurs, blood may appear as red vomit, coffee-ground material, black tarry stool, or red rectal blood, depending partly on location and transit time.","change":"Significant blood loss can affect circulation and reduce delivery of blood to organs. A person may develop weakness, faintness, pallor, sweating, a rapid pulse, low blood pressure, confusion, or reduced urine output, although any single finding needs interpretation in context.","cues":"Ask what the blood looked like and whether it was vomited or passed, while assessing mental status, vital signs, and visible bleeding. Faintness, pallor, sweating, or a rapid pulse raises concern; iron or bismuth can also darken stool.","distinction":"Black stool is not automatically melena, since iron and bismuth can darken stool. An initially normal hemoglobin is only a snapshot and does not by itself exclude acute blood loss.","related":["perfusion","platelets","liver","fluid-loss"],"title":"Recognizing Gastrointestinal Bleeding","clinicalBridge":"Tissue perfusion depends on adequate circulating blood flow. Black, sticky stool may suggest an upper gastrointestinal source, while faintness, pallor, sweating, and a rapid pulse raise concern about circulation; anticoagulant use adds relevant context, but the appearance alone does not confirm the source.","readingAim":"Which observed findings would you report first when the bleeding source remains uncertain?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s044","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Gastrointestinal Bleeding","Recognizing gastrointestinal bleeding"],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Gastrointestinal Bleeding - Gastroenterology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/overview-of-gastrointestinal-bleeding"},{"title":"Evaluation and treatment of gastrointestinal bleeding in ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11141076/"}]},{"id":"focus-s045","definition":"Portal hypertension is increased pressure in the portal circulation, often associated with cirrhosis. It can enlarge collateral veins in the lower esophagus and upper stomach, where rupture may cause gastrointestinal bleeding.","structure":"The portal circulation carries blood from abdominal organs toward the liver. With portal hypertension, collateral veins may enlarge in the lower esophagus or upper stomach; these varices can bleed, while liver-related clotting problems may worsen blood loss.","normal":"Normally, blood from abdominal organs travels through the portal circulation to the liver, and small collateral veins do not become prominent bleeding sites. When portal pressure rises, collateral pathways can enlarge into varices that may rupture and bleed.","change":"Cirrhosis can contribute to portal hypertension, enlarged varices, and impaired clotting. If a varix ruptures, bleeding may be sudden and severe; however, ulcers and other nonvariceal causes can also produce gastrointestinal bleeding in a person with cirrhosis.","cues":"Sudden, painless vomiting of blood in a person with cirrhosis makes variceal bleeding a concern, especially alongside pallor, light-headedness, or other signs of circulatory compromise. These findings warrant urgent team attention but do not confirm the bleeding source.","distinction":"Known varices are a warning sign, not proof that they caused the current bleed. Endoscopy is important because other sources, including ulcers, remain possible.","related":["liver","abdominal-veins","platelets"],"title":"Varices and Portal Hypertension","clinicalBridge":"Portal-systemic collaterals and varices form when portal pressure redirects blood through alternative veins. In a person with cirrhosis and sudden upper gastrointestinal bleeding, variceal hemorrhage is an urgent possibility, but endoscopy is needed to identify the source because nonvariceal bleeding can also occur.","readingAim":"Why would you prepare for possible variceal bleeding while keeping other sources in consideration?","prerequisite":"liver","skill":"s045","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Varices and Portal Hypertension","Liver failure and portal hypertension"],"sources":[{"title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices"},{"title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices?ruleredirectid=746"}]},{"id":"focus-s046","definition":"Acute pancreatitis is inflammation and injury of the pancreas that begins when digestive enzymes activate inside pancreatic cells. It can also affect circulation and other organs.","structure":"The pancreas is an abdominal gland whose cells contain digestive enzymes. In acute pancreatitis, the key context is enzyme activation within these cells, followed by injury and inflammation in the gland and possible effects beyond it.","normal":"Acinar cells release protein-digesting enzymes in inactive forms. Ducts carry them into the duodenum, where activation lets them digest food proteins away from the gland. Other pancreatic enzymes, including amylase and lipase, are already active when secreted.","change":"In acute pancreatitis, digestive enzymes activate inside pancreatic cells and injure the gland. Inflammation can cause edema and sometimes necrosis; inflammatory mediators may also increase capillary leak and reduce vascular tone, shifting fluid out of circulation and affecting organs such as the lungs and kidneys.","cues":"Steady, often severe upper abdominal pain, sometimes radiating to the back, with nausea or vomiting can fit pancreatitis. Gallstones are one possible cause. A high enzyme result supports concern in context, but neither one symptom nor an isolated result establishes the whole diagnosis or severity.","distinction":"A high pancreatic enzyme result is not, by itself, proof of pancreatitis or a measure of its severity. Less pain after analgesia also does not establish that inflammation or organ function is improving.","related":["inflammation","fluid","gut","pancreas"],"title":"Pancreatic Enzyme Activation","clinicalBridge":"Inflammation and capillary leak help explain how a local pancreatic injury can affect other organs. In this case, pain and vomiting occur alongside a markedly raised lipase. Even when the pain eases, changes in breathing or circulation can still signal a systemic problem.","readingAim":"If pain eases but breathing worsens, what would you reassess?","prerequisite":"inflammation","skill":"s046","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Pancreatic Enzyme Activation","Acute pancreatitis"],"sources":[{"title":"The Exocrine Pancreas: digestive enzymes","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK54127/"},{"title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Gastroenterology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/pancreatitis/acute-pancreatitis"},{"title":"Acute Pancreatitis - Gastrointestinal Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/pancreatitis/acute-pancreatitis?ruleredirectid=741"}]},{"id":"focus-s047","definition":"Intestinal obstruction blocks movement through the bowel, while ischemia means intestinal tissue receives inadequate blood flow. Perforation is a breach that can allow intestinal contents to cause peritonitis.","structure":"The intestine is a hollow digestive tube with a wall supplied by blood flow. Obstruction concerns movement through its lumen; ischemia concerns the bowel wall's blood supply, and perforation can expose the abdominal lining to intestinal contents.","normal":"Normally, intestinal contents move through the bowel while blood flow supplies the intestinal wall. These functions support the bowel's activity without provoking peritoneal irritation; the structures and processes involved are distinct, even though more than one can be affected in a deteriorating patient.","change":"With obstruction, passage is impaired and pain, vomiting, distension, and inability to pass stool may occur. With ischemia, reduced or interrupted blood flow threatens tissue; injury may progress to peritonitis. A perforation can also cause peritonitis, and either process may contribute to shock.","cues":"Vomiting, obstipation, distension, or changed bowel sounds can suggest obstruction. Severe pain that greatly exceeds early abdominal tenderness raises concern for ischemia, especially with vascular or low-flow risks; a soft abdomen or initially normal lactate does not reliably exclude it.","distinction":"A soft abdomen early in severe pain does not rule out intestinal ischemia. Peritoneal signs may develop later with advancing injury, so do not treat their absence or a single test result as reassurance.","related":["gut","perfusion","ischemia"],"title":"Intestinal Flow and Injury","clinicalBridge":"Tissue perfusion is central to interpreting this case: sudden severe diffuse pain with little tenderness may reflect threatened intestinal blood flow, especially with atrial fibrillation as a relevant risk. A normal initial lactate does not settle the concern; communicate the pattern and its evolution.","readingAim":"What would you predict as ischemia advances?","prerequisite":"gut","skill":"s047","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Intestinal Flow and Injury","Obstruction, ischemia and perforation"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia"},{"title":"Acute Abdomen - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459328/"}]},{"id":"focus-s048","definition":"Abdominal compartment syndrome occurs when sustained pressure within the abdomen compromises organ function. Pressure elevation alone is not enough to establish the syndrome.","structure":"The abdominal cavity contains organs including the intestines and kidneys, while the diaphragm forms its upper boundary. Rising pressure in this enclosed space can affect venous return, cardiac output, breathing, and organ perfusion rather than remaining a local abdominal finding.","normal":"Normally, the abdominal contents and diaphragm allow circulation, breathing, and kidney function to proceed without pressure-related compromise. Venous return supports cardiac output, the diaphragm contributes to ventilation, and blood flow supports renal and intestinal function; changes in pressure can disturb several of these processes.","change":"When abdominal pressure rises and remains elevated, venous return and cardiac output can fall, the diaphragm can be displaced upward and ventilation impaired, and renal and intestinal perfusion can decrease. The syndrome is associated with new organ dysfunction, not pressure elevation in isolation.","cues":"In a critically ill adult, abdominal distension alongside falling urine output, rising ventilator pressures, or hemodynamic worsening raises concern for pressure-related organ dysfunction. These findings have other possible causes, and abdominal appearance or palpation alone cannot confirm or exclude the syndrome.","distinction":"A distended or tense abdomen is a warning sign, not a pressure measurement. Pressure elevation without new organ dysfunction is not, by itself, abdominal compartment syndrome.","related":["compartment","peritoneum","diaphragm"],"title":"Abdominal Pressure Effects","clinicalBridge":"Fascia and compartment pressure provides a useful framework for this case: pressure within the abdominal space can constrain venous return, elevate the diaphragm, and impair kidney function. The worsening urine output, ventilator pressures, and circulation form a concerning trend, but require objective assessment and consideration of other causes.","readingAim":"Which organ changes would you predict from rising abdominal pressure?","prerequisite":"compartment","skill":"s048","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Abdominal Pressure Effects","Abdominal compartment syndrome"],"sources":[{"title":"Abdominal Compartment Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430932/"},{"title":"Intra-abdominal hypertension/abdominal compartment syndrome of ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12273658/"}]},{"id":"focus-s049","definition":"Abdominal trauma can injure the abdominal wall, organs, or blood vessels. Bleeding may remain internal, while hollow-organ leakage can lead to peritonitis.","structure":"The abdomen includes its wall, solid and hollow organs, and blood vessels. After blunt or penetrating injury, damage may be internal and not visible at the skin, while injury to a hollow organ can affect the surrounding abdominal lining.","normal":"Normally, the abdominal wall and organs remain intact, and blood vessels support circulation without internal bleeding. Hollow organs contain their contents; when their walls are intact, leakage does not provoke peritonitis. An early examination can provide only a view of findings present at that time.","change":"Trauma can damage vessels and cause internal bleeding, reducing effective circulation and potentially contributing to shock. A hollow-organ injury can allow contents to leak and cause peritonitis. Pain or tenderness may initially be mild, obscured by other injuries, or difficult to assess reliably.","cues":"After trauma, increasing tachycardia, altered attention, hypotension, reduced urine output, abdominal tenderness, or distension can raise concern for internal injury. A seat belt mark or mild early discomfort neither confirms nor excludes injury; trends and the mechanism matter.","distinction":"A reassuring early abdominal examination does not prove that internal injury is absent, particularly when another injury distracts from abdominal symptoms. A negative FAST also does not exclude every injury.","related":["peritoneum","perfusion","shock"],"title":"Hidden Abdominal Injury","clinicalBridge":"Circulatory shock helps connect this case's rising heart rate and declining attention to possible internal bleeding after trauma. The initial mild abdominal discomfort does not settle the question, especially with a distracting leg injury; changing vital signs and examination reliability deserve continued attention and communication.","readingAim":"What would you predict if internal bleeding progresses?","prerequisite":"peritoneum","skill":"s049","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Hidden Abdominal Injury","Abdominal trauma"],"sources":[{"title":"Overview of Abdominal Trauma - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/abdominal-trauma/overview-of-abdominal-trauma"},{"title":"Blunt Abdominal Trauma - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431087/"}]},{"id":"focus-s050","definition":"A gastrointestinal anastomosis is a surgically created join between parts of the digestive tract. If the join fails, intestinal contents and bacteria can reach surrounding tissues.","structure":"The anastomosis is the surgical connection between bowel segments, with surrounding abdominal tissues nearby. Its integrity matters because a defect can allow intestinal contents and bacteria to enter the area around the join.","normal":"When the join remains intact, intestinal contents stay within the digestive tract. Surrounding tissues are not exposed to bowel contents through a defect, so the anastomosis does not create a route for local contamination.","change":"If the join fails, intestinal contents and bacteria may enter surrounding tissues. This can produce localized inflammation or an abscess; spread through the abdomen may lead to peritonitis and sepsis. Poor blood supply, tension, and technical failure can contribute.","cues":"After surgery, fever, unexplained tachycardia, increasing abdominal pain, leukocytosis, or hypotension can raise concern, especially when several findings evolve together. A nearby collection on CT may support concern, but neither one symptom nor a negative scan settles the question.","distinction":"A negative scan does not always exclude a leak, and a nearby collection alone does not establish its extent. Interpret findings alongside the patient’s stability and clinical course.","related":["anastomosis","peritoneum","inflammation"],"title":"Anastomotic Leak Concern","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article “Anastomosis and tissue healing” explains how a surgical join depends on tissue integrity and blood supply. Here, connect that structure to nearby contamination: a failed join can seed local inflammation or an abscess, while spread may produce peritonitis and sepsis; the clinical course remains important.","readingAim":"What changes would make a possible leak more concerning despite an earlier negative scan?","prerequisite":"anastomosis","skill":"s050","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Anastomotic Leak Concern","After gastrointestinal surgery"],"sources":[{"title":"Operative Management of Anastomotic Leaks after ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6494610/"},{"title":"Treatment and Prevention of Postoperative Leakage after ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10299500/"}]},{"id":"focus-s051","definition":"Refeeding risk is a potentially harmful metabolic response when nutrition resumes after prolonged poor intake or malnutrition. Electrolyte shifts, increased thiamine demand, and fluid changes can affect several organ systems.","structure":"The relevant context is the body’s intracellular electrolyte and vitamin stores during prolonged poor intake, followed by renewed nutrition. Phosphate, potassium, and magnesium shifts can affect cardiac, respiratory, muscular, and neurologic function.","normal":"During starvation, intracellular electrolyte and vitamin stores can become depleted. When nutrition resumes, glucose and insulin rise; this response shifts phosphorus and potassium into cells, magnesium may also fall, and glucose metabolism increases thiamine demand.","change":"If stores are depleted, these shifts can further lower measured phosphate, potassium, or magnesium. Phosphate depletion is a key clue, while increased thiamine demand and sodium and fluid retention may add strain. Together, these changes can impair organ function.","cues":"A history of prolonged poor intake, weight loss, or low baseline electrolytes raises concern. After nutrition resumes, falling phosphate, potassium, or magnesium with new weakness, breathing difficulty, confusion, edema, or rhythm changes warrants attention, but no single finding establishes the cause.","distinction":"A low phosphate result alone is not the whole pattern. Consider the timing of nutrition, trends in several electrolytes, symptoms, and possible fluid-related effects rather than interpreting one result in isolation.","related":["phosphate","insulin","fluid"],"title":"Refeeding-Related Changes","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article “Phosphate and cellular energy” helps explain why falling phosphate can matter beyond a laboratory result. In refeeding risk, renewed nutrition shifts electrolytes and increases thiamine demand; connect the timing and trends with new weakness or breathing difficulty rather than viewing each finding alone.","readingAim":"Which trends and new symptoms would make electrolyte changes after nutrition resumes more concerning?","prerequisite":"phosphate","skill":"s051","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Refeeding-Related Changes","Nutrition and refeeding risk"],"sources":[{"title":"Refeeding Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564513/"},{"title":"Refeeding syndrome: a challenging entity","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10123452/"}]},{"id":"focus-s052","definition":"Acute anemia is a rapid reduction in circulating red cells, often from hemorrhage or hemolysis. It can impair oxygen delivery before the body has time to compensate.","structure":"Red cells circulate through the blood and carry oxygen to tissues. In acute blood loss, red cells and plasma leave the circulation together, while hemolysis can also reduce circulating red cells and impair oxygen delivery.","normal":"With red cells circulating in the blood, oxygen can be delivered to tissues. Hemoglobin and hematocrit measurements describe the blood’s red-cell content, but their meaning depends on the timing and context of a suspected acute loss.","change":"In sudden bleeding, red cells and plasma are lost together, so early hemoglobin and hematocrit may appear unchanged. As plasma volume is restored, measured anemia may become more apparent. Hemolysis is another possible cause of an acute reduction in circulating red cells.","cues":"Tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, cool or clammy skin, altered mental status, or a suspected bleeding source can signal impaired perfusion. These cues are not specific to anemia, and an initially reassuring hemoglobin does not rule out acute blood loss.","distinction":"Do not treat an early normal hemoglobin as proof that blood volume is safe after sudden bleeding. Interpret the result with the patient’s stability, possible cause, and changing clinical signs.","related":["hemoglobin","oxygen-debt","perfusion","cells","marrow"],"title":"Acute Anemia and Delivery","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article “Hemoglobin and oxygen delivery” links circulating hemoglobin with oxygen transport. In suspected acute blood loss, red cells and plasma may leave together, so an early hemoglobin can appear unchanged even as perfusion worsens; connect bedside changes with the possibility of evolving anemia.","readingAim":"Why might signs of poor perfusion matter more than an initially reassuring hemoglobin after acute blood loss?","prerequisite":"hemoglobin","skill":"s052","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Acute Anemia and Delivery","Anemia and oxygen delivery"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Anemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537232/"},{"title":"Acute Anemia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK537232/"}]},{"id":"focus-s053","definition":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation, or DIC, is widespread clotting activation that consumes platelets and coagulation factors. It can cause both bleeding and injury to organs from microvascular clots.","structure":"DIC involves clotting activity throughout the circulation rather than only at one site. Platelets and coagulation factors are consumed as thrombin and fibrin form, while fibrinolysis breaks down fibrin and generates D-dimer and other products.","normal":"Platelets and coagulation factors are available to support clot formation. In DIC, clotting becomes widespread, generating thrombin and fibrin throughout the circulation; fibrinolysis also breaks down fibrin, producing D-dimer and other products.","change":"As widespread clotting continues, platelets and coagulation factors are consumed. This can leave the patient vulnerable to bleeding while microvascular clots injure organs. Rapid DIC may cause bleeding; slower DIC more often presents with venous or arterial thrombosis.","cues":"Unexplained bleeding or thrombosis in a high-risk condition can raise concern. Falling platelets or fibrinogen, lengthening PT or PTT, and high D-dimer may support a changing pattern, but no single result establishes DIC and trends matter.","distinction":"DIC is not simply a bleeding problem: widespread clot formation can consume the materials needed to control bleeding and can injure organs. A near-normal early result does not rule out a slower evolving pattern.","related":["platelets","inflammation","liver"],"title":"DIC and Consumption","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article “Platelets and the clotting cascade” introduces the components consumed in DIC. Connect falling platelets and fibrinogen with widespread thrombin and fibrin formation: the same process can contribute to bleeding at one site and microvascular organ injury elsewhere.","readingAim":"How can widespread clotting activation produce both bleeding and organ injury?","prerequisite":"platelets","skill":"s053","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["DIC and Consumption","DIC and platelet consumption"],"sources":[{"title":"Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) - Hematology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/coagulation-disorders/disseminated-intravascular-coagulation-dic"},{"title":"Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) - Blood ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/blood-disorders/bleeding-due-to-clotting-disorders/disseminated-intravascular-coagulation-dic"}]},{"id":"focus-s054","definition":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is an immune reaction in which antibodies activate platelets after binding to platelet factor 4 and heparin. Platelet numbers may fall while the risk of clotting rises.","structure":"Platelets are small circulating cell fragments that help form clots. In HIT, the relevant context is platelet factor 4 (PF4) bound to heparin in the bloodstream, where antibodies can engage and activate platelets.","normal":"Normally, platelets circulate in the blood and help limit bleeding by gathering at sites of vessel injury and supporting clot formation. Their number and activity are balanced with the body's need to maintain blood flow through uninjured vessels.","change":"In immune-mediated HIT, antibodies bind PF4-heparin complexes and activate platelets, increasing thrombin generation and clot formation. Activated platelets are also consumed, so the platelet count can decline even as the central danger shifts toward thrombosis rather than bleeding.","cues":"A platelet decline several days after heparin exposure, especially alongside a new or worsening clot, raises concern for HIT. The count alone is not diagnostic; consider the exposure timeline, possible thrombosis, and other explanations for the decline.","distinction":"A low platelet count does not by itself mean a bleeding disorder. In HIT, platelet activation and consumption can occur together, so the heparin timeline and evidence of thrombosis matter.","related":["platelets","arterial","perfusion"],"title":"Heparin-Related Platelet Activation","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Platelets helps explain why a falling count does not always signal bleeding risk alone. In HIT, antibody-driven platelet activation promotes thrombin and clot formation, so connect the laboratory trend to heparin exposure timing and possible thrombosis.","readingAim":"What combination of exposure timing and findings would raise concern for HIT?","prerequisite":"platelets","skill":"s054","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Heparin-Related Platelet Activation","HIT versus immune thrombocytopenia"],"sources":[{"title":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3097589/"},{"title":"Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK482330/"}]},{"id":"focus-s055","definition":"Febrile neutropenia is fever occurring when neutrophil defenses are reduced. Because these cells help contain infection, inflammation may be less visible even when infection is serious.","structure":"Neutrophils are white blood cells found in circulation and tissues, supplied by the bone marrow. They help respond to bacteria and fungi at body sites such as the mouth, lungs, abdomen, urinary tract, skin, and vascular access sites.","normal":"Normally, neutrophils help recognize and contain invading organisms, contributing to local inflammatory signs such as redness, swelling, or pus. Their presence supports a visible response at an infected site, while circulating counts can help describe the body's available defense.","change":"With profound neutropenia, fewer neutrophils are available to contain organisms, including microbes from normal mouth or gut flora. Local inflammatory signs may be muted, so infection can advance without the redness, pus, leukocytosis, or urinary white cells often expected.","cues":"Fever in a patient with known or possible neutropenia is concerning, even when examination findings are subtle. Check for changes across likely infection sites and consider treatment history and the neutrophil trend; absence of a clear focus does not rule out infection.","distinction":"A quiet examination is not an all-clear in neutropenia. Fever may be the clearest warning because the weakened inflammatory response can hide usual local signs.","related":["neutrophils","barrier","inflammation","marrow"],"title":"Fever with Neutropenia","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Neutrophils clarifies why fever can matter even when redness or pus is absent. When neutrophil defenses are reduced, organisms may spread while local inflammation is muted; interpret the patient's fever alongside the count, symptoms, and treatment context.","readingAim":"Why might fever be important when local signs of infection are absent?","prerequisite":"neutrophils","skill":"s055","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Fever with Neutropenia","Febrile neutropenia"],"sources":[{"title":"Neutropenia - Hematology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia"},{"title":"Neutropenia - Neutropenia - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/hematology/leukopenias/neutropenia?ruleredirectid=746autoredirectid=37881"}]},{"id":"focus-s056","definition":"Tumor lysis syndrome occurs when rapid tumor-cell breakdown releases substances that overwhelm the body's ability to clear them. The resulting electrolyte and waste-product changes can affect the kidneys and heart.","structure":"The relevant structures include tumor cells, blood, and kidney tubules. When tumor cells break down, potassium, phosphate, and nucleic acids enter the blood; nucleic acids are converted to uric acid, and the kidneys must clear this increased load.","normal":"Normally, the kidneys filter blood and excrete waste products, while electrolyte levels support cell and heart function. Tumor-cell turnover releases substances gradually enough for the body to process and clear them without a sudden, harmful accumulation.","change":"With rapid tumor-cell destruction, potassium and phosphate rise in the blood, while nucleic acids are metabolized into uric acid. When kidney clearance cannot keep pace, uric acid and calcium phosphate may injure renal tubules; electrolyte shifts can lower calcium and contribute to dangerous rhythms.","cues":"A changing pattern of potassium, phosphate, uric acid, calcium, and kidney-function results is concerning in a patient with a high-burden or treatment-sensitive cancer. Weakness, nausea, reduced urine output, cramps, confusion, or arrhythmias may occur but are not specific.","distinction":"Tumor lysis can occur before cancer treatment begins, so treatment timing alone does not exclude it. Interpret the laboratory pattern in the clinical context rather than treating one abnormal result as diagnostic.","related":["nephron","potassium","phosphate"],"title":"Tumor Lysis and Renal Load","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Nephron helps connect rising waste products to kidney injury. During rapid tumor-cell breakdown, uric acid and calcium phosphate can accumulate when renal clearance cannot keep pace; relate kidney-function trends to the accompanying electrolyte pattern and cancer context.","readingAim":"What pattern of metabolic and kidney changes would make tumor lysis concerning?","prerequisite":"nephron","skill":"s056","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Tumor Lysis and Renal Load","Tumor lysis syndrome"],"sources":[{"title":"Management of Adverse Effects of Cancer Therapy - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/oncology/principles-of-cancer-therapy/management-of-adverse-effects-of-cancer-therapy"},{"title":"Tumor Lysis Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK518985/"}]},{"id":"focus-s057","definition":"A transfusion reaction is a harmful response that develops during or after a blood-product transfusion. Causes and threats vary, so a new concerning change should not be assumed to have a single explanation.","structure":"The relevant context is the patient's circulation during or after a blood-product infusion. Blood cells, donor antibodies, inflammatory mediators, and infused volume can interact with the recipient, producing effects that involve the blood, lungs, and cardiovascular system.","normal":"Normally, transfused blood components circulate to support the recipient's needs without provoking harmful reactions. The patient's breathing, circulation, and temperature remain without a new concerning change attributable to the infusion.","change":"In a reaction, incompatibility, donor antibodies, inflammatory mediators, contamination, or excess volume can produce overlapping symptoms. Depending on the mechanism, effects may include red-cell destruction, shock, allergic airway symptoms, or pulmonary edema.","cues":"New fever, chills, itching, hives, pain, dyspnea, hypotension, or red urine during or after transfusion can signal a reaction, but no single cue establishes its cause. Flank pain and red urine raise concern for hemolysis; crackles and jugular venous distension may suggest volume overload.","distinction":"A mild-looking initial symptom does not rule out progression or identify the reaction type. Fever and breathlessness can occur with different mechanisms, so interpret the pattern rather than relying on one sign.","related":["hemoglobin","fluid","oxygenation"],"title":"Recognizing Transfusion Reactions","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Hemoglobin helps frame why red-cell destruction can affect the patient's circulation and produce red urine. During transfusion, however, symptoms overlap across immune, inflammatory, and volume-related reactions, so connect each new cue to the broader clinical pattern.","readingAim":"Which accompanying cues could help distinguish possible transfusion-reaction mechanisms?","prerequisite":"hemoglobin","skill":"s057","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Transfusion Reactions","Transfusion reactions"],"sources":[{"title":"Transfusion Reactions","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK482202/"},{"title":"Table 3.2c, [Transfusion Reactions and Related Nursing Interventions[]].","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK594497/table/ch3bloodproducts.T.transfusion_reactions/"}]},{"id":"focus-s058","definition":"Systemic lupus erythematosus is an immune-mediated inflammatory disease that can affect several organs. Kidney injury may develop without obvious urinary symptoms.","structure":"The kidneys filter blood through glomeruli within each nephron, while small vessels and surrounding tissues support filtration. In lupus, immune-driven inflammation can affect these filtering structures, allowing findings of kidney injury to appear in urine even when the person feels no urinary discomfort.","normal":"Normally, glomeruli filter blood while retaining blood cells and most large proteins; the nephron then adjusts the filtrate as it becomes urine. This coordinated process supports waste removal and fluid balance while keeping red cells and much of the body’s protein in circulation.","change":"With lupus-related kidney inflammation, filtering structures can become injured and less selective. Protein may pass into urine, and red cells can enter the filtrate, producing proteinuria or red-cell casts. Kidney involvement may also accompany hypertension or edema, yet remain quiet from the patient’s perspective.","cues":"Ankle swelling and elevated blood pressure alongside urine protein or red-cell casts can signal possible kidney involvement, even without urinary discomfort. These findings do not establish lupus nephritis by themselves; consider the broader clinical pattern and promptly communicate concerning changes.","distinction":"A positive ANA is a sensitive screening clue, not proof of lupus or kidney injury. Fatigue alone is nonspecific, so organ-specific evidence and the overall pattern matter.","related":["nephron","filtrate","inflammation"],"title":"Lupus-Related Kidney Injury","clinicalBridge":"The nephron normally filters blood while keeping blood cells in circulation. In this case, protein and red-cell casts in urine, paired with swelling and elevated blood pressure, suggest possible disruption of that filtering function and warrant focused communication despite absent urinary discomfort.","readingAim":"What urine and blood-pressure changes would raise concern for kidney involvement?","prerequisite":"nephron","skill":"s058","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Lupus-Related Kidney Injury","Autoimmunity and organ injury"],"sources":[{"title":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Rheumatology and ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/musculoskeletal-and-connective-tissue-disorders/systemic-rheumatic-diseases/systemic-lupus-erythematosus-sle"},{"title":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/en-pr/professional/musculoskeletal-and-connective-tissue-disorders/autoimmune-rheumatic-disorders/systemic-lupus-erythematosus-sle"}]},{"id":"focus-s059","definition":"Diabetic ketoacidosis develops when insulin action is insufficient and the body produces ketones from fat. Ketones contribute to metabolic acidosis, while high glucose promotes fluid and electrolyte loss.","structure":"The liver, bloodstream, kidneys, and body tissues participate in this process. When insulin is insufficient, fat breakdown supplies fatty acids that the liver converts to ketones; these circulate in blood, while glucose in urine draws water and electrolytes out of the body.","normal":"Normally, insulin helps cells use glucose and limits excessive fat breakdown. The liver and other tissues maintain a balance of fuels, while the kidneys regulate water and electrolytes. This keeps ketone production from overwhelming the body’s acid-base balance.","change":"When insulin is insufficient, counterregulatory hormones promote glucose production and fat breakdown. The liver converts released fatty acids into ketones that contribute to acidosis. Meanwhile, high glucose causes urinary water and electrolyte losses, which can reduce circulating volume and impair perfusion.","cues":"Vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration, tachycardia, and deep labored breathing can accompany ketonemia and acidosis. In this case, low potassium adds concern because insulin can lower serum potassium further; interpret these cues with laboratory findings and the clinical context.","distinction":"A glucose result alone does not establish or exclude DKA. Ketones and acidosis are important parts of the pattern, and illness or other causes can produce overlapping findings.","related":["insulin","ketones","buffer"],"title":"Ketones and Diabetic Acidosis","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term ketones links fat breakdown to the acidosis in this case. Deep breathing can accompany that acid-base disturbance, while vomiting and dehydration reflect fluid losses. Low potassium is also important context because insulin can shift potassium into cells.","readingAim":"How would ketones and acid-base findings change your interpretation of glucose and deep breathing?","prerequisite":"insulin","skill":"s059","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Ketones and Diabetic Acidosis","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"sources":[{"title":"Hyperglycemic Crises","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/endotext/hyperglycm-dka-hhs/"},{"title":"Acute Complications of Diabetes Mellitus - Endocrinology","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/diabetes-mellitus-and-hypoglycemia/acute-complications-of-diabetes-mellitus"}]},{"id":"focus-s060","definition":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state is a pattern of severe hyperglycemia and dehydration with elevated serum osmolality. Ketones and acidosis are often limited, though presentations can overlap with DKA.","structure":"The kidneys filter blood and regulate water and electrolytes, while the bloodstream carries glucose and water to tissues. When glucose spills into urine, water follows; rising serum osmolality can then affect the brain and alter mental status.","normal":"Normally, the kidneys conserve or excrete water as needed, helping maintain circulating volume and a stable concentration of dissolved substances in blood. The brain functions within this regulated environment, supporting attention, movement, and awareness.","change":"In HHS, glucose in urine draws water and electrolytes with it, producing substantial fluid loss and reduced kidney perfusion. Dehydration can worsen hyperglycemia and raise serum osmolality; this hypertonicity may impair brain function, causing confusion or other neurological changes.","cues":"Several days of frequent urination and poor intake followed by marked dryness and confusion can fit this mechanism, especially with high glucose and osmolality but limited ketosis or acidosis. These cues suggest HHS but do not rule out overlap or another cause.","distinction":"A normal or high serum potassium does not show that total body potassium stores are adequate. Prominent ketones and acidosis may point toward DKA or a mixed presentation rather than classic HHS.","related":["fluid","sodium","cerebral"],"title":"Hyperglycemia and Hyperosmolarity","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term fluid compartments helps connect urinary water loss with profound dehydration and reduced kidney perfusion. As serum osmolality rises, the brain may be affected, linking this patient’s confusion to the metabolic pattern without treating confusion alone as a diagnosis.","readingAim":"How might ongoing urinary water loss affect mental status and kidney perfusion?","prerequisite":"fluid","skill":"s060","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Hyperglycemia and Hyperosmolarity","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state"],"sources":[{"title":"Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar Syndrome","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/endotext/hyper_synd/"},{"title":"DIAGNOSIS AND...","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK620700/"}]},{"id":"focus-s061","definition":"Hypoglycemia occurs when glucose availability falls below what the body needs, particularly the brain. Symptoms can range from sweating and tremor to confusion or reduced consciousness.","structure":"The bloodstream carries glucose to the brain, where neurons depend on it as a key fuel. Insulin and food intake influence glucose availability; a delayed meal after insulin exposure can shift the balance and leave the brain with less usable fuel.","normal":"Normally, food supplies glucose to the bloodstream, and insulin helps regulate its movement into tissues. The brain receives glucose through the circulation to support neuronal activity. Counter-regulatory responses can help defend glucose, although warning symptoms may be less reliable in some people.","change":"When glucose falls, the brain may receive less fuel, disrupting neuronal function and causing confusion, unusual behavior, or reduced consciousness. Sweating and tremor may accompany the body’s response. Recurrent episodes, chronic illness, or long-standing diabetes can blunt these warning signs.","cues":"After insulin exposure and a delayed meal, sweating followed by confusion with a low bedside glucose supports concern for hypoglycemia. The sequence is meaningful but not conclusive by itself; consider the measured result, symptoms, intake, medication timing, and illness together.","distinction":"Confusion can be a hypoglycemia cue even when sweating or tremor is absent, because warning symptoms may be blunted. New confusion should not be attributed to low glucose without assessment of the broader context.","related":["neurons","arousal","insulin"],"title":"Glucose and Brain Function","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term neurons makes clear why a falling glucose level can affect attention and behavior: the brain relies on glucose as fuel. Here, insulin exposure and a delayed meal precede sweating and confusion, a pattern that calls for assessment of glucose and context together.","readingAim":"What changes might follow if glucose availability to the brain continues to fall?","prerequisite":"neurons","skill":"s061","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Glucose and Brain Function","Hypoglycemia and inpatient diabetes"],"sources":[{"title":"Understanding hypoglycemia in hospitalized patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4153389/"},{"title":"Hypoglycemia (Nursing) - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568695/"}]},{"id":"focus-s062","definition":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) helps the kidneys conserve water. Too little effective ADH activity can cause large volumes of dilute urine, while too much can dilute the sodium in the blood.","structure":"The mechanism links circulating antidiuretic hormone with the kidney tubules, where water is retained, and with the bloodstream, where serum sodium and osmolality reflect the balance between water and solute.","normal":"When effective ADH activity is appropriate, the kidneys retain water in a way that helps maintain fluid balance. Urine output and concentration, serum sodium and osmolality, and the person’s fluid status can be considered together to understand this balance.","change":"With deficient ADH release or an impaired kidney response, less water is retained, so urine may become abundant and dilute while dehydration and rising serum sodium and osmolality develop. Excess ADH instead retains water, diluting serum sodium and osmolality and risking cerebral edema.","cues":"In this case, repeated thirst and large volumes of clear urine occur alongside dry mucosa and rising serum sodium and osmolality, with low urine osmolality. Together, these cues suggest impaired ADH effect, but assessment and team evaluation are needed.","distinction":"Thirst or frequent urination alone does not establish DI. Interpret urine findings, serum trends, fluid status, and neurologic findings together; a normal-looking fluid examination does not rule out SIADH.","related":["adh","nephron","sodium","fluid"],"title":"ADH and Water Balance","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Antidiuretic hormone helps connect the kidney’s water-retaining response to this patient’s urine and serum pattern. Use that link to predict how reduced effective ADH activity could produce dilute urine while serum sodium and osmolality rise, without treating one cue as a diagnosis.","readingAim":"If effective ADH activity falls, what changes would you predict in urine concentration and serum trends?","prerequisite":"adh","skill":"s062","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["ADH and Water Balance","Water balance: DI and SIADH"],"sources":[{"title":"21.4 Pituitary Disorders - Medical-Surgical Nursing","url":"https://openstax.org/books/medical-surgical-nursing/pages/21-4-pituitary-disorders"},{"title":"Disorders of sodium balance - PMC","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1410848/"}]},{"id":"focus-s063","definition":"Adrenal crisis occurs when cortisol availability cannot meet the body’s usual or stress-related needs. It can cause dangerously low blood pressure and may occur during illness or after steroid interruption.","structure":"The relevant context is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and adrenal glands, which provide cortisol for stress needs. In primary adrenal failure, aldosterone may also be deficient; central disruption usually preserves aldosterone.","normal":"Cortisol supports the body’s response to stress by helping maintain blood-vessel responsiveness to catecholamines and supporting glucose production. With adequate cortisol for current needs, these functions help the circulation and glucose supply meet physiologic demands.","change":"When cortisol is insufficient for basal or stress needs, vascular responsiveness and glucose production can be impaired, contributing to hypotension, shock, or hypoglycemia. Primary adrenal failure may also reduce aldosterone, worsening sodium loss and potassium retention; central failure usually preserves aldosterone.","cues":"Here, recent abrupt interruption of long-term glucocorticoids and gastrointestinal illness precede vomiting, weakness, hypotension, and declining alertness. This combination raises concern for crisis and warrants prompt escalation; a potassium result within the reference range does not settle the question.","distinction":"Normal potassium does not exclude adrenal crisis, particularly with central adrenal insufficiency, where aldosterone is generally preserved. Consider the history, acute illness, blood pressure, and changing alertness together.","related":["cortisol","hypotension","shock"],"title":"Cortisol and Adrenal Crisis","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Cortisol and stress response explains why illness can raise the body’s need for cortisol and why interruption of chronic glucocorticoids matters. In this case, connect that stress demand to vascular responsiveness, hypotension, and reduced alertness, while recognizing that normal potassium does not exclude concern.","readingAim":"If illness increases cortisol need after steroid interruption, which changes would you expect to communicate?","prerequisite":"cortisol","skill":"s063","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Cortisol and Adrenal Crisis","Adrenal crisis"],"sources":[{"title":"Adrenal Crisis - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499968/"},{"title":"Adrenal Insufficiency","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/endotext/adren-insufficiency/"}]},{"id":"focus-s064","definition":"Severe thyroid dysfunction can become a life-threatening emergency. Thyroid storm involves acute worsening of severe hyperthyroidism, while myxedema coma is a life-threatening complication of longstanding hypothyroidism.","structure":"The thyroid gland produces hormones whose activity affects the body broadly. Severe changes can appear in multiple systems, so the relevant context includes temperature, mental status, gastrointestinal symptoms, breathing, and cardiovascular findings rather than one isolated sign.","normal":"In ordinary circumstances, thyroid hormone activity supports the body’s ongoing physiologic state. Assessment considers the person’s history and overall pattern of function; a single laboratory result or symptom does not provide the whole picture.","change":"With severe hyperthyroidism, an acute worsening can bring fever, restlessness or confusion, gastrointestinal symptoms, tachycardia, or cardiovascular collapse. Longstanding hypothyroidism can instead progress to profound hypothermia, reduced responsiveness, seizures, or respiratory depression, often during a precipitating illness or exposure.","cues":"In this case, infection is followed by fever, marked agitation, vomiting, a rapid irregular pulse, and increasing confusion in a person with hyperthyroidism. This pattern is concerning for thyroid storm and calls for prompt escalation, without waiting for thyroid test results.","distinction":"A normal T3 does not rule out hypothyroidism; it may remain normal despite primary thyroid failure. Interpret thyroid tests alongside the clinical pattern and history.","related":["thyroid","temperature","arousal"],"title":"Severe Thyroid Emergencies","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Thyroid hormone and metabolism helps frame why severe thyroid dysfunction can affect several body systems at once. Here, infection precedes fever, agitation, vomiting, a rapid irregular pulse, and confusion, a concerning pattern that merits prompt escalation rather than waiting for a single test result.","readingAim":"If hyperthyroid features worsen during infection, which new cues would make you escalate promptly?","prerequisite":"thyroid","skill":"s064","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Severe Thyroid Emergencies","Severe thyroid dysfunction"],"sources":[{"title":"Hyperthyroidism","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/thyroid-disorders/hyperthyroidism"},{"title":"Hypothyroidism - Endocrinology - Merck Manual Professional...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/thyroid-disorders/hypothyroidism"}]},{"id":"focus-s065","definition":"Pressure injury develops when sustained pressure restricts small-vessel blood flow and damages tissue. Friction, shear, moisture, limited mobility, and reduced tissue tolerance can add to the risk.","structure":"The relevant structures are the skin and underlying tissues over bony prominences or beneath medical devices, where sustained pressure can restrict small-vessel blood flow. Mobility, circulation, moisture, and support surfaces shape the local context.","normal":"With adequate blood flow and pressure relief, skin and underlying tissue receive oxygen and are less likely to be injured by sustained loading. Regular assessment and movement help identify areas where pressure, moisture, or friction may threaten tissue tolerance.","change":"Sustained pressure can reduce small-vessel blood flow and oxygen delivery, damaging tissue; friction and shear add injury, while moisture can weaken skin. Limited mobility, impaired circulation, chronic illness, and inadequate nutrition can further reduce tissue tolerance.","cues":"The patient cannot reposition independently, and the heel has persistent redness that does not fade with light pressure, along with tenderness. This combination is consistent with an early pressure injury and signals a need for prompt pressure relief and communication.","distinction":"Blanchable redness is a warning to relieve pressure, not proof of a pressure injury. Persistent nonblanchable color change on intact skin suggests injury, while purple or maroon change may signal deeper damage.","related":["skin","perfusion","interstitium"],"title":"Pressure and Skin Injury","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Skin layers and pressure links a sustained load over the heel to restricted small-vessel flow and reduced oxygen delivery. In this patient, persistent nonblanchable redness and tenderness are cues for prompt pressure relief and communication, not findings to leave until a later reassessment.","readingAim":"If heel redness persists without blanching, what tissue change would you anticipate and what should follow?","prerequisite":"skin","skill":"s065","unit":"skin","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Pressure and Skin Injury","Pressure injury and skin perfusion"],"sources":[{"title":"Pressure Injury Prevention and Wound Management - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK543831/"},{"title":"Pressure Injuries - Pressure Injuries - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-pt/professional/dermatologic-disorders/pressure-injury/pressure-injuries"}]},{"id":"focus-s066","definition":"Infiltration or extravasation happens when infused fluid or medication leaves a vessel and enters nearby tissue. Some leaked medications can injure that tissue, depending on the substance and exposure.","structure":"The relevant structure is the vessel wall and the surrounding tissue at a peripheral or central access site. The cannula should deliver fluid into the vessel; leakage places infusate in nearby tissue, where exposure can irritate or injure cells.","normal":"Normally, an access device directs fluid through the vessel and into circulation, while the vessel wall keeps it separate from surrounding tissue. This allows the infusion to reach the body through the intended route without exposing nearby cells to the infusate.","change":"With infiltration or extravasation, fluid crosses out of the vessel and accumulates in surrounding tissue. Local exposure can cause irritation or injury, particularly with a vesicant. Continued delivery can increase the amount and duration of tissue exposure, even if some fluid still enters circulation.","cues":"New burning, tingling, pain, redness, or swelling near the access site, especially alongside a slowed or stopped infusion, can suggest leakage. Poor blood return may add concern, but no single finding confirms extravasation; interpret the pattern and site context.","distinction":"Some blood return or ongoing infusion does not rule out leakage. Pain along a vein alone may reflect irritation rather than extravasation, so consider it with swelling, skin changes, and infusion behavior.","related":["interstitium","exchange","barrier"],"title":"Infusion Leakage and Tissue Injury","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Interstitial space and extravasation explains why fluid outside a vessel can collect around cells rather than travel with blood. In this case, new burning plus swelling and slowed flow links access-site leakage to tissue exposure, without making any one cue definitive.","readingAim":"If burning begins during an infusion, what additional changes would make leakage more concerning?","prerequisite":"interstitium","skill":"s066","unit":"skin","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Infusion Leakage and Tissue Injury","Infiltration and extravasation"],"sources":[{"title":"Guidelines for the management of extravasation","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7431942/"},{"title":"Table 4.2c, [Complications/Unexpected Outcomes of CVADs].","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK594495/table/ch4centrallines.T.complicationsunexpecte/"}]},{"id":"focus-s067","definition":"Cellulitis is an infection of the skin and tissue beneath it. A deeper infection can spread through fascia and damage tissue rapidly, making early recognition important.","structure":"The affected structures are the skin, underlying soft tissue, and, in a deeper infection, fascia. A break in the skin can provide entry for organisms; spread into deeper tissue may impair its blood supply and lead to tissue damage.","normal":"Intact skin acts as a protective barrier against organisms entering underlying tissue. When that barrier is broken, local defenses and blood supply help contain and respond to infection, limiting its spread through skin and deeper structures.","change":"In cellulitis, infection involves the skin and tissue beneath it, producing localized inflammation. A necrotizing soft-tissue infection can advance through deeper tissue and fascia, impair blood supply, and cause tissue death. This deeper process may progress quickly beyond what the surface initially shows.","cues":"Warmth, redness, swelling, and tenderness can fit cellulitis, but do not establish its depth. Rapid spread, pain greater than visible changes, blisters, discoloration, numbness, or systemic illness raise concern for deeper infection and warrant prompt communication.","distinction":"A red, warm limb is not automatically a routine skin infection. Disproportionate pain or rapid progression can signal a deeper process, while muted pain does not exclude serious infection.","related":["barrier","inflammation","neutrophils"],"title":"Recognizing Deeper Skin Infection","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Protective barriers and infection helps connect a skin break with possible entry into underlying tissue. In this case, advancing redness, severe pain, a tense blister, fever, and confusion make deeper spread a concern, though no single surface finding determines the diagnosis.","readingAim":"Which new changes would shift your concern from localized cellulitis toward deeper infection?","prerequisite":"barrier","skill":"s067","unit":"skin","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Deeper Skin Infection","Cellulitis or deep infection?"],"sources":[{"title":"Cellulitis - Infectious Disease - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/infectious-diseases/bacterial-skin-infections/cellulitis"},{"title":"Necrotizing Soft-Tissue Infection - Infectious Disease","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/infectious-diseases/bacterial-skin-infections/necrotizing-soft-tissue-infection"}]},{"id":"focus-s068","definition":"Wound healing is the process by which damaged tissue repairs itself and the wound closes. Progress depends on viable tissue, adequate perfusion, suitable moisture, and advancing wound edges.","structure":"The relevant structures are the wound bed, its edges, and the surrounding tissue. Healing may occur with edges together, from the base when they cannot meet, or after delayed closure; perfusion and the local wound environment influence progress.","normal":"In a healing wound, viable tissue supports repair, appropriate moisture allows cells to move across the wound, and the edges advance or remain approximated. Adequate perfusion supplies the tissue, while the wound’s appearance changes in keeping with its healing course.","change":"Healing can stall when tissue is devitalized, perfusion is poor, infection or inflammation is present, moisture is excessive or inadequate, or edges are rolled or undermined. New separation, drainage, pain, or redness may indicate a change from the expected course.","cues":"Compare the wound with its earlier appearance. Increasing redness, drainage, pain, or separation can signal a problem; deeper tissue or organs visible through an opening is especially concerning. A stalled wound has several possible barriers, so report observed changes rather than assuming their cause.","distinction":"A wound does not necessarily heal faster when it is dried out; appropriate moisture supports repair, while excess moisture can damage surrounding edges. A stalled appearance alone does not identify which healing barrier is responsible.","related":["perfusion","inflammation","skin"],"title":"Wound Progress and Healing Barriers","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Tissue perfusion connects blood supply with the viability of wound tissue. In this case, new separation with more drainage, pain, and redness differs from an unchanged incision and may signal impaired healing; the observed change merits communication without assuming its cause.","readingAim":"If an incision newly separates, what changes from its prior appearance would you report?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s068","unit":"skin","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Wound Progress and Healing Barriers","Wounds and healing"],"sources":[{"title":"28.2 Wound Healing Process - Medical-Surgical Nursing","url":"https://openstax.org/books/medical-surgical-nursing/pages/28-2-wound-healing-process"},{"title":"Wound Dehiscence - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551712/"}]},{"id":"focus-s069","definition":"Acute compartment syndrome occurs when pressure rises within a closed space surrounded by fascia. The pressure can reduce tissue perfusion and injure muscle and nerves.","structure":"The affected structure is a closed fascial compartment, often in a limb after fracture, crush injury, or bleeding. Because fascia does not readily expand, swelling or blood within the space can compress its contents, including muscle, nerves, and small vessels.","normal":"Normally, a fascial compartment contains muscle, nerves, and vessels while allowing blood to reach the tissues and nerves to support sensation and movement. The surrounding fascia provides structure without obstructing this circulation or function.","change":"When swelling or bleeding increases pressure inside the closed compartment, venous outflow is impaired first, followed by reduced tissue perfusion. Ischemia can worsen swelling, creating a cycle that threatens muscle and nerve function and may cause irreversible injury.","cues":"Increasing pain out of proportion to the injury, pain with passive stretch, a tense compartment, or altered sensation can raise concern. These findings require attention to the overall pattern; a warm foot or palpable pulse does not exclude early compromise.","distinction":"A palpable pulse does not rule out compartment syndrome because tissue perfusion may be impaired while a larger artery still carries blood. Pallor, paralysis, or absent pulses are late findings, not prerequisites for concern.","related":["compartment","muscle","nerve"],"title":"Pressure Within a Fascial Compartment","clinicalBridge":"The foundation concept Fascia and compartment pressure explains why swelling inside a closed space can compress its contents. Here, worsening pain, a tense compartment, and pain with passive toe movement suggest threatened tissue perfusion even though the foot remains warm and a pulse is palpable.","readingAim":"With a palpable pulse after a fracture, what other changes could still suggest rising compartment pressure?","prerequisite":"compartment","skill":"s069","unit":"msk","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Pressure Within a Fascial Compartment","Acute compartment syndrome"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Compartment Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK448124/"},{"title":"Compartment Syndrome - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/fractures/compartment-syndrome"}]},{"id":"focus-s070","definition":"Fat embolism syndrome is a systemic illness that can follow fat droplets entering the microcirculation. It involves inflammation and impaired blood flow in small vessels, affecting the lungs, brain, skin, and other organs.","structure":"After long-bone or pelvic fractures and some procedures, fat droplets may enter small blood vessels. The relevant context is the body’s microcirculation, including vessels supplying the lungs, brain, and skin.","normal":"Normally, small blood vessels carry blood to tissues so they can receive oxygen and support organ function. Fat droplets may be present without causing the full syndrome, so their presence alone does not establish widespread illness or organ dysfunction.","change":"In fat embolism syndrome, fat droplets and an inflammatory response impair microcirculation. Reduced effective blood flow can affect multiple organs, with respiratory impairment, cerebral changes, and skin findings emerging after injury; timing and severity vary, and the process may sometimes be fulminant.","cues":"After a long-bone or pelvic fracture, new breathing difficulty or hypoxemia together with mental-status change and petechiae raises concern. Anemia or thrombocytopenia may add support, but these findings are nonspecific and a normal early chest radiograph does not exclude the syndrome.","distinction":"Fat emboli are not the same as fat embolism syndrome: droplets may be present without systemic illness. A normal early chest radiograph does not rule out an evolving complication.","related":["embolus","oxygenation","cerebral"],"title":"Fat Embolism Syndrome","clinicalBridge":"Pulmonary arterial obstruction is a useful foundation for thinking about impaired blood flow, but this case involves fat droplets and inflammation in the microcirculation rather than a single large-vessel blockage. Connect the fracture timing to changing oxygenation, mental status, and skin findings.","readingAim":"Which evolving findings after a long-bone fracture would make you raise concern?","prerequisite":"embolus","skill":"s070","unit":"msk","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Fat Embolism Syndrome","Fractures and fat embolism"],"sources":[{"title":"Fat Embolism and Fat Embolism Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK499885/"},{"title":"Fat embolism syndrome - PMC - National Center for ...","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3047848/"}]},{"id":"focus-s071","definition":"Rhabdomyolysis is skeletal muscle breakdown that releases muscle contents into the circulation. These substances can contribute to kidney injury and dangerous electrolyte changes.","structure":"The relevant structures are skeletal muscle cells and the kidneys. After crush injury or other muscle damage, released myoglobin and electrolytes circulate in blood, while the kidneys filter blood and are exposed to these substances.","normal":"Normally, skeletal muscle cells contain proteins and electrolytes that support contraction, and the kidneys filter blood while regulating water and dissolved substances. Muscle injury can release cell contents, while kidney function and urine output provide important context for assessing possible complications.","change":"With muscle breakdown, creatine kinase, myoglobin, and electrolytes enter the circulation. Myoglobin and electrolyte disturbances can injure the kidneys, and severe rhabdomyolysis may lead to acute kidney injury and dangerous electrolyte changes; the degree of risk cannot be inferred from urine color alone.","cues":"After crush trauma or another risk, muscle pain or weakness, a raised creatine kinase, and urine dipstick blood with few red cells can support concern for rhabdomyolysis. Check reported urine output and kidney and electrolyte trends; dark urine may be absent.","distinction":"Tea-colored urine is a clue, not a requirement, and the classic symptom triad is uncommon. A normal urine color does not rule out muscle breakdown or its complications.","related":["myoglobin","nephron","potassium"],"title":"Crush Injury and Rhabdomyolysis","clinicalBridge":"The nephron filters circulating substances, so it helps connect muscle-cell damage with possible kidney injury. In a crush injury, raised creatine kinase and dipstick blood with few red cells support concern for myoglobin release; urine output and kidney and electrolyte trends add context.","readingAim":"What findings beyond urine color would support concern for rhabdomyolysis?","prerequisite":"myoglobin","skill":"s071","unit":"msk","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Crush Injury and Rhabdomyolysis","Crush injury and rhabdomyolysis"],"sources":[{"title":"Rhabdomyolysis - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/rhabdomyolysis"},{"title":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Nephrology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acute-kidney-injury/acute-kidney-injury-aki"}]},{"id":"focus-s072","definition":"ICU-acquired weakness is loss of strength that develops during or after critical illness. Critical illness myopathy, peripheral neuropathy, prolonged immobilization, or a combination may contribute.","structure":"The relevant structures are skeletal muscles and peripheral nerves after critical illness or prolonged bed rest. Muscle changes affect force generation, while nerve changes can affect movement, sensation, and reflexes across the body.","normal":"Normally, skeletal muscles generate force when activated by nerve signals, allowing purposeful movement. Peripheral nerves also carry sensory information. After critical illness, preserved strength supports routine movement; reported tiredness alone does not mean that muscle force or nerve function has changed.","change":"Critical illness myopathy affects muscle, while critical illness polyneuropathy affects peripheral nerves; both may contribute to generalized weakness. Prolonged immobilization can also contribute. The pattern may include diffuse wasting, or weakness with sensory changes and reduced reflexes, but findings do not establish a cause by themselves.","cues":"Difficulty rising from a chair or lifting both arms after prolonged critical illness suggests possible generalized weakness, especially when strength is objectively reduced. Note onset, distribution, function, sensation, and reflexes; sudden focal asymmetry or breathing, swallowing, or speech concerns warrant prompt escalation.","distinction":"Fatigue is a report of tiredness; weakness is reduced force despite effort. Do not assume objective post-ICU weakness is simply fatigue or deconditioning.","related":["muscle","nerve","nmj"],"title":"ICU-Acquired Weakness","clinicalBridge":"Skeletal muscle and peripheral nerves work together to produce movement and sensation. In a recovering ICU patient, difficulty rising or lifting both arms can reflect true generalized weakness rather than tiredness alone; the pattern and associated sensory or reflex changes help guide team assessment.","readingAim":"What would help you distinguish fatigue from true weakness?","prerequisite":"muscle","skill":"s072","unit":"msk","kind":"clinical","aliases":["ICU-Acquired Weakness","ICU-acquired weakness"],"sources":[{"title":"Weakness - Neurology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/symptoms-of-neurologic-disorders/weakness?ruleredirectid=741ruleredirectid=450"},{"title":"Rehabilitation in ICU-Acquired Weakness: An Evidence-Based Narrative Review - PubMed","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42220872/"}]},{"id":"focus-s073","definition":"Sepsis is a life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated response to infection. It can impair tissue blood flow and affect multiple organs.","structure":"The relevant context is the circulation and small vessels supplying organs such as the kidneys, lungs, liver, brain, and heart. Infection-related inflammatory changes can affect blood-vessel tone and the distribution of blood through these tissues.","normal":"Normally, circulation delivers blood to organs, supporting their oxygen and other needs. Small vessels distribute blood through tissues, while organ function is reflected in cues such as attention, breathing, and urine output; changes in one cue alone do not identify a cause.","change":"In sepsis, inflammatory mediators can widen blood vessels and disrupt small-vessel blood flow. Tissues may receive inadequate oxygen even when cardiac output is initially increased, and poor perfusion can impair multiple organs. Persistent circulatory and metabolic abnormalities despite fluid resuscitation characterize septic shock.","cues":"With possible infection, new confusion, reduced urine output, breathing difficulty, or falling blood pressure raises concern for organ dysfunction. Fever and fast heart rate or breathing may occur, but cues can be subtle; warm skin or a clean incision does not rule out sepsis.","distinction":"Warm skin and a normal temperature do not exclude sepsis or poor tissue perfusion. A screening score can prompt evaluation but cannot establish or rule out the diagnosis.","related":["perfusion","shock","inflammation","lactate"],"title":"Sepsis and Organ Dysfunction","clinicalBridge":"Tissue perfusion links circulation to organ function. In a postoperative patient with possible infection, new inattention and reduced urine output may signal impaired organ function even when skin feels warm or the incision looks clean; connect these changes and escalate concern promptly.","readingAim":"Which organ-function changes would concern you despite warm skin?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s073","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Sepsis and Organ Dysfunction","Sepsis and organ dysfunction"],"sources":[{"title":"Sepsis and Septic Shock - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/sepsis-and-septic-shock/sepsis-and-septic-shock"},{"title":"Early Recognition and Initial Management of Sepsis in Adult Patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK598311/"}]},{"id":"focus-s074","definition":"Shock occurs when tissue perfusion is too limited to deliver enough oxygen to cells. Different mechanisms can cause it, and more than one mechanism may contribute at the same time.","structure":"Shock is a whole-circulation problem rather than a lesion in one organ. The relevant context is the relationship among circulating volume, cardiac filling and pumping, vessel tone, and resistance to flow through the tissues.","normal":"In effective circulation, the heart and blood vessels maintain flow that carries oxygen to tissues. Blood volume supports cardiac filling, the heart moves blood forward, and vessel tone helps distribute it. Together, these functions support organ activity, even as the body's needs vary.","change":"In distributive shock, vessel dilation and abnormal blood flow can reduce effective tissue perfusion despite a blood pressure that has not fallen. In hypovolemic, cardiogenic, or obstructive shock, the main disruption instead involves circulating volume, heart function, or impeded filling or outflow; mechanisms can overlap.","cues":"In this case, infection history, new confusion, reduced urine output, warm flushed skin, and a bounding pulse form a concerning pattern for impaired perfusion. No single cue proves shock or its cause; compare the findings and their trends with the history and broader assessment.","distinction":"A preserved blood pressure does not rule out shock, because tissue perfusion can be inadequate before pressure falls. Warm skin or a bounding pulse alone does not establish distributive shock.","related":["perfusion","hypoperfusion","hypotension","fluid-loss","lactate"],"title":"Recognizing Shock Mechanisms","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term Tissue perfusion links this lesson to the delivery of oxygenated blood to cells. Use that connection to interpret the patient's confusion and reduced urine output alongside the warm skin and bounding pulse, rather than treating blood pressure as the sole indicator of circulation.","readingAim":"What pattern would raise concern for poor perfusion despite a reassuring blood pressure?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s074","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Shock Mechanisms","Comparing shock states"],"sources":[{"title":"Shock - Critical Care Medicine","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock"},{"title":"Shock - Shock - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/shock-and-fluid-resuscitation/shock?ruleredirectid=741wruleredirectid=5"}]},{"id":"focus-s075","definition":"An arterial blood gas, or ABG, reports measurements that help describe acid-base status, ventilation, and oxygenation. Interpreting the values requires relating them to one another and to the patient's condition.","structure":"An ABG is a sample of arterial blood analyzed in the context of breathing and metabolism. Its pH reflects the balance between carbon dioxide, shaped by ventilation, and bicarbonate, the metabolic component; oxygenation is assessed separately.","normal":"Normally, ventilation influences arterial carbon dioxide, while metabolic processes and the kidneys influence bicarbonate. Their balance is reflected in the pH. When one component changes, the other may shift in compensation, moving pH toward its usual range without necessarily removing the underlying cause.","change":"Here, shallow breathing can reduce carbon dioxide removal, allowing PaCO2 to rise and contributing to respiratory acidosis. An elevated bicarbonate may be compensatory, but the values alone do not establish how long the change has been present. PaO2 on supplemental oxygen does not describe ventilation by itself.","cues":"The reduced alertness and shallow breathing are bedside concerns that accompany the ABG's low pH and elevated PaCO2. The bicarbonate elevation may represent compensation, but does not prove duration or exclude a mixed disorder; interpret oxygenation separately and consider the oxygen being given.","distinction":"A near-normal pH does not prove normal acid-base status, because compensating or opposing processes can mask a disturbance. A PaO2 measured during supplemental oxygen does not rule out impaired ventilation.","related":["buffer","ventilation","oxygenation"],"title":"Interpreting Blood Gas Patterns","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term Acid-base balance helps explain why pH must be read with PaCO2 and bicarbonate rather than alone. In this case, connect shallow breathing with the elevated PaCO2, then consider the bicarbonate and supplemental oxygen context before judging compensation or ventilation.","readingAim":"What would you compare to decide whether the bicarbonate change is compensation or part of a mixed disorder?","prerequisite":"buffer","skill":"s075","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Interpreting Blood Gas Patterns","Reading an arterial blood gas"],"sources":[{"title":"Arterial Blood Gas Analysis: Fundamentals, Interpretation ... - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536919/"},{"title":"Acid-Base Disorders - Nephrology - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/acid-base-regulation-and-disorders/acid-base-disorders"}]},{"id":"focus-s076","definition":"Potassium is an electrolyte that helps nerve, muscle, and heart cells maintain electrical activity. A low serum potassium can reflect depleted body stores or a shift of potassium into cells.","structure":"Potassium is found mainly inside cells, while the serum result samples the small amount circulating outside them. The relevant context includes gastrointestinal and renal losses, medication effects, acid-base status, and factors that move potassium between blood and cells.","normal":"Potassium gradients across cell membranes support electrical signaling in nerves and muscles, including the heart. The kidneys adjust potassium excretion, and gastrointestinal intake and losses also affect the body's stores. Serum concentration reflects the balance of these processes and shifts between intracellular and extracellular spaces.","change":"Several days of diarrhea can remove potassium from the body, lowering available stores and contributing to weakness and altered cardiac electrical activity. By contrast, insulin or beta 2 agonist stimulation can shift potassium into cells, lowering the serum value without necessarily indicating the same degree of total-body depletion.","cues":"In this case, leg weakness and a prominent U wave accompany the low potassium result, while diarrhea supports gastrointestinal loss. These findings warrant communication and contextual review, but no single symptom or ECG feature establishes the cause or the full extent of depletion.","distinction":"A low serum potassium is not a complete measure of total-body stores: it may reflect loss or redistribution. Few symptoms or an unremarkable ECG alone would not rule out clinically important hypokalemia.","related":["potassium","nephron","buffer"],"title":"Understanding Low Potassium","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term Potassium and electrical excitability connects the serum result to weakness and the ECG finding. In this case, relate the diarrhea to possible potassium loss, while reviewing medication exposure and magnesium findings with the care team to help interpret the mechanism.","readingAim":"Which history and findings would help distinguish gastrointestinal potassium loss from redistribution into cells?","prerequisite":"potassium","skill":"s076","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Understanding Low Potassium","Fluids and electrolytes"],"sources":[{"title":"Hypokalemia - Nephrology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/nephrology/electrolyte-disorders/hypokalemia"},{"title":"Hypokalemia: a clinical update","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5881435"}]},{"id":"focus-s077","definition":"Inhalation injury is damage to the airway or lungs from heat, smoke, or combustion products. It may occur with or without visible burns, and its effects can evolve after the initial exposure.","structure":"The relevant structures extend from the upper airway to the lower airways and lungs. Exposure context matters: hot smoke often injures the upper airway, steam may carry heat farther into the lower airways, and combustion chemicals can damage tissue or impair cellular oxygen use.","normal":"Normally, the upper airway conducts air toward the lungs, where lower airways and alveoli support gas exchange. Intact airway tissue allows airflow, while protective reflexes and mucosal surfaces help limit injury. Breathing and oxygen delivery depend on these structures remaining open and functional.","change":"After smoke exposure in an enclosed space, heat and combustion products can injure airway tissue. Swelling may progress in the upper airway, while lower airway injury can become apparent later as oxygen needs rise or lung compliance falls. Carbon monoxide can impair oxygen carriage and release to tissues. Cyanide can impair a cell’s ability to use oxygen.","cues":"Here, facial burns, singed nasal hair, and soot in sputum support concerning exposure, while new hoarseness and noisy breathing raise concern for evolving upper airway compromise. These cues do not define the full injury; ongoing reassessment matters because airway and lower-lung effects may develop over time.","distinction":"An initially quiet airway does not establish that inhalation injury is absent or that the risk has passed. Visible burns are not required for airway or lung injury.","related":["barrier","airway","oxygenation"],"title":"Recognizing Inhalation Injury","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term Protective barriers and infection connects the skin's barrier role with the airway's vulnerability to smoke and heat. In this case, link enclosed-fire exposure and soot to airway injury, then connect new hoarseness and noisy breathing with possible evolving swelling.","readingAim":"Which changes after smoke exposure would increase concern that airway injury is progressing?","prerequisite":"barrier","skill":"s077","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Inhalation Injury","Burns and inhalation injury"],"sources":[{"title":"Smoke inhalation lung injury","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2396464/"},{"title":"Smoke Inhalation - Injuries; Poisoning","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/burns/smoke-inhalation"},{"title":"Burns - Injuries; Poisoning - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/burns/burns"}]},{"id":"focus-s078","definition":"A healthcare-associated infection is an infection acquired during care; a venous catheter can be one possible route for organisms to reach the bloodstream. Fever or chills raise concern but do not identify the source.","structure":"A venous catheter passes through the skin into a vein, creating an access path between the external environment and the bloodstream; the insertion site and hub are key interface points.","normal":"With appropriate insertion and maintenance, the skin barrier, clean access practices, and intact device care help limit organism entry while the catheter provides prescribed vascular access. Reviewing whether the device remains needed reduces exposure time.","change":"If organisms enter along the catheter or through contaminated access, they may reach the bloodstream and cause infection. Risk is influenced by device choice, insertion and maintenance practices, and time in place; a normal-looking insertion site does not exclude bloodstream infection.","cues":"New fever, chills, or other signs of sepsis in a catheterized patient signal a need for assessment. Site appearance, dressing condition, recent access, and other possible infection sources provide context but cannot establish or rule out the catheter as the source.","distinction":"Fever is a reason to investigate, not proof that the catheter caused infection. A clean-looking site does not rule out bloodstream infection, and routine scheduled replacement is not a substitute for reviewing ongoing need and maintaining the line properly.","related":["barrier","inflammation","neutrophils"],"title":"Catheter Infection Concerns","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Protective barriers and infection explains how skin and other barriers limit organism entry. This article extends that idea to a venous catheter, where access practices and duration affect exposure, while fever remains a signal to investigate rather than proof of a line source.","readingAim":"Why can a normal-looking catheter site fail to rule out bloodstream infection?","prerequisite":"barrier","skill":"s078","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Catheter Infection Concerns","Healthcare-associated infections"],"sources":[{"title":"Effective nursing interventions for infection prevention and control in ...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41270713/"},{"title":"Prevention of Central Line-Associated Bloodstream Infections","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/5666696"}]},{"id":"focus-s079","definition":"Central nervous system infection can affect the brain or its surrounding structures. After transplantation, immunosuppression increases vulnerability, and infection may resemble medication, metabolic, or vascular problems.","structure":"The central nervous system, including the brain and its neural networks, supports awareness, movement, and other functions. After solid organ transplantation, reduced immune responses can make infection less typical or delay its recognition.","normal":"Neural networks support alertness, behavior, and coordinated responses to the environment. After transplantation, immunosuppressive treatment reduces immune activity; this helps manage the transplant context but also leaves the person more vulnerable to infections, including infections involving the central nervous system.","change":"A central nervous system infection may arise from organisms acquired from a donor or environment, or from reactivation of latent infection. With reduced immune responses, symptoms can be less typical or recognition delayed, and neurologic changes can overlap with medication, metabolic, or vascular effects.","cues":"New confusion, altered alertness, headache, vomiting, seizure, or focal deficit in a transplant recipient warrants attention, with or without fever. These findings signal a need to assess and communicate the change; none alone identifies infection as the cause.","distinction":"Do not assume a new neurologic change is a medication effect just because the patient takes immunosuppressants. Infection is one important possibility, but metabolic and vascular causes also remain in consideration.","related":["neurons","cerebral","inflammation"],"title":"Neurologic Changes After Transplant","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Neurons and neural signaling introduces how neural activity supports awareness and behavior. This article applies that foundation to a transplant recipient, where new confusion or other neurologic changes may reflect infection but cannot distinguish it from metabolic, medication-related, or vascular causes alone.","readingAim":"Which new neurologic changes in a transplant recipient would prompt timely communication?","prerequisite":"neurons","skill":"s079","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Neurologic Changes After Transplant","After organ transplantation"],"sources":[{"title":"COVID-19 and solid organ transplantation: Finding the right balance","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9251959/"},{"title":"Central Nervous System Infections in Pediatric Solid Organ Transplant Recipients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13478769"}]},{"id":"focus-s080","definition":"Preeclampsia is a pregnancy-related condition involving new hypertension and possible injury to organs. It can occur without proteinuria, while HELLP can occur without hypertension or proteinuria.","structure":"The placenta and maternal blood vessels form the circulation that supports pregnancy and organ perfusion. Dysfunction in this placental and vascular system can affect the brain, kidneys, liver, lungs, and placenta itself.","normal":"Placental circulation supports pregnancy through maternal and placental blood flow. When the vascular system functions normally, it maintains organ perfusion and an intact vessel lining; these structures provide the context for recognizing changes that may affect maternal organs.","change":"In preeclampsia, placental and vascular dysfunction can reduce organ perfusion and disrupt the vessel lining, producing hypertension, proteinuria, or other organ findings. Proteinuria is not required when new hypertension accompanies end-organ damage; HELLP may occur without hypertension or proteinuria.","cues":"New hypertension with severe persistent headache, visual change, right upper abdominal pain, dyspnea, or reduced urine output is concerning and warrants assessment. A negative urine dipstick does not settle the question; the overall findings and relevant laboratory results need clinical review.","distinction":"A negative urine dipstick does not rule out preeclampsia when new hypertension and organ concerns are present. HELLP can also occur without hypertension or proteinuria, so neither finding alone is an all-clear.","related":["placenta","perfusion","platelets"],"title":"Maternal Vascular Warning Signs","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Placental circulation describes blood flow between the maternal system and placenta. This article builds on that structure to connect placental and vascular dysfunction with reduced organ perfusion and possible injury, while showing why a negative urine dipstick cannot exclude concern.","readingAim":"What findings would keep preeclampsia in consideration despite a negative urine dipstick?","prerequisite":"placenta","skill":"s080","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Maternal Vascular Warning Signs","Maternal critical illness"],"sources":[{"title":"Preeclampsia and Eclampsia - Gynecology and Obstetrics - Merck Manuals Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/preeclampsia-and-eclampsia/?autoredirectid=23422"},{"title":"Preeclampsia and Eclampsia - Gynecology and Obstetrics","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/preeclampsia-and-eclampsia"}]},{"id":"focus-s081","definition":"After cardiac arrest, reduced or absent blood flow can injure the brain, and injury may continue after circulation returns. Neurologic findings require interpretation alongside timing and possible confounders.","structure":"Cerebral blood vessels deliver blood to brain tissue, which depends on that flow to support neural function. During cardiac arrest, cerebral blood flow may be absent or reduced; after circulation returns, the brain remains vulnerable to further injury.","normal":"With adequate circulation, cerebral blood flow supplies the brain. Neural function can then support consciousness and responses such as command following, movement, and reflexes; observations of these functions provide information about the neurologic state.","change":"During cardiac arrest, absent or reduced blood flow can injure the brain. After circulation returns, reperfusion and secondary injury can continue; hypoxemia, hypotension, fever, seizures, and metabolic disturbances can worsen injury, while sedation and temperature management can affect examination findings.","cues":"Persistent unresponsiveness or abnormal neurologic findings after arrest warrant careful reporting. Pupillary responses, motor response, command following, and myoclonus are considered in context; sedation, temperature management, and metabolic problems may influence findings, and no single sign establishes prognosis.","distinction":"A concerning examination finding is not, by itself, a certain outcome. Prognosis should account for timing and confounders and draw on several complementary assessment methods rather than relying on one sign or test.","related":["cerebral","oxygen-debt","perfusion"],"title":"Brain Injury After Cardiac Arrest","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Cerebral perfusion explains why brain tissue depends on blood flow. This article applies that idea to cardiac arrest, where low flow can initiate injury and injury may continue after circulation returns; examination findings also require context and cannot establish prognosis alone.","readingAim":"Why should a post-arrest neurologic finding be interpreted alongside timing and confounders?","prerequisite":"cerebral","skill":"s081","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Brain Injury After Cardiac Arrest","Recovery after cardiac arrest"],"sources":[{"title":"Neurological prognostication after cardiac arrest - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13633088/"},{"title":"Prognostication of patients after cardiopulmonary resuscitation","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7807999/"}]},{"id":"focus-s082","definition":"Heat illness occurs when the body gains heat faster than it can lose it. Severe hyperthermia can disrupt brain function and affect multiple organs.","structure":"The structure of concern is the body’s thermoregulatory system, especially the brain’s temperature-control processes and skin circulation. In heat exposure or strenuous exertion, sweating and increased blood flow to the skin help transfer heat away from the body.","normal":"Normally, sweating allows heat to leave as moisture evaporates, while increased skin blood flow brings heat toward the body surface. Together, these responses help keep internal temperature within a range compatible with normal brain, muscle, and organ function.","change":"When heat gain exceeds heat loss, these cooling responses may be overwhelmed, particularly with dehydration, humidity, exertion, or impaired sweating. Severe hyperthermia can then provoke inflammation and dysfunction in the brain and other organs, including muscles, kidneys, liver, lungs, heart, and coagulation system.","cues":"After heat exposure, confusion, unusual behavior, poor coordination, seizure, or coma signals possible brain dysfunction; a very high core temperature adds concern. Sweating may still be present. These findings raise suspicion but do not by themselves establish heatstroke or exclude other causes.","distinction":"Moist skin does not rule out heatstroke, and dry skin is not required. Heat exhaustion generally preserves central nervous system function, so altered mental status with heat exposure warrants greater concern.","related":["temperature","inflammation","perfusion"],"title":"Heatstroke and Thermoregulation","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term thermoregulation links the athlete’s hot environment and exertion to the body’s cooling responses. When heat gain overwhelms sweating and increased skin blood flow, brain function may become impaired; confusion and unsteadiness therefore matter even when the skin remains moist.","readingAim":"How would altered mental status change your interpretation of heat exposure and sweating?","prerequisite":"temperature","skill":"s082","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Heatstroke and Thermoregulation","Temperature emergencies"],"sources":[{"title":"Heatstroke - Injuries; Poisoning - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/heat-illness/heatstroke"},{"title":"Overview of Heat Illness - Injuries; Poisoning - MSD Manual ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/heat-illness/overview-of-heat-illness"}]},{"id":"focus-s083","definition":"Toxic exposure effects depend on the substance, amount, route, timing, and person exposed. A pattern of findings may suggest a substance class, but it does not prove the cause.","structure":"The structure of concern is the patient’s airway and breathing in the context of a possible toxic exposure. Effects may involve the brain and other organs, while the exposure history—including substance, route, amount, timing, and co-exposures—helps frame interpretation.","normal":"Normally, the brain supports alertness and breathing, and the respiratory system moves air to support oxygen exchange. A patient’s usual level of consciousness and breathing provide context for recognizing change, while exposure details help clinicians consider possible causes without replacing bedside assessment.","change":"A toxic substance may alter consciousness, breathing, or other body functions; effects can be delayed when a metabolite is involved or organ injury develops later. Mixed exposures may blur expected patterns, so findings such as sedation or agitation need interpretation alongside the patient’s stability and history.","cues":"Slow, shallow breathing with drowsiness and pinpoint pupils can suggest an opioid toxidrome, while agitation, tachycardia, and sweating can suggest a sympathomimetic pattern. These are clues, not proof; mixed exposure and other causes of altered consciousness remain possible.","distinction":"A positive urine drug screen does not establish that a substance caused the current symptoms. Routine screens detect only a limited range, and the result may reflect exposure that does not explain the present condition.","related":["airway","neurons","liver"],"title":"Toxic Exposure and Breathing","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term airway connects the reported medication containers to the immediate bedside concern: drowsiness with slow, shallow breathing. That pattern may suggest an opioid toxidrome, but it cannot identify a precise substance or exclude co-exposure; breathing and exposure history both inform the team’s assessment.","readingAim":"What bedside finding would make breathing stability more urgent than identifying the substance?","prerequisite":"airway","skill":"s083","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Toxic Exposure and Breathing","Toxic exposures"],"sources":[{"title":"General Principles of Poisoning - MSD Manuals","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/poisoning/general-principles-of-poisoning"},{"title":"Symptoms and Treatment of Specific Poisons - Injuries","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/poisoning/symptoms-and-treatment-of-specific-poisons"}]},{"id":"focus-s084","definition":"Submersion injury occurs when being under water impairs breathing, creating a risk of low oxygen. Lung effects may progress after rescue, so early appearance alone does not tell the whole course.","structure":"The structures of concern are the airways and alveoli, the small lung spaces where gas exchange occurs. After submersion, fluid aspiration or reflex airway closure can impair breathing; later inflammation and reduced surfactant function may further disrupt oxygen movement.","normal":"Normally, air reaches the alveoli, where oxygen moves into blood and carbon dioxide moves out. Open, stable alveoli support this exchange, while effective breathing supplies fresh air. This provides the basis for oxygen delivery to the brain, heart, and other organs.","change":"After submersion, aspiration or reflex airway closure can reduce ventilation and cause hypoxia. Lung injury may evolve through inflammation and impaired surfactant function, leaving some regions poorly ventilated and worsening oxygenation. Cold exposure may also contribute to hypothermia.","cues":"Cough, wheeze, anxiety, altered consciousness, rapid or labored breathing, or cyanosis after rescue can signal respiratory injury. Increasing effort despite an initially reassuring appearance raises concern for worsening hypoxia, but no single cue establishes its severity or cause.","distinction":"A person who appears quiet or briefly well after rescue is not necessarily free of evolving lung injury. Removing water from the lungs is not a useful substitute for supporting breathing and observing for change.","related":["alveoli","oxygenation","surfactant"],"title":"Submersion and Lung Injury","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term alveoli connects the swimmer’s increasing breathing effort to impaired gas exchange. Aspiration or airway closure can reduce air reaching these lung spaces, while evolving inflammation and surfactant dysfunction may worsen oxygenation; initial apparent recovery does not rule out this progression.","readingAim":"How could worsening alveolar function explain increasing breathing effort after rescue?","prerequisite":"alveoli","skill":"s084","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Submersion and Lung Injury","Submersion injury"],"sources":[{"title":"Drowning - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/drowning/drowning"},{"title":"Drowning - Injuries; Poisoning - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/injuries-poisoning/drowning/drowning?query=773"}]},{"id":"focus-s085","definition":"Pain is a sensory and emotional experience, while sleep is a recurring period of rest organized by day-night rhythms. In critical care, discomfort and interruptions can alter how a patient behaves and rests.","structure":"The relevant context includes the patient's ability to communicate, the body and face during care, and the bedside environment across day and night. An endotracheal tube may prevent speech, while turning and repeated nighttime care can expose discomfort and interrupt familiar routines.","normal":"Normally, a patient who can communicate reports pain directly, and familiar day-night routines support sleep. When speech is unavailable, pain may still be expressed through behavior; a painful turn can provoke grimacing or tension, while repeated nighttime interruptions can disturb rest.","change":"Here, the endotracheal tube limits verbal report, not the possibility of pain. Turning may trigger a behavioral response resembling the patient's usual pain behavior, and interruptions may disrupt sleep; neither behavior nor sleepiness alone establishes the cause or confirms relief.","cues":"Tension and grimacing during a planned turn, especially when a family member recognizes them as usual pain behavior, are cues to assess discomfort. They do not prove pain; inability to answer and a disrupted night also complicate interpretation.","distinction":"Quietness or sleepiness after medication is not proof that pain is controlled. Agitation is not specific to pain and may have other causes, including delirium.","related":["pain","sleep","delirium"],"title":"Assessing Pain and Protecting Sleep","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Pain pathways and perception helps connect a painful turn with possible grimacing or tension without treating behavior as a diagnosis. Sleep and circadian rhythm adds why repeated nighttime interruptions matter, while delirium remains a competing explanation for agitation.","readingAim":"What would you predict about the patient's behavior and rest during a painful turn after an interrupted night?","prerequisite":"pain","skill":"s085","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Assessing Pain and Protecting Sleep","Pain and sleep in critical care"],"sources":[{"title":"Untitled","url":"https://sccm.org/tab-pages/icu-liberation-bundle-(a-f)?PWP57f5689392c54854bccac791a7d69f2f=true"},{"title":"All SCCM Guidelines","url":"https://www.sccm.org/clinical-resources/guidelines/guidelines"}]},{"id":"focus-s086","definition":"Frailty is reduced physiologic reserve across body systems, which makes a person more vulnerable to stress. Nutritional risk can contribute to loss of muscle and function, while functional decline can also make eating harder.","structure":"The relevant structures are the body systems that support strength, mobility, cognition, and independent daily activity. In this context, nutrition and physical function interact: difficulty shopping, preparing food, chewing, or swallowing can affect intake, while reduced reserve can make daily tasks harder.","normal":"With adequate reserve, a person can generally meet daily demands and recover from ordinary stressors while maintaining function. Food intake supports the body, and mobility and activity support strength and independence; these capacities vary between people and are best understood against the person’s own baseline.","change":"With frailty, reserve and intrinsic capacity are reduced across systems, so stressors can have a greater effect on function. Poor intake or weight loss can contribute to muscle loss, while weakness, fatigue, or difficulty managing daily tasks can further limit shopping, cooking, and eating.","cues":"Look for a pattern over time: unintentional weight loss or poor intake alongside slower gait, weakness, fatigue, reduced activity, falls, or increasing help with daily tasks. Cognitive, medical, mood, oral-health, and social concerns may contribute; no single cue establishes frailty.","distinction":"A positive frailty screen is a prompt for fuller assessment, not a diagnosis. One reassuring symptom or score does not replace attention to the broader pattern and trajectory.","related":["muscle","swallow","fluid"],"title":"Frailty and Nutritional Decline","clinicalBridge":"Skeletal muscle and deconditioning helps connect reduced activity and intake with changing strength and function. In this lesson, the nurse links reported weight and meal trajectory to mobility and daily tasks, while treating a positive screen as a prompt for broader assessment.","readingAim":"What combination of intake and functional changes would prompt you to communicate a broader concern?","prerequisite":"muscle","skill":"s086","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Frailty and Nutritional Decline","Frailty and nutritional decline"],"sources":[{"title":"Frailty in older adults: detection and management in primary care. Fisterra clinical practice guideline","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13754928/"},{"title":"Nutrition and Frailty in the Older Adult: A Case-Based Approach to Assessment, Diagnosis, Intervention, Monitoring, and Evaluation in Preoperative Care","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13762823/"}]},{"id":"focus-s087","definition":"Obesity can increase the work of breathing and reduce respiratory reserve. If ventilation while awake becomes inadequate, carbon dioxide can rise, although other causes must also be considered.","structure":"The relevant structures are the chest and respiratory system that move air in and out of the lungs. In a person with obesity, excess adiposity adds mechanical load to breathing; the context includes awake ventilation and, when present, coexisting sleep-disordered breathing.","normal":"Normally, respiratory muscles move air to support ventilation and carbon dioxide removal. The body’s ventilatory response helps adjust breathing to meet this need. Oxygen saturation reflects oxygenation, but it does not by itself show whether ventilation is adequate or carbon dioxide is being cleared.","change":"When added mechanical load and a blunted ventilatory response reduce awake ventilation, carbon dioxide can accumulate. Over time, the kidneys may retain bicarbonate as compensation. Obstructive sleep apnea may coexist, but this pattern also requires evaluation for other lung, medication-related, neuromuscular, or metabolic causes.","cues":"Unexplained dyspnea, daytime sleepiness, morning headaches, or hypoxemia may raise concern, especially with sleep-disordered breathing. Elevated serum bicarbonate can suggest chronic carbon dioxide retention but is not diagnostic; awake hypercapnia is assessed with an arterial blood gas.","distinction":"A reassuring oxygen saturation does not establish adequate ventilation. Elevated bicarbonate can support concern for chronic compensation but cannot, by itself, confirm obesity hypoventilation syndrome.","related":["ventilation","oxygenation","vq"],"title":"Obesity and Respiratory Reserve","clinicalBridge":"Ventilation and carbon dioxide provides the foundation for understanding why oxygen saturation alone cannot show whether a patient is clearing carbon dioxide. In this lesson, the nurse connects added breathing load and awake hypercapnia with the need to communicate the full respiratory pattern.","readingAim":"Which findings would help you judge ventilation as well as oxygenation?","prerequisite":"ventilation","skill":"s087","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Obesity and Respiratory Reserve","Obesity and respiratory reserve"],"sources":[{"title":"Treatment / Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542216/"},{"title":"Evaluation and Management of Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome. An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6680300/"}]},{"id":"focus-s088","definition":"As illness advances, reduced alertness, swallowing, and secretion clearance can change breathing sounds and intake. Comfort-focused care depends on assessing the patient’s experience and following documented goals of care.","structure":"The relevant context is the upper airway and the person’s ability to swallow and clear secretions. Near the end of life, reduced alertness or swallowing can allow secretions to pool, while the sound they produce does not directly reveal whether the patient feels distress.","normal":"Normally, alertness and swallowing help protect the airway, and a person can clear secretions. Breathing sounds and eating patterns are interpreted alongside the person’s comfort and condition. When goals focus on comfort, care is guided by observed distress, the documented plan, and reassessment.","change":"As illness advances, alertness, swallowing, intake, and the ability to clear secretions may decline. Pooled secretions can create noisy breathing without visible struggle, while reduced intake or turning away food can occur as death approaches; other treatable causes may still need consideration in context.","cues":"Assess the patient’s breathing effort and observable comfort, not the sound or a family member’s distress alone. Noisy breathing at rest may reflect pooled secretions, while visible struggle or new distress warrants communication with the clinical team within the care plan.","distinction":"Noisy breathing does not by itself prove that the patient is distressed. Reduced intake near death does not mean that forcing food or fluids will restore comfort.","related":["swallow","airway","delirium"],"title":"Comfort and Noisy Breathing","clinicalBridge":"Swallowing and airway protection helps explain how reduced alertness or swallowing can affect secretion clearance. In this lesson, the nurse distinguishes the sound of pooled secretions from observed patient distress, assesses comfort, and communicates changes in line with documented goals of care.","readingAim":"What would you assess to distinguish a concerning sound from the patient’s experience of distress?","prerequisite":"swallow","skill":"s088","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Comfort and Noisy Breathing","End-of-life symptom care"],"sources":[{"title":"Last Days of Life (PDQ®)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK65868.14/?report=printable"},{"title":"Symptom Relief for the Dying Patient - Special Subjects","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/the-dying-patient/symptom-relief-for-the-dying-patient"}]},{"id":"focus-s089","definition":"Delirium is an acute change in attention and awareness with additional cognitive changes, often fluctuating over time. Sedation lowers arousal and can limit meaningful assessment, but does not by itself explain every cognitive change.","structure":"The relevant structures are brain systems supporting attention, awareness, and cognition. In the ICU context, sedative timing can change arousal, while critical illness, medications, infection, dehydration, hypoxia, or metabolic problems may contribute to an acute and fluctuating cognitive change.","normal":"With usual arousal, a person can register and sustain attention, follow what is happening, and respond in a way consistent with their baseline cognition. Repeated observations and information from family or caregivers can help establish that baseline and identify meaningful change over time.","change":"Sedation can lower arousal enough to prevent a meaningful delirium assessment. Separately, delirium involves an acute disturbance in attention and cognition that can fluctuate; it may appear as agitation or as withdrawal, slowed responses, or lethargy, and can have multiple contributors.","cues":"Look for a recent change from baseline, including difficulty sustaining attention, disorganized thinking, altered awareness, or cognition that varies over hours. Consider quietness and lethargy as possible cues too, and compare observations with medication timing and caregiver reports.","distinction":"Reduced arousal from sedation and delirium are not interchangeable labels. Quietness or drowsiness alone cannot distinguish them, and orientation alone may miss impaired attention.","related":["arousal","neurons","sleep"],"title":"Delirium or Sedation?","clinicalBridge":"Attention and arousal helps separate a patient’s level of wakefulness from the ability to follow and sustain attention. In this lesson, the nurse connects fluctuating attention and disorganized responses with a possible acute cognitive change, while sharing arousal and medication timing with the team.","readingAim":"What observations would help distinguish reduced arousal from a new fluctuating change in attention?","prerequisite":"arousal","skill":"s089","unit":"psych","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Delirium or Sedation?","Delirium or sedation?"],"sources":[{"title":"Delirium - Neurology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/neurologic-disorders/delirium-and-dementia/delirium"},{"title":"ICU Delirium - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559280/"}]},{"id":"focus-s090","definition":"Agitation is a nonspecific increase in arousal, verbal activity, or movement, not a diagnosis. It can accompany medical illness, delirium, substance effects, or psychiatric conditions, and causes can overlap.","structure":"Agitation is observed through a person’s movement, speech, attention, and interaction with others. The surrounding clinical context matters: a sudden change, fluctuating awareness, abnormal vital signs, or difficulty communicating may point toward a need to assess for medical causes.","normal":"A person’s usual level of arousal and attention supports communicating needs and participating in assessment. When behavior changes, comparing the person’s current attention, awareness, movement, and speech with their usual state can help identify whether the change is acute or fluctuating.","change":"With agitation, verbal or motor activity and arousal increase, while attention or awareness may also become altered. If the change reflects delirium or another medical problem, focusing only on behavior can delay recognition of the underlying concern; more than one cause may be present.","cues":"Restlessness or hostility together with new confusion, fluctuating attention, fever, abnormal breathing, or abnormal oxygenation raises concern for a medical contributor. These cues do not identify a cause by themselves; an acute change or concerning vital signs should be communicated for evaluation.","distinction":"A calmer patient is not necessarily medically stable. De-escalation can make assessment easier, but it does not resolve abnormal findings or establish the cause of agitation.","related":["arousal","delirium","neurons"],"title":"Agitation as a Clinical Signal","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Attention and arousal helps frame why a change in attention or awareness matters alongside increased movement or speech. In this case, fluctuating attention, fever, and abnormal oxygenation make continued medical assessment important even after the patient becomes quieter.","readingAim":"What would you reassess if the patient became calmer but remained newly inattentive?","prerequisite":"arousal","skill":"s090","unit":"psych","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Agitation as a Clinical Signal","Agitation and de-escalation"],"sources":[{"title":"Treatment / Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK493153/"},{"title":"Medical Evaluation and Triage of the Agitated Patient - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3298208/"}]},{"id":"focus-s091","definition":"Alcohol withdrawal is a range of nervous system and autonomic changes that can follow sudden cessation after prolonged heavy alcohol use and physical dependence. Symptoms can progress to a potentially fatal severe state.","structure":"The central nervous system and autonomic functions are the context for withdrawal-related changes. After prolonged heavy alcohol use, sudden cessation can be followed by increasing nervous system activity, with observable changes in movement, awareness, and signs such as sweating or a rapid pulse.","normal":"Central nervous system activity supports ordinary awareness and bodily function. In a person who is not experiencing withdrawal, these functions do not show the withdrawal-related progression described here; a history of heavy alcohol use and recent cessation provides important context for interpreting new symptoms.","change":"After prolonged heavy use, sudden cessation may produce increasing nervous system and autonomic activity. Symptoms can progress from tremor, sweating, or nausea to seizures, hallucinations, or delirium tremens, which includes confusion and autonomic instability and can be fatal.","cues":"Sweating, tremor, and nausea after alcohol cessation may signal withdrawal but do not establish it. New disorientation and frightening visual experiences alongside autonomic changes raise concern for progression to severe withdrawal; injury, infection, low blood sugar, or other substances may also contribute.","distinction":"Hallucinations with clear consciousness are different from delirium tremens, which involves confusion and autonomic instability. Neither presentation proves that withdrawal is the only cause of altered mental status.","related":["neurons","arousal","temperature"],"title":"Recognizing Withdrawal Progression","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Neurons and neural signaling provides context for how altered nervous system activity can affect awareness and behavior. Here, the progression from tremor and sweating to disorientation, hallucinations, and autonomic changes raises concern for severe withdrawal while other causes remain possible.","readingAim":"Which changes in this progression would make you communicate greater concern?","prerequisite":"neurons","skill":"s091","unit":"psych","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Withdrawal Progression","Substance withdrawal"],"sources":[{"title":"Alcohol Use Disorder and Alcohol Withdrawal - Mental Health - Merck Manual Consumer Version","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/mental-health-disorders/alcohol-use-disorder/alcohol-use-disorder-and-alcohol-withdrawal"},{"title":"Alcohol Toxicity and Withdrawal - The Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/illicit-drugs-and-intoxicants/alcohol-toxicity-and-withdrawal?media=full"}]},{"id":"focus-s092","definition":"Suicide risk reflects the interaction of a person’s history, current circumstances, changeable factors, and protective supports. A screening result identifies a need for further assessment; it does not predict an individual outcome.","structure":"The relevant structure is the communication and decision context around safety assessment: the person’s statements, history, current changes, access to means, observed behavior, and information from others. These sources are considered together rather than relying on one response or score.","normal":"Good safety practice uses sensitive questions about current and past thoughts, plans, intent, access to means, preparations, and attempts. It also considers recent changes, protective supports, and collateral information, while treating screening as a starting point for a fuller assessment rather than a conclusion.","change":"The decision context changes when current stress or other near-term concerns appear alongside historical vulnerability or new information. A denial of current thoughts remains one part of the picture; it does not erase a past attempt, severe insomnia, or reported preparatory behavior.","cues":"In this case, the patient denies current suicidal thoughts but reports a past attempt and several nights of very little sleep; a family member reports that valued belongings were given away. These details warrant further assessment and communication, without treating any single one as a prediction.","distinction":"A denial of current thoughts or the presence of family support does not settle risk. A screening result or promise to remain safe is not a substitute for comprehensive assessment and safety planning through the care team.","related":["focus-s090","focus-s093","focus-s098"],"title":"Suicide Risk: Beyond Denial","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Sleep and circadian rhythm offers context for noticing severe insomnia as one current factor among several. In this case, the patient’s denial should be considered alongside the past attempt, relationship stress, limited sleep, and family report, not treated as the whole assessment.","readingAim":"What information would you gather alongside the patient’s denial before deciding on next steps?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s092","unit":"psych","kind":"practice","aliases":["Suicide Risk: Beyond Denial","Suicide risk and immediate safety"],"sources":[{"title":"Suicide: Assessment and Management","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK617057/?report=printable"},{"title":"Suicide Risk in Children and Adolescents: Assessment ... - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK576416/"}]},{"id":"focus-s093","definition":"Anxiety, depression, and trauma-related symptoms can occur or worsen after critical illness and may persist after discharge. They can coexist with physical or cognitive difficulties, but symptoms alone do not establish a diagnosis.","structure":"The relevant structure is the communication and follow-up context after critical illness: the patient’s report of mood, anxiety, memories, distress, and effects on daily function. Screening and referral processes help the care team consider concerns without treating a screening result as a diagnosis.","normal":"Supportive follow-up invites the patient to describe mood, anxiety, distressing memories, and effects on daily activities. The nurse documents concerns and their functional impact, uses established screening processes, and communicates findings so the patient and care team can consider appropriate next steps.","change":"After critical illness, new or worsened anxiety, low mood, or difficulty recalling the ICU stay may become part of the recovery picture. These concerns can persist beyond discharge and may occur with physical or cognitive problems; they do not by themselves identify a specific condition.","cues":"Several months after an ICU stay, this patient reports persistent anxiety, difficulty piecing together memories of the stay, and low mood interfering with usual activities. This pattern warrants documentation and communication, but does not by itself establish a diagnosis or explain every difficulty.","distinction":"Difficulty recalling an ICU stay is a clue to explore, not proof of PTSD. Symptoms should be considered with their functional impact and the broader recovery context rather than treated as a diagnosis on their own.","related":["focus-s123","focus-s099","focus-s096"],"title":"Mental Health After Critical Illness","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Attention and arousal helps distinguish a patient’s report of difficulty recalling events from a definitive explanation for it. Here, persistent anxiety, low mood, and effects on daily activities are concerns to document and communicate for team-based screening and follow-up.","readingAim":"What would you document and communicate before discussing follow-up with the patient and team?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s093","unit":"psych","kind":"practice","aliases":["Mental Health After Critical Illness","Anxiety, depression and trauma"],"sources":[{"title":"Care of ICU survivors in the community: a guide for GPs","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5604827/"},{"title":"Post–intensive Care Syndrome - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10772956/"}]},{"id":"focus-s094","definition":"A refusal is a patient’s decision not to accept a proposed treatment. Informed refusal depends on a discussion that supports a meaningful choice, not on whether the patient agrees with the clinician.","structure":"The structure is the consent conversation: the patient, clinician, and care team exchange information about the condition, likely course, options, benefits, burdens, and uncertainties so the patient can make a meaningful choice.","normal":"In desired practice, the conversation checks what the patient understands about the condition and what may happen without treatment. It also covers available options, their likely benefits and harms, relevant burdens, and uncertainties, while allowing the patient to ask questions and express priorities.","change":"The decision process becomes harder to interpret when questions remain unanswered, understanding appears inconsistent, or communication barriers interfere with exchange. These are reasons to clarify the discussion; they do not, by themselves, establish impaired capacity or explain why the patient refuses.","cues":"At bedside, listen for the patient’s account of the condition, likely course without treatment, and available options. Notice unanswered questions, inconsistent explanations, or communication barriers, while recognizing that a refusal or disagreement alone cannot establish impaired capacity.","distinction":"Refusal is not a capacity test. A puzzling choice calls for further discussion and communication with the care team, not an automatic conclusion that capacity is impaired.","related":["focus-s096","focus-s100","focus-s124"],"title":"Understanding Treatment Refusal","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Attention and arousal addresses a different clinical domain. A refusal should not be treated as evidence of altered attention; focus first on the consent conversation, the patient’s understanding, unanswered questions, and barriers to communication.","readingAim":"What would you clarify before interpreting a refusal?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s094","unit":"psych","kind":"practice","aliases":["Understanding Treatment Refusal","When a patient declines care"],"sources":[{"title":"Informed Consent - Special Subjects - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/special-subjects/medicolegal-issues/informed-consent?media=full"},{"title":"Consentimiento informado - Temas especiales - Merck Manuals","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/es-us/professional/temas-especiales/cuestiones-medicolegales/consentimiento-informado"}]},{"id":"focus-s095","definition":"Moral distress occurs when a clinician recognizes an ethically appropriate course but feels unable to carry it out because of constraints. Repeated unresolved episodes can leave lingering distress and contribute to workplace stress and burnout.","structure":"The structure is the care-team and organizational context in which a clinician identifies an ethical concern, communicates it, and seeks a response. Relevant constraints can include orders, communication failures, or limited resources.","normal":"In desired practice, the clinician clarifies the ethical concern, connects it to the patient’s expressed goals and available facts, and raises it respectfully with the responsible clinician and team. Structured discussion or an ethics consultation can help address the concern through established pathways.","change":"When a clinician believes a patient-centered course is being blocked by an order, poor communication, or limited resources, the ethical conflict may produce powerlessness or lingering distress. Repeated unresolved episodes can accumulate as moral residue; distress signals a concern to examine, not a final treatment decision.","cues":"Listen for a specific values conflict: the clinician can name a patient-centered obligation but feels prevented from acting. Powerlessness or lingering distress may accompany it. Workload strain alone can be exhausting without this ethical conflict, and distress does not settle the care decision.","distinction":"Moral distress is not simply workload strain or a reason to change treatment independently. It can signal a need for team or system attention, but does not by itself determine whether treatment should continue.","related":["focus-s096","focus-s098","focus-s101"],"title":"Recognizing Moral Distress","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term Attention and arousal belongs to a separate clinical domain. Here, the focus is the clinician’s ethical conflict and constraints, not a finding about a patient’s cognition; identify the concern and use team or organizational pathways to address it.","readingAim":"What specific ethical concern would you raise with the team?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s095","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Recognizing Moral Distress","Advocacy and moral distress"],"sources":[{"title":"Moral Distress, Burnout, and the Intensive Care Unit Environment - PubMed","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43003538/"},{"title":"Facing Moral Distress: Why We Need to Enhance ... - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12670416/"}]},{"id":"focus-s096","definition":"Goals-of-care decisions bring clinical information about options and uncertainty together with what matters to the patient. Shared decision-making is a collaborative process that joins clinical expertise with the patient’s values.","structure":"The structure is the goals-of-care conversation among the patient or surrogate, clinicians, and care team. It provides a setting to explain options and uncertainty, elicit the patient’s values, and explore the surrogate’s understanding and preferred role.","normal":"In desired practice, clinicians explain relevant options, likely benefits and harms, and uncertainty. They invite the patient or surrogate to share what matters to the patient, check understanding, and deliberate together toward medically appropriate, value-concordant care.","change":"The discussion is less collaborative when information is presented without exploring the patient’s values, when family members are left to decide without clinical guidance, or when clinicians decide alone. The conversation may need to adapt to the patient’s capacity and preferred decision-making roles.","cues":"Listen for whether the team explains options and uncertainty, asks what matters to the patient, and checks the surrogate’s understanding and desired role. An information-only presentation or unsupported request that family decide may signal that deliberation is incomplete.","distinction":"A family meeting is not a ballot that transfers the whole decision to relatives. Clinicians contribute medical knowledge, while the patient or surrogate contributes knowledge of the patient’s values.","related":["focus-s094","focus-s100","focus-s125"],"title":"Collaborative Goals of Care","clinicalBridge":"The foundation article Attention and arousal is distinct from a goals-of-care discussion: observations in that domain cannot supply a patient’s values. This conversation must make room for the patient’s preferences, clinical uncertainty, and the surrogate’s understanding and role.","readingAim":"What would show that clinical information and patient values are being considered together?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s096","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Collaborative Goals of Care","Goals of care and family meetings"],"sources":[{"title":"Shared Decision Making in Intensive Care Units: An American College of Critical Care Medicine and American Thoracic Society Policy Statement","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/4788386"},{"title":"Prevalence and predictors of shared decision-making in goals ...","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10590503/"}]},{"id":"focus-s097","definition":"Clinical inquiry connects a practice concern with available evidence, nursing expertise, client needs, and the care environment. A focused question can guide a search and help identify what outcome should be evaluated.","structure":"The structure is the practice-improvement process: a team defines a recurring concern, frames a question, searches and appraises evidence, integrates it with local needs, and evaluates outcomes. The project’s purpose helps distinguish evidence-based practice from quality improvement or research.","normal":"In desired practice, the team clarifies the concern, identifies a measurable outcome and time frame, and uses PICOT terms to frame a searchable question. It then searches reliable sources, appraises evidence, integrates findings with client needs and the care environment, and evaluates the result.","change":"When practice varies or outcomes raise concern, a single impression or documentation pattern may not show which approach is best. The team needs to define what it wants to learn: a local process improvement, an evidence-based practice question, or a research question.","cues":"Look for recurring practice variation, outcome patterns, safety concerns, or uncertainty about alignment with evidence. Check whether records provide meaningful information: documentation of a process is a signal about what was recorded, not proof that the intended outcome was achieved.","distinction":"A well-completed record does not by itself show that an intervention is effective or that the intended outcome was reached. One study or personal preference alone is not enough to settle a practice change.","related":["focus-s101","focus-s098","focus-s099"],"title":"Framing Clinical Inquiry","clinicalBridge":"The foundation term Tissue perfusion illustrates why an inquiry must name the outcome being assessed. Records showing that a process was documented do not, by themselves, show whether an intended outcome changed; define a measure and time frame for evaluation.","readingAim":"What population, approach, outcome, and time frame would focus the inquiry?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s097","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Framing Clinical Inquiry","Clinical inquiry"],"sources":[{"title":"QUALITY AND EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICE","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/openrnnmpc2e/ch9/"},{"title":"Nursing Professional Development Evidence-Based Practice","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK589676/?report=printable"}]},{"id":"focus-s098","definition":"A handoff is the transfer of care information and responsibility from one person or setting to another. It is complete when the receiver understands the relevant information and explicitly accepts responsibility.","structure":"The structure is the clinical handoff: a communication exchange between the outgoing and incoming care team members. It includes the patient’s situation, relevant background, current assessment, requested next action, and clear transfer of responsibility.","normal":"In a complete handoff, the sender organizes key information, such as with SBAR, and states any pending action. The receiver has an opportunity to ask questions, confirms understanding, and acknowledges responsibility, closing the communication loop before the sender relinquishes accountability.","change":"When the incoming nurse is interrupted before confirming who will complete a pending assessment, the information may have been shared but the transfer is not confirmed. Unclear ownership and missing acknowledgment leave responsibility with the outgoing nurse until the receiver understands and accepts it.","cues":"A pending assessment, an interrupted exchange, unanswered questions, or no receiver acknowledgment are signs that the handoff may remain incomplete. A written note can provide useful information, but by itself does not establish that the receiver understood or accepted responsibility.","distinction":"A message marked sent or a chart entry is not proof of a completed handoff. The key distinction is whether the receiver acknowledged and accepted responsibility for the next action.","related":["focus-s101","focus-s095","focus-s097"],"title":"Closing the Handoff Loop","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Attention and arousal helps explain why a receiver needs an opportunity to focus on key information. Still, a handoff is a communication process, not a measure of someone’s health; check-back and explicit acceptance make task ownership clear.","readingAim":"What would you confirm before considering responsibility transferred?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s098","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Closing the Handoff Loop","Collaboration and handoff"],"sources":[{"title":"TeamSTEPPS for Office-Based Care: Communication","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps/officebasedcare/module3/office_comm.html"},{"title":"Section 1: Overview of Key Concepts and Tools","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/communication/overview/index.html"}]},{"id":"focus-s099","definition":"Teach-back is a way to check whether an explanation was clear by inviting a patient or family member to describe the information in their own words. Show-me adds a practical check when the person needs to demonstrate a task.","structure":"The structure is the clinician-patient teaching exchange, especially when discussing a new diagnosis, medicine, device, or home-care instruction. The clinician explains a manageable portion, invites the patient’s own account or demonstration, and responds to any gap in understanding.","normal":"In desired practice, the clinician uses clear language, then asks the patient to explain the plan in their own words. For a practical task, the patient can show what they will do. If the explanation or demonstration reveals a gap, the clinician explains differently and checks again.","change":"A patient’s “yes, I understand” does not show what they understood. In this case, inability to demonstrate the inhaler instructions signals that the explanation may not have been usable or clear. The next step in the teaching exchange is to revisit the explanation and check understanding again.","cues":"A confident yes followed by difficulty explaining or demonstrating the task suggests understanding is not yet established. The cue points to a need to clarify the teaching, not to a judgment about the patient’s effort, intelligence, or ability to remember.","distinction":"Teach-back is not a quiz of the patient’s memory. A verbatim repeat or yes-or-no assurance does not necessarily show that the person can explain or use the information.","related":["focus-s100","focus-s094","focus-s123"],"title":"Checking Understanding with Teach-Back","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Attention and arousal underscores that receiving information depends in part on being able to attend to it. Teach-back does not diagnose an attention problem; it helps the clinician check whether the explanation worked and adjust communication when a gap appears.","readingAim":"What would you ask the patient to explain or demonstrate next?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s099","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Checking Understanding with Teach-Back","Teaching with teach-back"],"sources":[{"title":"Use the Teach-Back Method: Tool #5","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/improve/precautions/tool5.html"},{"title":"Teach-Back","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/patient-safety/reports/engage/teachback.html"}]},{"id":"focus-s100","definition":"Language access means making appropriate spoken and written language support available so patients can participate in care. For a patient with limited English proficiency, this includes using the preferred language and qualified medical interpretation when needed.","structure":"The structure is the care conversation and its language supports: the patient’s preferred language, the clinician’s explanation, a qualified medical interpreter when needed, and written information the patient can use. This is a communication context, not a judgment about the patient’s culture or ability.","normal":"In desired practice, the clinician asks which language the patient prefers for health information and uses clear wording. When needed, a qualified medical interpreter and suitable written materials support accurate communication, patient participation, and the opportunity to ask questions about care.","change":"Here, the patient manages brief English conversation but struggles to discuss the care plan and asks to use Vietnamese. Brief greetings and nodding do not establish understanding of detailed information; relying on a relative to interpret can compromise accuracy or privacy.","cues":"Difficulty discussing the care plan, a request to use another language, or reliance on nodding after a detailed explanation can signal that communication support is needed. These cues do not establish confusion; ask about preference and assess whether the message is understood.","distinction":"Limited English proficiency is not evidence of impaired understanding or a lack of interest in care. A relative’s willingness to interpret does not make that person equivalent to a qualified medical interpreter.","related":["focus-s099","focus-s094","focus-s124"],"title":"Language Access in Care","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Neurons and neural signaling describes how nervous systems support communication, but it does not explain a patient’s language preference. Language access is a care-process responsibility: use the patient’s preferred language and appropriate interpretation rather than treating a language difference as a clinical deficit.","readingAim":"What support would help confirm that the care plan is understood?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s100","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Language Access in Care","Language access and equitable care"],"sources":[{"title":"Factors Preventing...","url":"https://openstax.org/books/clinical-nursing-skills/pages/5-4-diversity-equity-and-inclusion"},{"title":"Chapter 3 Diverse Patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/n/openrnnf/ch3diversepatients/"}]},{"id":"focus-s101","definition":"Systems thinking treats safety events as outcomes that can emerge when human actions meet weaknesses in complex work processes. It examines both the visible action and the conditions surrounding it.","structure":"The relevant structure is the operating-room communication and decision context: the scheduled procedure, team handoffs, interpreter access, time-out workflow, interruptions, and monitor placement that shape what staff can see and confirm before action.","normal":"In desired practice, the team confirms the planned procedure through a reliable time-out, shares information in a way the patient can understand, and keeps the field observable. These steps support a common understanding before the procedure begins.","change":"In this case, a delayed list and last-minute team change coincided with an interruption, no interpreter, difficult monitor visibility, and no time-out; the patient received a different hand procedure than scheduled. These conditions may have aligned with the active error, but do not establish a single cause.","cues":"The bedside signal is the mismatch between the scheduled hand procedure and the procedure performed. It warrants prompt attention and factual communication; the mismatch alone cannot identify whether interruption, handoff, visibility, or another condition contributed.","distinction":"A wrong procedure is not, by itself, proof that one clinician is inherently unsafe or that retraining alone will prevent recurrence. Review the observable action alongside potential latent conditions.","related":["focus-s098","focus-s097","focus-s095"],"title":"Reviewing a Wrong-Procedure Event","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Attention and arousal offers a way to consider how an interruption may coincide with an attention lapse, without treating that possibility as established cause. In this case, the procedure mismatch also calls for review of communication, workflow, and room conditions.","readingAim":"Which observable conditions would you examine alongside the active error?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s101","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Reviewing a Wrong-Procedure Event","Systems thinking and safety"],"sources":[{"title":"Systems Approach","url":"https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/systems-approach"},{"title":"System-Focused Event Investigation and Analysis Guide - AHRQ","url":"https://www.ahrq.gov/patient-safety/settings/hospital/candor/modules/guide4.html"}]},{"id":"focus-s102","definition":"An arterial waveform is the line tracing pressure changes recorded by an arterial catheter and transducer. Its shape can help show whether the displayed pressure is being transmitted reliably.","structure":"The arterial catheter connects to fluid-filled tubing and a transducer, which displays the pressure waveform. The setup and transducer position provide context for interpreting the tracing and its pressure values.","normal":"Normally, pressure changes travel through fluid-filled tubing to the transducer, producing a recognizable waveform with a systolic upstroke, a decline, a dicrotic notch, and diastolic runoff. The displayed pattern helps relate the recorded values to the heart’s pressure cycle.","change":"With excessive damping, pressure transmission is blunted: the systolic value may appear low and the dicrotic notch may disappear. Insufficient damping can instead exaggerate the systolic value and add spikes. Air, clot, or a kink can interfere with transmission; waveform distortion warrants checking the setup and clinical context.","cues":"A flattened contour or missing dicrotic notch can suggest excessive damping; sharp spikes can suggest insufficient damping. A pressure that differs from the patient’s condition or a cuff reading is a cue to assess the signal, not proof that either measurement is correct.","distinction":"A striking systolic value does not by itself establish a true pressure change when the waveform is distorted. Mean pressure is less affected by damping, but waveform quality and the patient’s condition still matter.","related":["arterial","map","hypotension"],"title":"Arterial Waveform Quality","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Arteries, veins and pressure explains how pressure is transmitted through the circulation. This article extends that idea to the measurement pathway: tubing and transducer quality affect how faithfully arterial pressure changes appear on the waveform and displayed values.","readingAim":"What would you check when the displayed pressure and waveform do not fit the patient?","prerequisite":"arterial","skill":"s102","unit":"cardio","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Arterial Waveform Quality","Arterial waveform quality"],"sources":[{"title":"Arterial Lines - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499989/"},{"title":"Should I Target the Blood Pressure from the Arterial Line or ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12733910/"}]},{"id":"focus-s103","definition":"A vasoactive infusion affects vascular tone to support blood pressure. If the medicine leaks from a peripheral catheter, its local effects can injure nearby tissue.","structure":"The relevant context is the peripheral catheter and the surrounding skin and tissue, including areas near and beyond the insertion site. Both the local site and the patient’s overall hemodynamic response need assessment during the infusion.","normal":"Normally, a peripheral catheter delivers the ordered infusion into the circulation, where its vascular effects can support blood pressure. The site remains under observation because peripheral administration can provide timely treatment while requiring suitable access and vigilant monitoring.","change":"If the infusion leaks into nearby tissue, its vasoconstrictive effects can cause local injury. Swelling, blanching, coolness, discoloration, pain, leakage, skin injury, or vesicular changes may appear even while the infusion seems to run or the patient’s blood pressure improves.","cues":"New swelling, blanching, coolness, discoloration, leakage, pain, skin injury, or vesicular changes around the catheter raise concern for leakage or tissue injury. These findings call for prompt communication and reassessment; no single local sign alone confirms the cause.","distinction":"An improved blood pressure does not show that the catheter site is safe. A changing site needs attention even when the patient appears to respond systemically to the infusion.","related":["perfusion","arterial","shock","stroke-volume"],"title":"Peripheral Vasoactive Infusion","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Tissue perfusion links circulation with delivery of blood to tissues. Here, the infusion can support systemic pressure while leakage creates a separate local concern: assess the patient’s response and the catheter area rather than letting one reassure you about the other.","readingAim":"What site changes would you communicate despite an improved blood pressure?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s103","unit":"cardio","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Peripheral Vasoactive Infusion","Vasoactive infusions and response"],"sources":[{"title":"Navigating the veins: A comprehensive review of vasoactive ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13248048/"},{"title":"Feasibility and Safety of Peripheral Intravenous Administration of ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7648440/"}]},{"id":"focus-s104","definition":"A left ventricular assist device (LVAD) is an implanted pump that moves blood from the left ventricle to the aorta. Its external controller and power source connect through a driveline.","structure":"The LVAD pump moves blood from the left ventricle to the aorta. A driveline connects the implanted pump to an external controller and power source; adequate blood reaching the left ventricle supports pump flow.","normal":"Normally, the pump moves blood from the left ventricle into the aorta. Continuous-flow devices may produce little or no palpable pulse, although the patient’s own heart can add pulsatility. Assessment therefore considers the person’s perfusion and symptoms as well as device information.","change":"Pump flow can be impaired when less blood reaches the left ventricle, as with volume depletion, or with right ventricular failure, rhythm problems, or mechanical obstruction. A low-flow alarm can have several causes, so the device signal does not identify the cause by itself.","cues":"Weakness after diarrhea, a dry appearance, and a low-flow alarm can fit possible volume depletion, which may reduce blood reaching the pump. Assess perfusion, symptoms, rhythm, connections, and device information; the alarm alone does not establish why flow is low.","distinction":"An absent palpable pulse alone does not establish cardiac arrest or poor perfusion in a continuous-flow LVAD patient. A reassuring device display also cannot replace assessment of the person.","related":["output","preload","left-ventricle"],"title":"Ventricular Assist Devices","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Cardiac output describes blood flow produced by the heart. An LVAD adds a mechanical pump that moves blood from the left ventricle to the aorta, while pump flow still depends on blood reaching the ventricle and on the patient’s overall condition.","readingAim":"What would you assess before interpreting an LVAD low-flow alarm?","prerequisite":"output","skill":"s104","unit":"cardio","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Ventricular Assist Devices","Ventricular assist devices"],"sources":[{"title":"Left Ventricular Assist Device Management in the Emergency ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6123099/"},{"title":"Continuous&#x02010;flow left ventricular assist devices: Management in the emergency department","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7493560/pdf/EMP2-1-362.pdf"}]},{"id":"focus-s105","definition":"A tracheostomy provides a direct route into the trachea. Reliable airflow depends on a correctly positioned, patent tube and an open route for ventilation.","structure":"The airway context includes the tracheostomy tube, the trachea, and whether the patient also has a usable upper airway. For a laryngectomy patient, the airway is through the stoma, so the bedside airway information matters.","normal":"Normally, a correctly positioned, patent tracheostomy tube provides a route into the trachea for airflow and ventilation. The usable route depends on the patient’s airway anatomy, so distinguishing a patient with a patent upper airway from a laryngectomy patient informs the emergency pathway.","change":"A blocked inner cannula or displaced tube can interrupt oxygen delivery. Displacement may also direct air into surrounding tissue, and the tube may no longer provide a reliable tracheal route. Waveform capnography is an early assessment tool but does not prove ideal position.","cues":"New difficulty breathing or ventilating, suspected blockage or displacement, and a changed waveform capnography tracing are urgent cues. They support emergency assessment but do not alone establish the tube’s exact position or the cause of deterioration.","distinction":"A visible tracheostomy tube is not automatically a dependable airway. A changed capnography waveform warrants urgent assessment but does not by itself confirm ideal or exact tube position.","related":["airway","ventilation","oxygenation"],"title":"Tracheostomy Airway Emergencies","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Conducting airways explains the passages that carry air toward the lungs. A tracheostomy creates a direct route into the trachea, but movement, blockage, or displacement can disrupt that route; the patient’s anatomy and airway cues guide urgent assessment.","readingAim":"What would you assess and communicate if ventilation becomes difficult after a move?","prerequisite":"airway","skill":"s105","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Tracheostomy Airway Emergencies","Tracheostomy emergencies"],"sources":[{"title":"Airway Establishment and Control - Critical Care Medicine ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/critical-care-medicine/respiratory-arrest/airway-establishment-and-control?qt=tracheostomy"},{"title":"Update on management of tracheostomy","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7807977/pdf/main.pdf"}]},{"id":"focus-s106","definition":"Prone positioning turns a patient onto the abdomen to change how the lungs inflate and receive ventilation. In selected patients with moderate-to-severe ARDS, this can improve ventilation-perfusion matching and reduce uneven lung stress and strain.","structure":"The lungs contain many regions that expand during breathing, but illness can make their inflation uneven. During prone positioning, the chest and lung regions change orientation relative to gravity, redistributing ventilation and regional load; the face and other pressure points also contact the supporting surface.","normal":"In a more evenly functioning lung, air reaches regions that can expand, and ventilation is distributed with less regional imbalance. When a patient is repositioned, gravity and body position alter how lung regions inflate, so ventilation and mechanical load can become more evenly distributed across the lungs.","change":"In ARDS, prone positioning can make inflation and ventilation more even, improving ventilation-perfusion matching and reducing regional stress and strain. It does not simply inflate every collapsed region, and sustained contact with the support surface can also contribute to pressure injury or facial swelling.","cues":"Oxygenation may improve after turning, but that single change does not show that lung injury is limited or that the patient is otherwise stable. Follow trends in oxygenation, respiratory mechanics, hemodynamics, skin, and facial swelling; new pressure or swelling findings need attention.","distinction":"A better oxygen number is not proof that lung stress is reduced or that the whole intervention is safe. Proning redistributes uneven lung load, while pressure-related effects require separate observation.","related":["vq","oxygenation","skin"],"title":"Proning and Lung Protection","clinicalBridge":"Ventilation and perfusion matching describes how airflow and blood flow work together for gas exchange. Proning may improve that match by making lung inflation more even, but oxygenation alone cannot show whether regional stress is reduced or pressure effects are developing.","readingAim":"After oxygenation improves with proning, what other changes would you check before judging the response?","prerequisite":"vq","skill":"s106","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Proning and Lung Protection","ARDS proning and pressure protection"],"sources":[{"title":"Prone positioning in ARDS - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13562319/"},{"title":"Prone position in ARDS patients: why, when, how and for whom","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7652705/"}]},{"id":"focus-s107","definition":"A pleural drainage system removes air or fluid from the space around a lung so the lung can re-expand. Its water seal allows air to leave while limiting its return to the pleural space.","structure":"The pleural space lies between the lung surface and the chest wall. After thoracic surgery, a chest tube connects this space to a closed drainage unit; the unit collects fluid, and its water seal acts as a one-way gate, with suction used only as prescribed.","normal":"Normally, the pleural space supports lung expansion through pressure relationships between the lung and chest wall. With a closed drainage system, air or fluid can leave through the tube, while the water seal helps prevent air from flowing back into the pleural space.","change":"If air continues to enter the system, continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber can suggest an air leak. A change in breathing or oxygenation may accompany a patient problem, but chamber behavior alone cannot establish what is happening; tubing, dressing, and drainage-unit conditions also matter.","cues":"Water-seal movement with breathing can reflect tube patency, while continuous bubbling there can suggest an air leak. New shortness of breath and lower oxygen saturation alongside bubbling increase concern and call for prompt assessment and team communication; a quiet chamber alone proves little.","distinction":"Do not treat all chamber movement or bubbles as the same finding. Interpret the specific water-seal behavior alongside the patient’s respiratory status and the entire drainage system.","related":["pleura","oxygenation","airway"],"title":"Pleural Drainage After Surgery","clinicalBridge":"Pleural space and lung recoil helps explain why removing air or fluid can support lung re-expansion. Water-seal bubbling is a system observation, not a diagnosis; connect it with breathing, oxygenation, vital signs, and the visible condition of the tube and drainage unit.","readingAim":"How would you combine the patient’s respiratory findings with the specific water-seal behavior?","prerequisite":"pleura","skill":"s107","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Pleural Drainage After Surgery","Pleural drainage after thoracic surgery"],"sources":[{"title":"Care of a Chest Tube - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556088/"},{"title":"Chapter 6 Manage Chest Tube Drainage Systems - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594490/"}]},{"id":"focus-s108","definition":"ECMO moves blood through a pump and oxygenator to support gas exchange, circulation, or both while the heart or lungs recover. The support route depends on whether blood returns to the venous or arterial circulation.","structure":"ECMO circuits connect to blood vessels through cannulas and route blood through a pump and oxygenator. Venovenous ECMO returns blood to venous circulation and supports gas exchange; venoarterial ECMO returns blood to arterial circulation and can support both circulation and gas exchange.","normal":"In ordinary circulation, the heart moves blood through the lungs for gas exchange and then through the body. With venovenous ECMO, the circuit supports gas exchange while the heart circulates blood; with venoarterial ECMO, circuit return to the arteries also supports circulation.","change":"ECMO changes the route blood takes, and the mode affects how findings should be interpreted. With femoral arterial venoarterial support, the cannulated-side limb may have impaired perfusion; oxygenation can also differ between the upper and lower body, and circuit changes can signal oxygenator problems.","cues":"A newly cool or pale limb on the side of femoral arterial cannulation raises concern for local perfusion change, even if overall blood pressure is stable. In venoarterial support, different oxygenation readings between upper and lower body also warrant focused assessment and prompt communication.","distinction":"A stable overall blood pressure does not rule out impaired perfusion in one limb. Likewise, a reassuring oxygenator reading does not guarantee that every body region receives equally oxygenated blood.","related":["perfusion","circulation","oxygenation"],"title":"ECMO Mode and Perfusion","clinicalBridge":"Tissue perfusion means blood flow reaches body tissues. ECMO mode and cannula location help interpret whether a cool, pale limb or differing regional oxygenation may reflect a localized concern, even when an overall measure such as blood pressure has not changed.","readingAim":"What would you assess and communicate when a cannulated-side limb becomes cool and pale?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s108","unit":"resp","kind":"clinical","aliases":["ECMO Mode and Perfusion","ECMO support and surveillance"],"sources":[{"title":"Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation in Adults - NCBI - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK576426/"},{"title":"Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO): What We Need to Know","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9363689/"}]},{"id":"focus-s109","definition":"A postoperative epidural hematoma is a collection of blood near the spinal cord or nerve roots that can compress them. New or worsening pain, weakness, sensory loss, or bladder dysfunction can signal a developing neurologic problem.","structure":"The epidural space lies near the spinal cord and nerve roots at the operative level. After spinal surgery, blood at the site may be insignificant or may compress nearby neural structures; the clinical importance depends on symptoms and examination changes, with imaging helping evaluate the cause.","normal":"The spinal cord and nerve roots carry signals that support movement, sensation, and bladder function. After surgery, expected recovery should not involve a new neurologic deficit; comparing current motor, sensory, pain, and bladder findings with prior observations helps identify change.","change":"If blood accumulates in the operative epidural space and compresses the cord or nerve roots, pain may worsen and neurologic function may decline. Weakness or sensory loss can follow pain, but patterns vary; imaging helps determine whether a compressive hematoma is present.","cues":"Suddenly worsening back or radiating leg pain followed by weaker movement than on the prior assessment is concerning, but does not by itself prove the cause. New sensory loss or bladder dysfunction also merits urgent communication and focused neurologic observation.","distinction":"Do not dismiss a new deficit as routine postoperative pain, and do not assume that blood seen at the surgical site is necessarily causing symptoms. Symptoms require timely evaluation; the team determines the cause and next steps.","related":["nerve","cord","bladder"],"title":"Postoperative Neurologic Change","clinicalBridge":"Peripheral nerves and sensation connect neural function with bedside findings such as weakness, radiating pain, or sensory loss. After spinal surgery, comparing these observations with baseline can help communicate a changing pattern without assuming that one finding establishes a hematoma.","readingAim":"What changes would you communicate first when pain is followed by new weakness or sensory loss?","prerequisite":"nerve","skill":"s109","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Postoperative Neurologic Change","Spinal surgery: new neurologic deficit"],"sources":[{"title":"Postoperative Spinal Epidural Hematoma: Risk Factor and ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2688151/"},{"title":"Perspective: Postoperative spinal epidural hematomas (pSEH ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10629307/"}]},{"id":"focus-s110","definition":"ALS progressively weakens muscles used for breathing and coughing. This can make ventilation and airway clearance less effective, while other problems may also cause new respiratory symptoms.","structure":"The diaphragm is the main muscle supporting breathing, while respiratory muscles move air and cough helps clear the airways. In ALS, these structures are affected by progressive muscle weakness.","normal":"Normally, the diaphragm contracts to support inhalation, and respiratory muscles help move air in and out. An effective cough helps clear airway material. Together, these functions support breathing during activity, sleep, and changes in position.","change":"With ALS, progressive weakness can limit the diaphragm and other breathing muscles, making breathing harder during activity, at night, or while lying down. Weakness can also reduce cough effectiveness, impairing airway clearance and increasing vulnerability to respiratory deterioration.","cues":"New breathlessness, difficulty lying flat or breathing at night, increased work of breathing, or a weaker cough are reasons to assess and compare with baseline. Fever, crackles, or hypoxia may raise concern for infection or aspiration, but no single cue establishes the cause.","distinction":"Do not assume a new breathing change is ordinary ALS progression. A feeding tube does not prevent aspiration from secretions or reflux, and absence of fever or witnessed choking does not rule out an urgent problem.","related":["diaphragm","airway","oxygenation"],"title":"ALS Respiratory Weakness","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Diaphragm and work of breathing explains how diaphragm effort supports ventilation. In ALS, weakening respiratory muscles can make lying flat or sleeping harder and reduce cough effectiveness; comparing these changes with baseline connects the structures to the reported respiratory change without assuming its cause.","readingAim":"How might diaphragm and cough weakness affect breathing during sleep or when lying flat?","prerequisite":"diaphragm","skill":"s110","unit":"neuro","kind":"clinical","aliases":["ALS Respiratory Weakness","ALS and ventilatory weakness"],"sources":[{"title":"Emergencies in Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13749253/"},{"title":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) | Health","url":"https://health.nih.gov/health-topics-a-z/als"}]},{"id":"focus-s111","definition":"CRRT is a continuous treatment that removes fluid and solutes from the blood. Interruptions can make the treatment actually delivered differ from the prescribed plan.","structure":"CRRT operates through an extracorporeal circuit, where prescribed treatment intensity and net fluid removal are distinct goals. The treatment record and the patient’s cumulative intake and output provide context for evaluating what was delivered.","normal":"When CRRT runs as planned, the delivered treatment can be compared with its prescription, while fluid removal is tracked as a separate goal. Recording treatment time, interruptions, fluid removal, and intake and output helps the team evaluate progress toward both goals.","change":"A pause for a procedure, circuit issue, or filter change reduces treatment time and may alter the therapy actually delivered. A positive fluid balance despite a removal goal may reflect interruptions or changed fluid inputs; it does not by itself show that the prescription was inappropriate.","cues":"A positive cumulative balance alongside a prescribed net-removal goal is a signal to verify the treatment record and assess the patient. Documented pauses matter, but the balance alone cannot identify why the goals differ or establish whether the treatment plan was delivered.","distinction":"A prescription describes the intended treatment, not proof of what occurred. Do not infer delivered therapy from prescribed settings alone or independently change CRRT settings or fluid-removal targets.","related":["fluid","nephron","filtrate"],"title":"CRRT Delivery and Balance","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Fluid compartments helps frame why recorded intake, output, and net balance matter to the patient. For CRRT, compare those observations with actual treatment time and interruptions: a prescribed removal goal does not establish what fluid was removed or what treatment was delivered.","readingAim":"What might explain a positive balance when CRRT was prescribed to remove fluid?","prerequisite":"fluid","skill":"s111","unit":"renal","kind":"clinical","aliases":["CRRT Delivery and Balance","CRRT interruptions and fluid balance"],"sources":[{"title":"Drug Dosing During Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy in Critically Ill Patients: A Practical Narrative Review","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13708363/"},{"title":"How To Prescribe And Troubleshoot Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy: A Case-Based Review","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8741005"}]},{"id":"focus-s112","definition":"Acute mesenteric ischemia is impaired blood flow to the intestines that deprives tissue of oxygen. Intestinal injury can progress and contribute to systemic illness.","structure":"The mesenteric circulation supplies blood to the intestines through arterial inflow and venous outflow. The intestinal mucosa forms a barrier, and its condition can change as blood flow and oxygen delivery are disrupted.","normal":"Normally, mesenteric blood flow supplies the intestinal tissue with oxygen. The mucosal barrier helps contain intestinal contents. When circulation supports the tissue, this barrier helps limit the movement of bacteria and other substances into the body.","change":"When arterial inflow, venous outflow, or overall splanchnic perfusion is disrupted, intestinal tissue becomes oxygen-deprived. Mucosal injury can weaken the barrier and release bacteria, toxins, and vasoactive mediators, potentially contributing to systemic inflammation and organ failure.","cues":"Abrupt severe abdominal pain with a soft abdomen and little tenderness is concerning, particularly with atrial fibrillation or low-flow illness. Early tests, including lactate, may be unrevealing; worsening pain or later guarding and rigidity add concern but do not alone confirm the diagnosis.","distinction":"A soft abdomen or initially normal lactate does not rule out early mesenteric ischemia. Do not wait for peritoneal signs or late test changes before escalating concern about a time-critical presentation.","related":["gut","perfusion","inflammation"],"title":"Mesenteric Ischemia","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Intestinal mucosa and mesenteric flow links intestinal blood supply with the mucosal barrier. When flow is disrupted, severe pain may appear before obvious abdominal findings because tissue injury can be progressing while the barrier and surrounding structures have not yet produced late signs.","readingAim":"Why could severe abdominal pain precede marked tenderness or an abnormal early test?","prerequisite":"gut","skill":"s112","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Mesenteric Ischemia","Mesenteric ischemia"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia"},{"title":"Acute Mesenteric Ischemia - Gastrointestinal Disorders ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/acute-abdomen-and-surgical-gastroenterology/acute-mesenteric-ischemia?qt=Intestinal%20Ischemia"}]},{"id":"focus-s113","definition":"Portal hypertension can enlarge collateral veins in the esophagus or stomach into fragile varices. If a varix ruptures, it can cause severe upper gastrointestinal bleeding.","structure":"Portal-systemic collateral vessels form when blood is redirected around increased pressure in the portal circulation. In cirrhosis, enlarged fragile veins may develop in the distal esophagus or stomach and become a potential bleeding source.","normal":"Normally, blood flows through the portal circulation toward the liver. When portal pressure rises, blood can be redirected through collateral vessels. These vessels may enlarge over time, including as veins in the esophagus or stomach.","change":"With cirrhosis and portal hypertension, collateral veins can become enlarged and fragile. A rupture can cause acute bleeding, while cirrhosis may also be associated with nonvariceal sources such as ulcers, erosions, or a Mallory-Weiss tear.","cues":"Sudden hematemesis or melena in a person with liver disease raises concern for upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Tachycardia or falling blood pressure signals instability; brisk bleeding may appear as hematochezia. These findings do not establish that varices are the source.","distinction":"Cirrhosis raises concern for varices but does not identify the bleeding site; evaluation, including endoscopy, is needed to distinguish sources. An early normal hemoglobin does not outweigh active bleeding or signs of instability.","related":["abdominal-veins","liver","platelets"],"title":"Variceal Bleeding","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Portal-systemic collaterals and varices explains how increased portal pressure can redirect blood through fragile vessels. In cirrhosis, rupture may produce hematemesis or melena, but other sources can bleed too, so bedside cues signal urgency without confirming the site.","readingAim":"Why should bleeding cues in cirrhosis prompt concern without proving a variceal source?","prerequisite":"abdominal-veins","skill":"s113","unit":"gi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Variceal Bleeding","Variceal bleeding and cirrhosis"],"sources":[{"title":"Esophageal Varices - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf - NIH","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448078/"},{"title":"Varices - Gastroenterology - Merck Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/gastrointestinal-disorders/gastrointestinal-bleeding/varices"}]},{"id":"focus-s114","definition":"Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) and transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO) can both cause sudden breathing difficulty and lung infiltrates after a transfusion. They arise through different mechanisms, so the overall clinical pattern matters.","structure":"The lung’s alveoli and surrounding capillaries form the exchange surface where oxygen enters blood. In this case, acute injury to the capillary barrier or increased hydrostatic pressure can move fluid into the lungs and impair gas exchange.","normal":"Normally, the thin barrier between lung capillaries and alveoli allows oxygen and carbon dioxide to exchange while limiting fluid entry into air spaces. Blood flow and fluid balance support this exchange without flooding the alveoli.","change":"With TRALI, inflammatory activation can increase capillary permeability and allow fluid into lung tissue. With TACO, volume stress raises hydrostatic pressure and drives fluid outward. Either pathway can impair oxygen exchange and produce acute hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates after transfusion.","cues":"Abrupt dyspnea, worsening oxygenation, and bilateral infiltrates after transfusion signal acute lung trouble but do not identify its cause. Fever and relative hypotension can support consideration of TRALI; neck vein distention, hypertension, or edema can support TACO, but none is decisive alone.","distinction":"Bilateral infiltrates are a shared finding, not proof of either reaction. The absence of obvious volume-overload signs does not establish TRALI, and fever does not rule out TACO.","related":["alveoli","inflammation","edema"],"title":"Acute Lung Changes After Transfusion","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Alveoli and gas exchange connects the new hypoxemia to fluid affecting the lung exchange surface. In this case, transfusion timing and the pattern of fever, blood pressure, and volume cues help the team consider competing mechanisms without treating any one cue as diagnostic.","readingAim":"Which findings would shift your concern toward hydrostatic fluid movement rather than capillary leak?","prerequisite":"alveoli","skill":"s114","unit":"heme","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Acute Lung Changes After Transfusion","Transfusion lung injury or overload?"],"sources":[{"title":"Managing Fresh-Frozen Plasma Transfusion Adverse Effects: Allergic Reactions, TACO, and TRALI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK597370/"},{"title":"Pulmonary Complications of Transfused Blood Components","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3727894/"}]},{"id":"focus-s115","definition":"Hyponatremia is a low serum sodium concentration that may reflect excess water relative to body solute, rather than simply loss of sodium. If blood is hypotonic, water can shift into brain cells and cause neurologic changes.","structure":"Serum water and solute balance affects the brain, where cells respond to changes in blood tonicity. In this case, low measured serum osmolality provides context for interpreting the low sodium and the patient’s new inattention and nausea.","normal":"Normally, water and dissolved solutes remain balanced enough to limit harmful shifts across cell membranes. The brain’s cells function within this regulated environment, while the kidneys adjust water excretion in response to signals such as vasopressin.","change":"When serum becomes hypotonic, water moves into brain cells. Rapid changes can produce swelling and serious neurologic symptoms; chronic changes may be less obvious. Concentrated urine can be consistent with vasopressin activity, but it does not by itself establish the cause of hyponatremia.","cues":"New confusion, inattention, headache, nausea, gait change, seizure, or unusual drowsiness may accompany hyponatremia but are not specific to it. Relate symptoms to the sodium trend, measured osmolality, glucose, urine findings, volume cues, and medication history.","distinction":"A low sodium result does not prove that the patient has lost total-body sodium. Concentrated urine and a relevant medication are clues, not confirmation of SIADH.","related":["sodium","adh","cerebral"],"title":"Hyponatremia and Brain Changes","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Sodium and water shifts helps explain why a low measured osmolality matters: hypotonic blood can move water into brain cells. In this case, new inattention and nausea alongside the laboratory pattern warrant communication, while urine concentration and medication history remain clues rather than a diagnosis.","readingAim":"What change would make you treat this sodium pattern as more urgent to report?","prerequisite":"sodium","skill":"s115","unit":"endo","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Hyponatremia and Brain Changes","Sodium and neurologic change"],"sources":[{"title":"Checking your browser - reCAPTCHA","url":"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279055/"},{"title":"Hyponatremia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/portal/utils/pageresolver.fcgi?recordid=6aaf58fb42ea1349fbeb305c"}]},{"id":"focus-s116","definition":"Acute skin failure describes loss of skin barrier and temperature-regulating function associated with severe circulatory stress and reduced skin perfusion. Mottling can signal poor skin perfusion, but it does not establish skin failure.","structure":"The skin’s small blood vessels supply the tissue and help maintain its barrier and temperature-regulating functions. In this case, sepsis-related circulatory stress may reduce skin perfusion, while a discolored area over a pressure-bearing region also needs separate assessment.","normal":"Normally, blood flow supplies skin tissue, supports its barrier functions, and helps regulate heat. Skin appearance and temperature can vary, but circulation supports viable tissue; pressure-bearing areas also require assessment for localized injury rather than assumption based on location alone.","change":"During severe circulatory stress, reduced skin perfusion may contribute to cool or mottled skin and, in some cases, tissue damage. Mottling is not itself skin failure, and a lesion over a pressure-bearing area may have more than one possible explanation.","cues":"Mottling, cool or clammy skin, delayed capillary refill, or evolving discoloration can signal a perfusion concern. Interpret these observations alongside their distribution and progression, blood pressure, lactate, and overall illness; the findings alone do not name the skin condition.","distinction":"Mottling is a warning cue, not a diagnosis of acute skin failure. A pressure-bearing location alone does not establish pressure injury or distinguish it from skin failure.","related":["perfusion","skin","interstitium"],"title":"Skin Changes During Perfusion Stress","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Tissue perfusion links the patient’s sepsis and low blood pressure context to cool, mottled skin. The finding raises concern for reduced cutaneous blood flow, but lactate, pressure, lesion distribution, and evolution add context rather than proving skin failure or pressure injury.","readingAim":"What pattern of skin change would increase your concern for evolving tissue injury?","prerequisite":"perfusion","skill":"s116","unit":"skin","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Skin Changes During Perfusion Stress","Skin failure and pressure injury"],"sources":[{"title":"Incidence and risk factors of acute skin failure in patients with ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13713861/"},{"title":"Skin Failure: Results of a Think Tank Hosted by the National Pressure ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12537035/"}]},{"id":"focus-s117","definition":"Early mobility can help limit weakness and other effects of critical illness, sedation, and immobility. Readiness changes with the patient’s respiratory, cardiovascular, and neurologic status, so new instability calls for reassessment with the team.","structure":"Skeletal muscles, lungs, and the cardiovascular system work together during movement: muscles need oxygen delivery, while breathing and circulation respond to increased demand. In this case, new desaturation and reduced engagement alter the context for the planned transfer.","normal":"Normally, movement recruits skeletal muscles and increases their need for oxygen, while breathing and circulation adjust to support activity. A patient who is alert and stable may participate with coordinated support and attention to lines and devices.","change":"Critical illness and immobility can contribute to weakness and reduced function, while activity increases respiratory and circulatory demands. New desaturation or difficulty engaging during preparation changes the safety picture and warrants pausing the planned activity for team review.","cues":"New desaturation, altered consciousness, reduced cooperation, or cardiovascular instability during preparation can signal that the patient’s status has changed. These cues do not explain the cause, but they differ from prior stability and matter for reassessing the planned activity.","distinction":"A ventilator or an indwelling line alone does not automatically make mobilization unsafe. Conversely, prior cooperation and stability do not ensure readiness when new changes appear.","related":["muscle","perfusion","oxygenation"],"title":"Mobility Readiness and New Changes","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Skeletal muscle and deconditioning explains why mobility can support function while also increasing muscular oxygen demand. In this case, new desaturation and reduced engagement differ from the earlier stable pattern, so activity readiness needs team reassessment rather than reliance on prior status.","readingAim":"What new finding would make you pause the planned transfer and seek reassessment?","prerequisite":"muscle","skill":"s117","unit":"msk","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Mobility Readiness and New Changes","Early mobility and safety"],"sources":[{"title":"Effectiveness, Safety, and Barriers to Early Mobilization in the Intensive Care Unit","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7714600/"},{"title":"Protocolized strategies to encourage early mobilization of critical ...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11805459/"}]},{"id":"focus-s118","definition":"Placental abruption is the placenta separating from the uterus before birth. The separation can cause bleeding and reduce the area available for fetal oxygen exchange.","structure":"The placenta attaches to the uterine wall and provides the surface for maternal-fetal exchange. In abruption, part of that attachment separates before birth, and blood may collect behind the placenta or leave through the vagina.","normal":"Normally, the placenta remains attached to the uterine wall, providing a broad surface for exchange that supports fetal oxygen needs. Maternal blood circulates near this surface, while the fetus depends on the placenta to receive oxygen across it.","change":"When the placenta separates early, the surface available for fetal oxygen exchange is reduced. Blood may escape visibly or remain concealed behind the placenta. Continued or chronic separation can compromise fetal growth or reduce amniotic fluid, while maternal blood loss may also worsen.","cues":"Bleeding after midpregnancy, abdominal or uterine pain, tenderness, uterine irritability, shock, or a nonreassuring fetal heart pattern can raise concern. Bleeding may be slight or absent when concealed, and ultrasound may appear normal, so findings require clinical assessment.","distinction":"Scant bleeding or a normal ultrasound does not rule out abruption. Pain and tenderness may favor abruption over the often painless bleeding of previa, but symptoms overlap and previa must be excluded before pelvic examination.","related":["placenta","oxygen-debt","perfusion"],"title":"Placental Separation and Exchange","clinicalBridge":"Placental circulation normally supports fetal oxygen exchange across an attached placenta. In this case, trauma followed by uterine pain, tenderness, scant bleeding, and fetal heart changes raises concern that separation has reduced the exchange surface, even though ultrasound shows no collection.","readingAim":"What combination of maternal and fetal findings would keep you concerned despite scant bleeding?","prerequisite":"placenta","skill":"s118","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Placental Separation and Exchange","Obstetric hemorrhage"],"sources":[{"title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - Gynecology and ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/gynecology-and-obstetrics/antenatal-complications/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae"},{"title":"Placental Abruption (Abruptio Placentae) - Women's Health ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/home/women-s-health-issues/complications-of-pregnancy/placental-abruption-abruptio-placentae"}]},{"id":"focus-s119","definition":"Accidental hypothermia occurs when a person becomes dangerously cold after environmental exposure. Changes in consciousness can signal increased risk, even when the person can still speak.","structure":"The brain supports awareness and responsiveness, while the myocardium must maintain circulation. During accidental hypothermia, cooling affects these structures in different ways: brain oxygen demand may fall, but the cold myocardium can become unstable and vulnerable during movement.","normal":"With adequate circulation, the brain receives oxygen and supports usual awareness, while the myocardium maintains the circulation that delivers oxygen. In cold conditions, reduced brain oxygen demand may offer some resilience when circulation is poor, but this protection is limited.","change":"As hypothermia impairs consciousness, confusion or reduced responsiveness can signal greater clinical concern and is associated with greater cardiac arrest risk. At the same time, a cold, unstable myocardium may deteriorate with movement; rescue collapse can involve afterdrop and other physiologic changes.","cues":"Ask about cold exposure and assess the person’s exact responsiveness, circulation, rhythm, and any reliable temperature reading. Unusual behavior or confusion matters even if the patient answers questions. Other conditions can also alter consciousness, and shivering or estimated temperature is unreliable.","distinction":"Being able to speak does not establish normal cognition or low risk. Altered consciousness is a warning sign, not a precise measure of core temperature.","related":["temperature","conduction","cerebral"],"title":"Cognition in Accidental Hypothermia","clinicalBridge":"Temperature describes the cold exposure context, but cognition adds an important clinical signal. Here, the patient can answer questions yet cannot follow a simple instruction; that confusion is associated with cardiac arrest risk in hypothermia, so apparent wakefulness should not reassure the nurse.","readingAim":"What change in responsiveness would make you raise concern in a cold patient who can speak?","prerequisite":"temperature","skill":"s119","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Cognition in Accidental Hypothermia","Accidental hypothermia"],"sources":[{"title":"Accidental hypothermia–an update: The content of this review is endorsed by the International Commission for Mountain Emergency Medicine (ICAR MEDCOM)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/5025630"},{"title":"Accidental hypothermia: direct evidence for consciousness as a marker of cardiac arrest risk in the acute assessment of cold patients","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/8895778"}]},{"id":"focus-s120","definition":"Graft rejection occurs when a recipient’s immune system attacks a transplanted organ. New changes in the graft’s function can raise concern, but symptoms such as fever are not specific to rejection.","structure":"An allograft is an organ transplanted from another person into the recipient. The recipient’s immune system can recognize and attack it; immunosuppressive treatment reduces rejection risk but also weakens defenses against infection.","normal":"Normally, the transplanted organ performs its role while the recipient’s immune response is controlled by prescribed immunosuppressive treatment. Graft function is assessed through organ-specific findings and trends, interpreted in the context of the time since transplantation.","change":"If immune activity injures the graft, its function may decline. Rejection patterns differ by mechanism and timing: hyperacute rejection involves preexisting antibodies, acute rejection can be T-cell-mediated or antibody-mediated, and chronic rejection involves progressive graft injury and vascular change.","cues":"After kidney transplant, rising creatinine, reduced urine output, hypertension, weight gain, or graft tenderness can signal new dysfunction. Fever may occur with rejection but also raises concern for infection. Timing helps interpretation, yet no single finding confirms the cause.","distinction":"Fever alone does not prove rejection; infection and other causes remain possible. Interpret it alongside graft-specific changes, laboratory trends, and timing after transplant.","related":["nephron","neutrophils","barrier"],"title":"Recognizing Graft Dysfunction","clinicalBridge":"The nephron supports kidney filtration and urine formation, so rising creatinine and reduced urine output can point to changing kidney graft function. One week after transplant, fever with graft tenderness adds concern for rejection, while infection remains a competing possibility requiring team evaluation.","readingAim":"Which graft-specific changes and timing would you include when reporting fever after transplant?","prerequisite":"nephron","skill":"s120","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Recognizing Graft Dysfunction","Graft dysfunction and rejection"],"sources":[{"title":"Acute transplantation rejection","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535410/"},{"title":"Overview of Transplantation - Immunology; Allergic Disorders - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/transplantation/overview-of-transplantation?ruleredirectid=743"},{"title":"Overview of Transplantation - Overview of Transplantation - MSD Manual Professional Edition","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/en-gb/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/transplantation/overview-of-transplantation"}]},{"id":"focus-s121","definition":"SIRS is a body-wide inflammatory response to infection or noninfectious stress. It describes a pattern of response, not a diagnosis of infection or organ failure.","structure":"SIRS involves systemic immune and endothelial responses throughout the body, including effects on coagulation and the microcirculation. Infection, trauma, surgery, pancreatitis, and other sterile stressors can trigger this response and create a need to assess organ function.","normal":"Normally, endothelial function, coagulation, and microcirculation help sustain blood flow and support organ function. Inflammation can be part of the response to infection or tissue injury, but the presence of a response pattern alone does not establish organ dysfunction.","change":"With SIRS, inflammatory pathways can disrupt endothelial function, coagulation, and microcirculation. This may compromise organ function. When infection causes life-threatening organ dysfunction, the condition is sepsis; dysfunction in two or more organ systems is described as MODS.","cues":"A pattern or trend in temperature, pulse, breathing, and white blood cell findings may signal systemic stress. New confusion, rising oxygen needs, impaired perfusion, or declining urine output may suggest organ dysfunction, but context is needed to interpret the cause.","distinction":"SIRS does not by itself establish infection or predict organ failure. Conversely, an incomplete SIRS pattern does not guarantee that organ function is stable.","related":["inflammation","perfusion","oxygen-debt"],"title":"SIRS and Organ Function","clinicalBridge":"Inflammation can follow sterile tissue stress as well as infection. With pancreatitis, rising pulse and breathing rates and a temperature change may reflect SIRS, but new confusion and increasing oxygen needs raise concern for evolving organ dysfunction and warrant prompt communication.","readingAim":"Why should you assess changing organ function even when infection has not been confirmed?","prerequisite":"inflammation","skill":"s121","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["SIRS and Organ Function","SIRS and evolving organ failure"],"sources":[{"title":"Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome: molecular mechanisms and ... - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13365620/"},{"title":"Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome - StatPearls - NCBI","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547669/"}]},{"id":"focus-s122","definition":"Anaphylaxis can cause airway narrowing through bronchoconstriction and tissue swelling. Angioedema is deeper swelling that can obstruct the upper airway, with or without hives.","structure":"The upper airway—the tongue, pharynx, and laryngeal entrance—forms the passage from the mouth and nose toward the lungs. Here, the bedside decision concerns a rapidly changing airway after a suspected medication-triggered reaction.","normal":"Normally, an open upper airway lets air pass with little resistance, and the voice remains clear. When swelling or narrowing develops, airflow may become restricted; anaphylaxis can also cause bronchoconstriction, adding lower-airway difficulty to upper-airway changes.","change":"Normally, the tongue and throat leave room for air to pass and speech to remain clear. In this case, mediator-related swelling after a likely trigger may narrow that passage; deeper angioedema can obstruct it, even without hives, while bronchoconstriction may contribute to breathing difficulty.","cues":"New hoarseness, noisy breathing, tongue or lip swelling, and worsening breathing after a likely trigger are concerning together. Stridor can suggest upper-airway narrowing, but no single cue confirms its cause; monitor for progression and escalate urgently.","distinction":"Hives are not required for dangerous angioedema: their absence does not make airway swelling safe. Wheezing and stridor can point to different airway locations, but either breathing change in this context warrants urgent attention.","related":["airway","inflammation","barrier"],"title":"Evolving Upper-Airway Swelling","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Conducting airways helps locate the concern: swelling at the tongue or throat can restrict the passage for airflow, while bronchoconstriction affects lower airways. Linking structure to hoarseness, stridor, or increasing breathing difficulty supports prompt escalation without waiting for hives or testing.","readingAim":"What airway change would make you escalate promptly?","prerequisite":"airway","skill":"s122","unit":"multi","kind":"clinical","aliases":["Evolving Upper-Airway Swelling","Recognizing a deteriorating airway"],"sources":[{"title":"Anaphylaxis - Allergy and Immunology - Merck Manual ...","url":"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/allergic-autoimmune-and-other-hypersensitivity-disorders/anaphylaxis?qt=anaphylaxis"},{"title":"Angioedema - Allergy and Immunology - MSD Manual Professional ...","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/immunology-allergic-disorders/allergic-autoimmune-and-other-hypersensitivity-disorders/angioedema?autoredirectid=24802"}]},{"id":"focus-s123","definition":"Post-intensive care syndrome (PICS) describes new or worsened physical, cognitive, or mental health impairment after critical illness. Consider other contributors alongside the patient’s pre-illness baseline. These concerns can overlap and affect life after discharge.","structure":"The follow-up conversation is the context for comparing the patient’s current function with their pre-illness baseline. The nurse gathers the patient’s and, when appropriate, family’s observations across mobility, daily activities, thinking, and mood.","normal":"Supportive follow-up invites the patient to describe changes from their usual function, including mobility, daily tasks, memory, attention, and mood. The nurse listens across these domains, uses family observations when appropriate, and communicates concerns without presuming a diagnosis.","change":"Normally, recovery conversations consider physical, cognitive, and emotional function together. Here, difficulty climbing stairs, missed appointments, family-observed forgetfulness, and anxiety span several domains; they merit assessment and communication, but do not by themselves establish PICS or exclude other causes.","cues":"Weakness affecting stairs, difficulty keeping track of appointments, family-noted forgetfulness, and persistent anxiety are signals to explore together. Their timing after critical illness is relevant, but each concern is uncertain in cause and should be compared with baseline.","distinction":"Improving strength does not establish complete recovery, because cognitive or emotional concerns may remain. Conversely, changes after an ICU stay should not automatically be labeled PICS; other causes may need evaluation.","related":["focus-s093","focus-s099","focus-s117"],"title":"Assessing Recovery Across Domains","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Skeletal muscle and deconditioning can help frame the reported difficulty climbing stairs as a physical-function concern. It does not account for forgetfulness or anxiety, so pair this observation with questions across cognitive and emotional domains and communicate the combined picture to the care team.","readingAim":"What other recovery domains would you assess alongside mobility?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s123","unit":"psych","kind":"practice","aliases":["Assessing Recovery Across Domains","Supporting recovery after an ICU stay"],"sources":[{"title":"Post–intensive care syndrome","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10772956/"},{"title":"Epidemiology","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558964/"},{"title":"Post-intensive Care Syndrome: an Overview - PMC - NIH","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5506407/"}]},{"id":"focus-s124","definition":"Spirituality can involve meaning, purpose, and connection through faith, relationships, nature, art, or other sources. Culturally responsive care asks what support the individual wants rather than inferring it from identity or religious affiliation.","structure":"The conversation between nurse and patient is the decision context: the patient expresses uncertainty about what is happening and becomes tearful. The nurse can acknowledge this cue and ask what, if any, support would feel helpful.","normal":"In person-centered communication, the nurse listens without imposing an interpretation, acknowledges expressed feelings, and asks what gives the person strength or hope. The patient chooses whether to discuss beliefs or accept support, and may request quiet, a chaplain, or another form of connection.","change":"Normally, support follows the patient’s stated preference rather than assumptions. Here, the patient’s question and tears invite gentle clarification; saying they are not religious does not specify what support they want, nor does it establish spiritual distress or well-being.","cues":"The patient’s words and tears are cues to pause and ask what the experience means to them. Their statement that they are not religious does not determine whether they want conversation, quiet, a chaplain, or no additional support.","distinction":"Tears do not prove spiritual distress, and not being religious does not mean a person lacks sources of meaning or connection. Ask rather than infer, and do not substitute the nurse’s beliefs for the patient’s preference.","related":["focus-s100","focus-s096","focus-s125"],"title":"Asking About Sources of Support","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Pain pathways and perception reminds nurses that a reported physical experience and an expressed need for meaning are different kinds of information. Attend to each without treating one as an explanation for the other, and let the patient guide whether any additional support is wanted.","readingAim":"What could you ask to learn what support the patient wants?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s124","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Asking About Sources of Support","Culturally responsive care"],"sources":[{"title":"Chapter 18 Spirituality","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK591821/"},{"title":"35.2 Applications of Spirituality to Health Care ... - OpenStax","url":"https://openstax.org/books/fundamentals-nursing/pages/35-2-applications-of-spirituality-to-health-care"}]},{"id":"focus-s125","definition":"Palliative care can support quality of life alongside disease-directed treatment by addressing physical symptoms and psychological, social, and spiritual needs. The patient’s own priorities guide what support is offered.","structure":"The nurse-patient conversation is the decision context: the patient reports pain and says they no longer know what gives them strength, while the daughter requests prayer. The nurse attends to the patient’s stated needs and preferences.","normal":"Person-centered care addresses reported discomfort within nursing scope, orders, and local protocols, while also making space for emotional and spiritual concerns. The nurse listens, asks what matters to the patient, and coordinates requested support with the patient’s agreement.","change":"Normally, the patient’s preferences guide spiritual or cultural support. Here, pain calls for assessment, and the patient’s words invite an open question about what might help; the daughter’s request does not establish that the patient wants prayer or other support.","cues":"The reported pain and statement about lost sources of strength are distinct cues to explore. The daughter’s request provides context but does not confirm the patient’s preference; ask the patient what matters and what support, if any, they want.","distinction":"Palliative care does not require stopping disease-directed treatment. A family member’s request for prayer is not a substitute for asking the patient, and the patient’s words alone do not establish a particular belief or desired support.","related":["focus-s096","focus-s124","focus-s088"],"title":"Responding to Patient Priorities","clinicalBridge":"The foundation topic Pain pathways and perception helps distinguish the patient’s reported pain as a physical concern that needs attention from the statement about strength as an invitation to explore support. Assess both on their own terms, and let the patient guide any requested spiritual or other care.","readingAim":"How will you address pain while learning what support the patient wants?","prerequisite":null,"skill":"s125","unit":"ethics","kind":"practice","aliases":["Responding to Patient Priorities","Comfort, dignity and caring practices"],"sources":[{"title":"The Delivery of Person-Centered, Family-Oriented End-of-Life Care","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK285676/"},{"title":"Chapter 18 Spirituality - Nursing Fundamentals - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591821/table/"}]}];
const ROUTE=[{"id":"oxygen","title":"From air to a living cell","level":"Foundations","why":"Trace one oxygen molecule from the air to the cell that needs it. Then distinguish a low oxygen reading from low delivery to tissue.","items":["cells","circulation","alveoli","gas-gradient","oxygenation","ventilation","hemoglobin","exchange","perfusion","hypoperfusion","hypotension","s016","s052"]},{"id":"signals","title":"Read the bedside signals","level":"Foundations → assessment","why":"Connect pressure, electrical activity, breathing, attention, skin and urine output to the structures that produce those signals.","items":["arterial","output","stroke-volume","preload","afterload","map","s001","conduction","s005","neurons","arousal","s027","nephron","oliguria","s040","skin","s065","pain","sleep","s085","s098","s099"]},{"id":"system","title":"Understand one organ at a time","level":"Mechanisms","why":"Learn what each organ normally regulates, then use a common change in that function to explain a finding.","items":["left-ventricle","s004","coronary","ischemia","s002","airway","diaphragm","s020","swallow","s021","pleura","s022","csf","icp","cerebral","s033","filtrate","bladder","s041","gut","s044","liver","s045","pancreas","s046","insulin","s061","adh","sodium","s062","fluid","s076","buffer","s075","marrow","neutrophils","s055","platelets","s054","barrier","s068","muscle","s072","delirium","s089"]},{"id":"urgent","title":"Recognize a time-sensitive threat","level":"Clinical application","why":"Compare threats with overlapping signs. Ask what is blocked, compressed, underfilled or unable to exchange oxygen.","items":["shock","fluid-loss","s074","s003","aorta","s006","pericardium","s007","s013","pulmonary-artery","embolus","vq","s019","s023","s122","s028","s029","s038","s039","cord","s030","compartment","nerve","s069","s070","s067","interstitium","s066","s112","s047","peritoneum","s048"]},{"id":"connected","title":"Connect failing systems","level":"Integrated reasoning","why":"Follow the consequence of an initial injury into another organ. Interpret trends and avoid treating a single sign as proof of a cause.","items":["inflammation","lactate","s073","s121","edema","surfactant","s018","s024","s042","s034","s035","s053","s056","s058","ketones","s059","s060","cortisol","s063","thyroid","s064","potassium","s115","phosphate","s051","myoglobin","s071","oxygen-debt","s084","s083","temperature","s082","s119","s077"]},{"id":"transitions","title":"Anticipate complications and transitions","level":"Complex assessment","why":"Use anatomic relationships to anticipate what could change after trauma, surgery, transfusion or a change in the care setting.","items":["valves","s011","s012","s014","s009","s008","s025","s049","anastomosis","s050","abdominal-veins","s113","s037","s109","s057","s114","placenta","s080","s118","s078","s079","s120","s116"]},{"id":"support","title":"Understand organ support","level":"Advanced application","why":"Connect a monitor or device to the physiology it can support, what its signal actually measures, and what it may miss.","items":["s102","s010","s103","s015","s104","peep","s017","s106","s107","s105","s108","s026","nmj","s036","s110","s031","s032","s043","s111","s081"]},{"id":"person","title":"Integrate recovery and person-centered care","level":"Synthesis","why":"Bring physiology, function and the patient’s own priorities into the same reasoning process. Finish with cumulative clinical rounds.","items":["s087","s086","s117","s090","s091","s092","s093","s123","s094","s095","s096","s100","s124","s088","s125","s097","s101"]}];
